of Teflon materials: Polytetrafluoroethylene (TFE),
and fluorinated ethylene-propylene (FEP). TFE is a
straight chain structure of carbon and fluorine that
begins to sublime into a gas above 620° F. FEP is a
branched chain chemical structure that becomes a
fluid above 500° F.
In the Apollo spacecraft, Teflon was used as (1) a
coating for Beta fibers to reduce fiber friction, (2) a
fabric for coveralls (fig. 20), (3) a film to wrap
electric wire harnesses, and (4) insulation for electric
wires.

