MATERIALS USAGE CATEGORIES

A major consideration in the development of the MSC criteria has
been the actual usage of materials in the spacecraft. This is important
because different material usages dictate the specific test and acceptance criteria to be applied. For example, a large amount of material in
a critical area of the crew bay requires more flammability tests and a
more restrictive acceptance criteria than a minor exposed nonmetallic
material. For this reason, material usages were divided into different
categories as discussed earlier in this paper.
The following is a description of each category used to identify
where materials are used in the spacecraft.
1. Category A — Major exposed materials in the crew-bay atmosphere: This category includes material applications that are essentially
unlimited with respect to quantity, proximity to ignition sources, or
exposure to cabin atmosphere and are used extensively throughout the
spacecraft. As a minimum, the materials that fulfill the following functions are included in this category.
a.

Debris traps or nets

b.

Outer layers of space-suit materials

c.

Couch cushions
