APPENDIX B

ELECTRICAL WIRE INSULATION AND ACCESSORY FLAMMIBILITY TEST
OF LUNAR TEST ARTICLE 8 KYNAR SLEEVING

TEST OBJECTIVES

This test was designed to screen flammable wire insulation and
electrical-accessory materials that were proposed for use in habitable
areas of the lunar module. In addition, the test was designed to establish that electrical wire insulation and accessories were not capable
of sustaining combustion in the designated test atmosphere after removal
of the ignition power source, which provided current overloads up to
the melting point of the electrical wire.
COMPONENT DESCRIPTION

The test bundle of seven H-film-insulated wires, of which six were
12 inches long and one was 13 inches long, was inserted into a length
of lunar test article 8 (LTA-8) Kynar sleeving. One-half inch of insulation was stripped from each end of the 13-inch wire. A crimp spade lug
terminal was used at each end to terminate the overloaded 13-inch wire
and to provide a heat sink.
The sample holder consisted of two horizontally mounted electrical
connections (knurled bolts spaced 12 inches apart). Three nonflammable
supports were provided to support the center and ends of the sample wire
bundle. The electrical terminals were connected to the ignition power
source ( f i g . B-l).
IGNITION LOCATION

The external electrical power supply was connected to the electrical
terminals on the 13-inch wire in the sample bundle. The power source was
capable of providing a large, steady current through the 13-inch wire so
that a high temperature was achieved quickly. In addition, the power
source was capable of supplying a current 10 percent greater than the
nominal fusion current of the wire.

TEST DESCRIPTION

The sample was mounted in the holder and installed in the test
chamber. The test chamber was set up to provide an atmosphere of
30 percent oxygen/70 percent nitrogen at a pressure of 1^.7 psia. A
UO-ampere current was applied to the wire; the current was increased
in 5-ampere increments at 1-minute intervals.
Smoke was observed at 90 seconds after test initiation, at which
time the current was ^5 amperes. At 100 seconds, the Kynar melted
and the H-film bundle was exposed. At ikO seconds, when a current of
50 amperes was applied, the Kynar sleeving ignited and burned vigorously,
and the wire failed at lU3 seconds. The material continued to burn
even after the ignitor power had been removed.
POST-TEST RESULTS
From the post-test inspection, it was determined that the Kynar
sleeving had burned away from the wire bundle almost completely. Only
a small portion of the sleeving remained at the ends of the wire bundle
near the electrical connections. The wire had failed and was severed
in several pla"
ntrs:19730064278,ntrs,Proceedings of the NASA Conference on Materials for Improved Fire Safety,,41,"from the wire bundle almost completely. Only
a small portion of the sleeving remained at the ends of the wire bundle
near the electrical connections. The wire had failed and was severed
in several places ( f i g . B-2). The material was not self-extinguishing
in the test atmosphere. From this flammability test, it was determined
that the sleeving was unacceptable for use in the prescribed environment.

