LUNAR MODULE MOCKUP TESTS

In addition to the command module tests, 72 flammability tests were
conducted in an interior mockup of the LM in a 100-percent-oxygen atmosphere at 5-8 psia, which is a worst-case pressure for the LM. (The nominal caMn operating pressure is 5-0 psia.) Fires were started at
locations selected as representative of potential LM ignition hazards.
Three tests (12, 16, and Ul) are discussed in this paper.

Test 12
The purpose of this test was to determine the flammability characteristics of the PLSS and the possibility of fire propagation to other materials. An external ignitor was located on the bottom left-hand rear
edge of the PLSS hard case that was not protected by the Beta thermal
cover (fig. 5-17).
Test.- Ignition occurred at T + 18 seconds. The flame remained
localized in the lower left-hand corner until T + 1 minute UO seconds.
At T + 1 minute U5 seconds, soot and smoke were observed, and the flame
slowly propagated upward. More soot and smoke were observed at
T + 3 minutes 30 seconds, and haze formed in the cabin at T + k minutes.
The flame propagated almost to the top of the PLSS at T + 6 minutes
55 seconds. The cabin was hazy, but visibility was not reduced appreciably. Sparks flew out into the cabin. The test was terminated at
T + 7 minutes 30 seconds. At termination, the flame impinged on the
bottom and front of the onboard-data stowage container.
Visibility.- The cabin atmosphere was hazy, but visibility was not
reduced appreciably.
Temperature and pressure.- No significant cabin ambient-temperature
or pressure increase was observed.
Gas analysis (for toxic products).- The analysis indicated 9-5 ppm
carbon tetrafluoride present in the gas sample.
Post-test inspection.- Inspection revealed that the fire propagated
on the PLSS hard case under the Beta thermal cover. The entire lefthand side and approximately one-third of the top and bottom of the PLSS
hard cover were destroyed. The inside of the PLSS was covered with a
layer of soot. Soot particles were present throughout the cabin, and
some burning or melting of the PLSS therma"
ntrs:19730064278,ntrs,Proceedings of the NASA Conference on Materials for Improved Fire Safety,,55,"bottom of the PLSS
hard cover were destroyed. The inside of the PLSS was covered with a
layer of soot. Soot particles were present throughout the cabin, and
some burning or melting of the PLSS thermal cover occurred (figs. 5-18
and 5-19).
Summary.- The results of this test indicated that, although the fire
was somewhat slow in starting, the material in the PLSS hard cover
5-12

provided the fuel and propagation path that caused the localized fire to
become widespread. The purpose of this test was to determine the extent
of dripping or sparking and the possibility of secondary ignition of materials such as the Viton protective floor cover, the suit with Lexan
helmet, or Velcro on the ascent-engine cover.

Test 16
Ignition was caused by an electrically overloaded Teflon (TFE), netjacketed, silver-plated, copper-shielded cable located above the Trevarno
(fiber glass with silicon and resin) ceiling panel and the commander's
station. The overload was caused in a 22-gage, single-shielded conductor
housed in the cable (fig. 5-20).
Test.- Ignition occurred at T + 2 minutes U5 seconds on the forward
end of the wire above the center ceiling. Flame and sparking were observed, and the fire propagated for some distance along the cable. The
65-ampere overload current was intermittent, indicating a possible electrical short to the metal jacket. No smoke buildup in the cabin area
was observed, and the fire self-extinguished. The test was concluded at
T + 8 minutes.
Visibility.- Visibility in the cabin was not affected.
Temperature and pressure.- No cabin ambient-temperature or pressure
increase was observed.
Gas analysis (for toxic products).- The analysis indicated 3.1 ppm
carbon tetrafluoride present in the gas sample.
Post-test inspection.- Damage occurred along the full length of the
metal cable jacket. The silver plating on the copper jacket was burned
off, leaving copper exposed in that zone. No damage was done to the
Trevarno ceiling. Data recorded during the test showed that the current
varied during the time of the sparking, which would indicate that the
overloaded wire experienced intermittent shorting to the metal jacket
(fig. 5-21).
Summary.- An excessive amount of flashing and sparking occurred;
however, no fire propagated to other materials, and only localized damage
resulted from this test.

Test 1+1
A test more severe than the preceding ones was required to evaluate
the thermal protection provided by the space suit when exposed to a fire
5-13

of considerable magnitude. It was also necessary to determine whether
propagation to adjacent panels would occur. An external ignitor was
located between the pages on the left-hand side of the procedures manual
mounted above the display and keyboard (DSKY) on panel h. In addition,
two food packets were located on the procedures manual (fig. 5-22).
Test.- The procedures manual was ignited and, along with the food
packets, burned for 30 minutes before self-extinguishment, at which time
the test was terminate"
ntrs:19730064278,ntrs,Proceedings of the NASA Conference on Materials for Improved Fire Safety,,56,"on the procedures manual (fig. 5-22).
Test.- The procedures manual was ignited and, along with the food
packets, burned for 30 minutes before self-extinguishment, at which time
the test was terminated. The most severe fire occurred during the first
5 minutes; then the fire gradually subsided to a low level.
Visibility.- Visibility was not affected.
Temperature and pressure.- This test produced some significant increases in cabin ambient and component temperatures, but no increase in
cabin pressure. The maximum cabin ambient-temperature increase, from
72° to 120° F, occurred at the rear of the cabin 20 minutes after ignition. This temperature receded to a level of 100° F approximately
30 minutes after ignition. A maximum temperature of l80° F occurred
inside the helmet. The maximum temperature inside the suit was 150° F,
and the maximum external suit temperature was 210° F. It should be
noted that the suit environmental control system was not functioning
during this test. At a thermocouple location 30 inches above the ignition point, the temperature reached a maximum of l60° F.
Gas analysis (for toxic products).- The analysis indicated 30.7 percent carbon dioxide, 126 ppm carbon monoxide, and 1.8 ppm carbon tetrafluoride present in the gas sample. Ethane and methane were detected,
but no quantitative results were obtained.
Post-test inspection.- The procedures manual and food packets were
destroyed completely. The wire insulation to the attitude control assembly was destroyed, and the handle was darkened from the heat. The
Trevarno guard around the DSKY was damaged, and the EL overlays on
panel 3 warped and cracked. Other areas and the suit were not damaged
(fig. 5-23).
Summary.- Despite the magnitude of the fire, no significant propagation or heat damage occurred to materials other than those in the immediate vicinity of the fire.

MOCKUP TEST SUMMARY

Because most of the nonmetallic materials in the command module had
been tested previously at a component level and found acceptable, the

5-lU

high degree of flammability of some materials and the substantial areas
affected ""by some of the fires were unexpected. For example, in a
l6.2-psia, 100-percent-oxygen atmosphere, the degree of flame propagation along the Ladicote flame-retardant coating on the back of the
electrical panels was much greater than expected. Before full-scale
testing was initiated, a number of component tests had indicated that
Ladicote was a satisfactory flame retardant; however, during fullscale testing, the Ladicote failed to prevent excessive propagation.
Other tests that showed certain components to be unacceptable in the
spacecraft configuration (although component tests had shown these items
to be acceptable) were the tests associated with the silicone-laminate
scuff covers, Teflon chafe guard, and silicone wire-bundle spacers. The
variations in the results of component tests when compared to the results of full-scale tests indicate that , although individual-componen"
ntrs:19730064278,ntrs,Proceedings of the NASA Conference on Materials for Improved Fire Safety,,57,"rs, Teflon chafe guard, and silicone wire-bundle spacers. The
variations in the results of component tests when compared to the results of full-scale tests indicate that , although individual-component
testing remains a necessary first step, the results cannot be taken as
conclusive when applied to a complete spacecraft configuration. Such
variables as location, heat-sink characteristics, airflow patterns,
proximity to other materials, which can be represented only in fullscale tests , are significant factors in determining the flammability
characteristics of individual and integrated components.
A summary of the command module and lunar module tests is presented
in table I. As indicated in this table, the number of unacceptable fires
in the command module tests was notably proportional to the oxygen partial pressure.
Corrective action was taken for those cases requiring
termination or when inadequate or marginal egress time occurred.

CORRECTIVE ACTIONS

Command Module
Teflon wire wrap, 16.2 psia, 100 percent oxygen.- No satisfactory
replacement materials or alternate systems have been found to replace
the Teflon chafe wraps; therefore, the test results were instrumental
in the decision to use a 60-percent-oxygen/^O-percent-nitrogen launch
atmosphere in the command module. No hazard exists in a 6.2-psia,
100-percent-oxygen environment.
Circuit breaker panels, 16.2 p s i a , 60 percent oxygen/UQ percent
nitrogen.- The protection used is adequate for all spacecraft. The
only propagation that could occur is during prelaunch operations. In
this situation, the crew would have adequate time to egress. No hazard
exists in 6.2-psia, 100-percent-oxygen atmosphere; therefore, no corrective action was required.

5-15

Protection against toxic products of combustion.- All of the data on
composition of spacecraft cabin atmospheres during and after the fire
tests were furnished to the Medical Research and Operations Directorate
for assessment of hazard to the crew. One result of this assessment was
the provision of emergency oxygen breathing masks to be worn by the
crew in the event of fire in the spacecraft when crewmen are not suited.

Lunar Module
Portable life support system.- All exposed edges of the PLSS hard
cover, which is aluminum honeycomb covered with fiber glass, were protected with an aluminum tape for lunar module 3. Portable life support
systems for subsequent vehicles have a complete cover built of multilayers of nonflammable thermal protective materials. Subsequent tests
verified the adequacy of this corrective action.
Teflon, net-jacketed, shielded cable.- This wire bundle was overwrapped with Hi-Rap Teflon tape. Subsequent tests verified the adequacy
of this corrective action.

CONCLUDING REMARKS

As a result of information obtained from these full-scale flammability tests , it was concluded t h a t , with the presently available materials,
a spacecraft atmosphere of 16.2 p s i a , 100 percent oxygen should not be
used. However, the materia"
ntrs:19730064278,ntrs,Proceedings of the NASA Conference on Materials for Improved Fire Safety,,58,"full-scale flammability tests , it was concluded t h a t , with the presently available materials,
a spacecraft atmosphere of 16.2 p s i a , 100 percent oxygen should not be
used. However, the material changes and other changes that have been
made in the command module and lunar module have resulted in a firesafe
configuration in the l6.2-psia, 60-percent-oxygen/UO-percent-nitrogen
launch atmosphere and in the 6.2-psia, 100-percent-oxygen inflight atmosphere. Consequently, these two atmospheres were selected for launch
and flight, respectively.
The full-scale mockup tests demonstrated that an extensive testing
program for individual materials and components alone will not necessarily provide a safe composite configuration in a hazardous environment. Materials with low burning rates may provide propagation paths
when used in long vertical runs. Firebreaks between flammable materials
are highly desirable. Nonflammable covers and coatings are of considerable value in reducing the fire.hazard.
The design approach of using slew-burning materials, firebreaks,
and protective covers and of avoiding propagation paths is probably
suitable for other potential applications, such as aircraft, land vehicles, and surface and underwater vessels.

5-16

With the above design lessons intensively applied, future requirements for repetitive mockup testing will be minimized and the fire
safety of vehicles can be greatly improved.

