TEST 5, MSC-PA-D-67-13

A schematic illustrating the flammability-test method used to qualify potting compounds described in this document is shown in figure 9-1•
A connector under test is shown in greater detail in figure 9-2.
The prepared sample is mounted on a horizontal holder and is positioned in a test chamber that can be pressurized with gaseous oxygen.
The ends of the wire, as shown in figure 9-2, are connected to the electrical connections of the chamber. As shown in the schematic of figure 9-1, current up to 100 amperes can be applied to the wire going into
the potting material.
The test chamber is filled to the desired pressure with oxygen,
usually 6.2 psia pure oxygen or 16.5 psia 60 percent oxygenAo percent
nitrogen. The 6.2 psia pure oxygen represents the environment of the
Apollo command and lunar modules during flight. The 60-percent-oxygen/
UO-percent-nitrogen atmosphere at a pressure of 16.5 psia corresponds
to the command module environment on the launch pad before lift-off.
After the test-chamber pressure has stabilized, a current of 55 amperes
is applied to the wire. If ignition or considerable degradation of the
potting material is not obtained in 1 minute, the current is increased
by 5-ampere increments until such time as the wire fails or ignition
occurs. The criterion of acceptability is that the potting material
shall not sustain combustion in the pressurized-oxygen test atmosphere
following removal of the ignition source. The test is designed to cause
ignition by electrical current overloads, which can provide temperatures
up to the melting point of the electrical wire. The specification provides that, if ignition of the pott"
ntrs:19730064278,ntrs,Proceedings of the NASA Conference on Materials for Improved Fire Safety,,97,"t is designed to cause
ignition by electrical current overloads, which can provide temperatures
up to the melting point of the electrical wire. The specification provides that, if ignition of the potting compound occurs before wire
failure, current shall be reduced to 0 amperes to evaluate the selfextinguishing characteristics of the potting compound.
A more severe test also has been applied to some potting materials
by using the test shown in figure 9-3. For this test, two connectors
are mounted one above the other in the test chamber, and the lower connector is ignited by using a silicone rod wound with Nichrome wire.
The criterion for this test is that flame from the burning of the lower
connector (if it burns) must not ignite the upper connector (ref. 9-1 )•

