APPENDIX A

FEDERAL AVIATION AGENCY ACCEPTANCE TEST PROCEDURES
FOR FLAMMABILITY

The following is an acceptable test procedure for showing compliance
with part 25, section 25.853 of ""Airworthiness Standards: Transport Category Airplanes,"" Federal aviation regulations by the Federal Aviation
Agency (change 19, April 23, 1969).
1. Conditioning — Specimens must be conditioned at 70° ± 5° F and
at 80 ± 5 percent relative humidity until moisture equilibrium is reached.
Only one specimen at a time may be removed from the conditioned environment. The specimen is removed immediately before it is"
ntrs:19730064278,ntrs,Proceedings of the NASA Conference on Materials for Improved Fire Safety,,169,"at 80 ± 5 percent relative humidity until moisture equilibrium is reached.
Only one specimen at a time may be removed from the conditioned environment. The specimen is removed immediately before it is subjected to the
flame.
2. Specimen Configuration — The specimen must be no thicker than
the minimum thickness to be qualified for use in the airplane. Rigid and
flexible specimens, 4-1/2 by 12-1/2 inches or of the actual size used in
the airplane, must be clamped in a metal frame so that the two long edges
and one end are held securely. The frame must be such that the exposed
area is at least 2 inches wide and 11-1/2 inches long, unless the actual
size used in the airplane is smaller. In the case of fabrics, the weave
direction that corresponds to the most critical burn rate must be parallel
to the longest dimension.
3. Apparatus — The tests must be conducted in an appropriate-sized
sheetmetal cabinet that has a door containing a glass insert for the
purpose of observing the burning specimen. The cabinet top must contain
a baffled vent, and baffled holes or similar means of ventilation must be
near the bottom of the cabinet. Larger panels need not be tested in this
apparatus but must be tested in similar draft-free conditions.
h. Horizontal Test — A minimum of three specimens must be tested
and the results averaged. Each specimen must be supported horizontally.
The surface that will be exposed to the air, when installed in the aircraft, must be face down for the test. The specimen must be ignited by
a Bunsen burner or Tirrill burner that has a nominal 3/8-inch-insidediameter (i.d.) tube that is adjusted to provide a flame 1-1/2 inches in
height when the air is completely shut off. The specimen must be placed
so that the edge being tested is 3/H inch above the top of, and on the
center line of, the burner. The flame must be applied for 15 seconds
and must then be removed. The char length must be noted when testing for
compliance with section 25-853(a) of the previously mentioned Federal
aviation regulations. To determine the burn rate for compliance with

18-15

section 25.853(b) of the regulations, the following steps must ""be followed: (l) a minimum of 10 inches of the specimen must be used for timing purposes, (2) approximately 1-1/2 inches must burn before the burning
front reaches the timing zone, and (3) the average burn rate must not
exceed h inches per minute. The material also is acceptable if, in testing for compliance with section 25.853(b) of the regulations, the specimens do not support combustion after the ignition flame is applied for
15 seconds or if the flame extinguishes itself and subsequent burning
without a flame does not extend into the undamaged areas.
5. Vertical Test —A
minimum of three specimens must be tested
and the results averaged. Each specimen must be supported vertically.
Ceiling or floor panels may be tested with any edge down. Rigid specimens of materials mounted vertically in the airplane must be oriented
for the"
ntrs:19730064278,ntrs,Proceedings of the NASA Conference on Materials for Improved Fire Safety,,170,"veraged. Each specimen must be supported vertically.
Ceiling or floor panels may be tested with any edge down. Rigid specimens of materials mounted vertically in the airplane must be oriented
for the test in the same manner as oriented in the airplane. The specimen must be ignited by a Bunsen burner or Tirrill burner with a nominal
3/8-inch-i.d. tube adjusted to provide a flame 1-1/2 inches in height
when the air is completely shut off. The center line of the burner must
be in line with a surface of the material being tested or, in the case
of fabricated units, must be in line with the surface exposed to the air
in the airplane. The lower edge of the specimen being tested must be
3A inch above the top of the burner. The flame must be applied for
12 seconds and then must be removed. The char length must be noted.
6. Char Length — The char length for fabrics and coated fabrics
is the distance from the specimen end that was exposed to the flame to
the end of a lengthwise tear made through the center of the charred area.
The tear must be made as follows. A hook must be inserted in the specimen at one side of the charred area, 1/U inch from the adjacent outside
edge and 1/4 inch in from the charred end of the specimen. A weight, of
sufficient size that the weight and hook together equal the total tearing load specified in the following table, must be applied gently to the
specimen by grasping the corner of the cloth at the edge of the char opposite from the load and then raising the specimen and weight clear of
the support. The total tearing load for various weights per square yard
of test cloth is as follows.

Weight per square yard
of test cloth, oz

Total tearing load, Ib

2 to 6

0.25

More than 6 to 15

.5

More than 15 to 23

.75
1.0

More than 23

18-16

On materials other than fabrics, the char length is the total length of
the specimen consumed or charred by burning. The length is measured from
the ignition edge to a point that cannot be punctured by a ballpoint pen
(or equivalent) when the pen is moved progressively from unburned to
burned areas.

