approximately $60 per set. Members of the astronaut rescue team wear a
specially constructed aluminum—over-Nomex jacket, bib coverall, hood,
gloves, and boots (fig. 19-12). Ordinary thermal underwear is worn under
the protective garm"
ntrs:19730064278,ntrs,Proceedings of the NASA Conference on Materials for Improved Fire Safety,,177,"s of the astronaut rescue team wear a
specially constructed aluminum—over-Nomex jacket, bib coverall, hood,
gloves, and boots (fig. 19-12). Ordinary thermal underwear is worn under
the protective garments. The price of a rescue—team suit is approximately
$275. The specifications for this suit are given in reference 19-5.
Figure 19-13 shows an astronaut, a rescue-team member, and a closeoutcrew member in a typical rescue operation.
The needs of KSC have been effectively met by using Nomex in the
two different types of protective garments. The tests that have been
performed and the operational uses to which KSC has put Nomex indicate
that it also can be used in other ways. The application of Nomex as
protective clothing for firemen is obvious. It can be used as clothing
for hospital patients, as seat covers in vehicles, as protection against
many types of chemical hazards, and in a variety of other applications
in the safety field. Nomex presents no physiological problems under
normal usage conditions, including having an acceptable degree of outgassing.

PART II

THIN PLASTIC SHEETING

Thin plastic sheeting (film) is used extensively in many applications at KSC. Critical parts that must be kept clean and particle free
from the time of arrival through installation on the space vehicle and
finally launch are packaged in several different plastics. Moreover,
plastic sheeting protects certain spacecraft openings and sensitive
areas from the weather. Some plastic sheets provide a completely controlled environment around critical vehicle/spacecraft sections.

19-5

An ideal plastic film for KSC must meet the following criteria.
1. The film must have a high transparency so that the status of
a part or item of equipment may be checked without disrupting a clean
environmental seal.
2.

The film must be noncombustible.

3. The film must be antistatic, permitting no buildup of electrical
static charges.
H. The film must be compatible with all propellants likely to contact the material.
5.

The film must have low particle-shedding characteristics.

Laboratory tests developed at KSC (ref. 19-6) to verify the listed
properties have checked the following qualities.
1.

Degree of transparency.

2. The flame resistance, as determined in accordance with the best
specifications currently available, which are as follows:
ASTM D 568-61, ""Method of Test for Films Under 0.05-inch Thickness""
ASTM D 635-63, ""Method of Test for Films Over 0.05-inch Thickness""
ASTM D l692-6?T, ""Method of Test for Cellular Films""
ASTM D 1692-59T, ""Method of Test for Plastic Foams and Sheeting""
3. Static charge formation, as determined by Pan American Environmental Health Laboratory Method 6-703, ""Measurement of Static Charge
Formation on Materials."" This method consists of applying friction
against rotating disks of wool or Teflon at approximately 50 percent
relative humidity. The charge and decay time are recorded on a stripchart recorder.
h. Effects of propellants, as determined by visual"
ntrs:19730064278,ntrs,Proceedings of the NASA Conference on Materials for Improved Fire Safety,,178,"nst rotating disks of wool or Teflon at approximately 50 percent
relative humidity. The charge and decay time are recorded on a stripchart recorder.
h. Effects of propellants, as determined by visual observation of
the effects of contact and by measurement of any temperature increase.
5- Particle count, as determined in accordance with specification FED-STD-209.

19-6

More than 20 different types of plastic film have been tested to
date. Some of these exhibited excellent antistatic properties and low
fiber-shedding characteristics but proved to be combustible. Others are
noncombustible but accumulate static charges and shed fibers. Some of
the films lost antistatic and self-extinguishing properties with age or
after exposure to sunlight and a salt air atmosphere. Table 19-1 lists
some of the films that have been tested and the results obtained from
the tests. Even though there is no single sheeting material that satisfactorily meets all the safety requirements for a given application,
considerable use has been made of a number of the available materials.
The need exists for further development of sheeting materials that will
have all the desired properties for the various applications.

CONCLUDING REMARKS

This paper has discussed some of the special needs of Kennedy Space
Center in the area of protective garments for personnel engaged in hazardous emergency operations, and the safety requirements for thin plastic
sheeting used to protect critical components from the environment. After
thorough testing, Nomex was chosen as the material from which to make
protective clothing for rescue and firefighting crews for use in air
atmospheres. It should be noted that there are now available several
recently developed materials with improved performance characteristics
that can be used for firefighting and rescue-clothing applications.
Extensive use has been made of several thin plastic sheeting materials; however, no currently available material entirely satisfies all
the safety requirements of the Kennedy Space Center. The need exists
for further development of sheeting materials that can more adequately
meet all requirements for the various uses and applications of these
materials.

