APPENDIX A

FLAME-IMPINGEMENT TESTER

The procedures for operation of the flame-impingement tester are as
follows.
1.

The candidate thermal layup is mounted in the sample holder.

2.

The sample holder is moved to the vertical position.

3. The sample holder with the sample is placed in the vacuum
chamber, which is evacuated and backfilled to the desired pressure with
the desired combination of gases (pure oxygen, oxygen/nitrogen, oxygen/
helium, or other).
U."
ntrs:19730064278,ntrs,Proceedings of the NASA Conference on Materials for Improved Fire Safety,,204,"he sample is placed in the vacuum
chamber, which is evacuated and backfilled to the desired pressure with
the desired combination of gases (pure oxygen, oxygen/nitrogen, oxygen/
helium, or other).
U. The flow of natural gas and oxygen is started, and a heated coil
of wire is used to initiate a flame.
5. The ratio of the natural gas to the oxygen that is being supplied is adjusted until the flame temperature, as monitored at the
lowered-sample position, is brought to the desired temperature. The
temperature is readily controllable to within the accuracy of the thermocouple read-out system.
6. An insulated shutter is closed over the flame to prevent premature heating of the test sample.
T.

The sample is lowered remotely to the horizontal position.

As the sample reaches the horizontal position, it strikes a microswitch that causes the shutter to open and the flame impinges on the
side of the sample.
Warning: Precautions must be taken to ensure that propane does not
accumulate in the chamber before flame ignition and cause an explosion
hazard. Three precautionary steps are mandatory for this test.
1. Equipping the chamber with a low-pressure rupture disk with
sufficient cross-sectional area to preclude a dangerous pressure rise
in the event of an explosion.
2. Placing a transparent protective shield between the test conductor and the chamber for each test.
22-20

3. Evacuating the chamber and starting over if the burner does not
ignite in a short time. This problem will exist only until an ignitable
ratio is effected. The system should be equipped with separate valves
for setting propane/oxygen ratios so these valves need not be used to
turn off the fuel at the completion of the tests.

22-21

APPENDIX B

SHORT-CIRCUIT IGNITION TESTER

The procedures used to qualify the cotton underwear and the other
nonmetallic materials inside the space suit are as follows.
1. The material (cotton or other) is mounted above, but not
touching, the flat copper disk.
2. The apparatus with the sample is placed in the vacuum chamber,
which is evacuated and backfilled with oxygen or a gas mixture.
3. A power supply that simulates the spacecraft power supply is
wired to impose the requisite voltage across the needle electrode and
the copper disk.
k. The needle is shorted to the copper disk, and a variable resistor is used to establish some arbitrary short-circuit current. The
needle, which is scraped across the test material, frays the material
as would a broken wire. The resulting arc is observed to determine
whether sufficient energy exists to ignite the material.
5. If the material does ignite, a lower short-circuit current
level is established, and the process is repeated until the minimum
current level capable of igniting the test sample at the usage voltage
level is determined.

22-22

