On Earth it has long been understood that gravitationally induced buoyancy dominates the spread of
fires.
The placement of fire detectors in ceiling
locations, for example, anticipates the predictable
upward bulk motion of combustion products in normal
gravity.
Now, as a practical matter, the testing of
spacecraft fire-safety technology,
including fire
detection and intervention equipment as well as
material-flammability
screening, must generally be
performed in ground-based facilities where, in contrast to the use environment,
buoyant motion dominates.
Thus the planning for adequate levels of
safety in spacecraft requires not only an understanding of the low-gravity, fire-related
behavior of

