Toxic combustion-product
gases, rather than
burns, are responsible for most human casualties from
fires.
Carbon monoxide is the principal lethal agent
in fire products, but plastics may also generate cyanides, chlorides, fluorides, and other toxic gases.
The human response to contaminants depends on both
the concentration and the duration of exposure
(Kaplan,
IgTg).
Acceptable
limits
derived
from
normal-gravity
tests
may not apply
to space.
Reduced
convective
transport
of oxygen affects
the temperature
and completeness
of combustion,
altering
the
nature
and concentration
of combustion
products.
A
space-station
candidate
guideline
(DG-HMS-I03,
in
Peercy
and Raasch,
1985b) urges the establishment
of
contaminant
threshold
limit
values
for
space,
presumably including
those
generated
by fires.
