For the fuel-rich mixture, ordinary diffusion is the most important process in determining the burning velocity. In contrast to the fuel-lean flame, the fuel-rich flame does
not propagate without ordinary diffusion. In the fuel-rich case, not enough heat can be
transferred by conduction alone to sustain the reactions. However, because of the excess of
hydrogen radicals, ordinary diffusion alone can spread enough radicals ahead of t h e front
t o initiate chemical reactions and sustain the flame.

