Examples of European

micro~ravity

combustion research programs

This chapter summarizes brietly some of the European micro gravity research programs using
the above listed facilities. The emphasis is mainly put on new research topics.
Microgravity combustion experiments with gas phase t1ames is conducted during the
parabolic nights of the Caravelle aircraft by the group at the Laboratoire d'Aerothermique of
the CNRS at Meudon. Part of the investigation is devoted to the effects of gravity on
polyhedral t1ames. Propane-air premixed names are burner stabilized and tests are conducted
under normal gravity. reduced gravity and at 1.8 g during the pull-up period of the aircraft
Several theoretical results are verified on cellular tfames in the polyhedral configuration and
it is shown that gravity does have a stabilizing effect for a downward propagating t1aroe. On
average. it is observed that one facet of the polyhedron is lost at reduced gravity in those
contigurations where the number of facets are between 5 and 8 under normal gravity (ref. 8).
Another part of the research program focuses on the int1uence of gravity on laminar premixed
and diffusion gas names (refs. 9 and 10).
Another example of microgravity combustion experiment using the parabolic nights of the
Caravelle is the research program on high pressure droplet burning conducted at the CNRSLCSR at Orleans. A High Pressure Droplet Burning Facility has been developed for this
purpose. It allows the investigation of burning and vaporizing single droplets up to an ambient
pressure of 12 MPa. A powerful image analysis system is added to the facility. N-heptane and
methanol droplets undergoing pure vaporization or burning are investigated. Preliminary
results show that the pressure effect on the gasification process differs strongly depending on
the gasification regime and that the instantaneous gasification rate can he obtained hy the
lcchniqucs developed 1refs. 11. 12).
With the availability of the Bremen drop tower. new combustion experiments have started to
take advantage of the very low gravity levels provided by this facility. One recent research
program is developed by the team of the CNRS-LCPC in Poitiers. in col"
ntrs:19930010989,ntrs,The Second International Microgravity Combustion Workshop,,22,"ombustion experiments have started to
take advantage of the very low gravity levels provided by this facility. One recent research
program is developed by the team of the CNRS-LCPC in Poitiers. in collaboration with the
University of California, Berkeley (ref. 13). The global aim of the research is to study the
diffusion name combustion of gaseous, liquid and solid fuels in a non-buoyant low velocity.
nat plate boundary layer established in an oxidizing now. The research program is planned in
progressive stages. The forced now boundary layer tlame combustion produced by a gaseous
name injected through a porous plate is first studied in the Bremen Drop Tower. An
experimental apparatus has been developed and the first drops were realized in 1992.
Methane names were ignited under both normal gravity and micro gravity and the name
behaviour is recorded by CCD cameras and thermocouples. Several parameters have been
varied. Preliminary observations indicate that micro gravity names are wider and more
diffuse, and less t1ickering. The name temperature is lower in micro gravity (ref. 13). In
parallel. modelling activities devoted to similar problems are conducted at the Universidad
Politecnica de Madrid (ref. 14),
The Bremen Drop Tower allowed also the development of another international collaboration
on the topic of high pressure droplet evaporation between Gennan and Japanese teams (ref.
15).
Another example of new micro gravity combustion research in Europe is provided by the
future use of the Texus sounding rocket program by the team of the Polytechnic University of

22

Madrid. They developed the conceptual design and expected operational characteristics of a
multipurpose Microgravity Combustion Module for sounding rocket experiments. The same
tearn proposed also to carry out one particular experiment on flame spreading with forced
convection along the surface of a solid fuel at reduced gravity. It is proposed to use hollow
cylinders of PMMA samples of 8 mm external diameter and 4 mm internal diameter. Three
series of experiments are proposed including the int1uence of small now velocities at constant
ambient composition with parallel and cross now conditions. and the int1uence of oxygen
concentration. In parallel. a theoretical program will be developed and will include the search
of an analytical solution for name spreading under forced convection in all upwind or
opposed now cases and an approximate theory of the downwind name spreading processes.
This program is also conducted in collaboration with CISE. (Milano. Italy) and CNRJCNPM
(Milano. Italy) concerning the development of laser diagnostics for micro gravity combustion
applications.
