Answer:
(1) Several groups in Europe arc develop ing lase r d iagnost ics relevant for microgravity combustion. Previous
studies at the istitute Moto ri - CNR in Nap les, It a ly, h ave shown that the velocity. diameter and temperature
of homogeneous droplets can be obtained by m easuring the light scattered at two timely a ngles. These
techniq ues are presently extended to non-ho mogeneous droplets by developing a theoretical model for the
light-scattering by droplets with a rad ial g rad ie nt of the refractin! index to determine the temperature and
s pecies proriles inside mullicomponenl droplets.
In parallel, a differential interferomete r to determine the temperature distrihution around a burning droplet
is being developcd at ZARM, Uni\·ersily of Bremen, Germany.
The Research Institute on Propulsion and Energetics of CNR in collaboration with CISE, both in Milan ,
Italy, arc developing a planar laser induced fluorescence system for combustion diagnostics in microgravity.
A multi-purpose PUF system based on a 2-0 solid state array scnsor is under development. The objective is
to investigate the ~ tructure of diffusion Ilames by measuring molecular distributions. The simultaneous
detection of the reso nant scattering will give indication of the presence of particulate matter such as soot
and/or li4Uid droplets. Also, temperature measurements will be obtained by exploiting the anomalous
nuorescence of pyrene. A PUF system is also under developmcnt at ZARM. University of Bremen, with the
support of the German space agency. Thc system is based on a tunable narrow band excimer laser, a sheetforming optical device and an intensificd digita l high-speed CCO camera.
(2) An cxperimental program on Ihc simulation of pool /ires wilh a gas flame stabili zed above n porous
