The knowledge of fullerenes formation in fl ames has grown significantly during
the short time since this process w as first established. The effects of combustion
conditions on the distribution and yields of C60 and C70 fullerenes have been studied and
the amount of fullerenes formed at different distances or times through the reaction zone
of selected flames has been measured. Flame synthesis is found to offer not only an
alternative method for large scale fullerenes production, but an ability to control the
distribution of products (e.g., the C;o/C 60 ratio) over a larger range than previously
realized. Also, a range of fullerenes including metastable isomers can be produced in
flames 24 • Plausible mechanisms of fullerenes formation in flames have been proposed
an d subjected to preliminary kinetics testing against data 25 . These advances are
summarized below.
