Introduction
Combustion synthesis or self-propagating high temperature synthesis (SHS) is effected by heating a reactant mixture,
to above the ignition temperature (Tig) whereupon an exothermic reaction is initiated which produces a maximum or
combustion temperature, Tc. These SHS reactions are being used to produce ceramics, intermetallics, and composite
materials.' One of the major limitations of this process is that relatively high levels of porosity, e.g. , 50%, remain in the
product. Conducting these SHS reactions under adiabatic conditions, the maximum temperature is the adiabatic
temperature, Tad, and 6H (Tad) = 0, Tad = Tc. If the reactants or products go through a phase change, the latent
heat of transformation needs to be taken into ac"
