Faeth et al. reported on studies conducted 011 laminar diffusion !lames, which are oriented towards better
understanding the soot and thermal radiation emissions from luminous !lames. The results presented for low
pressure diffusion names were quite novel. Low pressure conditions were used to simulate microgravity
conditions and revealed new insight in smoke-point behavior and soot formation processes. A major
adva ntage of this low pressure envirOllment is the opportunity for extensive measurements of name
properties over periods of time longer than than typically available in current microgravity facilities. These
experiments hold the potential for providing a unique environment in which to study soot formation and the
development of state relationships aimed at predicting soot formation in turbulent combustion systems.
Atreya et al. reported on their modeling efforts with respect to soot formation in counternow diffusion
names. These modeling results are being compared to recent normal gravity name measurements and \vill
be extended to zero gravity studies to be initiated soon in the NASA-Lewis 2.2 sec microgravity drop tower.
Ku e t al. similarly reported on their efforts to model turbulent !lames in a microgravity ellvirollment in which

