Excitable Dynamics in High-Lewis Number
Premixed Gas Combustion at Normal and Microgravity
Howard Pearlman
NRC Research Associate
NASA Lewis Research Center
Cleveland, Ohio
Abstract
Freely-propagating, premixed, gas flames in high-Lewis (Le) number, quiescent mixtures are studied
experimentally in tubes of various diameter at normal (1g) and microgravity (l1g). A premixture of lean
butane and oxygen diluted with helium, argon, neon, nitrogen or a mixture of mUltiple diluents is examined
such that the thermal diffusivity of the mixture (and to a lesser extent, the mass diffusivity of the rate-limiting
component) is systematically varied. In effect, different diluents allow variation of the Le without changing
the chemistry. The flames are recorded with high speed cinematography and their stability is visually
assessed.
Different modes of propagation were observed depending on the diameter of the tubes (different
conductive heat loss), the composition"
ntrs:19960008386,ntrs,The 3rd International Microgravity Combustion Workshop,,436,"igh speed cinematography and their stability is visually
assessed.
Different modes of propagation were observed depending on the diameter of the tubes (different
conductive heat loss), the composition of the mixture and the g-Ievel. At Ig, four modes of propagation were
observed in small and intermediate diameter tubes (large conductive heat loss): (1) steaClily propagating
flames, (2) radial and longitudinal pulsatin~ flames, (3) ""wavering"" flames and (4) rotating spiraf flames. AS
the diameter of the tube increases, the radial modes become more pronounced while the longitudinal modes
systematically disappear. Also, multiple, simultaneous, spatially-separated ""I?acemaker"" sites are observed in
intermediate and large diameter tubes. Each site starts as a small region of high luminosity and develops into
a flamelet which assumes the· form of one of the forementioned modes. These flamelets eventually interact,
annihilate each other in their regions of intersection and merge at their newly created free-ends. For very
large tubes, radially-propagating wave-trains (believed to be ""trigger waves"") are observed. These are
analogous to the radial pUlsations observed in the smaller diameter tubes. At ~~, three modes of propagation
have been observed: (1) steadily propagating flames, (2) radial and longitudinal pulsating flames and. (3)
multi-armed, rotating flames.
Since the pulsating mode exists at I1g and 19, buoyant flicker is not the mechanism which drives the
pulsations. Moreover, all of the instabilities at Ig and I1g have characteristic frequencies on the O(lOOHz).
This value is lower than the fundamental, longitudinal acoustic frequencies of the tubes which suggests that
the instabilities are not acoustically. driven.
The patterns formed by this reaction bear remarkable similarities with the patterns formed in most
excitable media when the behavior of the system is driven by couplings between chemical reaction and
diffusion (e.g., Belousov-Zhabotinsky reaction, patterns in slime molds, spiral waves in the retina of a birds
eye). While it is recognized that the chemica1 mechanism associated with this premixed gas reaction is
exponentially sensitive to temperature and undoubtedly different from those which govern ereviously
observed excitable media (most are isothermal, or weakly exothermic, liquid phase reactions), simIlar spatiaI
and temporal patterns should not come as a complete surprise considering heat and mass diffusion are selfsimilar.
It is concluded that this premixed gas system is a definitive examyle of a diffusive-thermal, gas-phase
oscillator based on these experimental results and their favorable comparIson with theory.
I. Introduction
To verify the existence of the high-Le number diffusive-thermal instabjlity in premixed gas
combustion, I1g tests were initially I?erformed using a mixture of butane and oxygen (liluted with helium
(Le=3) in a flame tube opened at tlie Ignition-end and closed at the other end. Based on a scale analysis,"
ntrs:19960008386,ntrs,The 3rd International Microgravity Combustion Workshop,,437,"I1g tests were initially I?erformed using a mixture of butane and oxygen (liluted with helium
(Le=3) in a flame tube opened at tlie Ignition-end and closed at the other end. Based on a scale analysis, the
frequency of the oscillation (co) is expected to scale with the square of the laminar burning velocity divided
by the thermal diffusivi~ of the mixture and the amplitude of tlie oscillation (5) is expected to scale with the
tfiermal diffusivity divided by the burning velOCity. At I1g, the laminar burning velocity in near-limit
mixtures is not affected by buoyant convection and thus may be lower than the burning velocity at IS.
Hence, by reducing the flame speed, co should decrease and 5 should increase, making it easier to see. This
idea, originally proposed by Ronney (1], seemed plausible considering the only e:xperimental evidence to
support the eXistence of the high-Le number, diffusive-thermal instability [2] was complicated by
complexities of heat loss to the burner and sensitivity to the local hydrodynamic field. By conSidering
freefy-propagating flames in quiescent mixtures, rather than burner-stabilized flames, the heat loss and
hydrodYnamiC problems are eliminated. Also, at 118, buoyant convection is eliminated as a potential source
of any observed instability. This was the starting pomt approximately two years ago.
During the past two years, we leamed several new things about the high-I.:e number diffusive-thermal
instability:
1. The instability exists at 19 and I1g.
433

2. The instability manifests itself in several different forms depending on the composition of the
mixture (hence, Le), the conductive heat loss to the walls, and the g-level.
3. A critical Lee (>1) exists. Below Lec (but still >1), the flames propagate in a stable, unifonn
manner. Above Lee, the flames are unstable.
These findings are described in this paper. The J.l.g experimental apparatus and results is presented in
Section 2 followed by the 19 tests on downwardly propagating flames in Section 3. A brief.summary and
description of work-in-progress is presented in Section 4.
2.

Microgravity Apparatus and Results

Freely-propagating flames are studied experimentally at j.Lg in the 2.2 sec drop tower at the NASA
Lewis Researcli Genter [3]. The tests are performed in a 14.3cm Ld., polycarbonate flame tube, 40.Ocm in
len21b. The tube is permanently closed at one end and initially sealed With a mylar membrane at the opposite
ena. A hot-wire/ nitrocellulose igniter is situated on the nonreactive side of the membrane. The resistively
heated wire ignites the nitrocellulose releasing approximately 4O.0J. This is sufficient to completely consume
the membrane, thereby open the ignition-end to ambient conditions, and ignite the mixture. The flame shape
and its evolution are recorded at 2ooframes/sec with a high-speed Millikan 16mm movie camera. Although
higher framing rates are desirable, the visible light from the flame is insufficient to leave a trace on the film at
rates g"
ntrs:19960008386,ntrs,The 3rd International Microgravity Combustion Workshop,,438,"ded at 2ooframes/sec with a high-speed Millikan 16mm movie camera. Although
higher framing rates are desirable, the visible light from the flame is insufficient to leave a trace on the film at
rates greater than 2S0 frames/sec, even with the most sensitive film. A schematic of the drop rig is shown in
Fig. 1.
~
The tube is filled by the partial pressure method to local atmo~heric pressure, 740-750 Torr, with a
mixture of C4HlO-OZ-He. A fan is installed on the closed end of tlie tube to mix the reactants prior to
ignition. The fan is turned on during the fill procedure and it is turned off approximately five minutes prior
to the drOI'-. This is about five times longer than a characteristic viscous dissl{>ation time (53 sec) based on
the ratio of the square of the radius of the tube to the kinematic viscosity of helium at room temperature.
Careful scrutiny of the film suggests that a multi-armed, rotating wave and a radial and longitudinally
pulsating flame exist as the flammability limit is approached. The characteristic temporal frequencies of both
the pulsating and traveling waves are on the order of 70-100 cycles/so
Despite the limited camera resolution, three distinct modes of propagation can be identified
according to the volumetric fuel concentration in the mixture,
(1) Stable propagating flames occur in mixtures containing 1.30% (or more) C4HlO,
(2) Rotating waves occur in mixtures containing 1.21-1.22% C4fllO, and
(3) Combined radial and longitudinal pulsations occur in mixtures containing 1.21-1.2S% C4HlO.
Figu}""e 2 shows an axial view of a radial and longitudinally J?ulsating flame. Each pulsation cycle starts with a
bnght, centrally-located, ""pacemaker"" region. This bnght region radially expands and decreases in
luminosity as the flame propagates axially into the reactants. As the flame nears its minimum intensity and
maximum surface area (when it aJ?proaches the wall), another bright region appears at (or near) the center
and the cycle repeats. Although it IS not clear whether these bright zones are the flame or are traveling on the
surface of a much less visible flame, the latter is presently sp€?culated to be the case since it would otherwise
be difficult to justify why the radial propagation speed (as high as 2.Om/s) is roughly ten times the axial
flame propagation speed. One possible ar~ent, proposed by Art Winfree, sug~ests that the phase of the
oscillation may vat)' from one part of the flame to another where the ""observed' speed is proportional to
the inverse of the pliase gradient This would allow for infinitely large radial ~eds.
The rotating wave (Fig. 3) observed at J.Lg consists of six periodic, azimuthally-distributed bright and
dark. zones which rigidly rotate around the axis of the tube, tracing out a helical trajectory as the flame
axially propagates into ilie reactants. While this mode appears to be unique to J.Lg, additional tests are planned
to determine whether a correlation exists between this multi-armed structure"
ntrs:19960008386,ntrs,The 3rd International Microgravity Combustion Workshop,,439,"he flame
axially propagates into ilie reactants. While this mode appears to be unique to J.Lg, additional tests are planned
to determine whether a correlation exists between this multi-armed structure and a multi-armed, rotating
spiral wave observed at 19 (discussed in Section 3).
3. Normal-Gravity Apparatus and Results

Three different internal diameter (Ld.) Plexiglas tubes (S.l, 10.2 or 14.3cm), approximately 1.2m in
length, are used to study downwardly propagating flames at 19. The bottom of the tubes is permanently
closed with a Plexi$las window while the other end is initially closed with a plate connected to a solenoid.
When the solenoid IS activated, the plate is lifted vertically off of an o-ring seal. Similar to the J.l.g tests, a hot
wire/ trinitrocellulose igniter is situated at the open-end (top).t
The tube is filled by the partial pressure method or mass flow rate with a mixture of C4HlO, 02 and
an inert (or inert mixture). A fan is attached to the removable end plate and used to ensure homogeneous
mixing of the reactants. It is turned off approximately five minutes after the last constituent is added at which
time the mixture is allowed ten minutes to quiesce prior to ignition; this waiting period is at least ten times the
viscous dissipation time (S3 sec).

t Additional tests were performed using the same tubes with a membrane and igniter at the open-end (similar
to the j.Lg tests) to eliminate the possibility of any convective flow induced by the movable end plate.
434

Hi~-speed video is obtained using two Kodak EktaPro intensified video cameras each recording the
flame at 1000 frames/sec. One camera is axialW positionedm theiniddle of the tube (field of view is 20cm)
while a second camera obtains a radial view from a mirror J?Ositioned at a 45° angle with respect to the base
(closed-end) of the tube. The video outputs are recorded usmg a high-speed tape drive and tlien downloaded
at 30 frames/sec to VHS video.
A summary of the ''instabilities'' is given for each tube diameter. The word ""instabilities"" is in
quotes because it can be a misnomer; these modes of propagation are quasi-stable for most experimental
conditions, except for exceptionally near-limit flames which svontaneously transition between modes.
Also, note that all of the observed ""instabilities"" eXIst within approximately 0.2 molar percent (or
less) C4HlO from the lean flammability limit (LFL) where the LFL's are determined experimentally to be
1.52, 1.44 and 1.29 percent C4HlO in a 2l%02-He environmentfor the 5.1, 10.2 or 14.3cm Ld. tubes,
respectively. Conductive heat loss apparently governs the limit in the small tubes whereas buoyant
convection and radiative losses govern tlie limit in the larger diameter tubes.

5.lcm Ld. tube: In the smallest tube tested, four modes of propagation (Fig. 5) are observed:
(a) stable, uniformly-propagating, axisymmetric flames in mixtures containin~ 1.68% C4HlO (or more),
(b) longitudinal, axisymmetric pulsations (ro.... 170-200"
ntrs:19960008386,ntrs,The 3rd International Microgravity Combustion Workshop,,440,"des of propagation (Fig. 5) are observed:
(a) stable, uniformly-propagating, axisymmetric flames in mixtures containin~ 1.68% C4HlO (or more),
(b) longitudinal, axisymmetric pulsations (ro.... 170-200Hz) in mixtures containmg 1.60-1.67% C4BlO, and
(c) tristable modes of propa~ation in mixtures containing 1.52-1.59% C4RIO. The flame alternates between
three modes: (1) a longItudinal pulsation (Fig. 5b), (2) a ""wavering"" mode, characterized by a bright
spot OI;t the flame swa~g from one side of the flame to the other, tracing out a nonaxisymmetric, zigzag path (Fig. 5c), or (3~ a rotating spiral wave (Fig. 5d). While the longitudinal oscillations are easy to
visualize, the ""wavering' mode and spiral wave deserve some attention. By propagating in a zig-zag or
spiral path, the flame can gain a foothold in the reactants without havmg to propagate in discrete,
10ngituOinal steps. In this way, the flame can propagate in a spatiall}' continuous fasliion. Interestingly,
many crystals grow in an analogous way [4]. The molecules escape from the solid into the surroundirig
vapor at a rate dependent on the surface temperature and the molecules condense on the surface at a rate
prop()rtional to the vapor concentration. At very slight supersaturations (as low as 1%), crystals grow in a
spiral ramp or a series of interlaced, zig-zag~ing concentric rings, due to pure diffusion. Rather than
counterdiffusion of a single species, counterdiffusion of heat and mass apparently induce similar spatial
patterns in our premixed gas flame.
lO.3cm i,d. tube Four modes are also observed in a tube with twice the Ld. (Fig. 6):
(a) stable, uniformly propagating, axis~etric flames in mixtures containing 1.56% C4HlO (or more),
(b) combined radial and longitudinal pulsations in mixtures containing 1.48±o.Ol to 1.54% C4HlO (while the
amplitude of the longitudinal pulsation is measurably smaller than in the 5.lcm Ld. tube),
(c) trinaI)' mode of propagation in mixtures containing 1.45 to 1.48±O.Ol % C4HlO. Similar to the flames in
the S.lem i.d. tube, tfie flame alternates between three modes: (1) a combined radial and longitudinal
pulsation, (2) a ""wavering"" mode, or (3) a rotating spiral wave.
14.3cm Ld. tube Similar modes are observed in a larger diameter tube (Fig. 7a,b,c). Once again,
(a) stable, uniformly propagating, axisymmetric flames occur in mixtures containing greater than 1.50%
C4HtO (or more),
(b) radial pUlsations occur in mixtures containing 1.35-1.50% C4RlO, and
(c) bistable modes of propagation occur in mixtures containing 1.29-1.35% C4HlO. The flame alternates
between a rotating spiral wave and a radial pulsation.
In addition, multiple, simultaneous {>acemaker sites are observed at the same cross-section of the tube
for these very near-limit mixtures. Each ""SIte"" develops into a radial pulsation or a rotating spiral wave (CW
or CCW). Since the flames compete for the same reactants, they inevitably coalesce, annihilate one another in
their regions of intersection and"
ntrs:19960008386,ntrs,The 3rd International Microgravity Combustion Workshop,,441,"ps into a radial pulsation or a rotating spiral wave (CW
or CCW). Since the flames compete for the same reactants, they inevitably coalesce, annihilate one another in
their regions of intersection and either merge at their free-ends or separate into flamelets which ultimately
meet again. In Fig. 7d, the initiation and development of two counter-prol?agating radial pulsations are
shown. In Fig.7e, two co-rotating spiral waves have developed separately and then Joined at their pivoting
endpoints to form a two-armed rotating spiral wave.
30.5cm x 30.5cm x 153cm square duct:
A few preliminary tests have been performed in a large square
duct, open at the top and close<l at the bottom. Mixtures containing 1.32 to 1.47% C4RlO were tested, wnere
the LFL has been determined to be 1.32%C4HlO in a 21%02-He environment (compare with the value of
1.29% in the 14.3 cm Ld. tube). For this range of experimental parameters, multiple, radial propagating
wave-trains appear and propagate outward in a very systematic fasmon. In the smaller tubes, tfiese ""wavetrains"" appear as radial pulsations since the characteristic spacing between waves is on the order of the tube
diameters. In other words, the radial propagating flames previously observed quench at the tube walls before
another radial (concentric ring) wave develops.
These wave-trains are believed to De ""trigger waves""--waves of chemical activity whose existence
depends on the interaction of reaction and diffuSIOn. They are characterized by the fact that they can be

435

blocked by impermeable barriers. Analogous waves have been observed in the Belousov-Zhabotinsky [5]
reaction and should not be confused with kinematic waves (phase or frequency gradient waves) which are
independent of diffusion [6]. Experimental evidence which supports this contention.will be presented under
separate cover.

4. Discussion and Work-in-Progress

To summarize the results so far, freely-propagating flames in C4H 10-02 mixtures diluted with v.ure
helium. exhibit several different modes of propagation near the LFL. Tliese modes have been classIfied
according to the volumetric ~rcent fuel in the mixture (i.e., equivalence ratio) from which the Le can be
determined. Also, above a critical equivalence ratio, the flames propagate in a steady manner and do not
visually display any evidence of ""instability.""
Prefimmary tests have also been performed using four component mixtures where the fourth
component is a second ""inert"" gas (Ne, Ar, N2) such that the Le of the 6ulk mixture is systematically varied
without varying the equiValence ratio. Experimentally, the percent He in the earlier mixtures is reduced and
replaced With an eqUIvalent percent of an additional inert. These results indicate that flames which are
""unstable"" (do not propagate in a steady manner) in a pure He-diluted mixture stabilize above a critical
[He]/[diluent] ratio. Tliis suggests that the Le of the bulk mixture must be sufficiently greater than one for
onset of ""instab"
ntrs:19960008386,ntrs,The 3rd International Microgravity Combustion Workshop,,442,"a steady manner) in a pure He-diluted mixture stabilize above a critical
[He]/[diluent] ratio. Tliis suggests that the Le of the bulk mixture must be sufficiently greater than one for
onset of ""instability."" Stabihty diagrams (Le vs. conductive heat loss) are currently being constructed where
the values of the mixture Le's are evaluated using CHEMKIN thermodynamic transport data as input into the
Sandia multicom'ponent transport code.
As mentioned before, the spatio-temporal patterns fOImed by this premixed gas flame rival those
observed in other excitable media. Both systems display rotating spiral waves and concentric ring waves.
Initially, these ring waves in our premixed system appeared to be pulsations since the wavelength (~acing
between rings) is on the order of the tube diameter. In addition, thif!remixed gas reaction dlSl?lays
simultaneous, multiple ""pacemaker"" sites which initially propagate as thou they were independent entities.
They eventually collide and quench in their regions of mtersection, due to ocal depletion of the rate-limiting
component (C4H10), and then continue to Clevelop in the form of a combine<l entity or break-up into
distorted shapes. This is representative of chemical tUrbulence.
Perha(>s, it is also worth noting that analogous modes of propagation have been observed in solid
flames [7] wmch also have very large Le's due to their very small mass difusivities. Those who have studied
these phenomena have seen steady flames, auto-oscillations, spinning spiral waves, and ""chaotic"" modes.
Interestingly, the ""chaotic"" modes appear when points in the combustion wave move around independently
of one another. These phenomena may be simifar to the thermally-driven instabilities that are otiserved in
our premixed gas flames, where the ""chaotic"" mode seen in the solid flames may correspond to the
existence of multiple, simultaneous pacemaker sites which subsequently perform their own dynamics.
Unfortunately, one cannot easily see the face of the moving front in the solid pllase.

