6. LINEAR STABILITY ANALYSIS
Comparison of the asymptotic results for slow growth with the intrinsic rate in figure 4b indicates
the cost of the volume fraction gradients required to transport particles to the growing crystallite. This
difference presents the possibility that a perturbation in the interface, generatin g a surface with a shorter
length scale and, therefore. a larger gradient, will be unstable, leading to a nonspherical mode of growth.
34-560

 

To examine this, we follow Lan ger"" and assume a small amplitude periodic perturbation of the interface
between fluid and solid. Substitution of the corresponding perturbations to the volume fraction profiles
into the governing equations yields a linear, pseudosteady problem that determines whether the perturbation grows or decays with time. Langer ' s results for the same base states with analogous boundary conditions suggest that both the rapidly and slowly growing crystallites should be unstable to sufficiently long
wavelength disturbances. Here, we verify the diffusion - controlled limit in detail, revealing the parametric
dependence peculiar to hard - sphere colloids.
