The osmotic pressures diverge at maximum packing for the appropriate phases and the short-time selfdiffusion coefficient nes to zero at random close packing. The chemical potential difference vanishes for
coexistence and diverges at close packing.
34-564

ven
The intrinsic growth rate or interfacial velocity gi by the Wilson-Frenkel law (2) is zero for
O f,, since the driving force is zero, and vanishes at close packing along with DS°. Consequently, a
maximum exists in the vicinity of melting.''- 5 Scalin g with the critical nucleus size and the diffusion time.
however, produces a dimensionless rate that decays monotonically from a finite value at freezing (fig. 4b).
The normalized rate of diffusion-limited growth produces a maximum in X at X>l. due to the effect of
