Since the flame was faint and hard to see clearly in the recorded videos, the growth of the char
patterns shown in Figs. 5 was used to calculate char spread rate. As the distance between the
spreading char front and traveling flame front appeared to be nearly constant, it was assumed that

the char spread rate was the same as flame spread rate. The position of the char fiont spreading
toward upstream was measured at different times for the external flow velocities of 2 c d"
ntrs:19990020826,ntrs,Third United States Microgravity Payload: One Year Report,,68,"assumed that

the char spread rate was the same as flame spread rate. The position of the char fiont spreading
toward upstream was measured at different times for the external flow velocities of 2 c d s and 5
c d s ; the results are plotted in Fig.7. As shown in this figure and discussed above, the flame
hardly spread when only one side of the sample was ignited. After the second ignition at the side
opposite fiom the first ignition, the flames started to spread. At 2 c d s , it appears that char
spread at an apparently steady rate shortly after the second ignition but at 5 c d s , initially char
spread slowly and then reached the apparently steady rate. Both apparently steady char spread
rates are about the same; they were 0.1 1 0.01 c d s . Since the char spread rate for the case of 5
cmls appears to increase with an increase in distance fiom the ignited area, as shown in Fig.7,
some caution is needed for the spread value at an external flow velocity of 5 c d s .

