Introduction
This is a flight definition project with the specific objective to investigate the kinetics of
nucleation and crystal growth in micro gravity for high temperature inorganic oxide melts that
have the potential to form glass when cooled. The present project is related to of our previous
NASA projectl that was concerned with glass formation for high temperature containerless
melts in microgravity. The previous work culminated in two experiments 2,3 which were
conducted aboard the"
ntrs:19990040241,ntrs,NASA Microgravity Materials Science Conference,,154,"ious
NASA projectl that was concerned with glass formation for high temperature containerless
melts in microgravity. The previous work culminated in two experiments 2,3 which were
conducted aboard the space shuttle in 1983 and 1985 and which consisted of melting
levitated, spherical (6 to 8 mm diameter) samples in a Single Axis Acoustic Levitator
(SAAL) furnace at 1500° C.
Of special relevance to the present project was the finding that glass formation for a CaOGa20 r Si02 composition melted in microgravity was nearly three times more likely than for
the same composition melted at I-g (on earth). This finding was based on the comparison
of the critical cooling rate for glass formation (defined as the slowest rate at which a melt can
be cooled without crystallizing) for the identical composition measured at I-g (on earth) with
the cooling rate which the containerless melt experienced in micro-g (space), see Fig. 1. The
improvement in glass formation for the containerless melt prepared in micro-g was attributed
to the suppression of heterogeneous nucleation that usually occurs at the melt-container
interface on earth (I-g). Unfortunately, the maximum improvement in glass formation for
containerless melts in microgravity could not be determined because of the unplanned
termination of the third flight experiment due to the Challenger accident.
A second important finding of equal importance was that the glass obtained from the space
melted sample, which had been prepared from a deliberately, chemically inhomogeneous
(hot-pressed) sample, was considerably more chemically homogeneous than the control
sample prepared on earth (I-g). Melt homogenization or mixing at I-g is known to be
caused primarily by gravity-driven convection. Why a glass prepared in micro gravity,
where gravity-driven convection is ideally absent and the melt would be more nearly
stagnant, was more chemically homogeneous, remained unclear.
It is interesting to note, however, that similar results have been obtained from other

