Although significant velocity flows are generated by the violent bubble oscillations, the
time scale of the fluid motion (on the order of microseconds) is still quite long when
compared to molecular time constants (tens of picoseconds). The spatial scales of the
bubble and its oscillations are also still very large when compared to the size of the
fluctuating water molecular clusters described by the classical nucleation processes. Under
these circumstances, we believe that pressure waves emitted during the violent periodic
bubble collapses are responsible for the ice nucleation. A simple argument based on the
water phase diagram and classical nucleation theory concepts suggests that a positive
pressure wave up to 10 GPa is capable of nucleating the solid phase2 because of the
freezing point shift.

