GROUND-BASED EXPERIMENTS

I

Previous ground-based study established that the conditions under which cellular and dendritic
microstructures form are precisely where convection effects are dominant in bulk samples . We

I

have performed ground-based experiments investigating the potential of several methods to reduce

I

convection in terrestrial experiments. The methods studied include use of magnetic fields (axial

I

and transverse) and the use of very small diameter samples of metallic alloys.

I

In order to see if the application of a magnetic field can suppress convection, directional

I

so lidification experiments were carried out in presence of axial and radial magnetic fields. It was
observed that the application of a transverse magnetic field does not reduce the extent of solutal

I

mixing due to convection . In addition, the transverse magnetic field introduces an anisotropy in

I

the fluid flow that distorts the cellular mushy zone morphology. Thus , another technique was
developed to reduce convection. In this technique, A series of experiments has been carried out in

I

the AIAwt% eu alloy system using thin tubes of diameters varying from 6.0 mm down to 0.4

I

mm. A bundle of thin samples of varying diameters was placed inside the 6 mm diameter tube, and
all samples were directionally solidified simultaneously in a single experiment. As a result, the

I

thermal profile and the translation rate imposed on the samples were identical except for the degree

I

of convection which depends on the tube diameter. Since convection effects are gradually reduced

I

I

as the sample size is decreased, quantitative evaluation of convection effects on microstructural
development can be obtained. The results of this series of experiments are illustrated in Fig. 1
which shows microstructures as a function of both the sample size and growth velocities at a given
temperature gradient G= 10 Klmm.

