The combined observations of the USML-l and USML-2 experiments demonstrate the
effects of even small convective flows on the growth processes of this multi-component,
multi-reaction, low pressure, solid-vapor transport system. The morphological
investigations of the substrate-layer interfaces (heterojunctions) strongly suggest that the
formation and propagation of defects is influenced by gravity-related processes during the
very initial period of deposition. The combined results show the effects of micro gravity on
the chemical and structural homogeneity during the transient period. These fmdings are of
general importance for heteroepitaxial growth, independent of the particular growth method
employed.

