solutions to compare with the adiabatic temperature profiles in Figure 6-2, at a low stretch rate (5 s-_),
middle stretch rate (98 s-'), and the blow-off limit (turning point at 1942 s-_). We also include the
radiative extinction turning point temperature profile (a=0.011 s_), along with a profile from the region
on the left side of the isola, above the turning point (a=0.025 s_). The figure shows that the temperature
profiles are quite different, due to changes in the flow regime as well as radiative emission from the
surface. As before, the high stretch solutions are blown against the surface. As stretch rate decreases, the
flame moves away from the surface, and reaches a maximum temperature. In the region where surface
radiation effects become important, the flame temperature decreases, while the flame continues to move
away from the surface. Prior to extinction, the flame can no longer sustain the large standoff distance,
and q'alls back' towards the surface. This drop-offtoward
