When we plot this expression as l/T, the result is a straight line, with a as the intercept and ,u:/3k as the
slope. Polarizability is then defined at the intercept, where 1/T --_ 0. (Or X = t_).
Then the intercept gives a, the polarizability,

as approximately

0.237 cubic Angstroms.

In the calculation of the binary diffusion coefficient in the TRAN program [Kee el al. 1986], the
polarizability tbr a polar molecule appears in the calculation of a collision integral, which is internal to the
TRAN computational package. A priori insight into the variation of the diffusion coefficient with
polarizability of a polar molecule is therefbre not available. For this reason, we used the TRAN program
to assess differences in diflhsion coefficients with different polarizabilities.

119

The differences in polar-nonpolar diffusion coefficients for trioxane were assessed for polarizabilities of
zero and 0.237. The value for solid trioxane lies within this range, so that the extremes of the range of
known values of polarizability for trioxane are included. (The dielectric constant Ibr gas phase trioxane is
unknown, possibly because trioxane decomposes so rapidly to formaldehyde in the gas phase. It is
possible that gas phase trioxane has a higher dielectric constant, and thus a higher polarizability, but we
don't consider this possibility here because no evidence for this exists.) The results showed no
differences to the third significant figure in the binary diffusion coefficients. Thus, we conclude that the
polarizability does not significantly affect the transport characteristics of this problem, and any error in
the estimate will have little effect. The value a = 0.237 is retained in the database for trioxane.

