A4.4.2

Parametric

Variation

of the Surface Radiation

Properties

The model equations are solved numerically by the time-marching scheme described in T'ien et al.
[1978], using the properties of PMMA from T'ien [1986] (with the exception of surthce radiative
properties, which are varied parametrically) and the two-flux radiation equations are solved by integration
in rl, the nondimensional spatial coordinate. To understand how gas-phase radiation interacts with the
surface processes (absorption, emission, reflection) and the consequences of this interaction on the flame,
we chose four cases with the extreme combinations of surface absorptivity and emissivity. We computed
these cases with variation of absorptivity and emissivity in Equation (A4-7) over the range of stretch rates
from blow-offto low speed quenching. These cases are:
Case 1: a = 1; e = 1; full absorption and full emission (a 'black' surface)
Case 2: a = 1; e = 0; full absorption and no emission (i.e., largest radiation energy gain to surface; a
'cold' surthce)
Case 3: & = 0; e = 0; no absorption (fully reflecting) and no emission

128

Case 4: a = 0: e = 1; no absorption (fully reflecting) and full emission (largest radiation energy loss from
the surihce)
These parametric extremes permit us to c"
ntrs:20010066768,ntrs,Effects of Gas-Phase Radiation and Detailed Kinetics on the Burning and Extinction of a Solid Fuel,,102,"lly reflecting) and no emission

128

Case 4: a = 0: e = 1; no absorption (fully reflecting) and full emission (largest radiation energy loss from
the surihce)
These parametric extremes permit us to consider a series of interesting questions: What is the importance
of surface absorption in both the presence and absence of surface emission? How does a fully reflecting
surface affect the flame heat balance? How does the flame respond in the presence of large surface
radiation loss (non-absorbing;
fully emitting surface)? Additionally, these cases could represent some
physically realizable situations. For example, Case 2 mimics a liquid surface behavior, with little
emission (due to low surface temperature) but full absorption.

