Therefore the image still
shows perturbations
of the flow above the flame. It is important to note that the area where
the flame is present remains un-seeded, showing that the flames deflect the airflow. Figure 1
shows good coincidence
between the visible flame and the recorded CH emissions, the CH
zone being, as expected, slightly below the visible flame zone. The complete analysis of the
different video and PIV recordings leads to a detailed description of the low velocity reacting
flow, especially
on the influence
of the flame on the flow field.
Furthermore,
these
measurements
provide a better definition of the flame length and stand-off distance, since
they allow to identify the presence of a reacting zone beyond the limits of the visible flame.
