Clouds in air were produced and ignited in microgravity.
Two types of powder,
160 I_m average diameter (by Wah Chang Company) and -325 Mesh (< 44 i_m, by Alfa Aesar)
were used. The flame velocity for the coarse particles varied in the range of 0.02 - 0.1 m/s, at
equivalence ratios in the range of 4.8 < _ < 6. The t'me particle flames propagated at a velocity of
0.3 m/s at _ = 2.2 and 1.8 m/s at a higher particle loading, (_ = 3.1. The appearance of flames of
clouds of titanium particles was qualitatively similar to those of zirconium. Individual partic!¢
flames could be clearly visible in all the runs. Particle explosions were observed in only one
experiment
with the fine particles and lower mass load (equivalence ratio of 2.2, of. Fig. 2). The
experiments
have shown that the titanium combustion products do not agglomerate as much as
the products of zirconium combustion as was observed in the earlier tests [3]. Instead, a large
number of very fine particles were observed in the
:::
combustion
products, indicative of significant vaporphase reaction followed by the product condensation.
The phase
compositions
of combustion
products
formed in experiments with freer Ti particles were
analyzed
and the results of these
analyses
are
illuslxated in Fig. 3. It is interesting that significant
amounts of TiN are found in the products in addition
to rutile and anatase, two modifications
of Ti2Os. It
can be noted
that based
on the relative
peak
intensities, more of the TiN (and less of Ti203) was
formed in a run with the higher Ti powder load (_ =
3.1), in which no particle explosions were observed.
These observations
are consistent
with the earlier
results
