wave structure
includes a layer with a
pressure gradient,
where the gas motion is induced
by the production
or consumption
of the gas
in the reaction as well as by thermal expansion
of the gas. The width of this zone determines
the
scale of the combustion
wave in the porous medium.
RAPIDLY
PROPAGATING
LIQUID
FLAMES
IN GRAVITATIONAL
FIELDS
We
consider
the combustion,
in a gravitational
field, of a porous solid, in which the high temperature
ahead of the reaction zone destrws
tile solid, due, e.g. to melting of sorne of the solid components.
Thus, a suspension
is formed, consisting
of a liquid bath containing
solid or liquid particles.
Tile
resulting combustion
wave is referred to as a liquid flame. Processes such as heat and mass transfer
as well as chemical reactions
in tile suspension
determine
the structure
of the liquid flame and its
propagation
velocity. Under the influence of gravitational
forces there is tile possibility
of relative
motion of tile liquid and solid. Previous
theoretical
analyses considered
the rate of heat transfer
between
the solid and liquid phases to be sufficiently
large that their two distinct
temperatures
rapidly equilibrated
to a single temperature.
In addition
to this case, we also consider
the case
when the rate of heat transfer is not so large and the model involves the separate
temperatures
of
the solid and liquid phases.
_\Te filLd that multiplicity
of traveling wave structures
is possible.
In
particular,
in addition
to a low velocity structure,
which is essentially
the same as tl,at obtained
from tile one temperature
description,
we find a high velocity structure,
which does not exist in the
one temperature
description,
but rather depends on tile fact that the solid and fluid temperatures
differ from each other. Both structures
can exist for the same parameter
values in a given range.
We describe the dependeuce
of the combustion
characteristics
of tile two structures
on gravitational
forces and other factors. Ill particular,
we compare the characteristics
in gravity and microgravity
environments.
GAS PHASE
INFLUENCE
ON LIQUID
FLAMES
IN GRAVITATIONAL
FIELDS
A porous solid is ignited at one end. A high temperature
combustion
wave then propagates
through
the sample. IlL
this process, melting of some or all tile components
is often observed.
Therefore,
we
study combustion
waves propagating
through a medium whose combustion
temperature
exceeds the
melting temperatures
of many components.
The solid matrix is thus destroyed
by tile propagating
combustion
wave due to melting ahead of the reaction
zone, and a liquid bath is formed which
contains
gaseous bubbles.
TILe waves propagate
in the presence of a"
ntrs:20010074064,ntrs,Filtration Combustion in Smoldering and SHS,,4,"x is thus destroyed
by tile propagating
combustion
wave due to melting ahead of the reaction
zone, and a liquid bath is formed which
contains
gaseous bubbles.
TILe waves propagate
in the presence of a gravitational
field. Due to
the effect of gravity, there is relative motion between tile rising bubbles and the descending
bath,
which affects the composition
of the medium,
its thermophysical
properties,
tile ""liquid flame""
structure,
and the propagation
velocity.
To enhance our understanding
of phenomena
associated
with tile interaction
of the relative motion with the propagating
combustion
wave we formulate
and
anMyze a relatively simple mathematical
model of liquid flames in a gravitational
field. We describe
the wave structure
and combustion
characteristics
including the combustion
velocity. We compare
our results to existing experimental
observations
and suggest new experiments
to be performed.
We consider tile effects of gravity and, in particular,
examine both microgravity
and large gravity
conditions.

