The RPE is a measure of the pointing stability of the payload. Its spectral content is determined

by the time interval of definition, which often arises naturally from the physical properties of the payload.
For instance, for imaging instruments which accumulate photons over discrete integration periods, image
quality is dependent on the stability of the payload attitude during the integration periods.

Pointing €nor Sources: Pointing error sources can be classified according to their time
dependence.
Lona Term Effects consist of bias errors and drift errors.
A bias error is a residual fixed offset error which is stable throughout the spacecraft mission. Drift errors
are variations due to aging effects which appear slow in time but have no periodic character.
Medium/Lona Term Effects result from error sources which are very often of harmonic nature. their
period of oscillation is normally of the Order of the orbital or half orbital period (period may be also
shorter or longer). The error has a mean of zero so that it does not contribute to the bias errors. The
amplitude and phase may also vary (drift) with time. Thermoelastic deformations are an example of
harmonic errors which are often related to the orbital period (eclipse and hot case).

736

Short Term Effects are often considered as random errors because they vary in an unpredictable
manner, and in short duration as compared to the orbital period. They are assumed to have a Gaussian
distribution with standard deviation

