Conclusion
A sub-grid model was developed to account for electro-viscous effect in lab-on-chip
microfluidics system. The model was successfully implemented into CFR-ACE+, and validation
study was performed. The present model predicts straight micro channel flow well and
qualitatively predicts T-junction flow correctly. The electro-viscous effects were found to be
quite significant and reduce flow rate (up to 40% in T-junction flows), when compared to the
classical laminar theory. Further refinement of the model to account for realistic velocity profile
and non-linear Boltzmann distributions within EDL are desirable. Further validations with
carefully designe"
ntrs:20050215300,ntrs,The 2004 NASA Faculty Fellowship Program Research Reports,,33,"al laminar theory. Further refinement of the model to account for realistic velocity profile
and non-linear Boltzmann distributions within EDL are desirable. Further validations with
carefully designed complex micro flow experiments are also recommended.
