In Figure 5 electromagnetic energy is incident from the left as E0. A portion of
that energy is reflected from mirror 3, with reflectance r3, as ER and a portion transmitted
to mirror 2 and so forth. If none of the mirrors are perfectly reflecting there will be
transmitted energy represented as ET. The analysis of coupled Fabry-Perot structures, as
the one shown in Figure 5, must take into account not only the magnitude and phase of
the reflected and transmitted energy at each mirror but also the optical path length of each
cavity. The analysis proceeds virtually identically to that in reference 1 for the ring
resonators. The following analysis applies to coupled optical ring resonators as well as to
coupled Fabry-Perot resonators and we will adopt the language of the optical resonators.
