θ1

Figure 1 Illustration of Snell’s Law with n2 > n1

Total Internal Reflection (TIR) is the effect of light reflecting predominantly inside the
medium of high refractive index, instead of refracting and passing through a boundary to
a medium with lower refractive index. TIR occurs when θ 2 ≥ θ c . The critical angle, θ c ,
is when θ 1 = 90° . At that point, all light at the n2:n1 boundary reflects back into material
n2.1 TIR is critical to transmitting light through a waveguide, such as an optical fiber or a
piece of quartz tubing, Fig 2.

