frontiers in colloidal engineering. Colloids are small
enough that they behave much like atoms and can be
used to model their behavior. Their size, shape, and
interactions enable us to control them, hence their
importance in industry. Paints, aerosols, and foams are
examples of manufactured colloidal mixtures. Colloids
also appear frequently in nature (e.g. milk). Understanding the properties of colloids should help scientists to
specifically engineer them for the manufacture of new
materials and products and to provide better control for
existing products. One example is the perfection of
stabilizers. These play a significant role in many multibillion dollar industries. The gravitational effects of
sedimentation and convection have distorted these
measurements in the past, and with this mask removed,
we are able to refine our understanding of phase separation.

