(InSPACE-2/3) - 2002 to 2012
• The majority of InSPACE hardware launched in 2002 with the goal of determining the true 3-D low-energy
(equilibrium) structure of an Magneto-rheological (MR) fluid while under DC and pulsed magnetic fields.
• -2 observed a regime of buckling instability in the 3-D structures under a particular magnetic field strength
and pulse frequency.
• -3 is currently installed in the MSG on the ISS and will investigate the effect of non-spherical superparamagnetic particles on the kinetics and formation of these aggregate structures that affect the viscoelastic properties of MR fluids. Operations are scheduled to start in Nov. 2012.

The Shear history Extensional Rheology Experiment (SHERE [II]) - 2007 to 2011
• The SHERE hardware was designed to study the effects of pre-shearing by rotation on a polymer fluid
while it is being stretched (elongated). In many industrial polymer processing operations the material
experiences a complex flow history with both shear and extensional characteristics.
• SHERE II extended the studies by adding a rigid inert filler to change the fluid properties.

