Detection Capability
An alarm threshold had to be established for the Sky lab
detectors. This threshold determined the sensitivity of the
detection system, or, more importantly, the size to which a
flame must grow before it could be detected . Moreover, the
threshold had to be set high enough to preclude the incidence
of false alarms.
A prediction of the detection capability from the known
spectral calibr.ttion curve of the detector tube was rendered
impossible by a complete lack of srectral radiance data for
fircs at applicable wavelengths. Further, any slIch data
obtained in tlte laboratory under normal gravitational
conditions would inadequately represent a fire burning in the
low-pressure, oxygen-rich , zero-gravity environment of the
Skylab. Convection currents are established around Earthbound fires maintaining supplies of fresh oxygen to the fbmes;
under zero-gravity condition there will be no natural convection around the flame, and fires can be expected to burn less
fiercely?') Some convection will be maintained by the
Skylab ventilation systems in the crew-living areas but several
of the fire detectors will monitor enclosed compartments
through which air is not circulated.
