<body><div class=""page""><p></p>
<p>Sub Topic: Laminar Flames 
</p>
<p> 1 
</p>
<p>2024 Spring Technical Meeting of the Central States Section of The Combustion Institute 
</p>
<p>May 12 - 14, 2024 
</p>
<p>Case Western Reserve University in Cleveland, OH 
</p>
<p> 
</p>
<p>The effects of pressure and optical thickness on radiative losses 
</p>
<p>in spherical diffusion flames in microgravity 
 
</p>
<p>Kendyl A. Waddell1,*, Daniel L. Dietrich1, Vedha Nayagam2 
 
</p>
<p>1NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, Ohio, USA 
2Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, Ohio, 44106, USA 
</p>
<p>*Corresponding Author Email: kendyl.a.waddell@nasa.gov 
 
</p>
<p>1. Introduction 
</p>
<p>In microgravity combustion, heat loss due to radiation plays a large role. Combustion 
</p>
<p>products are able to accumulate in the flame region and radiate heat, while the heat release from 
</p>
<p>the flame remains relatively constant. This causes the flame temperature to decrease until 
</p>
<p>combustion can no longer be sustained, a phenomenon known as radiative extinction [1]. 
</p>
<p>Microgravity spherical diffusion flames have been studied for many years, typically in 
</p>
<p>droplet [2–6] and burner supported [7–10] configurations. The two configurations are similar, 
<"
ntrs:20240003229,ntrs,The Effects of Pressure and Optical Thickness on Radiative Losses in Spherical Diffusion Flames in Microgravity,,1,"</p>
<p>Microgravity spherical diffusion flames have been studied for many years, typically in 
</p>
<p>droplet [2–6] and burner supported [7–10] configurations. The two configurations are similar, 
</p>
<p>differing primarily by the boundary conditions at the surface of the droplet or sphere. The 
</p>
<p>burner-supported configuration is, however, computationally easier to study since the mass flux 
</p>
<p>and temperature at the burner surface are prescribed rather than being part of the 
</p>
<p>solution. During hot-flame burning and radiative extinction of spherical droplet flames the 
</p>
<p>ISS experiments show that the change in droplet size is relatively small (e.g., [4]), and if the 
</p>
<p>gas-phase is quasi-steady then the burner-supported configuration can provide accurate insights 
</p>
<p>into the radiative extinction process [10–12], provided the boundary conditions at the burner 
</p>
<p>surface match the burning rate and droplet surface temperature in the experiment. 
</p>
<p>Radiative heat transfer in combustion systems has been studied extensively, with particular 
</p>
<p>attention paid to the accuracy of different radiation property models and radiative transfer 
</p>
<p>equation (RTE) solution methods [13–17]. The three main types of radiation models, in order of 
</p>
<p>increasing accuracy, are the grey gas, statistical narrow-band (SNB), and line-by-line models. 
</p>
<p>Grey gas models do not account for the change of radiative properties with wavelength and tend 
</p>
<p>to result in unacceptable accuracy, and line-by-line models are highly accurate but often too 
</p>
<p>computationally expensive. The SNB method strikes a good balance between accuracy and 
</p>
<p>computational time, thus the SNB method is used here. The RTE is solved using the discrete 
</p>
<p>ordinates method. 
</p>
<p>When solving the RTE it is common to use one of two treatments. The first is that radiators 
</p>
<p>both emit and absorb radiation, i.e., the system is optically thick. This is the more accurate 
</p>
<p>treatment but can be computationally expensive. The second treatment is to assume radiators 
</p>
<p>only emit radiation but do not absorb it, i.e., the system is optically thin. This can greatly reduce 
</p>
<p>computation times, but is often unable to reproduce experimental behavior without additional 
</p>
<p>correction factors [13–17]. 
</p>
<p>During recent droplet experiments performed aboard the International Space Station (ISS) 
</p>
<p>radiance measurements were taken that captured both the total flame radiance and the radiance </p>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class=""page""><p></p>
<p>Sub Topic: Laminar Flames 
</p>
<p> 2 
</p>
<p>from water vapor in the 5-7 µm range at various ambient pressures [4]. Those results are 
</p>
<p>compared here to the results from a transient numerical model of burner supported flames. The 
</p>
<p>influence of using the optically thick or optically thin assumption on the accuracy of the 
</p>
<p>nume"
ntrs:20240003229,ntrs,The Effects of Pressure and Optical Thickness on Radiative Losses in Spherical Diffusion Flames in Microgravity,,2,"d here to the results from a transient numerical model of burner supported flames. The 
</p>
<p>influence of using the optically thick or optically thin assumption on the accuracy of the 
</p>
<p>numerical predictions is also examined for the spherically symmetric diffusion flames. 
</p>
<p> 
</p>
<p>2. Methods 
</p>
<p>Droplet experiments were carried out aboard the International Space Station (ISS) as part of 
</p>
<p>the Cool Flames Investigation (CFI) subset of the Flame Extinguishment Experiment (FLEX) set 
</p>
<p>of experiments. A detailed description of the experimental hardware can be found in references 
</p>
<p>[4–6]. Both free floated droplets and droplets supported by a 40 µm carbide fiber were used. 
</p>
<p>Initial droplet diameters ranged from 4 to 4.5 mm. The fuel was pure dodecane, and the ambient 
</p>
<p>was air (0.21/0.79 O2/N2). Ambient pressure ranged from 0.5 to 5 atm. 
</p>
<p>Two radiometers were used to measure flame radiance. The first was a wideband radiometer 
</p>
<p>that could detect flame radiance from the visible to the far infrared, and the other was a 
</p>
<p>narrowband radiometer that could detect radiance in the 5-7 µm range, where water vapor has 
</p>
<p>strong absorbance. More detail on the radiometers can be found in [6]. 
</p>
<p>Numerical simulations were performed using the SphDiff program [8,10,9,18,19], which is a 
</p>
<p>modification of Sandia PREMIX [20]. This uses the Chemkin-II package [20]. This numerical 
</p>
<p>model has been used in various studies of burner supported spherical diffusion flames, and has 
</p>
<p>been shown to accurately reproduce the experimental results [8,10,9,18,19]. The chemical 
</p>
<p>kinetics mechanism from UCSD [21] was used. A full model description can be found in 
</p>
<p>[10,18,19]. 
</p>
<p>The inner boundary was the burner surface, at a radius of 2.25 mm. Mass flow rate and 
</p>
<p>burner temperature were prescribed, and species mass flux and energy were conserved. The outer 
</p>
<p>boundary was at a radius of 100 cm and temperature and species concentrations were prescribed 
</p>
<p>there. The domain was sufficiently large that there was no change in species or temperature near 
</p>
<p>the outer boundary. 
</p>
<p>An adaptive grid algorithm was used, as in [10]. The domain was divided into three grid 
</p>
<p>areas: an inner area, a reaction area, and an outer area. The inner area follows the same algorithm 
</p>
<p>as [19]. The reaction area has 200 grid cells with 0.001 cm spacing and was centered about the 
</p>
<p>peak temperature. The grid spacing in the outer area increased by 5% for each grid cell moving 
</p>
<p>outward. A total of 373 grid cells were used, which satisfied grid independence. 
</p>
<p>The radiation model is described in detail in [18,19]. The model considered radiation heat 
</p>
<p>loss from CO, CO2, and H2O. A detailed absorption/emission statistical narrow band model was 
</p>
<p>used to calculate the absorption coefficient for"
ntrs:20240003229,ntrs,The Effects of Pressure and Optical Thickness on Radiative Losses in Spherical Diffusion Flames in Microgravity,,3,"19]. The model considered radiation heat 
</p>
<p>loss from CO, CO2, and H2O. A detailed absorption/emission statistical narrow band model was 
</p>
<p>used to calculate the absorption coefficient for these species. For optically thin simulations, 
</p>
<p>absorption was neglected. Radiation properties were taken from the HITRAN database [22] with 
</p>
<p>wavenumbers ranging from 150 to 9300 cm-1. The data was divided into 366 bands, each of 25 
</p>
<p>cm-1, and properties were averaged over each band considering a Lorentz spectral line shape of 
</p>
<p>random spacing and strength. The radiative transfer equation was solved using a discrete-
</p>
<p>ordinates method with 20 ordinates [18,19]. Radiation from soot was not considered in these 
</p>
<p>simulations, as experimental observations show luminous soot does not typically form in these 
</p>
<p>flames after the ignition transient owing to the large radiative losses [10–12]. 
</p>
<p>For the simulations the fuel was pure heptane, owing the lack of availability of reduced 
</p>
<p>chemical kinetic models for dodecane combustion. It was shown in [6] that flame radiance for 
</p>
<p>different hydrocarbons was similar despite their different compositions. Thus, it is reasonable to 
</p>
<p>compare the results of heptane simulations to those of dodecane experiments. As in the </p>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class=""page""><p></p>
<p>Sub Topic: Laminar Flames 
</p>
<p> 3 
</p>
<p>experiments, the ambient was air (0.21/0.79 O2/N2). Mass flow rate was held constant at 0.9 
</p>
<p>mg/s, the burner surface temperature was held constant at the boiling point of heptane at each 
</p>
<p>pressure, and pressure was varied from 0.5 to 5 atm. The burner size, temperature, and mass flow 
</p>
<p>rate were chosen to best replicate the conditions from the droplet experiments. 
</p>
<p>3. Results and Discussion 
</p>
<p>Figure 1a shows the simulated temporal evolution of flame temperature and flame radius for 
</p>
<p>the 0.5 atm, 1 atm, and 5 atm cases. These simulations were performed using the SNB method 
</p>
<p>with emission and reabsorption, unless otherwise indicated. The evolution of flame temperature 
</p>
<p>is nearly invariant with pressure, and all three flames extinguish at temperatures close to 1200 K. 
</p>
<p>Although not shown, after hot flame extinction these flames transition to cool flames (0.5 and 1 
</p>
<p>atm) or warm flames (5 atm) depending on the pressure. Warm and cool flames are not discussed 
</p>
<p>here. Unlike temperature, both the rate of flame growth and the flame radius at extinction 
</p>
<p>decrease with increasing pressure. 
</p>
<p> 
Figure 1b shows the simulated temporal evolution of total flame radiance and H2O radiance 
</p>
<p>for the 0.5 atm, 1 atm, and 5 atm cases. Total radiance decreases with increasing pressure, while 
</p>
<p>H2O radiance increases. Thus, the fraction of total radiance that comes from water vapor (the 
</p>
<p>radiance ratio) increases with increasing"
ntrs:20240003229,ntrs,The Effects of Pressure and Optical Thickness on Radiative Losses in Spherical Diffusion Flames in Microgravity,,4,"nce decreases with increasing pressure, while 
</p>
<p>H2O radiance increases. Thus, the fraction of total radiance that comes from water vapor (the 
</p>
<p>radiance ratio) increases with increasing pressure. Qualitatively, the temporal evolution of the 
</p>
<p>flame agrees well with the behavior observed during droplet experiments aboard the ISS. 
</p>
<p> Figure 2 shows the results from the droplet experiments compared to the numerical results. 
</p>
<p>Total flame radiance, H2O radiance, and radiance ratio are all shown as a function of pressure, 
</p>
<p>from 0.5 to 5 atm. These points were taken at the peak total radiance value in both the 
</p>
<p>experiments and simulations, which occurs just before flame extinction (see Figure 1b). 
</p>
<p> 
