The total flame radiance shows no dependence on pressure experimentally and is roughly 25 
</p>
<p>W at all pressures. The numerical results show a slight decrease with increasing pressure, from 
</p>
<p>26.5 W to 23.5 W, as pressure increases from 0.5 to 5 atm. The difference in these trends is 
</p>
<p>likely caused by the different boundary conditions for each configuration. 
</p>
<p>Figure 2 also shows H2O radiance, and in both the experiments and simulations it is seen to 
</p>
<p>increase with increasing pressure. The magnitude of the H2O radiance is higher in the 
</p>
<p>simulations compared to the experiments, likely owing to the limitations of the experimental 
</p>
<p>hardware. The values from the experiments are taken from a radiometer with a filter that only 
</p>
<p>allows radiation in the 5-7 µm range to pass through, and the radiometer’s response is not flat in 
</p>
<p>this range [6]. Water vapor also emits radiation outside of this range, so it is expected that the 
</p>
<p>measured magnitude would be less than the numerical predictions. 
</p>
<p>As H2O radiance increases with increasing pressure while total radiance remains relatively 
</p>
<p>constant, the radiance ratio is also increasing with increasing pressure, as show"
ntrs:20240003229,ntrs,The Effects of Pressure and Optical Thickness on Radiative Losses in Spherical Diffusion Flames in Microgravity,,5,"dictions. 
</p>
<p>As H2O radiance increases with increasing pressure while total radiance remains relatively 
</p>
<p>constant, the radiance ratio is also increasing with increasing pressure, as shown in Fig. 2. Water 
</p>
<p>vapor is contributing more to the overall radiative loss as pressure increases, at the expense of 
</p>
<p>radiative losses from CO2. The other radiator, CO, has a negligible contribution in all cases. In 
</p>
<p>the experiments the radiance ratio increases from 0.3 to 0.55 as pressure increases, and in the 
</p>
<p>simulations, it increases from 0.45 to 0.65 as pressure increases. 
</p>
<p>The reason for the increase in H2O radiance ratio with increasing pressure is likely due to the 
</p>
<p>optically thick nature of the system. To explore this further, additional simulations were 
</p>
<p>performed with the reabsorption term in the RTE neglected, to reflect an optically thin system. 
</p>
<p>The results for one of these cases at 1 atm are shown in Fig. 3. Fig. 3a shows the temporal 
</p>
<p>evolution of flame temperature and flame radius for the optically thin and optically thick cases. 
</p>
<p>The flame grows at the same rate in both cases, but the flame temperature drops much faster in 
</p>
<p>the thin case owing to excessive radiative losses. This causes the flame to extinguish after only 2 
</p>
<p>s in the optically thin case, compared to 12 s in the optically thick case. 
</p>
<p> 
