Total radiance and H2O radiance are shown in Fig. 3b. When reabsorption is neglected, the 
</p>
<p>total radiance increases, which is the expected behavior. However, the radiance contribution 
</p>
<p>from H2O decreases in the optically thin system. The reason for this involves cross interactions 
</p>
<p>between H2O and CO2, as will be explained below. 
</p>
<p>Figure 4 shows how the total, H2O, and CO2 radiance vary with pressure for the optically thin 
</p>
<p>and thick cases for pressures of 0.5 to 5 atm. Like in Fig. 2, these points were all taken when the 
</p>
<p>total radiance was at its peak value, just before extinction. The total r"
ntrs:20240003229,ntrs,The Effects of Pressure and Optical Thickness on Radiative Losses in Spherical Diffusion Flames in Microgravity,,6,"lly thin 
</p>
<p>and thick cases for pressures of 0.5 to 5 atm. Like in Fig. 2, these points were all taken when the 
</p>
<p>total radiance was at its peak value, just before extinction. The total radiance exhibits a stronger 
</p>
<p>decrease with pressure in the optically thin cases than in the optically thick cases, decreasing 
</p>
<p>from 28.5 W at 0.5 atm to 19.5 W at 5 atm. For the optically thin cases the decrease in total 
</p>
<p>radiance is reflected in both the CO2 and H2O radiance values, with CO2 decreasing from 22.5 W 
</p>
<p>to 16 W and H2O decreasing from 6 W to 3.5 W as pressure increases. The CO2 radiance follows 
</p>
<p>the same trend in the optically thick cases, but H2O radiance increases, as mentioned previously. 
</p>
<p> 
Figure 4 shows that the H2O radiance is consistently lower in the optically thin cases, while 
</p>
<p>the CO2 radiance is consistently higher. One would expect both values to increase, with more 
</p>
<p>energy being lost in the optically thin cases due to the lack of reabsorption. The reason for this 
