<p> 6 
</p>
<p>unexpected behavior is due to the interactions between CO2 and H2O radiation. Namely, in the 
</p>
<p>optically thick case a portion of the radiation that is emitted by CO2 is then absorbed by H2O and 
</p>
<p>re-emitted. This increases the amount of H2O radiance compared to the optically thin case, while 
</p>
<p>correspondingly decreasing the amount of CO2 radiance. 
</p>
<p>To further explain this phenomenon, it is useful to examine the spatial properties of these 
</p>
<p>flames. Figure 5a compares the optically thick and thin CO2 and"
ntrs:20240003229,ntrs,The Effects of Pressure and Optical Thickness on Radiative Losses in Spherical Diffusion Flames in Microgravity,,7,"e amount of CO2 radiance. 
</p>
<p>To further explain this phenomenon, it is useful to examine the spatial properties of these 
</p>
<p>flames. Figure 5a compares the optically thick and thin CO2 and H2O mole fraction profiles vs 
</p>
<p>radius for the 1 atm case. These profiles are taken at the same point as Fig. 4, where the total 
</p>
<p>flame radiance is at its peak value. The profiles are significantly broader in the optically thick 
</p>
<p>case, owing to the longer combustion duration (12 s v. 2.5 s) which allows for a larger product 
</p>
<p>build up. The peak CO2 and H2O mole fraction values are similar between the two radiation 
</p>
<p>models. 
</p>
<p>Figure 5b compares the integrated radiance profiles for the total, CO2, and H2O radiance 
</p>
<p>between the optically thick and thin cases. The effect of reabsorption at the inner and outer edges 
</p>
<p>of the flame can be seen by examining the radiance profiles. In the optically thick case, 
</p>
<p>reabsorption near the burner surface leads to less loss throughout the flame region. The total 
</p>
<p>radiative loss decreases slightly from its peak value of 26.5 W to a final value of 25.5 W. The 
</p>
<p>effect of reabsorption is even more apparent in the CO2 profile, which decreases from a peak 
</p>
<p>value of 16 W to a final value of 12.5 W. H2O has the smallest effect, decreasing from a peak of 
</p>
<p>13 W to a final value of 12.5 W. 
</p>
<p> 
Looking at the case without reabsorption, the total radiative loss is 26 W, CO2 loss is 21 W, 
</p>
<p>and H2O loss is 5 W. The lack of reabsorption leads to the radiative losses from CO2 to be higher 
</p>
<p>throughout the entire domain. Water vapor, however, has higher losses in the optically thin case 
</p>
<p>only in the region closer to the burner. Moving outwards the optically thin H2O profile plateaus, 
</p>
<p>while the corresponding optically thick profile keeps increasing. In the same region (roughly 2 
</p>
<p>cm to 6 cm), the difference between the optically thick and thin CO2 profiles grows, and there is 
</p>
<p>net absorption of CO2 radiation after 3 cm. This supports the idea that CO2 radiance is being 
</p>
<p>absorbed and re-emitted by H2O in this region. 
</p>
<p>This explanation for the radiative behavior of CO2 and H2O in optically thick flames at least 
</p>
<p>partially explains the experimental observation of the increase of the radiance ratio with 
</p>
<p>increasing pressure. As pressure increases, the divergence between the radiative losses from H2O 
</p>
<p>for the optically thick and optically thin cases increases as well. The optically thin assumption is 
</p>
<p>a more realistic approximation at lower pressures, since fewer molecules lead to less absorption. 
</p>
<p>Absorption levels increase with increasing pressure, allowing H2O to absorb and re-emit more 
