</p>
<p>4. Conclusions 
</p>
<p>Results from microgravity droplet experiments were presented and compared to results from 
</p>
<p>a transient numerical model using a spherical porous burner. The effects of pressure and the 
</p>
<p>optical thickness of the radiation model were considered. The main conclusions are as follows. 
</p>
<p>1. Experimentally, total flame radiance is independent of pressure. Radiance from H2O 
</p>
<p>increases with increasing ambient pressure, comprising 30% of the radiative losses at 0.5 
</p>
<p>atm, and 55% at 5 atm. 
</p>
<p>2. The numerical model was shown to accurately predict the trends of the experiments, 
</p>
<p>despite the differences in fuels and configurations. Total flame radiance decreased 
</p>
<p>slightly in the model from 26.5 W to 23.5 W, likely due to differences in boundary 
</p>
<p>conditions. The contribution to radiative losses from H2O in the model increased from 
</p>
<p>45% to 65% as pressure increased from 0.5 to 5 atm. The difference in magnitude 
</p>
<p>between these values and the experimental results is attributed to the inability of the 
</p>
<p>experimental measurement to completely capture H2O radiation. 
</p>
<p>3. Simulations comparing optically thick and optically thin radiation models were used to 
</p>
<p>further explain the experimental behavior. The optically thin assumption radically 
</p>
<p>decreased combustion duration due to excessive radiative losses. Radiative losses from 
</p>
<p>CO2 increased when reabsorption was neglected, while radiance levels from H2O 
</p>
<p>decreased. This behavior was attributed to the fact that H2O absorbs and re-emits 
</p>
<p>radiation from CO2 in the optically thick case. 
</p>
<p>4. Due to the optically thick nature of the system, absorption levels are increased as ambient 
</p>
<p>pressure increases. This allows H2O to absorb and re-emit larger amounts of CO2 
</p>
<p>radiation at higher pressures, increas"
ntrs:20240003229,ntrs,The Effects of Pressure and Optical Thickness on Radiative Losses in Spherical Diffusion Flames in Microgravity,,9,"ature of the system, absorption levels are increased as ambient 
</p>
<p>pressure increases. This allows H2O to absorb and re-emit larger amounts of CO2 
</p>
<p>radiation at higher pressures, increasing the fraction of radiative losses that come from 
</p>
<p>H2O. 
</p>
