<p>A. Preliminary Studies 
It is well understood that fires 
</p>
<p>extinguish via two different 
phenomena: either due to high flow 
velocity (e.g. blowing out a birthday 
candle), shown in the orange blow off 
region; or due to radiative heat loss, 
shown in the blue quench region of 
Figure 2. Because the intent of this 
depressurization evaluation was to 
determine if materials would extinguish 
due to lack of oxygen availability, it was 
important to first select materials that 
had high blow off velocities for this 
evaluation. Materials with high blow off 
velocities will continue to burn despite 
turbulent flows during depressurization 
until sufficient oxygen is removed to 
induce extinguishment. 
</p>
<p>Preliminary tests were performed on 
different materials to aid in the selection 
of best test materials by characterizing blowoff velocities required for each material. 
</p>
<p>Figure 3 shows different materials evaluated during the initial test series. Linear behavior was assumed at higher 
velocities and utilized a modified ISO 4589-4 (High Velocity Oxygen Index) test apparatus. Modifications allowed 
for concurrent, automated ignition of samples within a closed chamber in order to control pressure. These preliminary 
tests showed that extinguishment as a result of high flow blowoff was not expected to occur in 30% oxygen until flows 
</p>
