<p>IV. Analysis 
The maximum velocity measured from the flow meter placed between the two test chambers was used to 
</p>
<p>characterize the velocities associated with depressurization time. 
The maximum chimney velocity was then calculated by 
comparing the diameter of the chimney around the test coupon, 
for single tests only, and the diameter of the pipe where the flow 
meter was placed. This number was then normalized based on the 
pressure difference between the two chambers as outlined in 
Equation 1. The Sierra 620S mass flow meter is based on a 
standard reference pressure of 14.7 psia (101.35 kPa) measured 
in standard cubic feet per minute (scfm). 
</p>
<p>The depressurization rate decreased with time as shown in Figure 7, with a steep initial pressure drop that gradually 
decreased as the test chamber pressure dropped to approximately 1 psia (6.9 kPa). Depressurization time was 
determined by the approximate time it took the test and vacuum chambers to reach equilibrium. 
</p>
<p> 
Figure 7. Test Chamber Pressure Decrease for Varying Depressurization Times 
</p>
<p>V. Results and Discussion 
Conceptually, depressurizing a spacecraft during a fire event would be expected to extinguish the fire by either 
</p>
<p>providing a high rate of flow to ‘blow off’ the fire or by achieving sufficiently low enough pressure such that there 
would be insufficient oxygen to maintain the flame. This study evaluated the effect of depressurization on three 
materials. The effectiveness of high velocity flow was evaluated by looking at various flow rates achieved through 
varying depressurization rate, location in the test chamber, and presence or absence of a ‘chimney’ to maximize local 
gas flow. The effectiveness of minimizing pressure was assessed at various depressurization rates. 
</p>
<p>A. Effect of High Velocity Flow 
No flowrates could be achieved that were sufficiently high to extinguish the cotton t-shirt or ABS. This was true 
</p>
<p>for all depressurization rates and both the open cabin configuration and chimney configuration. Nomex, however, was 
extinguished in two of the four tests. For the 15 second (s) and 26s depress rates, the Nomex extinguished in 
