<p>approximately 4s and 6s, respectively. Flow velocity 
across the surface was estimated to be 
approximately 14,752 mm/s, and 9,388 mm/s, 
respectively. Notably, the flame characteristics 
changed significantly at the onset of 
depressurization as can be seen in the center images 
of Figure 8. When compared with the leftmost 
images (pre-depress), it can be seen that the flames 
transition from smooth upward flow to a highly 
erratic unpredictable flow. While this may be 
intuitive (sudden high velocity flow across the 
surface), it shows that the flow increases the area of 
impact for the flame to the surrounding volumes. In 
operation, this risks exposing the flame to local 
areas that otherwise may not have been impacted by 
the burning material. 
</p>
<p>For moderate (2-minute) and slow (15-minute) 
depress rates, the Nomex burned to completion. 
Figure 9 shows a comparison of the control test 
where no depressurization occurred and the 2-
minute depress. The slowest depress rate resulted in 
a total burn time of 40s, which was identical to the 
control burn (no depress). Interestingly, the 
moderate depress resulted in complete burn of the 
Nomex in 26s. This shows that for areas that do not 
receive sufficient flow for blow off, the increased flow to the Nomex fire increases the burn rate of the material. This 
was observed for both the cotton t-shirt and ABS as well. For cotton t-shirt and ABS, the baseline tests showed that it 
required 30s and 270s, respectively, to completely consume the test samples. By comparison, the air flow from the 
</p>
<p>rapid depress (15s) reduced the time it took to 
consume the sample materials to 20s and 207s, 
for cotton and ABS, respectively. For seven 
distinct tests, Table 4 shows the material burn 
rates and flame heights prior to depress and 
during depress. The significant increase in 
burn rate on all materials demonstrates the risk 
of the increased air flow caused by 
depressurization when the rates are insufficient 
to achieve blow off. 
</p>
<p>It is worth noting that the Nomex material 
was chosen as a candidate material that self-
extinguished at 30% oxygen and 14.7 psia in 
the J-configuration. Through this test series, it 
was noted that the material burned through 
when ignited with edge ignition. This 
highlights the difference in the surface ignition 
when applying the J-configuration and the 
standard edge ignition test configuration. This 
was noted as a “borderline” material that only 
passes in particular configurations at 30% 
oxygen, to represent more common flight 
hardware (neither worst nor best case). 
</p>
