Fig. 1. Spherical ﬂame around a fuel droplet suspended on a quartz ﬁber in microgravity.
technique was widely used in microgravity experi-
ments not only for single-droplet combustion [4,5]
and vaporization [6] at high pressures and droplet ig-
nition [7,8], but also for multiple-droplet combustion
such as two-droplet combustion [9,10] and droplet-
array combustion [11,12]. The quartz-ﬁber diameter
was approximately 125 µm, an order of magnitude
smaller than the droplet diameter (typically about
1 mm). Effects of the quartz ﬁber on the droplet com-
bustion became increasingly important at a later stage
of burning when the droplet diameter approached
the quartz-ﬁber diameter [13]. Avedisian and Jack-
son [14] reported the effect of the ﬁber on the soot
patterns and burning rate of the suspended droplet.
Free-droplet techniques have been developed in
which the droplet burns in microgravity without any
suspending ﬁbers. Kumagai et al. [15] generated a
free droplet in normal gravity by rapid retraction
of the quartz ﬁber from which the droplet was sus-
pended, and ignited the free droplet in micrograv-
ity immediately after the start of the free fall of
the experiment apparatus. Other free-droplet genera-
tion techniques employ a piezoelectric droplet gen-
erator [16], two opposing fuel-supply tubes that are
rapidly retracted [17], two opposing quartz ﬁbers that
are rapidly retracted [18], or a thin metal wire with a
droplet that is electrically heated [13]. In most tech-
niques, it was difﬁcult to observe the burning phe-
nomena with high resolution by taking a magniﬁed
picture. There is low probability of realizing a station-
ary droplet after the droplet generation, and even if it
is realized, the droplet shifts due to a slight asymme-
try at ignition and during burning [13]. The SiC ﬁber,
whose diameter is one order of magnitude smaller
than that of the quartz ﬁber, has been recently used as
the suspending ﬁber [19,20]. However, small droplet
generators that generate the droplet on the SiC ﬁber
with a high success rate and that are applicable to ex-
periments for purposes such as multiple-droplet com-
bustion and droplet ignition have yet to be realized.
Our research proposed a new droplet-generation
technique that overcomes as many disadvantages of
existing droplet generation techniques as possible.
Multiple droplets could be generated simultaneously
with a high success rate on SiC ﬁbers through the
present technique. We reported the experimental re-
sults of ﬂame spread along linear droplet arrays using
our new technique.
2. Droplet generation techniques
2.1. Classiﬁcation of existing droplet-generation
techniques
Ideal droplet combustion is spherically symmetri-
cal and one-dimensional. In order to realize it, a free
single droplet should be burnt in zero gravity with-
out any relative velocity to the ambient gas. The high
success rate of the experiment is favorable in view of
the applicability of the experimental method to space
experiments.
The existing droplet-generation techniques are
classiﬁed as follows:
(A) Droplet generation on a quartz ﬁber by fuel sup-
ply through a hypodermic needle;
(B) Free droplet generation by rapid retraction of one
or two quartz ﬁbers;
(C) Free droplet generation by rapid retraction of two
stainless-steel fuel-supply tubes;
(D) Free droplet generation by a piezoelectric droplet
generator;
(E) Free droplet generation by electrically heating a
thin metal wire with a droplet;
(F) Droplet generation on SiC ﬁber by rapid retrac-
tion of two stainless-steel fuel-supply tubes; and
(G) Droplet generation on SiC ﬁber by a piezoelec-
tric droplet generator.
The characteristics of each technique are listed in
Table 1. They are explained in the following.
Technique (A) is the most common [1,4–12]. An
advantage of this technique is in placing the droplet
in space, but there is a disadvantage in that the quartz
ﬁber can affect droplet vaporization and combustion.
In a typical case, the quartz-ﬁber diameter is 125 µm
while the droplet diameter is 1 mm. As explained
later, another disadvantage is that the droplet sus-
pended on the 125-µm quartz ﬁber is not spherical,"
