Technique (B) generates free droplets by rapidly
retracting one or two quartz ﬁbers. In the former, a
quartz ﬁber suspending the droplet is rapidly retracted
upward in normal gravity, and when the upwardly
released droplet starts to fall, the experimental appa-
ratus also starts to fall [15]. In the latter, a droplet is
ﬁrst suspended at the overlap of two quartz ﬁbers, and
then the stationary free droplet is generated by rapid
retraction of two opposite ﬁbers in microgravity [18].
Contrary to Technique (A), Technique (B) has the ad-
vantage that the droplet burns without any contact,
but also has the disadvantage that the possibility of
generating a nearly stationary droplet, whose relative
velocity to the ambient gas is very low, is low. Even
if the free droplet is stationary at the initial stage in
microgravity, the droplet cannot maintain the initial
position for the full burning period [13].
As in Technique (B), the possibility of generating
a nearly stationary droplet is low in Techniques (C),
(D), and (E). However, the droplet generator in Tech-
nique (E) can be small, so that it is suitable for an
experiment in a limited space such as high-pressure
combustion experiments [13].
In Technique (F), the low success rate for droplet
generation, which is one of the disadvantages in Tech-
nique (C), was increased by using an SiC ﬁber as a
droplet suspender [19]. This technique was applied in
droplet-combustion experiments conducted in a space
shuttle [21]. Since the suspending-ﬁber diameter in
Technique (F) is one order of magnitude smaller than
that in Technique (A), the deviation of the droplet
shape from sphericity is negligibly small (in con-
trast to the case in Technique (A)). This point will
be explained later. In this technique, similar to Tech-
nique (C), two opposite stainless-steel fuel-supply
tubes should be rapidly retracted, and thus it is dif-
ﬁcult to make a small apparatus. This technique is not
suitable for multiple-droplet experiments like droplet-
array combustion experiments. This technique also
has two disadvantages. The droplet vibrates initially,
largely due to the rapid retraction of the stainless-steel
tubes from the droplet [22], and the droplet sometimes
shifts along the SiC ﬁber during burning [22].
Technique (G) is based on Technique (D). Con-
trary to Technique (D), SiC ﬁbers are used to place
the droplet without droplet shift velocity [20]. Small
droplets injected by a piezoelectric droplet genera-
tor impinge successively on the intersection of two
SiC ﬁbers to generate a speciﬁc size droplet. Ave-
disian and Callahan [20] compared combustion of a
free droplet by Technique (D) and a ﬁber-supported
droplet by Technique (G) and reported that they were
clearly in agreement concerning the evolution of
droplet diameter. This technique enables us to make
a small apparatus; however, it is considered to take a
long time to generate a relatively large droplet (e.g.,
1 mm). In experiments with multiple droplets such as
a droplet array, it is probably difﬁcult to align the di-
rection of each droplet injection and its corresponding
ﬁber intersection. It is very difﬁcult to align them in
advance in normal gravity, especially when this tech-
nique is to be used in space experiments, and it is"
