Recently, Enomoto et al. [23] performed ignition
experiments of solid particle clouds in micrograv-
ity. Prior to the microgravity experiment, liqueﬁed
n-eicosane was supplied to intersections of SiC ﬁbers
stretched in a lattice, and the droplets solidiﬁed at
room-temperature air in normal gravity. In this tech-
nique, it is difﬁcult to use a fuel that is not solidiﬁed
at room temperature. It is desirable to develop a tech-
nique by which droplets are placed on SiC ﬁbers with
a high success rate.
2.2. A new droplet-generation technique
This section proposes a new droplet-generation
technique. A schematic diagram of the new technique
is shown in Fig. 2. A liquid fuel is supplied to the
intersection of two ﬁne SiC ﬁbers through the corre-
sponding ﬁne glass tubes. The droplets are generated
on the intersections by retracting the glass tubes. The
suspending ﬁber used in the present study was a 14-
µm-diameter SiC ﬁber (Hi-Nicalon, Nippon Carbon).
The outer diameter of the glass tube tip was about
40 µm.
In Technique (A), a liquid fuel is supplied to the tip
of a 125-µm quartz ﬁber through a stainless-steel tube,
and the droplet is generated by retracting the tube.
However, it is impossible to place the droplet on an
SiC ﬁber whose diameter is one order of magnitude
smaller than the droplet diameter using a commercial
ﬁne stainless-steel tube. We enabled generation of the
droplet on 14-µm SiC ﬁber by supplying fuel through
a glass tube whose tip diameter was narrowed down
to 40 µm by a pipette generator (PC-10, Narishige).
The rapid tube retraction seen in Technique (F) is not
required in our technique.
Characteristics of the present technique are de-
scribed below:
(A) Nearly spherical droplet shape;
(B) A large range of droplet diameters generated;
(C) A high success rate of droplet deployment on SiC
ﬁbers;
(D) No droplet drift during burning;
(E) No effects of droplet vibration associated with
droplet generation in experiments;
(F) Rapid droplet generation;
(G) Applicability to multiple-droplet experiments;
(H) Small droplet-generation device.
Droplets of 0.3 to 1.5 mm can be generated in nor-
mal gravity through the present method. In normal
gravity, even droplets of less than 1 mm sometimes
fall off the SiC ﬁber intersection during the glass tube
retraction, if the tip of a fuel supply glass tube and the
ﬁber intersection are not in good alignment. The suc-
cess rate is even lower in generating droplets greater
than 1 mm. We achieved droplet placement on the
ﬁber intersection in microgravity through the same
droplet generation process that had a low success
rate of droplet generation in normal gravity. This fact
proves that it is possible to generate droplets within
a wide range of the droplet diameter with a high suc-
cess rate using glass-tube retraction in microgravity.
Our success rate of droplet generation in micrograv-
ity is 100%.
2.3. Effects of suspending ﬁbers
Our proposed technique eliminates many disad-
vantages in existing droplet-generation techniques.
Unlike free-droplet techniques, however, the suspend-
ing ﬁber may affect the droplet combustion. We will
discuss suspending ﬁber effects on droplet shape and
heat transfer in the following.
First, we describe the effect of suspending ﬁbers
on droplet shape. Fig. 3 shows photographs of droplets
supported in microgravity on the tip of a 125-µm
quartz ﬁber and on the intersection of 14-µm SiC
ﬁbers. Both droplets were generated under the same
fuel-supply condition. n-Decane of 2.67×10−10 m3,
which corresponds to the volume of a sphere with a
diameter d = 0.80 mm, was supplied through a ﬁne
glass tube. The shape of the droplet supported on
