Second, we discuss the effects of suspending ﬁbers
on thermal conduction outside and inside the droplet.
Typical thermal properties of air, n-decane, quartz
glass, and SiC ﬁber (Hi-Nicalon, Nippon Carbon)
[24] used in this study are listed in Table 2. Table 3
geometrically compares the 125-µm quartz ﬁber and
X-shaped 14-µm SiC ﬁbers. The present SiC ﬁber is
a SiC ﬁber with a low oxygen content [24] whose
thermal conductivity is an order of magnitude smaller
than that of pure SiC.
In considering heat transfer outside the droplet,
heat-transfer characteristics in the suspending ﬁber
and in the ambient gas differ due to differences in
physical properties of the suspending ﬁber and the
ambient gas. As shown in Table 2, ρCp of the sus-
pending ﬁbers is three orders of magnitude greater
than that of air, so the temperature of the suspend-
ing ﬁbers rises more slowly than that of air. Since the
suspending-ﬁber temperature is less than the ambi-
ent gas temperature in droplet vaporization or ignition
experiments, and less than the ﬂame temperature in
combustion experiments, the suspending ﬁber absorbs
heat in these experiments. Table 3 shows the heat ca-
pacity ratio VfρfCpf/VgρairCpair of the suspending
ﬁber and air, where Vf denotes the suspending ﬁber
volume and Vg the gas volume between the droplet
and the ﬂame. Here, the ﬂame diameter D is used as
a reference scale and the maximum ﬂame diameter of
8 mm for 1-mm droplet combustion at atmosphere is
expressed as D [25]. The heat capacity ratio shows
that more than 50% of the heat needed to heat the gas
inside the ﬂame is required to heat the quartz ﬁber.
The amount of heat needed to heat the SiC ﬁber, how-
ever, is negligible.
Table 2 also shows that the thermal diffusivity of
the suspending ﬁber is an order of magnitude smaller
than that of air. The distance of thermal conduction at
elapsed time t is generally expressed as l ≈(at)1/2.
The combustion time of an initially 1-mm-diameter
droplet is about 1 s. The distance of thermal con-
duction along the SiC ﬁber after 1 s is about 2 mm
and thus is smaller than the maximum ﬂame radius
of about 4 mm. This suggests that the temperature of
the suspending ﬁber rises due to thermal conduction
from the gas rather than by thermal conduction along
the suspending ﬁber. The heat is transfered into the
droplet by the resulting temperature gradient along
the ﬁber axis.
Next, we discuss heat transfer inside the droplet.
As shown in Table 2, ρCp of the suspending ﬁbers is
of the same order of magnitude as that of n-decane.
