ever, is an order of magnitude greater than that of air.
This suggests that the suspending ﬁber temperature is
less than the liquid temperature near the droplet sur-
face, but near the droplet center, the suspending ﬁber
temperature is greater than the liquid temperature.
The amount of heat supplied to the droplet through
the suspending ﬁber is proportional to the tempera-
ture gradient and Sfλf, where Sf denotes the cross-
sectional area of the ﬁbers and λf the thermal con-
ductivity of the ﬁber. Table 3 shows Sfλf and Sdλair,
where Sd denotes the surface area of the droplet with
its diameter d = 1.0 mm and λair denotes the thermal
conductivity of air. Sfλf of the SiC ﬁber is found to
be about 4% of Sdλair. While the temperature gradi-
ent along the ﬁber is initially less than the gas-phase
temperature gradient at the droplet surface and these
gradients become nearly equal at a later stage, the
heat supplied to the droplet through the SiC ﬁber is
much smaller than the heat supplied to the droplet
from the gas phase. Sfλf of quartz ﬁber is about 30%
of Sdλair when d = 1.0 mm and is over 100% when
d = 0.50 mm. As noted above, the quartz ﬁber is
harder to heat than the SiC ﬁber. Therefore, the quartz
ﬁber serves as a heat sink at an early stage of combus-
tion, but serves as a heat source to the droplet at a later
stage of combustion. The heat-sink effect at the early
stage is seen in the numerical results on suspended-
droplet vaporization by Shih et al. [26]. Kumagai [27]
demonstrated in droplet-combustion experiments in
normal gravity that the vaporization rate of a sus-
pended droplet increased with the thermal conduc-
tivity of the suspending ﬁber. This result shows the
heat-source effect of the suspending ﬁber at a later
stage of combustion.
Last, we brieﬂy discuss suspending ﬁber effects on
radiative heat transfer. As shown later in Fig. 6, some
parts of the SiC ﬁbers showed yellow luminosity dur-
ing burning. To evaluate radiative heat from the ﬁber,
we assume that the SiC ﬁber in a ﬂame radiates with
the same temperature distribution along the ﬁber as
that in the gas. The temperature distribution around
the burning droplet was taken from Ref. [25]. We also
assume that the emissivity and absorptivity of SiC
ﬁber is unity. It is found that the radiative heat loss
rate from 14-µm X-shaped SiC ﬁbers is less than 1%
of the heat release rate of droplet combustion when
d = 1.0 mm. Since the emissivity of quartz ﬁber is
an order of magnitude smaller than that of SiC ﬁber,
the radiative heat loss from the 125-µm quartz ﬁber
is negligible but the ﬁber becomes a nonnegligible
heat sink as discussed above. In ﬂame spread along
a droplet array, unburnt droplets may be inﬂuenced
by the radiative heat from SiC ﬁbers around a burning
droplet. As will be explained in Section 3.4, however,
the radiative heat received by the next droplet is too
small to affect the ﬂame spread.
3. Experiments of ﬂame spread along fuel-droplet
arrays
We conducted microgravity experiments of ﬂame
spread along fuel-droplet arrays using the new-droplet
generation technique proposed in Section 2.2. The de-
tails of the experiments are described in this section.
3.1. Experimental apparatus and procedures
Fig. 4 depicts a simultaneous multiple-droplet
generator based on the new droplet generation tech-
nique. Two SiC ﬁbers with diameters of 14 µm were
stretched in an X shape normal to the direction of the
ﬂame spread, and the droplet was placed at the in-
tersection of the ﬁbers. Glass tubes of 1 mm outer
diameter were inserted into the fuel reservoir tank
connected to the fuel supply microsyringe. The tip
of each glass tube was narrowed to about 40 µm.
Multiple droplets of equal size were simultaneously
generated by the fuel supply through the glass tubes
onto the intersections of the X-shaped ﬁbers. There
are two movable parts in Fig. 4. Their positions were
controlled in traverse stages driven by stepping mo-
tors. First, each glass tube tip came in contact with the
corresponding intersection of the suspending ﬁbers
by moving Stage 1. Next, the plunger of the microsy-
ringe with fuel was pushed by Stage 2 to supply the
fuel. The initial droplet diameter was controlled by
the displacement of Stage 2. Stage 1 was then moved
back to the initial position to separate the glass tube
tips from the ﬁber intersections and to leave droplets
there. One of the end droplets, called the ﬁrst droplet,
was ignited by electrically heating a wire loop made
of an iron–chrome alloy. The droplet heating time by
the hot wire was about 0.3 s. The diameter of each
droplet generated was measured in advance for every
set of fuel supply glass tubes. In our experiment, we
only used sets of fuel supply glass tubes for which
