A schematic diagram of the experimental appa-
ratus is shown in Fig. 5. The apparatus consists of
the simultaneous multiple-droplet generator shown in
Fig. 4, a combustion chamber whose inside temper-
ature can be precisely controlled, a droplet-array el-
evator by which the droplet-array supporting frame
is transported into the combustion chamber in mi-
crogravity, and two video cameras. Flame spreading
inside the combustion chamber was photographed by
a digital video camera through the window at the top
of the combustion chamber. Droplet generation was
monitored using a mini CCD camera.
One advantage of the apparatus is that it takes only
a few seconds from the start of droplet generation out-
side the combustion chamber to the ignition of the
ﬁrst droplet of the array inside the chamber. When
a high-volatility fuel is used, the prevaporization of
fuel during the droplet array generation process af-
fects droplet array combustion [28]. This apparatus is
useful when using a high-volatility fuel whose pre-
vaporization is substantial. When the droplet gener-
ation and ignition processes were optimized without
transporting the droplet array into the chamber, the
time from the start of droplet generation to ignition
could be reduced to about 1 s. We plan to experi-
ment with a high-volatility fuel in future work. In this
study, similar to the experiment by Kato et al. [12],
n-decane, a less volatile fuel, was used, and we ob-
served ﬂame-spreading behavior of linear droplet ar-
rays in microgravity. We conducted these micrograv-
ity experiments at the Micro-gravity Laboratory of
Japan (MGLAB) and the drop-experiment facility of
Yamaguchi University.
Sections 3.2 and 3.3 report some of the results of
preliminary experiments showing the potential of our
