spread along a linear droplet array in microgravity (S =
6 mm, d0 = 0.86 mm, Ta = 298 K).
apparatus. Section 3.4 compares these results with ex-
isting ones.
3.2. Simultaneous multiple-droplet generation in
microgravity
Microgravity experiments on droplet array gen-
eration and ignition used the simultaneous droplet-
generation technique. Fig. 6 presents sequential pho-
tographs of the droplet generation at each intersection
of SiC ﬁbers in microgravity. The start of a micro-
gravity condition was set as t = 0 s. The fuel was
supplied onto the ﬁber intersections in microgravity.
The glass tubes were retracted from the intersections
after the fuel supply was delivered, and the droplets
were placed there. The glass tube retraction was com-
pleted at t = 3.40 s. The ﬁrst droplet was ignited by
a hot-wire igniter at t = 3.67 s. Fig. 6 proves that the
whole process of droplet-array generation can be done
in microgravity. In normal gravity, some droplets fall
off ﬁbers after the glass tube retraction depending on
the alignment of the suspending ﬁber intersection and
the glass-tube tip. Even for the same alignment, all the
droplets were placed on the SiC ﬁbers in micrograv-
ity. Therefore, it is much easier to generate the droplet
on the SiC ﬁber in microgravity than in normal grav-
ity. The present technique is applicable to space ex-
periments, in which all the experimental processes,
from the droplet generation to combustion, are con-
ducted in microgravity."
