3.3. Generation and transportation of large droplets
in microgravity
We also performed microgravity experiments on
droplet-array generation and transportation. The drop-
let diameter was set as 1.5 mm. When a relatively
large droplet is used, the droplet-array system can be
scaled up, so we can compensate for a relatively low
temporal resolution of the video system. In normal
gravity, it was quite difﬁcult to suspend such large
droplets from the intersection of the SiC ﬁbers. Fig. 7
shows droplet-array generation and transportation for
a case with 1.5-mm droplets. The experimental se-
quence was as follows. In normal gravity, the fuel was
supplied through the glass tubes to the intersections of
the SiC ﬁbers. In microgravity, the glass tubes were
then retracted from the intersections, and six 1.5-mm
droplets were placed at the SiC ﬁber intersections and
were transported into the combustion chamber. The
1.5-mm droplets did not ﬂy off the ﬁbers at either
the start or at the end of the transportation into the
chamber. We conﬁrmed that such droplets could be
transported even when the gas temperature inside the
combustion chamber was 600 K. Theoretical analy-
ses [29,30] and a numerical calculation [31] have
been conducted on the ﬂame spread of droplet ar-
rays at high temperatures. The present experimental
technique enables ﬂame-spread experiments at high
temperatures. Results from high-temperature experi-
ments will be shown in the next report.
3.4. Flame-spread rate and ﬂame-spread limit of a
fuel-droplet array in microgravity
This section compares ﬂame-spread rates and the
ﬂame-spread limit in microgravity with existing ex-
perimental results [12].
Fig. 8 illustrates the ﬂame-spread behavior of an
n-decane droplet array for the droplet spacing S =
8 mm and the initial droplet diameter d0 = 1.0 mm
at room temperature. Each photograph displays the
ﬂames just after they spread around each droplet. For
the ﬂame spread to the third and subsequent droplets,
the leading ﬂame was separated from the previous
ﬂame. Fig. 9 depicts the temporal variation of ﬂame-
edge positions. The spreading stopped and started
repeatedly. Three data points, the positions of the
leading edge and posterior edge of the leading ﬂame
and the leading edge of the previous group ﬂame,
are plotted against the same time when the leading
ﬂame was separated from the previous group ﬂame,
e.g., t = 0.27, 0.57, 0.87 s, etc. The two ﬂames then
merged into one group ﬂame. Fig. 10 shows the burn-
ing behavior at t = 0.27 s. This image is one video
frame before the frame at t = 0.30 s shown in Fig. 8.
A luminescent half-circle shape was observed in the
left half part around the second droplet, as can be seen
in Fig. 10. This suggests that a premixed ﬂame propa-
gation occurred in the ﬂammable mixture around the
second droplet.
As can be seen in Fig. 9, the instantaneous ve-
locity of the ﬂame’s leading edge varied with time.
When the premixed ﬂame propagation occurred as
suggested in Fig. 10, the instantaneous velocity of the
ﬂame’s leading edge should have been much greater.
