Kato et al. [12] demonstrated the dependence of
the ﬂame-spread rate of n-decane and n-hexadecane
droplet arrays in normal gravity on the droplet spac-
ing, using ﬂame diameter as a characteristic length
scale instead of the initial droplet diameter d0. They
reported that the ﬂame-spread rate had a maximum
value when the ﬂame radius corresponded to the
droplet spacing. However, microgravity experiments
by Kato et al. [12] did not conﬁrm this. In general,
the ﬂame radius perpendicular to the gravity direction
does not vary with time very much in normal gravity,
but in microgravity, the ﬂame radius ﬁrst increases
with time, attains a maximum and then decreases. For
S/d0 = 3.5, for which the ﬂame-spread rate had a
