Last, we brieﬂy discuss the effect on the ﬂame
spread of radiant heat from the SiC ﬁbers in hot
gas. As seen in Fig. 6, some parts of the SiC ﬁbers
were heated during the ﬂame spread. Unburnt droplets
might have been inﬂuenced by their radiative heat. If
the effect of the radiative heat is not negligible, the
ﬂame-spread rate may increase and the ﬂame-spread
limit may became wider than in the case without ra-
diation. To evaluate this effect, we will assume that
the SiC ﬁber in a ﬂame radiates with the same tem-
perature distribution along the ﬁber as that in the gas,
and that only SiC ﬁbers, which have a greater radi-
ation absorption coefﬁcient, absorb the radiative heat
reaching around the next droplet, and SiC ﬁbers inside
the next droplet heat up the next droplet. As a refer-
ence, we consider the droplet heating by which the
gas-phase equivalence ratio at the droplet surface at-
tains unity, e.g., the droplet heating from 300 to 330 K
for n-decane. Through this estimation, we ﬁnd that
the radiative heat received by the next droplet is four
orders of magnitude smaller than the heat required for
the temperature rise of the next droplet. This radia-
tive heat is almost independent of the droplet spacing.
Therefore, the effect of radiation from the SiC ﬁber
on ﬂame spread of the droplet array is negligible.
4. Conclusions
We proposed a new droplet-generation technique
for droplet vaporization and combustion experiments
in which as many disadvantages of existing droplet-
generation techniques were overcome as possible.
The droplet can be generated with a high success
rate on X-shaped 14-µm SiC ﬁbers by fuel sup-
ply through a ﬁne glass tube. We developed a si-
multaneous multiple-droplet generator based on the
new droplet-generation technique. We demonstrated
short-time generation of multiple droplets in micro-
gravity and generation of a large-droplet array and
its transportation into a high-temperature combustion
chamber. Our technique is applicable to experiments
for various purposes such as vaporization, ignition,
and combustion of multiple droplets. The technique
is also effective in droplet-array combustion experi-
ments using a high-volatility fuel, whose prevaporiza-
tion is substantial.
We performed ﬂame-spread experiments for n-
decane arrays at room temperature and atmospheric
pressure in microgravity. They reconﬁrmed that the
ﬂame-spread rate had a maximum value at a spe-
ciﬁc droplet spacing and that there exists a ﬂame-
spread limit in terms of the droplet spacing. How-
ever, a greater ﬂame-spread rate and a greater ﬂame-
spread limit were obtained with our method, using"
