1. Introduction
For
over
50 years,
scientists
have
been
studying
droplet
combustion in microgravity, beginning with the modest one-
second drop-tower experiments by Kumagai [1] in Japan in 1957.
For heptane droplets, his measurements veriﬁed attainment of
quasi-steady combustion, in which the square of the droplet
diameter decreases linearly with time, in accord with theory. More
recently, with the availability of long-duration space-based micro-
gravity platforms, researchers were able to study larger droplets
with longer burning histories [2–4]. In these and other experi-
ments, combustion of n-heptane has been extensively investigated,
recently in part because of its consideration as a component of sur-
rogate fuels for diesel and gasoline engines [5]. The conventional
wisdom thus far has been that small heptane droplets, once ig-
nited, burn to completion, extinguish in a ﬂash as a consequence
of impurity or product accumulation, or extinguish diffusively [6]
at a ﬁnite droplet diameter, whereas larger droplets extinguish
radiatively as a result of excessive radiant heat loss from the ﬂame
zone [7]. Diffusive extinction of small droplets occurs as a conse-
quence of the diffusion time, which decreases with decreasing
droplet diameter, becoming less than the chemical time required
to complete the heat release in the gas phase. On the other hand,
radiative extinction occurs for large droplets because, as the
droplet diameter is increased, the rate of radiant loss, which is
proportional to the ﬂame volume, increases with respect to the
rate of heat release, which is proportional to the ﬂame diameter.
Following either one of these last two modes of extinction, the
droplet simply evaporates in the hot environment, without any
chemical heat release in the gas phase. Theoretical models
and numerical simulations [8], with detailed chemical kinetics
geared towards high-temperature chemistry, have supported this
understanding.
In this article, we report, for the ﬁrst time, experimental obser-
vations in which large n-heptane droplets exhibit dual modes of
combustion and extinction. First the droplet undergoes normal
burning with a visible ﬂame surrounding the droplet and extin-
guishes radiatively at a relatively large droplet size. This event is
followed by continued rapid quasi-steady vaporization of the drop-
let without any visible ﬂame detected, which ends abruptly, at a
point called ‘‘second-stage extinction’’ here, leaving behind a smal-
ler droplet, which then either experiences normal time-dependent
evaporation in the hot surrounding environment or sometimes
apparently grows slightly through condensation of the vapor in
the cloud that forms upon extinction, or through migration of the
vapor-cloud particles to the droplet surface.
Two-stage burning has been reported and studied previously
for bi-component fuel droplets (e.g., [4]), explained qualitatively
by the lighter component supporting the initial ﬂame, with the sec-
ond period involving combustion of the heavier component, and
both stages involve traditional hot ﬂames. Dual-mode combustion
has not previously been discussed for pure-fuel droplets, nor have
