doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2012.07.012,doi,Can cool flames support quasi-steady alkane droplet burning?,2,1,"cool ﬂames been mentioned in that context. Historically, cool
ﬂames usually occur in premixed systems, where they are known
to produce transient ﬂame propagation and eventually lead to igni-
tion that establishes hot-ﬂame combustion, generating ignition
pulses commonly associated with engine-knock phenomena. Here
we present, for the ﬁrst time, observations for which the closest
explanation that we have been able to ﬁnd is that they result from
cool ﬂames that support quasi-steady burning of liquid fuel drop-
lets, in a non-premixed environment in which cool ﬂames are not
currently expected to arise. We are unable to provide deﬁnitive
proof of this interpretation which, if correct, may necessitate
augmentation of currently known cool-ﬂame chemistry. Since,
however, we ﬁrst observed this new phenomenon more than
2 years ago and have been trying without complete success to
understand it ever since, we feel that it is important to bring our
observations and our attempts at analysis to the attention of the
community in the hope that others may contribute insights as well.
It may be worth reporting that, before accepting a possible rela-
tionship to cool-ﬂame chemistry, the authors attempted to develop
other explanations, such as continued vaporization after ﬂame
extinction (which could not be made to agree quantitatively with
the observed extended d-square law) and diffusion-controlled sur-
face catalytic oxidation (which required unreasonably large oxygen
diffusion coefﬁcients to match the measured droplet-consumption
rates). The suggestions introduced below therefore were preceded
by other unsuccessful lines of investigation.
2. Experiments
The experiments were conducted on board the International
Space Station (ISS) using the Multi-User Droplet Combustion
Apparatus (MDCA) installed in the Combustion Integrated Rack
(CIR) facility as a part of the Flame Extinguishment Experiments
(FLEXs). The cylindrical combustion chamber in the CIR has a
free-volume of approximately 95 l. The MDCA uses an opposed-
needle deployment technique [3] to freely deploy droplets of cho-
sen initial size in microgravity. In addition, for some of the FLEX
tests the droplets were tethered by a ﬁne silicon carbide ﬁlament
to ﬁx their position. Ignition of the free-ﬂoated or tethered droplet
was accomplished using two symmetrically positioned hot-wire
igniters. The experimental diagnostic system consisted of a
black-and-white, back-lit droplet image-capture camera, a UV-sen-
sitive ﬂame-imaging camera ﬁltered to observe OH-chemilumi-
nescence at a 310 nm wavelength, and a CCD color camera with
a wider view angle. All the camera images, captured at 30 frames
per second, were digitally stored on-board the CIR and down-
linked at a later time for analysis. The color camera also had
real-time down-link capability and was used to conduct experi-
ment operations from the ground at the Telescience Support
Center located in NASA Glenn Research Center. Prior to an experi-
mental run, the combustion chamber is ﬁlled with the desired
ambient gas mixture, consisting of oxygen, nitrogen, and some-
times carbon dioxide, at a selected pressure. Typically, several
droplet burns were performed in each environment before venting
the combustion chamber to space vacuum and reﬁlling. Further de-
tails of experiments and operational procedures can be found in
Dietrich et al. [9]. Over one hundred droplet burns were carried
out using n-heptane fuel in varying ambient gas mixtures, with ini-
tial droplet diameters in the range of 2–4 mm, primarily at 0.7 and
1 atmosphere pressures, when this article was prepared. Here we
report only the results for large droplets for which the dual mode
was observed.
From the digital images, the droplet diameter d and the ﬂame
diameter df were obtained as functions of time using an image-anal-
ysis software [10]. Figure 1 shows the square of the droplet diameter
and the ﬂame diameter, plotted as functions of time, for three typi-
cal free-ﬂoated cases, corresponding to ambient gas mixtures of 21%
oxygen 79% nitrogen (approximating normal air), 18% oxygen 82%
nitrogen, and 18% oxygen 67% nitrogen 15% carbon dioxide, all by
volume, at one atmosphere pressure. Because of the increasing dilu-
tion, the initial droplet size is decreased sequentially for these three
cases, to assure adequate hot-wire ignition and radiant extinction of
the hot ﬂame. The gap in the middle diameter-square trace corre-
sponds to a period during which the droplet was not in the view
of the back-lit image-capturing camera. Two different measure-
ments of ﬂame diameters were obtained, one from the UV-camera
and the other from the color camera, but for clarity only the former
is shown, the latter diameter of peak visible intensity being soot-re-
lated and about three-quarters of the diameter shown. Immediately
following ignition at time t = 0, the square of the droplet diameter
decreases almost linearly with time; meanwhile the ﬂame diameter
increases in size. At around 6–10 s into burning, the ﬂame-diameter
data abruptly end, indicating cessation of the detectable visible-
ﬂame and OH⁄ emissions, marked by a vertical line in Fig. 1. Never-
theless, the square of the droplet diameter continues to decrease lin-
early until around 35, 26, or 13 s when the slope abruptly changes to
a lower value indicating a reduced rate of vaporization. This point is
indicated by another vertical bar in Fig. 1, and at that point a vapor
cloud invariably is seen by the visible camera to begin to form and
grow. Similar results were found for a range of ambient gas mixtures
and at three different pressures during these experiments. Insets in
Fig. 1, from data for the middle record, show a representative UV-
camera ﬂame image taken during the hot-ﬂame stage (top), a backlit
droplet image taken during the second stage (middle), and a vapor-
cloud image taken by the visible-light camera subsequent to the
second extinction (bottom). The slight increase in the dro"
doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2012.07.012,doi,Can cool flames support quasi-steady alkane droplet burning?,3,2,"tween radiative and second-stage extinction, from the plots in
Fig. 1. As may be inferred from Fig. 1, the former exhibits greater
variation with time than the latter, consistent with radiative
extinction preventing full attainment of quasi-steady burning.
The value of K during the second stage remains quite constant, typ-
ically at a value slightly less than that at the end of the ﬁrst stage,
indicating that the quasi-steady burning rate is smaller for the sec-
ond stage. A method for calculating the quasi-steady burning-rate
constant from droplet-combustion theory, employing thermody-
namic and transport properties of the system, has been developed
and tested against the heptane measurements of Kumagai and
others ([11,12], p. 66). The result of using this method for the
present experiments is summarized in Fig. 2, which compares
the measured values, on the vertical scale, to the predicted values,
on the horizontal scale, predicted under the assumption of com-
bustion to hot-ﬂame stable products, principally carbon dioxide
and water vapor (which may not be true for the second stage,
but it is made in the absence of knowledge of what the products
may be). It may be seen from this ﬁgure that, while the predictions
agree well with the hot-ﬂame experimental results in the literature
[1,13], they over-predict the second stage experimental results
substantially. On the other hand, if it is assumed that only 50% of
the hot-ﬂame heat release occurs in the second-stage, then the
predictions fall along the line representing agreement. This
suggests that in the second stage only about half as much heat is
released as in the hot ﬂame.
Given a ﬁxed temperature range, the heat release affects the
predicted burning-rate constant primarily through a factor ln
(1 + B), where B denotes the transfer number, approximately the
ambient oxidizer mass fraction times the ratio of the heat release
per unit mass of oxidizer consumed to the heat of vaporization
per unit mass of fuel. Values of B, which are relevant to all mass-
transfer problems, can be calculated from the measured burning-
rate constant according to the formula K = (8/ql)(k/cp)ln(1 + B),
where k and cp denote the thermal conductivity and the speciﬁc
heat at constant pressure, respectively, for the gas, and ql is the
density of the liquid. Values for the second stage, obtained from
this formula, employing the experimental values of K and the tem-
perature-dependent prescription ([12], p. 66) for calculating gas
properties, range from about B = 3 to B = 5, generally decreasing
with increasing dilution of the atmosphere, as they should; results
are summarized in the appendix. Associated adiabatic ﬂame
temperatures, Tf, (needed for property evaluations), also can be
calculated from the 50% heat release, and they, too, of course tend
to decrease with increasing dilution. They range from about 1150 K
to 1430 K. Since the ﬁnite-rate chemistry causes the actual ﬂame
temperatures to be less than the adiabatic values by amounts on
the order of 100 K, the true temperatures would appear to be
roughly in the range of 1000–1400 K, but, especially in view of
the unknown stoichiometry, there are uncertainties in these esti-
mates. Exploratory computations have suggested that ﬂame tem-
peratures are unlikely to be much higher but may be somewhat
lower.
It is of interest to compare these ﬂame temperatures with the
temperature ranges over which cool-ﬂame phenomena have been
observed for heptane. As indicate in our later discussion cool-ﬂame
chemistry involves two sequential O2 additions to the heptyl
radicals and to products formed therefrom, accompanied by
isomerizations, resulting in a carbonyl compound often termed
ketohydroperoxide. Cool ﬂames are associated with the negative-
temperature-coefﬁcient (NTC) range of autoignition, typically
observed between 600 K and 900 K, involving the chain branching
from the carbonyl compounds that are responsible for the low-
temperature ignitions. These O2 additions actually occur at all tem-
peratures, but above a maximum temperature, the higher-temper-
ature end of the NTC range, they result in only short-lived,
extremely low-concentration molecules that are unimportant in
all combustion processes, including ignition. This maximum
temperature varies with experimental conditions, such as pressure,
and surely is different for different combustion processes. The con-
sequent uncertainties are large enough that we cannot be sure
whether the temperature ranges of cool ﬂames and of the present
experiments coincide; they may or may not, but, in any event, the
temperatures almost certainly are not as high as those of hot
ﬂames.
3.2. Kinetic parameters
The calculated adiabatic ﬂame temperatures Tf and measured
second-stage extinction diameters dce may be employed to esti-
mate overall chemical reaction-rate parameters for the second
stage. That can be done for a one-step overall Arrhenius reaction
on the basis of the classical analysis of diffusion-ﬂame structures
and extinction by activation-energy asymptotics [14], originally
derived in the context of counterﬂow ﬂames, but expressible in
general conﬁgurations ([12], pp. 76–80), and applied to droplet
combustion (in the diffusion-ﬂame regime) by Law [6,15]. When
the overall reaction is of ﬁrst order with respect to both fuel and
oxidizer, there is a general formula ([14,12], p. 80) for a reduced
Damköler number de for extinction in terms of c, the ratio of the
differences between the heat ﬂux to the fuel and air sides of the
thin ﬂame to their sum, and for droplet burning it can be shown
that c = [B(1  m) + a(1 + m)  2m]/[(B + a)(1 + m)], where m denotes
the ratio of the ambient oxidizer mass fraction to the stoichiome-
tric oxidizer-to-fuel mass ratio, and a is the difference between the
thermal enthalpy per unit mass of the gas at the droplet surface
temperature and in the ambient atmosphere, cp(Ts  T1), divided
by the heat of vaporization per unit mass of the fuel. In terms "
doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2012.07.012,doi,Can cool flames support quasi-steady alkane droplet burning?,4,3,"ambient gas, and WO represents the molecular weight of the oxi-
dizer (the oxygen molecule). The left-hand side of this equation is
calculated from the measured droplet extinction diameters and
the previously computed gas and energetic properties, and it is plot-
ted against the calculated (1/Tf), as shown in Fig. 3. From the slope
of the linear least-square ﬁt of this data, the overall activation tem-
perature Ta is obtained, and, since that value and T1 are both con-
stant for all tests, they can be employed in the ﬁrst term on the
right-hand side to calculate the prefactor A. In this way the overall
activation temperature is found to be 11,000 K, and the frequency
factor A = 1011 cm3/mol s.
These reaction-rate parameters pertain to a one-step overall
approximation to the second-stage chemistry. For heptane autoig-
nition there is a successful empirical four-step approximation
[16] (with Arrhenius rate parameters) that provides good agree-
ment with heptane autoignition measurements and that has been
used to model cool-ﬂame combustion [17]. Two of the steps pertain
to hot-ﬂame chemistry and two to the cool ﬂame. These last two de-
scribe formation of an intermediate species from the fuel molecule
and two oxygen molecules with a high activation energy and
prefactor and small heat release, followed by the intermediate com-
bining with nine oxygen molecules to form equilibrium products in
a step with a lower activation energy and prefactor and large heat
release. Although a diffusion-ﬂame extinction analysis has not been
performed with this two-step description, previous experience
with similar problems suggests that it would lead to results similar
to those of a one-step analysis having rate parameters close to those
of this last step, expected to be controlling because its lower activa-
tion energy produces a broader reaction zone with the preceding
step embedded within it. That last intermediate-consumption step
in fact has an activation temperature of 13,230 K, not far from the
11,000 K found here, with a prefactor 500 times the present value
of A. While not close, the values are much closer than for any other
overall step, and deﬁnitive comparisons must await results of diffu-
sion-ﬂame extinction analyses by activation-energy asymptotics
with the two-step empirical description. The two-step chemistry,
however, ultimately results in the same heat release as the hot
chemistry, twice that estimated here, and so close agreement
should not be expected, although the analysis could readily be
performed with the reduced heat release.
An important outstanding question concerns the overall num-
ber of oxygen molecules consumed per fuel molecule if the second
stage is a cool mode. While the analysis was performed here on the
basis of the assumption that it is the same as in the hot ﬂame, the
resulting value of the activation temperature is only weakly depen-
dent on that assumption, mainly because the stoichiometric fuel-
oxidizer mass ratio is so small, resulting in very small values of
m. If the reaction were such that the fuel consumed fewer oxygen
molecules, for example only two, then the resulting Ta would not
be strongly affected, but the calculated value of A could increase
by as much as a factor of 100 (and the reaction would occur closer
to the fuel surface). The vapor-cloud development strongly sug-
gests less overall consumption of oxygen because it is very difﬁcult
to envision so much condensible product formation with so much
oxygen present in the products. The actual overall stoichiometry of
the second-stage combustion observed here thus remains an extre-
mely relevant outstanding question.
If cool-ﬂame chemistry is operative, then static stability consid-
erations indicate that the ﬂame would not be stable in the region
where the reaction rate decreases with increasing temperature
and instead would have to sit at the position of the maximum reac-
tion rate in the NTC curve. In that case, through an energy balance
the chemical kinetics would essentially determine the ﬂame tem-
perature, which would likely be slightly less than 700 K, the prod-
ucts would not contain any H2O, CO2, or even CO but instead would
involve heptylketoperoxide, formaldehyde, and some alkenes, and
our preceding analysis should be correspondingly revised.
4. Discussion
The results reported here strongly suggest that there are two
stable modes of quasi-steady, spherically symmetrical n-heptane
droplet burning. In one mode the continuing classical evaporation
of the droplet is sustained by the well-known exothermic chemis-
try of the hot ﬂame, as it oxidizes the fuel vapor in a quiescent
atmosphere such as air. This mode involves a bright visible ﬂame
that includes continuum radiation from soot production, the extent
of which decreases with decreasing droplet diameter during burn-
ing [13], the combustion eventually ending (unless disrupted by
soot accumulation) with ﬂame extinction at very small droplet
diameters. In the other mode, the combustion chemistry releases
roughly half as much heat, so that the principal combustion
products can no longer be carbon dioxide and water vapor, and
the quasi-steady evaporation rate is lower; moreover, the ﬂame
temperature is considerably lower, and the chemistry is slower,
so that extinction occurs at a larger droplet diameter, more readily
amenable to measurement. Overall chemical reaction-rate param-
eters for the combustion, calculated from the measured extinction
diameters by the preceding prescription, are closer to previously
derived empirical reaction-rate parameters for cool-ﬂame chemis-
try than to any other known rate parameters, suggesting possible
similarities between cool-ﬂame chemistry and the combustion
chemistry that supports this slower quasi-steady burning mode.
This cool mode, thus far seen only after extinction of a hot ﬂame
around a droplet that is too large to continue to support it, has
never been produced by ignition of a smaller droplet, one that"
doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2012.07.012,doi,Can cool flames support quasi-steady alkane droplet burning?,5,4,"alkyl radical that arises from the initial hydrogen-atom extraction
from the fuel by an oxygen molecule, the two additions being
interspersed with isomerizations, producing alkylpreoxy and alkyl
hdroperoxy radicals, eventually isomerizing to a carbonyl com-
pound, from which branching leads to low-temperature autoigni-
tion [18–20]. There seems to be no indication in this chemistry
that the formation of a stable condensible product may develop.
Barring diffusive escape, the carbonyl may lead to autoignition,
which should produce a transition to a hot ﬂame, so there is moti-
vation to focus attention on pre-carbonyl reactions.
If the vapor cloud is not heptane, would it be possible, after the
ﬁrst or second cool-ﬂame oxygen addition, for two of the radicals to
combine, in this case forming a stable condensible compound con-
taining fourteen carbon atoms? A step towards answering such
questions
would
be
to
collect
and
chemically
analyze
the
condensed vapor (as well as the extinguished droplet), but that,
unfortunately, is not possible in the current ISS experiment conﬁg-
uration. Nor can the ISS conditions yet be reproduced on Earth,
since access to the necessary ten-second time periods in quiescent
atmospheres is prevented by buoyancy. Another outstanding prob-
lem concerns the chemical mechanism by which the extinguishing
hot ﬂame establishes the cool mode of combustion; this must be
quite complicated. Much more research is needed.
One helpful thing would be to compare the experimental results
with results of numerical droplet-combustion computations that
include known cool-ﬂame chemistry in the conservation equations
which are integrated numerically. This has been done for micro-
gravity experiments involving the time-dependent autoignition
of alkane droplets in heated oxidizing atmospheres, demonstrating
the necessity of including the cool-ﬂame chemistry if agreement
with the ignition experiment is to be obtained [21], and corre-
sponding computations for conditions under which the dual mode
is seen do indeed indicate a transition to cool-ﬂame control,
although there are some noticeable differences from experiment
[22,23]. Although simpliﬁcations in the chemistry can be made,
ultimately in adding only four steps to the hot-ﬂame chemistry
[24], this degree of reduction has not yet been completed for al-
kanes larger than propane, the available heptane systematically re-
duced chemistry involving a larger number of steps [25]. A
potential problem is that none of these chemical descriptions seem
to have in them signiﬁcant production of condensible product spe-
cies. Computations predicting post-extinction condensation are
difﬁcult. It may be necessary to augment the chemical-kinetic
mechanisms by including condensible product species not previ-
ously considered, if the vapor cloud were to turn out to be n-hep-
tane. A number of additional chemical-kinetic and computational
investigations thus appear to be warranted.
5. Implications of the results
There are various possible long-term implications of these re-
sults. One aspect concerns the production of fuels that reduce
emissions in applications such as HCCI (homogeneous charge com-
pression ignition) engines. A number of studies [26,27] have inves-
tigated the use of cool-ﬂame chemistry to generate from diesel
fuels modiﬁed fuels which become more environment-friendly
by being amenable to production of homogeneous fuel–air mix-
tures. Basically they eliminate the need to burn diesel–fuel sprays,
which are notorious for pollutant production. These conversion
processes rely on partial combustion in temperature-controlled,
macroscopic reactors, but considerations of possibilities of use of
cool-mode combustion of individual droplets may generate en-
tirely different design concepts. Another area of importance lies
in questions about ﬁre safety in space vehicles. Since the cool mode
of droplet combustion persists after hot-ﬂame extinction, safety
procedures based only on considerations of hot ﬂames may be
inadequate for assuring safety under all conditions. To neglect
the possibility of continued combustion in the cool mode would
be somewhat analogous to neglecting smoldering as a possible ﬁre
hazard for cellulosic materials. Just as there may be a transition
from smoldering to ﬂaming, so might transitions from the cool
mode to the hot mode occur under suitable conditions.
The well-known differences in cool-ﬂame behavior of different
fuels suggest possible corresponding differences in the existence
and characteristics of the cool mode of droplet burning for different
fuels. For example, normal alkanes are known to exhibit more vir-
ulent cool ﬂames than branched alkanes, for reasons that are
becoming understood, and cool ﬂames are more prevalent in the
larger normal alkanes, only hints of their possible existence for eth-
ane being available, for example [24]. One recent ISS test with n-oc-
tane has, in fact, exhibited an apparent cool mode that is more
pronounced than what was seen with n-heptane, as have even more
recent tests with n-decane. It would be very interesting to preform
corresponding experiments with higher normal and branched al-
kanes, such as iso-octane, for example, which should have less dra-
matic cool-mode behavior than n-octane, if what has been seen
here is indeed related to cool ﬂames. Extensive similar testing of a
greater variety of fuels thus could help to sort out what is really
happening. There is much more work to do before the question
posed in the title of this article can be answered deﬁnitively.
Finally, we would like to emphasize the scientiﬁc importance of
performing carefully controlled fundamental experiments in facil-
ities such as those afforded by the International Space Station. The
phenomenon reported here was not anticipated when the experi-
ments were designed, and the test matrix was established without
its knowledge or consideration. Its emergence was a complete sur-
prise. This underscores the b"
