doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2013.09.011,doi,Isolated n-heptane droplet combustion in microgravity: “Cool Flames” – Two-stage combustion,2,1,"ible ﬂame, eventually resulting in diffusive extinction at a ﬁnite
drop size. Thereafter, additional time-dependent evaporation into
the surrounding environment was observed, in many cases yield-
ing a surrounding aerosol cloud [6]. The dual mode of combustion
prior to extinction was only observed for sufﬁciently large initial
droplet diameters; at smaller droplet diameters, classical single
stage burning phenomena were observed.
Previously, dual high temperature ﬂaming combustion was evi-
denced for droplets composed of two components of widely differ-
ent saturation temperatures (e.g. heptane/hexadecane, methanol/
dodecanol) [7,8]. In these studies, the two stage burning rate char-
acter was attributed to liquid phase ‘‘batch-like’’ distillation with
the initial lighter component vaporization supporting the initial
ﬁrst stage ﬂame, and the heavier component supporting the second
stage ﬂame. Prior to the present FLEX experiments, dual-mode
combustion of a single component liquid fuel droplet had not been
noted. The connection of a low temperature burning – to our
knowledge, the possibility of a low temperature diffusive burning
conﬁguration for isolated droplets was never been proposed or
hypothesized prior to the FLEX experiments.
In reporting these recent results emerging from the FLEX exper-
iments, Nayagam et al. [6] performed an initial analysis of the ob-
served dual-stage combustion phenomena. Measured droplet
burning rates and extinction diameters were utilized to extract
effective heat release rate, overall activation energy and pre-expo-
nential factors for the process, and the observations were speciﬁ-
cally related to low-temperature, oxidative reaction chemistry
typical of ‘‘Cool Flames’’. The analysis concluded that the tempera-
ture during the second stage burn ranged in between 1000 and
1400 K assuming a 50% heat release rate. The authors hypothesized
that if the second stage combustion was indeed a ‘‘Cool Flame’’,
then the cool ﬂame chemistry taking place would have to sit at
the position of the maximum reaction rate in the negative temper-
ature coefﬁcient (NTC) curve, resulting in a ‘‘ﬂame temperature’’ of
700 K with heptylketoperoxide, formaldehyde and alkenes being
the major reaction products instead of H2O, CO2 and CO.
