doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2013.09.011,doi,Isolated n-heptane droplet combustion in microgravity: “Cool Flames” – Two-stage combustion,3,2,"Since the accidental discovery by Sir Humphery Davy in 1810
and the coining of ‘‘Cold Flames’’ by Harry Julius Emeléus in 1928,
‘‘premixed cool ﬂames’’ have intrigued researchers. Classically, ‘‘pre-
mixed cool ﬂames’’ are transient propagating waves that proceed
through a premixed fuel/air mixture with one or more occurrences
leading to hot ignition of the remaining reactants. Quasi-steady,
cool ﬂame phenomena have also been produced in ﬂowing pre-
mixed reactants, typically followed by a second stage hot ﬂame.
Bradley and coworkers [9] studied cool ﬂame and second-stage
ignition phenomena in a quasi-steady conﬁguration by using a ver-
tical ﬂow reactor arrangement during the oxidation of acetalde-
hyde and propionaldehyde. They obtained a ‘‘Cool Flame’’ (pale
blue, 425 C) and a second-stage ﬂame (deeper blue, 690 C)
by increasing the oxygen concentration. They further conducted
mass spectrometry to determine the kinetic processes responsible
for the ﬂames.
Fairlie et al. [10,11] studied transient ‘‘Cool Flames’’ and oscilla-
tory ﬂames for a premixed propane-oxygen static reactor experi-
ment under microgravity conditions. Their study involved both
experimental and numerical aspects. The onset, spatial growth
and stability of the ‘‘Cool Flame’’ was attributed to the interactions
among the elementary reactions and rates governing negative tem-
perature coefﬁcient (NTC) behavior and the temperature ﬁeld con-
trolled by thermal diffusion to the reactor wall. Foster and
Pearlman [12] and Pearlman [13] conducted additional numerical
studies on the ‘‘Cool Flame’’ conﬁguration and formation at terres-
trial, partial and near-zero gravity conditions. They simulated the
same unstirred reactor system as that of Fairlie and co-workers.
In their pioneering work Tanabe and co-workers [14] studied the
effects of natural convection on the two stage ignition of n-dode-
cane droplet. They employed ﬁne thermocouple and interferome-
ter to detect the presence of invisible cool ﬂame. Moriue et al.
[15] utilized similar tethered droplet conﬁguration to experimen-
tally measure the ignition delays of ‘‘Cool Flame’’ and hot ﬂame
appearance for single n-decane, n-dodecane, n-tetradecane and
n-hexadecane droplets in a multiphase system. The ﬂames were
produced by inserting the droplet in a temperature controlled fur-
nace and observing the ﬂames by Michelson interferometry. Simi-
lar experiments were also investigated numerically by Schnaubelt
et al. [16].
It is well known [17] that for premixed n-heptane–air, low and
intermediate temperature chemistry important to autoignition in-
volves the reaction of n-heptyl radicals, R, with molecular oxygen,
O2 (see Fig. 1). The addition/isomerization/further addition of O2
and the thermodynamic reversibility of RO2 and QOOH formation
are responsible for degenerate branching phenomena and the
continuing reactions produce hydrogen peroxide and heat, eventu-
ally leading to ‘‘hot ignition’’. The ‘‘hot ignition’’ resulting from the
rapid decomposition of hydrogen peroxide [18] denotes the begin-
ning of the intermediate temperature kinetic regime, where reac-
tions auto-thermally accelerate the kinetic rates to conditions
eventually dominated by chemical chain branching (high temper-
ature kinetic regime) [19]. Recent detailed analyses of the autoig-
nition phenomena have appeared [20,21] with the latter paper
considering multi-stage ignition in non-premixed counter ﬂow
conﬁguration, each essentially identifying the same controlling ki-
netic phenomena and the importance of auto-thermal reaction
acceleration upon the occurrence of hot ignition.
Though the phenomena observed in the FLEX experiments is
likely associated with low temperature chemistry that the associa-
tion is the same as classical ‘‘premixed cool ﬂame’’ behavior seems
incongruous since the FLEX experiments diffusive phenomena.
The low temperature oxidation behaviors that lead to cool ﬂame
observations require (at least partial) premixedness at relatively
low temperature conditions. In fact, combustion predictions for
the burning behavior of diffusion ﬂames of hydrocarbon fuels as
well as isolated fuel droplets in microgravity have frequently been
conducted with detailed chemical kinetic models devoid of low
temperature degenerate branching reactions [3]. The seeming di-
lemma presented by the FLEX observations stimulated the present
numerical studies to further elucidate the inﬂuence of the coupled
physico-chemical kinetic processes that result in the dual-mode
burning behavior.
Simulations were conducted using a recently developed, one-
dimensional,
sphero-symmetric,
transient
combustion
model
[22], and the predictions are compared against the recent ISS n-
heptane droplet experiments [1]. Predictions compare favorably
with the experimentally measured droplet and ﬂame diameter
evolution. Special attention was given to delineating similarities
and differences for classical NTC, ‘‘Cool Flame’’ kinetic behavior ob-
served in premixed conditions or in quasi steady non-premixed
conditions such as counter ﬂow conﬁgurations. Using the model,
special attention is given to elucidating the effects of different
atmospheric conditions (CO2, He) and initial droplet sizes on the
predicted behavior. Finally, the prospects that similar behavior
should be observed with other large carbon number n-alkane drop-
lets are discussed.
