In the model, the volume boundaries are deﬁned to coincide
with the liquid–gas interface, and the far ﬁeld (two hundred times
the initial droplet diameter) boundary is well deﬁned using Dirich-
let conditions and remains ﬁxed in the simulations. The Dirichlet
conditions imposed on the far-ﬁeld are of ﬁxed ambient composi-
tion and temperature (298 K). The inner most liquid node is cen-
tered at the origin, providing the required no-ﬂux condition.
3. Results and discussions
In order to observe the effects of low and intermediate temper-
ature kinetics, a full detailed n-heptane chemical kinetics is used
[24,25] in the present work. The detailed n-heptane chemical
kinetics model consists of 1038 species and corresponding 2739
reaction steps. To assist in keeping the chemical kinetics numeri-
cally tractable for spatio-temporal simulations, the detailed chem-
ical kinetics model was reduced, employing the path ﬂux analysis
method (PFA) [26] and retaining detailed model predictive charac-
ter for all kinetic regimes. The reduced mechanism consists of 128
species and 565 elementary reactions and is essentially identical to
that used in [27]. As shown in Fig. 2, the reduced mechanism with
128 species is sufﬁciently comprehensive to reproduce the low
temperature regime, including turnover temperature, NTC behav-
ior, and hot ignition for both shock tube ignition delay and ﬂow
reactor oxidation. Reasonable ﬁdelity of experimental observations
is also achieved. All the droplet combustion simulations presented
in this current study are conducted with this reduced mechanism
unless otherwise noted and will be referred to as the ‘‘detailed’’
model’’. The results achieved with the detailed kinetic model are
also compared with predictions achieved with a ‘‘high tempera-
ture’’ n-heptane chemical kinetic model derived previously [28].
The ‘‘high temperature’’ model of Chaos et al. [28] was speciﬁcally
developed for high temperature oxidation and includes no low
temperature species or kinetic reactions.
Figure 3 shows the predicted droplet diameter, burning rate,
ﬂame diameter and peak gas temperature evolution using these
two kinetic models along with the experimental measurements
[1]. The experimental conditions are do = 3.91 mm, 0.21 XO2, 0.79
XN2, one atmosphere pressure and is referred to as the base case.
It can be seen that the predictions from the two kinetic models
are distinctively different. The ‘‘high temperature’’ model predicts
radiative extinction of the droplet at a large droplet size due to
radiative heat losses [2,29]. After extinction, the droplet is pre-
dicted to undergo evaporation in the quiescent environment,
which is evident in the droplet diameter regression and burning
rate evolution results presented in Fig. 3a and c. The ﬂame diame-
ter evolution predictions with ‘‘high temperature’’ model show an
abrupt decrease corresponding to the ﬂame extinction. Consistent
with our previous works [2,22], the predicted peak temperature ra-
dial location is utilized as the ‘‘ﬂame position’’ marker. As a conse-
quence the ﬂame diameter shows continuing evolution even after
extinction.
The ‘‘detailed’’ model shows signiﬁcantly different combustion
characteristics, while qualitatively capturing experimental obser-
vations. The ‘‘detailed’’ predictions indicate that following the vis-
ible ﬂame extinction, which corresponds to the typical radiative
extinction, droplet burning transitions to a second stage, where
droplet diameter regression rate remains suggestive of continued
‘‘burning’’. In addition, the ‘‘detailed’’ model predicts extinction to-
wards the end of the second stage ‘‘burn’’. During this second stage,
the ‘‘ﬂame’’ diameter, i.e., the radial location of maximum temper-
ature is found to be very close to the droplet surface, much closer
than for high temperature combustion. In the experiments, to-
wards the end of the ﬁrst stage the ﬂame showed oscillations right
before it visibly extinguished and the combustion transitioned to
