doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2013.09.011,doi,Isolated n-heptane droplet combustion in microgravity: “Cool Flames” – Two-stage combustion,6,5,"the second stage. The predicted ﬂame diameter evolution does not
depict any such oscillatory pattern. A possible reason to this ob-
served discrepancy is the slow convection that exists due to the
slow drift of the droplet during the experiments. This unsteady
convection is not considered in the current model. During the sec-
ond stage it can be seen that the maximum burning temperature is
signiﬁcantly lower than the ﬂame temperature experienced in the
ﬁrst stage burn, having a value of 750 K (Fig. 3d).
The predictions from the ‘‘detailed’’ model are very similar to
the ‘‘high temperature’’ model in the ﬁrst stage, the primary differ-
ence being the temporal location of the transition. In comparison
to the experiments, the ‘‘high temperature’’ model shows an earlier
extinction of the visible ﬂame. In contrast, the ‘‘detailed’’ model
predicts the transition to occur slightly later, in good agreement
with the experiments. Even though the ‘‘detailed’’ model qualita-
tively captures the experimental measurements, the second stage
burning rate is signiﬁcantly over-predicted, resulting in an un-
der-prediction of the second stage extinction diameter. The pre-
dicted extinction diameter was found to be 0.35 mm, a factor of
3.5 smaller than the experiment.
The ‘‘detailed’’ model predictions were further analyzed to ob-
tain insights into the parameters controlling the two stage burning
phenomena. The predictions from the ‘‘detailed’’ model showed
very good agreement with the ﬁrst stage measurement data,
including the radiative heat loss resulting in cessation of high tem-
perature burning. However, the second stage combustion phenom-
ena are not as well predicted. To further analyze how the
observations relate to low temperature kinetics, simulations were
conducted by perturbing each of the low temperature reactions
individually (increase/decrease by a factor of 2). The simulations
quantify the sensitivities of the average burning rate (Kb avg) and
extinction diameter (dext) due to each reaction and also provide a
visualization of the uncertainty bandwidth of the predictions. Fig-
ure 4 summarizes the inﬂuence of the rate of each of the low tem-
perature chemical kinetic reactions on the predicted droplet
diameter and burning rate evolution. The bandwidth in the predic-
tions presented in Fig. 4 corresponds to the difference in the pre-
dictions resulting from the variation in the low temperature
reaction rates. It is apparent that the low temperature reaction
rates signiﬁcantly inﬂuence the second stage burning predictions
even within their uncertainty limits.
Figure 5 depicts the prominent sensitivity coefﬁcients of droplet
burning rate and extinction diameter predictions to the reaction
rates for the second stage burn. The reactions having the most
inﬂuence on the second stage burn were found to be isomerization
of QOOHO2 to ketohydroperoxide (QOOHO2 ? Ketohydroperox-
ide + OH) and dissociation of QOOHO2 (QOOHO2 ? QOOH + O2);
Q = C7H14 for this case. In general, increase in the rate of
QOOHO2 ? Ketohydroperoxide + OH increased the burning and
consequently decreased the extinction diameter. The dissociation
rates inversely affect the observations. At temperatures lower than
approximately 900 K, the b-scission of alkyl radicals and internal
H-atom abstraction reactions are slow to occur due to their high
activation energies [17]. Under these conditions most of the alkyl
