netic sub-models.
After identifying the most sensitive reactions for the second
stage burn, simulations were conducted by modifying the reaction
rates of these ten reactions with a factor of two to achieve maxi-
mum variations in the burning parameters. The reaction rates for
the QOOHO2 ? Ketohydroperoxide + OH reactions were decreased
by a factor of 2 and the reaction rates for the QOOHO2 ? -
QOOH + O2 reactions were increased by a factor of 2. Clearly, the
processes represented collectively by these ten reactions suggest
a need for further improvements in terms of mechanistic structure
and rate uncertainties for the subject reactions. Such an investiga-
tion is beyond the scope of the present work.
Figure 7 presents the comparison of the measured and predicted
droplet diameter and burning rate evolution utilizing the revised
model (modiﬁed rates for QOOHO2 ? Ketohydroperoxide + OH and
QOOHO2 ? QOOH + O2) described in the previous paragraph. The
results in the following sections are all obtained by utilizing this re-
vised kinetic model. It is evident that the predictions are in better
agreement with the measurements. The revised second stage ﬂame
temperature prediction (700 K) is 50 K lower than the base predic-
tion (Fig. 7b). The ﬂametemperature evolutionalso shows that in the
second stage the ﬂame temperature steadily increases and then
starts to decrease again followed by sharp drop during extinction.
Comparisons of the original and revised model predictions are also
summarized in Table 1. The predicted average burning rate is ob-
tained by time-averaging the instantaneous burning rate (Ko) be-
tween t = 0.1tb and t = 0.95tb where tb is the total burn time
(ignition to extinction). Comparing the base detailed kinetic and re-
vised model results, the revised average burning rate (Ko avg) and
extinction diameter predictions of 0.385 mm2/s and (dext) 0.95 are
lower and higher than the base predictions by a factor of 1.15 and
2.38, respectively. The second stage revised model predictions of
averageburningrateandextinctiondiameterareonlyslightlydiffer-
ent than the experimental observations. The disparities are poten-
tially within the experimental differences for truly quiescent
observations produced by well known perturbing effects of slow
drop/gas relative velocity produced by drift. It should also be noted
that in the experiments some sooting is observed due to the pre-
vaporized fuel in the system. The pre-vaporization of the fuel occurs
during the formation of the droplet. Sooting is not considered in the
current model. The presence of the soot at the very beginning of the
combustion experimentsis thereforea possible reasonfor the higher
Ko prediction during the ﬁrst stage. Ignition delay time and oxidation
speciation predictions with the revised kinetic model were also
found to result in improved agreement in the low and NTC temper-
ature regime predictions for reﬂected shock ignition delay and ﬂow
reactor reactivity data (provided as Supplementary materials).
