doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2013.09.011,doi,Isolated n-heptane droplet combustion in microgravity: “Cool Flames” – Two-stage combustion,9,8,"than the conductive losses during this phase of combustion.
Clearly, the transition to second stage low temperature burning
is initiated by the heat loss behavior. As the second stage burning
is established, the heat generation and continuing conductive
losses determine the burning behavior. Within the negative tem-
perature coefﬁcient region of the governing chemical kinetics,
increasing heat release relative to heat losses results in increased
reaction zone mean temperatures and decreased chemical reaction
rates, slowing the heat release rate. On the other hand, increasing
loss rates would decrease mean temperatures, increasing reaction
rates and heat release rate. Thus within reaction temperatures be-
tween the turnover temperature and the hot ignition temperature,
a quasi steady low temperature burning phenomenon is estab-
lished. Losses exceeding heat release rate commensurate with
the turnover temperature will result in insufﬁcient chemical reac-
tion rates to sustain low temperature burning. On the other hand,
should heat loss be exceeded by heat release, driving the temper-
ature above the hot ignition temperature, then high temperature
burning can be re-initiated. As a result of these interactions, the
second stage ﬂame positions itself close to the droplet surface at
a location where the rates of heat generation and loss with temper-
ature changes are balanced, as is evident in Fig. 3.
During the second stage the Qgeneration is found to be 17 W
which is signiﬁcantly lower than the assumed 50% heat release rate
of Nayagam and coworkers [6]. On the basis of a time averaged
heat generation rate of 150 W in the ‘‘high temperature’’ ﬁrst stage
the heat generation in the second stage is found to be only 11.5%.
It is interesting to further note that during the second stage the
dominant heat loss mechanism transforms to conduction from
radiation. This is due to the fact that during the second stage as
the ﬂame is very close to the droplet surface the radiative heat loss
decreases signiﬁcantly.
