The larger alkenes, 1-butene (C4H8–1) and 1-pentene (C5H10–1),
are formed primarily in hot fuel-rich regions (89% and 82% respec-
tively) through the decomposition of heptyl radicals (C7H15 ? nC3-
H7 + C4H8–1, C7H15 ? C2H5 + C5H10–1).
Figure 13 shows the spatial proﬁle of the species at t = 8.0 s,
which is just before the transition to second stage burning. As
the radiative heat loss becomes substantial (Fig. 8), there is signif-
icant drop in the gas temperature and the ﬂame moves closer to
the droplet surface. As the transition to the second stage initiates,
the CO2 and H2O concentrations decrease along with decreasing
production of small carbon number alkenes. However, it is inter-
esting to note an increase in the parent fuel alkenes i.e. 2-heptene
(C7H14–2), 3-heptene (C7H14–3). Concentrations of hydrogen perox-
ide (H2O2), formaldehyde (CH2O) and acetaldehyde (CH3CHO) be-
come signiﬁcant, indicating a transition to low temperature
reactivity (Fig. 1a). As the droplet completely transitions to (nearly
quasi-steady) second stage burning (Fig. 14), the CO concentration
increases. In addition to the increases in 2-heptene, 3-heptene,
H2O2, CH2O and CH3CHO concentrations, the production of keto-
hydroperoxides becomes signiﬁcant. A ﬂux analysis indicates that
the bulk of the fuel undergoes H abstraction reactions by OH rad-
icals and to a much lesser extent, by HO2 radicals. Beta scission
reactions of the heptyl radicals formed cease to be signiﬁcant,
and all react with O2 to form _RO2 radicals which then undergo alkyl
peroxy radical isomerization to form QOOH. The QOOH have two
competing consumption pathways: (1) formation of QOOHO2
through O2 addition; and (2) decomposition to alkenes, carbonyl,
QO, OH and HO2. During the second stage combustion of the droplet,
amajor portionof the QOOHundergoes cyclizationofthe diradicalto
form cyclic ethers (QO). The decomposition of QOOH results only in
radical propagation. QOOHO2 surviving the decomposition reaction
participates in the isomerization processes to form ketohydroperox-
ides. It is not until the formation of ketohydroperoxides that degen-
erate chain branching is reestablished which essentially is initiated
through the decomposition of the ketohyroperoxides to form oxy-
genated radical species and OH. During the second stage burn,
99% of larger alkenes (i.e. 2-heptene, 3-heptene) production is
through the QOOH decomposition reactions (QOOH ? C7H14 + -
HO2). Unlike the high temperature reactivity case, 85% of 1-pentene
is formed by the decomposition of the QOOH (QOOH ? OH + CH3-
CHO + C5H10–1) whereas the remaining 15% result from the disso-
ciation of heptyl radicals (C7H15 ? C2H5 + C5H10–1). Similarly 73%
and26%of1-buteneisproducedthroughtheQOOHdecomposition
pathway (QOOH ? OH + C2H5CHO + C4H8–1) and dissociation of the
heptyl radicals (C7H15 ? nC3H7 + C4H8–1) respectively. Among the
other low temperature intermediates, 80% of the CH3CHO is pro-
duced through the decomposition channel of the ketohydropero-
xide (Ketohydroperoxide ? CH3CHO + C3H7COCH2 + OH) and 18%
