oxidation of CH2CHO (CH2CHO + O2 ? CH2O + CO + OH) where the
CH2CHO is produced almost exclusively (95%) by ketohydroperox-
ide ? C4H9CHO + CH2CHO + OH. Lastly, the majority of H2O2 in the
fuel rich side is produced from the H abstraction from the fuel by
HO2 radicals (nC7H16 + HO2 ? C7H15 + H2O2) – with 80% of the
HO2 contribution coming from QOOH decomposition (QOOH ? C7-
H14 + HO2). Figure 15 shows the species proﬁle at t = 34.0 s (the
droplet burning is approaching extinction). It should be noted that
in this second stage the ﬂame temperature is 700 K. Therefore
the observed second stage combustion is not strictly similar to a
classical premixed ‘‘Cool Flames’’ where reactions at temperatures
below the turnover result in accelerating the rate into the negative
temperature coefﬁcient region, causing chemical times to become
too long in comparison to available characteristic residence times
within the cool ﬂame structure [30]. On the basis of the peak tem-
perature and temperature distribution, the observed second stage
droplet burn lies entirely within the temperature range between
the turnover temperature and the hot ignition condition (Fig. 1b).
Additionally, unlike classical premixed ‘‘Cool Flames’’, this unique
two stage burning phase evolves from a high temperature phenom-
enon to a low temperature burning condition; reverse direction
(Fig. 1b).
