doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2013.09.011,doi,Isolated n-heptane droplet combustion in microgravity: “Cool Flames” – Two-stage combustion,13,12,"that low temperature reactivity governs the second stage combus-
tion. Evolution of the smaller alkenes and alkynes (Fig. 16b) shows
similar trends as those for CO2 and H2O, decreasing in their produc-
tion rate in the second stage. It is interesting to note in the low
temperature burning state, the peak acetylene (C2H2) concentra-
tion (which is a soot precursor) drastically decreases, indicating
soot free-combustion even with larger n-alkanes as fuel. The
experimental measurements [1] also showed no soot shell forma-
tion in these two stage combustion events. Contrary to the smaller
alkene yields, the concentrations of larger parent fuel alkenes (2-
heptene, 3-heptene) that were negligible in the ﬁrst stage undergo
sharp increases as transition to the second stage takes place
(Fig. 16c). The major source of these larger alkenes is the decompo-
sition of the QOOH molecules, not through radical beta-scission
reactions. Both 1-butene (Fig. 16b) and 1-pentene (Fig. 16c) shows
an increase at the ﬁrst stage, decrease at transition followed by an
increase during the second stage combustion. This observation cor-
responds to the change in the formation pathway from H abstrac-
tion from heptyl radicals at high temperature to decomposition of
QOOH at low temperatures. The low temperature reactive charac-
ter is also evident from the peak ketohydroperoxide temporal pro-
ﬁles (Fig. 16d). As extinction is approached, there is a sharp rise in
their peak concentration. The higher ketohydroperoxide concen-
tration indicates that the degenerate chain branching from the
ketohydroperoxide decomposition as temperatures approach the
turnover temperature (600 K) cannot produce sufﬁcient reaction
rates to maintain the reaction structure. It has also been reported
by Curran et al. [17] that the ketohydroperoxide decomposition
characteristically is a higher activation energy process typically
associated with a induction time before the chain branching prod-
ucts accelerate the fuel oxidation and heat release at low temper-
atures. The reduced rate of ketohydroperoxide decomposition is
also
evident
in
