CH2O
and
CH3CHO
temporal
proﬁles
(Fig. 17a), which show no sharp increase in response to the in-
creased ketohydroperoxide (Fig. 16d) concentrations. H2O2 and
QO are found to be prominent during the second stage burn and
maintain an almost quasi-steady condition. All the QO are formed
from QOOH via QOOH ? OH + QO reactions.
In the FLEX experiments [1,6], two stage burning extinction is
followed by aerosol cloud formation surrounding the droplet. The
composition of this vapor cloud is not fully known, but is likely
to be composed of raw fuels, and larger intermediate oxidation
products including larger alkenes, ketohdroperoxides, and cyclic
ethers. The total mass of these species was estimated by integrat-
ing the amounts present from the droplet surface to the far ﬁeld for
each reaction time and are presented in Fig. 18. From the proﬁles, it
can be seen that after extinction (t = 37 s), there exists signiﬁcant
amounts of unburned fuel, larger alkenes (2-heptene, 3-heptene),
ketohydroperoxides
and
cyclic
ethers
supporting
the
above
hypothesis.
