doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2013.09.011,doi,Isolated n-heptane droplet combustion in microgravity: “Cool Flames” – Two-stage combustion,14,13,"summarized in Fig. 19. The ﬁgure shows that for smaller do in air
the droplet undergoes single stage high temperature combustion
with no extinction. This is observed for both 2.0 mm and 3.0 mm
do. It is not until an initial droplet diameter of 4.0 mm that the
two stage combustion is observed. As the initial droplet diameter
is increased the associated radiative heat losses start to become
signiﬁcant [2]. The two stage combustion behavior is initiated by
the heat loss from high temperature reaction conditions. Quasi
steady second stage burning has never been observed as a direct
result in droplet auto ignition experiments. With increasing do
the high temperature combustion period starts to become smaller
and onset to the second stage ‘‘Cool Flame’’ occurs earlier. For
do > 3.0 mm, the ﬂame standoff ratio (FSR) (Fig. 19b) and peak tem-
perature (Fig. 19d) during the second stage is independent of the
initial droplet diameter.
Since the transition to the second stage combustion is governed
by the heat loss, ambient diluent conditions that promote heat loss
have the potential to initiate the transition to and sustainment of
second stage combustion. Figure 20 shows the inﬂuence of ambi-
ent carbon dioxide concentration on the droplet diameter regres-
sion, burning rate, FSR and peak gas temperature for a droplet of
3.0 mm initial diameter. The simulations were conducted by
keeping ambient oxygen mole fraction ﬁxed at XO2 = 0.21 and
systematically replacing the N2 with CO2. The initial droplet diam-
eter of do = 3.0 mm was so chosen because in air it does not under-
go two stage combustion. It can be seen that introducing a small
amount of CO2 into the ambient prompts a transition to the second
stage. With just 5% CO2 replacement of nitrogen as diluent, drop-
lets sizes that undergo complete combustion in a single stage with
nitrogen as diluent transition to two stage combustion behavior.
Since CO2 is a radiatively participating species, its increased pres-
ence in the ambient enhances the radiative heat loss [2] and trig-
gers the second stage combustion. As the CO2 mole fraction is
increased, the transition to the second stage occurs earlier
(Fig. 20b–d), and the extinction diameter increases. Consequently
the high temperature burn time becomes shorter. For XCO2 > 10%
a quasi-steady ﬁrst stage burn is never attained. The burning rate
during the high temperature phase is found to decrease with
increasing CO2 mole fraction. Increasing the concentration of CO2
reduces the average thermal conductivity and also increases spe-
ciﬁc heat and hence the heat capacity of the surrounding gas mix-
ture, compared to that with N2 diluent alone. The collective result
is diminished heat feed-back from the ﬂame to the droplet, reduc-
ing the droplet burning rate constant Ko in the high temperature
burn (Fig. 20c). The enhancement of the radiative heat loss with
increasing CO2 mole fraction also increases the rate of decrease
of the peak gas temperature with time during the ﬁrst stage burn
