doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2013.09.011,doi,Isolated n-heptane droplet combustion in microgravity: “Cool Flames” – Two-stage combustion,15,14,"(Fig. 20d). Apart from the earlier transition the increased CO2 mole
fraction does not have a signiﬁcant inﬂuence on second stage burn-
ing, once established, as is apparent in the FSR, burning rate and
peak temperature temporal evolution. The predicted ﬁrst and sec-
ond stage extinction diameter for the different CO2 concentration
is provided as Supplementary material (Table S1).
Figure 21 summarizes the effect of ambient He mole fraction in
the observed two stage combustion behavior. The overall inﬂuence
of He on droplet diameter regression, Ko, FSR and peak gas temper-
ature are presented in Fig. 21a–d. A do = 3.0 mm and XO2 = 0.21 was
held constant whereas the N2 was systematically replaced with He.
Similar to CO2 diluent, introducing 5% He in the ambient was found
to trigger the two stage behavior. Helium in the ambient enhances
the diffusive/conductive heat transfer [2]. This establishes the fact
that controlling/altering the heat loss is a possible way to initiate
the transition to second stage, low temperature burning. Increase
in the helium mole fraction increases the droplet diameter regres-
sion rate the high temperature burning rate and the extinction
diameter. The increasing He concentration in the ambient in-
creases Ko and hence results in shorter total burn time and early
extinction. Even though higher He concentration increases Ko,
counter intuitively it also increases dext this is due to the fact that
the higher thermal diffusivity of He leads to improved heat transfer
to both the droplet surface and the far-ﬁeld. The heat feedback to
the surface is increased at larger droplet sizes, but as the droplet
burning progresses, the losses to the far-ﬁeld dominate as the
ﬂame volume decreases. Counter to CO2 the second stage burn
time decreases as He concentration is increased. Unlike the
radiative heat loss which has minimal inﬂuence on the ﬁrst stage
burn, the diffusive/conductive heat loss is dominant in the second
stage (Fig. 8). The increased diffusive/conductive heat loss of he-
lium results in a decreased duration of the second stage burning.
The results for 70% helium/21% oxygen case, indicates that the
second stage burning is present, but very short lived, prior to
extinction. This result suggests that earlier experiments conducted
with such mixtures at larger initial diameters [4] that were sug-
gested from observations to undergo single stage burning and radi-
ative extinction might in fact might have had a second stage short
term phase of burning that was not resolvable within the uncer-
tainties of the experimental diagnostics (there would have been
non luminous ﬂame during the short second stage burn). The
predicted ﬁrst and second stage extinction diameter for the differ-
ent He concentration is provided as Supplementary material
(Table S2).
4. Conclusions
Recently observed two stage n-heptane droplet combustion
experiment is simulated using a transient spherosymmetric model
with detailed chemistry that included both high temperature and
low temperature kinetics. Predictions using this kinetic model
were found to be in good agreement with earlier predictions of
high temperature combustion using models without low tempera-
ture kinetics, as well as the observed two stage burning phenom-
ena. Counter to classical ‘‘premixed cool ﬂame’’ phenomena, the
observed two stage diffusive combustion of an isolated droplet is
