doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2013.09.011,doi,Isolated n-heptane droplet combustion in microgravity: “Cool Flames” – Two-stage combustion,16,15,"initiated from a hot ﬂame ignition, which then through heat loss
transitions to a low temperature burning conﬁguration. Simula-
tions indicated that the two stage behavior observed in the present
experiments is indeed dictated by low temperature reactivity at
conditions lying above the negative temperature coefﬁcient turn-
over temperature and below the hot ignition temperature. Quasi-
steady second stage burning results from a dynamic stability of
increasing heat release with decreasing heat loss, and decreasing
heat release with increasing heat loss as a result of the reaction
rate gradient with temperature in the NTC regime. Reaction tem-
peratures below the turnover temperature should result in reac-
tion
extinction,
whereas
if
temperature
should
exceed
hot
ignition conditions, high temperature combustion can be re-estab-
lished. These result suggest the likelihood that multiple high tem-
perature/low temperature burning conditions may exist for certain
ranges of ambient gas, drop diameter, initial gas temperature and
reaction pressure.
A sensitivity analysis of the reaction rates identiﬁed the
QOOHO2 decomposition reactions (QOOHO2 ? Ketohydroperox-
ide + OH and QOOHO2 ? QOOH + O2) to be the most sensitive
reactions for the second stage droplet combustion of n-heptane.
The extinction was found to coincide with a ketohydroperoxide
buildup during the later stage of the burn, suggesting a signiﬁcant
decrease in degenerate branching from a lack of further reaction.
As noted above, a principal controlling parameter the observed
two-stage burning behavior was found to be the mechanism and
variation of heat loss with experiment conﬁguration. For moderate
sized droplets in air, excessive heat loss during the ﬁrst stage
combustion was found to govern the transition to the second stage.
In the second stage the major heat loss was observed to be due to
diffusion/conduction. The parametric studies indicated that drop-
lets with do > 3.0 will produce the two stage combustion. It also
further reiterated that parameters that enhance heat loss can initi-
ate the two stage behavior. Small concentrations of carbon dioxide
or helium replacement of nitrogen were found to initiate the
phenomena.
The dependence of the phenomena on fuel properties is hypoth-
esized to relate to their low temperature reactivity/heat release
properties. For example, at atmospheric pressure, isomeric alkanes
of equal carbon number and increased branching would at some
level of branching result in insufﬁcient low temperature kinetic
reactivity to support two stage droplet burning. Likewise, n-al-
kanes blending with materials that suppress low temperature reac-
tivity, such as aromatics, should similarly suppress the probability
of two-stage burning. Moreover, anti-knock inhibitor addition to
alkanes should also induce similar behavior. Finally, the changes
in burning behavior are also expected to be a function of ambient
pressure and ambient initial temperature. All of the above issues
should motivate additional isolated microgravity droplet combus-
tion experiments and are the subject of continuing numerical mod-
eling research similar to that demonstrated here. Recently studied
higher pressure n-heptane droplet combustion experiments have
observed second stage transition to hot ignition, followed by an-
other stage of low temperature burning. These data are currently
being analyzed and simulated, and the results will be discussed
in a future work.
