3.2
N-octane Results
The burning characteristics of four different n-octane droplets burning in oxygen/helium/nit-
rogen mixtures at one atmospheric pressure are shown in Fig. 2. In these tests the helium dilution
is gradually increased at expense of nitrogen while the oxygen mole fraction is held constant at
0.21. Test R298 results shown in Fig. (2a) corresponds to a droplet with D0= 3.6 mm burning in
air (O2:N2 = 0.21:0.79) with no helium dilution. Both hot-ﬂame and cool-ﬂame burning modes
were found to exist with the respective burning rates of 0.48 and 0.38 mm2/s. Prior to extinction
the visible ﬂame showed an oscillatory instability where the ﬂame develops a hole which opened
and closed quickly before complete ﬂame extinction. This ﬂame oscillation is clearly captured
by the broadband radiometer output. The Df values obtained by ﬁtting a circle to the UV-image
of the droplet and calculating an effective ﬂame diameter from the area of the circle is unable to
