3. Experimental results 
3.1. Ignition delay time 
The astronaut would manually position the igniter within a mil- 
limeter of the tip of the rod and activate the igniter until a sta- 
ble ﬂame was achieved. It could be diﬃcult to see the ﬂame in 
the glare of the igniter, so he would turn off the igniter to assess 
ﬂame viability. If the ﬂame appeared to be going out, he would 
quickly turn the igniter back on to strengthen the ﬂame and again 
check ﬂame viability after a few seconds of applying power. This 
ignitor on and off cycle might occur several times in a given test. 
The ignition delay ( t ign ) in each test was deﬁned as the time from 
when the igniter was ﬁrst turned on to the last time the igniter 
was turned off and a stable ﬂame persisted. Ignition generally oc- 
curred at a forced ﬂow of 5 cm/s, so the ignition delay is plotted 
in Fig. 2 against the initial oxygen concentration alone. The symbol 
sizes reﬂect the initial rod radius. Because the igniter is positioned 
and activated manually, there is some inherent scatter in the igni- 
tion time, but some trends are clear. 
At higher oxygen concentrations, ignition occurred within ap- 
proximately 40 s. Below an initial oxygen concentration of 16%, the 
ignition delay time increased signiﬁcantly. Larger rods take longer 
to ignite, especially at lower oxygen concentrations (up to 230 s). 
This suggests that the weaker ﬂames at low oxygen cannot be"
