This difference between oxygen ignition limits and an oxygen 
extinction limits is an important factor to consider for a ﬂammabil- 
ity test. For example, NASA’s Test 1 [16] is an upward ﬂame spread 
test that has a ﬁxed ignition time of ∼25 s, which is shown in 
Fig. 2 as the bottom shaded region. If the material is not ignited by 
this time, it passes the test. However, as one can see from Fig. 2 , a 
longer ignition time may successfully ignite the material, especially 
at the lower oxygen concentrations. 
3.2. Quenching extinction 
Samples burned within the ﬂow duct and the combustion prod- 
ucts exited the duct and mixed with the gas in the 250 l work vol- 
ume of the MSG, so the oxygen very gradually decreased during 
the test. To determine the quenching limit, the ﬂow was decreased 
incrementally and the solid phase temperature distribution was al- 
lowed to equilibrate to the new ﬂame for at least 100 s before the 
ﬂow was changed again, to ensure each ﬂame was steady. Quench 
tests lasted up to 28 min. Near-quenching ﬂames for each rod size 
are shown in Fig. 3 (top). Flow and oxygen are not the same, but 
each represents the lowest oxygen test for that rod size. These all- 
blue ﬂames are roughly hemispherical in shape. 
Figure 4 shows a detailed time history of ﬂame measurements 
(ﬂame length, width, standoff distance, aspect ratio, rod tip posi- 
tion, and radiometer reading) of the lowest oxygen quenching test, 
where the initial oxygen setting was 14.8% O 2 (by volume) and 
the ﬁnal oxygen reading was 13.6 % O 2 . The pre-quenching ﬂame 
from this r = 0.476 cm rod radius test is the center top image in 
Fig. 3 . The ﬂow was incrementally decreased throughout the test, 
as shown by the red stepped line. The ﬂame length stopped grow- 
ing at the 2 cm/s to 1.5 cm/s ﬂow change, and shortly thereafter the 
Fig. 4. Time history of ﬂame measurements and ﬂow from the lowest oxygen 
quenching test for a 0.476 cm radius rod. 
Fig. 5. Flame length compared to the clear bubble layer length. Inset ﬂame image 
shows the bubble layer length. Data is from the same test as Fig. 4 . 
rod regression reached a apparently steady state regression rate of 
0.0385 mm/s. 
Flame measurements show that the ﬂame standoff distance is 
nearly constant. The ﬂame length asymptotes down to nearly con- 
stant length at each subsequent ﬂow until the last one leading to 
extinction. The ﬂame length and radiometer signal track each other 
very closely. The ﬂame width shows a smooth increase up to the 
2 cm/s to 1.5 cm/s ﬂow change, and then a slow decrease until the 
last ﬂow change were it decreases more quickly. The aspect ra- 
tio (L/W) decreases incrementally to 0.5 at extinction, indicating 
a hemispherical ﬂame at the quenching limit. Quenching occurred 
over 100 seconds after the last ﬂow change, which is on the or- 
der of the solid phase response time t s . The cause of quenching 
extinction is evaluated further in Section 4.3 . 
The ﬂame length minus the standoff distance matches the 
clear bubble layer length just before each ﬂow change, as shown 
in Fig. 5 , indicating that the bubble layer formation is due to 
heat ﬂux from the ﬂame, as shown in the ﬂame image inset in 
Fig. 5 (@1100 s). The dense small clear bubble layer (indicated by 
the arrow in the ﬂame inset image) tracks the length closely ex- 
cept just after the ﬂow is turned down. The ﬂame adjusts quickly 
to the ﬂow change, but the bubble layer length can only shrink at"
