After the last ﬂow change, the ﬂame length does not reach a 
steady size, but shrinks and begins to oscillate. The radiometer sig- 
nal provides the best temporal reading of these oscillations. The 
radiometer reading for the last 30 seconds of the test are shown 
in Fig. 6 . The radiometer signal oscillates at approximately 1.1 Hz 
as shown in the inset to Fig. 6 , and drops to a value of 1.05 over 
a period of a few seconds followed by a large oscillation. The 
ﬂame appears to recover back to smaller scale oscillations. After 
a few seconds, the radiometer signal again drops to 1.05 followed 
by another large oscillation. The ﬂame then extinguishes. A still 
ﬂame image also inset into Fig. 6 shows a similar test that cap- 
tured a ﬂame just prior to extinction, showing the ﬂame length 
shrinks forward to basically the ﬂame standoff distance on its way 
to quenching extinction. 
These oscillations are very similar to those observed for candle 
ﬂames in a quiescent environment [17] , since the ∼1 cm/s convec- 
tive ﬂow is at the level of diffusive ﬂows. The open tail of the small 
hemispherical ﬂame allows fuel vapor to escape downstream. This 
mixes with oxygen and forms a ﬂammable mixture. The ﬂame 
ﬂashes downstream through this mixture and consumes the reac- 
tants. This larger ﬂame locally depletes the oxygen concentration, 
weakening the ﬂame brieﬂy. For a large amplitude oscillation, the 
local oxygen depletion become low enough to destabilize the ﬂame 
anchor at the stagnation region, and the ﬂame extinguishes en- 
tirely. The oscillation frequency is controlled by the diffusive trans- 
port (mixing) rates of the reactants and the inherent heat losses 
[17] . The radiative loss has been shown to have the same effect 
as changing the thermal-diffusivity of the reactants [18] resulting 
in oscillations with thermo-diffusive characteristics even for unity 
Lewis number. 
3.3. Blowoff extinction 
Blowoff limits were typically determined after a stable rod re- 
gression was established at a modest ﬂow speed. The astronaut 
would then turn up the ﬂow to obtain blowoff. Blowoff was de- 
ﬁned as the instant when the stagnation region developed a hole 
[19] as shown in the lower row of ﬂame images in Fig. 3 . The cen- 
ter blowoff image is of a 5.18-second Zero Gravity Research Facil- 
ity drop test, since no ISS blowoff tests were obtained with the 
intermediate rod size. A green LED illuminates the drop test rod. 
Fig. 7. Time history of stretch rate and radiometer signal for two local blowoffs for 
a 0.318 cm rod. 
The ﬂames in the drop tower blowoff tests are signiﬁcantly smaller 
than the ISS ﬂames, as can be seen in Fig. 3, due to the reduced 
level of fuel preheating resulting from the short burn time prior to 
blowoff. Previous research [6,7] , 20 , 21 ] and Section 4.3 indicate that 
blowoff extinction is not sensitive to heat losses. 
After the stagnation region blows off, the subsequent annu- 
lar ﬂame leading edge would oscillate upstream and downstream 
multiple times before completely blowing off. During one test, we 
attempted to re-establish the envelope ﬂame after local blowoff. 
Only once could we successfully re-establish the stagnation region 
after local blowoff by quickly reducing the ﬂow to a value below 
the blowoff velocity. A second attempt was not successful. 
The stretch rate and radiometer data from these blowoffs are 
shown in Fig. 7 . In each blowoff, the astronaut quickly turns up 
the ﬂow from a low stretch rate to a high stretch rate and the 
ﬂame blows downstream. The astronaut then quickly turns the 
ﬂow down to a stretch rate near the minimum ﬂammability (dis- 
cussed in Section 3.4 ). Figure 7 shows that the two attempts were 
quite similar, but the results were not. For the ﬁrst blowoff, the 
ﬂame hole is initially about 9 mm in diameter, but within 2 s 
moves back forward of the rod tip position (yellow line in Fig. 8 ) 
and closes up in 7 seconds after the ﬂow is turned down, as shown 
in the top row of Fig. 8 . The radiometer signal is higher since the 
ﬂow is slightly higher and the ﬂame is visibly longer than the pre- 
blowoff ﬂame. 
A subsequent attempt 55 seconds later resulted in a slightly 
larger initial ﬂame hole of 10 mm that was not able to move for- 
ward of the ﬂame tip and re-establish the stagnation region, as 
shown in the bottom row of Fig. 8 . This 10 mm ﬂame hole per- 
sisted for over a minute even after the ﬂow was turned down again 
to the initial ﬂow before the ﬂame destabilized and blew off com- 
pletely. 
While the ﬂow proﬁle was quite similar for the two blowoffs, 
the oxygen had depleted more by the second blowoff. This is ap- 
parent in the reduced sooting for the second pre-blowoff ﬂame. 
This second blowoff is interpreted as indicating that once the ﬂame 
is stabilized farther than the thermal length downstream of the 
stagnation region ( ℓ = 2 mm), the ﬂame is unable to re-propagate 
upstream for the low oxygen levels tested (X O2 ﬁnal = 14.5% for 
this test) even though the fuel is already preheated. This is consis- 
tent with tests for opposed-ﬂow ﬂame spread over rods that were 
also done as part of BASS-II [14] , where opposed ﬂow ﬂame spread 
was not observed below 16.4 % O 2 at any ﬂow."
