3.4. Flammability boundary 
For each test, the ﬂow speed at which the ﬂames extinguished 
and the last ﬂow speed at which a stable ﬂame was observed (or 
the ﬂow at which the ﬂame did not blowout in the drop time for 
drop tests), along with the post-burn oxygen sensor reading were 
used to generate a ﬂammability boundary, shown in Fig. 9 . Error 
bars reﬂect the uncertainties in oxygen and ﬂow, and are mostly 
smaller than the symbols. 
The x axis of Fig. 9 uses a forced stretch rate which is deﬁned 
as a = 3/2 U/r ∗(r / r o ) 1/2 . The square root modifying factor is used 
to correct for the different rod sizes. The gas phase thermal length 
scale [7] is deﬁned as ℓ = ( α/ a ) 1/2 , which is proportional to r 1/2 for 
a given velocity. All stretch rates are normalized to the base rod 
radius (r o = 0.318 cm) using the ratio of these thermal lengths. 
The thick gray ﬂammability boundary in Fig. 9 represents the 
border between stable ﬂames and extinguished ﬂames. Along this 
boundary the ﬂame is sensitive to stochastic perturbations that can 
lead to extinguishment [22] . The boundary is deﬁned by the com- 
bined empirical equations shown by the red (quenching branch, 
exponential expression) and black (blowoff branch, linear expres- 
sion) dashed lines. 
X O2 = 6 exp ( −0 . 5 a ) + 0 . 0167 a + 13 . 4 
(1) 
The minimum in this boundary occurs at a = 8 to 12 s −1 at 
approximately 13.5% O 2 , in good agreement the predicted stretch 
rates at the minima in the ﬂammability boundary [6,7,20,21] . 
The drop tower blowoff data, given by the purple plus symbols 
and dashed line, agree very well with the ﬂight data. The fact that 
the blowoff boundary is linear with ﬂow makes it possible to ex- 
trapolate blowoff data down to zero stretch rate and use that oxy- 
gen value as an accurate but conservative estimate of the mini- 
mum oxygen concentration for material ﬂammability in space. This 
may provide a consistent methodology to de-rate materials based 
on normal gravity ﬂammability tests accounting for buoyant ﬂow 
to ensure the material will not burn in space. 
The quenching branch is ﬁtted using an exponential decay (red 
dashed curve). In this region, heat losses dominate to cool the 
ﬂame to extinction through the strong dependence of reaction rate 
in the Arrhenius term, as was modeled by T’ien et al. [6,7,20,21] , 
and will be evaluated in Section 4.3 . This boundary is very steep 
for small stretch rates when plotted on a linear x axis as shown in 
Fig. 9 . 
Figure 10 replots the same data as Fig. 9 but with a logarith- 
mic x axis. Note that the classic “U”-shaped ﬂammability bound- 
ary is evident when a logarithmic x-axis is used. This expands the 
quenching branch while making the linear blowoff branch appear 
curved. It also emphasizes the uncertainty in the ﬂow at the very 
low velocities ( < 1 cm/s) along the quenching branch. The mini- 
mum oxygen concentration of 13.5% O 2 at a stretch rate of 8 to 12 
s −1 is signiﬁcantly lower than the observed upward ﬂame spread 
limit of 15.4% O 2 (in normal gravity with an additional 4 cm/s 
forced ﬂow [23] ), indicating the material remains ﬂammable un- 
der the weak ventilation only obtainable in microgravity. This 
‘Goldilocks’ zone of ﬂow is unfortunately typical of spacecraft ven- 
tilation ﬂows near surfaces. Earthbound ﬂammability tests need"
