concentration is assumed to be unity at the fuel surface. Eq. (2 ) is 
solved for the critical temperatures along the extinction boundary, 
T crit (not constant) by using the oxygen and stretch dependence 
from Eq. (1 ). 
The resultant critical ﬂame temperatures along the ﬂammabil- 
ity boundary are shown in Fig. 17 as a function of stretch rate. The 
critical temperature drops as stretch rate decreases. For quench- 
ing extinction ( a < ∼10 s −1 ), the critical ﬂame temperature drops 
more precipitously. This is due to the reduction in heat ﬂux from 
the ﬂame relative to the intrinsic heat losses, as will be shown in 
Section 4.3 . Critical ﬂame temperatures are used in the next sec- 
tion to estimate heat ﬂux from the ﬂame to the fuel. 
