4.4. Rod regression rates 
The predicted 2 zone average rod regression rates using the 
two-zone model are compared with the measured average regres- 
sion rates, as shown in Fig. 19 . There is signiﬁcant scatter in the 
measured average regression rates since the ﬂow and oxygen var- 
ied during each test, but the values are of the correct magnitude 
compared to the predicted 2 zone rod regression rates. 
It is also possible to compare the 1-D solution to Eq. (5) with 
prior pseudo-1-D ﬂame experiments [28–30] by assuming all ar- 
eas for heat transfer are the same and then solving the equation 
for V r , also shown in Fig. 19 . Stretch rates are also deﬁned as U/D 
for the 1-D geometry, using the base rod size. The 1-D predicted 
regression rates agree quite well with existing data [28–30] where 
the scale of the ﬂames is much wider than the ﬂame standoff dis- 
tance, resulting in pseudo-1-D ﬂames. The burning rates for the 
two-zone model are signiﬁcantly higher than the 1-D solution for 
a given stretch rate due to the increased surface area for heat ﬂux 
and fuel vaporization in the 3-D cylindrical geometry. 
4.5. Estimate of heat loss along the ﬂammability boundary 
The terms of Eq. (5) are plotted in Fig. 20 as a function of 
stretch rate. The graph is plotted with logarithmic axes to expand 
the quenching region ( a < 10 s −1 ) where the heat losses become 
important. The total ﬂame heating rate to the surface drops lin- 
early with decreasing stretch rate as was shown in Fig. 17 . The 
minimum heat ﬂux at unity stretch rate is 1 W/A h = 1.57 W/cm 2 , 
which is in the range of literature values for the critical heat ﬂux 
for ignition of PMMA [31] . Along the blowoff branch ( a > 10 s −1 ), 
fuel vaporization parallels the total ﬂame to surface heat ﬂux. Heat 
loss is relatively small, with the solid conduction from the surface 
in depth being most signif i cant, but it decreases with decreasing 
stretch rate, mirroring the Peclet number. Flame radiative "
