1. Introduction 
This study proposes a technique to emulate the burning of con- 
densed phase fuels in microgravity. A circular porous burner with 
gaseous fuel, called the Burning Rate Emulator (BRE), was devel- 
oped to simulate the burning of condensed fuels. This emulation 
is achieved by matching four key properties: heat of combustion, 
surface temperature, smoke point, and heat of gasiﬁcation. Zhang 
et al. [1] demonstrated that these properties suﬃciently emulate 
burning behavior in normal gravity. The present study involves an 
extension to microgravity conditions. A goal is to determine if the 
BRE can provide a cost-effective method to study the ﬂammability 
of various materials aboard spacecraft. 
The advent of human-crew space missions presented the pos- 
sibility of many hazards, notably ﬁres such as that on the Russian 
Mir space station [2] . NASA has established protocols for ﬁre safety 
and control in spacecraft. To determine the ﬂammability of mate- 
rials in spacecraft, NASA uses a pass-fail test called the Upward 
Flame Propagation Test or Test 1 [3] . It measures the ﬂame prop- 
∗Corresponding author. 
E-mail address: jimq@umd.edu (J.G. Quintiere). 
1 Retired. 
agation across a sample exposed to a radiant heat ﬂux in normal 
gravity. NASA also recommends the use of Test 2, involving a cone 
calorimeter to provide an external heat ﬂux. 
Ohlemiller illustrated that the NASA test results do not agree 
with the ﬂammability tests established by NIST [4] . The Earth- 
based tests involve signiﬁcant uncertainty and are insuﬃcient to 
outline the characteristics of a diffusion ﬂame in reduced gravity 
conditions [5] . The ideal scenario would be to perform burn tests 
aboard the International Space Station (ISS) of potential materials, 
but this is not feasible. Another option may be to use a BRE burner 
aboard the ISS to determine the ﬂammability conditions for these 
materials in terms of the four characteristic properties. The NASA 
Test 2 can measure three of these properties and hence, a combi- 
nation of the BRE technique with the NASA standard tests could 
deliver a robust safety protocol. 
Microgravity combustion experiments have been conducted 
since the 1950s in ground-based microgravity facilities and aboard 
spacecraft [6–15] . Extensive experiments have been conducted to 
examine microgravity condensed fuel ﬂames [16–25] , but quies- 
cent steady burning conditions have rarely been obtained. The 
initial microgravity tests on material ﬂammability relied on ﬂame 
spread or ambient ﬂow. Kimzey [6,7] conducted ﬂame spread 
experiments aboard the Skylab space station and parabolic 
