uously. Ivanov et al. [8] also conducted experiments aboard 
the Mir space station to study concurrent ﬂow ﬂame spread 
across Delrin, polymethylmethacrylate (PMMA), and high-density 
polyethylene (HDPE). The ﬂames continued to spread as long as 
there was a minimal ﬂow (0.3 cm/s). Flame spread experiments 
aboard the space shuttle achieved unsteady burning for thick fuels 
like PMMA without ambient ﬂow [9] . In contrast, Ramachandra 
et al. [10] demonstrated steady ﬂame spread for thin fuels made 
of ashless ﬁlter paper in quiescent microgravity. 
Another prominent example of quasi-steady burning is the 
burning of candle ﬂames by Dietrich et al. [11,12] on the space 
shuttle and the Mir space station. The ﬂames were hemispheri- 
cal and burned for up to 45 min. These tests and those of Ref. 
[10] indicate that quasi-steady burning is possible in quiescent mi- 
crogravity. 
