The combustion of fuel droplets in microgravity can be quasi- 
steady [13–15] and can result in radiative extinction. However, 
some droplets can support cool ﬂame burning even after radiative 
extinction of the hot ﬂame [16–19] . 
Low-momentum jet diffusion ﬂames [26,27] have shapes that 
are similar to those of the BRE burner. These ﬂames burned for up 
to 230 s on the space shuttle. These observations suggest the BRE 
burner could also support long-term microgravity ﬂames. 
The use of gas burners to emulate the steady burning of con- 
densed phase fuels was initiated by Corlett [28] , de Ris et al. 
[29,31] , and Kim et al. [30] . The large-scale burner developed by de 
Ris and Orloff [31] had a sintered-bronze surface and used gaseous 
fuel. It studied the effects of radiation and orientation on turbu- 
lent diffusion ﬂames in normal gravity. It involved a uniform fuel 
ﬂux throughout the burner, which differs from the behavior of 
condensed fuels. However, the burning conditions of several con- 
densed fuels were correlated to those of the sintered burner ﬂame 
using the Spalding B number. Despite the uniform fuel ﬂux, con- 
densed fuel emulation was possible. 
The use of porous plate burners in microgravity has been lim- 
ited. Brahmi et al. [32] used such a burner in cross ﬂow. Some 
quasi-steady ﬂames were observed. At very low oxidizer velocity 
(5 mm/s), a quasi-steady elliptical ﬂame was achieved. 
The current BRE burners, with diameters of 25 and 50 mm, are 
fabricated based on the porous burner introduced by de Ris and 
Orloff [31] . The burners are developed to model small pool ﬁres. 
The 50 mm burner has been used in normal gravity to mimic the 
burning of condensed fuels such as methanol, heptane, PMMA and 
polyoxymethylene (POM) [1] . The gaseous fuels used for emula- 
tion included propylene, ethylene, and methane diluted with ni- 
trogen. Lundström et al. [33] studied the ignition and extinction of 
condensed-phase fuels by using the 25 mm BRE burner in normal 
gravity. 
The objective of this study is to evaluate the BRE burner and 
concept in NASA Glenn’s 5.18-s Zero Gravity Research Facility. Pre- 
vious drop experiments with a 25 mm burner were demonstrated 
by Zhang et al. [34] . The unsteady behavior of BRE ﬂames is exam- 
ined using a transient analysis and relevant steady-state theory is 
employed to correlate the results. 
2. Experimental 
Two gaseous fuel BRE burners, with diameters of 25 and 
50 mm, are used. A schematic of the 50 mm burner is shown 
in Fig. 1 . The burner face is a copper plate perforated with 71 
round holes. A ceramic ﬂow straightener ensures uniform fuel ﬂux 
( ±10%), and the sidewalls are made of 1 mm thick 304 stainless 
steel. Additional speciﬁcations are shown in Table 1 . 
The measurements include the heat ﬂux from the ﬂame to the 
burner surface, the heat ﬂux sensor temperature, and the temper- 
ature of the burner surface, all recorded at 100 Hz. Two heat ﬂux 
sensors (Medtherm) and K-type thermocouples are installed on the 
burner surface for this purpose. These measurements are made at 
two locations, one at the center of the burner and the other at a 
radius R ∗. The mass ﬂow rate of the fuel is measured by a Sierra 
mass ﬂow meter with maximum ﬂow rate of 1 standard liter per 
minute (slpm) on a nitrogen basis. The fuel mass ﬂux at the burner"
