Microgravity was obtained in NASA Glenn’s 5.18-s Zero Grav- 
ity Research Facility. The g-level attained in the drop facility is 
about 10 μg. A total of 49 tests are completed, 18 with the 25 mm 
burner and 31 with the 50 mm burner. The burner is installed in 
a quiescent chamber and it is surrounded by a chimney to obtain 
symmetric ﬂames. Methane, ethylene, and nitrogen-diluted ethy- 
lene are the three fuels. The ambient pressure and oxygen mole 
fraction, and the fuel ﬂow rate are varied to ascertain their effects. 
These parameters help to assess the ﬂammability of the emulated 
condensed fuel. 
The four key fuel properties emulate the burning of diverse 
condensed fuels. The heat of combustion and the smoke point are 
obtained from literature [35,36] , while the other two properties are 
measured during the tests. The surface temperature is that of the 
copper plate. The heat of gasiﬁcation, L , is given by the ratio of the 
average net heat ﬂux and the fuel ﬂux, 
