diameter liquid and solid pool ﬁres in normal gravity [1] . The burn- 
ing rate in the 5-s microgravity tests is plotted as a function of the 
heat of gasiﬁcation in Fig. 11 . This illustrates the potential for the 
burning of real fuels in microgravity. Despite variations in burner 
diameter and sooting tendencies of the fuels, the data correlate in- 
versely. For the four fuels emulated in normal gravity, their cor- 
responding burning ﬂuxes behave similarly at slightly higher val- 
ues than the microgravity data [1] . (Pool ﬁres in 1-g: Heptane, 
L = 0.48 kJ/g gives 15 g/m 2 -s; Methanol, L = 1.24 kJ/g gives 11 g/m 2 - 
s; PMMA, L = 1.6 kJ/g gives 6.4 g/m 2 -s; and POM, L = 2.1 kJ/g gives 
9 g/m 2 -s.) Interpreting the BRE results as representative of real fu- 
els suggest that materials with uncommonly high heats of gasiﬁca- 
tion may burn in microgravity while having diﬃculty, unassisted, 
in normal gravity. 
Continuing to view the microgravity 5-s data as representative 
of real fuels, the effect of pressure and oxygen can be assessed for 
a particular fuel. In the tests, the pressure of the ambient air was 
varied from 0.5–1.0 atm whereas the oxygen mole fraction ranged 
from 0.21–0.30. The theoretical burning rate for a speciﬁc fuel, as 
given by Eq. (17) , depends on D , h c,ox , and Y O 2 , ∞ , but not on pres- 
sure. For example, for the ethylene-based fuels, h c,ox is constant 
for all levels of dilution with nitrogen, and therefore the theoret- 
ical burning rate is constant. Also, when the burning rate is non- 
dimensionalized as ˙ 
m 
′′ c p D/k , the effect of burner diameter is seen 
to vanish. 
The experimental effects of oxygen and pressure are examined 
for a given fuel by selecting data in Fig. 11 that have nearly the 
same value of L . The tests chosen to study the inﬂuence of am- 
bient pressure on the dimensionless burning rate are shown in 
Table 5 and Fig. 12 . Consider the data grouped as three condensed 
phase fuels with values L = 1.16 + /- 0.07, 1.29 + /- 0.02 and 2.15 + /- 
0.2 kJ/g in Fig. 12 a. Using the same data points, it is more clear to 
plot them as dimensionless mass ﬂux vs. pressure for the “same 
fuels” as shown in Fig. 12 b. It is evident that the burning rate is 
constant for every case and does not depend on ambient pressure. 
Fig. 12 also indicates that the dilution of ethylene with nitrogen 
has no effect on the experimental dimensionless burning rate, in 
agreement with the steady-state theory. 
To examine the effect of oxygen concentration on burning, two 
sets of tests were selected, each having nearly the same heat of 
gasiﬁcation and 1 atm ambient pressure, as shown in Table 6 . 
Figure 13 correspondingly indicates that the burning rate rises with 
ambient oxygen for each of the surrogate condensed phase fuels. 
8. Conclusions 
A burner to emulate real condensed fuel ﬂames is considered. 
Known as the Burning Rate Emulator (BRE), it is based on the hy- 
pothesis that steadily burning condensed fuels can be emulated us- 
ing gases. Its application in microgravity is explored using NASA 
Glenn’s 5.18-s Zero Gravity Research Facility. The test parameters 
were adjusted to emulate different condensed fuels. The 5-s exper- 
iments provided an extensive insight into quiescent microgravity 
burning. Ignition and sustained burning were achieved for gaseous 
fuel mixtures with heats of gasiﬁcation in the range of 0.6–6 kJ/g, 
but steady conditions were not fully achieved during the drop. The 
BRE ﬂames during 5 s can be related to the steady burning of typ- 
ical plastics and liquids. Further analysis and longer-term exper- 
iments on the ISS are needed to establish a steady ﬂammability 
domain in microgravity. A successful demonstration could help to 
improve spacecraft ﬁre safety. 
The ﬂames became hemispherical near the end of the drop 
tests. However, they continued growing throughout the 5 s. A sim- 
ple transient analysis was developed and applied to examine tran- 
sient effects. The analysis suggested that the heat ﬂux and burn- 
ing rate after 5 s were nearly steady. A steady-state axisymmetric 
combustion model demonstrated a correlation for a broad range 
of data. However, the results are dependent on the diameter of 
the burning region, and there is indication that the effect of gas- 
phase radiation is signiﬁcant for the 50 mm burner. The ellipsoidal 
combustion model indicated that the steady ﬂame would be much 
greater in size than observed at 5 s. It is unknown if further ﬂame 
growth in microgravity would lead to radiative cooling and extinc- 
tion. 
Future work will further analyze the unsteady problem and the 
effects of radiation. The measured temperature of the burner sur- 
face, designed as a calorimeter, will be analyzed to better under- 
stand the heat ﬂux distribution over the surface. 
