Fig. 1. Diagram of concurrent ﬂame spread over a thin sample. The fuel is shown edge-on, and only half of the ﬂame is plotted (symmetry is assumed with respect to the 
sample center plane). Conductive and radiative ﬂuxes (q c and q r ) are depicted. Left: upward spread in an open domain in a gravitational ﬁeld. The ﬂow accelerates as it 
moves up through the ﬂame. Right: concurrent spread in forced ﬂow inside a ﬂow tunnel. 
include gas-phase ﬂow time, diffusion time, chemical time and the 
solid heat-up and conduction times. All of these are affected by 
the types and magnitudes of oxidizer ﬂows. Furthermore, the inﬂu- 
ences of some of these parameters on ﬂame spread are profoundly 
affected by gravity-induced buoyant ﬂows. Oxygen is drawn into 
the ﬂame reaction zone as combustion products are transported 
away in the buoyant plume. As illustrated in the left part of Fig. 1 , 
this buoyant ﬂow can be acceleratory along an upwardly spread- 
ing ﬂame. Starting at the base of the ﬂame with a very modest 
magnitude, the ﬂow accelerates upward as the size of the ﬂame 
increases, and in extreme cases can reach velocities of several tens 
of meters per second [5] . This buoyant ﬂow affects the mass ﬂow 
rate of both oxygen and fuel at the ﬂame location, the chemical re- 
action rate at the ﬂame, and the heat transfer rate from the ﬂame 
to the fuel surface, which in turn will govern the ﬂame stabil- 
ity and its spread rate. In the absence of gravity, a ﬂame would 
spread by diffusion of heat and mass in a quiescent oxidizer, or 
by forced convection if there are externally induced gaseous ﬂows 
near the fuel surface. Although the basic mechanisms of ﬂame 
spread are the same with or without gravity, the relative magni- 
tudes and characteristics of the transport of heat and mass and the 
ﬂame chemical kinetics may be different, which may cause differ- 
ent types of ﬂame spread. 
In the absence of gravity, ﬂame spread behavior is not entirely 
resolved. Aboard an orbiting spacecraft such as the International 
Space Station (ISS), gravitational forces are miniscule. As a result, a 
ﬂame aboard the ISS cannot generate any signiﬁcant buoyant con- 
vective ﬂow. However, a slow forced ﬂow is present because of 
ventilation (about 10–20 cm/s). Natural convective ﬂow and forced 
ﬂow differ in both their detailed structure near burning surfaces 
and their development along those surfaces, as shown for concur- 
rent ﬂame spread in Fig. 1 . Moreover, the magnitude of this forced 
ﬂow aboard spacecraft is much smaller than the peak buoyant ﬂow 
induced by ﬂames in normal gravity (between 0.3 m/s at the ﬂame 
base to several m/s downstream). Given the differences in the con- 
vective ﬂows, the response of the ﬂame in these two environments 
remains a central question. In normal gravity, an upward spreading 
ﬂame usually grows and accelerates. Will this happen in micro- 
gravity when a ﬂame is only exposed to a forced convective ﬂow? 
How large can a ﬁre become in microgravity? How do ﬂammabil- 
ity and ﬂame stability change? In addition to these fundamental 
scientiﬁc questions, there are very practical safety concerns since 
there are limited escape options from a spacecraft. Unfortunately, 
because of ﬂame-induced buoyancy, it is very diﬃcult to reproduce 
on Earth the environmental conditions of a spacecraft, particularly 
the low velocity ﬂows. Consequently, ﬁre testing of materials to be 
used in spacecraft requires extrapolations of normal gravity tests 
(with certain assumptions), utilization of reduced gravity facilities, 
or alternative approaches to diminish the effects buoyancy. While 
it is challenging to perform large-scale combustion experiments 
aboard orbiting platforms, the results provide a crucial connection 
between 1-g tests, small-scale microgravity experiments, and the- 
ory. 
1.1. Experimental background 
Over the years, the combustion and ﬁre research community, 
led by NASA, has conducted microgravity experiments on ﬂame 
spread and growth over solids in microgravity. The tests conducted 
using ground–based facilities such as drop towers [6–8] and air- 
planes ﬂying parabolic trajectories [9,10] were limited by the short 
microgravity test duration, while past space experiments were lim- 
ited to small samples due to safety considerations on the Inter- 
national Space Station. For example, in the recent BASS (Burning 
and Suppression of Solids) project on the ISS [11] , ﬂame spread 
experiments were carried out in a small ﬂow tunnel using cotton- 
ﬁberglass fabric samples measuring only 2.1 cm x 10 cm. 
One of the main ﬁndings of solid burning in microgravity is 
that gaseous and surface radiation are important in low-intensity 
ﬂames typical of those near extinction [12–14] . In previous normal 
gravity work, radiation in ﬁres was considered an important mech- 
anism for ﬂame development only when the ﬂame size became 
greater than about 40 cm, at which point these large ﬂames yielded 
substantial soot radiation [15] . In microgravity, radiation (net loss 
from the surface and gas phase) becomes comparatively more im- 
portant, not because the radiative ﬂux increases, but rather be- 
cause of the absence of natural convection. When the convective 
velocity decreases, the ratio of radiative loss rate to the heat gen- 
eration rate increases, so the ﬂame temperature decreases and ra- 
diative losses alone, from the ﬂame’s gas-phase ﬁeld and from the 
fuel surface, are suﬃcient to lead to ﬂame quenching [12] . This has 
a large impact on key ﬂame phenomena such as ignition, ﬂame 
spread, and extinction [16] . In many low-speed microgravity exper- 
iments, the small ﬂames near the extinction limit have no visible 
soot [6] , which is a consequence of suppressed soot formation at 
reduced ﬂame temperatures."
