D.L. Urban et al. / Combustion and Flame 199 (2019) 168–182 
In addition to the magnitude of the ﬂow velocity, the direction 
of ﬂame spread with respect to the direction of ﬂow is impor- 
tant [17,18] . Opposed ﬂame spread occurs when the two directions 
are against each other (e.g. downward spread in buoyant ﬂow and 
upstream spread in forced ﬂow). Concurrent ﬂame spread occurs 
when the two directions are the same (upward spread in buoyant 
ﬂow and downstream spread in forced ﬂow). In opposed spread, 
the controlling zones for ﬂame spread and stabilization are collo- 
cated at the ﬂame base. Assuming that upstream gas-phase con- 
duction is the dominant heat transfer mode, the size of this region 
is given by the thermal length α/U where α is the gas thermal dif- 
fusivity and U is the ﬂow speed in this zone. While the thermal 
length scale is typically very small, it controls ﬂame stabilization 
and forward heat conduction. Because the heat ﬂux and the pre- 
heat length have the opposite dependence on this length scale, a 
canceling effect renders the spread of ﬂames over a thin fuel in 
opposed ﬂow relatively insensitive to the air speed except near the 
blow-off limit [3,19–21] . For a thick fuel, however, the ratio of the 
forward gas phase conduction and the heat conduction into the 
solid renders opposed-ﬂow ﬂame spread dependent on the ﬂow 
speed [3,19–21] . In addition, gas phase chemical kinetics may af- 
fect the spread rate and even cause extinction (blow-off) of the 
ﬂame at high ﬂow speed [3,22] . At very-low-ﬂow speeds in mi- 
crogravity, radiation heat losses and chemical kinetic effects may 
also cause extinction (quenching) of the ﬂame [12,23–26] . Thus, 
although in purely-forced-ﬂow opposed spread, the downstream 
ﬂame size (e.g. ﬂame length) does not affect the spread rate sig- 
niﬁcantly, in the presence of gravity the induced ﬂow velocity will 
increase as the ﬂame becomes larger affecting both the heat trans- 
fer from the ﬂame to the solid and the gas phase chemical kinetics. 
In concurrent-ﬂow ﬂame spread, the controlling zones for ﬂame 
spread and stabilization are separate and distinct [17,18] . Flame 
spread depends on the length of the ﬂame and its thermal plume, 
an important distinction compared to the opposed-ﬂow case which 
normally depends only on the thermal length scale. 
In typical concurrent-ﬂow, ﬂame spread conﬁgurations (includ- 
ing upward spread in normal gravity and forced ﬂow at elevated 
air velocity), the solid preheating and pyrolysis rates are domi- 
nated by convection, and the external ﬂow velocity will therefore 
inﬂuence both the ﬂame length and the heat ﬂux from the ﬂame 
to the solid surface [3,27–29] . Since there are marked differences 
in the velocity distributions between a buoyant-induced ﬂow on 
Earth and a purely-forced ﬂow in microgravity, it is expected that 
there will be notable differences in ﬂame spread in buoyant and 
forced ﬂows. In upward spread over a solid surface, for example, 
the buoyant induced ﬂow starts to accelerate from the ﬂame base 
until it is balanced by viscous drag caused by the solid surface. This 
ﬂow acceleration entrains oxygen into the ﬂame. The pyrolysis rate 
and consequently the ﬂame length increase with ﬂow velocity, so 
therefore an incipient ﬂame spread may continually grow from the 
point of ignition [30–33] . Also, concurrent-ﬂow ﬂame spread de- 
pends on fuel thickness because of the requirements to heat and 
pyrolyze the solid. In turn, the fuel vapor which is generated in 
the pyrolysis region controls ﬂame length. When the fuel is thick 
it may not readily burn out and consequently the ﬂame length can 
increase as the ﬂame spreads. However, the ﬂame standoff distance 
also increases and as a result the convective heat ﬂux from the 
ﬂame to the solid is reduced. The relationship between these two 
effects determines whether the ﬂame spread rate will increase or 
remain constant [27,34] . 
When the fuel is thin, however, the burning region may slowly 
lengthen or remain constant due to fuel burn-out, and the ﬂame 
spread rate may become constant regardless of whether the ﬂame 
is in a natural convection [35] or forced ﬂow [36] environment. 
Some investigations have shown that an upward-burning ﬂame 
over thin narrow paper samples in reduced pressure could reach 
a steady rate and with a constant (limiting) ﬂame length [28,37] . 
This means that the sample burnout rate was equal to the pyroly- 
sis advance rate. It should be noted that as pressure is reduced, the 
buoyant ﬂow velocity and its effect on the ﬂame spread rate are 
reduced; and the ﬂame standoff distance from the solid surface in- 
creases. In an experiment of ﬂow-assisted horizontal spread burn- 
ing paper samples in a wind tunnel in normal gravity [36] , it was 
also found that pyrolysis front spread rate became constant once 
the pyrolysis length reached a steady value. In this case, the hor- 
izontal conﬁguration limited buoyant effects on the ﬂame spread 
rate. 
Apart from these exceptions (when the ﬂame reaches a lim- 
iting length), most reasonable-scale experiments suggest upward 
ﬂame spread on Earth is a continuous growth process. This raises 
the natural question: Will concurrent ﬂame spread in purely-forced 
ﬂow follow the same trend? The answer has important practical 
implications for spacecraft ﬁre safety as it inﬂuences how large of a 
heat release rate can be tolerated in a spacecraft. Up until this cur- 
rent research effort, there were no opportunities to perform large- 
scale combustion experiments in microgravity. Early spacecraft ﬁre 
safety approaches were instead based solely on the results of 1- 
g testing and extrapolations from small-scale, low-gravity testing. 
Consequently, spacecraft ﬁre safety protocols and procedures car- 
ried a level of risk that could not be quantiﬁed. 
1.2. Theoretical background 
To help better understand the pertinent issues for the current 
work, a brief descripti"
