171 
The models calculate a ﬂame spread rate, generally based on the 
pyrolysis front progress, as a function of the ﬂow velocity, pyroly- 
sis length, and time. Based on the characteristics of laminar bound- 
ary layer ﬂames, the models predict an increasing spread rate for 
upward buoyant ﬂow [31] but a constant spread rate for purely- 
forced ﬂow [36,39] . These predictions follow from considerations 
of the heat ﬂux on the solid surface as determined by the ﬂame 
standoff distance, temperature, and length. In laminar upward free 
ﬂow the length of the ﬂame dominates the decrease of the heat 
ﬂux with distance. However, in forced ﬂow both effects cancel each 
other. Regarding the effect of the forced ﬂow velocity, forced ﬂow 
models [27,40,41] predict a spread rate that increases with the ﬂow 
velocity, although experimentally it becomes constant at relatively 
large velocities [34] . 
Another analytical approach to model the concurrent-ﬂow case 
is to view it as an ignition problem with ﬂame spread rate calcu- 
lated as the ratio of a heating length (ﬂame length) to an ignition 
time [1–4,20] . Following this approach, the analytical equation for 
the concurrent ﬂame spread rate over a thin solid is obtained [3] : 
