The predicted dependence of the spread rate on the ﬂow ve- 
locity is somewhere between a one-half power and linear, depend- 
ing on the dependence of the pyrolysis length on the ﬂow veloc- 
ity. Also, since initially the pyrolysis length increases with time, 
the predicted spread rate also increases with time. However, as 
the ﬂame spreads over the solid surface the pyrolysis length may 
become constant [35,36] , in which case Eq. (5) would predict a 
constant spread rate. Although this seems to agree with the ex- 
periments of Markstein and de Ris [35] , their sample tested may 
not have been long enough to verify this result rigorously. In ad- 
dition, it should be kept in mind that Eq. (4) holds for laminar 
ﬂow, whereas for turbulent ﬂow, Eq. (5) would show different de- 
pendences on the problem parameters. Also, the ﬂame tempera- 
ture would be affected by turbulence and ﬂow oxygen concentra- 
tion. Such dependences do not seem straightforward as shown in 
earlier experiments [45] . It is clea"
doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2018.10.012,doi,Flame spread: Effects of microgravity and scale,5,4,"172 
D.L. Urban et al. / Combustion and Flame 199 (2019) 168–182 
Motivated by an experiment [37] where steady, upward ﬂame 
spread was observed over narrow paper samples in reduced 
pressure, a modeling effort [40] was initiated to look for steady 
solutions over thin solids. The two dimensional model examined 
low-speed forced ﬂow, which is more relevant for microgravity ap- 
plications. In order to speed up the computation, steady equations 
were discretized and iteratively solved, with the ﬂame spread rate 
being an eigenvalue to the problem. The steady formulation neces- 
sitated assuming complete fuel burnout, because any leftover inert 
material would be left behind (see the right part of Fig. 1 ) and 
the ﬂame would effectively move into an ever-thickening bound- 
ary layer making a steady solution intractable. Hence the model 
assumes no inert in the fuel and a one-step, zeroth-order py- 
rolysis reaction so the solid can burn out completely. A ﬂame- 
ﬁxed coordinate system effectively pins the ﬂame in the domain, 
facilitating a steady formulation, and a ﬁnite-rate, gas-phase reac- 
tion is included to address the ﬂame stability and extinction ques- 
tion. This requires an elliptic description and the use of Navier–
Stokes equation [46] in the ﬂame base zone. Because the size of 
this zone varies with ﬂow velocity, a non-dimensional grid is used 
in the numerical solution to assure ﬂame structure resolution. It 
is essential to determine the local solid burning rate at the ﬂame 
base since the solid burning rate is the highest there. For a short 
ﬂame in low-speed forced ﬂow, a large portion of the pyrolysis fuel 
mass is ejected at the ﬂame base, which in turn controls the ﬂame 
length. The model [40] predicts steady spread with a constant lim- 
iting length for wide (2-D) sample. The predicted pyrolysis length 
is found to be proportional to ﬂow speed, and the steady ﬂame 
spread rate is also proportional to the ﬂow speed, consistent with 
the expression in Eq. (6) . 
This early model includes only surface radiation loss and not 
ﬂame radiation. A follow-up two-dimensional model [41] added 
the contribution from gas-phase radiation from CO 2 and H 2 O by 
solving the discrete ordinate radiation transfer equation. The model 
was applied to both low-speed forced ﬂow and low-gravity buoy- 
ant ﬂow. Again, iteration was used to obtain steady solutions. Com- 
pared with the aforementioned results [40] , gas radiation modi- 
ﬁes the ﬂame length, spread rate and ﬂame extinction limit. These 
quantities are found to be very sensitive to both the gas and the 
solid radiation. In the forced-ﬂow computation, ﬂame length in- 
creases when radiation losses are reduced. When both gas and 
surface radiation are eliminated, no steady solution could be ob- 
tained. This suggests that for the two-dimensional adiabatic ﬂame 
(i.e., without radiative losses), either steady spread is not possi- 
ble (even for a thin solid) or the ﬂame is too long for the compu- 
tational domain. The resolution of this question depended on the 
development of an unsteady model. 
Using a two-dimensional transient model in low-speed forced 
ﬂow, the question of limiting length has been investigated for both 
thin and thick solids [47] . With gas and surface radiation included, 
the thin sample reaches steady spread as expected. The diffusion 
ﬂame over the thick sample lengthens initially, but the growth rate 
slows down with time. At the end of the computation, the ﬂame 
tip growth rate is four order of magnitudes smaller than its initial 
value. This suggests that the ﬂame (or pyrolysis) length asymptot- 
ically reaches a limit. Since there is no burnout for the assumed 
thick solid, the ﬂame reaches a stationary non-propagating state. 
Analysis of the heat ﬂuxes in the preheat zone shows that at the 
pyrolysis front, the net surface radiation loss balances with the 
convective input from the gas phase. Thus the net heat ﬂux to the 
solid approaches zero, consistent with the result that the growth 
rate of the pyrolysis front was very small. This numerical exper- 
iment supports a previously-advanced argument that for a wide, 
thick solid to reach a limiting length, suﬃcient surface radiative 
loss is needed at the pyrolysis front to balance the convective heat 
input from the ﬂame [28] . For a narrow sample, the lateral mixing 
and cooling from ambient air through the edge of the ﬂame can be 
an additional or even dominant factor. 
For a thin solid, the numerical model predicted a faster pyrol- 
ysis front spread rate than that of the pyrolysis base, i.e. ﬂame 
length increase. However, the rate of pyrolysis front advance slows 
down as the ﬂame lengthens, and eventually a steady spread with 
a constant ﬂame length is reached. The ﬂow entrance length ef- 
fect is also signiﬁcant for a fuel which leaves behind an inert ma- 
terial after the ﬂame passes. As will be shown, a short entrance 
length affects the velocity at the ﬂame base, and this creates a 
ﬂame length overshoot that will be expounded upon later. 
While a number steady spread experiments over thin solids in 
both buoyant and forced ﬂows have been carried out, the possibil- 
ity of a nearly-stationary, non-growing ﬂame over a thick solid was 
seldom reported. There was one normal gravity experiment burn- 
ing several 120-cm-long wood samples having thicknesses ranging 
from 3.2 to 25.4 mm [48] . In the absence of externally applied ra- 
diation, the upward ﬂame stops growing before reaching the top 
of the sample, thus attaining a limiting length. Because the de- 
crease of convection and the increasing inﬂuence of radiation, the 
possibility of reaching a limiting length is expected to be greater 
in lower-speed ﬂow in microgravity. However, that requires both a 
long test duration and a large enough sample to test this hypothe- 
sis. This is one objective of the present work. 
