Figure 2 illustrates the placing of the experimental ﬂow tunnel 
in the Cygnus vehicle adjacent to the International Space Station. 
The vehicles are launched to the ISS with Saﬃre stowed onboard. 
Saﬃre stayed in the resupply vehicle and remained dormant while 
the vehicle was berthed with the ISS and its supplies were un- 
loaded. After all supplies had been transferred, the crew carefully 
stowed trash in the resupply vehicle, leaving adequate free vol- 
ume to conduct the Saﬃre experiments. Once the vehicle departed 
ISS, the Saﬃre tests were completed and all data was relayed to 
Earth. Finally, the vehicle was guided for destructive reentry into 
the Earth’s atmosphere. 
2.2. Experimental design 
Three Saﬃre experiments have been conducted on the Cygnus 
spacecraft so far, with Saﬃre-1, -2 and -3 taking place on CRS 
OA-6 (Commercial Resupply Services Orbital ATK-6), CRS OA-5 and 
CRS OA-7, respectively. Saﬃre-1 and -3 burned a large piece of 
a composite, cotton-ﬁberglass fabric in different airﬂow velocities. 
Two smaller samples of the same material were burned as part of 
Saﬃre-2. Also included in Saﬃre-2 was a ﬂat PMMA slab. Although 
concurrent ﬂame spread was the primary objective of the experi- 
ments, opposed spread tests were also conducted. The full details 
of the Saﬃre experimental set-up can be found in Jomaas et al. 
[50] , so only a brief description is given in the following. 
The hardware, shown in Fig. 3 was a rectangular ﬂow tunnel 
measuring 46 cm x 51 cm in cross section and 109 cm in length. 
In Saﬃre-1 and -3, the fuel sample was a thin cotton-ﬁberglass 
composite fabric measuring 94 cm long by 40.6 cm wide and was 
mounted on a metal frame in the center of the ﬂow tunnel. The 
entrance ﬂow velocity was 20 cm/s for the Saﬃre-1 and 25 cm/s 
for the Saﬃre-2 experiments. The mass of cotton in the sample 
was 52 g which accounted for 75% of the total mass of the fab- 
ric (area density 18.05 mg/cm 2 ). This fuel loading was an order of 
magnitude larger than anything studied to date (both in width and 
length, so two orders of magnitude in area), and due to its sig- 
niﬁcant size, it consumed 1.5% of the available oxygen in the ve- 
hicle. The role of the non-combustible matrix of ﬁberglass (25% 
by weight) was to prevent the fabric from cracking and curling, 
as such behavior would destroy the symmetry of the ﬂame and 
complicate the data analysis and subsequent model optimization. 
This custom-made combustible material, called ‘SIBAL fabric’ in 
earlier work, has been used in previous small-scale tests including 
the BASS series aboard ISS [11] . Using a fabric with a large cot- 
ton content has practical applicability since crew clothing is pre- 
dominantly cotton. Note that an upstream boundary layer will be 
formed because of the presence of inert ﬁberglass and unburnt 
cotton residues, as depicted in Fig. 1 (right). 
The large samples installed for Saﬃre-1 and -3 are shown in 
the left image of Fig. 3 . The smaller samples (nine samples, each 
29 × 5 cm) for Saﬃre-2 are mounted in cutouts in a metal sample 
card, shown in the right image of Fig. 3 . Of special note, Samples 
5 and 6 are the same SIBAL fabric used in Saﬃre-1 and -3. During 
each test, a uniform ﬂow with designated magnitude is introduced 
at the tunnel entrance. 
Saﬃre-1 was performed in 21.7% O 2 at a tunnel entrance ﬂow 
speed of 20 cm/s. Saﬃre-3 was performed in 21.2% O 2 at 25 cm/s 
ﬂow. Saﬃre-2 was performed in 22.1% O 2 at a tunnel entrance ﬂow 
speed of 20 cm/s and 25 cm/s for Samples 5 and 6, respectively. 
For Saﬃre-2, the SIBAL fabric samples were placed in the sample 
card in positions 5 and 6 as shown in Fig. 3 . Ignition for the fab- 
ric samples was caused by a resistively-heated hot wire (29-gage 
Kanthal®, 0.286 mm diameter, 3.85 A, powered for 8 s). 
The samples in both Saﬃre-1 and -3 were instrumented with 
ignitors on both ends so concurrent and opposed ﬂow tests could 
be performed. Since opposed spread reaches steady state within 
a short distance ( ∼10 cm), only a small portion of the long sam- 
ples were allocated to the opposed spread. Turning off the ﬂow 
quenched the ﬂame before the second igniter was ﬁred. 
Two cameras were used to record the burns at 30 frames per 
second, with each camera imaging just over half of the sample. 
The ﬂow tunnel was generally dark to aid ﬂame imaging. However, 
the fuel sample was illuminated with a green LED light periodi- 
cally (for 0.4 s every 2 s) so that the pyrolysis char front (dark zone 
on the sample) could be seen. Four radiometers measured front 
and backside radiation coming from the ﬂame and fuel. Each ra- 
diometer covered just over half of the sample, with two on each 
side to provide an indication of symmetry between the front and 
back halves of the ﬂame. Six thermocouples were arrayed to pro- 
vide temperature measurements at key locations on and near the 
fuel. Oxygen concentration, carbon dioxide, and pressure were also 
recorded just upstream of the fuel in the ﬂow tunnel. In addition 
to the Saﬃre instrumentation, the vehicle provided smoke detector 
data. 
3. Experimental results and discussion 
The burning of the two large fabric samples (Saﬃre-1 and 
Saﬃre-3), the two smaller fabric samples (Saﬃre-2), and one thick 
PMMA sample (Saﬃre-2) are presented. The overall outcomes of"
