Since the ﬂame tip was very dim, tracking the pyrolysis front 
from the lit images provided a more reliable measure of the 
ﬂame spread. An individual ﬂame image (upper right) is pre- 
sented in Fig. 4 , showing the location of the pyrolysis front, the 
burnout contour at the gaseous ﬂame base, and a smoldering zone 
where glowing, orange spots of surface char oxidation occur spo- 
radically after the gas-phase ﬂame has passed. In addition, the 
green-illuminated sample is superimposed to give a sense of the 
scale. The distance between the pyrolysis front and the ﬂame base 
burnout location is taken as the pyrolysis length (the length of the 
smoldering zone is not included). It should be noted that the visi- 
ble ﬂame length is of the same order as the pyrolysis length [51] . 
We also note that from the images in Fig. 4 , the pyrolysis front 
and pyrolysis base are roughly ﬂat except near the two edges of 
the sample. The spread is essentially two-dimensional for this wide 
sample. The pyrolysis front and burnout front (pyrolysis base) were 
tracked along the center line of the sample. The pyrolysis length is 
deduced from the difference of the two curves. The spread veloc- 
ities of the pyrolysis front and of the pyrolysis base can be visu- 
alized from the slope of the pyrolysis tracking data. Initially the 
pyrolysis front moves very fast. The pyrolysis base starts moving 
after the sample burns out, but at a slower rate. This is the growth 
period where the ﬂame length increases rapidly. The initial rapid 
advance of the pyrolysis front for the short ﬂame is due to a small 
ﬂame standoff at the pyrolysis front. Eventually the pyrolysis front 
slows down and matches the burnout rate at the pyrolysis base, 
and steady spread results. 
The progress of the pyrolysis front and pyrolysis base (burn- 
out) shown in Fig. 4 has similar characteristics to that observed 
in normal gravity with a different thin cotton textile [35] and a ﬁl- 
ter paper [36] . However, instead of a monotonic approach to steady 
state, Fig. 4 shows that there is an overshoot of the ﬂame or pyrol- 
ysis length which peaks at about 30 s. The overshoot region last for 
approximately 100 s before a quasi-steady 2 state is reached. This 
was initially a surprising result, but can be explained based on the 
entrance-ﬂow boundary layer effect [47,52] . Referring to the illus- 
tration in Fig. 1 (right), an upstream boundary layer develops over 
a thin solid if there is inert or fuel remaining after the ﬂame pas- 
sage, which is the case for the SIBAL fabric used in the Saﬃre-1 
2 Here, “steady state” is qualiﬁed to acknowledge the fact that as the ﬂame moves 
downstream, it is in a gradually thickening boundary layer and sees a slowly chang- 
ing effective ﬂow velocity at the ﬂame base. 
experiment. Assuming a Blasius proﬁle, the boundary layer thick- 
ness increases very fast near the leading edge of the sample or the 
sample card (see Fig. 3 for the sample placement) but away from 
the leading edge, the growth of boundary layer is very gradual. De- 
tailed modeling [52] shows that the Saﬃre-1 ﬂame is expected to 
be inside the velocity boundary layer (also shown later in Fig. 11 ). 
At the start of the burn, the ﬂame is close to the entrance and sees 
a larger effective air velocity in the thin boundary layer, hence a 
large burning rate and a longer ﬂame result. When the ﬂame base 
moves downstream, the boundary layer growth slow down and 
a quasi-steady spread with lower spread rate is found. Although 
the boundary layer effect has been observed in earlier work and 
is still under present investigation for ﬂame spread in opposed 
ﬂow [21,22,53,54] , its effect on concurrent ﬂame spread has sel- 
dom been mentioned. Part of the reason for this is due to the 
construction of the SIBAL fuel, which leaves behind an unburned 
solid after the ﬂame passes, and therefore maintains the boundary 
layer initiation point. A thin paper, in contrast, might completely 
be consumed so that the boundary layer never forms upstream of 
the burnout point. Still, the current results are important as they 
provide understanding of boundary layer effects on ﬂame spread 
for more general scenarios involving real materials that might be 
encountered in an actual ﬁre. 
With the ﬂame reaching a quasi-steady state suﬃciently down- 
stream of the entrance, the implication is that the ﬂow veloc- 
ity proﬁle at the ﬂame base changes slowly with spread dis- 
tance. In the quasi-steady region, the spread rate is 1.75 mm/s 
and the pyrolysis length is about 4 cm—a surprisingly short 
ﬂame given our experience with upward-spreading (concurrent- 
ﬂow) ﬂames on Earth [35, 36, e.g.]. Finally, there is a slight in- 
crease of pyrolysis length near the end of the experiment. The 
reason is unclear, although since the ﬂame is now near the 
end of the tunnel, local ﬂow acceleration may be occurring as 
the airstream begins to neck-down as it approaches the exit 
fans. 
For Saﬃre-3, the large sample was burned in the higher ﬂow 
speed of 25 cm/s. The pyrolysis tracking results are qualitatively 
similar to those presented above for Saﬃre-1. The steady pyroly- 
sis length is 5 cm (vs. 4 cm in Saﬃre-1). The steady spread rate is 
2.32 mm/s (vs. 1.75 mm/s in Saﬃre-1). The overshoot of pyrolysis 
length reaches 8.2 cm (vs. 6.2 cm in Saﬃre-1). The larger values of 
the spread rate, and the pyrolysis length and its overshoot are the 
result of the larger ﬂow velocity (25 cm/s versus 20 cm/s)."
