Figure 5 shows the temperature measurements as a function 
of time for Saﬃre-1 and the locations of the six thermocouples. 
Two sets of thermocouples were deployed in two stream-wise lo- 
cations at different heights from the fuel surface as indicated. The 
two surface thermocouple traces are essentially identical, suggest- 
ing steady state is reached, consistence with the pyrolysis tracking 
data in Fig. 4 . 
Temperature traces as a function of distance can be constructed 
using the steady ﬂame spread rate to perform a coordinate trans- 
formation. This is shown in Fig. 6 with x = 0 being at the pyrolysis 
base. It is clear that the two surface temperature proﬁles are essen- 
tially identical. The pyrolysis plateau is around 4 cm long, which 
is consistent with the pyrolysis length measured from the imag- 
ing data. The surface temperature data (0 cm height) along with 
the gas-phase data at four heights provide a rough reconstruction 
of the 2-D temperature ﬁeld of the ﬂame. Although the data is 
sparse, the following observations can be made. In the region just 
upstream of the ﬂame base (0 < x < 0.5 cm) the temperature peaks 
between 0.3 and 0.8 cm above the surface. Just downstream of the 
ﬂame base ( −1 cm < x < 0) the temperature peaks between 0.8 and 
1 cm above the surface. Further downstream ( −6 cm < x < −1 cm), 
the temperature peaks between 1 and 2.5 cm above the surface. 
In the far downstream thermal plume region (x < −6 cm), the 
recorded temperatures are relatively low, suggesting a short 
gaseous ﬂame and implying substantial ﬂame radiative loss which 
has been shown theoretically to shorten the ﬂame in microgravity 
[41] . The highest temperature recorded (without radiative loss cor- 
rection) by the thermocouples at heights 0.8 and 1 cm is 1080 °C. 
The actual maximum temperature should be slightly higher but it 
is diﬃcult to determine by how much. 
Figure 7 presents the readings from the wide-angle radiometers 
indicated in Fig. 3 . Both Saﬃre-1 readings at 20 cm/s, and Saﬃre-3 
readings at 25 cm/s are shown. For both cases, the readings from 
the downstream radiometers are similar to those upstream but 
the maximum is slightly higher. This is reasonable since there is a 
slow heat-up of the tunnel with time and because of the way the 
ﬂame is stabilized (anchored on the upstream end, and fanning out 
downstream). As a result, the downstream radiometers are able to 
view more of the projected ﬂame area. At the higher airﬂow speed, 
the emitted radiation is higher indicating that the ﬂame is longer 
and stronger, consistent with the measured pyrolysis lengths and 
spread rates. The back radiometer traces (not shown) yielded vir- 
tually identical data, demonstrating ﬂame symmetry from front to 
back. 
The test results for Sample 5 in Saﬃre-2 are shown in Fig. 8 . 
This is the same fabric material used in Saﬃre-1 and -3 but is 
smaller (29 cm long x 5 cm wide). The ﬂow entrance velocity is 
the same as in Saﬃre-1 (20 cm/s) but the O 2 % is marginally higher 
(22.1% in Saﬃre-2 vs. 21.7% in Saﬃre-1). The ﬂame images on the 
right side of Fig. 8 show a rounded ﬂame tip; suggesting lateral 
mixing and heat loss effect for this narrower sample. Tracking 
along the centerline is presented on the left side in Fig. 8 . Com- 
pared with that in Fig. 4 , the pyrolysis length shows only a slight 
overshoot. This can be explained by the location of Sample 5 in 
Saﬃre-2. Figure 3 shows that sample 5 is located in the middle 
of the metal sample card. Since the velocity boundary layer starts 
at the leading edge of the metal sample card which is more than 
30 cm upstream, the ﬂow over the sample is already in the slow 
growing part of the boundary layer. The effective velocity that the 
ﬂame base sees only changes mildly over the entire burning du- 
ration and the spreading is quasi-steady. Detailed modeling of this 
test veriﬁes this interpretation [52] . 
