In the quasi-steady period, the pyrolysis length is 4.5 cm and 
the spread rate is 2.03 mm/s, both slightly greater compared to the 
values for the wide sample in Saﬃre-1. At ﬁrst glance this appears 
to be counterintuitive, although there is a slightly higher oxygen 
concentration. Modeling work in [52] suggests that side diffusion 
of oxygen from the edges of the narrow sample boosts the burning 
rate around the ﬂame base zone, thus overcoming the downstream 
cooling effect and producing a slightly larger pyrolysis length on 
the centerline. 
4. Numerical model and computational results 
A transient three-dimensional model based on [11,55] is refor- 
mulated to simulate the Saﬃre-1 and Saﬃre-2 experiment. Using 
the actual tunnel dimensions and sample locations, high-ﬁdelity 
geometric simulations are conﬁgured. The physical model consists 
of the fully three-dimensional, transient Navier–Strokes equations 
of mass, momentum, energy and species conservation. One-step, 
second-order overall ﬁnite-rate Arrhenius kinetics are adopted for 
the gas-phase reaction. The gas radiation transfer equation is 
solved by an S-N discrete ordinate method with CO 2 and H 2 O 
as participating species. Radiation absorption coeﬃcients for the 
gas mixture are based on local Planck mean absorption coeﬃcient 
with a correction factor [56] . The emittance of the solid fuel varies 
with the local sample thickness according to formula given in [57] . 
These data were obtained from measurement using FTIR in an inte- 
grated sphere. Radiation has a large inﬂuence on the microgravity 
low-ﬂow-speed ﬂames, as elaborated later. 
In the solid fuel, heat conduction in all three dimensions is in- 
cluded in the energy conservation equation. Thermo-gravimetric 
analysis (TGA) data for the SIBAL fabric were used to deduce the 
pyrolysis kinetics [11] . A two-step decomposition model was for- 
mulated. In the ﬁrst and major step, 80% of the combustibles were 
evolved in the temperature range 30 0–40 0 °C, and the remaining 
20% were evolved at higher temperature range from 400 to 675 °C. 
This second step is believed related to the surface smoldering pro- 
cess in the experiment. Smoldering is not included in the present 
modeling work. 
Another feature of the model is the resolution of the ﬂame sta- 
bilization zone near the pyrolysis base with small computational 
grids. As shown in Fig. 6 , the temperature gradients are very steep 
there. Since the ﬂame base moves, a grid distribution scheme was 
developed to follow the ﬂame and make most eﬃcient use of the 
ﬁner grid structure there [58] . 
Before the experiment was conducted, the model accurately 
predicted the ﬂame spread rate of Saﬃre-1 to within 20% but the 
ﬂame length was over predicted. Overall, the computed results 
were able to reproduce most of the experimental ﬁndings for the 
thin SIBAL fabrics in Saﬃre-1 and -2. The model yields additional 
information and insight of the spreading processes including the 
effect of the upstream boundary layer. Many such ﬁndings have 
been reported in [52] . Only a few selected computational results 
are given below. 
Figure 9 is computed three-dimensional ﬂame proﬁles for 
Saﬃre-1 after quasi-steady state has been reached. The ﬂame is as- 
sumed to be symmetric with respect to the centerline. The ﬂame 
cutaway along the centerline shows the fuel vapor reaction rate 
contors. Two contours of fuel fraction remaining in the solid (0.95 
and 0.2) are also shown. The 0.95 fuel fraction contour is close to 
the pyrolysis front. The 0.2 contour is near the ﬂame base. The 
ﬂame base stays close to the 0.2 contour because the that por- 
tion of the fuel remaining evolves too slowly to support a gaseous 
ﬂame (see the previous comments on the pyrolysis kinetics). In 
the experiment, this portion of the fuel is likely to smolder (ev- 
ident as glowing orange spots in the experiment after the ﬂame 
passes). Smoldering is not modeled in the present work. On the 
right side, the surface temperature contours are plotted. Also plot- 
ted are the solid mass pyrolysis rate (mg/cm 2 s) distributions over 
the sample surface. The solid mass burning rate in the pyrolysis 
zone is very non-uniform along the ﬂow direction. A large peak 
is located just under the ﬂame base where the ﬂame standoff is 
the smallest and the heat transfer rate to the fuel is the great- 
est. To determine the ﬂame length, especially for short ﬂames in 
microgravity low-speed ﬂow, it is necessary to resolve this ﬂame 
base zone as it provides the majority of fuel mass ﬂux into the 
ﬂame. 
Figure 10 presents the corresponding computed three- 
dimensional ﬂame proﬁles for Sample 5 in Saﬃre-2, after 
quasi-steady state is reached. For this narrower sample, the py- 
rolysis front is curved and the ﬂame is highly three-dimensional, 
consistent with the experimental ﬁndings."
