To get more insight into the heat transfer mechanisms on ﬂame 
spread, a plot of the the individual heat transfer modes is given 
in Fig. 11 for the Saﬃre-1 case. Along the centerline on the sam- 
ple surface, the plotted distributions include: convective heat ﬂux 
(Q c ), radiative ﬂux from ﬂame to fuel surface (Q r,in ), radiative heat 
ﬂux emission from the surface (Q r,out ), net radiative ﬂux on sur- 
face (Q r, net = Q r,in – Q r, out ), and the net total heat ﬂux (Q net = Q c 
– Q r,net ). The scale shown is meant to emphasize heat balance in 
the preheating zone which is for x ≥8 cm. One sees that convec- 
tive heat ﬂux (Q c ) decreases rapidly downstream, due to a combi- 
nation of boundary layer thickness increase and temperature de- 
crease. The ﬂame radiative input which has comparable magnitude 
at the pyrolysis front, decreases slower than the convective ﬂux so 
that there is more heat input from ﬂame radiation than from con- 
vection. The radiative emmisive loss from surface (Q r,out ) is large 
in the preheat zone such that the the net radiation on the surface 
Q r,net is negative, i.e. a loss. The net total heat ﬂux (Q net ), which 
determines the ﬂame spread rate, is a small quantity, being the 
difference of the two large quantities, Q c and Q r,net . 
This detailed comparison suggests the importance of radiative 
transfer on ﬂame spreading in microgravity. Previously, It was 
thought that the low velocity regime in which radiation is dom- 
inant occurs when the ﬂow velocity is less than 10 cm/s [56] . This 
may be derived from studies in opposed ﬂow and in concurrent 
ﬂow where there is no upstream boundary layer effect. As seen 
from the top plot in Fig. 11 , the present ﬂame is immersed inside 
the boundary layer where the ﬂame, in particular the ﬂame base, 
sees a lower ﬂow velocity than in the free stream (a velocity pro- 
ﬁle is plotted in Fig. 11 , top, slightly upstream of the ﬂame base). 
The smaller effective ﬂow velocity helps to amplify the radiation 
inﬂuence. 
To further test the importance of radiation, the following 
numerical experiment was performed. Using a two-dimensional 
model (a reduced version of the three-dimensional version), for the 
wide sample experiment in Saﬃre-1, but with both the gas and the 
surface radiation turned off. The computed ﬂame was found to ac- 
celerate with time. The prolysis front reaches the end of the sam- 
ple (94 cm long) in 20 s. The pyrolysis length increases continuosly 
to 50 cm in 20 s. This results seems to be consistent with previ- 
ous effort using steady two-dimensional adiabatic ﬂame without 
radiative loss that steady solution is not readily obtainable [41] . 
Although we cannot be completely sure what will happen if the 
sample length is much longer, the big discrepency between the 
adiabatic ﬂame simulation and the present experimental results 
strongly underscores the importance of radiative loss in micrograv- 
ity ﬂames [13] . 
5. Comparison with other related works 
5.1. Comparison with upward spread over SIBAL fabric in normal 
gravity 
Upward spread tests in normal gravity using the same SIBAL 
fabric have been conducted. Long, narrow samples (2, 5, 7, or"
