8.3 cm wide; 150 cm long) were burned in normal air [59] and an 
unusual behavior was reported. The ﬂame for a 5-cm-wide sample, 
for example, quickly lengthens after ignition. At 20 s, the pyrolysis 
front spreads about 80 cm from the ignition point and the pyroly- 
sis length is 35 cm. Then the ﬂame on one side of the fuel sample 
retreats downstream and blows off. The remaining one-sided ﬂame 
gets shorter and weaker (color becomes more bluish). It continues 
to spread upward, reaching a new steady-state spread rate about 
half of what it was before one side of the ﬂame blew off, and 
having a pyrolysis length of around 20 cm. This is a self-induced 
blow-off phenomenon. It also occurred for the 2-cm-wide samples 
but not for 7-cm or wider samples. 
A separate large scale experiment using SIBAL fabric was car- 
ried out in an environmental chamber in which the pressure can 
be varied [60] . The sample size is 50 cm wide and 100 cm long. The 
ﬂame continuously increases in length at normal pressure. When 
the pressure is suﬃciently reduced (0.442 atm.), steady spread is 
achieved with a limiting pyrolysis length of approximately 34 cm 
and a steady spread rate of 3.4 cm/s. Another set of experiments 
at reduced pressure are those of [44] , although the size of the ex- 
periments is small, comparable with those of Saﬃre 2. The results 
show that as the pressure is reduced the pyrolysis progress, the 
pyrolysis length, and the spread rate approach those measured in 
microgravity in the Saﬃre 2 experiments. These and other stud- 
ies using a variety of thin solid fuels and sizes [28,35,37] demon- 
strate that steady spread is possible for upwardly-spreading buoy- 
ant ﬂames in normal gravity. Steady spread is facilitated if the 
ﬂame length is kept in check, for example: by entrainment of cool- 
ing air via lateral mixing (for a narrow sample); by increased heat 
