loss (for a one-sided ﬂame); and by lowering pressure (reducing 
heat feedback from the ﬂame to the fuel). In microgravity, a re- 
duced convective ﬂow makes the effect of radiative heat loss more 
prominent. The limiting ﬂame length and the spread rate are an 
order of magnitude smaller than those we see in normal gravity. 
To compare with the surface temperature data from the Saﬃre- 
1 experiment, the measurement from an upward spreading ﬂame 
in normal gravity is given in Fig. 12 . The surface thermocouple 
is located 15 cm from the bottom of the sample in an incipient 
ﬂame. Figure 12 shows that there is a pyrolysis zone where the 
temperature is relatively ﬂat (75–78 s). The small peak at approx- 
imately 81 s is the ﬁberglass/fuel residue temperature heated by 
the ﬂame near the ﬂame base. The slow temperature decay after 
the peak is due to the extended smoldering zone. By comparison, 
in forced ﬂow, the surface temperature over the pyrolysis zone in- 
creases continuously toward the ﬂame base as shown in Figs. 5 and 
6 without a prominent ﬂat region. This affects the burning rate dis- 
tribution and is thought to be a consequence of the ﬂame shape 
difference in the two different ﬂow environments. In forced ﬂow, 
the ﬂame opens up, or fans out, with increasing ﬂame standoff
downstream. In buoyant ﬂow, the ﬂame downstream tends to stay"
