due to the increasing rate of induced ﬂow velocity with distance. 
While the local burning rate is not measured, the model computa- 
tion shows large differences in its distribution. As mentioned be- 
fore, burning rate in microgravity can have large peak near the 
ﬂame base, especially for short ﬂames. 
Comparing spreading ﬂames of similar size in forced and buoy- 
ant ﬂows can further reveal the more subtle inﬂuences of ﬂow ﬁeld 
on these ﬂames. This was done in [61] numerically for a ﬂame in 
low-speed forced ﬂow and low gravity buoyant ﬂow. Differences in 
the burning rate and surface heat ﬂux distributions for these two 
ﬂow cases were attributed to the different character of the velocity 
ﬁeld. 
5.2. Comparison with other microgravity spread results using SIBAL 
fabric 
Figure 13 compares the result of the current Saﬃre experiments 
with other microgravity experiments using the same composite 
cotton fabric (SIBAL fuel) but with different scales and ﬂow speeds. 
These include both concurrent and opposed ﬂows and ﬂow tun- 
nel of different sizes. The data for the 1-cm and 2-cm wide sam- 
ples were obtained in the BASS experiment using a small tunnel 
with dimension 7.6 cm x 7.6 cm x 12 cm [7] . Looking at the concur- 
rent spread data (all taken in the quasi-steady state), Fig. 13 shows 
that the spread rate of the 2-cm-wide sample decreases approx- 
imately linearly with air velocity and quenching was observed to 
occur when the air velocity is too low. The linear increase of the 
spread rate with the ﬂow velocity conﬁrms the predicted trend 
from both analytical and numerical models [36,40] . The quenching 
of the ﬂame at low ﬂow velocities is also predicted in the model 
[40] . In low-speed ﬂow, oxygen supply is reduced and the ﬂame 
standoff increases. The reduced convection leads to a low-intensity 
ﬂame which ampliﬁes the radiative losses. The losses lower the 
ﬂame temperature and leads to ﬂame quenching. 
All other concurrent spread data also show that spread rate 
increases with airﬂow velocity. However, with just two data 
points in the Saﬃre tests, it cannot be fully proven that the 
relationship is linear for larger samples. The 1-cm spread rates 
are below the 2-cm data, as expected due to width effects on the 
spread rate [29,47,62] . But for the Saﬃre samples having widths 
of 40.6 and 5 cm, the spread rates are even slower. This seemingly 
unexpected result is due to the fact that the BASS tunnel was sub- 
stantially smaller than the tunnel used for the Saﬃre experiments. 
The smaller tunnel facilitated an effective ﬂow speed increase in 
the ﬂame because of hot gas expansion. 
Figure 13 also depicts the opposed-ﬂow spread rates. The 2- 
cm-width data have a dependence of the opposed ﬂame spread 
on air velocity ﬁrst increasing, reaching a peak at around 15 cm/s 
of airﬂow and then decreasing. This trend has been shown previ- 
ously [3,7,22] , and verify the predictions from theoretical models 
[3,51,53,63,64] . The ﬂame quenching at low ﬂow velocity is due 
to radiation heat losses and low oxygen diffusion to the ﬂame, 
and the ﬂame blow-off at large ﬂow velocities is caused by low 
Damköhler number effects on the ﬂame chemical kinetics. This 
trend can only be observed in microgravity because at normal 
gravity buoyancy prevents ﬂame spread testing at low forced ﬂow 
velocities [22] . The present 40.6 cm wide data were slightly below 
the 2-cm curve and seems to have a stronger dependence on the 
ﬂow velocity. Again, the location of the opposed ﬂame is about 
85 cm downstream of the tunnel entrance. The reduced velocity in 
the boundary layer that the ﬂame experiences at that location in 
comparison with that experienced by the smaller samples can be 
substantial. 
Comparing the concurrent and the opposed spread data for 
the 40.6-cm-width Saﬃre samples, it is clear that the concurrent 
ﬂame spread is faster (1.8 mm/s vs. 1.3 mm/s in 20 cm/s ﬂow; and 
2.3 mm/s vs. 0.98 mm/s in 25 cm/s ﬂow). However, this may not 
always be the case. At suﬃciently low airﬂow, a crossover can 
occur, i.e. opposed spread becomes faster than concurrent spread 
[18,22,53] . Based on Fig. 13 , the crossover velocity appears to be 
around 15 cm/s. The boundary layer entrance ﬂow effect and the 
crossover phenomena amplify the difference between buoyant up- 
ward spread and concurrent spread in purely forced ﬂow. In buoy- 
ant spread, upward is always faster than downward, at least in 
Earth gravity."
