5.3. Limiting length for a thick solid burning in concurrent ﬂow 
We have seen that spreading ﬂames in forced-concurrent ﬂow 
and in upward-buoyant ﬂow reach a limiting length. In the initial 
ﬂame growth phase, the ﬂame tip spreads faster than the ﬂame 
base initially but slows down as ﬂame lengthens. When the speed 
of the ﬂame base catches up with that of the ﬂame tip, a steady 
spread with a constant ﬂame length is reached. So steady spread 
is controlled by the movement of the ﬂame base. The ﬂame base 
moves when the burning sample can no longer generate enough 
pyrolyzate to support a gaseous ﬂame. This can occur when the 
solid completely burns out or when the pyrolysis rate is greatly re- 
duced by more complex pyrolysis kinetics such as those for a cot- 
ton sample. For the present SIBAL cotton-ﬁberglass sample, there 
is approximately 20% of the combustible left after the gas-phase 
ﬂame passes. But a slower surface smoldering reaction can follow. 
For thin solids, steady spread and associated limiting length are 
controlled by the ﬂame base movement, which has been loosely 
referred to as the sample ‘burnout’ process. The average heat ﬂux 
over the pyrolysis zone is thus important to determine the ﬂame 
length [60] . But, for short ﬂames in forced ﬂow, the spatial distri- 
butions of heat ﬂux and local burning rate are highly non-uniform. 
As demonstrated in Figs. 9 –11 , pyrolysis near the ﬂame base zone 
can dominate. From a theoretical point of view, resolving the struc- 
ture of this zone is crucial. 
If there is no sample burnout (or very slow burnout) such as 
the case with a suﬃciently thick solid, can a limiting length be 
reached? If so, this would be termed a nearly-stationary, non- 
growing ﬂame. As we mentioned in introduction, an earlier work 
[28] suggested this possibility by considering the heat balance in 
the preheat region. A transient numerical model [47] has also 
shown that radiative loss can balance convection in the down- 
stream region so that the pyrolysis tip spread rate asymptotes to 
zero (in the theoretical model, no solid burnout was assumed 3 ). To 
verify this possibility, we need a reasonably long and thick solid 
sample and suﬃcient test duration. Sample 9 from Saﬃre-2 ﬁts 
this requirement. 
Sample 9 is a 1-cm-thick cast PMMA sample measuring 5 cm 
(width) x 29 cm (length). The leading edge of the sample is slightly 
tapered to facilitate ignition. Flame occurs on both sides of the 
sample in the test. 
Figure 14 shows the time tracking of ﬂame tip and ﬂame base. 
The top-view ﬂame images are given in the inset. After an initial 
ﬂame length spike due to the igniter being on, the ﬂame tip re- 
treats to 1.2 cm and the ﬂame growth process starts. The tip speed 
slows down with time. The ﬂame tip approaches a limiting posi- 
tion after about 750 s. The test was terminated at 900 s (15 min). 
The ﬂame base starts a slow movement at ∼400 s. The movement 
is small and at 900 s, it is located at 5 mm. Note that the PMMA 
sample is tapered at the leading edge and PMMA may experience 
surface regression during the test duration. The limiting length is 
about 9 cm, slightly longer than those for the thin fabrics. Note that 
a previous microgravity experiment using a PMMA rod (0.476 cm 
radius) had a similar outcome, i.e. reaching a steady non-growing 
ﬂame. The ﬂame length increased nearly linearly with ﬂow velocity 
[66] . Limiting length is a function of many parameters including, 
ﬂow velocity, oxygen percentage, pressure and others. 
6. Conclusions 
For the ﬁrst time, large-scale and long-duration ﬂame spread 
in microgravity was studied experimentally. This was enabled 
3 By assuming a steady pyrolysis zone without radiation, a steady spread solution 
was obtained for a thick solid [65] . 
through a unique set of experiments conducted on an unmanned 
spacecraft while in orbit. Large fuel samples consisting of cotton- 
ﬁberglass fabric sheets were burned in a large ﬂow tunnel in two 
microgravity ﬂight experiments. Long-duration tests of a smaller 
fabric sample and a thick PMMA sample were also carried out in a 
separate mission. The main ﬁndings are: 
1. A spreading ﬂame in forced-concurrent ﬂow in micrograv- 
ity can reach a quasi-steady state with a slow spread rate 
and a constant ﬂame length. In the present experiment, with 
a tunnel entrance ﬂow of 20–25 cm/s, the limiting pyrol- 
ysis lengths are about 4–5 cm and spread rate are around 
2 mm/s. A numerical model suggests the importance of ra- 
diative losses for producing these short ﬂames. 
2. In comparison, for the same fabric material, sample size, 
and atmosphere, upward spread is accelerating with increas- 
ing ﬂame length in normal gravity. Steady spread in normal 
gravity can be achieved only when excessive heat loss is in- 
troduced (e.g. by burning only on one side), in low pressure, 
or with narrow samples (for most materials). Even in such 
cases, steady spread in normal gravity has a much longer 
ﬂame and faster spread rate compared to microgravity. 
3. The computed local solid burning rate varies drastically 
along the pyrolysis zone for short microgravity ﬂames, hav- 
ing a large peak close to the ﬂame base. This is also reﬂected 
in the measured surface temperature distribution. In con- 
trast, the surface temperature and presumably the burning 
rate are more uniform across the pyrolysis zone in upward 
spreading ﬂames. 
4. In forced ﬂow, if a boundary layer develops upstream of the 
ﬂame base, the effective ﬂow speed at the height of the con- 
current ﬂame can be lower than the imposed entrance ﬂow. 
This shortens the ﬂame and slows down the spread. How- 
ever, in the slowly-varying portion of the boundary layer 
away from its start, quasi-steady spread can be observed. 
5. A long-duration experiment burning a thick PMMA sample 
shows that the ﬂame in microgravity asymptotically reaches 
a limiting length wi"
