Experimental study of concurrent-ﬂow ﬂame spread over thin solids 
in conﬁned space in microgravity 
Yanjun Li a , Ya-Ting T. Liao a , ∗, Paul V. Ferkul b , Michael C. Johnston b , Charles Bunnell c 
a Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland 44106, OH, United States 
b Universities Space Research Association, NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland 44135, OH, United States 
c ZIN Technologies, Middleburg Heights 44130, OH, United States 
a r t i c l e 
i n f o 
Article history: 
Received 23 July 2020 
Revised 24 December 2020 
Accepted 25 December 2020 
Available online 12 January 2021 
Keywords: 
Concurrent-ﬂow ﬂame spread 
Microgravity combustion 
Conﬁned space 
Flame-wall interactions 
a b s t r a c t 
Concurrent ﬂow ﬂame spread experiments are conducted over thermally thin solid fuels in micrograv- 
ity aboard the International Space Station (ISS) under varying levels of conﬁnement. Samples of cotton 
ﬁberglass blended textile fabric are burned in air ﬂows in a small ﬂow duct. Baﬄes are placed parallel to 
the sample sheet, one on each side symmetrically. The distance between the baﬄes is varied to change 
the conﬁnement of the burning event. Three different materials of baﬄes are used to alter the radia- 
tive boundary conditions of the space that the ﬂame resides: transparent polycarbonate, black anodized 
aluminum, and polished aluminum. In all tests, samples are ignited at the upstream leading edge and 
allowed to burn to completion. The results show that at low ﬂow speeds ( < 17 cm/s), the ﬂame reaches 
a steady state for all tested baﬄe types and baﬄe distances. The spread rates and ﬂame lengths at the 
steady state increase ﬁrst and then decrease when the baﬄe distance decreases, resulting in an opti- 
mal baﬄe distance for ﬂame spread. Furthermore, there exists a limiting baﬄe distance below which the 
ﬂame fails to spread. It is concluded that the conﬁnement imposed by the baﬄes accelerates the ﬂow 
during the combustion thermal expansion and the baﬄes reﬂect ﬂame radiation back to the sample sur- 
face, both of which intensifying the burning. However, the conﬁnement also limits the oxygen supply 
and introduces conductive heat loss away from the ﬂame. At the same baﬄe distance and imposed ﬂow 
speed, ﬂame length and spread rate are largest for polished aluminum baﬄes, and lowest for transparent 
polycarbonate baﬄes. The differences are most prominent at intermediate tested baﬄe distances. While 
the radiative heat feedback from the baﬄes is expected to increase when the baﬄe distance decreases, 
ﬂame length and ﬂame spread rate are similar for all baﬄe types at small baﬄe distances as the com- 
bustion is limited by the reduced oxygen supply. 
