As predicted by Li et al. [10] , the optimal inter-baﬄe distance 
for the ﬂame spread occurs at D = 4.0 cm. The spread rate and 
ﬂame length reach the maximum values for all baﬄe types. Below 
this inter-baﬄe distance, the ﬂame spread rate decreases with the 
inter-baﬄe distance. The decreasing local ﬂow speeds encountered 
by the ﬂame certainly contributes to the decreasing ﬂame spread 
rate. Other effects that may result in the decreased ﬂame spread 
rate at small-baﬄe distances include oxygen starvation and con- 
ductive heat loss to the baﬄes [10] . At D = 1.0 cm, self-sustained 
ﬂame was not achieved and ﬂames extinguished after ignition 
power was off for any baﬄe types. Once again, at this small baﬄe 
distance, local ﬂow near the sample surface is greatly reduced by 
ﬂow viscosity. In addition, ﬂame can act as a pseudo-body and fur- 
