doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2025.114244,doi,Experimental determination of diffusion flame extinction boundaries over a PMMA sphere: The effect of sub-surface solid heating,2,1,"H2/O2/CO2 [5] combustion in stagnation flow. Numerous studies have 
since been performed including radiation for different fuels in both gas 
and liquid flames. Including detailed kinetics in the low temperature 
regime, complex flammability regimes including cool and ‘warm’ flames 
have been identified and the flame responses can be multiple valued as 
illustrated in [6].
Experimental mapping of the entire flammability boundary 
including limits at low flow speed (low strain rate) remains a challenge. 
This is because the magnitude of the flow required for quenching may be 
lower than what is possible in a buoyant induced flow in normal earth 
gravity. Because of the long flow residence time with the possibility of 
near-limit instabilities, the quenching extinction process may take a long 
time. To have a thorough scientific study, a long duration microgravity 
environment is needed. This is evident from recent successful combus­
tion projects using gaseous [7] and liquid fuels [8] in the International 
Space Station (ISS).
Solid fuel combustion testing requires even longer experimental 
durations because of the increased solid thermal inertia. Earlier tests in 
drop towers and airplanes needed to employ very thin cellulosic samples 
[9–11] to study flame spread and extinction limits. Tests using thicker 
solid in the space shuttle demonstrated that the extinction process can be 
very slow [12]. Extinction can be obtained by abrupt changes in test 
parameters such as sudden gravity, velocity, or pressure changes [e.g. 
13–15]. The extinction boundary obtained in these tests may be affected 
by the methodology due to rate-dependent hysteresis in the non-linear 
system and interesting near-limit phenomena may have been 
bypassed, especially for quenching. In long-duration tests, cylindrical 
rod samples were used in the Russian Mir space station [16] and in ISS 
[17]. Samples with wire geometry have been tested and modeled in the 
FLARE project also in ISS [e.g. 18,19].
Combustion of a spherical solid offers rich fluid mechanics features 
that affect heat transfer and combustion characteristics. When placed in 
an airflow stream, there are several distinct regions around the sphere 
exhibiting different fluid mechanics characteristics. In front of the 
sphere, stagnation point flow develops. Flames in this region are locally 
one-dimensional when the flame standoff distance is much smaller than 
the sphere radius, and experimental results can be compared with one- 
dimensional models using flow strain or flame stretch rates. This is also 
the flame stabilization region that controls the extinction characteristics 
of the envelope flame. The existence of the sphere shoulder and wake 
regions, on the other hand, can reveal additional multi-dimensional 
features of the extinction processes [20] and the flame growth 
process. In [21], flame growth and retreat are modeled using a 2-cm 
diameter sphere to compare with microgravity results in the BASS 
project aboard ISS. BASS is a prelude to the present project with less 
instrumentation, diagnostics, and flow control.
To obtain a low stretch diffusion flame in normal Earth gravity, large 
bottom burning has been proposed [22]. Experiment using large PMMA 
sample or burners have been performed [23–25]. Although these low 
buoyant stretch flames have yielded quenching limits in the same range 
predicted in forced flows in microgravity, the near-limit flame charac­
teristics are different: flamelets were found in [23–25] vs. flame pulsa­
tion observed in [20]. It appears that in addition to the local flow strain 
rate, there is an additional flame scale effect on the extinction dynamics.
The present work is from the Solid Fuel Ignition and Extinction – 
Growth and Extinction Limits (SoFIE-GEL) project. SoFIE is a NASA 
sponsored project for solid fuel combustion in microgravity. It consists 
of five sub-projects. GEL (Growth and Extinction) is the first sub-project 
recently carried out in ISS. In GEL, 4-cm diameter PMMA spheres have 
been chosen as the test samples. This sample size is large enough to have 
a well-defined front stagnation flow region that minimizes the flame 
curvature effect but small enough to fit into the test chamber in the 
Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) aboard ISS. For these samples, the 
surface regression distance is limited so that sample shape is approxi­
mately retained after each test. So, the samples can be re-used multiple 
times (typically three). This minimizes the number of samples needed to 
be sent to ISS and also reduces the astronauts’ effort to change samples. 
The sample is also large enough that the disturbance by the multiple 
embedded thermocouples is minimal.
The objectives of the GEL project include (1) to experimentally 
determine the flame growth and the extinction characteristics of a bulk 
solid (PMMA sphere) as a function of flow velocity, oxygen percentage, 
pressure and the degree of sample internal heating in microgravity, and 
(2) to develop a numerical model that can compare microgravity results 
with normal gravity results. The GEL experiment was carried out aboard 
ISS from January 13, 2023, to March 7, 2024. Seventeen PMMA samples 
were used and a total of 54 tests were performed. The cumulative 
microgravity sample burning time is about 7 h. In this paper, experi­
mental extinction limit data of 35 tests at 1-atm pressure are presented. 
Because of the unique ability to vary the flow velocity during the tests 
(down to the sub-buoyant value), the time sequences of several tests will 
be presented to highlight the flame response in these unusual micro­
gravity experiments. The solid temperature measurements from selected 
cases are chosen to show the effect of solid sub-surface heating on 
extinction.
2. Experiment setup [20]
The experiment is conducted using the SoFIE insert (main compo­
nents shown in Fig.1) which is placed inside the Combustion Integrated 
Rack (CIR) aboard the ISS."
doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2025.114244,doi,Experimental determination of diffusion flame extinction boundaries over a PMMA sphere: The effect of sub-surface solid heating,3,2,"the sensor located at the flow duct entrance. The SoFIE insert has a 
square flow duct (13.3 cm x 13.3 cm cross-section and 24.6 cm long). 
Forced flow ranging from 0.2 - 80 cm/s is generated by two fan banks. A 
flow sensor at the upstream end of the flow duct provides a flow velocity 
reading. The spherical PMMA sample (4 cm diameter) used in GEL is 
supported by a long stainless-steel tube (6.35 mm diameter). Two 
samples are mounted 120◦apart on an index system that can be rotated 
to install one sample at a time aligned with the flow duct.
ISS crew periodically performs initial hardware installation and pe­
riodic replacement of consumables (fuel samples, oxidizer gas cylinders, 
filters, adsorber cartridges, and igniters), along with debugging activ­
ities. The rest of the experiment is controlled remotely in real time from 
the NASA Glenn Research Center ISS Payload Operations Center 
(GIPOC). During the test, one sample is placed along the centerline of 
the flow duct and about 1 cm downstream from the duct exit for ignition. 
The igniter is an electrically heated coiled wire placed near the upstream 
stagnation point of the sphere. After ignition, the igniter is retracted. The 
sample can either stay at this location for the whole test or be translated 
into the duct.
Four fixed cameras are used for flame visualization. Three Sony FCB- 
MA130 cameras with a 1400×1200 pixel array (named SoFIE Cam1, 
Cam2, and Cam3) are located in a line along the duct, in an orthogonal 
direction to a 1.4 megapixel Allied Vision GC-1380CH ACME color 
camera assembly with a 1360×1024 pixel array. SoFIE Cam1 views the 
sample near the ignition location. SoFIE Cam2 and ACME are aligned 
and view the sample at the location after translating into the duct. SoFIE 
Cam3 is not activated in the cases presented in this paper.
Flame extinction can be reached by decreasing or increasing flow 
velocity, by reducing chamber pressure (venting), by adding nitrogen to 
reduce the oxygen concentration (while raising pressure), or through 
natural and gradual oxygen reduction (depletion) in the sealed CIR 
chamber (free volume = 85.7 liters). In this paper, extinction data are 
obtained by flow velocity variations and from oxygen depletion. All the 
tests reported here are near one atmospheric pressure (1 ± 0.05 atm) 
since the pressure increase during the burning is small. Note that real 
time oxygen concentration and flow velocity measurements are made at 
the flow duct entrance upstream of the sample. The percents of uncer­
tainty in flow velocity and oxygen measurements are estimated to be 
±0.25 %.
The PMMA sphere samples are custom cast with five embedded 
thermocouples as illustrated in Fig. 1. Three thermocouples are placed 
along the center line close to the forward stagnation point. They are used 
to deduce the subsurface temperature gradient, which is a measure of 
the degree of solid sub-surface heating. More details on the preparation 
and fabrication of the samples and the method to compute the temper­
ature gradient can be found in [13,26]. PMMA has been used as a 
representative sample of non-charring solid material in fire research. It 
is also a material used in spacecraft window applications because of 
excellent optical and material properties, despite the flammability risk 
[27].
This SoFIE-GEL experiment aboard ISS has many different features 
from the conventional operation of a normal gravity test. The video time 
sequence of ignition, flame growth and the flame response to flow ve­
locity change and oxygen depletion are very interesting. Several ex­
amples of test runs are given below to give the readers a better feeling of 
this unique space experiment. Complete videos for selected tests can be 
found in supplemental files.
3. Examples of test runs
To demonstrate the salient features of these long duration micro­
gravity experiments and how the extinction limits were determined, 
three example runs are presented here. In each case, recorded time se­
quences of ignition, flame growth, velocity, oxygen and pressure vari­
ations and radiometer readings are given. The gas phase heat conduction 
into the solid interior at the forward stagnation region were post- 
processed from the embedded thermocouples and added to the chart 
(more details on the temperature measurements will be given in the next 
section). Note that the flow velocity is actively controlled by the ground 
crews by varying the fan voltage. Although pressure can be adjusted, we 
did not control it during the tests in this paper. There is a small increase 
of pressure (< 5 %) during the test due to thermal gas expansion and 
combustion product accumulation in the closed chamber. The effect on 
the test results is considered negligible. On the other hand, oxygen 
depletion is an important way to reach extinction limits. Oxygen is 
measured in several locations in the chamber. For the analysis in this 
work, the one measured at the entrance of the flow duct is used as it is 
the oxygen concentration that the flame sees.
In the three tests to be presented, Example #1 (sample 1407B) is used 
to demonstrate how quenching is reached by reducing flow velocity. 
Example #2 (sample 1403C) is used to show how flame blowoffs are 
reached by increasing flow velocity. Example #3 (sample 1409B) is used 
to demonstrate how the quenching is obtained by decreasing oxygen 
percentage at an intermediate flow velocity. One of the main outputs of 
the experiments are the flame videos. The flame images show the flame 
color, size, tip and base locations, and the flame standoff distances. 
Several selected images from the videos at different times are shown to 
highlight the flame at different stages of the burning process. Complete 
videos can be found in the supplementary files. Two radiometers are 
located at two different locations in the test chamber. These radiometers 
have a wide viewing angles are not optimized for GEL. Their traces are 
