radiometers show an immediate drop of the readings when the ignitor is 
de-energized and retracted, but the flame survives and begins to grow 
again (the radiometer saturates between about 160–200 s). The video 
image at 142.7 s shows the flame is stabilized at the stagnation region at 
the front part of the sphere. At 195 s, the sample is moved into the flow 
duct where the ACME camera view provides orthogonal view images. At 
199 s, the video image shows a bright flame with tips extending 
downstream beyond the sphere. The flow is turned down to 9.7 cm/s at 
204 s. The flame is weaken as shown by the green radiometer signal but 
then slowly grows back. The flame image at 236 s is less intense than 
that at higher flow at 199 s even when the heat loss is less (1.11 vs. 1.30 
W/cm2). From 278 s and on, the flow is reduced in quick steps all the 
way to 1.25 cm/s. The flame tips retreat toward the front stagnation 
region and the color turns blue and fainter as shown in the 310.8 s 
image. Note also, the oxygen is also gradually depleted in the closed 
chamber. At 356 s, the flame is quenched (1.25 cm/s, 20.1 % O2, 
qc=0.72 W/cm2). Before quenching, the flame tip displayed upstream 
retreat and downstream pulsating motion for about 35 s (indicated by 
the oscillatory green radiometer signal). The pulsating amplitude in­
creases as the limit is reached. The pulsating frequencies are of the order 
of 1 Hz. Image at 339.6 s shows the flame tips reaching downstream 
during the pulsation. More details about the near-limit phenomena can 
be found in [20].
3.2. Example #2 (Test sample 1403C): blow-off by velocity increase
Fig. 3 shows the time sequence of Sample 1403C with selected video 
images. The conditions for ignition are 21 cm/s, 17.74 % O2 and 103.5 
kPa. Ignitor is on for 96 s (41–137 s). Sample is moved into the duct at 
192 s. Video image at 238.14 s shows the flame in 21 cm/s flow. Velocity 
is increased quickly in steps at 250 s to 75 cm/s at 290 s. During the 
velocity increase, the flame length quickly increases as shown in the 
295.24 s image (73.9 cm/s, 15.46 % O2). The upstream flame becomes 
bluish with a decreased flame standoff distance at the stagnation region. 
At 317.04 s (73.3 cm/s, 14.39 % O2), Local stagnation flame blowoff 
occurs with a flame hole developing at the stagnation region. The flame 
front retreats downstream as shown in the 321.39 s image. This flame 
front fluctuates in time and undulates in space but stays at the near wake 
region for 15 s. During this period, the oxygen drops from 14.39 % to 
13.89 %. Total flame blowoff occurs at 330 s. More details about the 
near-limit phenomena can be found in [20].
3.3. Example #3 (Test Sample 1409B): quenching by reduction of 
oxygen
Fig. 4 shows the time sequence of Sample 1409B with selected video 
images. Ignition conditions are: 17.2 cm/s flow, 19.1 % O2 and 102.3 
kPa. Ignitor is on from 40 to 138 s. Ignitor-supported flame first appears 
at 89 s. So, the ignition delay is 49 s. qc at the ignition start is 2.31 W/ 
cm2 and is 1.65 W/cm2 when the ignitor is turned off. At 159.79 s, the 
video image shows a growing flame with bright sooty color in the fuel 
rich region. The flow is turned down from 17.2 to 9.1 cm/s at 160 s. The 
image at 160.44 s shows that the flame color quickly becomes blue. The 
flame then continues its growth and heats up the solid. Image at 259.7 s 
shows the return of the sooty color and the flame tips are extended to the 
sides of the sample. The sample is moved into the flow duct at 265 s, and 
flow is reduced to 4.7 cm/s at 271 s and is maintained at this level all the 
way to extinction. From 271 to 601 s, the visible flame is entirely blue 
with nearly constant size (flame tip position) as shown in image 479.45 
s. This is quite interesting since although the oxygen decreases from 18.2 
to 15.25 %, the associated decrease in heat release rate may be offset by 
the heating up of the solid (qc varies from 0.89 to 0.44 W/cm2) during 
this 330 s period. After 600 s (approximately), the flame tip begins to 
retreat toward the upstream stagnation region with slow fluctuation. 
Larger amplitude flame tip pulsation cycles (retreat toward upstream 
and pulsate downstream) start at about 740 s. In each cycle the flame 
retreats further upstream, closer to the stagnation point, until quenching 
occurs at 758.4 s. Image 754.45 s captures the retreating flame. The 
quenching conditions are: 4.8 cm/s flow, 14.21 % O2, 104.9 Kpa and 
qc= 0.37 W/cm2. Since the flow velocity is maintained at a constant 
value, this quenching extinction is caused by oxygen depletion.
