4. Effect of sample sub-surface heat-up (flame loss to solid 
interior)
One of the main emphases of this paper is the effect of sample heat- 
up on the extinction limit. The heat-up of the solid sub-surface layer by 
the adjacent gas flame is different from preheating by an external source. 
As explained in [13], during the long period of transient heat-up of a 
bulk solid after ignition, there is a varying amount of heat exchange 
between the gas phase and the solid phase at the solid-gas interface. As 
the solid surface layer heats up, the gas flame loses less heat to the solid 
and becomes stronger (i.e. has a higher flame temperature). So, the 
extinction limit is varied depending on when the extinguishment process 
is applied. To quantify the degree of this heat exchange, the heat flux 
conducted into the solid interior is determined by using embedded 
thermocouples inside the solid sample. This is done at the flow stagna­
tion point region which is the flame stabilization zone. Note that the 
heat-up of the solid by the flame is directly related to heat loss from the 
gaseous flame. The more the solid is heated up, the less is the gas flame 
heat loss. In the next sections, we will first briefly review the thermo­
couple arrangement. Several cases of temperature data will be shown. 
They are used to estimate the heat loss rate parameter qc, as plotted in 
Figs. 2–4.
4.1. Embedded thermocouples
Fig. 5 is a computed tomographic image of a GEL sphere showing the 
thermocouple positions. This sphere has been burnt and quenched in a 
normal gravity then used in a microgravity test in NASA’s Zero Gravity 
Research Facility drop tower. Burned flight samples will be imaged in 
this way when they are returned to Earth for comparison with preflight 
images. A bubble layer next to the surface exists [29]. TC1, TC2 and TC3 
are placed at different distances from the surface along the centerline. 
They are used to deduce the temperature gradient at the flow stagnation 
point. TC4 and TC5 are placed at an angle from the centerline. These 
thermocouples were cast into the PMMA sample. So, their contacts with 
the solid are excellent. Casting thermocouples into the sample is a 
tedious and expensive process. But it avoids the conventional hole 
drilling practice which disturbs the solid especially with multiple 
thermocouples.
As described in detail in [13,26], after the PMMA samples were cast, 
computed tomography was performed for each sample. Table 1 lists the 
four samples with distances and angles for all their five thermocouples. 
In this work only the data from three thermocouples (TC1, TC2 and TC3) 
are used. Table 1 shows their placement angles are all small, i.e. they are 
approximately aligned with the flow direction. So, they are used to 
evaluate the solid temperature gradient in the stagnation flow region. 
The listed thermocouple distances in Table 1 are with respect to the 
initial unburnt sample surface. These distances will change because of 
surface regression during burning but the relative distances between 
thermocouples should be the same. To determine the distance between 
TC1 and the surface during burning and at extinction, the following 
procedure is used [13]. An exponential curve fitting using temperature 
data from TCs 1–3 is extrapolated to 380 ◦C (the assumed surface py­
rolysis temperature) to locate the burning surface. The distance between 
TC1 and surface is then determined from this curve fit (data listed in 
Table 2). If TC1 is exposed to the gas phase in a given test, only the data 
from TC2 and TC3 are used for the curve fit. With the sample surface 
location determined this way, the derivative of the temperature curve at 
the surface, (∂T/∂r)surface, is used to compute the heat loss into the solid 
interior, qc, where qc = λs(∂T/∂r)surface and λs is the PMMA heat con­
duction coefficient at the surface temperature (0.132 W/m K). The un­
certainty of the temperature measurement is relatively small (±5℃). 
But using assumed curve fitting to evaluate the gradient, the error is 
amplified. We estimate that the surface temperature gradient, hence qc, 
may have an uncertainty of 20 %.
4.2. Solid temperature measurement
Two sets of solid temperature data will be presented and compared. 
These two sets are chosen to highlight the influence of the gas phase 
conductive heat loss rate, qc (smaller qc means more solid subsurface 
heating). Set one with three test cases in Fig. 6 have approximately the 
same oxygen limits but different quench velocity due to different qc. The 
second set of two cases in Fig. 7 has the same quenching flow velocity 
but different oxygen limits due to different qc. Note that only two cases 
chosen here are the same as those in Section 3. Since only the temper­
atures from TC1-TC3 are used in this paper, only the data from these 
three thermocouples will be presented.
4.2.1. Solid temperature time history
Fig. 6 shows that the temperatures rise from ambient when the 
ignitor is turned on. TC2 and TC3 rise slower than TC1 because they are 
more in-depth (solid phase thermal lag). The temperatures reach the 
peak with a time delay after the flame extinguished. The amount of 
delay depends on the thermocouple bead distance from the surface. In 
1407A, for example, the flame quenched at 218 s, but TC1 peaked at 235 
s, TC2 peaked at 264 s and TC3 peaked at 370 s. The distance between 
the surface and TC1 (Δrs1) at the moment of flame extinction is 3"
