4.2.2. Parametric comparison 1: effect of qc on quenching flow velocity
Using the temperature data shown in Fig. 6, the estimated values of 
qc at flame extinction (quenching) for the three cases are computed to 
investigate its effect on extinction. Table 2 compares the three cases in 
Fig.6: 1407A, 1407B and 1403C. These three cases have a very similar 
oxygen percentage (20 ± 0.14 %), but their qc and quenching velocity 
are different: 1.07 W/cm2, 2.16 cm/s for 1407A, 0.72 W/cm2, 1.32 cm/s 
for 1407B and 0.5 W/cm2, 0.85 cm/s for 1403B So, less flame heat loss 
or more solid surface sublayer heat-up (i.e. smaller qc) lowers the 
quenching velocity. In these extinction experiments, smaller qc is ob­
tained with longer solid heat-up time as shown from Δtie in Table 2. Δtie 
is the total solid heat-up time before extinction. It is calculated from the 
time the ignitor is turned on to the time of the flame extinction. During 
this period, the solid is first heated by the ignitor then by the gas flame.
To facilitate the reading of the temperature history in Fig. 6, Δrs1, the 
estimated distances between the surface and thermocouple TC1 are also 
listed in Table 2. As this distance shrinks (due to longer burning time 
and/or repeated use of the same sample), the recorded temperature 
peaks are higher (closer to the surface).
Parametric comparison 2: effect of qc on quenching oxygen 
percentage
Fig. 7 presents the solid temperature data for Cases 1410A and 
1409B These two cases have a similar flow velocity (4.77±0.06 cm/s), 
but different qc and quenching oxygen concentrations :1410A (0.75 W/ 
cm2, 16.01 %) and 1409B (0.36 W/cm2, 14.21 %). Less flame loss or 
more solid heat-up lowers the quenching oxygen concentration. More 
data on the sample heat-up time Δtie and the distance between sample 
surface to TC1 at extinction are listed in Table2.
