The extinction boundary in Fig. 8 is not a sharp U-shaped curve as 
predicted in the steady model [3]. Instead, the limits are points clustered 
around a truncated U-shaped band (the shape of present data set may 
resemble a hockey stick). The data is truncated above flow velocity 
beyond ̴80 cm/s (a ̴60 s−1) because of the limitation of experimental 
setup. These extinction data points have different amounts of flame 
conductive heat loss rate into the solid (qc). The values of qc (W/cm2) 
are denoted next to the extinction data points. With a few exceptions, 
extinction points with smaller qc’s in the cluster tend to be flammable at 
lower oxygen concentrations than the neighboring points as illustrated 
by the two comparisons in the last section. This is physically reasonable, 
smaller qc means less heat loss from the flame and more solid heating, 
the present work provides the quantitative data for comparison. There 
are several points in Fig. 8 without a designated qc. This is because 
either the re-used sample has more than two thermocouples exposed to 
the gas phase so that the solid temperature curve cannot be computed, or 
repeated ignition has been applied, and therefore a simple exponential 
temperature curve fit is questionable.
In Fig.8, the blue points are the quenching extinction at low flow 
velocities or low strain rates. The red points are blow-off extinction at 
higher flow velocities or higher strain rates. There are more data in the 
low velocity side. And velocity is plotted using logarithmic scale to 
emphasize quenching extinction in this study. The trend of the flame 
quenching branch, i.e. higher oxygen limit at lower flow strain rate, are 
due to flame temperature reduction from radiative heat losses [3,30]. As 
flow strain rate (a) is reduced, the flame standoff distance increases as 
1/a1/2 and the flame heat release rate decreases (proportional to a). The 
non-dimensional surface radiative loss rate is amplified as 1/a1/2 and the 
non-dimensional gas phase radiative loss rate is amplified as 1/an (n is 
between 1/2 and 1 with 1 for an optically thin flame). The present re­
sults show that for a thick solid, in addition to these intrinsic radiative 
flame losses, extra conductive loss to the solid interior can also affect the 
extinction limits, especially on the quenching side.
The bottom of the extinction boundary band is located around 10 
cm/s or strain rate 7.5 1/s, which is the merging point of the blowoff and 
the quenching branches. For the limited data points we have, the 
blowoff boundary has a much lower slope as noted in a previous study 
using cylindrical rods [17]. SoFIE flow duct hardware limitations pre­
vent us from determining blowoff limits in higher flows. Fig. 8 shows 
that in higher velocities, there are two blowoff limits corresponding to 
stagnation blowoff and wake blowoff. The red point with black outline is 
the oxygen limit for local blowoff at the stagnation point and the one 
with simple red is the subsequent wake blowoff. As seen from Fig. 3, 
there may be considerable time delay between stagnation blowoff and 
wake blowoff since the flame is anchored in a lower velocity wake re­
gion at a lower strain rate. So, the limiting oxygen is lower for the wake 
blowoff limits.
In the experiments, quenching and blowoff are distinguished by 
observing the multi-dimensional flame extinction dynamics. The 
quenching sequence starts as the flame tips shrink toward the stagnation 
point. The flame extinguishes as a weak small flame. Blowoff starts with 
a hole in the upstream stagnation region and the flame retreats down­
stream to extinction. Quenching at sufficiently low stretch and blowoff 
at sufficiently high stretch may exhibit near-limit cyclic flame pulsa­
tions. In blowoff, the pulsating part is the upstream flame front near the 
sphere shoulder and the motion amplitude is small and somewhat 
irregular circumference wise. In quenching, the pulsating part is the 
downstream flame tip [20]. The magnitudes of pulsations can be very 
large (all the way from the front to the back of the sphere) near the limit. 
During the pulsation, the radiometers show a higher intensity peak, and 
the advancing front is bright and have a triple flame appearance. It is 
theorized that this is a partial premixed flame over a heated solid (by the 
previously retreated diffusion flame) where pyrolysis is still occurring, 
but its magnitude is not large enough to sustain a steady diffusion flame. 
So, a periodic pulsation appears. In Fig. 8, the average number of pul­
sation cycles and their amplitude decreases when the flow velocity ap­
proaches the bottom of the extinction boundary. In the intermediate 
flow velocity range, there is no tip pulsation on the low velocity side nor 
hole opening and flame base pulsation on the higher velocity side. Thus, 
flame response to velocity variation along the extinction boundary is 
smooth and continuous for the transition from one extinction branch to 
the other.
From the experimental videos, it is difficult to distinguish the two 
extinction modes in a narrow velocity range around 10 cm/s. some such 
extinction points are labeled as the in-between cases in open symbols in 
Fig.8. Note these in-between points are at the bottom of the extinction 
boundary. They are the lowest oxygen concentrations to sustain the 
burning of the sample (referred to as the fundamental limit in [3]. For 
PMMA, this and previous data [17] shows the oxygen limit can be below 
14 % O2. This is considerably lower than the listed normal gravity 
PMMA Limiting Oxygen Index (LOI) and upward flammability values in 
conventional flammability tables.
6. Conclusions
To enable a precise determination of the extinction l"
doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2025.114244,doi,Experimental determination of diffusion flame extinction boundaries over a PMMA sphere: The effect of sub-surface solid heating,8,7,"extinction boundary at intermediate flow velocities is determined. The 
choice of a sphere as the solid sample provides a well-defined stagnation 
point flow region that enables the comparison with existing one- 
dimensional theories in terms of flow strain or flame stretch rate. The 
multi-dimensional flame dynamics around the sides of the sphere pro­
vide additional information on the qualitative differences between the 
quenching and the blowoff processes.
One special feature of the present work is to determine the effect of 
solid sample sub-surface heating on the extinction limits. This solid 
heating is through the gaseous flame itself during the long transient 
burning period. By varying the burning duration before the extinction 
exercise, a different amount of sample heating or flame heat loss is ob­
tained. Multiple embedded thermocouples are used to deduce the stag­
nation point temperature gradient at the surface. This is an elaborate 
and tedious process to quantify the amount of flame heat loss to the solid 
interior and has not been done before.
Using oxygen and flow velocity (or strain rate) as coordinates, the 
experimental extinction boundary is a truncated U-shaped band. Data 
within the band has different amounts of flame heat loss. With a few 
exceptions, less flame heat loss or more solid heating yields a more 
flammable solid. The bottom of the U-shaped boundary has the lowest 
oxygen percentage. For PMMA, it is below 14 %, a value lower than 
those listed in conventional flammability tables but has previously been 
reported in [17,22]. It is interesting that the stretch rate for the oxygen 
minimum is close to 7.5 1/s similar to the stretch rate predicted [3] 
despite the simplifications assumed in the model (the predicted oxygen 
limit is off though). The determination of the extinction boundaries 
consists of 35 extinction limit data points in long duration microgravity 
and represents a tremendous team effort of many people.
