Novelty and Significance Statement
This flame extinction experiment over thick solid fuels (PMMA 
spheres) was carried out aboard the International Space Station. Long 
duration microgravity tests are needed for the quenching process at low 
flow velocities to develop. The data obtained is rare and the project has 
been >10 years in the making. Using oxygen and flow velocity (or strain 
rate) as coordinates, an extinction boundary for blowoff and quenching 
has been determined with flame conductive heat loss rate to the solid 
(related to the solid sub-surface heating) as an additional parameter. The 
quantitative measurement of flame-to-solid heat conduction from igni­
tion to extinction using multiple embedded thermocouples in the custom 
cell cast PMMA samples is also novel.
