doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2026.114803,doi,Flame spread over charring and non-charring materials in microgravity on ISS/Kibo,2,1,"The flammability of materials is influenced not only by the oxygen 
concentration in the surrounding atmosphere but also by the ambient 
flow velocity. Therefore, in evaluating material flammability, it is 
essential to understand how the limiting oxygen concentration (LOC) 
changes in response to variations in flow velocity.
The international collaborative research project FLARE, which fo­
cuses on fire safety in space, has proposed a method for predicting the 
flow-velocity dependence of LOC for thermally thin flat materials under 
opposed flow conditions. This method employs scale analysis to estimate 
the LOC based on experimental data obtainable under ground conditions 
[16–18]. In our previous study [19,20], the LOC prediction equation for 
a charring material—specifically, filter paper (Advantec No. 4A)—was 
compared with results from long-duration combustion experiments 
conducted aboard the ISS/Kibo module. The prediction was found to 
quantitatively reproduce the minimum LOC. A similar approach using 
scale analysis has also been applied by Bhattacharjee et al. to PMMA 
sheets [21], in which they compared their predictions with ISS-based 
long-duration experiments and successfully predicted the critical 
extinction velocity at which flame extinction occurs.
On the other hand, in the case of filter paper, it was found that under 
very low opposed flow velocity conditions, flames could persist for 
extended durations in a robust, quasi-spherical shape—even under 
conditions exceeding the predicted limiting curve. In such low-velocity 
opposed flow conditions, radiative extinction effects become more sig­
nificant; however, the Damk¨ohler number also increases. Thus, it has 
been suggested that flames on filter paper and PMMA can be sustained 
even under very low flow velocities [22]. Nevertheless, in the afore­
mentioned experiments with filter paper, robust flames were observed at 
flow velocities even lower than the predicted critical extinction velocity, 
i.e., under conditions in which a two-dimensional flame structure could 
no longer be maintained. Consequently, identifying the conditions under 
which such robust, three-dimensional flames emerge constitutes an 
important issue. Similar three-dimensional flame behavior under low 
flow velocity was also reported by Zhao et al. [23], who observed such 
flames during concurrent flame spread over a cotton–fiberglass com­
posite fabric (SIBAL fabric, a charring material like filter paper) onboard 
the ISS. They found that the flame adopted a three-dimensional shape 
and eventually extinguished under flow velocities below 3 cm/s. Kumar 
et al. also demonstrated through numerical simulations that 
three-dimensional flamelets are formed near the radiative extinction 
limit in a microgravity environment [24]. In addition, Legros et al. [25] 
for two-dimensional planar flames, and Guibaud et al. [26] for 
axisymmetric flames, reported that local quenching occurs at the trailing 
edge of the flame near the extinction limit due to radiative losses. These 
studies highlight the importance of properly modeling radiative heat 
transfer. As an experimental approach, Guibaud et al. [27] employed the 
Broadband Modulated Absorption/Emission (BMAE) technique to 
investigate flame spread under forced opposed-flow conditions in 
microgravity, enabling detailed measurements of the distribution of 
radiative heat feedback from the flame to the sample surface.
From July to October 2024, long-duration combustion experiments 
were conducted as part of the FLARE project, extending previous on­
board studies with filter paper. The first test material was a non-charring 
PMMA sheet (ACRYPLEN HBS006; Mitsubishi Chemical Corporation), 
which under ground conditions shows flame-spread behavior similar to 
filter paper. The second was a charring, flame-retardant meta-aramid 
fabric (NOMEX HT90-40; DuPont), characterized by its markedly 
different limiting oxygen concentration (LOC) [18,28,29]. Interestingly, 
in the case of NOMEX, robust three-dimensional flames—similar to 
those previously observed with filter paper—were observed not under 
extremely low flow conditions but rather at moderate flow velocities.
This study presents the results of these long-duration combustion 
experiments conducted on the ISS/Kibo module for both PMMA and 
NOMEX to elucidate their respective flame-spread characteristics. 
Furthermore, a previously developed predictive model—which has been 
shown to accurately reproduce the flammability map of filter paper—is 
applied to these materials. Special emphasis is placed on flame behavior 
near the quenching limit to highlight the fundamental differences be­
tween charring and non-charring materials.
2. Model for predicting flammability limits
In the scaling analysis, the limiting oxygen concentration (LOC) was 
determined for a two-dimensional flame in opposed flow conditions, as 
shown in Fig. 1, by establishing a thermal balance equation (Eq. (1)). 
The model is a simple formulation composed of three main components: 
the heat required to increase the preheat zone temperature to the py­
rolysis temperature over a zone of length Lg, radiative losses from the 
preheat zone to the surroundings, and conductive heat transfer from the 
flame to the sample through the gas phase, taking into account finite- 
rate kinetics. 
