The limiting oxygen concentration (LOC) was determined by varying 
the opposed flow velocity Vg in Eq. (2) and identifying the oxygen 
concentration at which the nondimensional flame spread rate η becomes 
zero. The resulting limiting curves for the three materials are shown in 
Fig. 2. The physical properties used for the predictions, the predicted 
MLOCs, and the critical opposed flow velocities are summarized in 
Table 1. Here, T10 and T50 represent the temperatures at which 10% and 
50% mass loss occur, respectively, as measured by thermogravimetric 
analysis (TGA). The pyrolysis temperature Tv is defined as the arithmetic 
mean of these two values [18]. The oxygen concentration and flow ve­
locity at the minimum point of each calculated limiting curve are 
defined as the MLOC and the critical opposed flow velocity for the 
respective material. The predicted limiting curves indicate that filter 
paper and PMMA exhibit nearly identical trends. In contrast, the 
limiting curve for NOMEX shows a higher LOC compared to the other 
two materials. On the radiation-extinction side (low-velocity side), the 
curve for NOMEX is located near a flow velocity Vg = 10–12 cm/s. These 
predicted limiting curves are also compared with the flame spread 
experimental results for PMMA and NOMEX conducted in orbit.
3. Experimental setup
The experiments were conducted using the Solid Combustion 
Experimental Module (SCEM) installed in the Kibo module on the 
International Space Station (ISS). An overview of SCEM is shown in 
Fig. 3. SCEM is a closed-circuit wind tunnel with an internal volume of 
35 liters. A sample card holding a flat sample (2 cm wide, 13 cm long) is 
placed in the test section, allowing experiments under both opposed 
flow and concurrent-flow conditions by varying the ignition position. 
Ignition was achieved by electrically heating a Kanthal wire in contact 
with the edge of the sample.
The oxygen concentration and ambient flow velocity within SCEM 
can be controlled within the ranges of 0–45% and 0–26 cm/s, respec­
tively. The behavior of flame spread was recorded using three visible- 
light cameras (frame rate: 20 fps) positioned perpendicularly, diago­
nally, and horizontally relative to the sample. Additionally, the sample 
surface temperature was captured using an infrared camera (detection 
wavelength: 7–13 μm, frame rate: 30 fps) with a measurable 
