4. Results and discussion
4.1. Flame spread behavior over PMMA
Fig. 4 presents the flammability map of PMMA obtained from orbital 
experiments, alongside the results for filter paper reported in Ref. 20. A 
comparison between the predicted limiting curves and the flame spread 
behavior of PMMA reveals that both blow-off and quenching extinction 
limits are accurately captured. In the case of filter paper, it was observed 
that under flow conditions well below the quenching limit, the flame 
transitioned from a two-dimensional structure to a three-dimensional 
spherical flame. While such robust flames were occasionally observed 
for filter paper under very low flow velocities (green circles in Fig. 4), 
PMMA flames extinguished upon reaching the quenching limit.
The experimental results near the quenching branch are shown in 
Fig. 5 and S1. In Run 1-#03, when the opposed flow velocity was high, 
puffing of molten PMMA and irregular flame ejections were observed. 
Subsequently, when a rounded, dim flame formed at Vg = 2 cm/s, py­
rolysis gases periodically appeared between T+70 s and T+75 s as a 
result of partial extinction, and the flame eventually extinguished after 
the flow velocity was reduced to Vg = 1 cm/s. Infrared images reveal that 
when Vg = 1 cm/s, the front edge of the preheat zone, which had been 
straight in the earlier stage of the experiment, became slightly curved 
just before extinction. Based on the infrared data, the flame radius at the 
moment of extinction was estimated to be 6.8 mm. Under the same 
condition, however, filter paper maintained a spherical flame for over a 
minute even after the atmosphere became quiescent [19].
