doi:10.1016/j.combustflame.2026.114803,doi,Flame spread over charring and non-charring materials in microgravity on ISS/Kibo,6,5,"This indicates that in PMMA, once the quenching branch is reached, 
the flame takes on a rounded shape but does not further reduce in size 
before extinguishing. As will be discussed later, when the opposed flow 
velocity decreases, the flame transitions from a two-dimensional to a 
spherical shape in order to reduce radiative heat losses. In such cases, a 
smaller flame radius corresponds to a shorter preheat zone length. 
However, PMMA does not appear capable of sustaining such small flame 
structures as observed in filter paper, and consequently, it is presumed to 
lose its robustness and extinguish. Similarly, in Run 1-#05 (see Fig. 5b), 
the PMMA samples quenched when the conditions reached the limiting 
curve. Infrared imaging shows that the front edge remained straight 
until the moment of extinction.
The phenomenon observed in filter paper, where robust flames 
accompanied by vapor cloud formation occurred, was observed in 
PMMA under the high-oxygen condition of run 1-#13 (see Fig. 6, oxygen 
concentration: 34%). Infrared images reveal that, under quiescent 
conditions, a markedly long preheat zone with a rounded front edge is 
formed, with the flame radius measured to be 8.3 mm. The vapor cloud 
is considered to be composed of pyrolysis gases that did not react and 
accumulated around the flame from the edge of the rounded flame [19]. 
In this case, rounded flames accompanied by vapor cloud formation 
propagated in a quiescent atmosphere beyond the limiting oxygen 
concentration, and the entire sample was completely consumed; how­
ever, such phenomena were extremely rare in PMMA flame spread. 
These findings suggest that, although PMMA tends to form a rounded 
flame shape upon reaching the quenching limit, it fails to maintain a 
small flame—approximately 5 mm in radius—as observed with filter 
paper, leading to extinction.
Turning to the blow-off branch, run 1-#09 involved experimental 
conditions beyond the blow-off branch. The flame spread behavior 
captured by the visible-light camera and the infrared camera is shown in 
Fig. 7. By incrementally increasing the opposed flow velocity, the flame 
