(Vg=16 cm/s), and extinguished when the conditions exceeded the 
limiting curve (Vg=18 cm/s). Observations of the flame at extinction (e. 
g., T+157 s) revealed that a part of the pyrolysis region had already 
extinguished, confirming that the flame was extinguished by blow-off. 
Infrared images show that the preheat zone length decreased with 
increasing flow velocity, which is a characteristic behavior observed 
during extinction due to blow-off.
Run 1-#11, predicted to be near the minimum limiting oxygen 
concentration (MLOC) (oxygen concentration 15%, flow velocity 
Vg=8 cm/s), showed gradual flame shrinking after ignition, with 
extinction occurring at T+140 s (see Fig. 8). This result matched the 
predicted limiting curve exactly. The oxygen concentration at extinction 
was 14.9%, which is consistent with the value obtained in previous 
parabolic flight experiments [16] and approximately 2% lower than the 
oxygen index (OI=16.77%) of PMMA. Thus, the combustion behavior of 
PMMA was accurately described by the predictive equation represented 
by Eq. (2). Moreover, no flame spread was observed beyond the pre­
dicted limiting curve, except under high-oxygen conditions.
Although PMMA sometimes exhibits complex behavior due to 
bubbling in the molten region, the flame becomes dim near the 
quenching and blow-off limits, allowing the model presented in Fig. 1 to 
accurately capture its limiting behavior. In this study, a simple 
assumption of a uniform radiative balance along the preheat zone was 
adopted, yet it was found to reproduce the LOC of PMMA with good 
accuracy. These results confirm that PMMA, as a representative non- 
charring material, conforms well to the model and exhibits well- 
defined and predictable flammability characteristics.
4.2. Flame spread behavior over NOMEX
The flammability map of NOMEX at an ambient pressure of 101.3 
kPa is presented in Fig. 9, along with the limiting curve calculated using 
Eq. (2). In the flame spread behavior of NOMEX, a transition to a 
rounded three-dimensional flame shape was observed as the opposed 
flow velocity decreased, similar to the behavior previously noted in filter 
paper. As in Fig. 5, these instances of three-dimensional flame spread are 
indicated by green circles in Fig. 9. The quenching branch of the limiting 
curve calculated by Eq. (2) is considered to represent the boundary 
between two-dimensional and three-dimensional flame regimes. 
Comparing the predicted curve with the experimental results suggests 
that the model appropriately estimates the effect of radiative heat loss.
Furthermore, for materials with high pyrolysis temperatures such as 
NOMEX, previous studies [18] have shown that, unlike filter paper or 
PMMA, a reduction in opposed flow velocity does not lead to a corre­
sponding decrease in the limiting oxygen concentration. This is sup­
ported by the behavior of the limiting curve in Fig. 9, which shows a 
monotonic increase in LOC as the flow velocity decreases within the 
range of Vg ≤25 cm/s. Consistent with this, the experimental data show 
no sustained flame spread at oxygen concentrations below the 
ground-based MLOC of 30.03% under flow velocities of 25 cm/s or less. 
However, for materials with a high pyrolysis temperature Tv, the form of 
Eq. (3) suggests that even a small measurement error in Tv can lead to 
significant uncertainty in the estimation of radiative heat loss. In Fig. 9, 
the limiting curves calculated with a ±10 K deviation in Tv are shown as 
thin blue lines. These results indicate that while the uncertainty in Tv has 
a relatively minor effect on the predicted MLOC, it can substantially shift 
