Next, the changes in flame shape in response to variations in opposed 
flow velocity are discussed for each initial oxygen concentration ranging 
from 30% to 36%. Fig. 10a presents the top-view images of the flame in 
Run 4-#06, where the initial oxygen concentration was 30%, i.e., near 
the MLOC. In this run, the opposed flow velocity was varied over time 
from 25 cm/s to 20 cm/s and then to 15 cm/s. When the velocity 
decreased to 15 cm/s, the flame became rounded, lost its axial direc­
tionality, exhibited lateral wavering, and was eventually extinguished.
A similar trend was observed in previous experiments on filter paper 
combustion [19], where a spherical flame persisted for a while under a 
quiescent atmosphere with 18% oxygen. In the SIBAL experiments by 
Zhao et al. [23], spherical flames were also observed under a concurrent 
flow velocity of 2.2 cm/s. In these cases, the diffusion velocity of oxygen 
was comparable to or exceeded the ambient flow velocity. It is notable in 
the present NOMEX experiment that the transition to a spherical flame 
occurred under a relatively high flow velocity of 15 cm/s. Although 
spherical flames are usually attributed to diffusive–thermal instabilities 
at low ambient flow velocities [32], Fig. 10a shows that they can also 
develop at relatively high flow velocities when the thermal balance in 
Eq. (1)
is disrupted. Transition to flamelets was observed in 
narrow-channel microgravity experiments by Olson et al. [33]. In that 
case, heat loss was intentionally increased by installing a copper plate, 
and similarly, in the flame spread of NOMEX, which has a high pyrolysis 
temperature, the increased heat loss is considered to be a possible cause 
of the transition to flamelets. Similar results were also reported by 
Matsuoka et al. for thick solid materials [34].
Figs. 10b and S2 show the results of Run 4-#01, in which the opposed 
flow velocity was decreased from 18 cm/s to 6 cm/s at an oxygen con­
centration of 32%. Compared to Run 4-#06, where the oxygen con­
centration was 30%, the flame in Run 4-#01 maintained a two- 
dimensional shape at Vg = 15 cm/s and transitioned to a three- 
dimensional shape at Vg = 10 cm/s, owing to the higher oxygen level. 
