Fig. 10c presents the results of Run 4-#14 under an initial oxygen 
concentration of 36%, where the opposed flow velocity was reduced 
from 8 cm/s to 2 cm/s. As the opposed flow velocity decreased, the 
flame width gradually narrowed, and a spherical flame was observed at 
Vg = 4 cm/s. These results indicate that, in higher oxygen atmospheres, 
the transition to a spherical flame occurs at lower flow velocities. These 
trends are consistent with the findings reported in Ref. 23.
An intriguing phenomenon was also observed. Fig. 10d shows the 
flame spread at a constant opposed flow velocity of 6 cm/s for 75 s under 
the same initial oxygen concentration of 36%. In this case, the flame 
exhibited a rounded shape and showed periodic lateral oscillations with 
a period of approximately 30 s. Such unsteady oscillatory flame 
behavior has been reported under quiescent conditions in numerical 
simulations by Kumar et al. [32]. The observation of similar oscillations 
at a moderate opposed flow velocity of approximately 6 cm/s in an open 
configuration, rather than in a narrow channel [33], is particularly 
noteworthy.
To analyze the interesting behavior observed in NOMEX, the time 
histories of flame area and preheat zone length under various conditions 
were derived from infrared camera images. As shown in Fig. 11, the 
bright regions in the infrared images correspond well with the flame 
locations identified in the visible images. Based on this correlation, the 
temperature distribution along the sample’s x-direction was measured, 
and the distance from the flame tip to the location showing a 0.1ΔT 
increase in temperature was defined as the preheat zone length, Lg [19,
20].
