It is evident from Eq. (8) that, unlike Eq. (5), Lg remains finite even as 
Vg approaches zero. Therefore, in the low-flow-velocity regime, the 
transition from a two-dimensional to a spherical flame leads to a 
reduction in the size of the preheat zone formed ahead of the flame front. 
Since Lg decreases with decreasing flame radius, smaller spherical flames 
are more effective at minimizing radiative heat losses.
Fig. 12 shows the relationship between opposed flow velocity and Lg 
for each oxygen concentration. The blue lines represent the theoretical 
curve of Lg for two-dimensional flame spread as given by Eq. (5). From 
the figure, it can be seen that when the experimental conditions lie 
within the limiting curve and a two-dimensional flame is observed 
(indicated by square symbols), the measured Lg for NOMEX agrees well 
with the theoretical prediction by Eq. (5), as was the case for filter paper. 
