of the preheat zone is determined primarily by the thermal properties of 
the gas phase and is largely independent of the material properties. 
When the opposed flow velocity decreases and the experimental con­
ditions fall outside the limiting curve, the flame shape becomes rounded 
(indicated by circle symbols), and Lg is found to be significantly shorter 
than that predicted by two-dimensional theory. In the three-dimensional 
flame regime, the preheat-zone length Lg is expected to vary according to 
Eq. (6), becoming smaller as Rf decreases. For example, in Run 4-#01 
(Fig. 10b), the flame radius gradually decreases as the opposed-flow 
velocity is reduced. This observation suggests that the increase in Lg 
caused by the flow deceleration—predicted by the two-dimensional 
relation of Eq. (5)—is mitigated by the transition to the three- 
dimensional regime described by Eq. (6), together with the concurrent 
reduction in Rf.
To investigate the relationship between the preheat zone length and 
flame size, Fig. 13 presents the time histories of Lg and the flame radius 
under these conditions. The flame radius is defined as the radius of a 
circle having the same area as the observed flame. In Fig. 13a, it is 
evident that Lg increases at the moment when the opposed flow velocity 
is reduced (T+44 s). When the flow velocity is switched from 15 cm/s to 
10 cm/s at T+74 s, the flame initially attempts to maintain an Lg cor­
responding to the new velocity. However, since this condition lies 
beyond the quenching branch, the thermal balance expressed by Eq. (2)
breaks down. Consequently, the flame rapidly transitions into a rounded 
shape, with its radius contracting to 4 mm and the preheat zone length Lg 
decreasing correspondingly to 5 mm. Then, when the flow velocity is 
further reduced to 6 cm/s at T+104 s, Lg sharply increases, and the flame 
radius becomes even smaller in response. Nevertheless, at this reduced 
radius, the thermal balance cannot be sustained, and the flame ulti­
mately extinguishes.
Based on these results, for the cases in which three-dimensional 
flames were observed in Fig. 12, the experimental data were 
compared with the curve given by Eq. (8). The empirical constant C1 in 
Eq. (8), which best matches the experimental results, was determined to 
be C1=2.3. For example, in Run 4-#04, as the flow velocity decreased, 
the flame radius Rf was observed to shrink from 5 mm (Vg=9 cm/s) to 4 
mm (Vg=6 cm/s). During this transition, the preheat zone length Lg 
decreased from about 6 mm to 5 mm, and this behavior is well captured 
by the prediction curve. Similarly, in Run 4-#14 and in the extinction 
case under quiescent conditions observed in Run 4-#12, the prediction 
curve in Fig. 12 reproduces well the measured values of both Rf and Lg.
