By solving Eq. (10), the LOC of three-dimensional flames for NOMEX 
was obtained as a function of Rf, as shown by the blue line in Fig. 9. The 
mean Rf observed under steady three-dimensional flame conditions, and 
the values immediately after flow switching under extinction conditions, 
are also plotted. The constant C2 in Eq. (11) was determined to be C2 =
0.93 to best fit the NOMEX data.
This model successfully captures the transition from two- 
dimensional to three-dimensional flame regimes as the opposed-flow 
velocity decreases. For example, in Run 4-#01, when Vg decreases to 
10 cm/s—beyond the two-dimensional limit—the flame is sustained in a 
three-dimensional form by reducing its radius to Rf = 4 mm. When Vg is 
further reduced to 6 cm/s, the flame attempts to shrink below this radius 
but cannot remain stable, leading to extinction. The NOMEX results 
indicate that extinction occurs at Rf = 3.5–4 mm, and that the limiting 
radius slightly decreases with increasing oxygen concentration.
A similar analysis applied to filter paper and PMMA is shown in 
Fig. 4. Using the same constants C1 and C2 determined for NOMEX, the 
method quantifies the previously qualitative correlation between flame 
radius and flow velocity reported in Ref. 19. For filter paper, the 
extinction radius is around Rf = 5 mm and decreases slightly with 
increasing oxygen concentration, similar to NOMEX. In contrast, PMMA 
exhibits a much larger extinction radius of Rf = 7.5–8 mm, indicating 
that it is far less likely to sustain a three-dimensional flame.
In NOMEX and filter paper, the flame could persist beyond the two- 
dimensional quenching limit by transitioning into a small spherical 
flame, whereas such behavior was rarely observed in PMMA. These 
three-dimensional flames appear to share structural similarities with 
fingering and smoldering, in that they are strongly influenced by heat 
losses [35]. In the present study, smaller spherical flames were found in 
materials that develop high-temperature char, suggesting that the char 
region may in part act as a localized thermal reservoir that helps sustain 
the flame at reduced radii. The observations further indicate that 
