doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2015.04.004,doi,Detection of spacecraft fire signatures and post-fire aerosols—Part I: Ground-based results,3,2,"600 °C. In general, lower temperature conditions were selected to
produce large amounts of organic volatiles with little thermal
oxidation of the sample. Higher temperatures were used to gen-
erate worst-case concentrations of particulates, and mid-range
values to generate high concentrations of carbon monoxide.
Flaming conditions were simulated by operating at high tem-
peratures, and igniting the exiting gas stream with a heated wire.
2.2. Sample materials, instrumentation, and test procedure
The sample materials were selected to be representative of
those used in spacecraft electrical circuitry and wiring, as this is a
primary inception mechanism for ﬁre scenarios. One fuel type
(referred to herein as the Standard Mix) was developed by the
Lockheed Missiles and Space Co., Inc., and is a mixture of twelve of
the most common polymers used in aerospace electrical and
electronics systems [8]. This material was subsequently used by
Sribnik, et al. for evaluating the removal of smoke contaminants
on the International Space Station [9]. The polymer components
are individually ground, then mixed and pressed into a pellet at
10,000 psi. Samples of Teﬂon
s (without pigment) and Kapton
s
insulation were also used, as well as a 50/50 mixture of the two
materials, since this combination is being evaluated for use in
spacecraft applications.
For each test point, a speciﬁed mass of sample material was
loaded into the smoke generator. Before beginning the test matrix,
reference gas standards were used to calibrate the various che-
mical species monitors. Most of these instruments were located
inside the glovebox, although in some instances they were oper-
ated externally using the sampling ports. A schematic diagram
illustrating the complete suite of instrumentation is shown in
Fig. 2. A number of laptop computers were used for controlling the
experiment, and for acquiring data from the smoke generator and
the measurement instruments. The time stamps were all syn-
chronized prior to the initiation of the test sequence. A Variac
controller was adjusted to heat the tube furnace to the desired
level, and power was applied for approximately 7 min. In the
ﬂaming tests, this was followed by energizing a hot wire located at
the exit of the generator. At the conclusion of the speciﬁed heating
interval, the circulating fans were operated for 1 min to mix the
volume of the glovebox, then turned off to prevent particulate
aggregation and other losses. The outputs of the measurement
instruments were then logged for roughly 20 min, or until the
readings had decayed to near the initial baseline values. The glo-
vebox exhaust fan was then used to vent the interior, allowing 20–
30 min between test points to establish new baseline readings.
2.3. Particle measuring instruments
Two reference instruments were utilized to measure the
aerosol number concentration and mass, respectively a Con-
densation Particle Counter (CPC; TSI PTrak Model 8525) and a
Tapered Element Oscillating Microbalance (TEOM; Fisher Scientiﬁc
Model PDM 3600). As seen in Fig. 2, dilution arms with inlet ﬁlters
were employed to ensure that all the instruments were operated
within their speciﬁed dynamic ranges. The sample ﬂows were
monitored using a pair of laminar ﬂow elements (LFE-1 and 2). The
pressure drop across each element was measured with a differ-
ential pressure gauge (Magnehelic Model W46V JB). Both the CPC
and TEOM and their internal pumps were independently cali-
brated prior to use.
A primary objective of these tests was to evaluate the perfor-
mance of a scattering sensor developed by NASA for early warning
ﬁre detection and the characterization of post-ﬁre environments.
This sensor is known as the Multi-Parameter Aerosol Scattering
Sensor (MPASS), and is described in further detail in Greenberg
and Fischer [10]. A picture of the sensor used in these tests is
shown in Fig. 3. This MPASS is a ﬂow through device, and is placed
in the sample line immediately preceding the TEOM.
3. MPASS design and measurement theory
The motivation for designing the MPASS sensor stems from
NASA’s overall effort in spacecraft ﬁre safety. This effort spans a
number of topical areas, including evaluation of the ignition,
ﬂammability, and ﬂamespread of relevant materials, prompt and
reliable ﬁre detection, suppression techniques, and post-ﬁre
monitoring and cleanup. The MPASS as conﬁgured in the test re-
ported here is not a ﬁxed design, but instead represents a sys-
tematic approach to the modeling, design, and fabrication of
sensors that can be adapted and improved as our understanding of
the requirements advances. The use of optical scattering stems
from the richness of information that is afforded, in combination
with the widely accepted view of its utility in early warning ﬁre
detection applications [11].
