doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2015.04.004,doi,Detection of spacecraft fire signatures and post-fire aerosols—Part I: Ground-based results,4,3,"As shown in Fig. 4, the MPASS consists of a coherent laser diode
source in combination with several detectors placed at speciﬁed
angular locations relative to the optical axis. In principal, a number
of sources of varying wavelengths and/or polarizations can be
exploited to characterize properties such as particle shape and
refractive index. Details of the device construction are provided in
reference [10]. The design objective aligns with early warning ﬁre
detection: rapid response, the elimination of false triggers, the
ability to identify the type of material(s) producing the smoke, and
the nature of the ignition event. The reduction of false triggers is of
particular importance in aerospace applications, due to the pos-
sible interruption of mission activities and the initiation of a
variety of automatic system responses.
In designing the MPASS, the goal is to optimize the accuracy in
measuring speciﬁc aerosol properties. Since the scattering results
from an ensemble of particles in the measurement volume, these
properties correspond to integral moments of the distribution,
such as volume (or mass) and surface area. As will be shown be-
low, the optimization can be performed relative to speciﬁed ranges
in both aerosol properties and sensor conﬁguration. The former
includes distribution properties such as count median diameter,
geometric standard deviation, etc., and material properties such as
the refractive index. The latter can include parameters such as
wavelength, polarization, and angular collection aperture.
Sensor design and optimization is based on a ﬁrst-principles
optical scattering model. In essence, the model represents the
scattering from a single particle, integrated over the particle size
distribution (PSD) of the aerosol, and normalized by a particular
integrated moment. In its most general form, the model in-
corporates all aspects of the optical properties of the particulates,
the statistical properties of the aerosol distribution, the attributes
of the incident light (including polarization), and the detection
geometry.
