The polarization dependent Mie scattering functions S1,2 used in
the calculations were Matlab implementations of functional forms
derived by Bohren and Huffman [12]. They convey the dependence of
the scattered ﬁeld on particle composition (refractive index), size and
wavelength. The subscripts 1,2 on the Mie scattering functions refer
to the direction of polarization, either perpendicular or parallel to the
scattering plane. In the present case, the incident laser light is un-
polarized, so the scattered power is the average of the two. Thus, the
integral sums the scattered light contributions from all particles in
the distribution, weighted by P d
( ), the size distribution function for
particles of diameter d. The integral over the particle size distribution
was computed numerically in Matlab using the adaptive Simpson
quadrature routine with the default error tolerance of 106. The
angular integration over the collection aperture was computed ex-
actly. This result was also compared with that obtained by treating
the central value as a constant across the angular aperture. For the six
degree collection angle of the device used here, the two results
agreed to better than one tenth of one percent in all cases. It is as-
sumed here that the particles are spherical, for which the scattered
power has no azimuthal angular dependence (it only depends on θ).
TEM analysis of material collected in similar experiments indicates
that this is a valid assumption for many but not all of the materials
tested [13], but a detailed characterization of the particle geometry
has not been performed. Nonetheless, the forward model can be
modiﬁed to accommodate non-spherical particle geometries if
needed.
It is clear from Eq. (1) that the scattered power is proportional
to the number concentration, N. The scattered power can also be
related to the surface area concentration (proportional to the
second moment) or the volume concentration (proportional to the
third moment) by making use of the identities
