A fundamental and useful feature here is the separation of the con-
stants CV1 and CV2. The former is a property of the hardware only, and
is uniquely determined by calibration with a reference aerosol. Any
aerosol can be used as long as the concentration and angular de-
pendent scattering properties are known. An improved method for
determining CV2, bounding the resulting measurement errors, and
optimizing the optical geometry is described as follows.
Although the distribution and refractive index of the aerosol to
be measured may not be known in advance, approximate ranges
for these parameters are often available. A candidate set is speci-
ﬁed, and is meant to designate the collection of aerosols with
characteristic parameters lying within these anticipated ranges.
Separate ranges can be speciﬁed for parameters such as the Count
Median Diameter, standard deviation, refractive index, or even the
distribution model itself. Expressed in in terms of this candidate
set, CV2 is given by
