M is the number of aerosol distributions comprising the candidate
set, and (Pi(θV)/V)model is the scattered power (normalized by vo-
lume concentration) for one distribution (distribution i, where i is
between 1 and M) (see Eq. (2)). Any of the variable ranges in the
candidate set can be uniformly populated, or weighted to reﬂect
expected conditional probabilities as dictated by the application.
In essence, then, CV2 as deﬁned represents an average over a set of
possible candidate aerosols.
As an example, one such aerosol size distribution i may be a
lognormal distribution characterized by a certain count median
diameter and geometric standard deviation, a second aerosol size
distribution j may be another distribution characterized by a dif-
ferent count median diameter and geometric standard deviation,
etc. There are M aerosol distributions deﬁned by M pairs of count
median diameter (CMD) and geometric standard deviation (GSD),
the pairs being obtained by using a range R1 of count median
diameter and a range R2 of geometric standard deviation; i.e., each
CMD/GSD pair is obtained by selecting a value of count median
diameter from the range R1 and a value of geometric standard
deviation from the range R2. Ranges R1 and R2 represent in-
formation about the aerosol. Larger ranges R1 and R2 correspond
to less information about the aerosol, while smaller ranges cor-
respond to more information about the aerosol.
The procedure for designing a sensor for measuring volume
concentration [or other moment quantity] and assessing its re-
sulting accuracy proceeds from the determination of CV2. To begin,
one must determine the optimal angle, θV, for the measurement.
This is done by utilizing any and all a priori information about the
candidate set. Then, one calculates the relative error in the cal-
culation of CV2(θV) as a function of θV. This quantity is deﬁned as
