The optimal value of θV is the one for which the relative error is
a minimum. Minimizing the relative error in this way ensures that
the conversion from optical power to volume concentration is as
independent of the modal parameters of the aerosol distribution
as possible. Clearly, the better one is able to constrain the modal
parameters of the aerosol distributions to which the MPASS is
possibly exposed (the smaller M), the better the estimates. For
example, for a single distribution, the error is zero. Referring to the
discussion of ranges R1 and R2 above, a single distribution would
correspond to a range R1 which is collapsed to just one value, and
a range R2 which is also collapsed to just one value, that is, the
count median diameter and geometric standard deviation for the
aerosol distribution are fully known, which is the case if the
aerosol to be measured is a known type of particles (see Eq. (3)).
Relative to the state-of-the-art, this approach is unique and
advantageous insofar as extensibility and optimization. Without
knowing the details of the aerosol being measured, the instrument
can be optimized for an anticipated range of interest, and a bound
established for the resulting measurement error within this range.
The hardware can be independently calibrated by virtue of the
determination of C1, and utilized to this conﬁdence level for any
aerosol representative of the candidate set. While not necessarily
optimized for the purpose, the same instrument can be applied to
aerosols with differing candidate sets and the associated error
bounds determined as well.
In the present study, the range R1 for CMD was 0.1–1.0 μm, and
the range R2 for GSD was 1.6–1.9. These values reﬂected the un-
derstanding of smoke particle distributions associated with typical
terrestrial ﬁre signatures [11], as more accurate data correspond-
ing to spacecraft electrical materials was not available when the
MPASS was originally designed. As detailed above, one detection
channel of the MPASS is optimized for the measurement of volume
concentration. To convert this volume measurement to mass, the
average density of the candidate aerosols must be speciﬁed. If the
densities of the candidate aerosols vary signiﬁcantly, then the
density information should be incorporated into the optimization
procedure, rather than used simply as a post-measurement mul-
tiplier. The refractive index is speciﬁed as purely real, and taken as
1.6, a representative value for pyrolysis products in gene"
doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2015.04.004,doi,Detection of spacecraft fire signatures and post-fire aerosols—Part I: Ground-based results,6,5,"optimal angle of detection θA for surface area measurement.
Separately, a “virtual” channel was constructed as a nonlinear
superposition of the two physical channels with the goal of pro-
ducing a composite signal that correlates with aerosol con-
centration, but is independent of the particle size distribution. A
challenge with both types of commonly used smoke detectors
(ionization and photometric) is providing a trigger or alarm level
that is distribution invariant. Calibrating the sensor to a single
aerosol results in either insufﬁcient or excessive sensitivity when
exposed to other aerosol distributions [13]. For example, ﬂame-
generated smokes exhibit relatively smaller count median dia-
meters, and in turn, smaller optical scattering cross sections. De-
pending on what aerosol is used for calibration, a photometric
smoke detector will either exhibit insufﬁcient sensitivity for
ﬂaming smokes, or excessive sensitivity for pyrolysis smokes. The
reverse behavior is observed for ionization-type detectors. This
combined feature remains a problem in utilizing smoke detectors,
and speciﬁcally in designating alarm levels. The general approach
is to increase detection sensitivity to avoid the possibility of mis-
sed or delayed alarming, but this comes at the expense of in-
creasing the probability of false triggers. Clearly, the ability to es-
tablish practical alarm levels becomes more challenging if the
sensitivity of the detector varies signiﬁcantly from one aerosol to
another. However, variations in detector response as a function of
the underlying aerosol distributions have received little attention.
The techniques developed her provide a means for bounding these
variations and reducing them.
This concept is demonstrated here for alarm levels corre-
sponding to either number or mass concentration. The signals
from the two channels were input to a third-order multivariate
polynomial model, although this functional form was not selected
on any particular underlying physical basis:
