A consistent feature in all tests is illustrated in Fig. 6. Shown are
the initial transients from the MPASS and the CSA-CP [15], a multi-
component gas sensor. Apparent is the delay in onset of detection
of roughly 160 s for the CSA-CP. This occurred despite the CSA-CP
being placed internal to the glovebox volume and the MPASS being
connected via a sample tube through one of the external ports.
This behavior is observed for all of the various gas composition
instruments, including a number of research devices being eval-
uated for this purpose. Given the criticality of response time in ﬁre
abatement, the temporal sensitivity of the particle sensor is an
important feature.
The overall trend in average particle size and mass production
is shown in Fig. 7, obtained directly from the two reference in-
struments. For each material group, the mass concentration and
dm¯ are plotted as a function of the tube heater temperature. Both
quantities are seen to increase with temperature until a peak value
is reached, followed by a reduction as the temperature is increased
further. This trend is not as clear in the mass concentration data
for Kapton and wire, but may be an artifact of the paucity of data
for these two materials. The inﬂection point with temperature is
also observed to occur roughly 50 °C higher than for the Standard
Mix. The geometry and heater temperatures used in these tests do
not precisely replicate actual overheating conditions, but it is
useful to observe that particle size and mass production for these
materials correlate with the temperature at which they are
pyrolized.
Fig. 8 illustrates the performance of the MPASS in measuring
mass, the original design intent. The results are observed to be in
good agreement when the material groups are segregated; i.e. the
Standard Mix and Teﬂon/Kapton combinations treated separately.
It is signiﬁcant that the individual test points correspond to dif-
ferent heater temperatures. This in turn produces aerosols with
varying modal parameters, validating the goal of measuring spe-
ciﬁed moments independent of these variations. That said, it is
also desirable to be insensitive to material type, and the differing
slopes in Fig. 8 demonstrate that this was not fully realized. The
densities of Teﬂon and Kapton are relatively large (2.2 and 1.4 g/cc,
respectively) compared to other common insulating materials, e.g.
