As discussed in Section 3, providing reliable smoke detectors
relies in part on establishing uniform trigger response. For a spe-
ciﬁed metric, e.g. number, mass concentration, etc., the response
should be invariant with aerosol modal properties. Eq. (4) was
introduced to explain how a single detector system can be to
measure a moment average, and desensitized to modal variations.
In a similar fashion, the two detectors in the MPASS were com-
bined as described in Eq. (5), using the entire test matrix as the
learning set. The results of the third-order multivariate inversion
are shown in Figs. 9 and 10 for mass and number concentration,
respectively. Shown in each ﬁgure is the variance, the average
deviation about the mean response. In the former, an 8.2:1 im-
provement in uniformity of response is observed. In the latter, a
2.1:1 improvement is obtained relative to that available from ei-
ther of the individual channels, despite the fact that the sensor
was not designed to measure the 0th moment average.
5. Discussion
Taken together, the sample materials, test matrix, and mea-
surement instruments provide useful information about the re-
sulting ﬁre signatures and strategies for detection and post-ﬁre
characterization. For spacecraft applications, the samples used in
these tests are of particular interest due their widespread use in
electronics assemblies and electrical wiring. In the ultimate sense,
hybrid, intelligent systems composed of both particle and gas
composition sensors can provide the most comprehensive means
for accurate and reliable response. Independently or as part of a
system, the parallel objective of maximizing the information
available from particle sensors remains.
The temporal responsivity contrast displayed in Fig. 6 reveals a
critical feature. The rapid nature of direct optical detection is not
equaled by species-selective technologies presently available.
While composition may provide a more deﬁnitive discrimination
against false events, striving to do so directly with rapid optical
detection offers a clear advantage. Implementing this successfully
is also attractive from the standpoint of overall system complexity
and cost.
From the perspective of device fabrication, Fig. 8 demonstrates
that a simple, single channel scattering sensor can determine a
speciﬁed integral moment (volume, in this case) with ﬁdelity
when appropriately designed. This is a useful ﬁnding, insofar as
the ability to effectively design early warning smoke detectors
relative to a pre-speciﬁed trigger metric. It is seen that the present
single detector conﬁguration displays a degree of undesirable
material dependency. However, it is noted that a single ﬁxed value
of np was used in the optimization, and more recent reduced-
gravity tests for these same materials provide more accurate va-
lues for the ranges in modal parameters [12]. Recent ground-based
tests have been performed with an identical facility, and the in-
clusion of a reference SMPS and TEOM will allow the details of the
size distributions to be better resolved. At present, the data ana-
lysis from these tests has not been completed. Reﬁnement of these
parameter values is anticipated to improve the response function
relative to the current design.
In the context of Eq. (5), it is seen that further, multi-fold im-
provement can be realized with an additional channel, retaining a
