doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2015.04.004,doi,Detection of spacecraft fire signatures and post-fire aerosols—Part I: Ground-based results,9,8,"device that is still comparatively simple. This result is demon-
strated in Figs. 9 and 10. Again, optical scattering sensors offer a
broader attraction, in that additional detection channels, as well as
source wavelengths and polarization can be included to address
and/or reﬁne multiple measurement parameters. This can be ac-
complished while in large part preserving the inherent simplicity
and low cost of the overall device.
The extension to two channels affords other useful information.
The information conveyed by size dependence is supported by the
signatures themselves, as seen for example in Figs. 7 and 9. The
former demonstrates segregation in mass average diameter among
materials, as well as a general trend in the relationship to pyrolysis
temperature. The latter speaks to the recognized distinction be-
tween ﬂaming (via ignition) and smoldering materials. The ob-
served nature of mass production rate is also a consideration. As
seen in Fig. 5, this rate remains relatively constant, independent of
signiﬁcant changes in the associated number concentration and
sample temperature. This promotes the perspective that using
mass loading as the sole basis for detection may omit useful in-
formation pertaining to the signature as well as possible back-
grounds. The suppression of nuisance triggers on the basis of
characteristic size provides further motivation for the construction
of sensor able to suitably resolve ﬁrst-order moments; i.e. various
moment diameters.
6. Conclusion
The tests described here are an initial and useful step in char-
acterizing ﬁre signatures from spacecraft materials, and in de-
signing sensors for space applications. The goal is to provide ad-
vanced sensors for both early warning ﬁre detection, and for the
characterization of post-ﬁre environments. The former involves
the reduction of false triggers, both through ﬂattening the sensor
response relative to predetermined metrics, and by the dis-
crimination of nuisance aerosols. The latter is reﬂective of our
increasing understanding of particle size and concentration insofar
as the linkage between exposure and respiratory health.
A number of materials relevant to spacecraft applications were
pyrolized and ignited using a tube furnace and glovebox facility
designed for this purpose. A suite of reference instruments were
utilized to characterize both the chemical and particle products
that were produced. A prototype scattering sensor for measuring
aerosol particles was simultaneously evaluated.
The resulting aerosols generally grouped in terms of average
particle size according to the speciﬁc material, and demonstrated a
consistent trend in size versus heater temperature. This difference
was also observed between smoldering and ﬂaming conditions.
This indicates the possibility that resolving size distributions or
integral moments might eventually be used to identify the nature
or evolution of an event, in addition to the discrimination of nui-
sance backgrounds. In all cases, the temporal response of the
scattering sensor was signiﬁcantly more rapid than the chemical
species sensors, reinforcing the utility of the former insofar as
immediacy of detection.
The scattering sensor demonstrated close agreement with the
reference TEOM in the determination of mass, a parameter for
which the design was optimized. Some residual material depen-
dence was observed, but is anticipated to be reduced when more
complete and accurate data on aerosol modal parameters becomes
available. Separately, a “virtual” channel was constructed as a
nonlinear superposition of the two physical channels of the scat-
tering sensor. A third-order multivariate inversion was applied,
with the goal of ﬂattening the detection sensitivity with respect to
the aerosol modal parameters. This process demonstrated multi-
fold improvement for the characterization of both number con-
centration and mass. This feature is a key step in the reduction of
false triggers by virtue of reducing the dependence of detection
sensitivity on speciﬁc aerosol modal parameters.
