Although (Eqs. (4) and (5)) are remarkable in their simplicity,
there is no universally accepted procedure for evaluating various
parameters. The temperature at which gas properties are eval-
uated, the value of Tf and Tv to be used in determining F, and how
to include the effect of the boundary layer on Vg are all subject to
differing interpretation [16,1,10].
3. Onset of the kinetic effect
One of the major challenges in acquiring data in the thermal
regime is the onset of gas-phase kinetic effect when the gas re-
sidence time tg,res decreases to the level of the combustion time
tg,chem. Their ratio, called the Damkohler number, decreases as the
same as the opposed ﬂow velocity is increased (at a given oxygen
level), leading to blow-off extinction. In the downward spreading
conﬁguration in a normal-gravity environment, buoyancy gener-
ated ﬂow, which scales with
