Quenching extinction of solid sphere diffusion flames induced by a sudden 
removal of gravity 
Michael C. Johnston a,c,d, James S. T′ien a,*, Sheng-Yen Hsu b, Ching-Wei Wu b, Sandra L. Olson c, 
Paul V. Ferkul d 
a Case Western Reserve University, USA 
b National Sun Yat-Sen University, Taiwan 
c National Aeronautics and Space Administration, USA 
d Universities Space Research Association, USA   
A R T I C L E I N F O  
Keywords: 
Flame extinction 
Thermally-thick solid 
Flame quenching 
Microgravity 
PMMA 
Transient flame response 
A B S T R A C T  
The response of a diffusion flame around a PMMA solid sphere to a sudden transition to zero-gravity is inves­
tigated both experimentally and numerically. The initial flame is established in normal gravity with a low speed 
forced flow in a 17% oxygen by volume atmosphere. An abrupt (step change) normal-to-zero gravity transition 
occurs when the test package is released in the drop tower. The dynamic flame response is recorded by video and 
modeled numerically. By the end of the 5.18-s drop, the flame tip retreats, and the flame base may either remain 
stabilized near the forward stagnation region or extinguish depending on several parameters. The two param­
eters investigated are: forced flow velocity and the degree of preheating in the surface layer of the solid sample. 
The amount of preheating or equivalently the conductive flame heat loss rate to the solid interior is varied by 
controlling the duration of the normal gravity burning before releasing to microgravity. The heat loss is quan­
tified by using embedded thermocouples to measure the solid subsurface temperature gradient near the stag­
nation point. The detailed numerical model reveals details of the flow field and flame structure including 
oscillatory extinction and quantifies the various transient gas-solid surface energy balance terms.   
1. Introduction 
Quenching is a flame extinction mode due to heat losses. For diffu­
sion flames, it has been shown that quenching can be achieved aero­
dynamically by reducing the oxidizer flow strain rate (flame stretch rate) 
[1]. In a low-stretch flame, the combustion heat release rate is reduced, 
and the ratio of heat loss (radiative and conductive) to heat release in­
creases. The lower flame temperature leads to extinction. In most ap­
plications on Earth, low flow strain rate cannot be achieved due to the 
ever-present and significant buoyant flow around the flame. However, 
in the microgravity conditions of orbiting spacecraft, forced air flow can 
be arbitrarily small. For fire suppression, flame quenching can be 
attained simply by turning down the circulating air flow in the space­
craft cabin [2]. 
There are several oxidizer atmospheres being considered for future 
spacecrafts and habitats, with varying oxygen percentage and pressure 
[2]. In addition, a large variety of materials are used, and many of these 
are characterized as thick solids. Flammability of thick samples is 
affected by the degree of heat-up of the solid subsurface layer, which 
needs to be quantified in order to assess the propensity of a material to 
burn. 
In this study, quenching of a diffusion flame over a large PMMA 
sphere is examined in normal gravity as it is suddenly transitioned to 
microgravity in a drop tower facility. The initial flame is in a mixed flow 
environment consisting of a normal gravity buoyant component and a 
low speed forced flow component. When the effective gravity is abruptly 
reduced to zero as the drop is initiated, the buoyant flow rapidly decays, 
and quenching can be induced depending on the oxygen percentage, the 
forced flow speed, and the degree of sample preheating. From the point 
of view of the flame, the energy used to heat up the sample is a heat loss. 
This heat loss can be quantified by using the sub-surface temperature 
gradient at the time of the drop initiation. More details will be given 
later. 
