2. Background 
2.1. Solid material flammability in reduced gravity (low-stretch flames) 
The first line of defense in spacecraft fire safety efforts is to select 
materials based on their flammability performance [2–5]. In micro- or 
partial-gravity, the reduced buoyant flows, coupled with low-speed air 
circulation currents, create an environment quite different than that in 
normal Earth gravity. In addition, there are several oxidizer atmo­
spheres under consideration for crew vehicles and habitats, which 
expand the scope of problem. A fundamental understanding of solid 
flammability as a function of gravity [6–12], flow velocity and oxygen 
percentage [e.g. Refs. [13–24], pressure [e.g. Refs. [12–14,23–32]], and 
sample size and configuration [e.g. Refs. [33–40]] yields important 
contributions both to combustion science and to the human space 
exploration initiatives. There are several reviews on flame spread and 
extinction over solids [e.g. Refs. [41–48]]. Microgravity solid combus­
tion has been reviewed specifically in Refs. [5,49,50]. 
Extinction limits are an important measure of flammability since 
they define the range of conditions for which a material cannot burn 
without external heat flux. When a solid burns, a diffusion flame is 
formed, and the rate of ambient oxygen supplied to the flame zone af­
fects the reaction rate. An oxygen supply rate that is too large can cause 
blowoff extinction while one that is too low can cause radiative 
quenching [1]. Low-speed radiative quenching requires the elimination 
or suppression of buoyant-induced flow, and so a microgravity envi­
ronment is normally needed for experimental studies [17–21,34,51–54]. 
Opportunities to perform long duration microgravity combustion 
experiments are scarce. A dedicated solid fuel combustion project SoFIE 
(Solid Fuel Ignition and Extinction) consisting of multiple investigations 
has recently begun aboard the International Space Station [55]. But just 
using Earth-based facilities, low-stretch buoyant diffusion flames can be 
obtained with the proper setup using large samples or burners as 
demonstrated in Refs. [56–59]. Partly because of the large sample sizes, 
different types of near-limit flamelets were observed before quenching. 
2.2. Spherical sample 
Small spherical solid fuels have been used as a surrogate in two phase 
particle combustion [76]. The use of larger solid spheres is less common. 
Burning spheres of different radii in microgravity drop tower tests shows 
the flame shape evolution under a slow forced convective flow [60] and 
demonstrates distinctive features from a fluid physics point of view. 
Flow around a large sphere has three regions with different fluid 
mechanic and heat transfer characteristics. These are the forward stag­
nation point, shoulder (equator), and wake. When a flame is stabilized 
around a sphere [61,62], the flame at the forward stagnation-point re­
gion can be considered locally one-dimensional with nearly uniform 
flame temperature and flame standoff distance. This simplified flame 
geometry has been used extensively in gaseous flames as well as in some 
solid flames [e.g. Refs. [63–65]]. In forced, buoyant or mixed flows 
around a sphere, stagnation point flow is characterized by the flow 
strain or flame stretch rate [7]: 
