2.3. Sample heating-up and flame heat loss 
Because of its large thermal inertia, a thick solid may take a long time 
to heat up. Even after ignition is reached and the igniter input is 
removed, the solid still continues to be heated by the adjacent flame. To 
illustrate the heat-up process, we use a one-dimensional solid and a 
Cartesian coordinates system shown in Fig. 1. The moving (relative to 
the lab) coordinate has y = 0 attached to the solid surface which has a 
regression rate r (cm/s). The energy balance equation for the solid is: 
