After the removal of the igniter heat input (qig = 0), Eq. (6) shows 
that λs(∂Ts/∂y)s, the heat flux into the solid interior, has to become small 
enough for the fuel mass flux ˙m to reach the critical value for ignition. 
With the presence of the external ignition heat flux (qig), this defines the 
critical heat flux limit of the igniter-assisted ignition. When the igniter is 
turned off (qig = 0), λs(∂Ts/∂y)s needs to be even smaller to permit self- 
sustained ignition [66]. After self-sustained ignition is achieved, the 
surface pyrolysis temperature is almost constant but the flame continues 
to heat the solid and ˙m increases until steady state is reached. From the 
point of view of the flame, there is a continual conductive heat loss from 
the flame into the solid as it heats up. 
Because of the large thermal inertia of the solid and the small solid 
burning rate, the characteristic time for the burning PMMA solid to 
reach steady state is around several minutes (~αs/r2), while the char­
acteristic time for the adjacent gas flame is less than 1 s (~a−1). So, the 
gas flame can be considered to be in a quasi-steady state. During this 
heat-up period, the gas flame continues to lose heat to the solid. So, in 
addition to intrinsic radiative losses, this is an additional conductive 
heat loss from the point-view of the gaseous flame that can affect 
extinction limits if an extinction maneuver (e.g., varying flow speed, 
reduce ambient oxygen percentage or depressurization) is exercised. As 
illustrated in Fig. 1, the flame during this period can have steeper solid 
temperature gradient near the surface which affects flame standoff, 
flame temperature and solid burning rate. 
Note that we have used a one-dimensional solid to illustrate the solid 
heat up transient. In the experiment to be discussed, the solid is a sphere. 
So, a steady solution such as in Eq. (4) does not exist. But the long 
transient of the solid temperature near the surface is qualitatively 
similar, especially near the stagnation point flow region. For sphere, the 
exact multi-dimensional conduction equation will be solved. 
2.4. How to quantify conductive heat loss from a quasi-steady gaseous 
flame? 
Theoretically, using a specified surface temperature or equivalently, 
a pyrolysis law relating surface temperature and burning rate, together 
with specifying λs(∂Ts/∂y)s in the interphase energy balance Eq. (5), the 
boundary conditions are complete for the quasi-steady gas-phase sys­
tem. So, with the knowledge of λs(∂Ts/∂y)s, we can address the gas flame 
extinction question without any additional information about the solid. 
Note that flame extinction corresponds to when the gas phase reactions 
cease. But, how to quantify the solid heat loss? 
