Regardless of the choice of terminology, experimentally we need to 
measure the sub-surface temperature gradient during the solid heat-up 
period to characterize the state of the quasi-steady flame. The flame 
heat loss (or equivalently the surface temperature gradient) is largest 
right after ignition and decreases monotonically with time. The flame 
temperature starts low and gradually increases with time. This is 
consistent with our experience of the color evolution in the burning of a 
bulk solid [e.g., 68]. In this work, the sub-surface temperature gradient 
is measured using an array of fine thermocouples near the flow stag­
nation plane. This is a meticulous task and is believed to be the first such 
effort. Details are given in the next section. 
3. Experimental 
3.1. Sample fabrication 
Cast polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) spheres of 4-cm diameter are 
used as the test samples. A key parameter in this combustion experiment 
is the degree of sample heat-up. Since the energy for sample heat-up 
after the ignition comes from the adjacent gas flame, the degree of 
sample heat-up is directly related to the conductive heat loss from the 
flame. For example, during the early heat-up period, the gas flame has a 
large heat loss and a lower fame temperature and is more susceptible to 
quenching. Quantifying the rate of conductive heat loss is therefore 
needed for the study of extinction limits during this period. For the 
present spherical sample, flame extinction (quenching or local blowoff) 
is controlled by the processes near the forward stagnation point region. 
So, the rate of heat loss to the solid is measured using a series of ther­
mocouples placed in the sample stagnation-point region. The sub- 
surface temperature gradient is deduced from the temperature profile 
and the heat loss rate is obtained by multiplying it with the solid thermal 
conduction coefficient. 
Drilling holes in the spherical fuel samples for the insertion of mul­
tiple thermocouples would be very challenging. Even if this could be 
done, contact between the thermocouple and the solid would be un­
certain. Instead, we have devised a new way to embed thermocouples 
during the PMMA casting process, described below: 
To locate the thermocouples in their desired positions, they are first 
affixed by local melting to a thin circular PMMA disc (1-mm thick and 4- 
cm diameter) as shown in Fig. 2. The beads are pressed slightly into the 
plate using a fine-tip soldering iron. A 304-stainless steel tube (3.175 
mm outside diameter, 0.71 mm wall thickness) is epoxied to the plate 
and serves both as the fuel sample holder and as a protective conduit for 
the thermocouple lead wires. The PMMA fuel spheres (4-cm diameter) 
are cast around each instrumented disc by a specialist manufacturer 
(Meisner Acrylic Casting1). Note that all the thermocouples are located 
on the same central plane of the cast sphere. Three of the thermocouples 
are along the symmetry line. As shown in the right image of Fig. 2, the 
first thermocouple is located 2–3 mm from the surface and the next two 
are approximately 2 mm apart. Two other thermocouples are placed off 
the symmetry line (15 and 30◦) to provide information on the extent of 
interior solid heating during the flame spreading phase. The thermo­
couples are K-type 40-gauge fine wire (0.0787 mm diameter) fine wire 
with PFA insulation. The bead size is about twice the wire diameter. The 
thermocouples were casted inside the PMMA sample, so the contact with 
the samples is excellent. The solid temperatures vary slowly so there 
should be no time lag issue. 
Computed tomography (CT) x-ray images of all the finished spheres 
are obtained (for example as shown on the right side of Fig. 2) in order to 
determine the locations of the thermocouple beads. Knowing the exact 
locations of the beads from the CT images allows us to compute the solid 
temperature profile, the temperature gradient at the surface, and the 
heat flux. The CT scan is repeated for each sample after a test if the 
sample is to be re-used for another experiment, since the surface retreats 
due to ignition and burning. The right side of Fig. 2 shows the CT scan 
after a burn test, and a bubble layer close to the surface is visible which is 
typical in the burning of cast PMMA [e.g. Ref. [75]]. Reduction of 
temperature gradient using this thermocouple arrangement has been 
done in a previous paper to study ignition [66] and more details can be 
found in Ref. [68]. 
3.2. Test setup 
In the experiment, a 4-cm-diameter PMMA fuel sphere is suspended 
by its 3.175 mm outer diameter stainless steel support tube in the center 
of a 21-cm-diameter vertically oriented pressurized wind tunnel as 
shown in Fig. 3(d). Upward gas flow is controlled and provided by two 
selectable oxidizer cylinders holding 21% and 17% oxygen by volume. 
This tunnel and all the support hardware is contained within a drop 
vehicle for use in the NASA Zero Gravity Research Facility’s (ZGRF) 
5.18 s drop tower. 
3.3. Experimental procedure 
The sphere is ignited in normal Earth gravity in the forward (bottom) 
stagnation region of the sphere by a 28 AWG Kanthal A-1 hotwire coil 
powered to 65 Watts for 20 s in an imposed low speed upward flow of 
21% oxygen. After the igniter coil is de-energized, it is retracted, and the 
flame is allowed to spread around the spherical sample. The gas is 
changed to 17% oxygen when the flame is well established. During the 
normal gravity burn, the interior solid temperature increases continu­
ously. The operator monitored the reading of the first embedded ther­
mocouple reading. After it reaches a target value (varied from test to 
test), the experiment vehicle is released causing a step change from 
normal gravity to microgravity. Fig. 3 (d) shows the test sample and 
igniter configurat"
