5
3.5. Test matrix 
Thirteen drops have been carried out. The test numbers are shown in 
Table 1 together with the testing parameters: flow velocity and sub- 
surface temperature gradient. In addition, the stagnation point flow 
strain rate is shown (related to flow velocity using Eq. (1)). The sub- 
surface temperature gradient is measured at the forward stagnation 
point region just before the drop. The conductive heat loss rate from the 
flame to the solid interior is also listed which is just the multiplication of 
the temperature gradient by the solid conduction coefficient at the 
surface temperature, λs (¼0.132 W/m/K). In the operation, the different 
amount of conductive heat loss is achieved by varying the amount of 
time that the sample is burned in normal gravity before the drop. So, 1-g 
flame is used as a sample preheat tool. Note that ignition is in 21% 
oxygen flow, oxygen is turned down to 17% before the drop (see Ap­
pendix 1). The last column in Table 1 lists the durations of 1 g burning 
for different test cases. These numbers are approximate with a range 
because the ignition duration is substantial. Typically, the igniter turn- 
on time is 25s. The actual ignition delay time can vary depending on 
oxygen percentage, flow velocity and igniter-sample distance [67]. 
Cutoff the igniter too fast may quench the flame that requires repeated 
ignition as indicated in some cases in Table 1. Energizing the igniter for 
too long results in an externally boosted strong flame. So, in Table 1, a 
range is listed based on the inclusion of a portion of the igniter-on 
duration (see Appendix 1 and [68]). It should be emphasized that this 
column of 1 g burning time is listed only for reference purpose. The 
measured heat loss in Table 1 is used for data analysis. 
3.4. Determination of heat loss to the solid interior 
In this experiment, the flame started at the forward stagnation point 
and spread downstream. The highest heat flux and the maximum gas- 
phase reaction rate are located in the forward stagnation region. 
Therefore, sub-surface heat flux to the solid interior in this region, λs(∂T/ 
∂y)|s, in Eq. (5) is used as the quantitative measure of the degree of solid 
heat-up in this work. The subsurface temperature gradient is evaluated 
at this location by using an exponential curve fit of the measured interior 
temperature data along the centerline of the sample sphere as well as an 
assumed surface pyrolysis temperature. The heat conductivity of PMMA 
is a function of temperature [69] as shown in Table 2. The value of λs 
used in this work is 0.132 W/m K, which is evaluated at 675 K, the 
approximate pyrolysis temperature. In comparison, λs is 0.2 W/m K at 
room temperature. The details on how to determine the temperature 
gradient are illustrated using an example in Appendix 1. 
3.6. Extinction boundary 
Fig. 4 is the flammability map. The horizontal axes are forced flow 
velocity after the drop and the corresponding flow strain rate (flame 
stretch rate) at the flow stagnation point. The vertical axes are the solid 
sub-surface temperature gradient prior to the drop and the corre­
sponding flame heat loss to the solid interior. All the thirteen tests in 
Table 1 are indicated in this figure. The tests for which the flame 
extinguished within the available 5.18 s of microgravity time are plotted 
as open circles, and those for which the flame survived are plotted as 
closed circles. An approximate extinction boundary is shown as a dotted 
line. The extinction boundary is better thought of as an uncertainty band 
to allow for stochastic extinction, especially with the limited number of 
test points. In addition, some of the surviving flames may go out given 
more microgravity time. The shape of the flame quenching boundary at 
low flow speeds has been predicted by a recent model [67]. The model 
also predicts that extinction boundary should drop off at higher velocity 
(blowoff) which is beyond our present velocity range. 
Also, two series (1 and 2) are denoted in Fig. 4 to isolate the effect of 
flow speed and the conductive heat loss into the solid. In Series 1, the 
freestream velocity is approximately the same, but the heat losses are 
varying. In series 2, the heat losses are approximately the same (1.43 ±
0.13 W/cm2), but the flow velocities are varying. They are highlighted 
for parametric study of the 1 g-to-0g transition study to be discussed 
later. 
3.7. 1 g–0 g transitional flames 
The time sequences of two drop tests are compared. In one, the flame 
extinguishes (Test #2) and in the other, it survives (Test #5). Digital 
video images of selected drop tests can be found in the supplemental 
files. 
The time series of photographs for Test #2 is shown in Fig. 5. The 
flow velocity, oxygen concentration, and internal temperature profile 
are established before the drop (t = 0 s). The heat loss into the solid 
