doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2024.104137,doi,Quenching extinction of solid sphere diffusion flames induced by a sudden removal of gravity,6,5,"Fire Safety Journal 146 (2024) 104137
6
interior at the stagnation zone is 1.45 W/cm2 and oxidizer forced flow 
velocity is 1.98 cm/s. Soon after the drop, the sudden reduction of 
buoyant air velocity creates a velocity imbalance at the initial reaction 
zone. An abrupt movement of the flame front away from the surface was 
noted. This gas-phase transient creates a temporary fuel-rich mixture at 
elevated temperature that is favorable to soot formation. Fig. 5 shows 
that soot starts at 0.2 s and reaches its brightest around 0.37 s after the 
drop. At the same time, the lack of the accelerated buoyant airflow 
causes the flame tip in the wake region moves away from the surface. 
After 0.7 s, the flame tip starts to retreat upstream towards the forward 
zone and sooting subsides. In some drop tests the soot blows off down­
stream, but here the soot uniformly dissipates, and the flame color turns 
from orange to blue. The flame tip progressively retreats towards the 
forward stagnation region and the standoff distance relaxes towards the 
solid surface before quenching. The non-monotonic flame movement at 
the stagnation zone has been reported in a previous work [70]. Note that 
the stagnation flow strain rate, af, for this case is 1.46 s−1 (Table 1). The 
estimated gas phase diffusion time scale in the stagnation region (1/af) is 
0.68 s, The gas residence time through the sphere shoulder (R/V∞ = 2 
cm/1.95 cm/s) is ~1 s. These are consistent with the time scales of flame 
variations observed in the experiment. 
The time sequence of pictures for Test #5 is shown in Fig. 6. 
Although Test #5 has a higher heat loss to the solid (2.38 W/cm2) 
compared with that in Test #2, the forced flow velocity is substantially 
higher (24 cm/s), the stagnation flame standoff distance is much 
smaller, and the flame survives the transition to microgravity. The brief 
transient appearance of soot right after the drop occurs further down­
stream in the wake zone where gas residence time is longer. With the 
higher flow, the gas-phase flame reaches a quasi-steady state sooner as 
evident in the last few images. Comparing the first and last images in 
Fig. 6, the difference in flame appearance between purely forced flow 
(last photo) and mixed flow (first photo) is clear. In forced flow, the 
flame is shorter with a more open flame tip, while in mixed flow the 
accelerating buoyant flow extends the flame tip downstream around the 
spew portion of the sample in the wake zone. Without the sample spew, 
it is expected that the mixed flow flame would be close tipped with a 
teardrop shape [60]. Because of the higher forced flow velocity, the 
mixed flow speed in the stagnation region does not appreciably differ 
compared to forced flow alone and so there is not much of a difference in 
standoff distance before and during the drop. Because the standoff dis­
tance doesn’t change much when the forced flow is high, the flame 
survives in microgravity despite the higher heat loss to the solid interior 
compared to Test #2. 
3.8. Effects of heat loss to the solid and flow velocity 
To isolate the effect of heat loss to the solid, we compare the flame 
images from three tests in Series 1 as indicated in Fig. 4. Here, the 
freestream forced-flow velocity is fixed at 6 cm/s, but solid heat losses 
levels are varied. Fig. 7 shows the images around 3.9 s after the drop. In 
Test #4 with heat loss 2.45 W/cm2, the flame retreats to the forward 
stagnation point region and quenches. In Test #11 (1.31 W/cm2) and 
Test #10 (0.85 W/cm2), the flames survive. Test #10, with smaller heat 
loss, has the brightest flame intensity. The correspondingly higher 
burning intensity in this test causes sub-surface bubble bursting evident 
as yellow flashes as shown in the images. 
To isolate the effect of flow velocity, we compare flame images from 
Series 2 as indicated in Fig. 4. For these tests, the heat losses to solid are 
all around 1.4 W/cm2 but flow velocity varies from 2 to 14.5 cm/s. Fig. 8 
shows that for Test #2, having a freestream velocity of 2 cm/s, the flame 
retreats upstream to the stagnation zone and extinguishes. The other 
tests with higher freestream velocities, survive the 5.18 s of micro­
gravity. The flame in test #9 at 14.5 cm/s is the strongest, extending 
well into the downstream region. It is clear that flame intensity and 
length depend strongly on the air flow velocity. 
