transient flow pattern and the solid-gas energy balance at the fuel sur­
face during this transition, we use a detailed transient numerical model. 
The key elements of the model are first described and then followed by 
computed solutions and a comparison with experiment. 
The transient, axisymmetric (two-dimensional) numerical model is 
created with ANSYS Fluent [71]. The full unsteady Navier-Stokes mo­
mentum, species, and energy equations are solved by direct numerical 
simulation. The solid is assumed to pyrolyze to a monomer (MMA fuel 
vapor) on the surface through a zeroth-order Arrhenius reaction. In the 
gas phase, a one-step overall combustion reaction between MMA vapor 
and oxygen is assumed. The solid interior is assumed to have pure heat 
conduction. Coupling with the gas phase is through the interfacial en­
ergy balance Eq. (5) and an assumed one-step surface pyrolysis reaction 
for the solid mass burning rate (g/cm3/s). Gas radiation is neglected in 
the model, so qr,in in Eq. (5) is zero in this simulation. 
