10 cm in radius x 35 cm long and contains 110,000 grid points. The 
minimum grid size is 0.1 mm near the solid surface, expanding up to 9 
mm far from the sphere. The sample sphere front surface is placed 10 cm 
downstream of the domain inlet. The solid-phase heat conduction model 
contains 100,000 grid points, with minimum size 0.1 mm–0.6 mm with 
the smaller grid close to the surface. The computational time step before 
the drop is 0.005 s, and 0.001 s after the drop. In most cases, compu­
tation ends 5.18 s after the drop (to match the available experiment 
duration). However, in some near-limit cases, the microgravity 
computation is continued for an additional 15 s to resolve the eventual 
fate of the flame. 
Inlet conditions are set to match those of each experiment test. A no 
slip condition is imposed on the outer wall boundary and zero gradient 
on the domain outlet. A no slip condition is imposed tangential to the 
solid fuel surface, but fuel mass is injected in accordance with the 
vaporization rate. 
4.2. Transient computation 
The unsteady computation starts with a simulated ignition proced­
ure. An energy input within a small volume (0.0264 cm3) in the up­
stream forward stagnation region of the PMMA sphere is sustained for 
10 s. During these 10 s, the igniter region generates 2.64 W of heat to 
reach a local maximum gas phase temperature of ~2400 K (including 
the combustion heat release). After the ignition source is removed, 
computation continues in normal gravity for a specified period before 
the removal of gravity in the model. For the purpose of this simulation, 
this normal-gravity burning period is referred to as the “sample preheat- 
up time” since the self-sustained flame in mixed flow will heat-up the 
outer sublayer of the solid interior during the flame development prior 
to the drop test. We use two preheating times in the simulations: 40 s and 
120 s. From the computed data of the solid interior, the sub-surface 
conduction heat loss at these two times is 2.60 and 1.45 W/cm2 
respectively, close to the experimental values of 2.45 and 1.31 as shown 
in Fig. 7. Note that we did not try to simulate the experimental ignition 
sequence and only tried to match the temperature gradient at the start of 
the experimental drop. 
After the desired preheating time in 1-g, gravity is turned off in the 
model (corresponding to dropping the sample package in the drop 
tower). Five computational runs are carried out with three forced-flow 
velocities and two preheating times. The preheating time of the solid 
is just the duration of normal gravity portion of the burn. These cases are 
listed in Table 4, with the prefix “M” designating a modeled computation 
for the corresponding experiment. The computed solid temperatures 
compare very well with the measured ones from the embedded ther­
mocouples during the preheat periods [68]. 
4.3. Modeling results 
Selected results of the numerical model are presented. The dynamic 
response of the surface heat fluxes will be highlighted. The flame states 
of the computed cases (extinction or survival) at the end of the 5.18 s 
microgravity duration are consistent to those in the experiment. Videos 
of transient flame and solid response can be viewed in the supplemental 
files. 
4.3.1. Computed case M−2 and comparison with experiment test #2 
An example calculation is shown in Fig. 9. The left side of each panel 
shows a brightened video frame from the experiment to help delineate 
the outer flame sheet standoff distance, and the right side shows the 
simulated velocity vector field with reaction contour overlays. The step 
change from 1 g to 0 g occurs at 120 s in the simulation (i.e., normal 
gravity preheat time is 120 s). The first panel shows the trickle forced 
flow of oxidizer far from the sphere at 2 cm/s and accelerating through 
the flame zone as it gets close to the sphere surface. The velocity 
downstream of the sphere is relatively high with a fast recirculation zone 
forming in the wake region. By t = 120.12 s (0.12 s after gravity is 
removed), the velocity field has already shed most of its excess mo­
mentum and the maximum velocities become significantly slower. The 
visible flame sheet and reaction contours have moved away from the 
sphere surface. At 0.36 s after the drop, the flame length is truncated, 
and a recirculation zone starts to form downstream of the flame. The off- 
axis vaporization provides lateral thrust that will push the flame tip 
outward shown by the red arrows. Some acceleration is still visible in the 
velocity vector field due to added mass from vaporization and reaction 
products combined with thermal expansion and confinement within the 
flow tunnel. By 120.9 s, the flame tip in the experiment has opened, with 
the model lagging by about 1 s. The panel at 122.46 s shows the flame tip 
retreating upstream towards the forward stagnation region as indicated 
by the black arrows. As the flame stabilizes, a recirculation zone reforms 
in the pocket between the sphere and the spew portion of the cylinder in 
the wake zone, indicated by arrows in the model and visible as collecting 
vapor in the experiment video. The experiment corresponding to this 
case extinguishes before the end of the 5.18-s drop, but the simulation 
requires 15 more seconds to reach flame extinction. Given the approx­
imate nature of the reaction kinetics and the lack of gas-phase radiation 
loss in the model, the comparison is reasonable. 
4.3.2. Oscillatory extinction 
The above Test#2 has a retreating flame tip in the 1 g–0 g transition 
that eventually leads to quenching in the forward stagnation region. 
However, some cases (e.g., Test #4) have an oscillatory type of extinc­
tion caused by the sudden change to a non-flammable condition. The 
modeled Case (M−4) with 40-s 1-g preheat captures the flame dynamics 
of this oscillation. This is shown next. 
