stagnation point, the energy used for pyrolysis ˙m L is 1 W/cm2 and the 
heat conducted into the solid is 1.72 W/cm2. The peak heat up of the 
solid, however, corresponds to the downstream flame tip location (2.9 
W/cm2 at θ = 25◦). This peak is due to (1) the flame tip being closer to 
the solid surface due to the lack of fuel vapor blowing and (2) a lower 
local surface temperature. An approximate uniform pyrolysis rate ex­
tends from 0◦to 18◦before dropping sharply. Since pyrolysis rate has a 
stronger dependence on surface temperature than the surface radiative 
loss (Arrhenius vs. 4th power), the pyrolysis rate drops earlier compared 
to the radiative loss. If we define the location of the flame tip using the 
peak of the solid heat-up profile, the flame length is slightly longer than 
the pyrolysis length. This is typical for spreading flames over solids. 
At t = 40 s, the gravitational force is turned off in the simulation. The 
response of the flame at t = 40.15 s is shown in Fig. 10(b). The velocity 
vectors are decreasing in amplitude as the flow field is no longer under 
the influence of buoyant acceleration and sheds its momentum. The 
velocity upstream of the sphere has already decreased significantly. The 
response of the fuel vaporization rate lags because the solid thermal 
response is relatively slow. The pyrolysis rate remains high ( ˙m L ~ 0.66 
W/cm2) and this mismatch pushes the flame near the stagnation region 
upstream. On the other hand, the flame tip, with less influence from the 
decreased local pyrolysis, remains close to the surface. The flame shape 
as represented by the reaction rate contour becomes warped. This can 
also be seen from the streamlines formed between the surface and the 
flame by the fuel vapor motion. The increased flame standoff in the 
stagnation region affects the energy balance on the surface. The heat 
conduction into the solid near the stagnation region becomes negative at 
this instance, meaning the pyrolysis is temporarily being driven by the 
heat from the solid instead of from the flame. Earlier one-dimensional 
simulations also revealed this transient feature [70]. 
The sudden increase of flame standoff is followed by the flame 
rebounding back toward the surface as shown in Fig. 10(c). At 40.5 s 
(0.5 s after the drop), the flame gets closer to the surface but not as close 
as it was in 1 g. The heat loss to the solid and the pyrolysis rate are also 
smaller. Several flame oscillations occur before the flame quenches. 
Fig. 11 plots the three energy terms on the right side of Eq. (5) and 
the flame conduction from gas phase (λg(∂T/∂y)g) at the stagnation point 
as a function of time (qr,in and qig are zero in the model). The gas-phase 
conduction oscillates because the flame standoff oscillates. The solid 
conduction loss (temperature gradient) at the surface follows the trend. 
The oscillation time scale is the gas-phase transient time (1/af). The 
surface temperature and radiative loss decrease monotonically but at the 
solid time scale. The pyrolysis rate has some minor fluctuations but is 
decreasing with time. At 42.1 s, the pyrolysis rate is too low, and the 
flame goes out. Fig. 10(d) gives the snapshot at 42.7s, 0.6 s after the 
flame goes out. 
Since the flame transition in the drop test is highly dynamic, visu­
alizing the details from the transient simulation in the supplemental 
videos can boost our understanding of the events. 
5. Conclusions 
A series of short-duration microgravity combustion tests on spher­
ical, thermally thick PMMA solid fuel samples are carried out in NASA 
5.18 s drop tower. The samples are heated by the normal gravity flame 
to a specified sub-surface heated state before their release into micro­
gravity. Thirteen tests are performed with forced flow velocity (2–25 
cm/s) and degree of flame conductive loss into solid interior at the 
forward stagnation point (0.5−3 W/cm2) as variables. Flame conductive 
heat loss into the solid interior has been deduced from a series of ther­
mocouples embedded at the flow stagnation point region. This is the first 
time such quantitative measurements have been carried out for thick 
solids. All the tests were in 17% oxygen, 1 atm pressure. The major 
findings are:  
(1) At the end of the 5.18s microgravity period, some flames quench 
while others survive. Low flow velocity and high flame heat loss 
to the solid favor quenching extinction. An approximate extinc­
tion boundary is drawn.  
(2) The flame is highly transient during the drop. Immediately after 
the drop, flame standoff increases. The fuel rich zone next to the 
flame enlarges and temporary sooting occurs as the buoyant 
driving force is removed while the hot solid generates excessive 
vapor as it responds more slowly compared to the gas phase. The 
flame then settles back to a new equilibrium position but in some 
cases an oscillation is triggered. For the cases that extinguished, 
the flame tip shrinks toward the stagnation point as a weak flame 
before going out. For the surviving cases, the flames in low-speed 
forced flow are blue in color with their tips fanning out, unlike the 
characteristic tear-drop shaped flames in buoyant flow.  
(3) A multi-dimensional numerical model reveals the flow field in 
detail and the gas-solid interface energy balance during the 1 g–0 
g transition. The flow pattern can be complicated with recircu­
lation eddies and flame tip flapping, especially during flame 
oscillation. Surface energy terms show that both the gas con­
duction and the heat loss to the solid interior are highly transient 
(in gas-phase time scale) and solid heat conduction can tempo­
rarily reverse direction at the surface. The surface temperature 
and the solid burning rate, on the other hand, change slowly 
according to bulk solid time scale. This phenomenon is difficult to 
resolve quantitatively in an experiment. The transient events in 
this study "
