The temperature profile along the sample sphere symmetry line is obtained by exponential curve fit of the thermocouple data using equation (A1) 
as shown in Fig. A1. To fit the solid temperature curve, the surface temperature is assumed to be at 650K which the approximate pyrolysis temperature 
of PMMA. A derivative is computed and evaluated at the surface according to Eq. (A2) that is multiplied by λs (assumed a constant value at 0.132 W/m 
K) to yield the heat loss to the solid interior. Measured data just before the sample drop to microgravity is used. Although a simple exponential fit is 
used in this work, a comparison with the more accurate Peclet fit suggested in Ref. [64] is plotted on Fig. A1 (dotted line) and shows good agreement 
with the simple exponential fit due to very slow material regression where Peclet number for each case is evaluated and fit by linear least squares 
regression. The temperature profile from the Peclet fit can be used in the heat flux evaluation in the same way as the exponential fit with nearly 
identical results. 
It should be noted that during the sample burning in normal gravity, both the gas flame and the solid temperatures evolve slowly. But during the 1 
g–0 g transient, the gas flame together with flame standoff distance varies quickly. According to the surface balance Eq. (5), the solid temperature 
gradient needs to respond to the gas-phase change instantaneously (no time delay in Eq. (1)). This very fast change of subsurface temperature gradient 
only involves a temperature variation in a very thin layer close to the surface because of the large thermal diffusivity of the solid. There is no clear 
experimental way to measure this dynamic variation. So, the heat flux to the solid interior just before the drop is used to characterize the heat loss into 
the solid interior in the experiment. The entire dynamic transient event is instead modeled in order to better resolve the details. 
