Flame extinction of thermally thick PMMA in Earth gravity versus 
microgravity: A comparison of Earth and on-orbit space experiments$
Christina Liveretou a
,∗,1, Jose Rivera a,1, Madeleine Bardy a,2, Carlos Fernandez-Pello a,3, 
Michael Gollner a,3, Lilly Etzenbach b,4, Maria Thomsen c,3, Sandra Olson d,5, Paul Ferkul d,6
a Department of Mechanical Engineering, University of California, Berkeley, 60 Hesse Hall, Berkeley, 94720, CA, USA
b Department of Aeronautics and Astronautics, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, 77 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, 02139, MA, USA
c Faculty of Engineering and Science, Universidad Adolfo Ibanez, Av. Diag. Las Torres 2640, Santiago, 7941169, Chile
d NASA Glenn Research Center, MS 77-5, 21000 Brookpark Rd, Cleveland, 44135, OH, USA
A R T I C L E  I N F O
Keywords:
Microgravity
Flame extinction
Limiting oxygen concentration
 
A B S T R A C T
Future spacecraft may operate at sub-atmospheric pressures and elevated oxygen concentrations to reduce 
preparation time for extra-vehicular activities. It is important to understand fire behavior and the fire risk 
of materials brought aboard a spacecraft. The limiting oxygen concentration (LOC) is one of the parameters 
used to determine the flammability of solid fuels. This work aims to compare the LOC for opposed flame 
spread extinction of polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) in sub-atmospheric microgravity and Earth-gravity 
environments. Experiments with cylindrical PMMA samples are conducted in a combustion chamber in the 
Integrated Combustion Rack (CIR) on the International Space Station (ISS), as well as on laboratory facilities 
on Earth, at sub-atmospheric pressures with a forced flow of 10 cm/s opposed (counter-current) to the flame 
spread direction. The oxygen concentration in the chamber decreases naturally at a constant ambient pressure 
as a result of the PMMA combustion until flame extinction. Results show the LOC in microgravity is lower 
than at Earth gravity, meaning that flame spread can be sustained at lower oxygen concentrations in reduced 
buoyancy. The findings confirm that flammability test methods conducted in normal gravity and atmospheric 
pressure are not conservative enough for the conditions future spacecraft are expected to operate in.
1. Introduction
The frequency of human space travel has increased over the past 
decade with the growth of private companies participating in space 
programs and increasing opportunities for non-astronauts to buy a 
ticket to space. At the same time, governmental organizations such as 
NASA are proposing longer space missions with the establishment of 
facilities on the Moon and Mars. With increased time spent in space, 
microgravity fire safety is of increasing importance. Environmental 
conditions inside the cabin of a spacecraft, termed Space Exploration 
Atmospheres (SEA), are very different from those encountered on Earth: 
besides the reduced gravity and therefore buoyancy, there are also low 
