velocity flows from ventilation systems, and potentially low pressures 
and elevated oxygen concentrations [1]. Given that egress or rescue 
from a spacecraft are difficult or impossible, the consequences of a fire 
could be catastrophic. Therefore, reducing the flammability risk is key 
to improving spacecraft fire safety.
The first step to reducing fire risk in space is to ensure every 
material that is used in a habitable spacecraft passes a flammability 
test. The primary test used by NASA is fire safety standard STD-6001B, 
in which the material is exposed to the same conditions expected in 
the spacecraft and an upward flame spread test is conducted [2]. This 
is a pass/fail test, and if a material passes, then it can be brought 
aboard. In addition to this binary test, it would be important to know 
the material flammability limits to reduce the need for retesting at 
each new condition. Another limitation is that the STD-6001B test is 
