doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2025.104625,doi,Flame extinction of thermally thick PMMA in Earth gravity versus microgravity: A comparison of Earth and on-orbit space experiments,2,1,"C. Liveretou et al.
conducted at Earth gravity and that the material is presumed to be less 
flammable in microgravity, which is not always the case. Although mi-
crogravity fire research is limited due to the difficulty with performing 
such experiments, several researchers have studied the differences in 
flammability in normal gravity versus microgravity and environmental 
factors that affect it [3,4].
In reduced gravity airplane experiments involving upward flame 
spread over thin solids for a short duration of time, Sacksteder et al. [4] 
noticed a significant reduction in the flammability limits of thin cellu-
losic fuel at reduced gravity levels. This result is consistent with the 
findings of Mizutani et al. [5] who mapped the Limiting Oxygen Con-
centration (LOC) of EFTE-insulated wires in microgravity versus Earth 
gravity at different external opposed flow velocities. In these tests, 
the LOC in microgravity is found to be 5%–10% lower than in Earth 
gravity. Takahashi et al. [6] experimentally showed the extinction 
limits of LDPE insulated nickel-chrome and copper wires in both gravity 
levels at different external opposed flow velocities. For both materials, 
across all flow velocities, they found that the LOC in microgravity is 
approximately 3%–4% lower than on Earth. Lastly, Olson et al. [7,8] 
used a centrifuge system inside a drop tower to simulate upward 
spread on three flame retardant materials in Mars [7] and lunar [8] 
gravity conditions. They found that the LOC in Martian gravity was 
up to 5.75% lower than normal gravity, and lunar gravity LOCs were 
even lower. This indicates that PMMA flammability is highest not in 
microgravity, but in partial gravity conditions, which is consistent with 
the predictions of Nakaya et al. [9]. The authors highlighted that 
Earth-based flammability tests may not be conservative enough and 
some materials may still be flammable under spacecraft conditions, 
recommending the inclusion of a factor of safety on Earth flammability 
limits.
Recently, Rivera et al. [10] outlined the existence of two differ-
ent flammability limits: LOC for flame spread (LOC-FS) and LOC for 
flame regression (LOC-FR). Following the observation that in a sealed 
chamber of continuously decreasing oxygen concentration, the flame 
initially spread along the length of the sample and later regressed into 
the core of the sample, LOC-FS represents the oxygen concentration at 
which the flame stops spreading along the length of the sample, while 
LOC-FR is the oxygen concentration at which regression ends and the 
sample extinguishes. In this work, the LOC is denoted as the oxygen 
concentration at which the flame visually extinguishes.
The present work seeks to combine the effects of pressure, oxy-
gen concentration, and the influence of buoyant forces by comparing 
experiments conducted in a laboratory scale combustion tunnel on 
Earth with tests performed aboard the International Space Station (ISS), 
where the force of gravity is negligible. The experiments presented 
here concentrate on the flame behavior as the oxygen concentration 
is reduced, and the differences in the subsequent flame extinguishing 
process between normal and microgravity. The ambient gas initially 
is air (21% oxygen volume fraction), but as the flame consumes the 
available oxygen in the combustion chamber, the oxygen concentration 
decays. Only sub-atmospheric pressures are considered because future 
spacecraft are expected to operate at low pressures and increased 
oxygen conditions in order to reduce stress on exterior walls and 
expedite astronaut preparation time before any extra-vehicular activity 
(EVA) [11]. Furthermore, experiments discussed in this paper deviate 
from the normoxic curve, which pairs low pressures with elevated 
oxygen concentrations, because the goal of the study is to characterize 
the extinction process of a vitiated environment. On Earth, the PMMA 
samples are exposed to an upward mixed (buoyant and forced) low 
velocity opposed flow, whereas in microgravity there is only the op-
posed low velocity forced flow. The comparison between LOC data and 
extinction processes in normal and microgravity can advise ways to 
improve Earth-based flammability standards for materials in SEA.
Fig. 1. Illustration of the pressure chamber with a cutout showing the com-
bustion tunnel.
2. Methods
Ground normal gravity experiments were conducted in a laboratory 
scale combustion tunnel measuring 125 mm × 125 mm × 600 mm 
which is placed inside a 105 L pressure chamber (see Fig. 1). A high-
capacity vacuum pump (Welch DuoSeal 1376) was used before each 
experiment to reduce the chamber pressure to the desired value. The 
system pressure was then continuously monitored with an electronic 
pressure transducer (Omega Engineering, Inc. PX309-015A5V). A fan 
below the test section recirculated gases and produced a forced flow 
velocity of 10 cm/s, a value which is similar to that induced by the 
Environmental Control and Life Support System (ECLSS) of a spacecraft. 
However, during ground experiments the flame induced a buoyant 
flow, so it was exposed to a combination of forced and free convection 
conditions.
The microgravity experiments were performed in a similar setup 
aboard the International Space Station (ISS), in the Combustion Inte-
grated Rack (CIR), containing a 100 L combustion chamber [12]. Inside 
the combustion chamber was the SoFIE insert enclosing the Material 
Ignition and Suppression Test (SoFIE/MIST) apparatus, within which 
the samples were allowed to burn. Similarly to the combustion chamber 
in normal gravity, the MIST apparatus was equipped with inlets to 
allow for the addition of oxygen and nitrogen gas into the test section, 
and an outlet connected to a vacuum pump. A fan was placed at one end 
of the test section, below the sample, and recirculated gases towards the 
sample to create an opposed forced flow of 10 cm/s.
The samples used in all tests"
