60 kPa, 80 kPa, and 100 kPa. Then, all gas inlets and outlets were 
closed. Once the ignition wire was energized and the sample ignited, 
the sample was allowed to burn and naturally consume the available 
oxygen. The volume fraction of oxygen in the chamber was recorded at 
a frequency of 1 Hz with an FDO2 optical gas sensor from PyroScience 
GmbH.
In each experiment, the flame eventually stopped spreading along 
the sample and regressed, shrinking to a blue hemisphere hovering 
at the top of the sample, flickering with increasing intensity until 
extinction. In order to keep the ambient pressure constant for the 
duration of each experiment, the chamber was vented occasionally to 
offset the pressure increase caused by the combustion process [13].
3. Results
Experiments were conducted at pressures of 40 kPa to 100 kPa 
with intervals of 20 kPa. For all pressures and particularly at lower 
pressures, once the sample is ignited the flame is often weak and is 
slow to establish in size and luminosity. Following ignition, the flame 
begins spreading along the length of the PMMA sample in the presence 
of the opposed flow oxygen gas mixture. The combustion process 
then accelerates initially and the flame becomes larger, consuming 
oxygen at a faster rate as it further pyrolyzes the unburned fuel. As 
the oxygen in the chamber decreases due to the combustion of the 
PMMA, the flame spread rate decreases, and the flame becomes weaker. 
Approaching extinction, the oxygen concentration is rlatively low and 
only a small, dim flame is sustained, as seen in Fig. 2. It should be 
noted that in the top row of Fig. 2, depicting the microgravity flame 
spread and extinction, the brightness and contrast in the images have 
been enhanced to make the flame more visible, as it is very dim in 
the original images. The LOC is difficult to pinpoint because at low 
volume fractions of oxygen, any minor perturbation can be enough 
to extinguish the flame [14]. Therefore, it is necessary to repeat each 
experiment between three and five times to reduce the uncertainty and 
ensure variability. In this work, normal gravity experiments are re-
peated following this recommendation. However, due to the limitations 
of conducting experiments in space, each condition in microgravity was 
only tested either once or twice. Special attention was paid to ensure 
the experiments were conducted in a precise manner and equipment 
fully calibrated to minimize sources of systematic and random errors.
The flame front position along the PMMA over time is show in Fig. 
3, with microgravity experiments being on the left plot and normal 
gravity on the right. The flame spread further along the length of 
the fuel in microgravity compared to earth gravity. For this figure, 
coordinate (0,0) corresponds to the time and position of the flame when 
the sample is lowered into position, which happens a few seconds after 
ignition. By that point, the flame was technically propagated by a small 
distance already, consuming the outer diameter of the sample, which 
is why some experiments present a flame regression below the 0 mm 
index.
The consumption of oxygen over the duration of an experiment 
is shown in Fig. 4. Microgravity experiments occasionally started at 
oxygen volume fractions lower than 21% to ensure that the low-oxygen 
extinction limits are reached before the end of the sample is ignited. 
The transparent shaded regions represent the standard deviation of the 
data, while the opaque curves depict the average value In Fig. 4, unlike 
Fig. 3, time=0 s corresponds to the moment the flame is ignited.
In Fig. 5 the oxygen volume fraction at extinction is shown for both 
microgravity and normal gravity, with the former being significantly 
lower than the latter. That is, microgravity combustion can be sustained 
in environments with oxygen concentrations approximately 3%–4% 
lower than on Earth. In microgravity, all samples were flammable at 
oxygen volume fractions greater than the Earth gravity LOC. Similarly, 
below the microgravity LOC, none of the samples were flammable. 
The primary difference between the normal gravity and microgravity 
environments is the presence of a buoyant convective current induced 
by the flame. At the scale of the Earth experiments, the buoyant flow 
is as large as 30–40 cm/s and acts on top of the 10 cm/s forced flow. 
At the beginning of an experiment, the large flame produces a larger 
buoyant flow, and then decreases with decreasing oxygen concentration 
as the flame length decreases. However, in microgravity the only flow 
past the sample is the forced flow. Past studies have shown that a 
small to moderate opposed flow helps the flame spread by mixing the 
gasified fuel and oxygen around the pyrolysis zone, transporting the 
oxidizer to the flame while increasing the convective heat transfer from 
the flame to the solid [15–17]. However, higher flow rates have the 
opposite influence on the spread rate, reducing the flame heat release 
rate. The transition between the two effects depends on the ambient 
oxygen concentration [18].
Both in the absence and presence of gravity, it is observed that 
for opposed flow flame spread the LOC is lower at higher ambient 
pressures. This implies that materials are less flammable as the ambient 
pressure is reduced. As the ambient pressure is reduced, the gas density 
decreases, the boundary layer grows thicker, resulting in the flame 
moving away from the surface, as seen in Figs. 6 and 7 [19]. This in 
turn affects the spread of the flame because the he"
