which is approximately 13.1 kJ/g [21]. Table 1 includes the values 
of the average HRR for each condition tested. It can be seen that the 
HRR increases with increasing pressure for all conditions tested which 
is aligned with the ambient pressure’s effects on the LOC. Reducing the 
gravity level appears to have the strongest effect, with microgravity 
experiments having a larger HRR compared to those in Earth gravity. 
The difference between the microgravity and Earth gravity HRR is a 
direct result of the larger oxygen consumption in microgravity due 
to the slower flame spread rate and longer time to extinction (Fig. 
8). Therefore, not only is PMMA more flammable in microgravity 
because of its low LOC, but more total energy is released during the 
longer burning times, making PMMA more dangerous in microgravity 
compared to Earth gravity.
4. Discussion
The process of flame spread over a solid fuel can be characterized by 
a series of ignition steps. The flame acts as an ignition pilot, transferring 
heat to and preheating the unburned fuel until it pyrolyzes, at which 
point the flame propagates to continue this process with more unburned 
