These two mechanisms can be combined for condition three, which 
stipulates that the sum of the heat transfer and chemical kinetics terms 
must be infinite, leading to an infinite ignition time, and therefore 
no flame spread [25]. The oxygen mole fraction, through the flame 
temperature, plays a critical role in the flame extinction process in 
all gravity conditions, as it directly affects both terms. Decreasing 
gravity reduces the flow velocity and serves to increase the pyrolysis 
time, through the slowed rate of heat transfer. However, at the same 
time it increases the Damkohler number through the increase of flow 
time. This means that as the mixed flow velocity (forced and buoyant) 
increases, the flame spread process is transitioning from a thermal 
regime, influenced by the balance between heat transfer from the flame 
to the fuel and solid re-rad"
doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2025.104625,doi,Flame extinction of thermally thick PMMA in Earth gravity versus microgravity: A comparison of Earth and on-orbit space experiments,7,6,"C. Liveretou et al.
40 cm/s on top of the 10 cm/s forced flow, in microgravity the flame 
was exposed only to the forced flow. Regardless of the level of gravity, 
the flame temperature is a critical factor in influencing the LOC as it 
drives both the heat transfer and the chemical kinetics mechanisms that 
control the rate of flame spread.
Reducing the ambient pressure had the effect of the PMMA extin-
guishing at a higher volume fraction of oxygen compared to experi-
ments conducted at 100 kPa. The justification for this behavior is that 
as the system pressure is reduced, the boundary layer grows thicker, so 
the heat transfer rate from the flame to the solid decreases. At the same 
time the reaction rate decreases and so does the Damkohler number. 
Therefore, more oxygen is required to maintain the spread of the flame.
The results of this work highlight that there are significant dif-
ferences between solid fuel combustion in normal gravity and mi-
crogravity. Given the large differences in the LOC between the two 
environments, it might be insufficient to conduct flammability tests 
on Earth for materials intended to be used in spacecraft. At the very 
least, the differences in flame spread and extinction behavior must 
be taken into consideration when analyzing the results of Earth-based 
flammability tests.
