A R T I C L E I N F O
Keywords:
Limiting oxygen concentration
Flame spread
Fuel regression
Sub-atmospheric pressure
A B S T R A C T
Flame spread over solid fuels is a key determinant of material flammability and fire growth. The primary 
motivation of this work is to provide information about flame propagation over solid fuels in atmospheres ex­
pected in futures spacecraft. Experiments with cylindrical black Polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) are conducted 
at sub-atmospheric pressures (40 – 100 kPa) in a sealed combustion chamber on the Combustion Integrated Rack 
(CIR) of the International Space Station (ISS), and in a similar chamber in normal gravity. The oxygen con­
centration in the chamber is left to decrease naturally at constant pressure as the PMMA burns. It is found that 
there is a LOC at which the flame stops spreading, referred here as the limiting oxygen concentration for flame 
spread (LOC-FS), and another at lower oxygen concentration at which the regressing flame extinguishes (LOC- 
FR). It is also found that both LOCs decreases with ambient pressure and are significantly lower in microgravity 
than at Earth. Results show that, at a given oxygen level, spread rates are lower in 1-g than in microgravity, and 
in microgravity the flame propagates to lower oxygen levels before extinguishing. Analysis of flame spread vs 
oxygen reveals a quadratic decay as oxygen is depleted consistent across conditions.
1. Introduction
Material flammability is often used as a metric in evaluating the 
potential fire risk associated with solid combustible materials, because it 
quantifies their potential for burning hazards [1]. Two primary markers 
of material flammability are flame spread (FS) and the subsequent 
burning of the exposed fuel surface, or fuel regression (FR) [1–4]. Flame 
spread is especially relevant, as it initiates the burning of the solid fuel, 
which in turn releases heat that propagates the fire. As the flame ad­
vances over the surface, it leaves behind a pyrolyzing and regressing 
material. The rate of this consumption is referred to as “regression rate” 
or “mass burning rate” [5]; here we use the term “fuel regression rate” 
for clarity. While regression has been extensively studied in the context 
of pool fires, its coupling with flame spread remains less explored, 
though recent experimental and modeling studies have begun to quan­
tify the coupled spread–regression process and the spread to regression 
transition under varying flow, pressure, and oxygen conditions [6–11]. 
Importantly, these coupled behaviors are not limited to cylindrical fuels; 
analogous regression and spread–regression interactions have also been 
reported for thick non-cylindrical fuels such as slabs/plates under forced 
flow environments [9–11]. Ongoing research has determined that FS 
and FR are not independent but sequential phenomena: flames spread 
until the limiting oxygen concentration for propagation (LOC-FS) is 
reached; beyond this point, surface regression may continue under a 
hovering flame until a second extinction limit (LOC-FR) is encountered 
[6]. These dual limits underscore that different mechanisms govern each 
stage. The transition from FS to FR is controlled by the transport pro­
cesses that determine whether sufficient heat to sustain propagation. 
Once FS halts, chemistry (residence-time vs reaction-time balance) be­
comes the bottleneck. The analysis in this study is focused on the flame 
spread limit, since this stage primarily determines whether a fire grows 
when it spreads across a solid surface.
Motivated by spacecraft fire safety [12,13], the present study in­
vestigates how sub-atmospheric pressure and gravity conditions affect 
flame spread and its transition near the LOC-FS limit. Experiments with 
cylindrical black PMMA samples are conducted in a sealed combustion 
chamber on the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) of the International 
Space Station (ISS) in the SoFIE/MIST test apparatus [14] and in a 
similar chamber in normal gravity. The chamber-averaged oxygen 
concentration decreases naturally at constant pressure as the samples 
