doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2026.104719,doi,Opposed flame spread limit over PMMA rods in normal and micro gravity,2,1,"burn to extinction, enabling us to observe both the FS and FR limits. By 
focusing on the first limit (LOC-FS), we isolate the transport-dominated 
mechanism responsible for spread cessation. Thus, the scope of this work 
is twofold: (i) to propose a theoretical framework to discuss the 
dependence of FS on oxygen depletion near LOC-FS, grounded in both 
experiments and theory; and (ii) to provide a better understanding of the 
distinct roles of thermal transport and chemistry in establishing the 
flame spread limit. For clarity, throughout this work “oxygen concen­
tration”, including in LOC definitions, refers to the gas-phase oxygen 
mole fraction, XO2, reported in % O2 numerically equivalent to oxygen 
volume percent for an ideal gas mixture.
2. Experimental methods
2.1. Normal gravity experiments
Experiments were carried out using a dedicated Earth gravity 
apparatus, designed to investigate the flammability of solid combustion 
materials in controlled environmental conditions [15]. A set of different 
schematic views of the apparatus is shown in Fig. 1.
The apparatus consists of a small-scale rectangular flow duct, with a 
125 mm by 125 mm cross section and 600 mm long as shown in 
Fig. 1–②. The sample is placed vertically at the center of the test section 
using an aluminum sample holder. Below, a wire mesh section serves to 
straighten the flow before entering the test section as indicated in 
Fig. 1–③. The apparatus is inserted in a 105 L pressure chamber 
(Fig. 1–⑤). A fan located 15 cm below the test section provides an up­
ward gas flow of 10 cm/s (see Fig. 1–④), similar to the air flow from the 
International Space Station's HVAC system [13]. The samples used in the 
tests are black cast polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) cylinders with 
dimensions of 12.7 mm in diameter and 55 mm in length (Fig. 1–①). 
Experiments were carried out for different pressure conditions ranging 
from 100 kPa to 40 kPa. A more detailed description of the apparatus is 
presented in Ref. [15].
2.2. Microgravity experiments
The microgravity tests were performed in the Material Ignition and 
Suppression Test (MIST) apparatus that was placed in the Solid Fuel 
Ignition and Extinction (SoFIE) testing facility. Some of the main com­
ponents and locations are presented in Fig. 2. Fig. 2–① Pre-fan inlet: a 
short sampling tube that draws gas (via the sensor pump) from the 
chamber, passes it through a filter, and delivers it to the analyzer cell 
where O2, CO2, and CO are measured. Fig. 1–② Gas Sensor Module 1 
and Module 2 (Fig. 1–③) contain the post-fan and pre-fan sampling 
probes, respectively, along with Chip Cap 2 temperature and humidity 
sensors. Fig. 1–④ post-fan inlet: a long sampling tube spanning to the 
opposite side of the chamber, draws gas via the sensor pump, filters it, 
and delivers it to Gas Sensor Module 1 for post-fan O2 and CO2/CO 
measurements. The SoFIE/MIST apparatus was contained in a 100 L 
combustion chamber in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) of the 
Destiny module of the International Space Station [14,16,17]. The 
SoFIE/MIST testing apparatus has similar characteristics and di­
mensions as the normal gravity MIST described above, although it is 
designed to minimize astronaut participation in the experiment by using 
robotic manipulation from the NASA Glen R.C. ground control center.
Experiment procedure: During the tests, all inlets and outlets of the 
pressure chamber are closed, so oxygen is gradually depleted in the 
chamber by the PMMA's combustion. Prior to ignition, the chamber is 
sealed and vented to reach the target operating pressure. A fan located 
upstream of the test section provides a nominal 10 cm/s gas flow past 
the PMMA sample, and the exhaust gases are recirculated within the 
chamber for both normal and microgravity experiments. Ignition is 
induced using an electrically heated nichrome wire at the top of the 
sample. Chamber oxygen is measured with an FDO2 optical oxygen gas 
sensor (PyroScience GmbH) located upstream in the recirculating flow 
path that supplies the forced flow to the test section. Because the fan 
operates continuously throughout each experiment, the measured value 
is interpreted as the bulk (chamber-averaged) oxygen mole fraction 
representative of the inflow mixture feeding the test section. While 
forced recirculation promotes mixing, the chamber-average value may 
differ temporarily from the local near-flame oxygen level due to plume- 
induced stratification and near-flame consumption; this is considered as 
a source of uncertainty. Chamber pressure is monitored with a pressure 
transducer and maintained by periodically extracting gas when the 
pressure to +2 kPa above the set point. Flame position, burning and 
extinction processes are recorded using a camera located outside the 
chamber, and the video images are analyzed using a FlameTracker 
processing application [18].
3. Results
After the ignition of the PMMA rod is established, a flame starts 
spreading along the solid fuel surface against the opposed forced gas 
flow. In normal gravity the opposed flow is a mixed forced and buoyant 
flow, with the velocity of the latter depending on the ambient pressure 
and oxygen mole fraction. As the flame spreads along the rod's surface, it 
leaves behind a regressing burning surface of the shape of a cone. Since 
