chamber's oxygen decreases due to the combustion of the PMMA, which 
causes the flame spread rate (Vf), and the fuel regression rate to also 
decrease. The flame characteristics also change as the chamber's oxygen 
is depleted. A visual description of the evolution of the flame front (yf) as 
it spreads at Vf along the PMMA rod is shown in Fig. 3, for a 100 kPa 
ambient pressure and normal gravity.
After ignition, there is small region where the flame rapidly grows 
but is still under the influence of the igniter and there is not clear view to 
trace the flame front (untracked region). Once the flame stabilize, begins 
to freely spread along the PMMA rod surface (FS) against the gas flow 
(Fig. 3-top). Subsequently, the flame advances over the surface of the 
PMMA at a rate, Vf, that decreases as burning progresses. During this 
phase, the solid material is mostly consumed radially at a rate r, causing 
the conical shape of the PMMA to become steeper. At a certain point, the 
flame ceases to propagate, Vf = 0; this transition limit is the LOC for 
flame spread, LOC-FS and represents the maximum distance propagated 
by the flame (yf = ymax). Since the PMMA downstream continues to burn 
(FR), the oxygen in the chamber continues to decrease, and the flame 
retracts somewhat until it anchors itself in the stagnation region behind 
the forward pyrolysis front. We call this receding process the flame 
spread to fuel regression, FS-FR, transition. It represents the time when 
the flame changes from a spreading to a regression phase. The PMMA 
continues to be consumed radially, and the height of the formed cone 
decreases significantly compared to the FS phase. As the oxygen 
concentration decreases the flame in the fuel regression zone creases and 
eventually extinguishes (Fig. 3-top). This is the LOC for fuel regression, 
LOC-FR.
Fig. 4 presents a sequence of photographs evenly timed after ignition 
comparing the flame characteristics and oxygen depletion from the 
normal gravity (left side) and microgravity (right side) experiments, for 
ambient pressure of 100 kPa in the top row and for 60 kPa ambient 
pressure in the bottom row. The photographs and oxygen readings are 
taken every 1 min for 100 kPa ambient pressure and every 30 s and 40 s 
for normal and microgravity respectively at 60 kPa ambient pressure. 
They illustrate the flame front progression as oxygen is consumed during 
spreading from ignition until transition limit. The photographs also 
show that the height of the cone does not change significantly as the 
oxygen concentration decreases across ambient pressures. When 
comparing pressures, it is observed that the flame propagation over the 
surface of the PMMA rods is larger at higher pressure and the time 
required to reach the spreading limit is shorter when the ambient 
pressure is reduced for both gravity environments. In microgravity 
(Fig. 4b), the flame spreads over the sample surface for a longer time and 
distance than in normal gravity, being the oxygen consumption more 
significant.
3.1. Flame front and flame spread rate
Fig. 5 shows the actual position and spread rate with time of the 
flame leading edge as the flame spreads over the PMMA rod surface for 
all the ambient pressures tested. Also shown in the figure is the chamber 
oxygen volume fraction as the oxygen is being consumed by the burning 
PMMA. Fig. 5a shows the data in normal-g conditions and 5(b) in 
microgravity conditions. The figure also includes markers that highlight 
the oxygen concentration at the specific flame spread thresholds (dashed 
lines) that the flame undergoes as the oxygen decreases. Results of Fig. 5
show that both in 1-g and μg, as the oxygen gradually decreases due to 
the combustion of the PMMA, the flame spread rate also decreases until 
it reaches an oxygen concentration where the flame no longer spreads. 
This oxygen threshold is defined here as the Limiting Oxygen Concen­
tration for Flame Spread (LOC-FS), reported in % O2 (numerically 
equivalent to oxygen volume percent for an ideal gas mixture).
It is also observed in Fig. 5 that after the LOC-FS is reached, as the 
oxygen is consumed further, there is a transition period where the flame 
leading edge moves gradually backward, in what appears to be a weak 
blow-off extinction. In normal gravity (Fig. 5a) the flame eventually 
stops receding and attaches to the cone behind the pyrolysis front. As the 
PMMA surface keeps regressing the cone angle becomes steeper, the 
oxygen concentration becomes smaller and the flame weaker, until it 
finally extinguishes (Fig. 5a). This oxygen threshold is defined here as 
the Limiting Oxygen Concentration for Fuel Regression (LOC-FR), re­
ported in % O2. Operationally, LOC-FR corresponds to the oxygen level 
at which the visible flame extinguishes in the regression (wake-stabi­
lized) regime, which is the criterion commonly recorded in LOI-type 
