doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2026.104719,doi,Opposed flame spread limit over PMMA rods in normal and micro gravity,4,3,"measurements [19]. Notably, LOC-FR is significantly lower than 
LOC-FS.
It is observed that reducing the chamber ambient pressure affects the 
flame's behavior across all measured parameters. Specifically, the LOC 
for the flame spread, and regression thresholds, are notably higher at 
reduced pressures. Correspondingly, the time required for the flame to 
reach these limits increases as the pressure increases. Furthermore, the 
distance traveled by the flame during its active phases was consistently 
shorter at lower pressures. This reduction in flame travel distance at 
lower pressures suggests that the flame's ability to maintain sustained 
combustion over larger areas is compromised, likely due to the dimin­
ished oxygen availability and altered air density (discussed more in 
Ref. [20]). Such observations are important for understanding the 
complex interplay between pressure, oxygen availability, and flame 
dynamics in confined spaces [21]. In this work, the flame spread rate 
(FSR) refers to the forward propagation speed of the leading flame front 
over the surface of the virgin fuel prior to the FS-FR transition. Any 
post-transition recession of the visible flame location is shown as a po­
sition change in Fig. 5 but is not interpreted as “negative FSR,” since it 
corresponds to regression mode burning rather than surface spread.
Fig. 6 shows a summary of the LOC limits reported in Fig. 5. When 
chamber pressure is reduced, both 1-g and microgravity demonstrate an 
increasing trend in LOCs. For the opposed-flow condition used in this 
study, the measured LOC-FS is consistently lower than the value ob­
tained in the 1-g configuration as seen in Fig. 6a. Note that in normal 
gravity, the opposed flow is a mixed forced and buoyant flow. Buoyancy 
induced velocities on the order of ~30–40 cm/s depending on config­
uration, which have been reported for similar PMMA flame spread 
configurations [20,22,23], whereas in microgravity the opposed flow is 
purely the nominal forced flow [24]. The trend persists into the LOC-FR 
limit, where the differences are consistent for all pressures tested as 
shown in Fig. 6b, showing that the limits in microgravity are around 3% 
(by oxygen volume fraction) lower than normal gravity. Reducing the 
pressure generally increases the LOC-FR across all tests, in agreement 
with the findings reported in Ref. [25].
These trends are consistent with the numerical study by Kumar and 
T'ien [11], who identified two stabilization modes for thick solid fuels 
under forced flow: a side-stabilized (surface-anchored) flame and a 
wake-stabilized (recirculation-supported) flame. Because the wake 
flame is sustained by recirculation/re-entrainment and experiences a 
markedly different upstream transport field than the side-stabilized 
flame, it can generally persist at lower oxygen (typically with a 
reduced burning intensity). In this framework, the limiting conditions 
reported in this work can be interpreted as: LOC-FS as the condition at 
which the side-stabilized spreading flame can no longer provide suffi­
cient forward heat feedback to sustain propagation over the virgin solid, 
whereas LOC-FR corresponds to extinction of the wake-stabilized 
burning that can remain after spread ceases. Consequently, the 
wake-stabilized extinction limit (LOC-FR) is expected to occur at lower 
oxygen than the side-stabilized spread limit (LOC-FS), consistent with 
LOI-like thick-fuel behavior. Kumar and T'ien [11] also reported hys­
teresis in the side-to-wake transition with oxygen; because the present 
experiments proceed under monotonic oxygen depletion, we do not map 
the reverse oxygen increasing transition.
3.2. Flame spread rate vs oxygen concentration
Fig. 7 presents the flame spread rate (FSR) as a function of the 
chamber oxygen for (a) normal gravity and (b) microgravity. The plot 
can be interpreted as a depletion trajectory curves where the oxygen 
decreases naturally from 21% toward LOC-FS in the sealed chamber, and 
each FSR value is paired with the concurrently measured O2(t). The 
resulting curves represents a time evolving path in terms of oxygen and 
FSR rather than a set of steady solutions obtained by prescribing a fixed 
oxygen as an independent parameter. Vertical dashed lines represent the 
LOC-FS limits for each pressure and gravity condition (as shown in 
Fig. 6a) where the flame spread rate is zero. At a given oxygen level, the 
FSR is systematically lower in 1-g than in microgravity across the 
ambient pressures tested, reflecting the enhanced convective losses 
imposed by buoyancy-driven flow. In microgravity, where buoyancy is 
absent, the flame maintains a higher spread rate for the same oxygen 
level and can spread at lower oxygen levels before propagation ceases. 
When comparing boundary cases, it is observed that at 40 kPa in 
microgravity, the FSR reaches zero at a slightly lower oxygen level than 
at 100 kPa in 1-g. This suggests the absence of buoyancy somewhat 
compensates for the reduction in pressure, lowering the oxygen 
requirement for flame spread in microgravity relative to normal gravity.
Across all pressures and environments, the measured decay of flame 
spread rate, Vf,exp, with decreasing oxygen follows a quadratic-like 
behavior: second-degree polynomial fits capture the data with high fi­
delity. Because the FSR process involves a coupled gas–solid heat feed­
back balance, it requires to bring the virgin PMMA to pyrolysis and 
overcome the heat losses [22,23,26,27]. Thus, increases in gas-phase 
reactivity with oxygen do not necessarily translate to proportional in­
creases in heat transfer and spread rate. This produces a behavior where 
the sensitivity of FSR to oxygen seems to be weaker at higher oxygen 
levels and becomes stronger as oxygen approaches the LOC-FS for the 
current experimental settings.
