doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2026.104719,doi,Opposed flame spread limit over PMMA rods in normal and micro gravity,9,8,"and microgravity, with attention to the mechanisms underlying extinc­
tion behavior. By conducting experiments in sealed chambers where 
oxygen naturally decayed at constant pressure, two distinct extinction 
thresholds were identified: the limiting oxygen concentration for flame 
spread (LOC-FS) and the limiting oxygen concentration for fuel regres­
sion (LOC-FR). These two limits were consistently separated across all 
conditions, highlighting that different processes govern the transition 
from a propagating flame front to a regressing, hovering flame. The 
comparative experiments revealed systematic effects of both gravity and 
pressure on flame behavior. In microgravity, flames sustained higher 
spread rates at a given oxygen level and propagated to lower oxygen 
levels than in normal gravity, reflecting changes in convective transport 
when buoyancy is absent. Reducing ambient pressure led to higher LOC 
limits, shorter propagation distances, and shorter times to reach 
extinction.
The decay of flame spread rate with decreasing oxygen was consis­
tently well described by a quadratic dependence anchored at the LOC-FS 
threshold. This mathematical form provides a compact representation of 
the observed behavior, and the fitted slope and curvature at the LOC-FS 
limit serve as useful descriptors of the local dynamics. The framework 
discussed in this work offers an interpretation of these trends: transport 
processes and chemical kinetics both contribute to the cease of propa­
gation and extinction behavior, though their relative significance can 
shift with conditions. Near the flame spread limit, heat transfer into the 
solid surface and the ability to sustain chemical reactions both play 
roles, while during fuel regression, the balance of these processes 
evolves further as the flame transitions to a hovering state. From a fire 
safety perspective, these findings emphasize that no single mechanism 
fully explains the limits of spread and regression. Instead, extinction 
reflects the interplay of transport and reaction, with each becoming 
more or less significant depending on gravity, pressure, and available 
chamber oxygen. The quadratic representation of the spread rate­
–oxygen relationship offers a simple but physically informed tool for 
interpreting experimental results.
