A R T I C L E I N F O
Keywords:
Opposed flame spread
Quenching limit
Sub-atmospheric condition
Low-velocity flow
Inert diluent gas
A B S T R A C T
The cabin environment of next generation inhabited spacecraft is characterized by sub-atmospheric pressures 
and elevated oxygen concentrations, creating unique combustion conditions for onboard solid materials. The 
influence of inert-gas dilution on flame spread and extinction is of critical importance for spacecraft fire safety. 
Experiments were conducted to investigate the opposed flame spread and extinction behaviors over a thick 
PMMA in low-velocity flow, focusing on the distinct influences of N2, Ar and CO2. The flamelets emerge under 
sub-atmospheric environment, and the critical flow-velocity decreases as oxygen concentration increases. The 
flame spread rate decreases with the reduced ambient pressure as predicted, but becomes independent of the gas 
species when the pressure is lower to a critical value. The decreased ambient pressure reduces the flame spread 
rate through thermal effect, although different dilutions influence through various effects. An effective Lewis 
number and P´eclet number are proposed as the governing parameters for near-limit flames, since they represent 
the thermophysical properties of materials and environmental conditions, respectively. A normalized map is then 
established to compromise the effect of flow velocity, ambient pressure and gas dilution. These findings provide 
fundamental insights into flame spread and extinction under sub-atmospheric, diluted conditions relevant to 
microgravity spacecraft environment.
1. Introduction
Flame spread behaviors over solid materials depend strongly on 
environmental conditions, most notably oxygen concentration, flow 
velocity and ambient pressure. Given the stark contrast between 
spacecraft cabin conditions and terrestrial environments, flame spread 
dynamics have remained a critical focal point for extraterrestrial fire 
safety research for several decades [1–3]. For further space exploration, 
NASA has proposed atmospheric specifications for next-generation 
space vehicles involving reduced total pressures of 52.7–58.6 kPa and 
elevated oxygen concentrations ranging from 30% to 34% by volume [4,
5]. Similarly, the suggested cabin environment for the Chinese Lunar 
Lander specifies a total pressure of (58 ± 4) kPa with an oxygen partial 
pressure maintained (21 ± 2) kPa [6]. These new atmospheric condi­
tions, combined with the low-velocity gas flows induced by the heating, 
ventilation, and air conditioning systems in microgravity, constitute a 
unique combustion environment for onboard materials. While prior 
studies 
have 
characterized 
flame 
spread 
behavior 
under 
sub-atmospheric, low-flow conditions [7], and inhibitory effects of inert 
gases have gained increasing attention [8–10], the specific efficacy of 
inert gaseous suppressants under low-pressure regimes remains insuffi­
ciently understood. Consequently, a systematic investigation into the 
fire-extinguishing mechanisms of these agents within sub-atmospheric 
environments is essential to ensure the safety of future crewed missions.
Previous studies [11,12] have demonstrated that under low-velocity 
flow conditions, a continuous flame front can become unstable and bi­
furcates into discrete flamelets, and a further reduction in either oxygen 
concentration or flow velocity eventually leads to flame extinction. The 
critical oxygen concentrations and flow velocities that govern the 
transition between self-sustained flame propagation and extinction 
define the flammability limits of a material [1,13]. Earlier investigations 
[7,11,14–16] have established that distinct flame propagation regimes 
and their corresponding flammability boundaries exist in atmospheric 
and sub-atmospheric environments. Furthermore, the composition of 
the ambient atmosphere significantly influences these limits [8–10]. At 
ambient pressure conditions, the critical airflow velocity required for 
