doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2026.104952,doi,Experimental characterization of near-limit flame spread in spacecraft environment with inert-gas dilution,2,1,"flame extinction decreases as the oxygen concentration increases [1,7,8,
11,17]. Due to variations in the specific heat capacity and thermal 
diffusivity of the ambient gases, the flammability limits of materials are 
significantly influenced by the surrounding atmosphere [8]. Specif­
ically, materials exhibit the most restrictive flammability limit in a 
helium-enriched environment, followed by O2/CO2 atmosphere, while 
the flammability range is broadest in O2/Ar atmospheres. Under 
sub-atmospheric conditions, the flammability limit of materials narrows 
as the ambient pressure decreases, whereas the stable regime for 
small-scale flames tends to expand [7]. Research on flame extinction 
indicates that the extinction occurring at low flow velocities is primarily 
attributed to an increased proportion of heat loss [12,16,18–20]. Studies 
have demonstrated that quenching extinction occurs when the radiative 
heat loss fraction reaches a critical value, denoted as 0.4-0.45 [19], or 
when the total heat losses account for about 80% of the total heat input 
from the flame [12,20].
As for flame spread, previous studies have been shown that for a 
thermally thick solid fuel, the flame spread rate increases with the 
opposed flow velocity [21–23], and Wichman et al. [13] found that the 
Lewis number (Le) has an effect on flame spread rate, and for 
thermally-thick solid fuel, the flame spread rate decreases with Le. 
Under near-limit conditions, due to the competition between the lateral 
oxygen diffusion and side heat loss, the flame spread rate may be 
increased at the sample side, and the uniform flame may separate into 
flamelets [11,12,24–27]. Furthermore, Matsuoka et al. [25] found that 
flame separation over thermally-thick solid fuels is primarily induced by 
the diffusive-thermal instability of the solid material, and the flame 
spread rate can be correlated with an effective Lewis number.
Combustion is an intricate process in which material pyrolysis, as 
well as gas-phase mass and heat transfer, are governed by a synergy of 
ambient conditions, solid-phase thermophysical properties, and gas- 
phase transport phenomena. Although interpreting flame propagation 
and extinction through the lens of heat loss captures the aggregate 
environmental influence, this approach often fails to decouple the ef­
fects of individual environmental parameters or integrate intrinsic ma­
terial properties. Furthermore, since oxygen concentration, convective 
flow velocity, atmospheric composition, and ambient pressure collec­
tively dictate flammability limits, it remains to be determined whether a 
unified descriptor can adequately characterize these multifaceted in­
fluences. Beyond individual impacts, it is equally imperative to explore 
the existence of a consolidated governing parameter capable of quan­
tifying their synergistic effects on flame dynamics.
The primary objective of this experimental investigation is to eluci­
date the mechanisms of flame spread and extinction under sub- 
atmospheric conditions, specifically within low-velocity oxidizer flows 
diluted by CO2, Ar and N2. Particular emphasis is placed on near-limit 
propagation behaviors, with the aim of establishing a correlation be­
tween flammability limits and diverse ambient parameters. Given that 
this study focuses on the radiation-controlled regime characteristic of 
low-flow environments, an extensive experimental matrix was executed. 
To provide a robust analysis, our experimental results are integrated 
with existing literature data to evaluate the influence of varying pres­
sures and atmospheric compositions. The experiments were conducted 
using a narrow-channel apparatus housed within a pressure-controlled 
containment chamber. This configuration allows for precise regulation 
of flow velocities, ambient pressures, and oxygen concentrations. The 
narrow-channel type apparatus serves as an effective ground-based 
analog for suppressing buoyancy-induced flows, making it a well- 
established surrogate for capturing the fundamental features of micro­
gravity flame spread [7,8,11,26,28–32]. Crucially, this setup facilitates 
the extended observation times required to study flame propagation 
over thermally thick fuels. Based on the collected data, flammability 
maps are constructed using gas flow velocity and oxygen concentration 
as coordinates. Finally, the complicated effects of ambient pressure and 
convective flow on flame spread modes, propagation rates, and extinc­
tion limits are systematically analyzed.
2. Experiments
The near-limit conditions investigated in this study involve critical 
parameters, including oxygen concentration, airflow velocity, and 
ambient pressure. In addition, the diluent gases employed consist of N2, 
Ar, and CO2. Three series of experiments were performed: (1) at atmo­
spheric pressure to determine the flammability limits of the material as a 
function of oxygen concentration and airflow velocity across various 
atmospheres; (2) in O2/N2 environments, to measure the flammability 
limits at representative sub-atmospheric pressures (58 kPa and 35 kPa); 
and (3) under low-pressure conditions (35 kPa and 20 kPa) to charac­
terize flame spread behavior when N2, CO2, or Ar were utilized as the 
diluent. While certain experimental results were previously reported [7,
8], the present work provides essential supplementary data on flame 
spread rates near the extinction limit and focuses specifically on flame 
characteristics of the third series.
The experimental system comprises a pressure-controlled contain­
ment chamber and a narrow channel apparatus. Atmospheric experi­
ments were conducted using the horizontal channel in an open 
configuration, whereas sub-atmospheric tests were performed by situ­
ating the channel within the sealed containment chamber. A schematic 
diagram of the experimental setup is illustrated in Fig. 1a. While the 
experimental setup is described in detail in Refs. [7,8], a bri"
doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2026.104952,doi,Experimental characterization of near-limit flame spread in spacecraft environment with inert-gas dilution,3,2,"pressure P2. Once the pressure stabilized, internal fans were operated for 
at least 5 min to achieve a homogeneous mixture, after which they were 
deactivated to allow the atmosphere to become quiescent. Following gas 
preparation, the channel fan was engaged to establish the required flow 
field. Upon flow stabilization, the resistive wire was energized for 5 - 
10 s to ensure successful ignition of the sample. Two cameras were 
utilized to record the ignition, flame propagation, and extinction pro­
cesses from both the lateral and frontal perspectives. As illustrated in 
Fig. 1b, the measurement locations (yL, yM, and yR) were explicitly 
defined for localized data acquisition. The experimental trial was 
stopped once the flame reached the downstream edge of the sample.
3. Results and discussion
3.1. Flame behaviors
Within the flammability limits, two distinct flame modes are 
observed as a function of low-velocity flow and/or oxygen concentration 
under constant pressure: the uniform flame and the flamelet. Typical 
flame images at P = 35 kPa with an oxygen concentration of 40% are 
presented in Fig. 2. In environments where CO2 is employed as the 
diluent, the flame consistently exhibits a blue hue across all flow ve­
locities. However, the luminosity diminishes as Vg decreases. It is 
evident that at high flow velocities, the flame front remains uniform. 
When the flow velocity is reduced to a critical value, Vg,cr, the contin­
uous flame front begins to contract and becomes corrugated, eventually 
transitioning from a uniform front into discrete flamelets. Specifically, in 
a 40% O2/60% CO2 environment, Vg,cr is approximately 15 cm/s, 
whereas with Ar as the diluent, Vg,cr increases to approximately 20 cm/s. 
Under both conditions, when Vg ≤9 cm/s, the flamelet in the central 
region of the sample undergoes quenching, while only the flames near 
the sample edges survive.
The time evolution of the flame leading edge position was tracked at 
three representative locations, from which the flame spread rates were 
derived. Four typical conditions are illustrated in Fig. 3. A comparative 
analysis reveals that when CO2 is employed as the diluent, the three- 
dimensional effects of the flame at an oxygen concentration of 40% 
are negligible, with the flame spread velocity at the edges remaining 
comparable to that at the centerline. However, at a higher oxygen 
concentration of 60%, lateral oxygen diffusion significantly enhances 
flame propagation at the edges, resulting in a greater velocity that 
substantially exceeds the centerline velocity. In contrast, within an Ar- 
diluted environment, the flame spread velocity at the edges surpasses 
that of the center across both investigated oxygen concentrations. This 
suggests that the influence of lateral diffusion at the edges is more 
pronounced in the presence of Ar [26].
3.2. Flame spread rate
To analyze the effect of flow velocity on flame propagation under 
different diluent gas conditions, the spread rate at the center of the 
specimen is used as the characteristic velocity for this analysis. Fig. 4
shows the variation of flame spread rate with airflow velocity at 
different CO2 concentrations under a pressure of P = 35 kPa. It can be 
observed that, for all three CO2 concentrations, the flame spread rate 
increases with increasing gas flow velocity. At the same gas flow ve­
locity, the differences in flame spread rate among the three CO2 con­
centrations are not significant, and as the CO2 concentration increases, 
the flame spread rate decreases slightly.
Fig. 5a shows the variation of flame spread rate varies with airflow 
velocity at P = 35 kPa in an environment where CO2 is the balance gas, 
under different oxygen concentrations. Similar to the flame spread 
characteristics in an O2/N2 environment, the flame spread rate in an O2/ 
CO2 environment also exhibits a region where it increases with 
increasing oxygen concentration. At an oxygen concentration of 40%, 
the flame propagation speed increased by a factor of 2.5 when the 
airflow velocity was increased from 10 cm/s to 25 cm/s. At an airflow 
velocity of 10 cm/s, when the oxygen concentration increased from 40% 
to 60%, the flame spread rate increased by a factor of 3.4. As the airflow 
velocity increased, the trend of increased flame spread rate due to higher 
oxygen concentrations became more pronounced. Fig. 5b shows the 
variation of flame spread rate with opposed flow velocity at different 
oxygen concentrations when Ar serves as the diluent gas under the same 
pressure. In an O2/Ar environment, flame propagation exhibits the same 
characteristics. This indicates that even when the environmental 
composition is altered, the influence of oxygen concentration on flame 
spread rate remains dominant.
Fig. 6 shows the variation of flame spread rate with airflow velocity 
in O2/N2 and O2/CO2 mixtures under two different ambient pressures. 
At P = 35 kPa, there is a relatively significant difference in flame spread 
rate between the two mixtures, whereas at P = 20 kPa, the difference is 
not significant. Under low-pressure conditions, because the absolute 
concentration of the equilibrium gas is very low, that is, the partial 
pressure is only 8 kPa, and its effect on flame propagation could be 
neglected. In a 20 kPa environment, if the absolute concentration of the 
