The aforementioned flame fingering patterns occur exclusively near 
the quenching limit, where radiative heat loss plays an important role. 
For opposed flame spread over thick solid PMMA plates under a stan­
dard atmospheric environment, the formation of flamelets has been 
observed by Matsuoka et al. [25] in a normal-gravity environment and 
by Zhu et al. [12] in a microgravity environment, and diffusive-thermal 
nature of the instability associated with the formation of flamelets in 
both experiments. Analytical approaches suggest that a critical effective 
Lewis number can be utilized to distinguish between uniform contin­
uous flames and discrete flamelets, indicating that flame separation is 
governed by coupled heat and mass transfer [12,25]. Furthermore, in 
near-limit opposed-flow smoldering over thin solid fuels, the charac­
teristic finger width correlates strongly with the heat loss intensity, 
while the separation distance between adjacent fingers is dictated by the 
gas-phase P´eclet number (Pe) [24].
Fundamentally, flame extinction and flamelet formation are gov­
erned by the delicate interplay between local oxygen supply and heat 
transfer [11,27]. The classical P´eclet number, defined as Pe = VgLc/ αg 
(where Vg is the opposed-flow velocity, Lc is the characteristic length, 
and αg is the gas-phase thermal diffusivity), characterizes the relative 
importance of advection to thermal diffusion. However, to accurately 
capture near-limit behaviors, it is necessary to incorporate the critical 
effect of oxygen mass diffusion into this governing parameter. For the 
diluent gases employed in this study (such as N2, Ar, and CO2), the 
gas-phase Lewis number (Le = αg/DO2, where DO2 is the mass diffusivity 
of oxygen) is approximately unity. This implies a strong heat and mass 
transfer analogy, yielding αg ≈DO2. To explicitly account for the in­
fluences of oxygen concentration and flow velocity on flame spread, an 
effective P´eclet number (Peeff) is defined by integrating the oxygen 
volume fraction: 
