doi:10.1016/j.firesaf.2026.104952,doi,Experimental characterization of near-limit flame spread in spacecraft environment with inert-gas dilution,6,5,"number (Leeff) framework originally proposed by Uchida et al. [32] for 
the combustion of thermally thin materials in confined spaces, and later 
adapted by Matsuoka et al. [25] to characterize flamelet behavior. In 
this formulation, the effective thermal diffusivity (αeff) is defined as the 
ratio of the conductive heat transfer term to the unsteady energy storage 
term. By accounting for the specific boundary conditions and multiphase 
heat transfer characteristics of the confined system, this formulation 
yields an αeff (and consequently a Leeff) that is significantly smaller than 
the conventional gas-phase values. Physically, Leeff delineates the 
competition between heat transfer, which drives flame propagation and 
oxygen mass transfer, which sustains the chemical reaction within this 
specific geometric configuration. The effective thermal diffusivity is 
expressed as: 
αeff =
λg(h −δ) + λsδ
ρgcg(h −δ) + ρscsδ
(2) 
where λ, ρ, and c represent the thermal conductivity, density, and spe­
cific heat, respectively, with subscripts g and s denoting the gas and solid 
phases.
For a thermally thick sample, the physical thickness of the solid is 
