These two dimensionless parameters effectively normalize the experi­
mental data, allowing for a distinct segregation of propagation regimes 
across diverse ambient conditions. Furthermore, the map incorporates 
low-pressure flamelet data obtained using various diluent gases, all of 
which align well with the theoretically established boundaries. To 
validate the robustness of this normalization, the data from Matsuoka 
et al. [25], which were obtained in a pure oxygen environment at at­
mospheric pressure using a narrow channel apparatus, were also pre­
sented in Fig. 8. Their results show that the maximum Leeff at which 
flamelet spread was confirmed was 0.027 with Lc = 2 mm, while the 
minimum value for uniform flame was 0.025 with Lc = 1 mm, and the 
corresponding Peeff are 20 and 40 respectively. These external data 
points significantly broaden the validated experimental range and 
exhibit excellent agreement with our findings. Consequently, the 
Leeff–Peeff framework provides a robust and universal characterization of 
flame propagation modes across a wide spectrum of operational 
conditions.
Despite this robust normalization, the observed flame spread exhibits 
distinct three-dimensional behaviors near the sample edges, particularly 
under Ar dilution at elevated oxygen concentrations (as shown in Fig. 3). 
These edge effects inherently introduce a degree of uncertainty into the 
evaluation of the global flame spread rate. This uncertainty propagates 
as quantitative fluctuations in the calculated thermal thickness (δ), 
thereby influencing the subsequent derivation of both the effective 
thermal diffusivity (αeff) and the effective Lewis number (Leeff) used to 
delineate theoretical boundaries in the flammability map. To mitigate 
this, the present analysis deliberately employs the centerline flame 
spread rate as the characteristic velocity (Vf). The centerline region 
effectively minimizes interference from lateral heat losses and trans­
verse oxygen diffusion, acting as the most stable, quasi-adiabatic portion 
of the flame front. Ultimately, while three dimensional edge effects may 
contribute to minor scatter in the theoretical boundary calculations, 
utilizing the centerline velocity accurately isolates the intrinsic opposed- 
flow heat and mass transfer mechanisms. This ensures that the funda­
mental qualitative trends and physical interpretations of the normalized 
correlations remain highly robust.
4. Conclusions
Flame spread and extinction behaviors in the low-velocity flow with 
different ambient diluent gases under sub-atmospheric environment 
were investigated using a horizontal narrow-channel experimental sys­
tem and a containment chamber. The flame spread behavior and spread 
rate with low-velocity flow were analyzed. The limited oxygen con­
centration on flame extinction with low-velocity flow with different 
ambient pressure and diluent gases were analyzed. The Peeff and Leeff 
were used to characterize the flammability map. The main conclusions 
are drawn as follows: 
(1) Among the three evaluated diluent gas environments (N2, Ar, and 
CO2), CO2 exhibits the narrowest overall flammability domain; 
paradoxically, it sustains a comparatively broader flamelet 
regime than the other diluents. Conversely, the O2/N2 mixture 
imposes the most restricted flamelet regime. Crucially, at a fixed 
oxygen concentration, the critical opposed-flow velocity gov­
erning the onset of flamelets does not exhibit a universal mono­
tonic trend; rather, it is highly sensitive to ambient pressure. At 
atmospheric pressure, the critical velocity follows the hierarchy 
Vg,CO2 > Vg,N2 > Vg,Ar, a sequence primarily dictated by the dif­
ferences in the specific heat capacities (thermal effects) of the 
respective diluent gases. However, as the ambient pressure is 
reduced to sub-atmospheric levels, this hierarchical relationship 
fundamentally shifts. This transition arises because the pressure 
reduction significantly enhances both gas-phase thermal and 
mass diffusivities, thereby allowing diffusive transport mecha­
nisms to compete with the bulk thermal capacity effects. 
Furthermore, under such sub-atmospheric conditions, the impact 
of lateral oxygen diffusion on the flame spread dynamics becomes 
increasingly pronounced at elevated oxygen concentrations.
(2) As the ambient pressure approaches the limiting condition, a 
critical ambient pressure emerges, below which the flame spread 
rate becomes essentially independent of the specific diluent gas 
species. With the reduction in ambient pressure, the heat effect, 
which can be characterized by specific heat capacity of the 
diluent, diminishes in its influence on the spread rate. This con­
trasts with atmospheric conditions, where different inert-gas 
diluents significantly influence the flame spread rate through a 
competition between thermal and diffusive effects.
(3) An effective Lewis number and P´eclet number are proposed as the 
governing parameters for near-limit flames, since they represent 
the thermophysical properties of materials and environmental 
conditions, respectively. Subsequently, a normalized flamma­
bility map is established to unify the coupled effects of flow ve­
locity, ambient pressure, and gas dilution. This integrated 
framework provides a robust tool for predicting flame behavior 
across diverse spacecraft cabin environments.
