1. Introduction 
1.2. Background 
Microgravity ﬂame radiation has been studied for several 
decades both because of its simpliﬁed physics and for its applica- 
tions in spacecraft ﬁre safety [1] . In normal gravity, radiation plays 
a weak role in the behavior of small-scale ﬂames due to a gravity- 
induced buoyant ﬂow. In microgravity, buoyancy no longer induces 
a convective ﬂow and radiative losses may be strong enough to 
lead to ﬂame quenching at low ﬂow velocities [2] . 
In this work, an opposing ﬂow ﬂame conﬁguration in micro- 
gravity is numerically modeled. An oxidizer mixture is blown over 
a thin, solid, PMMA fuel toward a ﬂame spreading in the upstream 
direction as depicted in Fig. 1 . The velocity of the opposing ﬂow 
is an important parameter in the spread rate and structure of the 
ﬂame and may be characterized by three regimes: kinetic, thermal, 
and radiative. 
In the kinetic regime, the opposed-ﬂow velocity is high and 
the residence time of the fuel-oxidizer mixture within the ﬂame 
is small. The rate of chemical kinetics within the reacting ﬂame 
front limits heat generation and combustion, eventually leading to 
blow-off extinction [3] . In numerical simulations, radiation is often 
ignored or simpliﬁed within the kinetic regime due to the dom- 
∗Corresponding author. 
E-mail address: prof.bhattacharjee@gmail.com (S. Bhattacharjee). 
inance of convective terms in scale analysis [4] , as described in 
the following section. As the opposed-ﬂow velocity decreases, the 
ﬂame enters the thermal regime, where chemical kinetics are as- 
sumed to occur inﬁnitely fast in comparison to the increased res- 
idence time. Within the thermal regime, the rate of heat transfer 
limits the spread rate of the ﬂame, which becomes largely inde- 
pendent of the opposed-ﬂow velocity. As the velocity decreases 
further into the radiative regime, possibly becoming quiescent, ra- 
diative heat losses from the gas and surface may lead to quenching 
of the ﬂame as the ﬂow is unable to provide suﬃcient oxidizer to 
sustain the heat losses. Radiation can no longer be neglected and 
is required to explain experimental results. 
For the opposed ﬂow ﬂame spread conﬁguration in this work, it 
has previously been found that both gas and surface radiation play 
important roles at low opposed-ﬂow velocities [4,5] and that the 
prominence of gas and surface radiation depends on fuel thickness, 
with very thin fuels showing stronger surface radiation [6] . While 
the primary role of radiation is heat loss from the solid and gas, 
radiation feedback from the hot gas to the upstream solid may pre- 
heat the fuel and promote ﬂame spread, in some cases outweigh- 
ing the gas radiative losses [7,8] . 
When introducing gas radiation, most numerical models simul- 
taneously include the gas-to-surface radiative feedback alongside 
the gas radiative losses [5,7,9] . This work seeks to quantify the ef- 
fects of the feedback by isolating each component of radiation: gas 
losses, surface losses, and feedback. A detailed radiation model that 
couples gas-to-surface feedback will be evaluated over the three 
ﬂame spread regimes and compared to simpliﬁed models of no ra- 
