this time, combustion must occur and radiation loss, if any, must 
make an impact. In the thermal regime, kinetics is considered in- 
ﬁnitely fast, and radiation loss negligible. The fundamental mecha- 
nism of ﬂame spread is sustainable transfer of heat from the gas to 
the virgin fuel to heat up the fuel from the ambient temperature 
to the vaporization temperature. 
Heat transfer involving the solid fuel may occur in four 
methods; gas-to-surface conduction, surface internal conduction, 
surface-to-environment radiation, and gas-to-surface radiation. In 
thin fuels, L g is much greater than surface half-thickness and the 
forward conduction of the solid is negligible compared to gas-to- 
surface conduction. Preheating the virgin fuel from the ambient to 
the vaporization temperature, and, hence the spread rate, is there- 
fore controlled by a competition between conduction, surface radi- 
ation, and gas-to-surface radiation. 
