While R g signiﬁes the importance of gas radiation loss com- 
pared to the dominant conduction heat transfer, only a fraction, f , 
of the gas radiation is absorbed by the solid with absorptivity α . 
The radiation feedback, therefore, should scale with αfR g . As can be 
seen from Eqs. (3) and (4) , the importance of radiation loss as well 
as radiation feedback in comparison to the dominant mechanism 
of conduction will increase in a microgravity environment where 
L g can be much larger in the absence of opposing ﬂow velocity. 
Conversely, as ﬂow velocity increases, the radiation timescales in- 
crease and the importance of radiative heat transfer can be ex- 
pected to be negligible in the kinetic regime where L g assumes its 
smallest values. 
2. Analysis and modeling 
The ﬂame is computed over half of the problem domain with 
a symmetry condition imposed at the centerline of the fuel thick- 
ness, leading to the 2D domain presented in Fig. 1 . The ﬂame is 
ﬁxed at x eigen , an unknown point arbitrarily deﬁned where T = 
1 . 2 T ∞ , and a unique spread rate V f that anchors the ﬂame at that 
position is desired. With the ﬂame being ﬁxed, the solid fuel ap- 
proaches the ﬂame at the desired unknown V f and the oxidizer 
mixture approaches at V f + V g . 
A number of simplifying assumptions are enforced to reduce 
the complexity of the problem. The ﬂame is considered to be 
spreading steadily as a 2D, laminar ﬂow with an optically thin and 
non-scattering media consisting of participating H 2 O and CO 2 . Py- 
rolysis and complete combustion are assumed to occur in the form 
of a single-step Arrhenius reaction. 
2.1. CFD solver 
The ﬂame spread model can be divided into two parts: a CFD 
model and a radiation model. The CFD model can further be di- 
vided into solid- and gas-phase solvers, which are cyclically solved 
to provide updated interface conditions to each other. The gas- 
phase solver is governed by mass, 2D momentum, species, and en- 
ergy conservation equations, given by Eq. (5 ) with variable deﬁni- 
tions in Table 1 , and solved using the SIMPLER method [12] . The 
