2.2. Radiation solver 
Due to the importance of 3D radiative effects, a fully 2D model 
is no longer suﬃcient to describe the ﬂame [7] . A fuel width of 3 
cm was chos"
doi:10.1016/j.ijheatmasstransfer.2020.119358,doi,A numerical investigation of radiation feedback in different regimes of opposed flow flame spread,4,3,"4 
S. Bhattacharjee and K. Dong / International Journal of Heat and Mass Transfer 150 (2020) 119358 
meshed into a grid and the intensities of 576 incident rays repre- 
senting a full hemisphere are integrated to calculate the radiative 
ﬂux. The incident intensities are discretized and calculated using 
the total transmittance nonhomogeneous (TTNH) model [13] . TTNH 
considers the effective temperature and pressure (dependent on 
the species proﬁles of the radiating molecules) of the path, which 
is derived from the CFD solver. H 2 O and CO 2 , but not MMA, are 
considered by TTNH as participating media. While H 2 O and CO 2 
are present throughout much of the computational domain, MMA 
is typically only present with notable concentration near the py- 
rolyzing surface. Soot formation is excluded to simplify the model. 
Although soot radiation can be signiﬁcant for thicker PMMA, for 
the thin fuels studied in this work, ﬂames are found (both in nor- 
mal and microgravity experiments) to be mostly blue in color. The 
neglect of soot radiation, which is expected to enhance radiation 
feedback, and the neglect of MMA vapor, which is expected to 
absorb the radiation from the gas and thus reduce the feedback, 
may have a mitigating effect. A much more sophisticated model is 
necessary to settle this deﬁnitively and extend the conclusions to 
sooty fuels. However, it is important to point out that despite the 
simplifying assumptions, the radiation model is adequate to high- 
light the competing nature of surface radiation loss and radiation 
feedback, an issue that has never before addressed in literature. 
Eqs. (11) –(15) are used to calculate a P , f , and ψ for use in the 
CFD solver. Although the CFD uses thin gas emission approxima- 
tion, the calculation of a P in the radiation solver does include both 
emission and absorption. The gas-to-surface radiation in the CFD 
is obtained from the parameters f and ψ. The CFD solver uses the 
new radiation parameters to obtained updated temperature and 
species proﬁles to be used as input for a new radiation solution. 
The CFD and radiation models are alternately solved until conver- 
gence is found among V f , a P , and f . A comprehensive description 
and validation of the CFD and radiation models is detailed else- 
