is the ﬂame temperature, deﬁned as the maximum temperature in the gas phase. 
six hours for coupled models, comparing favorably to Fire Dynam- 
ics Simulator, which may require a day or more for similar con- 
ditions. The opposed-ﬂow velocity is varied from near quench- 
ing to near blow-off and the radiation model is varied between 
non-radiating, surface-only, gas-only, uncoupled, and coupled. Two 
oxygen-nitrogen mixtures are considered: an atmospheric mixture 
with 21% oxygen and 79% nitrogen by volume and an elevated 50% 
oxygen and 50% nitrogen mixture to prevent quenching in a fully 
quiescent environment. The fuel half-thickness is set to a very thin 
20 microns to reduce the quenching velocity [2] , placing it well 
within the thin-fuel regime. Representative ﬂame images of the ra- 
diative, thermal, and kinetic regimes are displayed in Figs. 2 , and 
4 , respectively, at 21% oxygen. 
