aspects of radiation loss without feedback predictably weakens the 
ﬂame greater than any other radiation model. The addition of gas- 
to-surface feedback restores the strength of the ﬂame, increasing 
its size, temperature and spread rate. The 300 K temperature con- 
tour also extends further upstream, demonstrating the pre-heating 
effect of the gas radiation. The ﬂame temperature of the coupled 
model is most similar to the gas radiation model in all regimes, 
though the ﬂame structure is dissimilar in the radiative regime, 
and the spread rates differ in all regimes. The spread rates of the 
coupled ﬂames are similar to the surface radiation model in the 
thermal and kinetic regimes but differ in the radiative regime. 
Fig. 4. Temperature ﬁelds of the ﬁve radiation models at 21% oxygen in the kinetic 
regime with an opposed-ﬂow velocity of 100 cm/s. The development length extends 
to 122 mm. 
Low opposed-ﬂow velocities result in taller ﬂames with reduced 
lengths, temperatures and spread rates. High velocities also reduce 
length and spread rate but have the effect of ﬂattening the ﬂame 
and increasing the temperature. The ﬂame structures of the ﬁve 
radiation models also become more similar as velocity increases. 
In the radiative regime ﬂames, only the non-radiating model gen- 
erates suﬃcient heat ﬂux into the fuel to produce burnout, which 
changes the structure of the ﬂame. As the opposed-ﬂow velocity 
increases, burnout becomes more common. In Fig. 3 , only the un- 
coupled ﬂame is weak enough to prevent burnout, whereas all 
ﬂames in the kinetic regime in Fig. 4 cause burnout and have 
similar ﬂame structure. The delay in burnout in the uncoupled 
model causes minor discrepancies when compared against the 
other models at the same ﬂow velocity, which will become ap- 
parent later when analyzing the surface radiation. At 50% oxygen, 
similar trends are observed with respect to opposed-ﬂow velocity, 
though the ﬂames are generally stronger, larger, and more likely to 
cause fuel burnout."
