Fig. 5. The spread rates of the ﬁve models are plotted against reference veloc- 
ity from near quenching to near blow-off at two oxygen levels: 21% (solid lines) 
and 50% (dashed lines). Reference velocity is the sum of the spread rate and the 
opposed-ﬂow velocity, which act in opposite directions. 
To quantitatively analyze the ﬂame behavior, the spread rates 
of the ﬁve radiation models and two oxygen mixtures are plotted 
in Fig. 5 over the three ﬂame spread regimes. The non-radiating 
model (asterisks) displays strictly decreasing spread rate with ﬂow 
velocity, with no quenching at low velocities. Once radiation is 
added (with the exception of the 50% oxygen surface radiation 
model), the spread rate reduces at low velocities, giving the char- 
acteristic inverted U-shape plot. The spread rates of the radiating 
models increase with ﬂow velocity from quenched or quiescent 
spread to an inﬂection point, indicating the presence of the ther- 
mal regime, before reducing towards blow-off extinction. The gas 
(triangles) and surface (diamonds) radiation models each greatly 
reduce the spread rate, with the effect being most pronounced at 
low velocities. The contribution of gas radiation is stronger at high 
oxygen, reducing the quiescent spread rate by almost half. When 
both loss mechanisms are included (squares), the spread rate re- 
duces to the lowest of all models but is largely restored by the in- 
troduction of feedback (circles). In fact, outside the low velocity ra- 
diative regime, the coupled and surface models yield near-identical 
spread rates. Outside the radiative regime, feedback therefore ap- 
pears to cancel the effect of gas losses with regard to spread rate. 
Within the radiative regime, gas losses overwhelm feedback and 
spread rate reduces. 
The reported ﬂame temperature is deﬁned as the maximum 
temperature within the gas-phase and is plotted in Fig. 6 . The non- 
radiating model is almost independent of ﬂow velocity and is close 
to the adiabatic ﬂame temperature (2687 K at 21% oxygen and 
4 4 40 K at 50%). The ﬂame temperature of the surface model re- 
duces at low velocities but converges with the non-radiating model 
at high velocities. As ﬂow velocity increases, burnout occurs earlier 
in the fuel and the pyrolysis length shortens. Less surface area sur- 
vives to radiate and the difference between the non-radiating and 
surface models becomes negligible. Though the ﬂame temperature 
is a function of the gas-phase, surface radiation causes the surface 
to act as a stronger heat sink, increasing conduction from the gas- 
phase and reducing the ﬂame temperature slightly. Gas radiation 
reduces ﬂame temperature far more than surface radiation, even 
where burnout does not occur. At the lowest reported ﬂow veloc- 
ity, gas radiation reduces the ﬂame temperature by 57% at high 
oxygen and 40% at atmospheric oxygen. Adding surface radiation 
