locity at 21% oxygen (solid lines) and 50% oxygen (dashed lines). The three models 
with gas radiation show strong agreement. 
Fig. 7. Vaporization temperature, deﬁned as the maximum temperature of the solid 
fuel, is plotted against reference velocity. The three models with surface radiation 
show strong agreement. 
and gas-to-surface feedback to the gas model appears to have neg- 
ligible effect on ﬂame temperature. 
The vaporization temperatures of all models strictly increase 
with ﬂow velocity, as shown in Fig. 7 . The vaporization temper- 
ature of the non-radiating model is predictably the greatest fol- 
lowed by the gas model. In these two models, the surface is unable 
to radiate heat, which is its only heat loss mechanism (gas tem- 
perature above the surface always exceeds surface temperature). 
The three models that incorporate surface radiation show strong 
agreement, particularly at low oxygen. Burnout in the solid fuel 
causes all models to converge at high velocities as the effects of 
surface radiation are reduced. Surface radiation dominates the be- 
havior of the vaporization temperature at low and mid-range ve- 
locities where gas losses and feedback are minimally impactful. 
To assess the importance of radiative losses and feedback, ra- 
diative heat transfer as a percentage of heat generated by combus- 
tion is plotted against reference velocity in Figs. 8 and 9 , with the"
