non-radiating model being neglected. Fig. 8 plots surface radiation 
losses and Fig. 9 plots gas radiation losses and, in the case of the 
coupled model, gas-to-surface feedback. Summing the surface and 
gas radiative losses and subtracting feedback provides the total ra- 
diative losses. Remaining heat is lost via convection. Despite dif- 
ferences in the gas-phase losses between the gas, uncoupled, and 
coupled models, the total radiative losses for the three models are 
nearly identical. Radiation losses of the three models with gas radi- 
ation are signiﬁcant at low velocities, reducing as the opposed-ﬂow 
velocity increases. Just before quenching in the radiative regime, 
the losses are greater than 80% of the heat generated by combus- 
tion. When blow-off occurs in the kinetic regime, radiation losses 
are reduced to less than a quarter of that. The total radiation of the 
model with only surface radiation is much lower, with total losses 
being identical to the losses depicted in Fig. 8 . 
Isolating the surface loss percentages, Fig. 8 shows that all mod- 
els that incorporate surface radiation experience similar percent- 
age losses, which decrease with velocity. Burnout occurs closer to 
the ﬂame front and less area survives to radiate. The 50% oxygen 
ﬂames have a lower percentage loss than the 21% ﬂames, but due 
to greater heat generated by combustion, the magnitude of the sur- 
face losses is greater. A discrepancy in the uncoupled model occurs 
at 21% oxygen at 20 cm/s. At this velocity, burnout has occurred in 
the coupled and surface models but not the uncoupled model. Gas- 
to-surface feedback, though less than 5% at 20 cm/s, causes the 
fuel to burn out at a lower velocity. This affects the structure of 
the ﬂame as shown in the coupled and uncoupled ﬂame images in 
Fig. 3 . In ﬂames nearing the burnout velocity, feedback may be im- 
portant in determining the correct behavior of the ﬂame and fuel. 
Gas radiation, depicted in Fig. 9 , is much greater than surface 
radiation, indicating that gas radiation is a signiﬁcant contributor 
to radiative losses in all regimes. Unlike the monotonically decreas- 
ing surface radiation, uncoupled and coupled gas radiation tend to 
peak within the thermal regime and decrease rapidly afterwards, 
with all three gas radiation models now converging. This trend is 
most apparent at the lower oxygen level. From Fig. 5 , it is clear 
that ﬂame temperatures strictly increase with velocity, which may 
explain the increasing gas radiation from low to medium velocities. 
At high velocities, however, the ﬂame stretches and ﬂattens near 
the solid surface. Though the gas interior becomes hotter, its vol- 
ume diminishes and radiation reduces. Gas-to-surface feedback is 
relatively small, with a maximum of less than 15% in the radiative 
regime and decreasing to less than 1% in the kinetic regime. This 
corroborates the spread rate results in Fig. 5 showing that feedback 
becomes less important as velocity increases. 
The differences between the three models with gas radiation 
can be explained by surface radiation and feedback. In the gas 
model, the fuel surface does not radiate and, as shown by the va- 
porization temperatures, is hotter. Gas-to-surface conduction is re- 
duced, allowing the gas to retain and radiate a greater amount of 
heat. The coupled model emits more heat via gas radiation than 
the uncoupled model, but when subtracting the gas-to-surface 
feedback from its emitted gas radiation, the actual gas radiation 
lost to the environment is similar in both models. Though signif- 
icant in the radiative regime, the differences between the models 
reduce with ﬂow velocity as burnout reduces the size of the fuel 
surface. 
Fig. 10 depicts the heat ﬂuxes of the gas-surface interface 
for the coupled radiation model at 21% oxygen. Despite radiation 
losses accounting for the majority of heat lost from the ﬂame, con- 
duction dominates the gas-surface heat transfer and, in particular, 
the heat transfer of the leading edge, deﬁned by the region up- 
stream from the point of peak conduction (the leading edge). The 
heat ﬂuxes in Fig. 10 (a) result from an opposed-ﬂow velocity of 3 
cm/s. Though radiation is at its greatest at this ﬂow velocity, sur- 
face radiation and feedback each transfer only about 25% as much 
heat as conduction within the leading edge. Only downstream 
from the leading edge does the surface radiation overwhelm con- 
duction. This is what justiﬁes using the conduction length scale 
at the leading edge even in a strongly radiative ﬂame. While the 
ﬂame spread rate depends on the physics of heat transfer at the 
leading edge, the ﬂame size and ﬂame temperature are strongly 
affected by the downstream radiation losses and therefore the con- 
duction length scale cannot be used to, say, scale the ﬂame height 
in such ﬂames. 
A close look at the leading edge heat transfers in Fig. 10 will 
reveal that in the microgravity regime, the radiation feedback sur- 
passes the radiation loss from the surface and even mildly sur- 
passes the conduction heat ﬂux at a distance greater than 5 mm 
upstream of the ﬂame. Though feedback does not extend the pre- 
heating length, it entirely cancels the surface radiation losses in 
the leading edge. The heat ﬂuxes of a thermal regime ﬂame at 
20 cm/s, depicted in Fig. 10 (b) show similar trends, though con- 
duction has increased signiﬁcantly from the 3 cm/s ﬂame while 
radiation has increased only mildly. As a result, surface radiation"
