the leading edge but provides a small amount of net heat transfer 
into the fuel. In the radiative regime, surface losses and feedback 
are greater but mostly cancel each other, providing no net boost to 
the heat ﬂux at the leading edge. These results are quite consistent 
with the prediction from the scale analysis. 
4. Conclusions 
Five radiation models are evaluated at atmospheric and ele- 
vated oxygen levels at ﬂow velocities covering the range of the 
three ﬂame spread regimes over a thin PMMA fuel. While radia- 
tion is found to have a strong inﬂuence on ﬂame temperature in all 
regimes, it is most signiﬁcant at low ﬂow velocities of the so-called 
microgravity regime, where losses exceeded 80% of heat generated 
by combustion. Gas and solid phase radiation are both signiﬁcant 
in the radiative regime, decreasing in their inﬂuence as ﬂow veloc- 
ity increases. Surface radiation and feedback become negligible in 
the kinetic regime, whereas gas radiation continues to contribute 
up to 20% of heat losses before blow-off. Despite the large inﬂu- 
ence on heat transfer in the ﬂame, surface radiation and feedback 
into the leading edge is at most only 25% of the conductive heat 
ﬂux. Feedback provides pre-heating in the leading edge that largely 
cancels the surface radiative losses. Under such conditions, sur- 
face radiation is suﬃcient to describe spread rate and vaporization 
temperature, though gas radiative losses are still required for de- 
scribing gas-phase characteristics of ﬂame temperature and shape. 
Within the radiative regime, gas losses outweigh feedback and the 
net effect of gas radiation is to suppress spread rate. Feedback has 
little effect on the ﬂame and vaporization temperatures but may 
be required to predict the occurrence of burnout in ﬂames nearing 
radiative extinction in a microgravity environment. The presence 
of soot, which is neglected in this work, may signiﬁcantly enhance 
the feedback effect and should be considered in future work. 
