doi:10.1016/j.proci.2008.05.081,doi,Experimental comparison of opposed and concurrent flame spread in a forced convective microgravity environment,2,1,"In normal gravity and standard atmospheric con-
ditions, upward ﬂame spread is much faster than
downward ﬂame spread over most fuels in the
absence of forced airﬂow. Hirano studied down-
ward ﬂame spread over cellulose in air in a ﬂow
duct with opposed and concurrent ﬂow. He found
a region of stable opposed spread, but as soon as
the relative velocity became concurrent, the ﬂame
spread rate became rapid and unstable [7].
More recent microgravity experiments suggest
that ﬂames in low gravity may spread preferen-
tially in an opposed ﬂow direction. Olson et al.
[5] studied central ignition and spread across cel-
lulose in air in a small ﬂow duct aboard the Space
Shuttle, and found that the ﬂame spread preferen-
tially upstream. Flame spread rates were higher in
opposed ﬂow than concurrent ﬂow under the
same ﬂow conditions (up to 10 cm/s). In addition,
concurrent ﬂames could not survive below forced
ﬂows of 2 cm/s whereas the opposed ﬂames were
able to survive at 0.5 cm/s, which indicates that
the extinction boundary was skewed toward the
concurrent side of quiescence. Takahashi et al.
[8] found a similar result in ﬂows up to 15 cm/s
for three diﬀerent thicknesses of very thin non-
charring PMMA at three oxygen concentrations.
Without free convection from buoyant forces
bringing fresh oxidizer to the ﬂame or carrying
away heat or products, ﬂames in microgravity
must rely on diﬀusion or weak forced convection
for transport. Therefore, they diﬀer substantially
from those observed on earth. (Typical spacecraft
cabin air ventilation velocities are 5–20 cm/s [9],
which are far lower than buoyant velocities on
Earth.) However, despite the previous studies,
there has never been a side-by-side comparison
of the eﬀects of ﬂow magnitude and direction,
pressure, and oxygen on ﬂame spread measured
in the same apparatus for the same fuel.
The major objective of this paper is to compare
concurrent and opposed ﬂame spread in a range
of velocities at pressure/oxygen conditions as cur-
rently proposed for new NASA spacecraft [10].
Because there have been comparatively fewer con-
current ﬂame spread experiments in microgravity,
a second objective of this study is to better under-
stand forced-ﬂow, concurrent ﬂame spread.
2. Experimental apparatus
The ﬂame spread tests are performed in a low-
speed ﬂow tunnel mounted on a NASA Zero
Gravity Research Facility drop rig bus that has
previously been described in [11], with updates
to the ﬂow system and software control. The ﬂow
updates include a second gas reservoir and a
higher capacity back pressure control valve for
greater ﬂow capacity, and higher permeability
porous plates and honeycomb were installed to
improve high ﬂow distribution and laminarity.
Smoke wire tests, both in normal and in micro-
gravity, were performed to verify the ﬂow quality.
Tests were performed using a Kimwipes1
sample (full thickness surface area density 2 mg/
cm2) taped to a new sheet metal sample holder
with an igniter wire on either the upstream or
downstream end of the 5 cm wide by 10 cm long
sample (Fig. 1). The ﬂow was started before the
drop to establish steady ﬂow and test section pres-
sure prior to the drop rig release. The hot wire
igniter was energized at release so that ignition
occurred in microgravity. The microgravity period
lasted 5.18 s, of which about 2 s is typically used
for ignition and ﬂame spread away from the igni-
ter. When the drop rig reaches the bottom of the
evacuated drop shaft the test section is vented to
vacuum to extinguish the ﬂame.
Ignition and ﬂame spread were recorded by
two orthogonal color cameras with automatic
gain control. Flame shape, size, and spread rate
were measured using Spotlight software [12]. Rel-
ative
luminosity
is
compared
between
video
frames and between tests using a constant bright-
ness red LED in the corner of the ﬂame images
which also ﬂashes at release marking the drop in
the video. The auto-gain feature was used to
ensure each test was imaged at the appropriate
