3. Flame spread results
Thirty concurrent and ten opposed ﬂow tests
were conducted. The ﬂow velocity varied between
0 and 30 cm/s, the pressure varied between 3.6 and
14.7 psia, and the oxygen percentage ranged from
24% to 85%. First, we will present and discuss rep-
resentative ﬂame images. Then we will describe
the eﬀect of each of these variables, and present
correlations that combine the results into one
parameter.
Figure 1 shows front and side views of the
ﬂame during concurrent spread. The ﬂame base
is quite ﬂat in the lateral direction, while the ﬂame
tips and pyrolysis region have a curved shape and
are longest in the center of the sample. The front
view was useful to see the length of the pyrolysis
region, and to view the jagged edge where burnout
occurred, but the ﬂame tips are better viewed in
the side view.
Figure 2 contrasts side view images of opposed
and concurrent ﬂames under the same oxygen and
pressure conditions, and at the highest ﬂow veloc-
ity (30 cm/s) where the ﬂame spread rates are
nearly equal. The opposed-ﬂow ﬂame shows the
classic blue leading edges, followed by long bright
tails that project beyond an outer surrounding
ﬂame envelope. For opposed ﬂow, the ﬂame lead-
ing edge reaches a steady propagation rate almost
immediately (<1 s) while the ﬂame size continues
to grow for a few seconds (depending on the exact
conditions). In the concurrent case, the ﬂame base
remains stationary for 1s after ignition as the
ﬂame length develops. The ﬂame base accelerates
to a steady spread rate within 1 s once it starts
to propagate. The ﬂame length reaches a steady
state within the ﬁeld of view and time constraints
of the drop for some of the conditions presented
here. The concurrent ﬂame exhibits a wide tail
structure under most conditions, with a violet-
edged outer ﬂame envelope and orange inner tails
that can extend downstream past the outer ﬂame.
Flame tracking was generally done with the
edge view either manually or using an appropriate
threshold value for the target. The contrast
between the black background and the ﬂame is
strong, as shown in Fig. 1b and Fig. 2, and any
minor changes in camera gain once the ﬂame is
spreading will not signiﬁcantly aﬀect the tracking
of the steep intensity gradients that mark the
edges of the ﬂame.
We plotted the position of the ﬂame leading
edge (opposed) or ﬂame base (concurrent) vs. time
throughout the drop and measured the slope of
the linear region of the data. The ﬂame is tracked
on both sides of the fuel to determine spread rate,
Fig. 2. (a) Opposed and (b) concurrent ﬂame spread with
