3.3. Eﬀect of oxygen concentration and normoxic
conditions
In tests with ﬁxed ﬂow velocity (30 cm/s) and
pressure (10.2 psia), we found that the ﬂame
spread rate increased linearly with oxygen concen-
tration. Of more relevance to future space explo-
ration, however, are normoxic conditions where
the partial pressure of oxygen (rather than the
oxygen mole fraction) is held constant at the same
level as normal atmospheric air. Figure 6 presents
the eﬀect of oxygen percentage on ﬂame spread
rate for the case where the pressure is the normox-
ic equivalent for each speciﬁc oxygen percentage.
The ﬂow velocity varies depending on the test,
as noted in the legend. The graph shows that
regardless of ﬂow direction, pressure, or gravity
condition, the ﬂame spread (and therefore ﬁre
danger) is greater at higher oxygen concentra-
tions, even though the partial pressure of oxygen
is constant. The opposed ﬂow and quiescent (no
forced ﬂow) tests show the same dependence on
oxygen
percentage.
The
concurrent
tests
at
30 cm/s, by contrast, have a much shallower
slope attributed to a non-linear pressure depen-
dence. Due to ﬂame-ﬂow interactions described
in the discussion section, we were not able to
achieve stable concurrent ﬂames at 15 cm/s to
compare directly with the 15 cm/s opposed ﬂow
data; so we employed a correlation (discussed
next) to predict the behavior for 15 cm/s concur-
rent ﬂow. Comparing 15 cm/s ﬂame spread rates,
the opposed spread is faster than concurrent even
down to 21% oxygen.
3.4. Flame spread correlations
In order to account for the eﬀect of all the vari-
ables on one graph, it is useful to combine them
into one parameter. For normal gravity, down-
