tive and radiative heat transfer to the fuel), while
the pressure eﬀect is quite weak in this range. This
is in contrast with Bhattacharjee et al., who
showed a much stronger dependence on pressure
for fourfold thicker fuel in a quiescent environ-
ment [4]. Those tests were shown to be near-limit,
where heat losses such as radiation become impor-
tant. Indeed, even the near-limit data from
[1,11,16] show a fall oﬀfrom the correlation as
forced ﬂow and oxygen become low enough that
heat release is reduced to the point where the heat
losses inherent in the experiment become compa-
rable. Thus, the correlation can be viewed as a
worst-case (i.e., highest) prediction of spread rate
for a given oxygen and pressure condition.
For concurrent spread, there was no such
correlation in the literature that we could ﬁnd,
perhaps because in normal gravity the ﬂame
spread rate is often acceleratory. The forced
convective
heat
transfer
from
the
gas-phase
reaction to the unburned fuel surface is what
controls
the
concurrent
ﬂame
spread
rate
in microgravity. Using NuL  Re1=2
L
