numerical models for concurrent ﬂow [17,18].
The general success of the correlation parameter
indicates that forced convective heat transfer
from the ﬂame to the unburned fuel surface is
the controlling physical eﬀect in microgravity
concurrent ﬂame spread.
This correlation provided the best ﬁt to the
experimental data, as shown in Fig. 8. Here, all
our data are shown, along with some earlier
near-limit data from Grayson [13] and Pettegrew
[19]. The normoxic data ranged from 24% oxygen
at 12.8 psia to 85% oxygen at 3.6 psia, all at
30 cm/s ﬂow. The oxygen varies from 24% to
50% oxygen at 10.2 psia and 30 cm/s. Flow veloc-
ity varies from 1 to 30 cm/s at 34% oxygen and
10.2 psia. Pressure varies from 5 to 14.7 psia at
40% oxygen and 30 cm/s. For most of the range,
the spread rate depends linearly on the correlation
parameter, but at very low near-limit values the
data show a fall oﬀthat the linear ﬁt fails to cap-
ture, as predicted [18].
4. Discussion
In this section we elaborate on two points men-
tioned above: the fact that opposed spread is
found to be more rapid than concurrent under
many of the conditions examined, and the prob-
lem with obtaining stable concurrent ﬂames for
intermediate forced ﬂow velocities.
To show more clearly under what conditions
opposed ﬂame spread is faster than concurrent
ﬂame spread, Fig. 9 applies both ﬂame correla-
tions developed above to determine the boundary
where the two spread rates are equal. This was
done for two pressures: the normoxic equivalent
pressure and 1 atm. It is seen that over virtually
the entire range of spacecraft atmospheres the
opposed spread is faster, especially for the norm-
oxic case. Furthermore, for the realistic scenario
of a sample ignited in a central region rather than
at an edge the ﬂame will spread upstream. Any
downstream ﬂame would be even further inhibited
by the vitiated atmosphere generated by the
upstream ﬂame.
One of the most striking visual observations
from some of these tests was the unexpected inter-
action between the concurrent ﬂame and the ﬂow
at intermediate forced ﬂow velocities. Figure 4
shows a gap in data for the concurrent ﬂame
spread between 10 and 30 cm/s. In that range,
the ﬂame would ignite and then quickly develop
an opposed ﬂow leading edge ﬂame at the down-
stream edge of the ﬂame. It would then propagate
as an apparent opposed ﬂame as evidenced by its
shape and coloring. Often it would fold back over
on itself and become a concurrent ﬂame shortly
