doi:10.1016/j.proci.2008.05.081,doi,Experimental comparison of opposed and concurrent flame spread in a forced convective microgravity environment,7,6,"before the end of the drop (too near the end to
obtain useful spread rates data), but in some cases
the opposed ﬂame leading edge would persist
throughout the drop.
The smoke and particle motion in the tests
provide clues for understanding this behavior.
Smoke from ignition was observed to coil up
just downstream of the ignition kernel in many
tests, and. smoke far downstream of the igniter
was also seen traveling upstream opposite to
the forced ﬂow after ignition. Particles are seen
to deﬂect around the ﬂame as if the ﬂow were
going around an obstruction. These eﬀects are
best seen in the supplementary videos accompa-
nying this paper.
Based
on
observed
particle
and
smoke
motions, the ﬂame strongly aﬀects the ﬂow
around it. This was previously shown for ﬂame
spread over liquids, where the ﬂame presented
enough of a barrier that the main forced ﬂow
diverted around it [20]. This was also shown for
thermally thick solids in low velocity opposed
ﬂow [21], where the ﬂame was observed to modify
the ﬂow structure upstream of the leading edge of
the opposed ﬂow ﬂame in the same range of
forced ﬂows as reported in this work.
We hypothesize that thermal expansion from
the ignition and incipient ﬂame as well as Stefan
ﬂow from the pyrolyzing fuel are sources of ﬂuid
volume generated within the ﬂow ﬁeld, so that
in the proper Reynold’s number range, a ﬂow sep-
aration occurs at the leading edge of the ﬂame,
and a pair of recirculation cells forms downstream
of the ﬂame. For opposed ﬂow these cells are sim-
ilar to vortices behind a circular cylinder in cross
ﬂow, since the sample has burned away down-
stream of the ﬂame. In the concurrent case, the
cells resemble those behind a backward facing
step due to the no-slip condition imposed by the
sample surface.
Using a measured ﬂame ‘eﬀective diameter’
and a kinematic viscosity at Tﬁlm = 900 K, the
Reynold’s number for the onset of the observed
concurrent ﬂow ﬂame recirculation eﬀects is found
to be in the 30–50 range, which matches the Re
regime where bound vortices will form behind a
cylindrical object or a recirculation cell forms
behind a backward facing step. Due to the limited
microgravity time, it is unclear from our testing to
date if this recirculation cell would persist or if it is
an ignition transient that would eventually wash
away. Certainly, no numerical models we are
aware of predict this phenomenon, even as a tran-
sient behavior.
5. Conclusions
Microgravity ﬂame spread experiments in both
concurrent and opposed ﬂow have been carried
out with a thin cellulose fuel while independently
varying forced ﬂow velocity and direction, abso-
lute pressure, and oxygen mole fraction. We
found the following correlations ﬁt the data well:
Opposed:
