Here Vf is the ﬂame spread in cm/s, O2 is the
oxygen mole fraction, Vﬂow is the absolute value
of the forced ﬂow velocity in cm/s, and P is the
pressure in atm.
Other major conclusions include:
 Based on the concurrent ﬂame correlation’s
functional dependencies, it appears that lami-
nar boundary layer convective heat transfer
from the ﬂame to the unburned downstream
fuel controls the ﬂame spread rate.
 Using the correlations, opposed ﬂow ﬂames
are shown to spread faster than concurrent
ﬂow ﬂames under otherwise similar conditions
(pressure, oxygen concentration) in nearly all
spacecraft atmospheres.
 In the event of a ﬁre, ﬂame spread aboard
spacecraft is likely to occur in the opposed
mode as the upstream ﬂame spreads faster
and the downstream ﬂame is further inhibited
by the vitiated atmosphere of combustion
products from the upstream ﬂame.
