doi:10.1016/j.proci.2010.06.130,doi,Microgravity experiments of flame spreading along a fuel droplet array in fuel vapor-air mixture,2,1,"volatile and/or fuel droplets are large, spray
ﬂames spread from droplet-to-droplet in the front
of a macroscopic ﬂame. It is important to study
the ﬂame spreading along fuel droplet array.
Some experimental studies have been made on
ﬂame spreading along a fuel droplet array in stag-
nant environments [2,4,8,14,16], in which air was
employed as an ambient gas. Fuel droplets in real
spray combustion, fuel droplets are very ﬁne.
Therefore, surrounding air is quickly saturated
with fuel vapor from droplets. Even in a cold
air, ﬁne fuel droplets generate fuel vapor-air pre-
mixture around them when they pass through
the preheat zone of a macroscopic ﬂame. Flame
spreading along a fuel droplet array in fuel
vapor-air mixture is important area for funda-
mental research on spray combustion. However,
such a experimental research has not been carried
out yet.
In this work, ﬂame spreading along a fuel
droplet array in a fuel vapor-air mixture was
investigated experimentally. The fuel for a fuel
vapor-air mixture was identical to the fuel for a
fuel droplet array. Behavior of ﬂame spread along
a fuel droplet array was observed and ﬂame
spread speed was measured for various droplet
spacings and fuel vapor concentrations of a sur-
rounding gas under microgravity conditions.
2. Experimental apparatus and procedure
2.1. Experimental model
Figure 1 illustrates the droplet array which we
aimed to realize. The experimental model of the
present work is a droplet array that is a part of
three-dimensional droplet matrix. The ceramic
walls and observation windows of a test section
were designed to play a role like a symmetric
plane of heat and mass transfers between the
droplets located on the plane perpendicular to
the ﬂame spread direction. Microgravity experi-
ments were carried out to suppress natural con-
vection which breaks the scale-similarity and the
symmetry of ﬂame spread phenomena. The test
section was ﬁlled with fuel vapor-air mixture.
Liquid equivalence ratio /l, gas equivalence ratio
/g, and total equivalence ratio /t [17] in the test
section can be deﬁned so that it is possible to com-
pare results of the present work with those
obtained from prevaporized-spray combustion
experiments.
2.2. Experimental apparatus
Figure 2 shows the experimental apparatus.
The apparatus consists of a droplet array holder,
a droplet array elevator, a droplet array genera-
tor, a fuel feed pump, and a combustion chamber
with a shutter. All devices were controlled by a
programmable
sequencer.
The
droplet
array
holder was equipped with 10 sets of X-shaped sil-
icon carbide ﬁbers of 14 lm in diameter. Droplets
were suspended at the intersection of the X-
shaped ﬁbers. Droplet spacing was determined
by the spacing of the X-shaped ﬁbers. The droplet
array holder was mounted on the droplet array
elevator. The slider-crank mechanism driven by
a stepping motor was applied to the droplet array
elevator. Ten fuel droplets were simultaneously
generated by the droplet array generator which
consists of a slide stage, a fuel pool, 10 glass nee-
dles, and the fuel feed pump driven by a servo
motor. The outer diameter of ﬁne-drawn glass
needles was about 40 lm. The glass needles were
selected and grouped so that 10 droplets of
0.8 mm in initial diameter can be generated within
±5% accuracy.
Figure 3 illustrates the test section in the com-
bustion chamber together with a droplet array.
The test section is a rectangular parallelepiped
