dicular to the longer direction is a square, lwh on a
side. Droplets were located at equal spacing S on
the longitudinal center axis of the test section. One
end of the test section was open to simulate outer
edge of a droplet cloud, and the other end was
closed to simulate the symmetry center of the
droplet cloud. Flame spreading was initiated from
the open end side. Number of droplet was always
10. The ﬁrst droplet was ignited by a hot wire (a
iron-chrome wire of 0.39 mm in diameter), which
was mounted on the droplet array holder. The
tenth droplet was located S/2 away from the
closed end wall. Although lwh is variable, lwh
was ﬁxed at 25 mm in the present work. Ceramics
were employed to the test section wall to minimize
heat loss of a ﬂame. Observation windows are
double-glazed windows to avoid the condensation
of fuel vapor on the glasses. The test section was
equipped with a slit for insertion of a droplet
array, which was closed during ﬂame spread
experiment by the droplet array holder. Tempera-
ture of the test section was controlled with electric
heaters, a K-type thermocouple, a PID tempera-
ture control unit, and a variable power supply.
To generate fuel vapor-air mixture in the test
section, liquid fuel was injected into the test sec-
tion heated-up uniformly. During the preparation
of fuel vapor-air mixture, the droplet array holder
was evacuated beneath the combustion chamber,
and the open end and the slit of the test section
were closed by the shutter. The amount of injected
liquid fuel was always more than enough to gener-
ate saturated fuel vapor in the test section. Gas
equivalence ratio of the fuel vapor-air mixture,
/g, was calculated from the saturated vapor pres-
sure at the test section temperature on the base of
the Dalton’s law. The temperature of a fuel vapor-
air mixture varied from 303.6 to 324.1 K in the
present work depending on the gas equivalence
ratio. The temperature diﬀerence of the mixtures
was presumed to be negligible as compared with
the ﬂame temperature. After fuel vapor-air mix-
ture was formed in the test section, droplets were
generated by the droplet array generator and sus-
pended by the droplet array holder. When the
sequencer received a cue signal to start experi-
ment, the shutter was opened, the droplet array
was lifted up into the test section by the droplet
array elevator, and the ﬁrst droplet was ignited
by the hot wire.
A microgravity condition was produced by two
drop towers; one is the MGLAB drop tower in Toki
(microgravity duration: 4.5 s); the other tower is
our own small drop tower (microgravity duration:
1.1 s). Both microgravity levels are below 104 G0.
After saturated fuel vapor-air mixture was formed
in the test section, the experimental apparatus
installed into a drop capsule was ﬁxed at the top
of the drop tower. A droplet array was generated
by remote control. Two seconds after the cue signal
to start experiment was sent, the capsule was
dropped. The ﬁrst droplet was ignited under micro-
gravity condition. The behavior of a spreading
ﬂame was observed using a high-speed video cam-
era (frame speed: 30–100 fps, exposure time: open,
image size: 640  480 pixels).
When glass needles for droplet generation were
replaced, fuel discharge volume of the fuel feed
pump was readjusted. Backlit images of generated
droplets were recorded and their diameter was
measured. Droplets were almost spherical, but
slightly elongated in the gravity direction. Equiv-
alent droplet diameter was deﬁned as (a2b)1/3,
where a and b are the short and long diameters
of a droplet, respectively. Droplet spacing was
deﬁned as the average of center-to-center dis-
tances of droplets.
2.3. Experimental conditions
Microgravity condition was applied for all
experiments. n-Decane was employed as a liquid
fuel. Experiments were performed at atmospheric
pressure. The droplet spacing S was varied from
1.6 to 10.2 mm. The initial droplet diameter d0
was ﬁxed at 0.8 mm. The gas equivalence ratio
was varied from 0.2 to 0.7. The lower limit of pre-
mixed ﬂame propagation in the test section was
0.7 in the gas equivalence ratio.
3. Experimental results and discussion
3.1. Observation of ﬂame spreading behavior
Flame spreading behavior was classiﬁed into
three modes based on Refs. [11–13]. Figures 4–6
show sequential images and the history of leading
edge position of a spreading ﬂame of each mode.
Figure 4 shows ﬂame spreading behavior of Mode
1. Normalized droplet spacing S/d0 and gas equiv-
alence ratio /g were 2 and 0.5, respectively. The
leading edge of a group combustion ﬂame spreads
with constant speed and does not close between
droplets. Figure 5 shows ﬂame spreading behavior
of Mode 2, which was observed at S/d0 = 3.75 and
/g = 0.5. The leading edge of a group combustion
ﬂame closes once between burnt and unburnt
droplets, and then spreads to the vapor layer of
the neighboring unburnt droplet. Flame spread
speed is almost constant. Figure 6 shows ﬂame
