doi:10.1016/j.proci.2010.06.130,doi,Microgravity experiments of flame spreading along a fuel droplet array in fuel vapor-air mixture,5,4,"3.2. Flame spread speed
The speed of a diﬀusion ﬂame spreading along
a fuel droplet array is one of the important char-
acteristics of droplet array combustion. The ﬂame
spread speed Vs was measured at various droplet
spacings and gas equivalence ratios. The ﬂame
spread speed was deﬁned as the slop of the line
obtained using least squares method from the his-
tory of ﬂame leading edge position between the
third and tenth droplets.
Figure 7 shows the normalized ﬂame spread
speed as a function of the gas equivalence ratio.
The ﬂame spread speed was normalized by the ini-
tial droplet diameter on the basis of the similarity
theory Mikami et al. proposed in Ref. [14]. The
normalized ﬂame spread speed increases as the
increase in the gas equivalence ratio at all normal-
ized droplet spacings. The ratio of ﬂame spread
speed against the ﬂame spread speed at /g = 0.2,
Vs,0.2, was shown in Fig. 8 in order to understand
the droplet-spacing dependence of the increasing
eﬀect of the gas equivalence ratio on the ﬂame
spread speed. One can see that the inﬂuence of
the gas equivalence ratio on the ﬂame spread
speed becomes strong as the increase in the drop-
let spacings. As the increase in the gas equivalence
ratio, the heat release rate of the premixed com-
bustion zone at the outer edge of a spreading
ﬂame increases [18]. Heat transfer from the lead-
ing edge of a spreading ﬂame to the neighboring
unburnt droplet limits ﬂame spreading. At larger
droplet spacings, the heat transfer takes longer
time. Therefore, it is supposed that the increasing
eﬀect of the gas equivalence ratio on the ﬂame
spread speed becomes stronger at larger droplet
spacings because the heat release rate at the outer
edge of a spreading ﬂame increases as the increase
in the gas equivalence ratio.
