mixed ﬂame is cooled by unburnt droplets. There-
fore, the increase in the droplet spacing leads to
the increase in the speed of the propagating pre-
mixed ﬂame and consequently to the increase in
the spread speed of the diﬀusion ﬂame following
the propagating premixed ﬂame.
Flame spread induction time si was deﬁned as
the averaged time for a ﬂame to travel between
neighboring droplets. Normalized ﬂame spread
induction time si=d2
0 was derived from dividing
the normalized droplet spacing by the normalized
ﬂame spread speed.Figure 10 shows the normalized
ﬂame spread induction time as a function of the
normalized droplet spacing. The normalized ﬂame
spread induction time keeps nearly constant in the
range of 2 6 S/d0 6 3, and increases in the range of
S/d0 P 3.75. The reason why the ﬂame spread
speed increases in the range of 2 6 S/d0 6 3 in
Fig. 9 is that the ﬂame spread induction time is
almost constant although the droplet spacing
increases. The reason why the ﬂame spread induc-
tion time is almost constant in the range of 2 6 S/
d0 6 3 is supposed to be that the time required to
heat an unburnt droplet up to the temperature at
which a ﬂammable mixture layer is formed around
the droplet dominates the ﬂame spread induction
time. If the temperature of the ﬂame leading edge
is assumed to be independent of the normalized
droplet spacing, the time required to heat up an
unburnt droplet is also nearly independent of the
normalized droplet spacing. Since the total heat
transfer rate from the leading edge of a group com-
bustion ﬂame to unburnt droplets is assumed to be
independent of the normalized droplet spacing, the
time required to heat up one unburnt droplet is also
independent of the normalized droplet spacing.
3.3. Group combustion ﬂame
Behavior of group combustion ﬂame after a
spreading ﬂame reached to the tenth droplet was
observed and classiﬁed into the following three
types:
– Type G-I:
Shrinking of group combustion
ﬂame starts from the open end side of the test
section and extinction occurs at the tenth
droplet.
– Type G-II: Shrinking of group combustion
ﬂame starts from both the open and the closed
end sides of the test section, and extinction
occurs between the third and ninth droplets.
– Type G-III: Shrinking of group combustion
ﬂame starts from the closed end side of the test
section and extinction occurs near the ﬁrst
droplet.
Blue ﬂame was almost invisible after a spreading
ﬂame reached to the tenth droplet. Therefore,
the outer edge of luminous ﬂame was deﬁned as
