the case of G-I, a ﬂame spreads slowly, and burn-
out of droplets stars from the open end side dur-
ing ﬂame spreading (see Fig. 6). In the case of
G-III, which was observed at large gas equiva-
lence ratios and narrow droplet spacings, group
combustion ﬂame quickly expands to the direc-
tion normal to the ﬂame spreading, and the
shrinking starts from the closed end side toward
the open end side just after a spreading ﬂame
reaches to the tenth droplet. Almost all droplets
remain. In the case of G-II, the extinction location
of group combustion ﬂame shifts to the closed end
side as the gas equivalence ratio decreases or the
droplet spacing increases. It is supposed that the
shrinking of group combustion ﬂame from the
closed end side is caused by a shortage of oxygen.
In Fig. 11, the extinction type of group combus-
tion ﬂame was mapped as functions of the gas
equivalence ratio and the normalized droplet
spacing. Closed symbol indicates that all 10 drop-
lets burnt out when the group combustion ﬂame
self-extinguished. Solid line is the contour line of
