ratio was calculated from the amounts of fuel
vapor, liquid fuel (droplet) and air in the volume
occupied by one droplet ð¼ S  l2
whÞ. The extinc-
tion type strongly depends on the normalized
droplet spacing. As the decrease in the normalized
droplet spacing, the extinction type changes from
G-I to G-II, and then to G-III. The border of the
extinction types of G-II and G-III is the contour
line of /t = 2.0. The limit of the complete burnout
of droplets is determined by the total equivalence
ratio. In the region of /t 6 1, 10 droplets burn out
completely. It was found that the amount of oxy-
gen allocated to one fuel droplet determines the
extinction type of group combustion ﬂame. The
result can be seen as fundamental data on simpli-
ﬁed droplet cloud combustion which is initiated
by simultaneous ignition on the outer edge of
the droplet cloud. However, more detailed studies
are needed in order to consider relationship
between the group combustion mode which Chiu
et al. proposed [19] and the extinction type of
group combustion in this work.
4. Conclusions
Flame spreading along a 10-droplet-array at
microgravity was investigated to understand the
eﬀect of gas equivalence ratio of ambient gas on
ﬂame spreading behavior and ﬂame spread speed.
Conclusions follow:
(1) The gas equivalence ratio has little eﬀect on
the relationship between the ﬂame spread-
