the luminous ﬂame boundary; and rf is the maxi-
mum r value along the measured luminous ﬂame
boundary. Since the coﬂow velocity is relatively
small in this study, only the 40% CH4 ﬂame in
1 g is lifted and all other ﬂames are attached. Con-
sidering that the GRI mechanism will underpredict
the lift-oﬀheight of the highly diluted 40% CH4
ﬂames [9], and that the lift-oﬀheight can take a sig-
niﬁcant portion of the ﬂame length at high ﬂow-
rates, the modiﬁed ﬂame length eLf (obtained by
subtracting the lift-oﬀheight from Lf) is employed
in the current investigation to uncover better the
intrinsic physics of the problem.
Computed and measured values of eLf are illus-
trated in Fig. 4. These results are plotted as a
function of the mass ﬂow rate of the fuel mixture,
_mFM, based on the theory that laminar ﬂame
length should be proportional to the fuel mass
ﬂow rate [29]. Results in Fig. 4 reveal very good
agreement between computed and measured eLf,
where the diﬀerence between computed and mea-
sured eLf is always less than 15%. In 1 g, the aver-
age slopes of the computed and measured eLf
values with respect to _mFM on a log-log scale are
1.02 and 1.03; while in lg, the corresponding val-
ues are 1.12 and 1.07. These values reveal a strong
linear dependence, and the linearity shown in
Fig. 4 is in good agreement with model predic-
tions [29] and previous investigations of buoyant
[30,31] and nonbuoyant ﬂames [3,4,7]. It is also
observed in Fig. 4 that lg ﬂame lengths are longer
than their 1 g counterparts, with computed and
measured nonbuoyant ﬂames 22% and 28% longer,
on average, and the diﬀerence in eLf increases as
_mFM increases. This trend (i.e., decreasing eLf with
increasing gravitational acceleration) has been
reported previously in both microgravity ﬂames
[3,4] and centrifuge ﬂames [32].
Figure 5 shows the dependence of computed
and measured eLf on the mass ﬂow rate of just
the methane, _mCH4. We found that eLf is predomi-
nantly controlled by _mCH4 in both lg and 1 g, and
that the fuel stream dilution has negligible inﬂu-
ence on eLf. The overpredicted ﬂame length of
the 40% CH4 buoyant ﬂame at _mCH4  2 mg/s is
