Keywords: Flame spread; Microgravity; Extinction velocity; Radiative extinction; Space station 
1. Introduction 
When a solid combustible is ignited in the pres- 
ence of an opposing oxidizer flow, the resulting 
spread process is known as opposed-flow flame 
∗Corresponding author. 
E-mail address: prof.bhattacharjee@gmail.com (S. 
Bhattacharjee). 
spread. On the earth, the opposing flow can be 
generated by buoyancy even in a quiescent environ- 
ment. Opposed-flow flame spread over thermally 
thin fuels has been studied for decades [1] because 
of its importance as a fundamental topic in rela- 
tion to fire safety on earth or a space environment. 
The three regimes of flame spread are well known. 
The thermal regime is considered the dominant 
regime for which a closed-form solution for the 
flame spread rate was developed independently by 
