de Ris and Delichatsios [2,3] . In this regime, the 
opposing flow velocity V g is considered sufficiently 
low so that the residence time at the flame leading 
edge, the time spent by the oxidizer as it passes 
through the length L g ≈αg / V g at the flame leading 
edge, t res ≈L g / V g ≈αg /V 2 
g , is high enough for the 
combustion and pyrolysis chemistry to be consid- 
ered infinitely fast. At the same time, V g is consid- 
ered sufficiently high so that t res is small compared 
to the radiation time scales, time needed for radia- 
tive losses to be significant in comparison to con- 
duction from the flame to the virgin fuel, the domi- 
nant driving force of flame spread [4] . Conduction 
through the solid in the forward direction was also 
shown to be relatively unimportant for thermally 
thin fuels. A balance between the conduction from 
the gas and the energy required to heat up the fuel 
from its initial temperature T ∞ to a vaporization 
temperature T v can be shown to produce the same 
result (except for a factor of π/4) for flame spread 
rate as the exact analytical solution by de Ris 
and Delichatsios. The spread rate in the thermal 
regime is independent of flow velocity V g , inversely 
proportional to the fuel thickness, and directly pro- 
portional to a non-dimensional coefficient known 
as the de Ris coefficient F = ( T f −T v ) / ( T v −T ∞ ) , 
where T f is the adiabatic flame temperature, T v is 
the fuel vaporization temperature, and T ∞ is the 
ambient and virgin fuel temperature. 
As the opposed-flow velocity is increased, the 
residence time being inversely proportional to the 
square of velocity, finite-rate kinetics in the gas 
phase become important leading ultimately to 
blow-off extinction. This kinetic regime has been 
experimentally [5] and computationally [6] studied 
and the spread rate, normalized by the spread 
rate in the thermal regime, has been correlated to 
the Damkohler number, the ratio of the chemical 
and residence time. The downward spread in a 
quiescent 1-g environment can be considered a 
special case of opposed-flow flame spread with 
buoyancy induced flow providing the opposing 
flow velocity [7] . 
In a microgravity environment, the opposing 
flow can be very mild and even completely absent 
in the perfectly quiescent situation of zero gravity. 
Numerical [8–10] and experimental [11,12] studies 
have established the radiative regime (also known 
as the diffusive regime) in the mild opposed-flow 
environment in which the flame spread rate de- 
creases as the opposed-flow velocity is reduced 
leading to flame extinguishment [13] if the flow ve- 
locity is sufficiently low. This result is also depen- 
dent on other ambient conditions as steady spread 
over thin fuels in a perfectly quiescent environment 
has been established at oxygen levels higher than 
earth oxygen [14] . A radiation loss theory [15] was 
proposed with closed-form expressions for both 
thin and thick fuels in a low-velocity microgravity 
environment, which predicts radiative extinction at 
flow velocities below a critical velocity. Its existence 
and its dependence of fuel thickness and oxygen 
level have been experimentally verified recently [16] . 
In this work, recent experimental work for flame 
spread over thin sheets of PMMA performed in the 
International Space Station in which all the three 
regimes are covered for the first time for a given 
fuel in a single experiment is reported. Although 
data from the thermal and kinetic regimes are sepa- 
rately available for different fuels (PMMA, ashless 
filter paper, wood, etc.), the microgravity environ- 
ment is required to eliminate buoyancy effect and 
capture the radiative regime, what is unique in our 
experiments is that the same fuel is subjected to dif- 
ferent flow velocities with all other conditions re- 
maining unchanged. A computational model that 
has been validated by reproducing theoretical re- 
sults and experiments on downward configuration 
[17] is used to compare the spread rates and the evo- 
lution of flame shapes in the three regimes. Existing 
closed-form formulas in the thermal [3] and micro- 
gravity [15] regimes are compared to the data ob- 
tained from these experiments. 
