2. Experimental setup 
We take advantage of a combustion tunnel built 
for an earlier experiment (BASS: The Burning and 
Suppression of Solids) for this study known as 
BASS-II [18] . The flow tunnel in the International 
Space Station (ISS) is depicted in Fig. 1 . It is a 
combustion tunnel, a 76 mm square duct, where 
the PMMA fuel samples, 20 mm wide and about 
95 mm long are burned in an opposed-flow config- 
uration. A fan forces a flow of an oxygen-nitrogen 
mixture through flow straighteners from the right 
to the left and the sample is ignited at the left 
end. Depending on the flow velocity and oxygen 
concentration, a steady flame is established which 
spreads from the left end of the sample toward the 
right end. The oxygen level can be adjusted to 21% 
or below and the flow velocity can range from 0 
to 50 cm/s. Samples of two different thicknesses, 
100 μm and 200 μm, are used to explore the three 
regimes of flame spread, with a flame burning on 
both sides as depicted in Fig. 1. 
A hot wire anemometer monitors the flow ve- 
locity at the entrance of the test section. However, 
it is housed inside a 3 mm diameter solid enclosing, 
which introduces a time delay if the flow velocity is 
changed during the experiment. To mitigate this de- 
lay, the fan voltage which reflects real time flow ve- 
locity is used to shift the anemometer data as shown 
in Fig. 2 where the adjusted anemometer reading 
is obtained by normalizing the fan voltage and 
anemometer reading and shifting the anemometer 
reading to the left to match the fan time scale. It 
is also possible to obtain the flow velocity from the 
fan voltage, which is calibrated using the anemome- 
ter after every test. To change the flow velocity 
during a test, instructions were issued from the 
ground and the astronaut conducting the experi- 
ment would change the fan voltage until the de- 
sired flow velocity (as measured by the anemome- 
ter) is achieved. Readers can access the video of 
individual tests (at flame.sdsu.edu ) to see how the 
anemometer reading (displayed on the video along 
with fan voltage) changes during a particular test. 
Obviously, there are significant uncertainties in 
correlating a flow velocity with the instantaneous 
spread rate. Moreover, the flame leading edge could 
be as much as 10 cm away from the anemometer in- 
troducing a 2 s delay for a flow velocity of 5 cm/s. 
The boundary layer development could also affect 
the velocity as seen by the spreading flame. 
Experiments were conducted in the Micrograv- 
ity Science Glovebox of ISS by two crew members. 
Operation instructions were communicated from 
NASA Glenn Telescience Center in real time as the 
experiment progressed. Each test starts by creating 
a desired opposed-flow by adjusting the fan volt- 
age. After the flow stabilizes, the igniter is turned 
on until a visible flame is observed. The igniter is 
then turned off and the flame is photographed with 
a high resolution digital still camera with a frame 
rate of 1/s. A radiometer is used to confirm flame 
extinction when the visible flame goes out. In some 
of the tests, the flow velocity is changed during the 
spread to maximize the test matrix without hav- 
ing to burn additional samples. All data obtained 
from these experiments are available to the com- 
munity through a user-friendly web site flame.sdsu. 
edu . Test numbers mentioned in the result section 
are keyed to the first column of the Actual Data ta- 
ble in the BASS-II Experiments section of the web 
site. 
3. Data analysis 
