but the model has been validated by reproducing 
several analytical solutions including Emmon’s 
problem and de Ris solution for thin and thick 
fuels. 
The global-balance radiation model [20] is used 
for modeling gas and surface radiation. While the 
CFD solution is two-dimensional, the radiation 
model calculates the Planck mean absorption co- 
efficient, used in calculating the radiative source 
term, by equating the thin-gas expression for radia- 
tive losses from the entire computational domain to 
three-dimensional radiative loss from the domain, 
accurately calculated using a narrow band model. 
The radiation calculation accounts for losses due 
to the finite width of the sample. 
A set of computations is carried out by varying 
opposed-flow velocity and fuel thicknesses for the 
ambient conditions of 21/79 (percent by volume) 
mixture of oxygen and nitrogen at 1 atm pressure. 
The resulting spread rates are shown in Fig. 7 for 
the two fuel thicknesses studied. Radiative extinc- 
tion predicted by the computational results agrees 
remarkably well with theory and experimental re- 
sults. The sharp rise in spread rate with V g is more 
prominent in the experiment than in the theory 
( Fig. 6 ) and computation ( Fig. 7 ). The radiative 
regime is also consistent among experiment, theory, 
and computation. The flow velocity at which radia- 
tive extinction occurs is found to be independent of 
fuel thickness (in Eq. (2) , computations and exper- 
iments) and is the subject of a separate study [16] . 
The peak spread rates of 2.85 mm/s and 1.63 mm/s, 
however, are higher (by about a factor of 1.2) than 
the experimental and theoretical values. The criti- 
cal velocity at which blow-off extinction occurs is 
about 60 cm/s for 100 mm sample and 65 cm/s for 
200 mm sample, which are higher than the experi- 
mental value of 45 cm/s. A number of factors – the 
effect of boundary layer [19] , uncertainties in ex- 
perimental flow velocity, simplified kinetic model in 
the computational model – could be responsible for 
this discrepancy. 
