7. Conclusion 
The available flow tunnel in the International 
Space Station has provided a unique opportunity 
to study all three regimes of opposed-flow flame 
spread over thermally thin fuel. Two different thick- 
nesses of PMMA, 100 μm and 200 μm, are burned 
in an oxygen/nitrogen mixture with composition 
and pressure very close to atmospheric air with 
the opposing flow ranging from 80 cm/s to no flow 
at all. The spread rate obtained from frame-by- 
frame image analysis is compared to the radiative 
and thermal theories. The peak flame spread agrees 
well with the closed-form formula of de Ris and 
Delichatsios and the sharp rise in spread rate in the 
radiative regimes agree well with the closed-from 
formula of Bhattacharjee et al. The computational 
results also agree well with the experiments in the 
radiative and thermal regime and show very similar 
trends in the kinetic regime, leading to blow off ex- 
tinction at flow velocities close to what is observed 
in the experiments. The flame length predicted in 
the three regimes for two fuel thicknesses is also 
found to be consistent with the visible flame images 
from the experiments. 
