Fig. 2. Flame development of a 2.2 cm wide sample in 10 cm/s concurrent flow, 21% O 2 . (Test GMT96-T7) (a) Side-view 
flame tracking. (b) Side-view image sequence. Flow is from right to left. 
× 7.6 cm. The sample used is a composite cotton- 
fiberglass fabric blend (75% cotton and 25% fiber- 
glass by weight). The thickness of the sample is 
about 0.32 mm with an area density 18.2 mg/cm 2 . 
This fabric is custom-made for a previous project 
SIBAL (Solid Inflammability Boundary At Low- 
speed) [9] , hence it is here referred to as SIBAL 
fabric. One major advantage of this fuel sample is 
that the fiberglass mesh retains the sample struc- 
ture integrity after the combustible is consumed. It 
does not crack or produce curly ashes that are typi- 
cally seen in pure cellulose samples such as paper or 
cloth. This simplifies the experiments and facilitates 
comparisons with the theoretical model. The com- 
bustible content of SIBAL fabric (i.e. cotton cel- 
lulose) is a material used in astronaut clothing. In 
addition to [9] , experimental studies using SIBAL 
fabric can be found in [10,11] . SIBAL fabric is also 
one of the fuel candidates in the forthcoming large- 
scale fire tests in space due to the desirable proper- 
ties mentioned [12] . 
In the experiments, SIBAL fuel samples of two 
different widths, 1.2 cm and 2.2 cm, were used. Both 
have exposed length of 10 cm. The samples were 
mounted in stainless steel sample holders (shown 
in Fig. 1 b). The fuel sample and the sample holder 
were placed in the small flow duct. The tests were 
conducted in ambient oxygen percentage (molar 
fraction) between 16% to 21% and the forced flow 
velocity between 1 to 55 cm/s. During each test, the 
oxygen concentration was fixed while the flow speed 
could be varied. Ambient pressure was 1 atm. 
A hot wire was used to ignite the fuel sample 
leading edge so that the flame can spread in a con- 
current configuration. The flame growth process 
was recorded by two cameras. A side-view video 
camera provided edge-on images of the flat samples 
at standard video framing rates (29.97 frames per 
second). A top-view digital camera looked down 
the fuel surface and provided high resolution still 
front images at a variable rate of about one image 
every 1.2 s. When flow speed and oxygen concentra- 
tion were low, the flame became dim blue and was 
difficult to see in the side video camera. The top- 
view camera exposure times were long enough (1/8 
s–1/4 s) to capture high quality flame images even 
for dim flames right before quenching. These still 
images provide the time history of flame quenching 
and can be used to compare with transient model 
results. 
A total of 27 tests were carried out for SIBAL 
fabric samples in BASS-II, including 6 quenching 
tests, 3 non-ignited tests, and 1 blow-off test. In ad- 
dition, four BASS tests at 21% O 2 were selected. 
These 31 tests are listed in the table in Appendix . 
For cases with flow velocity variations, the starting 
and the final velocities are listed in the velocity col- 
umn. The velocity change rate varies from case to 
case, depending on the manual adjustment time by 
the various astronauts and the real time commu- 
nications between the astronaut and ground crew. 
Many of these tests were used to determine the 
low-speed quenching limits as a function of oxy- 
gen percentage. In some tests, the flame quenched 
quickly or was not ignited. These partially un- 
burned remnant samples were reused in later tests. 
These partially burned samples were ignited with 
a retractable igniter on one side of the sample at 
the fuel burnout position. These reused samples in 
general had a short unburned length and provided 
less quantitative data, thus are excluded from this 
paper. 
2.1. Flame development 
Figure 2 shows flame development of a typical 
example test (cataloged as GMT96-T7), a 2.2 cm 
sample in 10 cm/s flow and 21% O 2 . Figure 2 a 
shows side-view flame tracking of flame tip, 
flame base, and flame length (solid lines denote 
experimental data). Figure 2 b shows flame side- 
view video still images at different instances. In 
