Fig. 2 b, flow is from right to left. After the igniter 
was on, a strong flame was initiated on both sides 
of the solid fuel. The flame stabilized, grew in 
length, and moved downstream. At about 19 s, 
the flame reached a limiting constant length and 
a steady spread rate relative to the laboratory [13] . 
This steady spread state lasted for around 9 s. The 
flame images look almost identical during this 
stage, as seen at 20.17 s and 24.83 s in Fig. 2 b. 
Then the flame tip (or more precisely the flame 
preheating front) moved close to the end of the fuel 
sample (at 10 cm). The flame decayed in size until 
it completely burned out. The whole combustion 
process took about 40 s. A video clip of this test 
can be found in the electronic version of this paper 
as a supplementary material (video S1). 
2.2. Steady flame spread rate 
In cases where steady spread was reached, the 
spread rate was recorded. Steady flame spread rates 
in different flow speeds and oxygen percentages 
for both 2.2 cm and 1.2 cm samples are plotted 
in Fig. 3 (solid symbols). Note that in some cases 
flow speed was varied during the test. Steady flame 
spread here means that the flame reached a con- 
stant spread rate for more than 15 s. The results 
show that the flame spread rates are smaller for 
narrower samples, at lower flow velocities, and at 
lower oxygen percentages. This is consistent with 
our physical intuition. 
2.3. Flame quenching boundary 
Figure 4 shows a typical flame quenching se- 
quence in the BASS-II experiment. A 2.2 cm wide 
sample in 18.7% O 2 was first ignited at 10 cm/s flow. 
Flow speed was decreased to 2.2 cm/s incrementally 
after ignition. In this case, the flame was very dim 
in the side-view images, especially near quenching. 
Therefore, the top-view images and the top-view 
flame tracking were examined in this case. 
Figure 4 a shows flame tracking and flow speed 
vs. time. After ignition, the flame started to grow. 
As the flow speed was reduced, the flame length 
dropped accordingly. At 25 s, flow velocity was re- 
duced to 2.2 cm/s and held constant for the remain- 
der of the test. In Fig. 4 b, the top-view images show 
both the flame length and flame width continued to 
decrease. The flame seen from the top approached 
a circular-shaped blue flame. The size of the flame 
continued to shrink until extinction was reached. 
The post-burn photo in Fig. 4 b shows the narrow- 
ing of the burnt region as the flame shrunk toward 
extinction. A substantial part of the fuel was left 
unburnt along the two sides of the sample holder. 
This is a typical quenching extinction sequence. 
As the quenching limit is approached (in this case 
by turning down the flow velocity), the fuel pyrol- 
ysis rate and combustion heat release rate are re- 
duced due to the flame shrinkage, and radiation 
loss becomes a significant fraction of the heat re- 
lease. Furthermore, when the flame becomes small 
