enough, the flame surface to volume ratio increases 
and local conduction heat loss from the flame be- 
comes an additional mechanism contributing to 
extinction. Although this is a concurrent spread- 
ing flame, the quenching sequence shows similar 
characteristics in opposed flow [2] ; the flame be- 
comes circular, indicating the importance of three- 
dimensional heat and mass transfer near the limit. 
A video clip showing the above quenching sequence 
can be found in the electronic version of this pa- 
per as a supplementary material (Video S2). For 
wider samples, it is expected that a wide flame may 
first break up into multiple finger-shaped flamelets 
[14] and subsequently each flamelet will shrink to- 
ward extinction as the limit is approached. This is 
similar to what has been observed here. 
For this thin fabric fuel in concurrent flow, 
the burn time for a 10 cm long sample is about 
1–2 min. There is not enough time to fine-tune the 
flow speed. It is difficult to get to the desired near- 
quenching-limit state every time. Six quenching 
cases were achieved during BASS-II for the SIBAL 
sample, and four of them were for the 2.2 cm width 
samples. Along with the near-limit stable flame 
data, we were able to draw part of the quenching 
branch of the flammability boundary for 2.2 cm 
wide SIBAL fabric, as shown in Fig. 5 . Quenching 
flow velocities were found to be between 1 and 
5 cm/s with higher velocity at lower oxygen per- 
centage. The earlier two-dimensional theoretical 
models [1,9] predicted a U-shaped flammability 
boundary when plotted against the oxygen con- 
centration and the flow velocity. The trend of 
the quenching boundary obtained in this work 
supports this prediction. The bottom flat part of 
the U-shaped theoretical flammability boundary 
predicts the lowest oxygen percentage in which the 
combustion can happen. However, near this oxygen 
percentage limit, ignition of the sample is sporadic 
and there are several points in Fig. 5 marked 
as no-ignition. Here, no-ignition does not imply 
non-flammable. Since this part of the flammability 
boundary is expected to be rather flat (not sensitive 
to flow velocity), the flammability boundary cannot 
be determined accurately by the velocity-varying 
procedure in this work. A procedure with gradually 
decreasing oxygen is needed. In addition, because 
of the limited number of tests, the quenching 
limit for the 1.2 cm wide sample was not resolved. 
Nevertheless, it can be seen that the narrow sample 
has a smaller flammable domain, as expected. 
3. Computational results 
A previously developed three-dimensional 
model with self-adaptive moving grid [6] has 
demonstrated the capability to capture the flame 
shrinking process as in Fig. 4 . While the model 
provides high fidelity for the test sample config- 
uration, it assumes a simplified one-step overall 
finite-rate gas-phase combustion reaction, a one- 
step solid pyrolysis kinetics, and a non-spectral 
radiation model. In the present paper, the model is 
further developed to compare with the experiments 
quantitatively. A new two-step solid pyrolysis was 
assumed and the kinetics parameters were deter- 
mined experimentally. The pyrolysis model will be 
further explained in the following section. Other 
than solid pyrolysis, the numerical values of the 
parameters used in this work are the same as those 
in [6] , except for the fuel vapor Lewis number (Le F 
= 1.5), the reference solid latent heat at 300 K (X L 
= 300 J/g), and gas-phase reaction pre-exponential 
factor (B g = 10 13 cm 3 /g/s). 
3.1. Pyrolysis kinetics of SIBAL fabric 
Thermo-Gravimetric Analysis (TGA) was con- 
ducted using model Q500 TGA from TA Instru- 
ments to determine the rate of weight loss of 
SIBAL fabric. Several heating rates (5, 10, 15 and 
100 °C/min) in both nitrogen and air environment 
were performed. A representative TGA data at 
heating rate of 10 °C/min in nitrogen is given in 
Fig. 6 (solid lines). Figure 6 shows the percent- 
age of sample weight vs time. Note that there 
is a small weight drop for temperature less than 
100 °C in the TGA data. This is presumably from 
the vaporization of water moisture in the sample. A 
steep weight loss followed in the temperature range 
between 300–400 °C. In this range, approximately 
60% of the mass is lost. A secondary and slower 
weight loss occurs at elevated temperatures between 
400–675 °C. In this phase, approximately 15% of 
the mass is further lost. There is 25% of the mass 
leftover at temperatures greater than 700 °C which 
is the mass fraction of the inert fiberglass. 
The inset of Fig. 6 is the derived mass loss rate 
curve (non-dimensionalized by the sample initial 
mass). Clearly there are two peak reaction rates. 
