The magnitude of the first peak is much larger than 
the second. The first reaction is assumed to pro- 
duce the fuel volatiles along with an intermittent 
solid product (e.g. char). The gas-phase flame is 
thought to be mainly supported by the volatiles 
produced in the first reaction. During the flame 
spread process, the intermittent solid is either left 
unburnt behind the flame zone or further decom- 
posed into volatiles via the second reaction. The 
assumed two-step semi-global reactions are in the 
following form: 
Reaction 1: virgin fuel → fuel vapor + intermit- 
tent solid (char) 
Reaction 2: intermittent solid → fuel vapor 
For each step, the reaction rate is described 
by an Arrhenius equation with the kinetic param- 
eters to be determined. The proposed pyrolysis 
model was used in the GPYRO program [15] . The 
GPYRO program used the genetic algorithm to de- 
ter mine the inter mittent solid density and the ki- 
netics parameter set that best fit the experimental 
TGA test results. Note that although data of only 
one heating rate (10 °C/min) is shown in this pa- 
per, data in pure nitrogen for four different heat- 
ing rates were used in the optimization algorithm. 
The obtained optimized parameters are listed in 
Table 1 . 
The modeled mass and mass loss rates are plot- 
ted in Fig. 6 (dashed lines) to compare with the 
experimental data. Figure 6 shows that the pro- 
posed model captures the first reaction reasonably 
well and the second reaction less precisely. The sec- 
ond reaction is presumably char decomposition. It 
peaks at a higher solid temperature ( ∼575 °C) and 
its reaction rate is an order of magnitude smaller 
than that of the first reaction. During the flame de- 
velopment, the temperature of most parts of the 
solid is in the first pyrolysis reaction range. The im- 
portance of the second reaction in our modeling is 
minimal. Note that the water moisture vaporization 
is not modeled in this paper and the first pyrolysis 
reaction is basically of the first order ( n = 1.035). 
3.2. Comparison of model computations with 
experiments 
The numerical model was used to simulate the 
BASS burning geometry. Oxygen percentage, flow 
velocity, and temperature (300 K) are assigned at 
the duct inlet. Constant temperature (300 K), no- 
slip, and gradient-free for all species are assumed at 
the duct walls. All other boundary conditions and 
the initial conditions are the same as in our earlier 
work on upward burning [16] . The time history of 
the computed flame development is compared with 
the experimental data in Fig. 2 (dashed lines denote 
model results). The flame growth, steady spread, 
and decay processes are similar to the experimen- 
tal results. The spread rates of the steady spreading 
stage at different oxygen concentrations and flow 
speeds obtained by the model are compared with 
the experimental data on Fig. 3 (open symbols for 
the model results). The model tends to over-predict 
the spread rate for 1.2 cm wide samples and under- 
predict the spread rates for 2.2 cm wide samples, es- 
pecially near the low-velocity limits. Nevertheless, 
the overall model prediction of flame spread rates 
are reasonable, considering the sensitivity of the 
spread rate on the flow speed. 
To obtain the limiting velocities at the quench- 
ing boundary, the computation followed the same 
procedures as the experiments; the flow started 
with a high velocity and then was gradually turned 
down. The computational data points are plot- 
ted in the flammability map along with the ex- 
perimental data points in Fig. 5 . The quench- 
ing limiting velocities were reasonably predicted 
(with slight over-estimation). The quenching mech- 
anism was further examined by comparing steady- 
spreading flames in different flow velocities. Figure 
7 a shows sample surface profiles at two flow speeds, 
