4. Conclusions and future work 
A series of tests on concurrent flame growth and 
extinction over a thin flat cotton-fiberglass compos- 
ite fabric sample in low-speed purely forced flow 
was carried out in the International Space Station. 
The major conclusions are: 
(1) The flame can reach a limiting length with a 
steady spread rate in low-speed forced flow 
for narrow samples. 
(2) Low-speed flame quenching in concurrent 
flow over thin samples has been experimen- 
tally observed. As the limit is approached, 
the flame shrinks both in length and in width. 
Flame quenching is a three-dimensional 
process. 
(3) Since the quenching velocities are small (be- 
tween 1 and 5 cm/s), the quenching process 
at the limit is a slow process. Only in a long- 
duration microgravity environment can ac- 
curate limits be determined un-ambiguously. 
Determination of the quenching boundary 
also requires multiple tests with multiple 
samples. It is a costly operation in a space ex- 
periment. The BASS series offered the first 
and a rare opportunity to obtain such pre- 
cious data. 
(4) A three-dimensional flame spread model 
with pyrolysis kinetics from TGA data was 
used to compare the experimental results on 
flame development, spread rate, and quench- 
ing boundary. The overall comparisons are 
favorable. 
(5) The quenching mechanism was inspected 
using the numerical model. The increasing 
ratio of radiative loss rate to combustion 
heat release rate accounts for the extinction 
when the flame approaches the quenching 
limit. 
For a model intended to predict both flame 
spread rate and extinction limit, there are a num- 
ber of parameters that need to be empirically 
adjusted. These include the combustion kinetic 
rate constants (global reaction), some transport 
properties (e.g. fuel vapor Lewis number), and 
radiation parameters. Even with the solid pyrolysis 
kinetic constants determined by independent TGA 
tests, there is a question of whether pure nitrogen 
TGA data is the most appropriate for the com- 
bustion model. Considering all these complexities, 
the combined quantitative comparisons of flame 
development history, spread rate, and quenching 
extinction limits over a range of flow velocity 
and ambient oxygen percentage are reasonably 
