Fig. 2. Droplet suspension system. 
A droplet is generated by a thin glass needle of the 
droplet generator and suspended at the intersection 
point of two Al 2 O 3 /SiO 2 fibers (diameter: 7 μm; 
estimated heat conductivity: 2.6 W/(m • K) [13] ) of 
a droplet suspension system. Figure 2 shows the 
droplet suspension system, which is mounted on 
a movable arm of the droplet elevator. The two 
Al 2 O 3 /SiO 2 fibers are supported by two SiC fibers 
(diameter: 78 μm) and a stainless tube frame. The 
intersection point of the two Al 2 O 3 /SiO 2 fibers 
is located 3 mm above the intersection point of 
the two SiC fibers. A small glass bead ( < 0.11 mm 
in diameter) is fixed on the intersection point of 
the two Al 2 O 3 /SiO 2 fibers in order to prevent a 
droplet from falling down during the insertion of 
the suspended droplet into the electric furnace. 
A linear slider crank mechanism was applied to 
the droplet elevator to reduce the impact on the 
suspended droplet during the droplet-insertion 
process. The time required for the droplet-insertion 
process was 0.165 s. 
The high-pressure chamber and the electric fur- 
nace are equipped with three observation windows 
each. By switching the LED backlights (OptoSup- 
ply OSPW5161P, luminous intensity: 15 cd), the 
high-speed video camera can record backlit im- 
ages of a droplet at both the droplet-generation 
position and test position. Recording of droplet 
backlit images was started 0.3 s before the inser- 
tion process of the suspended droplet in order to 
measure the droplet diameter at the droplet gener- 
ation position, which was defined as the initial di- 
ameter d 0 . The CCD camera (SONY XC-555) and 
the lower thermocouple were employed to mon- 
itor the droplet generation process. The ambient 
temperature was measured with the upper ther- 
mocouple at a horizontal distance of 5 mm from 
the test position. Nitrogen was used as an am- 
bient gas, and n-hexadecane (critical temperature 
T cr : 722 K; critical pressure P cr : 1.41 MPa [32] ) was 
used as a fuel. The ambient temperature T a was 
Fig. 3. Droplet diameter measurement from backlit digi- 
tal image of an evaporating droplet. 
set at 773 K. The ambient pressure P a was var- 
ied from 1.0 to 3.0 MPa. The recorded backlit im- 
ages (Phantom Miro 3, image size: 800 pix × 600 
pix, intensity: 4096 gray-scale levels, speed: 200 fps, 
spatial resolution: 324 pix/mm) of an evaporating 
droplet were analyzed to obtain the temporal vari- 
ation in the droplet diameter with a self-made im- 
age analyzer program. The backlit digital image 
was divided into two-pixel lined sections, as shown 
in Fig. 3 . The intensity-profiles averaged in the x - 
direction were calculated for each section. The po- 
sitions of the droplet edge in each section were de- 
fined as the position of the pixel nearest to the sus- 
pender among the pixels having the intensity more 
than ( I A + 2 I B )/3, where I A and I B are the intensi- 
ties of the background and droplet, respectively. 
After separation of the suspender and droplet, the 
droplet edge was determined from the detected po- 
sitions. Ellipse approximation by the least mean 
square method was employed. The droplet volume 
was calculated on the assumption that the droplet 
was spheroidal. The droplet diameter d was defined 
as the diameter of the equivalent volume sphere. 
The detail of the droplet diameter measurement 
process was described in Ref. [33] . A droplet evap- 
orating in supercritical environments is surrounded 
by a very dense vapor. For such droplet images, the 
scan range of the light intensity level was narrowed 
around the light intensity level of the droplet edge. 
After analyzation, the detected droplet edge was 
superimposed on the original droplet image and 
was visually confirmed image by image. The initial 
droplet diameter was regulated within the range of 
0.36 to 0.44 mm. 
