Fig. 6. Backlit images of evaporating droplets in sub- and 
supercritical environments. 
Fig. 6 . Even in supercritical environments, the 
droplet edge was recognizable and maintained its 
spherical shape. This implies that the droplet tem- 
perature was below the critical temperature of the 
fuel. 
The squared-droplet-diameter histories normal- 
ized by the squared initial droplet diameter are 
shown in Fig. 7 . The straight dot-and-dash lines 
in the figure indicate the lines used for calcula- 
tion of the evaporation rate constant and the ini- 
tial heat-up period. The data obtained at 0.10 MPa 
[35] was added for better understanding of the 
ambient pressure dependence of droplet evapora- 
tion. In Ref. [34] , a droplet was suspended on a 
K-type thermocouple wire (diameter: 13 μm). Nor- 
mal gravity experiments confirmed that the differ- 
ence is negligible between evaporation rate con- 
stants obtained with the thermocouple suspender 
and with the Al 2 O 3 /SiO 2 suspender at 773 K and 
0.10 MPa. Under all conditions, the droplet diam- 
eter first became larger than the initial droplet di- 
ameter and then decreased. This increase in the 
droplet diameter observed at the beginning stage 
of droplet evaporation is due to slow evaporation 
and thermal expansion of the fuel droplet. The lack 
of data for a while after the end of droplet inser- 
tion ( t = 0 s) was due to the droplet vibration men- 
tioned above. The droplet vibration was excessive at 
an ambient pressure of 3.0 MPa due to low surface 
tension. At ambient pressures of 0.1 and 3.0 MPa, 
the squared non-dimensional droplet diameter de- 
creased almost linearly after the initial heat-up pe- 
riod. For an ambient pressure of 1.5 MPa, the 
period in which the squared non-dimensional 
droplet diameter almost linearly decreased was 
short. To check the error in the measurement of the 
droplet diameter, the standard deviation was cal- 
culated as a measure of the measurement repeata- 
bility. The minimum and maximum standard de- 
viations of the squared non-dimensional droplet 
diameter were 0.0043 at 1.0 MPa and 0.024 at 
2.0 MPa, respectively. The maximum error of the 
measured evaporation rate constant was estimated 
within ±14% at 2.0 MPa of ambient pressure. 
As a measure of the evaporation rate, the nor- 
malized droplet lifetime of 95 vol% evaporation 
and evaporation rate constant were measured from 
the normalized histories of the squared droplet di- 
ameter. The results are shown in Fig. 8 . The nor- 
malized droplet lifetime increased with the ambient 
pressure. At ambient pressures slightly above the 
critical pressure of the fuel, the normalized droplet 
lifetime increased quickly. The reason for this will 
be discussed in the final paragraph of this chapter. 
The evaporation rate constant in the supercriti- 
cal environments was successfully measured for the 
first time. The evaporation rate constant increased 
linearly with the ambient pressure, reached the 
maximum value at an ambient pressure of 1.5 MPa, 
and then decreased linearly. The calculation results 
of the evaporation rate constant by applying the 
measured droplet temperature [35] on the assump- 
tion of quasi-steady evaporation [21] agreed qual- 
itatively with the experimental results in subcriti- 
cal pressure conditions at 773 K. The increase in 
the evaporation rate constant at subcritical ambient 
pressure was attributed to the increase in the ther- 
mal conductivity of the fuel vapor/nitrogen mix- 
ture and the decreases in the liquid fuel density and 
evaporation latent heat. The decrease in the evap- 
oration rate constant at supercritical ambient pres- 
sures was possibly due to the increase in the iso- 
baric specific heat capacity of the fuel vapor near 
the critical point and the decrease in the tempera- 
ture difference between the droplet surface and am- 
bient gas. It was found that, in subcritical environ- 
ments, droplet evaporation becomes faster with an 
increase in the ambient pressure from the aspect of 
the evaporation rate constant, but the normalized 
droplet evaporation lifetime becomes longer. 
Figure 9 shows the normalized initial heat-up 
period and its ratio to the droplet lifetime as 
a function of the ambient pressure. The initial 
heat-up period increased linearly in the range of P a 
≤2.0 MPa, and then decreased. The increase in the 
ambient pressure leads to the increase in the boil- 
ing temperature. It is supposed that the increase in 
the initial heat-up period is caused by the increase 
in the sensible heat for heating a droplet from the 
initial droplet temperature to the evaporation tem- 
perature ( ∼wet bulb temperature). The increase 
in the evaporation rate also probably increases the 
initial heat-up period because a part of the heat 
transferring from surrounding gas to the droplet 
