The effect of ambient pressure on the initial 
heat-up period, the quasi-steady evaporation pe- 
riod, and the droplet evaporation lifetime is sum- 
marized in Table 1 . With an increase in the ambient 
pressure, the droplet evaporation lifetime increased 
at subcritical ambient pressures even though the 
evaporation rate constant increased. This is be- 
cause the increase in the initial heat-up period over- 
took the decrease in the quasi-steady evaporation 
period. The normalized droplet lifetime increased 
quickly at ambient pressures slightly above the crit- 
ical pressure of the fuel (see Fig. 8 ) because of the 
increases in both the initial heat-up period and the 
quasi-steady evaporation period. The initial heat- 
up period is found to determine the ambient pres- 
sure dependence of the droplet evaporation lifetime 
around the critical ambient pressure. In the case of 
n-heptane droplet evaporation, the droplet evapo- 
ration lifetime increased with the ambient pressure 
at subcritical ambient temperatures above 480 K 
[18] . The critical temperature of n-heptane ( T cr 
= 540 K [32] ) is lower than that of n-hexadecane. 
A small difference between the initial droplet tem- 
perature and the evaporation temperature leads to 
a small τ ih / τ 95 and a weak dependence of the ini- 
tial heat-up period on ambient pressure. The ambi- 
ent pressure dependence of the droplet evaporation 
lifetime of n-heptane at subcritical ambient tem- 
peratures is determined by the quasi-steady evap- 
oration period. 
4. Conclusions 
Microgravity experiments of droplet evapora- 
tion of n-hexadecane were carried out in sub- and 
supercritical environments. The ambient temper- 
ature was set at 773 K, which is above the criti- 
cal temperature of the fuel. The ambient pressure 
was varied from 1.0 to 3.0 MPa. Temporal varia- 
tions in the droplet diameter were measured from 
the recorded backlit images of the droplet. The 
evaporation lifetime and the evaporation rate con- 
stant were calculated from the histories of squared 
droplet diameter. The obtained understandings 
were summarized as follows: 
(1) Temporal variations in the droplet diameter 
were successfully obtained for droplet evapo- 
rations in supercritical environments. 
(2) The normalized droplet lifetime monotoni- 
cally increased with the ambient pressure. 
(3) The evaporation rate constant increased lin- 
early with the ambient pressure, reached the 
maximum value at an ambient pressure of 
1.5 MPa, which is slightly above the critical 
pressure, and then decreased linearly. 
(4) The initial heat-up period linearly increased 
with pressure in the range of P a ≤2.0 MPa, 
and then decreased. The ratio of the ini- 
tial heat-up period to the droplet lifetime in- 
creased with the ambient pressure, reached 
the maximum value of about 0.6 at an ambi- 
ent pressure of 2.0 MPa, and then decreased. 
(5) At subcritical ambient pressures, the droplet 
evaporation lifetime increased with the am- 
bient pressure even though the evaporation 
rate constant increased because the increase 
in the initial heat-up period overtook the de- 
crease in the quasi-steady evaporation pe- 
riod. In the case of fuels with a high crit- 
ical temperature, the initial heat-up period 
determines the ambient pressure dependence 
of the droplet evaporation lifetime in the en- 
vironments around the critical point of the 
fuel. 
