3.2. Flame structure 
In this work, we aim to understand the transi- 
tion from the opposed flame spread to fuel regression 
(mass-burning) in the progress of a flame on solid 
fuels . It is hypothesized that this transition occurs 
when the external flow velocity exceeds the local 
burning velocity of the laminar premixed flame, so 
that the flame moves downstream and re-stabilizes 
where the local flow velocity is smaller than the lo- 
cal burning velocity. 
This hypothesis assumes a triple flame in the 
flame leading edge, as illustrated in Fig. 3 (a), where 
the partial premixed flaming is propagating against 
the opposed flow stabilizing the “bulk” diffusion 
flame [26] . Such triple-flame structure may be indi- 
cated by the curved flame leading edge and its color 
change in Fig. 2 (d). In other words, when the op- 
posed flow velocity is larger than the local laminar 
burning velocity, the premixed flame in the leading 
edge can no longer be sustained directly against air- 
flow but move into the recirculation zone (i.e., cone) 
downstream. 
At the large opposed flow velocity, a counter- 
rotating vortex will be generated downstream above 
the fuel top surface, as illustrated in Fig. 3 (b). To- 
gether with the uprising pyrolysis gas from solid 
fuel, a stagnation surface and a thin diffusion flame 
sheet are formed right downstream the fuel top sur- 
face. Such structure is similar to the classical coun- 
terflow diffusion flame, but it is less uniform which 
has the fuel-rich and fuel-lean edges (right and left 
in Fig. 3 (b)). The fuel-rich edge is indicated by the 
yellow flame in Fig. 2 (b). Because the vortex is gen- 
erated from different directions, the yellow flame 
in the edge occurs in various locations (see Video 
2). Finally, a stable blue flame sheet is produced, 
probably because the regression in the middle pro- 
duces a larger recirculation zone, as illustrated in 
Fig. 3 (c). Numerical simulations with the Fire Dy- 
namics Simulator (FDS) 6.5 [27] demonstrate these 
flame behavior under the same environmental con- 
ditions (see Video 5). As a first approximation, the 
model assumes the infinitely fast flame chemistry 
and combustion occurring right after mixing. 
Further increasing the opposed flow velocity, 
the rotation of vortex becomes faster, and eventu- 
ally, the flame is blown off. A critical strain rate ( a ∗) 
may be defined for blow-off as: 
