FLEX-2 were conducted aboard the U.S. Experi- 
ment Module on the International Space Station 
(ISS) and showed a new finding that the d 2 -law in 
the droplet vaporization is maintained by the cool- 
flame appearing after the radiative extinction of the 
hot-flame [2-5] . 
Although droplet combustion has been stud- 
ied to grasp fundamental aspects of spray com- 
bustion in aero-engines, etc., such knowledge has 
not been well utilized to elucidate the spray com- 
bustion mechanism, and thus a large gap exists be- 
tween droplet combustion and spray combustion 
studies. In order to link the droplet combustion to 
the flame spread over randomly distributed droplet 
clouds through the flame spread between droplets 
and finally to the spray combustion, our research 
group performed the first combustion experiments 
aboard the Japanese Experiment Module, Kibo, on 
the ISS, titled “Elucidation of Flame Spread and 
Group Combustion Excitation Mechanism in Ran- 
domly Distributed Droplet Clouds (Group Com- 
bustion)” [6–9] . 
For the combustion of droplet clouds, Chiu’s 
group combustion theory is widely used [10] . How- 
ever, it cannot describe transient processes such 
as group-combustion excitation since the theory 
is based on the steady-state assumption. Mikami 
et al. [11,12] experimentally studied the combus- 
tion of premixed spray jets focusing on the exci- 
tation processes of a group flame. They observed 
phenomena in the leading flame area of the pre- 
mixed spray in a counter flow and showed that a 
group flame is excited through the transient pro- 
cesses of flame spread to unburned droplets and 
the interaction between burning droplets, and the 
flame is stabilized in the counter flow [12] . There- 
fore, it is important to understand the mechanism 
of group-combustion excitation through the flame 
spread over droplets. 
Since 2000, our research group has conducted 
microgravity experiments on the flame spread over 
droplets using the original technique to generate 
droplets on 14- μm SiC fibers with fuel-supply 
glass needles [13–16] . They have developed a new 
percolation model based on flame-spread char- 
acteristics in microgravity to describe the flame 
spread over the randomly distributed droplet cloud 
and studied the mechanism of group-combustion 
excitation [15 , 17] . In the “Group Combustion”
experiments performed in 2017 aboard Kibo/ISS, 
we successfully formed various droplet patterns 
in high-quality long-duration microgravity, such 
as randomly distributed large-scale droplet clouds 
[7 , 8] , droplet-cloud elements [9] , movable droplets, 
and droplet-cluster arrays, which cannot be formed 
in short-duration microgravity on earth and ob- 
served their flame spread behavior in detail. In 
the experiments of randomly distributed droplet 
clouds, the group-combustion-excitation limit was 
identified [8] . An unexpected anomalous behavior, 
i.e., a large-scale ignition of a droplet cluster 
Fig. 1. Back-illuminated image of a droplet cloud with 
the number of droplets M = 67 generated on a 30 × 30 
SiC-fiber lattice. The droplet number for each droplet is 
also shown. 
during the flame spread, was observed near the 
group-combustion-excitation limit [8] . This paper 
further reports this behavior and reports another 
anomalous behavior observed in the flame spread 
over randomly distributed droplet clouds, too. 
Furthermore, we report the results of the flame- 
spread experiment using a droplet-cluster array, 
which is a combination of two different droplet 
clusters, conducted to verify the hypotheses for the 
two types of anomalous burning behavior. 
2. Experimental method 
We used the Group Combustion Experiment 
Module (GCEM) [6–8] . n -Decane stored inside the 
syringe was supplied to the fuel-supply glass needle. 
The tip of the needle was moved by a three-axis tra- 
verse stage and thus to generate the droplet at the 
predetermined cross point of a 30 × 30 lattice with 
14- μm SiC fiber (Nippon Carbon, Hi-Nicalon) and 
4-mm fiber interval. Figure 1 shows the droplet 
cloud with 67 droplets used in this study. The 
droplet cloud with equal droplet diameters at igni- 
tion was realized by generating droplets one by one 
considering the droplet diameter regression due 
to the pre-vaporization during the droplet-cloud 
generation process. The vaporization-rate constant, 
which is required to predict the droplet diameter 
regression, was determined in the initial check-out 
phase for the functions of GCEM. The details are 
reported in Ref. [7] . A hot-wire igniter is placed 
at the center of the bottom side of the fiber lat- 
tice as shown in Fig. 1 and was used to start the 
flame spread. Since the maximum local equivalence 
