The effect of 14- μm SiC fiber on the flame spread 
over 1-mm droplets is also negligible as discussed 
in Refs. [7 , 8 , 13,19,20] . 
An LED backlight with a light-emitting area of 
30 × 146 mm was installed at the traverse stage. We 
used the back-illuminated still image to measure 
the initial droplet diameters taken by a digital video 
camera (Canon, EOS 5D Mark II) through a win- 
dow of the combustion chamber. The same camera 
was used to take movies of flame-spread behavior 
with a framing rate of 30 fps. Due to GCEM con- 
straints, the backlight position during the combus- 
tion experiments is limited only in the left area with 
30 × 146 mm of the fiber lattice shown in Fig. 1 . 
Therefore, the back-illumination was not used in 
the flame-spread experiments with randomly dis- 
tributed droplet clouds. In the flame-spread exper- 
iments with droplet-cluster arrays, two-cluster ar- 
rays with the same droplet arrangement burned at 
the same time in the fiber lattice: one cluster was 
back-illuminated and the other one was not. The 
droplet arrangements are shown in Section 3.2 . The 
exhaust gas was evacuated from the combustion 
chamber and then air was supplied from a cylin- 
der to set the ambient pressure of 0.1 MPa in the 
initial condition. Since GCEM was installed inside 
the Chamber for Combustion Experiment (CCE) 
on the cold plate of the Work Volume (WV) of 
the Multi-Purpose Small Payload Rack (MSPR) 
in Kibo, the released heat from the apparatus and 
combustion inside the combustion chamber was 
transferred to the cold plate, and thus the air tem- 
perature inside the combustion chamber was main- 
tained at 293 K in the initial condition. 
3. Results and discussion 
3.1. Flame-spread behavior over randomly 
distributed droplet clouds 
Two types of anomalous behavior, which had 
not been expected from parabolic flight experi- 
ments [21] and percolation simulations [17] , were 
observed in the present space experiments near 
the group-combustion-excitation limit. One is the 
large-scale ignition phenomenon of a droplet clus- 
ter reported in a previous paper [8] , and the other is 
the re-burning phenomenon by a slow flame prop- 
agation in a burned area. 
3.1.1. Large-scale ignition 
Figure 2 shows flame-spread behavior with a 
large-scale ignition for the mean droplet spac- 
ing ( S/d 0 ) m = 14.5, where the droplet pattern is 
shown in Fig. 1 and the averaged initial droplet di- 
ameter d 0 = 1.01 mm. The definition of the mean 
droplet spacing, ( S/d 0 ) m , is described in Ref. [8] . 
The same phenomenon occurred in the case for 
( S/d 0 ) m = 15.2, where the droplet pattern is the 
same as in Fig. 2 but d 0 is different from the case 
in Fig. 2 . In such flame spreads near the group- 
combustion-excitation limit, the flame spreads 
along complicated paths [8] . In Fig. 2 a, the flame 
spread starts from the center part of the lower side 
of the fiber lattice, and a group flame forms around 
Droplets 1–6, 9–12, 14 and 19–20 at t = 2.60 s. 
The droplet number for each droplet is shown 
in Fig. 1 The left part of the group flame does 
not spread to the lower left area, where Droplets 
7–8, 16, 18, 22 and 25 exist, and disappears at 
t = 2.97 s. The flame spreads to the center area with 
Droplet 33 via Droplets 26, 30, 29 and 34 from 
t = 2.97 s until t = 4.63 s. Shortly after the ignition 
of Droplet 33 at t = 4.63 s, a large-scale ignition oc- 
curs at t = 4.87–4.90 s. Such a curved long flame- 
spread path causes a relatively long waiting time for 
ignition. 
Droplets 7–8, 16, 18, 22, and 24–25 exist in- 
side the large-scale-ignition flame at t = 4.90 s. The 
flame radius measured from the center of each 
droplet is about 12 mm for Droplet 25, about 
24 mm for Droplet 18 and about 18 mm for Droplet 
8. At t = 8.37 s, Droplets 45 and 49 are ignited with 
a blue flame with a radius of about 14 mm mea- 
sured from Droplet 45, which is also much greater 
than the radius of the initial flame less than 4 mm 
without a long waiting time for ignition. Thus, 
this can also be regarded as a type of large-scale 
ignition. 
Therefore, we hypothesize that the droplet clus- 
ter showing the large-scale ignition existed initially 
outside the flame-spread limit but vaporized by 
heating from a group flame to form a flammable 
mixture with a relatively long waiting time for ig- 
nition, and the ignition of the flammable mixture 
by a flame coming from another route caused the 
large-scale ignition. According to Fig. 2 , the wait- 
ing time for large-scale ignition is about 4 s, which is 
much longer than the mean flame-spread time be- 
tween 1-mm droplets near the group-combustion- 
excitation limit, which is about 1 s [8] . Droplets 
are heated mainly by thermal conduction. The ef- 
fect of thermal expansion is not significant for 
droplet clouds with relatively large ( S/d 0 ) m near the 
group-combustion-excitation limit. Nomura et al. 
[22] measured the flow caused by the flame spread 
over n -heptane droplets and report that the ther- 
mal expansion induced flow is significant for the 
distance from the ignited droplet x / d 0 < 12 within a 
few tens ms after droplet ignition, which is much 
shorter than the flame-spread time. Since n -decane 
is less volatile than n -heptane, its effect is less 
significant. 
Figure 2 b shows enlarged photographs of Ar- 
eas A and B. In Area A, a blue luminosity ex- 
ists outside the blue group flame and lasts from 
t = 2.40 s until t = 2.67 s. Such a blue luminosity 
is also observed before the flame spread near the 
