The blue luminosity suggests that the hot-flame re- 
action of the lean mixture coming from the un- 
burned droplet is supported by heating from the 
diffusion flame. Mikami et al. [16] suggests such a 
non-propagating reaction occurs between the dif- 
fusion flame and the unburned droplet based on 
the temperature distribution measured by Thin Fil- 
ament Pyrometry (TFP). Such a reaction also en- 
hances pre-vaporization leading to the large-scale 
ignition. As suggested later in Section 3.2 , we ex- 
pect that the cool-flame would form before the ap- 
pearance of this blue emission and/or the large- 
scale ignition although the cool-flame emission is 
too low to detect by a digital video camera. 
3.1.2. Re -burning by a slow-flame propagation 
Figure 3 a shows the re-burning phenomenon 
by a slow-flame propagation in a burned area for 
( S/d 0 ) m = 13.3. The droplet pattern is the same 
as in Fig. 1 , but d 0 = 1.10 mm. The same phe- 
nomenon occurred in the case for ( S/d 0 ) m = 12.1 
as shown in Fig. 3 b but did not occur in the case 
for ( S/d 0 ) m = 14.5 as shown in Fig. 2 a, where the 
