this anomalous behavior we hypothesize that a part 
of the group flame extinguished due to the excessive 
radiative heat loss, and then the unburned mixture 
diluted by the combustion products was ignited by 
a flame from another route. 
3.2. Flame-spread behavior over droplet-cluster 
arrays 
In order to verify the hypotheses for the newly 
observed two types of anomalous burning behav- 
ior, additional experiments were conducted aboard 
Kibo/ISS using droplet-cluster arrays, which is a 
combination of droplet clusters. 
Figure 4 shows the flame spread behavior of 
droplet-cluster arrays to simulate the anomalous 
burning behavior shown in Section 3.1 . It shows 
back-illuminated photographs for some combus- 
tion phases, too. The flammable mixture leading 
to the large-scale ignition is simulated by the mix- 
ture of air and the pre-vaporized fuel of Cluster 
L, consisting of fiv e 1-mm droplets, heated by the 
burning of Cluster H, which consists of two 2-mm 
droplets. The droplet spacing between Droplets L1 
and H2 is 28 mm. The burned area with the diluted 
flammable mixture leading to the re-burning by a 
slow flame propagation is simulated by the area of 
Cluster H, which is expected to continue vaporiza- 
tion after the radiative extinction of the flame sur- 
rounding Cluster H. The burning of Cluster L is 
expected to play the role of re-ignition of the mix- 
ture in the burned area of Cluster H. Cluster L 
was ignited by the flame spreading from the up- 
per direction with a certain delay time, as shown in 
Fig. 4 . 
