doi:10.1016/j.proci.2022.07.053,doi,Appearance of cool flame in flame spread over fuel droplets in microgravity,2,1,"2450 M. Mikami, K. Matsumoto, Y. Chikami et al. / Proceedings of the Combustion Institute 39 (2023) 2449–2459 
ignition types depending on the ambient temper- 
ature and pressure: single stage ignition with cool 
flame, that with hot flame, and two-stage ignition 
with a transition from cool flame to hot flame. In 
the Flame-Extinguishment Experiments (FLEX) 
conducted aboard the International Space Station 
(ISS), a transition from hot flame to cool flame 
was observed after the radiative extinction of hot 
flame surrounding relatively large droplets [5–10] . 
Since the cool flame supports quasi-steady vapor- 
ization, the squared droplet diameter decreases lin- 
early over time until the cool-flame extinction oc- 
curs. 
Recently, Mikami et al. [15] suggested the ap- 
pearance of cool flame in flame spread over a 
randomly distributed droplet cloud based on find- 
ings in the “Group Combustion” experiments con- 
ducted aboard the Japanese Experiment Module 
“Kibo” on the ISS. The purpose of “Group Com- 
bustion” is to bridge the gap between droplet 
combustion and spray combustion through flame 
spread over droplet arrays, droplet-cloud elements, 
and randomly distributed droplet clouds [11–15] . 
The flame spread over droplets randomly dis- 
tributed on the two-dimensional SiC-fiber lattice 
was performed based on the percolation model to 
describe the group-combustion excitation consider- 
ing findings from microgravity experiments on the 
flame spread of droplet arrays [16–20] . The results 
show that anomalous phenomena, such as large- 
scale ignition and reburning by slow flame propa- 
gation, were newly observed [15] . The examination 
of the vaporization-rate constant in flame spread 
over the droplet-cluster array that well simulated 
anomalous phenomena suggests that the cool flame 
appears before those phenomena [15] . 
This study further investigated the appearance 
of cool flame during the flame spread over droplets 
using simpler patterns of droplets in microgravity 
aboard the ISS and at a drop-experiment facility. 
Furthermore, we used multiple methods to detect 
the cool flame during flame spread. 
2. Experimental apparatus and procedure 
Fig. 1 shows the droplet patterns used to study 
the cool-flame appearance during flame spread. 
The burning of Cluster H, which consists of two or 
three interactive droplets, heats Droplet L to form 
a flammable mixture and a cool flame around it. 
The droplet spacing between interactive droplets 
normalized by the initial droplet diameter, d 0 , is 4. 
The normalized droplet spacing between Droplets 
A and L, S AL / d 0 , ranges from 16 to 20. Three dif- 
ferent droplet patterns in Cluster H were employed 
for different heat input to Droplet L. The burning 
of Cluster H heats Droplet L least in Pattern A and 
most in Pattern C for the same S AL / d 0 . 
The microgravity experiments were conducted 
aboard “Kibo” on the ISS and at the drop- 
experiment facility in Yamaguchi University. As 
reported by previous papers [11–15] , we used the 
Group Combustion Experiment Module (GCEM) 
aboard the ISS. The droplets were one by one gen- 
erated at intersections of a two-dimensional square 
lattice with 14- μm SiC fibers (Nippon Carbon, Hi- 
Nicalon) at fiber intervals of 4 mm. The initial 
droplet size, d 0 , was approximately 1 mm in diame- 
ter. n -Decane was supplied through a fine glass nee- 
dle, the position of which was controlled by a three- 
axis traverse stage. A droplet array at droplet spac- 
ing of 12 mm was used to ignite Cluster H. A hot- 
wire igniter was used to start the flame spread. The 
flame spread occurred in air inside the combustion 
chamber. The air pressure and temperature inside 
the combustion chamber were respectively 0.1 MPa 
and 293 K in the initial condition. A digital video 
camera (Canon, EOS 5D Mark II) was used to ob- 
serve the flame spread behavior through the glass 
window of the chamber. An LED backlight was 
used to shoot the droplet image. The effect of 14- 
μm SiC fiber on the flame spread over droplets 
is negligible as discussed in Refs. [11,12,16,21,22] . 
The droplets were also tethered at intersections of 
SiC fibers in the experiments conducted at Yam- 
aguchi University. Unlike the experiments aboard 
the ISS, however, the initial droplet diameter, d 0 , 
was approximately 0.5 mm, and the hot-wire igniter 
directly ignited Cluster H due to the relatively short 
microgravity duration of 0.9 s. The droplet diame- 
ter during vaporization was measured based on the 
method described by Nomura et al. [23] . Within 
a certain range of d 0 , including d 0 = 1 mm and 
0.5 mm used in this study, the flame spread is con- 
trolled by diffusion and thermal conduction and 
therefore depends only on S AL / d 0 . If d 0 is relatively 
large, radiative heat loss is significant, and if d 0 is 
relatively small, the droplet burning is chemically 
controlled. 
The gas temperature can be measured based 
on the SiC fiber temperature measured through 
the thin filament pyrometry (TFP). Since the digi- 
tal video camera in GCEM is a visible-light cam- 
era, it can only detect temperatures higher than 
1100 K [13–14] . In order to detect the cool- 
flame temperature, which is lower than 800 K, 
we used a near-infrared camera (Allied Vision, 
Goldeye G033 SWIR) covering the wavelength 
range of 0.9 to 1.7 μm in the experiments con- 
ducted at Yamaguchi University. Furthermore, we 
used a mid-wave infrared camera (New Infrared 
Technologies, TACHYON 16k Plus) covering the 
wavelength range of 1 to 5 μm in the experi- 
ments in Yamaguchi University, so that it would 
detect infrared radiation from combustion prod- 
ucts, such as water vapor and carbon dioxide, 
which is weaker with the cool flame than with the 
hot flame. We detected the cool-flame appearance 
using the aforementioned camera similar to the 
cool-flame detection using radiometers in FLEX 
[8] ."
