Williams and Nayagam [9] theoretically showed 
that the cool flame surrounding the droplet also 
shows a diffusive extinction at a specific droplet 
diameter. Nayagam et al. [7,10] reported that 
the cool-flame extinction diameter for n -decane 
droplets is about 1 mm in air at atmospheric pres- 
sure. Figs. 8 and 10 show, however, that the droplet 
of 1 mm in initial diameter completely vaporizes 
with a cool flame. Moriue [24] reported that when 
a n -decane droplet of 0.7 mm in initial diameter 
is inserted into high-temperature air at 580–710 K 
at atmospheric pressure, single stage ignition with 
cool flame occurs. Therefore, the cool flame appear- 
ing around the droplet of 1 mm in initial diameter 
near the hot-flame-spread limit is not self-sustained 
unlike the cool flame surrounding relatively large 
droplets at room temperature but is supported by 
heat input from the hot flame and reduced heat loss 
from the cool flame due to the short-term existence 
of high-temperature region after the end of burn- 
ing of hot flame. 
3.2. Detection of cool flame around a droplet 
existing outside the flame-spread limit 
In Section 3.1 , an indirect method based on the 
vaporization-rate constant suggests that the cool 
flame appears around the droplet existing outside 
the hot-flame-spread limit. Although the cool flame 
is often detected through measurement of CH 2 O 
emission using an image intensifier, it is difficult to 
apply this method to the flame spread over droplets 
in which both the hot flame and cool flame ap- 
pear close to each other in time and space. This sec- 
tion detects the cool flame during the flame spread 
through other methods. 
The droplets are tethered by fine SiC fibers in 
this experiment. Since the response time of the 14- 
μm SiC fiber to the ambient temperature change is 
about 1 ms, the temperature of the SiC fiber rapidly 
follows the gas temperature change [19] . The first 
method detects the temperature rise due to the cool- 
flame appearance by measuring the fiber temper- 
ature. In order to cover the temperature range of 
cool flame, we used a near-infrared camera for the 
thin-filament pyrometry (TFP) and to detect the 
cool-flame appearance. 
Fig. 11 shows the flame-spread behavior for 
S AL / d 0 = 19 in Pattern B observed by the near- 
infrared camera. Several fibers are placed near 
Droplet L to detect the cool flame. Since this ex- 
periment was conducted in a drop-experiment fa- 
cility with 0.9-s microgravity duration, the initial 
droplet diameter is 0.5 mm and Cluster H was di- 
rectly ignited by a hot-wire igniter. The heating time 
was set so short that the ignition disturbance was 
as small as possible. Therefore, the heat transfer to 
Droplet L was not significantly affected by the hot- 
wire ignition but mainly depends on S AL / d 0 for the 
same droplet pattern. The image slightly doubles 
due to the effect of a dichroic mirror during the hot 
flame appearance. The luminance of fibers around 
Droplet L varies associated with an expansion and 
regression of a high-temperature field around the 
hot flame of Cluster H. Before the end of Cluster 
H burning, however, another weak luminance is ob- 
served to surround Droplet L and continues even 
after the end of burning of Cluster H. The fiber 
temperature corresponding to the weak luminance"
