The second method detects the cool flame 
during the flame spread by measuring the spa- 
tial distribution of infrared radiation from the 
combustion products using a mid-wave-infrared 
camera. The FLEX experiments aboard the ISS 
used radiometers to detect the radiation from the 
combustion products, especially from water va- 
por [8] . The transition from the hot flame to 
cool flame for relatively large droplets is cap- 
tured through a sudden decrease in the radiometer 
output. 
Fig. 12 shows the flame-spread behavior for 
S AL / d 0 = 19 in Pattern B observed by the mid-wave- 
infrared camera. The initial droplet diameter is 
0.5 mm and Cluster H was directly ignited by a 
hot-wire igniter. Since high-temperature soot inside 
the flame and fibers also emit infrared, the image 
includes the radiation not only from the combus- 
tion products but also from soot and fibers. The lu- 
minance around Droplet L at t = 0.53 s is due to 
the radiation from fibers, which is observed in the 
case without Droplet L, too. On the other hand, in 
the case with Droplet L, even at t = 0.71 s while 
the fiber radiation is no longer observed, a weak 
luminance is observed around Droplet L. This is 
due to the cool-flame appearance. It is interest- 
ing that the center of the weak luminance slightly 
shifts to Cluster H. This findings also suggests that 
the cool flame is supported by heat input from 
burning of Cluster H and reduced heat loss from 
the cool flame due to the short-term existence of 
high-temperature region after the end of burning 
Cluster H. 
Conclusions 
Phenomena appearing around a droplet existing 
outside the flame-spread limit were experimentally 
studied in microgravity. Different methods were 
used to detect the cool-flame appearance during the 
flame spread over droplets. The main findings are as 
follows: 
(1) Even if a droplet exists outside the hot-flame- 
spread limit, the heated droplet vaporizes 
and a flammable mixture layer forms around 
it, leading to the extension of the hot-flame- 
spread limit from another route. 
(2) A cool flame appears around the droplet ex- 
isting outside the hot-flame-spread limit and 
the vaporization completes with cool flame 
if the heat input from the hot flame is suf- 
ficiently large. This type of flame spread is 
called hot-to-cool flame spread. The defini- 
tion of flame spread should be extended con- 
sidering the cool flame. 
(3) Temperature measurement through TFP us- 
ing a near-infrared camera and combustion 
product distribution measurement using a 
mid-wave-infrared camera are useful to de- 
tect the cool-flame appearance during flame 
spread over droplets."
