be explained by the limits due to blow-off and flame extinction caused 
by radiation. If the limiting curve for a particular material can be 
quantitatively predicted, it becomes possible to discuss the fire safety of 
that material in any given environment. 
Currently, in the fire safety evaluation program led by JAXA, the 
FLARE project, one of the objectives is to quantitatively predict the 
flammability limits of solid materials from experimental results obtained 
through ground-based testing. Takahashi et al. proposed a method for 
quantitatively determining the blow-off branch by conducting down­
ward flame spread experiments with high-velocity airflow, which has 
been standardized as International Standard ISO 4589–4 in the FLARE 
project [19]. Therefore, it is believed that the blow-off branch can be 
predicted with relatively high accuracy using only ground tests. On the 
other hand, for the radiative extinction branch that appears under low 
flow velocity conditions, estimations were made based on the material’s 
thermal decomposition tests and the extinguishing conditions from past 
short-duration drop tower experiments, therefore, results from 
long-duration microgravity experiments have been awaited. 
In the FLARE project, long-duration combustion experiments using 
filter paper have been conducted in the Solid Combustion Experiment 
Module (SCEM) installed inside the ISS/Kibo module since May 2022 [1, 
18]. This article involved comparing the predicted flammability limits, 
derived from ground test results, with the outcomes of combustion ex­
periments conducted in orbit. We improved our previous model to more 
accurately predict the limiting curves under opposed flow conditions. 
This article details these advancements and findings. 
2. Prediction of flammability limits for materials under opposed 
flow conditions 
