Here, xd represents the characteristic length in the flame spread 
phenomena, and according to Ref. [10], xd is set at 0.04 m. The arbi­
trariness of this characteristic length is absorbed by the experimental 
pre-exponential factor A* in Eq. (5). Conversely, in the extinction 
branch, due to the slower ambient flow velocity during extinction and 
the increased stand-off distance of the flame, the extinction limit was 
estimated under the assumption of plug flow, maintaining the relative 
velocity to the flame as the bulk velocity Vg. 
To determine the flammability limits of solid samples from ground 
test results, it is necessary to know the material’s Oxygen Index OI and 
High-velocity Oxygen Index HOI, as well as its thermal decomposition 
temperature Tv and flame temperature Tf. Tv was calculated using the 
formula (T10+T50)/2, where T10 and T50 represent the temperatures at 
which 10 % and 50 % mass loss are observed, respectively. These tem­
peratures were measured through thermo-gravimetric analysis-differ­
ential thermal analysis (TG-DTA) tests. Tf is determined as the adiabatic 
flame temperature, derived from the material’s composition, its heat of 
combustion, and the surrounding atmospheric conditions. Table 1 pre­
sents the values obtained from ground experiments on filter paper, and 
Fig. 2 shows the predicted flammability limits of filter paper based on 
these values. In this analysis, the limiting curve was calculated by con­
necting points where η from Eq. (2) equaled zero. The radiative 
extinction branch was calculated using Eq. (4), and B2 was set to 4.20, 
based on previous drop tower experiments using PMMA as the sample 
material. 
3. On-orbit experimental setup 
The on-orbit experiments were executed utilizing the Solid Com­
bustion Experiment Module (SCEM), which was installed on the ISS/ 
Kibo Multi-purpose Small Payload Rack (MSPR) [21]. The SCEM, a 
compact circulating wind tunnel, facilitates various combustion exper­
iments, including wire combustion and flat sample combustion, by the 
interchange of a component known as an “experiment insert” (refer to 
Fig. 3). Specifically, Insert 2 is designed for flat sample combustion ex­
periments. In this setup, a solid sample, attached to a sample card, is 
centrally positioned in the test section, which has a cross-section 
measuring 14 cm × 14 cm. For optical measurements, an array of 
cameras is employed: three video cameras capturing at 22 to 26 frames 
per second (fps) from vertical, horizontal, and oblique angles relative to 
the sample; a high-speed camera recording at 200 fps from a 45-degree 
oblique angle; and an infrared camera operating at 30 fps (wavelength 
range: 8–13 μm) positioned vertically and viewing through a germanium 
window. Additionally, the apparatus includes a fan capable of varying 
the flow velocity around the sample, ranging from quiescent conditions 
to a maximum of 25 cm/s. 
In Fig. 4, it is shown that the sample card accommodates samples 
with dimensions up to 40 mm in width and 130 mm in length. The first 
specimen for the on-orbit combustion experiment utilized filter paper 
(Advantec No. 4A), characterized by dimensions and properties as fol­
lows: length of 130 mm, widths of either 40 mm or 20 mm, a thickness of 
0.12 mm, and an area density of 96 g/m2. In each experiment, a single 
card is extracted from the sample cartridge, transported to the test 
section’s center, and secured using electromagnets. Upon completion of 
an experiment, the card is similarly moved to the collection cartridge for 
storage. Sample ignition is facilitated by an electrically heated Kanthal 
wire (40 mm wide), activated at T + 5 s for a duration of 10 s, with video 
recording commencing from T + 0 s. By strategically positioning the 
ignition site either downstream or upstream, experiments can be con­
ducted under opposed flow or concurrent flow conditions, respectively. 
The ignition wire is designed for retraction post-ignition if needed. 
Within the SCEM, both the oxygen concentration and pressure are 
adjustable, ranging from 0 to 45 % and 0 to 101.3 kPa, respectively. 
Pressure gauges, oxygen concentration sensors, and thermocouples are 
installed within the SCEM for internal monitoring. The test section is 
equipped with green LED lighting, operational at various illuminance 
levels and time intervals. During the on-orbit experiments, the ambient 
flow velocity was either maintained constant or altered in a stepwise 
fashion. It is observed that the oxygen concentration within the SCEM 
diminishes as the sample combusts; for instance, burning a 130 mm × 40 
mm filter paper sample results in approximately a 1 % reduction in 
oxygen concentration. 
4. Results of on-orbit experiments and discussion 
Fig. 5 presents the results of flame spread over filter paper in the 
SCEM, accompanied by flammability limiting curves derived from the 
scale analysis. The gray curve denotes the initial prediction, which was 
calculated using Eqs. (4) and (5), as depicted in Fig. 2. The conditions for 
the on-orbit experiments were strategically chosen to be near the 
boundary of this predicted limiting curve. In total, 19 experiments were 
conducted under conditions of either opposed flow or quiescence. The 
red symbols indicate continuous flame spread under those conditions, 
