At the boundary between the radiation extinction region and the 
blow-off extinction region, an intriguing phenomenon was observed, 
reflecting the influences of both areas. In Run #26, the combustion 
experiment was initiated at an initial oxygen concentration of 15.5 %, 
approximately 1 % lower than its OI of 16.58 %. In this experiment, the 
initial opposed flow velocity was set at 10 cm/s, and was increased by 2 
cm/s every 20 s, reaching 18 cm/s at T + 80 s. The oxygen concentration 
eventually dropped to 15.2 % during the experiment. The infrared and 
top-view visible images shown in Fig. 7 reveal that the flame tempo­
rarily adopted a three-dimensional shape around T + 70 s but primarily 
spread in a two-dimensional manner, eventually extinguishing at T +
114 s. This suggests that Run #26 was conducted across the flamma­
bility limit of the filter paper, and although the flame temporarily 
adopted a three-dimensional shape, thereby experiencing the effects of 
radiative heat loss, it ultimately extinguished in a two-dimensional 
form. This implies that extinction occurred upon reaching the blow-off 
limit. For filter paper, it is experimentally confirmed that, as pre­
dicted, the limiting oxygen concentration becomes lower than its OI 
under conditions with an opposed flow velocity around 10 cm/s. 
Additionally, it is apparent that the blow-off branch, as described by Eq. 
(5) and derived from OI and HOI, accurately and quantitatively repli­
cates the observed phenomena. 
On the other hand, there were some observed discrepancies between 
the predicted and actual experimental results in the radiative extinction 
branch. It was initially expected that the minimum limiting oxygen 
concentration would be 14.0 % at an opposed flow velocity of 8 cm/s, 
and the flammability limit was expected to expand significantly at ve­
locities under 10 cm/s. Contrary to these predictions, in Run #01 (O2: 
14.3 %, Vg: 6 cm/s), the flame extinguished immediately post-ignition. 
In Run #02 (O2: 16.5 %, Vg: 6 cm/s), the flame transitioned to a 
three-dimensional form mid-spread. Moreover, in Run #30 (O2: 15.5 %, 
Vg: 8 cm/s), a three-dimensional flame was sustained until T + 100 s 
before extinguishing, suggesting a more substantial effect of radiative 
heat loss than initially projected (see Fig. 8). In Run#20 (O2: 15.5 %), 
the opposed flow velocity was changed from 6 cm/s to 5 cm/s at T + 20 
s. As the flow velocity was reduced, the flame rapidly transformed into a 
small spherical shape and extinguished. 
Based on these results, the effect of decreasing the opposed flow 
velocity Vg seems to be overly emphasized in the radiative loss param­
eter as defined by Eq. (4). In this equation, under the assumption of plug 
flow, the opposed flow velocity relative to the flame is set as the bulk 
velocity Vg. However, flow velocity measurements in the SCEM con­
ducted in orbit reveal that, even in low flow conditions like Vg = 4.5 cm/ 
s, the velocity at the flame height (~ 5 mm) is reduced below the bulk 
velocity, influenced by the boundary layer. Fig. 9 shows the result ob­
tained from smoke tests on the vertical velocity distribution inside the 
SCEM under a low flow condition [18], alongside photographs of flames 
under varying flow velocities. From these results, it is clear that even in 
low flow velocities, the flame front is embedded within the boundary 
layer. Therefore, it is essential to accurately estimate the flow velocity 
confronting the flame front. 
