The results of the calculations using Eqs. (5) and (8) for the limit 
curve of filter paper are shown in Fig. 2 with a blue curve. Table 2 
presents a comparison of how Rrad and Da values vary with opposed flow 
velocity Vg in the two models. In the updated model, it is observed that 
radiative and kinetic effects have comparable influences at an opposed 
flow velocity of approximately 16 cm/s, which is consistent with the 
experimental results shown in Fig. 7. 
For Eq. (8), the experimental constant B2′was set at B2’=1.08, based 
on conditions where a two-dimensional flame spread could no longer be 
maintained at oxygen concentrations above 21 %. The modified limit 
curve accurately represents the flammability limits obtained experi­
mentally, especially in the region between opposing flow velocities of 4 
cm/s and 16 cm/s, where the effects of both radiative flame extinction 
and blow-off are observed. In Fig. 2, the limiting curves resulting from 
merely changing the experimental factor B2 in Eq. (4) are also shown. It 
is found that simply altering B2 in the original model (indicated by the 
dashed curve) does not suffice to accurately represent the experimental 
results. This suggests that by appropriately considering the impact of the 
boundary layer developing on solid samples, the flammability limits of 
solid materials in microgravity environments can be accurately pre­
dicted. If the experimental constant B2’=1.08, which effectively repli­
cates the filter paper experiments, can also be effectively applied to 
other materials in upcoming orbital experiments, this implies that the 
flammability limits of solid materials, such as plastics, can be relatively 
accurately predicted using only the data from ground experiments. 
It is worth noting that while the modified model effectively predicts 
the minimum oxygen concentration, careful attention must be paid to 
flame behaviors in extremely low opposed flow conditions. As shown in 
Fig. 10, under conditions of low opposing flow velocities, the preheat 
zone length, Lg, is smaller than the predicted values. Reference [18] 
explains that while Lg diverges in the two-dimensional flame model 
(~αg/Vg), it assumes a finite value proportional to the flame radius in 
round flame configurations. Consequently, as the opposing flow velocity 
Vg decreases below the two-dimensional flame spread limit (depicted by 
the blue curve in Fig. 2), the flame shape transitions to a round, 
three-dimensional structure to minimize the preheat zone length Lg. This 
transformation in flame shape effectively reduces radiative heat losses, 
thereby allowing the flame to survive. 
5. Conclusions 
The long-duration combustion experiments of filter paper conducted 
in the ISS/Kibo module were meticulously analyzed, leading to the 
creation of a flammability map. By modeling blow-off and radiative loss, 
we calculated the limiting oxygen concentration in a microgravity 
environment using results from OI tests, HOI tests, TG-DTA, as well as 
the sample’s composition and combustion heat. This enabled us to 
determine the limiting curve of filter paper and compare it with the 
flammability map obtained from the orbital experiments. Although the 
blow-off extinction branch was quantitatively well-represented, it was 
discovered that under conditions with opposed flow velocity below 10 
cm/s, the actual limiting oxygen concentration was higher than the 
predicted limiting curve. This discrepancy is attributed to the influence 
of the boundary layer on the sample surface, where the preheating zone 
length ahead of the flame is inversely proportional to the square root of 
the opposed flow velocity, not the velocity itself. Consequently, when 
the radiative loss term was revised and the limiting curve recalculated, 
the updated limiting curve aligned well quantitatively with that from 
the orbital experiments. Thus, by accurately assessing the flow velocity 
opposing the flame front in both the radiation quenching and blow-off 
branches, we can precisely predict the flammability limits of thermally 
thin solid materials in a microgravity environment. These results are 
significant as they enable the prediction of the flammability of solid 
materials in space habitation environments based on ground test results. 
Novelty and significance statement 
The novelty of this research is in deriving a flammability map from 
the results of the first systematic long-duration combustion tests of filter 
paper conducted using the Solid Combustion Experiment Module 
(SCEM) aboard the ISS/Kibo and developing models that accurately 
predict the limiting curves quantitatively by considering the effect of the 
boundary layer on the sample. It has been clarified that the flow velocity 
facing the flame front is inversely proportional to the square root of the 
bulk velocity, even under low velocity conditions, based on the rela­
tionship between the preheat zone length and th"
