2. Experiment setup
The experiment is conducted using the SoFIE insert (main compo-
nents shown in Fig. 1) which is placed inside the Combustion Integrated
Rack (CIR) aboard the ISS. CIR is equipped with a power supply, filters,
lighting, gas cylinder intakes, radiometers, and oxygen, pressure, ve-
locity and CO, CO2 sensors. In this paper, oxygen percentage is reported
from the sensor located at the flow duct entrance. The SoFIE insert has a
square flow duct (13.3 cm x 13.3 cm cross-section and 24.6 cm long).
Forced flow ranging from 0.2 - 80 cm/s is generated by two fan banks. A
flow sensor at the upstream end of the flow duct provides a flow velocity
reading. The spherical PMMA sample (4 cm diameter) used in GEL is
supported by a long stainless-steel tube (6.35 mm diameter). Three
samples are mounted 120◦apart on an index system that can be rotated
to install one sample at a time with the flow duct.
Once the astronaut installs the samples and the oxidizer gas cylinders
in the rack, the rest of the experiment is controlled remotely from the
NASA Glenn Research Center ISS Payload Operations Center (GIPOC).
During the test, one sample is placed along the centerline of flow duct
and about 1 cm from the duct exit for ignition. The igniter is an elec-
trically heated coiled wire placed near the upstream stagnation point of
the sphere. After ignition, the igniter is retracted. The sample can either
stay at this location for the whole test or be translated into the duct.
Four fixed cameras are used for flame visualization. Three Sony FCB-
MA130 cameras with a 1400 × 1200 pixel array (named SoFIE Cam1,
Cam2, and Cam3) are in orthogonal direction of a 1.4 megapixel Allied
Vision GC-1380CH ACME color camera assembly with a 1360×1024
pixel array. SoFIE Cam1 views the sample near the ignition location.
SoFIE Cam2 and ACME are aligned and view the sample at the location
after translating into the duct. SoFIE Cam3 is not activated in the cases
presented in this paper.
All the extinction cases in this paper are obtained using a natural and
gradual oxygen depletion while the flow speed is held constant. In the
sealed CIR chamber (free volume = 85.7 liter), oxygen is slowly
consumed. This oxygen depletion method is the least intrusive (i.e., free
of external disturbances), which is important both for determining ac-
curate extinction limits and for observing spontaneous cyclical extinc-
tion dynamics [12,13,20]. Note that real time oxygen concentration
measurement is made at the flow duct entrance upstream of the sample.
The PMMA sphere samples are custom cast with five embedded
thermocouples as illustrated in Fig. 1. Three thermocouples are placed
along the center line close to the forward stagnation point. They are used
to deduce the subsurface temperature gradient, which is a measure of
the degree of solid sub-surface heating. More details on the preparation
and fabrication of the samples and the method to compute the temper-
ature gradient can be found in [14,15].
The 4-cm-diameter sphere was selected since it is a thick solid
representative of many non-thin materials used in spacecraft. The
spherical geometry has a richness of flame physics to study. When
placed in an airflow stream, there are several distinct regions around the
sphere, exhibiting different fluid mechanics characteristics. In front of
the sphere, stagnation point flow develops. Flames in this region are
locally one-dimensional when the flame thickness is much smaller than
the sphere radius, and experimental results can be compared with one-
dimensional models. This is also the flame stabilization region that
controls the extinction characteristics of the envelope flame. The exis-
tence of the sphere shoulder and wake regions, on the other hand, can
reveal additional multi-dimensional features of the extinction processes
(to be discussed later). PMMA spheres have also been employed in
[16-18].
3. Experimental results
Five tests are presented as listed in Table 1. Cases 1 to 3 are repre-
sentative of low-flow quenching, high-flow blowoff, and intermediate-
flow extinction, respectively. The differences between quenching and
blowoff are highlighted in Sections 3.1-3.3. In Section 3.4 and 3.5, a very
low flow test (Case 4) and a higher flow test (Case 5) are shown. These
last two tests reveal novel behavior and delineate the prominent features
of the solid extinction phenomena.
3.1. A quenching sequence
Fig. 2 shows images of a representative quenching sequence from
Case #1. During the extinction process (500 – 757.55 s), the flow ve-
locity slightly varies between 4.46 – 4.92 cm/s, and oxygen depletes to
extinction. There is a gradually increasing trend of the flow sensor
reading in this time range. It is a result of flow resistance change,
probably related to a slight decrease of chamber pressure during
quenching due to the combustion products filtering. Fig. 2(a) shows the
flame tip is at around 100◦(measured from the front stagnation point).
This is the furthest downstream that flame reaches as seen in the
tracking of tip2 (red color) in Fig. 3. As oxygen slowly depletes, the
flame tip slowly shrinks toward upstream (Fig 2(b)). As shown in Fig. 3,
the flame tip begins to have small fluctuations later (also illustrated in
Fig. 2(c)). The fluctuations have varied amplitudes and time periods.
Large amplitude cyclic flame tip pulsation and retreating starts much
later at about 744 s. With each pulsation cycle, the flame tip retreats
more toward upstream and results in a shorter instant flame length until
extinction occurred at 757.55 s.
Fig. 3 shows the flame tip tracking. Tracking is based on images in
the orthogonal direction of that in Fig. 2. Flame tip position is repre-
sented by the angle around the sphere with zero degrees at the forward
stagnation point. Positive and negative angles are used to track the left
and "
