There are ten completed flame tip pulsating and retreating cycles
before quenching (i.e., the flame reaches the back of the sphere) as
shown in the inset of Fig. 3. The pulsation to downstream takes more
time in the first two cycles but reaches a constant 0.4 s period for the
remaining. Note that when the net downstream pulsation speed reaches
its peak, the flame has its largest luminosity (Fig. 2(e)), consistent with a
previous work on blowoff oscillation over PMMA rods [20]. The
downstream pulsation speed is within the range of typical laminar
premixed burning velocities. After the flame pulsates downstream and
consumes the combustible mixture, the flame tip shrinks. The shrinking
time period ranges from 1.5 s to 0.8 s and the shrinking speed is much
slower than pulsating speed downstream. Note that in each cycle, the
flame tip shrinks more toward the upstream as shown in Fig. 3. So, on
the average the flame length is shrinking.
3.2. A blowoff sequence
Fig. 4 shows a representative blowoff sequence from Case #2 (34.28
cm/s flow). Fig. 4(a) shows that at 200 s, the flame is still away from
extinction at 16.6 % O2. The sample is located inside the flow duct. The
flame tip is slightly directed toward the centerline because of the flow
restriction inside the duct. The yellowish flame indicates soot formation.
When the ambient oxygen decreases, the flame turns blue.
Extinction starts with a flame hole at the stagnation point (Fig. 4(b)).
This local flame blowoff occurs when oxygen is below the limit (14.99
%) for this particular stagnation flow strain rate (25.7 s−1). The flame
base then retreats downstream to the near-wake region, where the flow
velocity is smaller (Fig. 4(c)). After the flame base retreats, the still hot
solid surface continues generating fuel vapor which mixes with
incoming oxygen. The flame base then pulsates upstream all the way to
the stagnation region (Fig. 4(e)). During the upstream pulsation, the
flame base shape is irregular, and part of the base is in the wake region
