3.3. Extinction at intermediate flow velocities and flow strain rates
In the previous sections we have shown how different the extinction
processes for low-flow quenching and high-flow blowoff are. In
quenching, the flame tip shrinks toward the upstream, in blowoff, the
flame base retreats toward the downstream. In quenching, the flame tip
pulsates while in blowoff, the flame base pulsates. One natural question
is how does the flame go to extinction at intermediate flow velocities
where the quenching and the blowoff boundaries meet? The meeting
point of the two branches has been referred to as the fundamental limit
where the oxygen limit is the lowest [3].
We have examined several test results in the intermediate flow range
between 9.22 and 11.5 cm/s (strain rates 6.92–8.23 s−1). They all
exhibit the same description on the extinction processes as follows. As
oxygen is depleted, flame tip shrinks but without any pulsation. Near
extinction, the flame base color began to fade (a prelude to local blowoff
or hole forming) but the flame goes out normally in one video frame
without any base pulsation either. So, the transition from quench to
blowoff is smooth along the extinction boundary. Case #3 in Table 1 is a
representative intermediate case (see supplementary video S3).
3.4. Self-sustained cyclic flame pulsation
Case #4 is an ultra-low velocity case at relatively high oxygen at-
mosphere. As shown in Fig. 5(a), the flame tip pulsating downstream
starts at 311 s, immediately after the nominal flow velocity is turned
down to 0.4 cm/s at 309 s, and lasts until quenching at 1237.88 s, for a
total period of 927 s. In contrast, the near-limit pulsation in Case #1 only
lasts for 18.84 s. In addition to flame tip position, test telemetries of
oxygen, pressure, and flow velocity are also plotted in Fig. 5(a). It shows
one side of the flame initiates the cyclic pulsation and the other side
starts much later at around 1040s. When the pulsation starts, oxygen
concentration is at 26.25 %. It gradually depletes to 20.77 % at
quenched extinction. During most of the time, the pulsations are under
elevated oxygen concentration. If the oxygen were not depleted, the
pulsative cycles would have lasted even longer. So, we will refer this
case as a “self-sustained” flame pulsation (i.e., limit cycle). During pul-
sations, though the nominal flow velocity is held constant at 0.2 cm/s
(except the 0.4 cm/s set flow in the beginning 10 s), the flow sensor
reading ranged from 0.16 to 0.5 cm/s. Similar to Case #1, there is a
gradual increase of flow velocity during the quenching process (Fig. 5
(a)). Starting at around 880 s, there is also flow velocity oscillations in
phase with the flame pulsation. Fig. 5(b) shows 10 s of the flame tip
tracking and the flow sensor reading near extinction. The flow velocity
fluctuation is thought to be the result of flame-flow interaction since the
flow is very weak and these large-scale flame pulsations affect the flow
resistance in the duct.
The 30-fps frame rate gives very good resolution of the flame
tracking in Fig. 5(b) within one cycle. The shape of the cycle shows that
the flame tip spends much less time at the downstream position than at
the upstream position. The trajectory of the tip is not sinusoidal or near-
sinusoidal like typical oscillations. This is because the present cycle has
two distinct time scales: the propagation time in a fuel-air mixture and
the preparation time to form a new mixture after the flame passage.
Fig. 5(b) shows that the preparation time is the longer of the two
although they are still within the same order of magnitude, close to the
mass diffusion time, D/δ2, where δ is the flame standoff and D is the
diffusion coefficient. In this low-flow case, escaping fuel vapor from the
flame-tip-sphere opening might be the dominant source of fuel vapor to
create a combustible mixture for pulsation, between the two sources
discussed previously in Section 3.1.
The pulsation frequency starts at 1.4 Hz, gradually decreases to 1 Hz,
and is statistically constant in a 640 s to 1200s period. The frequency
further drops to 0.8 Hz before quenching at 1238s. During this quasi-
steady period, the pulsation amplitude increases with time (Fig. 5(a)).
Fig. 6 presents the spread tracking of the flame tip1 in Fig. 5(a) in several
selected time periods. At the start of a pulsating cycle, the flame tip
position is set to zero. Flame tip position in the following frames of this
cycle is compared to the starting frame to determine the distance the
flame tip travels. The speed of the flame tip pulsation is slow in the
beginning, reaches peak in the middle, and slows down when reaching
the spreading limit. The peak pulsation speed is around 25 cm/s, within
the range of typical laminar premixed flame burning velocity. The tip
shrinking is fast at the beginning but slows down approaching to the
stagnation region. The tip stays at the starting position for a relatively
long time get enough fuel vapor for the next pulse. There are no sig-
nificant differences on the downstream pulsation speed in the beginning
of the cycles. The total time for each cycle slightly increases during this
quasi-steady period mainly due to the increase in the pulsation ampli-
tude. Note that there is also variation from cycle to cycle so the trend on
cycle frequency variation mentioned above is the statistica"
