Similar to Case #2, local blowoff occurs with a hole in the front
stagnation region. The flame then stabilizes, and the flame base anchors
at the shoulder of the sphere (Fig. 9(b)). The anchor flame base is
indicated by the intense color due to high reactivity. In this test, the side-
stabilized flame lasts for 40 s, around 35 s during which the flame is
spinning. With continued oxygen depletion, the flame breaks into
flamelets (Fig. 9(c) and (d)). The flamelet spins with frequencies ranging
from 2 to 3.5 Hz. During spinning, the flamelets cover a shrinking sur-
face area of the sphere due to oxygen depletion. With more and more
area exposed, the solid surface cools down until the fuel vapor generated
is not enough to sustain burning. This blowoff and flame spinning
sequence is very dynamic (see supplementary video S5).
4. Summary and discussions
This is the first report on the experimental findings from a recent
microgravity experiment aboard the International Space Station study-
ing the burning of a 4-cm diameter PMMA sphere. One of the main
objectives of the experiment is to study solid diffusion flame extinction
processes. Using an oxygen depletion method at specified flow veloc-
ities, both the transient details of the low-flow quenching and high-flow
blowoff processes are revealed.
Quenching starts with the flame tip shrinking toward the stagnation
region. Extinction occurs when the shrinking flame at the forward
stagnation point becomes too small. An early quasi-steady model over a
thin solid with surface radiative heat loss has predicted this trend [21].
The present experiment, however, shows that this trend of steady
shrinking flame is only true for the average flame. The actual quenching
process is very dynamic involving flame tip local fluctuation and
downstream cyclic pulsations all the way to the back of the sphere over
many cycles. During pulsations, the flame tip spreads into a partially
premixed mixture next to the hot solid surface. The mixture is created by
leakage of fuel vapor from the opening between the flame tip and the
solid surface and/or solid pyrolysis from the still hot solid after the flame
tip cyclic shrinking. In low stretch flames, this opening is large, which
enhances the importance of fuel leakage contribution to the cyclic pul-
sations. In a higher oxygen atmosphere (>21 %) and ultra-low flow
velocity (< 1 cm/s), pulsative motion can last for more than 15 min.
These implications for spacecraft fire safety need to be explored further.
Blowoff starts with a hole opening at the stagnation region in front of
the envelope diffusion flame. This is well known experimentally and
studied in detail in [20]. An early quasistatic numerical model on thin
solid also predicted the local blowoff of the stagnation flame and the
possibility of a side stabilized diffusion flame with increasing flow ve-
locity or decreasing Damkohler number [22]. Again, experiments show
that the extinction process is dynamic. Cyclic upstream pulsation and
flame base retreat can proceed to the stabilization of the flame at the
shoulder of the spherical sample. When the flame is weakened by
reduced oxygen, flamelets form and azimuthally spin around the sphere.
Other possible wake flame configurations have not been observed in the
present setup possibly owing to the presence of the sample supporting
rod.
Regarding the near-limit flame extinction dynamics: while cyclic
flame movement are observed both in quenching and in blowoff, there is
a profound difference between the two cases. The pulsating part is the
flame tip in quenching and the flame base in blowoff. The stagnation
region is the flame stabilization zone whereas the flame tip is not.
However, both cases are coupled with the hot solid surface which sup-
plies the fuel vapor for the flame to pulsate.
We note that blowoff and quench have been studied using a PMMA
cylinder aligned with the flow[5,20]. Using a large sphere, we have a
better-defined stagnation shoulder, and wake flow zones. The multiple
embedded thermocouples enable us to characterize the sample preheat
levels. The low-velocity control also provides better resolved quenching
limits.
Also, regarding the scale effect of the sample on quenching: low
stretch flames can be established using large samples in higher velocity
flows [6-8] or using smaller sample in lower velocity flow such as in the
present work. Although both can achieve the same low flame stretch
rate, the near-limit behavior can differ in the details. For a large sample,
flame breaks into flamelets, strips or holes before the quenching of the
complete flame. The flamelets are of the dimension of diffusion length.
In the present work using a smaller sample, there is pulsative motion but
no flamelet formation during the quenching process. So, the detailed
quenching and blowoff process can be multi-dimensional in nature.
They cannot be prescribed by the flow strain or flame stretch rate alone.
An additional length scale may be required.
One final remark on the diffusion flame extinction criterion. Even in
the one-dimensional description of diffusion flames, one may wonder
whether a single Damkohler number can be sufficient to describe both
blowoff and quenching limits. Both are extinction processes and the
concept of limiting ratio of chemical vs. flow times seems applicable.
However, quantifying the chemical reaction rate will need accurate
flame temperature information. The actual flame temperature will be
affected by heat loss, so additional non-dimensional heat l"
