Saffire
A B S T R A C T
Six Saffire experiments with SIBAL fabric (75 % cotton, 25 % fiberglass) were carried out in orbit aboard
different Cygnus vehicles after unberthing from the ISS. In five tests the ambient atmosphere was ISS cabin air,
which contained approximately 21 % O2 at ambient pressure. The sixth test was an exploration atmosphere (31
% O2 at 54 kPa). Relative humidity was measured but not controlled. In each experiment, after the concurrent
flame passed over the fabric, glowing smolder spots were observed to spread over the hot reactive char. Char-
acteristics of this smolder wave have been measured as a function of forced flow and relative humidity level. The
spots showed a slight preference to spread concurrently in air but had no preference in lateral spread. An increase
in the flow modestly increased the number of spots while an increase in ambient air relative humidity diminished
the length of the smolder wave and linearly decreased the number of spots due to the endothermic heterogenous
surface reaction C(s) + H2O > CO + H2. The smolder wave was found to have a lognormal distribution of
glowing spots relative to distance from the onset of smolder. The smolder wave spread at the same speed as the
flame after an ignition transient since the spots were generated just beyond the flame base when the hot reactive
char was exposed to the fresh oxidizer flow. The smolder wave was made up of growth and extinction phases.
The initial population growth occurs at an intrinsic growth rate, and after peaking, the population exhibits an
exponential decay in the number of spots as extinctions become dominant. The populations follow a logistic
population model with carrying capacity (K) and intrinsic growth rate (ro) parameters taken from the data and
assuming a starting population of unity.
1. Introduction
Smoldering combustion can occur in many different geometries, but
most real-world geometries are thermally thick (wood, foam, peat, etc.)
and experiments strive for either pseudo-one-dimensionality due to the
sensitivity
of
smoldering
combustion
to
heat
losses
or
two-
dimensionality which include radiative and convective losses [1-3].
Thermally thin geometries of common use include cigarettes and
incense [4-6]. These axisymmetric geometries enhance the oxygen
transport while minimizing surface heat losses, which play a critical role
in the strength of the smolder wave.
Very few experiments have studied planar thermally thin smolder
[7]. Due to the high heat losses from radiative loss and convective
cooling in this large surface to volume geometry, the smolder must be
highly exothermic or have some other heat source to provide support for
the endothermic pyrolysis.
Kellogg et al. [7] conducted experiments with horizontal smolder
over various papers and fabrics with varied potassium ion concentra-
tions, which is known to promote smoldering. They found a uniform
circular smolder pattern occurred where the smolder spread rate was
inversely proportional to the area density of the material once the po-
tassium concentration was high enough to promote the smolder
reaction.
The first known reported glowing smolder spots in microgravity [8]
were found to occur when ashless filter paper was doped with potassium
acetate. While most of the smolder spots spread upstream in air (reverse
smolder) due to an oxygen shadow effect, at the highest velocities tested
(6.5 cm/s) some downstream (forward smolder) spots were observed as
well.
Numerical models have predicted fingering forward smolder over a
thin fuel [9] as well as through a porous media [10-12]. Material
anisotropy effects are shown to play a role in the propagation and the
