doi:10.1016/j.proci.2024.105495,doi,Characterizing the wave of discrete glowing smolder spots generated at the base of a concurrent microgravity flame spreading over a cotton-fiberglass fabric,2,1,"stability of traveling wave solutions are discussed.
Smolder ‘holes’ were observed after upward flame spread over paper
samples near blowoff [13]. These ‘holes’ were glowing smolder spots
that began to propagate radially to create a glowing smolder ring as they
grew larger. The rings would locally extinguish, typically on the bottom
half, to create smolder arches.
The experiments reported in this work are most like [13] in that a
concurrent flame spreads across a fabric sample and the highly reactive
char left behind undergoes char oxidation when exposed to the ambient
air. This has been termed ‘glowing combustion’ in the literature [14]
that occurs without an associated pyrolysis process. Ohlemiller [3]
described this secondary char oxidation of char left behind after flaming
combustion of cellulosic materials as ‘Intense high temperature…reac-
tion fronts propagate intermittently in seemingly random directions
through the fibrous low-density char left behind by the main lower
temperature smolder front”. In this work we demonstrate there is sig-
nificant order to the seemingly random reaction fronts.
2. Experimental methods
Six Saffire experiments with SIBAL fabric (75 % cotton, 25 % fiber-
glass) [15] were carried out in orbit aboard different Cygnus vehicles
after unberthing from the ISS and in free drift. Olson et al. [15] has a
detailed description of the experiments and flame spread portion of each
test. The salient ambient test conditions are listed in Table 1. The at-
mosphere for Saffire I-IV was ISS cabin air, which contained approxi-
mately 21 % O2 at ambient pressure. In Saffire VI, the vehicle was
depressurized, and then the volume was measured by adding a known
quantity of CO2 and measuring the pressure difference. The CO2 was
scrubbed out and oxygen was added to raise the concentration to
exploration atmosphere conditions. Relative humidity of the cabin air in
the vehicle was measured but not controlled. All samples were 40.6 cm
wide except for flight II which had 5 cm wide samples (samples 5 and 6).
Saffire I and III were 94 cm long, Saffire IV and VI were 50 cm long,
Saffire 2 samples were 29 cm long positioned in the center of the duct,
and the 1 g sample was 100 cm long. Saffire V was not included here
because the fabric
sample for that flight
was cotton, not the
cotton-fiberglass fabric that is the focus of this paper.
The SIBAL fabric used for these tests is 75 % unbleached cotton and
25 % fiberglass. Each fiberglass strand is wrapped in the unbleached
cotton thread, and then the resulting composite thread is woven into a
fabric. This material has a high surface to volume ratio and these
characteristics classify it as a porous solid fuel rather than a condensed
fuel in an inert porous media [1].
In each experiment, after the concurrent flame passed over the fab-
ric, glowing smolder spots were observed to spread over the hot reactive
char. A blinking LED was used to briefly illuminate the sample every few
seconds to track the pyrolysis front for the flame spread portion of the
test, but all the analysis on the smolder wave used the dark images.
Saffire I and III used two cameras to cover the large samples. The images
were distortion-corrected to remove the distortion caused by the large
field of view and short working distance of the lenses. The surface view
images were a 1638 × 1325 pixels array. Saffire I-III had a resolution of
2.2333 pixels/mm and Saffire IV and VI had a resolution of 3.2 pixels/
mm. While the cameras recorded at 30 frames per second, not all frames
could be downloaded from the Cygnus vehicle due to limited radio
receiver coverage and limited mission duration, so the image timing is
not uniform. This is accounted for in the analysis in this work, but it does
preclude using some image analysis features that cannot correct for this.
The glowing smolder spots were tracked in several ways. The non-
illuminated images were first processed to remove the flame and
enhance the contrast between smolder spots and background for
tracking [16]. The actual number of spots in each image were counted
and the smolder wave was tracked using NASA software (Tracker-3 and
Spotlight 16 bit) [17,18] and the trajectory of each smolder spot (i.e.,
PIV) was analyzed using publicly available software (FIJI with plugins
Mosaic Particle Tracker 2D/3D and Chemotaxis Tool). [19-21].
3. Results
In normal gravity, after acceleratory upward flame spread over the
same SIBAL fabric, the glowing smolder spots grow into rings like Wang
et al. [13] as shown in Fig. 1a and more closely in Fig. 1b. In contrast, the
microgravity glowing smolder spots remain spots that propagate,
bifurcate, merge, and eventually extinguish, very similar to [8] but on a
much larger and more uniform scale.
This difference in smolder behavior is hypothesized to be caused by
the fact that in 1 g with no forced flow, the local lateral entrainment flow
creates a flow perpendicular to the smoldering surface while buoyant
flow is upward. This orthogonal flow situation is similar but inverse to
the ring-shaped smolder in horizontal smolder [8] where the buoyant
flow is perpendicular to the fuel surface while entraining flow is parallel
to the surface. The ring-like smolder, and the orthogonal flows do not
exist in 0 g purely forced axial flow. The mass addition to the gas-phase
from the smoldering is negligible compared to the 20 cm/s forced flows.
The microgravity glowing smolder spots all appear to be about the
same size in Fig. 1c. They are generated at the base of the flame in
discreet ‘dotted lines’ that are initially spaced about 1–2 diameters
apart.
The uniformity of the smolder is shown in Fig. 2 for each test. These
images were created by first removing the flame by using gamma
correction and contrast enhancement, and then projecting the image
stack to a single image in FIJI [19] using Z Project (Max Intensity).
The dark image of the flame and smoldering is shown above each
accumulated smolder image in F"
