The smolder patterns in Fig. 2 for Saffire I-III appear almost grid-like,
suggesting the smolder is following the threads of the fabric. Saffire IV
and VI are more chaotic in pattern, possibly due to the significantly
stronger and deeper smolder wave as shown by the flame images in Fig
1c and Fig. 2.
3.1. Population dynamics
The number of smolder spots was counted on each image using
software [17]. The number of smolder spots versus distance from the
igniter end are shown in Fig. 3. Saffire IV and VI had significantly more
smolder spots than I and III, and the trend with time is different. All tests
have the same rapid increases in the number of smolder spots for the first
~10 cm of propagation, but then Saffire I and III decay significantly to a
nearly steady value by about 30–40 cm. Saffire IV shows a slight decay
but sustains significantly more smolder spots. The decay in Saffire VI
falls between these two trends. The rapid rise and significant decay in
smolder spots have the same shape and magnitude as the observed
excess pyrolysis length (i.e., flame length overshoot) [15]. This transient
excess pyrolysis length was hypothesized to be a post-ignition flame
growth transient for the essentially two-dimensional flames where the
burnout time becomes very long until the preheat and pyrolysis lengths
develop (by 30-40 cm).
The amount of reactive char left behind also appears to be strongly
affected by this ignition transient where initially there is a dense smolder
wave but as the flame develops and reaches a steady size, the smolder
population reaches a steady state. In Fig. 3, the decays occur as the
initially uniformly flame-heated surface begins to cool and the heat
release from the localized reaction must increasingly offset heat losses.
The population N(t) of smolder spots can be empirically modeled
using a logistic model [23]. The logistic model describes the growth of a
population from an initial size No (=1 is assumed) to the carrying ca-
pacity K (resource limited population size). In these experiments, the
resource that is limited is hypothesized to be the quantity of hot reactive
char since ambient oxygen is plentiful. The intrinsic growth rate ro re-
flects the rapid initial exponential growth rate of the number of smolder
spots and was estimated to be 0.5 s−1 using an exponential fit to the early
smolder wave shape discussed later. The logistic equation is given in Eq.
(1):
