3.2. Water vapor effects on smolder
The decay in smolder population is a strong function of the ambient
relative humidity level as shown in Fig. 4. Fig. 4 plots the steady-state
number of smolder spots per unit width of sample (so Saffire II can be
included) versus% RH. The higher the relative humidity, the fewer
smolder spots are observed. The change in oxygen concentration and
pressure for Saffire VI (42 % RH data point) did not affect the observed
trend with humidity.
Water vapor is known to heterogeneously surface react with reactive
char via the surface reaction C(s) + H2O →CO + H2. This reaction is
strongly endothermic [24-26] and competes directly for active C(s)
surface sites with the char oxidation reaction C(s) + O2 →CO2, which is
exothermic at three times the standard enthalpy of change as the water
reaction [26]. Surprisingly, this important heterogeneous char-water
reaction is not present in any of the chemistry models discussed in the
recent smolder review article [1]. The endothermic water reaction, by
consuming active char sites (the assumed limiting resource) instead of
the exothermic oxidation reaction, reduces the smolder spot heat release
rate and reduces the smolder temperature [24]. This makes the smolder
spots more likely to extinguish due to heat losses, which would reduce
