gasification over a much higher range (4–16 % mole fraction water)
[27]. Increasing the forced flow velocity did increase the population
slightly in Saffire II between samples 5 and 6 for the same relative hu-
midity, although the flow variation was small relative to the humidity
variation (see Fig. 4).
3.3. Smolder spot trajectory analysis
ImageJ FIJI [19] was used with plugins (Mosaic and Chemotaxis
tool) [20,21] to track each smolder spot’s position versus time (PIV) and
graph the resulting trajectories for each test. Each trajectory starts at (0,
0) in the Chemotaxis tool graph.
The main graph in Fig. 5 shows the detailed trajectory information
for the most populous test, Saffire IV. The colored circles are plotted at
the Euclidian end points of each trajectory and are colored according to
the accumulated distance traveled. The center of mass of all trajectories
in mm is (0.0, 0.34), which is very close to zero (symmetric). Most of the
spots traveled less than 10 mm (Euclidian distance) while many traveled
an accumulated distance that was 2–3 times that, as shown in the
legend.
The black triangles in Fig. 5 provide an accumulated travel distance
distribution that exhibits an exponential decay. Exponential decay in
populations is common in lifetime analysis. In this application the
constant in the exponent provides a ‘half-life’ distance of 1/0.141 = 7.1
mm of accumulated distance for half of the smolder spot population.
In a chemotaxis-generated trajectory plot (inset to Fig. 5), each tra-
jectory is plotted, and color coded concurrent (black) or opposed (red).
All air tests showed a consistent trend in the propagation pattern.
Table 2 provide the breakout between concurrent and opposed trajec-
tories from the chemotaxis tool data for each Saffire flight as well as the
total number of trajectories for each Saffire flight. The fractions
