This fraction concurrent bias noted in Saffire I-IV in Table 2 may be
due to the warmer surface temperatures in that direction due to
convective heating from the smolder spot and the closer proximity to the
downstream flame and thus a less radiatively and convectively cooled
surface. However, since the average center of mass is less than a mm
from the origin, the relative distance of this average bias is quite small.
Saffire VI is notably different from this average with 0.45 opposed
and 0.55 concurrent and is much closer to an even mix of opposed and
concurrent. This is attributed to the higher oxygen concentration and
lower pressure in the ambient gas that allowed a faster diffusion of ox-
