release rate made the smolder spots less susceptible to the heat losses
hypothesized for Saffire I-IV.
3.4. Smolder wave analysis
The smolder wave starts as the reactive char exits the flame base and
encounters fresh oxidizer. The smolder wave spreads at the same rate as
the flame spread rate, which was 0.36 cm/s. To measure the smolder
wave as it moved down the sample, the video image was divided into 17
strips that spanned the width of the sample but were only 2.7 cm long in
the axial direction. The area smoldering in each strip is shown as a
function of time in Fig. 6. The smolder wave takes approximately 40 s to
completely pass through a video strip.
To better show the shape of the smolder wave, time is converted to
distance using the spread rate [15], and the central strips are shifted to
overlay the waves exclusive of end effects in Fig. 7. The smolder wave
has a lognormal distribution, rising rapidly from the onset of the smolder
wave to a peak at approximately 3 cm from the onset. It then has a slow
exponential decay, as shown by the red dashed lines. The overall length
of the wave is approximately 15 cm. The lognormal distribution is
characteristic of populations [28]. The peak smolder area of 5.5 cm2
relative to the area of the strip (2.7 cm x 40.6 cm) shows that a maximum
of 5 % of the surface area is smoldering whereas most of the wave has
considerably less surface area smoldering. The average smolder spot
propagation speed is approximately 7.1 mm/12.5 s, or 0.6 mm/s based
on the ‘half-life’ distance from Fig. 5 and the lifetime from Fig. 7. This is
significantly slower than the flame spread rate in this test of 3.6 mm/s
[15].
The same analysis was done for the other tests with similar results.
