doi:10.1016/j.proci.2024.105495,doi,Characterizing the wave of discrete glowing smolder spots generated at the base of a concurrent microgravity flame spreading over a cotton-fiberglass fabric,6,5,"that. The peak in the profile also occurs further down the wave at 5 cm
compared to 3 cm in Fig. 7. The wave is also shorter at just over 10 cm
compared to 15 cm in Fig. 7, although the time for the smolder wave to
pass is also about 40 s, which may be because of surface cooling via
radiation and convection independent of humidity. All these trends are
consistent with a weaker smolder wave at higher relative humidity.
Saffire VI fell between these two cases, with a wave length of 12 cm
and a peak at 4 cm from the onset. The wave was faster to pass at 30 s
due to the higher oxygen concentration promoting a faster char
oxidation.
It is useful to consider whether this smolder wave is forward smolder,
reverse smolder, or something else. The wave is clearly moving in the
direction of the air flow, which would suggest forward smolder. How-
ever, the wave is ‘seeded’ by the moving flame. Fig. 7 is essentially the
wave in smolder-onset fixed coordinates. Because the forced flow is
significantly faster than the smolder spot propagation rate, one could
also argue that the smolder could be opposed (reverse) smolder. How-
ever, the smolder spots spread in all directions, as shown in Fig. 3 with
only a slight preference for the concurrent direction in air. Since the
smolder is a surface reaction and is nearly agnostic to the flow direction,
application of a percolation model might be appropriate to describe the
smolder wave which appears to follow the fabric structure as shown in
Fig. 2. The author suggests further research in this area.
4. Conclusion
Six flight experiments were performed with a cotton-fiberglass blend
fabric. After the concurrent flame base passes by, the hot reactive char is
exposed to fresh oxidizer, and glowing smolder spots are generated, so
by definition the smolder wave spreads at the same rate as the concur-
rent flame that preceded it. The smolder spots initially appear as a
‘dotted line’ of red-glowing spots spaced apart by 1–2 diameters. Indi-
vidual spot tracking shows that the smolder spots slightly preferentially
spread downstream in air that disappears at higher oxygen and lower
pressure, but shown no preference in lateral spread. This contrasts with
the normal gravity smoldering that has a more ring-shaped propagation
pattern.
The smolder population initially grows exponentially with time as
the fresh reactive char is exposed to the oxidizer. After the initial
intrinsic growth, the smolder populations are observed to either reach a
steady value directly or decay to a lower steady state population size.
The steady population size linearly decreases as the relative humidity of
the ambient atmosphere increases due to the endothermic heterogenous
surface reaction C(s) + H2O > CO + H2. This chemical reaction is absent
from recent smolder model chemistry [1] and should be included in
future models as it competes directly with the exothermic heterogeneous
char oxidation reaction. Increasing the flow velocity has a much smaller
effect on increasing the population size. Logistic models of the smolder
spot population were shown to fit the data very well.
The characteristics of the smolder wave have been presented in detail
for the strongest smolder case and briefly for a weaker case, and sum-
mary information has been presented for the other flights. The strongest
smolder wave took approximately 40 s to pass by and had a steady state
15 cm length. The wave has a lognormal distribution that is character-
istic of populations. Smolder spots are generated at an intrinsic growth
rate of 0.5 s−1 initially . They extinguish in an exponential decay with an
average lifetime of 12.5 s characteristic of the exponential decay.
Since the smolder is a surface reaction and is nearly agnostic to the
flow direction, application of a percolation model might be appropriate
to describe the smolder wave. The author suggests further research in
this area.
Novelty and significance statement
This research is novel because it characterizes the microgravity
steady thermally thin planar smolder wave of discrete smolder spots
generated behind the base of a two-dimensional concurrent flame as the
surface of hot reactive char is exposed to the fresh oxidizer. This unique
geometry cannot be replicated on Earth because upward flame spread is
acceleratory and not two-dimensional. In addition, the smoldering
generated after flame passage over the same fuel in normal gravity has a
very different ring-like propagation pattern without a discernable wave
structure even for very large samples. The surface/heterogeneous re-
actions between the hot char and water vapor are clearly captured in this
unique geometry. The results provide significant exceptionally rare
experimental data that can be tested against models.
