doi:10.1016/j.proci.2024.105574,doi,Extinction of microgravity partially premixed flame aboard the Chinese Space Station,2,1,"flame can stabilize and be lifted at its roots, where the non-premixed fuel
and oxidizer mix and burn, and this non-premixed region can be
deterministic of the PPF structure [5].
As a PPF approaches its extinction state, the flame structure is sus-
tained until the collapse of the NPF branch or a decrease of primary
reactions in the non-premixed region, and the corresponding Damk¨ohler
number can be frequently used to describe the phenomenon. Such near-
limit process sometimes can be companied by flashback and blow-out
[7]. Therefore, the ultimate extinction of the non-premixed region de-
serves more attention due to that such a chemically strong branch
actually represents the flammability limit in a PPF as a result of local
diffusion and radiative heat loss.
It had been long suggested that flame strain and radiation can be
critical to extinction behavior. This is particularly evident in the
microgravity
co-flow
and counterflow
configurations
[8],
where
reduced gravity can largely eliminate the disturbance from natural
convection and thus facilitate the flame stability and further extend
flammability limit [9]. For counterflow flame extinction in micro-
gravity, the flame can be either suppressed when exceeding critical
strain rates or more resilient for larger radiative heat losses at lower
strain limit [10,11]. While flow strain inevitably plays a significant role
in the extinction of counterflow flames, in PPF the strain effect can be
less pronounced so that the radiation effect can be comparably influ-
ential [12], interplay of these effects as well as interaction between
different flame branches in the non-premixed region of a PPF requires
further discussions.
In the past few years, Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Ex-
periments (ACME) on the International Space Station (ISS) have shed
light on NPF behaviors. Topics investigated include soot formation
mechanisms [13,14], soot effect on flammability limits [15], electric
field effect [16] and radiation effect on extinction of low-momentum
spherical NPF [17,18]. These experiments have primarily concentrated
on the impact of soot formation and radiation on flame behavior and
radiation effects for low-momentum spherical NPFs. Yet, conditions
where the strain and radiation effects become comparable have received
less attention. The combined effect of flame strain and radiation can be
thus assessed by considering a soot-free, non-spherical PPF.
The present study demonstrates the very first experiments aboard the
Chinese Space Station (CSS) that address the aforementioned problems.
The PPF experiments are conducted using a co-flow burner under
microgravity conditions aboard the Chinese Space Station. Numerical
simulations for similar conditions are performed and compared with
experimental observation in terms of extinction dynamics.
2. Experimental setup and conditions
2.1. Experimental setup
Experiments for this study were conducted in the Combustion Sci-
ence Rack (CSR) aboard the CSS, using the Gaseous Combustion
Experiment Insert (GCEI) installed in the combustion chamber as part of
the Combustion Science Application System (CSAS, consisting of all the
physical equipment inside the CSR). As is shown in Fig. 1, the CSR with
GCEI is equipped with capabilities for gas supply, mixing, flow rate
control, optical diagnostics, and exhaust purification. The CSR provides
oxidizer, diluent (N2), and inert gas (Ar at this moment) to the GCEI
through a connecting ring on the combustion chamber. The GCEI itself
carries a fuel gas cylinder and a mass flow controller, shown in Fig. 2.
The oxidizer, diluent, inert gas and fuel gas enter individual channels in
a gas distribution block on the GCEI. Two proportioning solenoid valves
are used for each gas to precisely control the splitting ratio. Among the
three outlets, one is for purging ambient gas inside the GCEI combustion
chamber, the rest two are the central tube and co-flow exit that are used
to generate the flames. Hence, the composition and flow rate of these
three gas outlets are determined by the proportioning solenoid valves
and flow controllers.
The GCEI is also equipped with two environmental sensor modules,
each with a fuel sensor, a humidity and temperature sensor, an oxygen
concentration sensor, and an environmental pressure sensor, as shown
in Fig. 3. Three photomultiplier tubes (PMTs, Hamamatsu H10723–110,
H10723–04, and H10723–20) on the GCEI enable detection of weak
light from three separate wavelength channels (230~700 nm, 185~870
nm, and 230~920 nm, respectively). The PMTs are primarily used to
determine whether ignition is successful, and their capability can be
further extended to collecting cool flame signals. Two radiometers
(Dexter ST150, with 0.15~2.7 μm and 2.7~4.0 μm windows, respec-
tively) record the flame radiance intensity. A wide-angle camera
(DALSA G3-GC10-C1280, resolution 1280×1024 and framing rate 25
fps) inside the GCEI was used to monitor instantaneous progress of the
experiment inside the combustion chamber. This camera provides live-
streaming video to the ground operation center in Beijing.
Flame imaging during the experiments was via the CSR’s diagnostic
subsystem (cf. Fig. 1), with details shown in Fig. 4. With the 62 mm side
winders on the GCEI, the field of view for the flame is larger than 50
mm×50 mm. Despite the availablity of PIV using a CW-laser, 3 high-
speed CCD cameras (PCO DIMAX CS1, resolution 1296×1024) and 2
intensified cameras (UVIZHC-19, 432 nm for CH* and 308 nm for OH*
luminescence, respectively) on the CSR, the present paper only used
images from intensified cameras and the wide-angle camera on the
GCEI. These cameras were operating at 25 fps throughout the
experiment.
The co-flow burner with a total height of 161 mm used in this
experiment is also shown in Fig. 4. The burner is installed on the com-
mon burner plate to be supplied with gas resources. The central tube has
an inner diameter of 2 mm and an outer diameter of 3 mm, while t"
doi:10.1016/j.proci.2024.105574,doi,Extinction of microgravity partially premixed flame aboard the Chinese Space Station,3,2,"preferred for ignition and the gas composition and flow rates are tran-
sitioned to target experimental conditions for experiments of interests,
as is shown in Table 1. The transition of flow rate conditions was per-
formed after the stabilization phase when the flame height and width
remain unchanged and the switching was completed within 0.5 s
through the proportional solenoid valves. The sequence of mass flow
rate control for the entire experiment from ignition to extinction can be
found in supplemental materials. The igniter only outputs less than 30 J.
So compared to the 1200 J heat release from the flame, the thermal
effect from the igniter is relatively small.
Flame evolution including structure, height and width can be
extracted from images. The flame region was extracted from the CH*
and OH* images using the Otsu algorithm [19] to effectively subtract the
foreground from the background. Flame edges were extracted using the
Canny edge detection operator [20]. The flame width Wf can be derived
from the furthest left and right flame boundaries, and flame height Hf by
the upper and lower boundaries in the images. The radiometer data were
utilized to calculate an average flame temperature Tf based on the
imaged flame area. The radiometer readings were ground-calibrated
using a black-body furnace and the temperature was derived from the
radiance with the corresponding flame size.
2.2. Experimental conditions
The in-orbit experiment sequence comprises two groups using CH4 as
fuel, as shown in Table 2. The initial set of experiments (Experiments 1.1
to 1.5) produced lifted and near-blowout limit PPFs, with varying inlet
velocities (U) and gas volume fraction (X) for CH4, O2 and N2, where a
subscript “cen“ refers to central tube, and “co” refers to the coflow tube.
Experiment 2.1 was conducted under non-premixed conditions, main-
taining fuel flow rates identical to those in experiment 1.1. To transition
from the ignition condition to these extinction conditions, the fuel mass
flow rate remained unchanged and only oxidizer and diluent gas flow
rates were adjusted. This approach was chosen to ensure that the in-
tensity of the NPF region remained unchanged during the flame
extinction process. For experiments 1.2 to 1.5, no extinction but stable
lifted tri-branchial structures were observed. Detailed flame structures
can be found in the supplemental material. These experiments are listed
to exclude conditions without extinction. Therefore, the following dis-
cussion will only focus on the extinction dynamics of experiment 1.1 and
2.1. Experiment 1.1 refers to a PPF case, and 2.1 refers to a NPF case,
together with corresponding numerical simulations analyzing on the
strain and radiation effects.
3. Numerical simulation setup
The numerical simulation platform for the flame is based on the
MultiDiffFOAM [21], and the numerical simulation governing equation
is time-dependent with detailed transport process including Fickian
diffusion, Soret diffusion and the correction term. Thermal radiation
Qrad is included from radiative source term through the divergence of
radiative heat flux qr [22]:
