4. Results and discussions
The evolution of flame morphology during experiment process from
experiment 1.1 was shown at the bottom of Fig. 6 with an interval of 0.5
s (every 13 frames). This time series is designated as tprocess to differen-
tiate the time associated with extinction process. The flame height Hf
(red circles) and width Wf (blue circles) are plotted with an interval of
0.08 s. Additionally, the average flame temperature Tf, from the GCEI
radiometer, marked as black squares, is recorded every 0.5 s as a qual-
itative indicator of flame extinction. The background colors indicate the
three distinct phases identified based on the flame morphology.
In the stabilization phase (red background), the premixed combus-
tible gas is ignited, forming a well-defined tri-branchial flame structure.
A luminous cone representing the rich PF branch stabilizes at the burner
rim with a transient decrease in flame height. By decreasing the oxidizer
flow rate, the flame entered the tip-open phase (highlighted by the
yellow background; image brightness was increased by two times for
better illustration) where the rich PF branch weakens and is transported
to downstream. Concurrently, the closed flame tip representing the NPF
branch opens. This phenomenon has been previously noted by Hegde
Nyma [12] due to radiation and fuel leakage. The opened flame tip
continues to extend outward until it surpasses the flame width, signaling
the commencement of the extinction phase. The extinction phase is
indicated by the purple background (image brightness increased by
eight times) in Fig. 6, spanning 2.64 s (66 frames) in which the extinc-
tion phenomena are of our interests and hence we define these periods
using t = 0 to t = text, with text defined as the time for a sharp drop in
flame sizes and temperatures. Despite this drop, the flame collapse can
be observed after text, with very transient but still measurable flame
heights and widths.
