NPF region is lifted, heat loss to the burner wall can be ignored. The
open-tip NP flame forms a lifted “ring” structure with hooks at ends
marked by dashed circles, and the hook curvatures are towards the
coflow region and oxidizer sides, at both upper and lower ends of the
branch. After t = text, an abrupt decrease in both flame width and
average temperature marks the end of the extinction phase with the
ring-like flame structure. As shown by the comparison in Fig. 7, simu-
lated OH* distribution attains a good qualitative agreement with the
OH* images in terms of the extinction dynamic and instantaneous flame
structure.
Fig. 8 shows results from experiment 2.1. As shown, it demonstrates
a very similar transformation in flame structure as noted in experiment
1.1, with a tri-branchial flame structure developed from ignition to the
stabilization phase and a tip-open transition phase during the reduction
of oxidizer concentration. The process of tip-opening and flame verti-
cally shrinking is prolonged, followed by a relatively rapid ring-like
extinction.
The extinction of the NPF region in experiment 2.1 (cf. light blue
background in Fig. 8) is observed between 14.36 and 15.32 s. During
this period, the root of the NPF is lifted, followed by the global extinction
of the flame, characterized by the shortening from both ends of the NPF
branch due to local extinction. Then both flame height and width drop to
zero, indicating the final point of extinction. All the flame dynamics
reported were found very symmetric given that the concentric coflow
burner was used and the flow conditions were carefully designed (see
enlarged flame images in the supplemental material). Therefore com-
parison with 2D numerical simulations can be justified.
Fig. 9 illustrates the temporal evolution of the NPF front position
during the extinction phase of experiment 2.1 extracted from numerical
simulation. At t = 0 (blue circle), the NPF region exhibits a positive
curvature towards the fuel side. As extinction progresses, such curvature
substantially flips towards the oxidizer side and leads to a ring-like flame
with ending hooks forming.
Fig. 10(a) and (b) present the evolution of equivalence ratio and
flame index computed using the numerical simulation results along the
NPF branch. As shown, the NPF initially aligns with the stoichiometric
line at t = 0, with its edges spanning a flammability range of 0.6 to 1.1.
In comparison with the flame index shown in Fig. 10(b), the NPF is
diffusive (Gs < 0), but the two ends of this NPF branch appear to
extinguish in a premixed manner (Gs > 0). As extinction progresses, the
equivalence ratio gradually switches to the lean side and narrows to
between 0.85 and 0.92 with the premixed extinction at both ends
driving the collapse of the NPF.
The radiation effect is evaluated using log(Qrad/Qrea) from the nu-
merical simulation, where Qrad is radiative heat loss and Qrea is the local
reaction heat release rate, as shown in Fig. 10 (c). The data along the
NPF are divided into two distinct regions: a non-radiative NPF region,
where radiative heat loss is relatively minimal, and a radiative PPF re-
gion, identified by deviation from a data trend of the NPF region due to
radiative heat loss. During the extinction phase, the radiative PPF region
becomes prominent with radiative heat loss up to 13 %. In case of PPF
(cf. Fig. 11(b)), this loss can be above 40 % up to the extinction, as is
shown in the red and gray points when DaL comes to nearly 0, thus
acting as a critical factor in the evolution of the flame structure [17].
The temporal evolution of the DaL is shown in Fig. 10(d). As the
flame advances towards a co-flow region as shown by blue arrows in
Fig. 9 with lower velocity, DaL decreases. This reduction is further
influenced by a decrease in the heat release rate and expansion rate at
the flame front, leading to the overall reduction of DaL.
Correlations of non-dimensional flame speed SZ/SL and KaL is pre-
sented in Fig. 10(e). On one hand, the diffusion displacement speed
causes expansion of flame volume, increasing the total radiative heat
loss. On the other hand, it is expected that the flame diffusion
displacement speed of the NPF region is influenced by KaL and a cor-
responding Markstein number Ma [30]:
