premixed edges, which eventually triggers the extinction of the entire
flames.
5. Concluding remarks
The paper reported “ring-like” extinction in the experiments of PPFs
aboard the Chinese Space Station. Numerical simulations are conducted
and compared for a better illustration of the flame structure evolution
and the dominating factors for PPF extinction. The principal findings
include:
a. During the PPF extinction, the NPF region exhibits a ring-like
structure with partially premixed hook structures at both ends.
This extinction flame structure has also been shown by numerical
simulations.
b. The edges of NPF region are characterized by a narrowing range of
equivalence ratios and an increasing flame index. Notably, radiative
heat loss for these edges can occupy 40 % of the local heat release.
c. The strain rate effect is analyzed through correlations between
displacement speed and KaL. The analysis reveals an apparently
decreasing Markstein number in the NPF region towards near zero.
This indicates a diminishing influence of the strain rate effect on
flame propagation, leading to a shift towards radiation-controlled
extinction.
d. The radiation effect is further examined through correlations be-
tween normalized radiative heat loss and DaL. The identified critical
DaC for importance of flow effects decreases during extinction. The
maximum radiative heat loss gradually dominates local extinction at
the end of NPF region that exhibits PPF behaviors, ultimately trig-
gering the extinction of the entire NPF region.
Novelty and Significance Statement
The novelty of this research lies in its detailed exploration of the
extinction dynamics of non-premixed flames and partially premixed
flames aboard Chinese Space Station in a microgravity environment, an
area previously unexplored with such depth. It is significant because a
ring-like flame structure from co-flow configuration in microgravity is
first observed and it sheds light on the complex interaction between
radiation and strain effects in flame behavior, especially in partially
premixed flames under co-flow configurations. This study not only
provides new insights into flame structure evolution and the role of
radiation but also enhances our understanding of combustion processes
in microgravity.
