A B S T R A C T
Flame spread over solid fuels in microgravity is a fundamental phenomenon relevant to fire safety in spacecraft 
and space habitats. However, experimental data obtained under sustained microgravity conditions are still very 
limited because access to orbital research platforms is restricted. In the present study, flame spread over filter 
paper was experimentally investigated through orbital combustion experiments conducted aboard the Interna­
tional Space Station (ISS) using the Solid Combustion Experiment Module (SCEM) installed in the Japanese 
Experiment Module “Kibo.” Experiments were performed under various combinations of opposed-flow velocity 
and oxygen concentration to clarify flame spread characteristics in low-flow microgravity environments. Two 
distinct flame spread modes were observed: conventional two-dimensional (2D) flame spread and a unique 
spread mode referred to as flamelet spread. The flamelet spread was characterized by a small flame spreading in a 
zigzag manner along the sample surface and was mainly observed under extremely low-flow conditions near the 
flammability limit. Notably, flame spread was observed even below the limiting oxygen concentration predicted 
by a conventional two-dimensional flame spread model, suggesting that the conventional LOC concept may 
underestimate flammability limits under microgravity conditions. The measured flame spread rates for the 2D 
flame spread were typically 1.0–1.5 mm/s in the thermal regime, whereas the flamelet spread exhibited 
significantly lower spread rates. The preheating length decreased with increasing opposed-flow velocity, and the 
observed trends were reasonably reproduced by theoretical scaling relations. These findings provide new 
experimental insights into flame spread behavior over thin cellulosic fuels in microgravity and contribute to 
improving the understanding of solid fuel combustion relevant to fire safety in future space habitats.
Novelty and significance statement: This study reports orbital combustion experiments on flame spread over 
thermally thin filter paper conducted aboard the International Space Station. The results reveal a distinct flame 
spread mode, termed flamelet spread, which occurs under extremely low-flow conditions near the flammability 
limit and differs fundamentally from conventional two-dimensional flame spread. The observation that flame 
spread can occur even below the limiting oxygen concentration predicted by conventional models suggests that 
existing flammability criteria may underestimate material flammability in microgravity. These findings provide 
new insights into solid-fuel combustion in low-flow microgravity environments.
1. Introduction
Human activities in space are expanding beyond low-Earth orbit 
toward the Moon and eventually Mars, as exemplified by NASA’s 
Artemis program, which aims to return humans to the lunar surface. In 
parallel, private companies are actively developing various human 
spaceflight and commercial space activities. As a result, the frequency 
and duration of human space missions are expected to increase in the 
coming decades. Under such circumstances, ensuring the safety of 
astronauts becomes increasingly important. Three major hazards have 
been identified in human spaceflight environments: rapid depressur­
ization, fire, and air contamination. Among these, fire poses one of the 
most serious threats to crew safety because evacuation to the outside 
environment is impossible and ventilation capability is limited within 
spacecraft and space habitats. For this reason, understanding the com­
bustion behavior of materials under microgravity conditions is essential 
for the development of reliable fire safety technologies in future human 
space exploration. To address this issue, Prof. Fujita (Hokkaido Univ.) 
proposed a research program entitled Fundamental Research on 
