manned mission‒ (FLARE), aimed at improving fire safety in space 
habitats by investigating the influence of gravity on solid material 
combustion [1]. The FLARE project was selected by the Japan Aerospace 
Exploration Agency (JAXA) in 2012 as a utilization theme for the third 
phase of the Japanese Experiment Module “Kibo” aboard the Interna­
tional Space Station (ISS), and it remains ongoing as of May 2026. The 
project involves fourteen research institutions from four countries, 
including major space agencies such as JAXA, NASA, ESA, CNES, and 
DLR. These institutions are organized into four research groups, three of 
which conduct combustion experiments aboard the ISS “Kibo.” On May 
19, 2022, the first orbital combustion experiment on filter paper was 
successfully conducted within the FLARE project [2–4].
Flame spread over solid materials has been extensively studied for 
more than six decades in the fields of combustion science and fire safety. 
Many previous studies have focused on polymers, particularly 
polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) [5–7], to investigate the influence of 
material properties, sample dimensions, and environmental conditions 
on flame spread behavior. Various theoretical and numerical models 
have also been proposed, and their validity has been examined through 
comparisons with experimental data [8–10]. Flame spread over filter 
paper has also been widely investigated as a representative thermally 
thin cellulosic fuel. For example, Fernandez-Pello et al. measured flame 
spread rates over filter paper under various opposed-flow velocities and 
oxygen concentrations and discussed the governing mechanisms using 
the Damk¨ohler number [7]. Olson et al. investigated opposed-flow 
diffusion flames over thermally thin cellulosic fuels under quiescent or 
low-flow microgravity conditions and showed that the flammability 
limit consists of blow-off and extinction branches [11]. Duh et al. 
numerically studied flame spread over cellulosic materials and demon­
strated that extinction in low-oxygen regimes is primarily caused by 
radiative heat losses from the fuel surface [12]. Suzuki et al. investigated 
