theoretical model for opposed-flow flame spread and quantitatively 
evaluated extinction and spread characteristics in both opposed and 
concurrent flow configurations [15]. Bhattacharjee et al. experimentally 
demonstrated that the growth of the boundary layer significantly in­
fluences the flame spread rate in opposed-flow flame spread over ther­
mally thin filter paper [16]. Avinash et al. investigated diffusion flames 
spreading over thin cellulosic paper under both concurrent and opposed 
flow configurations in gravity-induced flows [17]. Wang et al. examined 
instabilities near extinction limits in concurrent-flow flame spread when 
the oxidizer flow velocity falls below a certain threshold [18].
Despite these extensive studies, experimental knowledge of flame 
spread over filter paper under microgravity conditions remains very 
limited. This is mainly because access to microgravity research plat­
forms is restricted, and the duration of microgravity provided by 
ground-based facilities such as drop towers is too short to investigate 
relatively slow phenomena such as flame spread over solid fuels. 
Therefore, experimental data obtained in orbital platforms are 
extremely valuable for understanding flame spread phenomena in the 
absence of buoyancy-induced flows.
The present study reports experimental results of flame spread over 
filter paper obtained aboard the ISS “Kibo.” Particular attention is given 
to key flame spread characteristics, including the limiting oxygen con­
centration (LOC), flame spread rate, and preheat zone length. The 
findings provide new insights into flame spread behavior under low-flow 
microgravity conditions and contribute to improving the understanding 
of solid fuel combustion relevant to fire safety in future space habitats.
2. Experimental setup and procedure
Experiments on flame spread over filter paper (ADVANTEC, hard­
ened filter paper No.4A) under microgravity conditions were conducted 
using the Solid Combustion Experiment Module (SCEM) installed in the 
Japanese Experiment Module “Kibo” aboard the ISS, as shown in Fig. 1
(a). The SCEM was developed by the JAXA in collaboration with IHI 
Inspection & Instrumentation Co., Ltd., and was launched to the ISS on 
May 21, 2020. The SCEM system consists of a combustion chamber, a 
gas supply and exhaust unit, a power and communication control unit, 
and multiple optical diagnostics. The combustion chamber has internal 
dimensions of 220 × 375 × 550 mm (L × H × W). Experimental devices 
referred to as “experiment inserts” can be installed inside the chamber, 
as shown in Fig. 1(b), allowing combustion experiments on various 
materials to be performed by replacing the insert.
For the present study, a flat-plate insert (Insert 2) designed for flame 
spread experiments over planar samples was used. The insert is equip­
ped with a circulation fan capable of generating a forced flow of up to 25 
cm/s in the direction indicated in Fig. 1(c). The flow velocity can also be 
varied during the experiment through a preprogrammed sequence. The 
test section is located at the center of the upper part of the insert, and 
honeycomb flow straighteners are installed on both sides to ensure a 
nearly uniform flow field in the test region. The pressure and oxygen 
concentration inside the combustion chamber are controlled by regu­
lating the supply rates of nitrogen and oxygen gases. The maximum 
operating conditions are 101.3 kPa and an oxygen concentration of 45%. 
During the experiments, the chamber pressure, oxygen concentration, 
and temperature were continuously monitored and recorded by onboard 
sensors. Even when the filter paper burned completely, the decrease in 
oxygen concentration was less than approximately 1%, indicating that 
the ambient conditions remained nearly constant during the tests. Two 
types of sample cards with different geometries were prepared to hold 
the filter paper specimens, as shown in Fig. 1(d). These configurations 
enabled flame spread experiments to be conducted for specimen widths 
of 20 mm and 40 mm, while the specimen length and thickness were 
fixed at 130 mm and 0.12 mm, respectively.
All flame spread experiments were carried out automatically ac­
cording to a sequence program prepared on the ground. The sample card 
was first extracted from a storage cartridge and transported to the test 
section using a vertical elevator mechanism. Ignition was achieved using 
a red-hot Kanthal wire with a thickness of 0.4 mm. Igniters were 
installed at both the upstream and downstream ends of the sample; 
ignition at the upstream end produced concurrent-flow flame spread, 
whereas ignition at the downstream end generated opposed-flow flame 
spread. After completion of each experiment, the sample card was 
removed from the test section by the elevator and returned to a recovery 
