behavior was recorded from multiple viewing angles using five cameras 
mounted on the top of the combustion chamber: three digital cameras 
(SVS-VISTEK, evo8051CFLGEA), a high-speed camera (IDT, Os10-4K), 
and an infrared camera (Vision Sensing, VIM-640G2U). A green LED 
was used as an external light source for visible imaging. As a result, the 
unburned region of the filter paper appears green in the captured im­
ages. The LED was operated in a blinking mode rather than continuous 
illumination, allowing the acquisition of both illuminated images (for 
observing the sample surface and char structure) and non-illuminated 
images (for clearly capturing the flame emission).
3. Results and discussion
3.1. Flame spread behavior
Figure 2 shows snapshots of the flame spread behavior over filter 
paper in microgravity under different opposed-flow velocities and oxy­
gen concentrations, captured by both visible and infrared cameras. In 
Fig. 2(a), a two-dimensional planar flame extends across the entire 
width of the sample and spreads steadily. Such behavior is commonly 
observed in ground-based flame spread experiments. In contrast, Fig. 2
(b) shows a markedly different spread mode: the flame becomes small 
and nearly spherical and spreads while meandering in a zigzag manner. 
This behavior has not been observed in ground experiments and re­
sembles the fingering behavior typically reported in smoldering com­
bustion. Fingering phenomena have also been reported in flaming 
combustion under specific conditions, such as in narrow gaps or 
microgravity environments [19–21]. Under such conditions, fingering 
instability—namely diffusive–thermal instability—can be enhanced by 
factors such as low oxygen concentration, large heat losses, and low flow 
velocity. As a result, the flame front splits into multiple flamelets, 
forming a characteristic fingering pattern. The flamelet spread observed 
in the present study shares several qualitative features with fingering 
behavior, including the emergence of localized flame structures under 
low-oxygen and low-flow conditions. However, unlike conventional 
fingering, in which the flame front splits into multiple branches, the 
present observations show that the flame front contracts into a single 
flamelet that spreads while exhibiting fingering-like motion. In addition, 
the present observations are limited to macroscopic flame spread over a 
solid fuel, and a direct identification of the underlying instability 
mechanism is beyond the scope of this study. Therefore, while a possible 
connection to fingering-type instability is suggested, further investiga­
tion is required to clarify the governing mechanism. In the present study, 
this spread mode is referred to as “flamelet spread,” in contrast to the 
conventional planar flame spread, which is hereafter referred to as “2D 
flame spread.”
Figure 3 classifies the observed flame spread behaviors under various 
combinations of opposed-flow velocity and oxygen concentration. Ar­
rows connecting the data points indicate sequential experiments con­
ducted using the same sample. The dashed line represents the 
flammability limit predicted by solving Eq. (1), which was derived from 
a scaling analysis of the energy balance equation based on a two- 
dimensional flame spread model for thermally thin materials [22–26]. 
This limit corresponds to the limiting oxygen concentration (LOC) for 
each opposed-flow velocity. 
