The orbital combustion experiments were mainly conducted under 
conditions close to the predicted flammability limit in order to validate 
the LOC prediction. When the oxygen concentration is higher than the 
predicted LOC, 2D flame spread is predominantly observed. In contrast, 
near the LOC—and even below it—flamelet spread becomes increas­
ingly dominant. It is particularly noteworthy that flame spread was 
observed even below the predicted LOC, including the condition of zero 
opposed-flow velocity (0 cm/s), without extinction. The flame spread 
model used to predict the LOC was originally developed under the 
assumption of two-dimensional flame spread and does not account for 
three-dimensional spread modes such as flamelet spread. In the present 
study, the observed flamelet spread is regarded as a form of sustained 
flame spread, although it differs from the conventional 2D mode, as 
continuous spread lasting over one minute was observed. If flamelet 
spread is treated as a form of flame spread, the flammability of filter 
paper predicted using Eq. (1) is underestimated. Consequently, the 
flammability limit in the low-flow regime should be extended beyond 
the prediction of the present model. It should be noted that the predicted 
LOC is derived from Eq. (1), which is based on a two-dimensional flame 
spread model, and therefore represents the minimum oxygen concen­
tration at which 2D flame spread can be sustained. Accordingly, the 
observation of flamelet spread below the predicted LOC arises from 
comparing a different spread mode with the LOC corresponding to 2D 
flame spread. In this sense, the discrepancy reflects the limitation of the 
model assumptions rather than a fundamental limitation of the LOC 
concept itself. These results suggest that the conventional LOC concept 
derived from two-dimensional flame spread models may not be suffi­
cient to describe flame spread behavior in microgravity, and that addi­
tional spread modes not captured by such models may need to be 
