3.2. Flame spread rate
The flame spread rate was determined by tracking the flame leading 
edge in the recorded videos and calculating the slope of the flame 
displacement with respect to time. For both the 2D flame spread and the 
flamelet spread, a clear linear relationship between flame position and 
time was observed, indicating that the flame spread had reached a 
steady state. In the flamelet mode, the convex flame leading edge was 
tracked frame by frame to obtain its displacement over time; the 
resulting linearity confirms quasi-steady propagation, and the spread 
rate was determined from its slope. Therefore, the reported flamelet 
spread rate represents the actual propagation of the flame leading edge, 
rather than a projected velocity along the sample axis. As shown in 
Fig. 2b, although the flamelet spread propagates in a zigzag manner, the 
spread rate remained nearly constant, suggesting quasi-steady flame 
spread. Representative plots of the tracked flame leading edge are pre­
sented in the Appendix.
Figure 4 presents the measured flame spread rates under various 
conditions. For the 2D flame spread, the spread rate was mostly higher 
than 1 mm/s. Furthermore, when the opposed-flow velocity exceeded 
approximately 10–15 cm/s, the flame spread rates were distributed 
within a narrow range of 1.0–1.5 mm/s and showed little dependence on 
the opposed-flow velocity.
In general, opposed-flow flame spread is categorized into three re­
gimes: the radiative (or microgravity) regime [27], the thermal regime 
[8,28], and the kinetic regime [29]. In the thermal regime, the effects of 
radiative heat loss from the preheated solid surface and finite-rate 
chemical kinetics are relatively small. When these two effects are 
neglected, the theoretical flame spread rate in the thermal regime be­
comes independent of the opposed-flow velocity. Based on this frame­
work, the filter paper used in the present study is considered to enter the 
thermal regime at opposed-flow velocities of approximately 10–15 
cm/s. Conversely, conditions with opposed-flow velocities below 
approximately 10 cm/s correspond to the radiative (microgravity) 
regime, where radiative heat losses become significant.
On the other hand, the flame spread rates for the flamelet spread 
were mainly lower than 1 mm/s. Such low flame spread rates have also 
been reported in previous studies investigating flame spread over filter 
paper in narrow channels or under microgravity conditions [19,21]. As 
described in the previous section, the flamelet spread was frequently 
observed under extremely low-flow conditions and near or even below 
the estimated flame spread limit. Under such conditions, the preheated 
region ahead of the flame becomes extended, which enhances radiative 
heat losses from the solid surface. In addition, the heat transfer from the 
