Using Eq. (1), the theoretical flame spread rate can be estimated. 
Figure 5a shows the calculated map of the theoretical flame spread rates 
within the predicted flammability limits obtained from Eq. (1). 
Figure 5b presents the theoretical flame spread rates as a function of 
opposed-flow velocity for oxygen concentrations ranging from 15% to 
60%. In Fig. 4, the flame spread rates measured at opposed-flow ve­
locities higher than 10–15 cm/s are considered to be in the thermal 
regime, where the corresponding oxygen concentrations are approxi­
mately 15–17%. In Fig. 5b, the curve for an oxygen concentration of 
15% exhibits a maximum around an opposed-flow velocity of approxi­
mately 10 cm/s, and the variation in flame spread rate between 10 and 
15 cm/s is very small. This behavior is consistent with the characteristic 
feature of the thermal regime in which the flame spread rate becomes 
nearly independent of the opposed-flow velocity. Therefore, the onset of 
the thermal regime inferred from the experimental observations agrees 
well with the theoretically predicted transition.
Figure 6 compares the experimentally measured flame spread rates 
with the theoretical predictions. Since Eq. (1) provides the flame spread 
rate only within the flammable region (i.e., at oxygen concentrations 
above the LOC), the data obtained outside the flammability limits shown 
in Fig. 3 are not included in this comparison. Accordingly, the validation 
presented in Fig. 6 is limited to conditions within the flammable region. 
The gray shaded region represents a ±20% deviation band. Although 
some scatter exists depending on the conditions, the experimental and 
theoretical values are of the same order of magnitude and show 
reasonably good agreement overall. These results demonstrate that Eq. 
(1) provides not only a reliable prediction of the flammability limits 
but also a reasonable estimation of the flame spread rates under the 
present microgravity conditions within its applicable range.
3.3. Preheating length
Similar to the evaluation of the flame spread rate in the previous 
section, the present study quantified the preheat zone by measuring the 
length of the preheat region, namely the preheating length. Figure 7
shows representative examples of the preheating length for the cases of 
2D flame spread and flamelet spread. In this study, the location where 
the sample surface temperature reached 300◦C was defined as the 
leading edge of the preheat zone, and the distance from this position to 
the flame leading edge was defined as the preheating length. This 
definition is based on infrared image analysis, where a clear temperature 
gradient was observed and the temperature at the front of this region 
was approximately 300◦C.
Figure 8 presents the preheating lengths under various experimental 
conditions. The preheating length monotonically decreased with 
increasing opposed-flow velocity. Around the boundary between the 
radiative (microgravity) regime and the thermal regime, estimated to be 
approximately 10–15 cm/s as discussed in the previous section, the 
preheating length ranged from approximately 5–10 mm in the lower 
velocity region to about 2–5 mm in the higher velocity region. This 
transition appears more clearly in the preheating length than in the 
flame spread rate, indicating that the size of the preheat zone is a key 
parameter characterizing the governing flame spread mechanism. The 
radiative heat loss from the preheated sample surface to the surround­
ings strongly depends on the size of the preheat zone. Therefore, when 
the opposed-flow velocity is below approximately 10 cm/s, the preheat 
zone becomes significantly larger, resulting in a substantial increase in 
radiative heat loss. The present results therefore support the interpre­
tation that conditions below 10 cm/s correspond to the radiative 
(microgravity) regime. This classification is also consistent with the 
regime transition inferred from the flame spread rate results.
The solid lines in Fig. 8 represent the theoretical preheating lengths 
calculated from the scaling relation given by Eq. (5) [3,4], evaluated for 
oxygen concentrations between 10% and 40% at each opposed-flow 
velocity. As shown in Eq. (5), the preheating length is a function of 
both opposed-flow velocity and oxygen concentration (since Pr ––– νg/αg, 
and both νg and αg depend on the oxygen concentration). Therefore, a 
map similar to that presented for the flame spread rate in Fig. 5 can also 
be constructed for the preheating length. 
