The model based on Eq. (5) successfully reproduces the dependence 
of the preheating length on the opposed-flow velocity. The theoretical 
analysis further indicates that the influence of oxygen concentration on 
the preheating length is weaker than that of the opposed-flow velocity, 
which is consistent with the experimental observations. Although some 
scatter exists in the experimental data, the predicted values are of the 
same order of magnitude as the measurements, indicating that the 
model reasonably captures the scale of the preheat zone. It should be 
noted that Eq. (5) was derived from a scaling analysis and therefore 
represents a characteristic length that describes the proportional re­
lationships among the governing parameters, rather than guaranteeing 
quantitative agreement in absolute values. In principle, a correction 
factor would be required to achieve quantitative agreement with the 
experimental data. However, since the objective of this study is to 
examine the validity of the scaling relation itself, no correction factor 
