unity). Nevertheless, the reasonable agreement observed in Fig. 8
without introducing a correction factor suggests that the scaling cap­
tures the dominant physical processes, and that the experimental defi­
nition of the preheating length based on a 300◦C threshold is, in 
practice, consistent with the theoretical characteristic length.
Finally, the present model was derived under the assumption of two- 
dimensional flame spread. Consequently, the model predicts an infinite 
preheating length at an opposed-flow velocity of 0 cm/s and does not 
provide a valid solution under this condition. To describe the preheating 
length observed in the flamelet spread near 0 cm/s, the model must be 
extended to account for the actual flame geometry and three- 
dimensional structure.
4. Conclusions
Flame spread over thermally thin filter paper was experimentally 
investigated under microgravity conditions aboard the ISS using the 
SCEM facility installed in the Japanese Experiment Module “Kibo.” The 
major findings of the present study are summarized as follows. 
(1) Two distinct flame spread modes were observed in the orbital 
combustion experiments: conventional two-dimensional (2D) 
flame spread and a unique spread mode referred to as flamelet 
spread. The latter was characterized by a small flame spreading in 
a zigzag manner and was mainly observed under extremely low- 
flow conditions near the flammability limit.
(2) Flame spread was observed even below the limiting oxygen 
concentration predicted by the conventional two-dimensional 
flame spread model. This result suggests that the conventional 
LOC concept derived from two-dimensional flame spread theory 
may underestimate the flammability limit under microgravity 
conditions.
(3) The measured flame spread rates for the 2D flame spread were 
typically 1.0–1.5 mm/s at opposed-flow velocities above 
approximately 10–15 cm/s, corresponding to the thermal regime. 
In contrast, the flamelet spread exhibited significantly lower 
flame spread rates, generally below 1 mm/s.
(4) The preheating length decreased monotonically with increasing 
opposed-flow velocity, and the transition between the radiative 
(microgravity) regime and the thermal regime was clearly re­
flected in the size of the preheat zone. The theoretical scaling 
relations reasonably reproduced the order of magnitude and ve­
locity dependence of both the flame spread rate and the pre­
heating length.
These findings highlight the importance of considering alternative 
flame spread modes when evaluating material flammability in micro­
gravity environments and provide useful insights for improving fire 
safety strategies in future space habitats.
