doi:10.1016/j.proci.2026.106327,doi,Numerical study of concurrent flow flame spread over thin solids in normoxic conditions in microgravity,,0,"In future space missions (e.g., NASA’s Artemis mission to go to the Moon), astronauts will spend longer and longer duration in space vehicles. To ensure mission success and crew member safety, a complete understanding of how fire spreads in microgravity is essential. This is especially true for enhanced oxygen and reduced pressure environments, i.e., the future space exploration atmospheres considered by NASA. In this study, two-dimensional transient Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) simulations are performed to understand concurrent-flow flame spread over a thin solid sample in microgravity in different ambient environments. The sample material, model geometry, and ambient conditions are chosen based on NASA’s recent large-scale space experiments, Saffire IV and Saffire VI. The model predicts the flame spread rates, flame lengths, and solid surface temperatures well in both experiments. The model also captures the higher flame spread rate in Saffire VI, despite a lower partial pressure of oxygen compared to Saffire IV. Parametric studies are then conducted to investigate the individual effects of oxygen and pressure on the flame spread and heat transfer processes. Dependencies of both convective and radiative heat fluxes on the environmental conditions are proposed. It is revealed that oxygen plays a more important role than pressure. Reducing pressure while enhancing oxygen leads to an increase in both heat fluxes. This is especially true for radiation. As a result, as the oxygen percentage increases along a normoxic curve, the dominant heat transfer mode transits from convection to radiation. The ratio between the two heat transfer modes is mapped against pressure and oxygen. Flame spread rate correlations are proposed for both heat transfer regimes. Compared to previous correlations in the literature, this approach balances complexity and prediction capability. Novelty and significance statement: Flame spread over a solid material under microgravity conditions has been recognized as a significant fire hazard for astronauts. In recent NASA spacecraft fire experiments (Saffire IV and VI), an unexpected trend of flame spread rate was observed in an enhanced oxygen and reduced pressure environment, highlighting the need for a robust model for the combined effects of ambient conditions. This study, by establishing correlations between flame heat input and ambient conditions, closes the gap between qualitative flame observations and quantitative heat transfer analyses. In addition, this study provides a means to identify the dominant heat transfer mechanism and explain why flames spread faster in reduced pressure and enhanced oxygen normoxic conditions, i.e., the planned exploration atmosphere in next-generation space vehicles. Through these, this study provides predictive tools for spacecraft atmosphere design and contributes to broader modeling efforts of radiative–conductive coupling in microgravity combustion."
