A R T I C L E I N F O
Keywords:
Limiting oxygen concentration
Flame spread
Extinction
Microgravity
Thickness
A B S T R A C T
An experimental study is presented of the path from ignition to extinction during opposed flame spread over 
cylindrical black PMMA rods in sealed environments, with emphasis on the effects of fuel diameter, pressure, and 
gravity. Experiments were conducted at sub-atmospheric pressures in a sealed combustion chamber on the 
Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) of the International Space Station (ISS), and in a similar chamber in normal 
gravity with three diameters tested in both gravity conditions. As the fuel burned in the sealed chamber, the 
oxygen concentration decreased naturally at nearly constant pressure, allowing the evolution of the flame from 
opposed spread to regression burning and eventual extinction to be tracked continuously. Two distinct limiting 
oxygen concentrations were identified: the limiting oxygen concentration for flame spread, LOC-FS, at which 
forward propagation ceases, and the limiting oxygen concentration for fuel regression burning, LOC-FR, at which 
the regressing flame finally extinguishes. Both limits increase as ambient pressure is reduced and are consistently 
higher in normal gravity than in microgravity. Fuel diameter introduces a different effect: increasing diameter 
raises LOC-FS but lowers LOC-FR, thereby widening the oxygen interval between spread cessation and final 
extinction, whereas smaller diameters bring the two limits closer together. The flame spread rate is also higher 
for smaller diameters and in microgravity at the same oxygen concentration and pressure. These results show 
that extinction of thick solid fuels cannot be characterized by a single limiting oxygen concentration and that fuel 
size affects the transition from flame spread to regression burning. These findings suggest that Earth based 
flammability screening alone may not capture all relevant near-limit burning behavior for spacecraft environ­
ments, particularly the distinction between flame spread cessation and continued regression burning under 
reduced pressure and altered gravity conditions.
Novelty and significance statement: Opposed flame spread over PMMA rods in sealed environments proceeds 
through two sequential related events: cessation of forward spread at LOC-FS and final extinction during 
regression burning at LOC-FR. Results show that reducing pressure raises both limits and lowers the FSR, 
bringing both limiting oxygen concentrations higher, and the rate of spread lower in 1 g compared to micro­
gravity. Also, fuel size affects the two limits in opposite directions: increasing diameter raises LOC-FS but lowers 
LOC-FR, widening the interval between spread cessation and final extinction, revealing that diameter does not 
only shift flammability markers; it changes the relative persistence of flame during spreading and regression 
burning. These coupled trends suggest that for near-limit burning of thick solids, under sub-atmospheric pressure 
and different gravity conditions, flammability is better characterized by a combination of both LOCs, and by their 
separation, rather than by a single metric.
1. Introduction
Flame spread over solid fuels is a key indicator of material flam­
mability because it determines whether a localized ignition develops 
into a growing fire [1]. In opposed flow spread over thick solids, the 
advancing flame preheats the virgin material ahead of the pyrolysis 
front while leaving behind a regressing burning surface. These two 
stages, flame spread (FS) and subsequent fuel regression burning (FR), 
