doi:10.1016/j.proci.2026.106460,doi,Gravity and thickness effects on the path from ignition to extinction in opposed flame spread over PMMA Rods,2,1,"define the path to extinction through two distinct limiting oxygen con­
centrations (LOCs) [2–5]. As oxygen decreases, the flame spread rate 
declines until the limiting oxygen concentration for flame spread, 
LOC-FS, is reached and forward propagation ceases. Beyond this point, 
surface regression, i.e., fuel pyrolysis and its associated combustion, may 
continue under a weaker hovering flame until a second limit is reached, 
referred to here as LOC-FR, corresponding to extinction of the regression 
flame [2–5]. Although this FS–FR sequence has been identified in pre­
vious studies, its transition dynamics remain less well quantified, 
particularly when fuel size is varied together with environmental con­
ditions [2–4].
This problem is especially relevant in confined environments, where 
combustion naturally depletes the available oxygen and allows the 
progression from flame spread to regression burning and extinction to be 
observed within a single experiment [2,3–6]. Previous studies have 
shown that ambient pressure and gravity significantly affect flame 
spread [4,7] and the LOC at extinction [8–10]. In contrast, the influence 
of fuel size on near-limit flame spread behavior remains less well 
quantified. For thermally thick fuels, geometric dimensions such as 
thickness or diameter affect the thermal response of the solid and may 
also modify the flame structure and surrounding flow field [4,7,11,12]. 
As a result, fuel diameter may influence not only the flame spread rate 
but also the separation between the two extinction limits.
In this study, opposed flame spread over thick cylindrical PMMA is 
examined under sub-atmospheric pressure in both normal gravity (1 g) 
and microgravity (μg). By tracking the oxygen-depletion path from 
ignition to extinction, the effects of pressure, gravity, and fuel diameter 
on flame spread and on the two limiting oxygen concentrations, LOC-FS 
and LOC-FR, are quantified. Particular attention is given to how diam­
eter changes the oxygen interval between cessation of spread and final 
extinction.
2. Experimental methods
Normal gravity experiments were conducted in a dedicated 1 g 
apparatus for assessing solid fuel flammability under controlled condi­
tions (Fig. 1). The setup consists of a rectangular flow duct with a 125 ×
125 mm cross-section and 600 mm length housed inside a 105 L pressure 
chamber. A cylindrical fuel sample is mounted vertically at the center of 
the test section, and a wire-mesh section upstream is used to straighten 
the flow. An axial fan located below the test section provides an upward 
opposed flow of 10 cm/s, representative of the nominal ventilation ve­
locity in spacecraft environments such as the International Space Sta­
tion’s HVAC system [4]. Black cast PMMA cylinders were tested at 
chamber pressures from 40 to 100 kPa. Diameters of 6.4, 9.5, and 12.7 
mm were examined in both 1 g and μg, while additional 20 and 28 mm 
cylinders were tested only in 1 g. A schematic view of the apparatus is 
shown in Fig. 1. Further details of the 1 g apparatus are given in [4].
Microgravity experiments were performed in the Material Ignition 
and Suppression Test (MIST) apparatus within the Solid Fuel Ignition 
and Extinction (SoFIE) facility on the International Space Station 
[13–15]. The system is contained in a sealed combustion chamber in­
tegrated into the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) in the Destiny 
module. The 1 g and μg test section and flow configuration closely 
mirror each other, including the nominal 10 cm/s opposed flow. The ISS 
hardware is designed for remote operation from NASA Glenn Research 
Center with minimal crew involvement.
The experimental procedure was similar in 1 g and μg. Before each 
test, the chamber was sealed and brought to the target pressure by 
vacuum pumping (1 g) or controlled venting (μg). Ignition was initiated 
with an electrically heated nichrome wire at the top of the sample. 
During burning, all chamber inlets and outlets remained closed, so ox­
ygen decreased naturally due to PMMA combustion while the exhaust 
gases were recirculated in the chamber by the fan.
The gas temperature in the chamber was not measured with a vali­
dated spatially resolved diagnostic. Therefore, the oxygen concentration 
reported here should be interpreted as the bulk, chamber-averaged ox­
ygen mole fraction from the recirculating gas feeding the test section, 
while the local flame environment may include transient variations in 
gas temperature and combustion product composition. Because recir­
culation promotes mixing but does not eliminate local depletion near the 
flame, this chamber-averaged value may differ transiently from the local 
near-flame oxygen level: oxygen consumption in the reaction zone and 
finite mixing within the recirculating chamber. This uncertainty may 
shift the absolute LOC values, but the same measurement definition was 
used for all tests, and the measured trends with pressure, gravity, and 
diameter were systematic. Thus, while the reported values should not be 
interpreted as local extinction concentrations, the main conclusions 
regarding the relative effects of pressure, gravity, and diameter remain 
unchanged.
Additional uncertainty in the reported LOC values arises from the 
oxygen-sensor accuracy, the temporal resolution associated with iden­
tifying the transition frame, spatial and temporal fluctuations in the 
recirculating chamber environment, and test-to-test repeatability. The 
observed variability was generally larger at lower pressure and for larger 
diameters, where near-limit flame motion and the regression-burning 
stage were less steady. Chamber pressure was monitored continuously 
and maintained within approximately +2 kPa of the set point. For 
clarity, throughout this work, oxygen concentration refers to the gas 
phase oxygen mole fraction, XO2, reported in %O2, numerically equiv­
alent to oxygen volume percent for an ideal gas mixture.
3. Results
3.1. T"
doi:10.1016/j.proci.2026.106460,doi,Gravity and thickness effects on the path from ignition to extinction in opposed flame spread over PMMA Rods,3,2,"sealed, combustion progressively depletes the available oxygen. As a 
result, the flame front position (yf), flame spread rate (Vf), and chamber- 
averaged oxygen concentration evolve together along a continuous path 
from ignition to extinction.
At a certain point, the flame ceases to propagate, Vf = 0; this tran­
sition limit is the LOC for flame spread, LOC-FS and represents the 
maximum distance propagated by the flame, yf = ymax. Subsequently, 
PMMA combustion may continue in a fuel-regression mode. During this 
transition, the flame no longer advances over virgin material but retreats 
slightly and stabilizes near the downstream stagnation region behind the 
former pyrolysis front. The solid then continues to regress primarily in 
the radial direction, and the conical burned region decreases in height. 
As oxygen continues to decrease, the regression flame weakens and 
eventually disappears; the oxygen mole fraction at this final visible 
extinction is defined as LOC-FR. A visual description is shown in Fig. 2, 
for a 100 kPa ambient pressure and normal gravity.
Fig. 3 highlights the effect of fuel diameter in the flame spread pa­
rameters. In both 1 g and μg, smaller rods exhibit longer flame travel 
distances before spread cessation and require longer times to reach both 
LOC-FS and LOC-FR. For all three diameters, these times are longer in μg 
than in 1 g. The figure also shows that, at 80 kPa, LOC-FS increases with 
increasing diameter, whereas LOC-FR shifts to lower oxygen as diameter 
increases.
Fig. 3 further shows that the oxygen interval between LOC-FS and 
LOC-FR narrows as diameter decreases in both gravity conditions, 
indicating that the regression-burning stage becomes progressively 
shorter for smaller rods. The amount of oxygen depleted between spread 
cessation and final extinction also decreases as diameter is reduced, 
showing that larger rods occupy a larger portion of the measured 
oxygen-depletion path in the regression-burning regime.
These trends indicate that fuel diameter influences not only the 
propagation stage, but also the persistence of burning after flame spread 
ceases. At a given diameter, the spreading flame is slower in 1 g than in 
μg throughout the propagation stage. For both normal and microgravity, 
the flame spreads faster over thinner diameters. The effects of pressure 
have already been reported in detail in ref [16], and the broader inter­
action among pressure, gravity, and diameter are addressed in the 
following sections.
3.2. Limiting oxygen concentrations
Fig. 4 summarizes the measured limiting oxygen concentrations for 
all pressures, diameters, and gravity conditions tested, for spreading, 
LOC-FS (squared bars in left panel), and fuel regression burning, LOC-FR 
(triangular bars in right panel). LOC-FR is the final visible flame 
extinction limit in the present configuration and is therefore more 
analogous to the extinction criterion recorded in Oxygen Index type tests 
[17] than LOC-FS, although the present opposed flow sealed chamber 
configuration differs fundamentally from the ASTM LOI apparatus. It 
should be noted that in normal gravity, the nominal imposed 10 cm/s 
flow velocity is mixed with a flame induced buoyant velocity in the same 
opposed flow direction, so the local flow experienced by the flame is 
mixed forced–buoyant rather than purely forced. Buoyancy induced 
velocities of order 30 cm/s, with values depending on configuration, 
have been reported for similar PMMA cylinder flame spread conditions, 
indicating that the buoyant contribution can be comparable to or larger 
than the imposed forced velocity [7,10,16,18]. In μg, buoyancy is sup­
pressed and the opposed flow is primarily the nominal forced flow [19]. 
Thus, the comparison between 1 g and μg is interpreted as a comparison 
at matched nominal imposed fan velocity, rather than at matched 
effective local opposed flow velocity.
The z-axis in Fig. 4 is intentionally inverted to help understanding 
the data, so taller bars indicate a lower oxygen mole fraction at the limit, 
i.e., conditions where the flame persisted to lower XO2 before spread 
cessation or final extinction due to oxygen consumption. For all cases, 
LOC-FS is higher than LOC-FR, consistent with the two limits sequence 
introduced above: forward propagation ceases first, while a weaker 
regression flame may persist at lower oxygen before final extinction. 
Both limits increase as the ambient pressure is reduced in the tested 
range from 100 to 40 kPa, indicating that sub-atmospheric conditions 
make both flame spread and regression burning more difficult to sustain.
For the three diameters, both measured LOC-FS and LOC-FR are 
consistently higher in 1 g than in μg at the same pressure, indicating that 
both cessation of spread and final flame extinction occur at higher ox­
ygen concentration in normal gravity. The higher 1 g limits are consis­
tent with the additional buoyancy-driven opposed component, which 
increase convective heat losses and modify flame attachment relative to 
the purely forced-flow μg configuration. This indicates that in 1 g 
buoyancy pushes the LOC limit toward the blow-off regime of flame 
spread [20].
In all cases tested the LOC-FS measured is higher than the LOC-FR. 
For example, for the 12.7 mm samples, at 80 kPa and normal gravity, 
the LOC-FS is 19.21% while the LOC-FR is 18.01%. Similarly in micro­
gravity, for the same conditions, the LOC-FS is 16.4% while the LOC-FR 
is 15.4%. It is notable that the LOC-FR is significantly lower than the 
LOC-FS, which emphasizes that the flame spread process is more sen­
sitive to the ambient parameters than the fuel regression burning. This is 
relevant since a spreading flame has an influence on the growth of fire 
while the burning regression of the fuel influences the heat release rate.
Fuel diameter affects the two limits in opposite directions. In 1 g, 
LOC-FS increases monotonically with diameter, whereas LOC-FR de­
