3.3. The rate of flame spread
The flame spread rate (FSR) during the path from ignition to 
extinction, shown in Fig. 5, was obtained from the local slope of the 
flame front position as a function of time of Fig. 3. Fig. 5 shows the 
evolution of FSR with oxygen concentration for the three diameters 
common to both gravity conditions in μg, with one subplot for each 
diameter and all four pressures included in each panel.
As seen in Fig. 5, in all cases tested, the flame spread rate decay 
continuously as oxygen is depleted and approaches zero at the corre­
sponding LOC-FS, marked by the vertical dashed line. Lower pressure 
shifts the FSR curves downward due to the flame stand-off distance to 
the solid surface increasing as the pressure is decreased [20], indicating 
slower spread throughout the oxygen depletion path, and also shifts 
LOC-FS to higher oxygen concentrations. Thus, in μg, reducing pressure 
both lowers the spreading rate and shortens the available oxygen win­
dow for propagation.
At a given pressure and oxygen level, reducing the rod diameter 
shifts the FSR to higher values in μg, consistent with previous studies 
showing that thinner PMMA cylinders spread faster under opposed flow 
conditions and that, in the broader thermally thick limit, the size 
dependence weakens as the fuel approaches an asymptotic thick fuel 
behavior [22–25]. Note that in Fig. 5 not all tests began at 21% O2. In 
some low-pressure cases, ignition was initiated at a lower initial oxygen 
concentration to ensure that extinction occurred before the flame front 
reached the end of the sample. This adjustment was necessary because of 
the practical constraints of chamber volume, sample length, and 
CIR-SoFIE operations. This was considered appropriate because tests 
performed with different initial oxygen levels produced similar FSR–XO2 
trajectories.
The corresponding 1 g curves for the common diameters show the 
same qualitative behavior, but with systematically lower FSR values 
than in μg at the same pressure. For simplification of presentation, Fig. 6
presents only the 1 g results at 100 kPa for the full diameter range, from 
6.4 to 28 mm, to highlight the diameter effect more clearly. At this 
pressure, the FSR decreases monotonically with increasing diameter 
over the entire oxygen range. At the same time, the vertical LOC-FS 
marker shifts to higher oxygen as diameter increases, showing that 
larger rods not only spread more slowly but also cease propagation 
earlier in the oxygen depletion path. The corresponding LOC-FR marker 
moves to lower oxygen, so the oxygen interval between the two limits 
becomes progressively wider as diameter increases, consistent with the 
LOC trends discussed above.
To compare the diameter effect, Fig. 7 shows FSR at a fixed oxygen 
concentration of 19.5% for all pressures and for the three diameters 
common to both gravity conditions. At a given pressure, FSR decreases 
with increasing diameter in both μg and 1 g. For example, at 100 kPa in 
μg, FSR decreases from 0.184 mm/s for 6.4 mm to 0.122 mm/s for 9.5 
mm and 0.101 mm/s for 12.7 mm; in 1 g at the same pressure it de­
creases from 0.090 to 0.050 and 0.020 mm/s. The same ordering is 
observed at the lower pressures, while all values remain systematically 
lower in 1 g than in μg.
Fig. 8 extends this comparison in 1 g at 20.5% O2, where the larger 
diameters can also be included. The same trend persists across the full 
range: FSR decreases from 0.168 mm/s for 6.4 mm to 0.125, 0.080, 
0.055, and 0.025 mm/s for 9.5, 12.7, 20, and 28 mm, respectively, at 
100 kPa. The reduction in FSR with increasing diameter is not 
