Taking together, results show that pressure and gravity primarily 
shift the overall flammability boundary, whereas fuel diameter changes 
the internal structure of the path to extinction. Increasing diameter 
raises LOC-FS but lowers LOC-FR, so the oxygen interval between 
cessation of spread and final extinction widens with rod size. This 
behavior suggests that diameter does not only change the intensity of a 
single burning mode; rather, it changes the relative robustness of two 
distinct modes: a leading edge flame spread mode and a post spread 
regression burning mode.
4. Discussion
A useful framework for interpreting the present results is the classical 
opposed flow description of flame spread over a thermally thick solid. In 
that formulation, the controlling region is located near the flame front, 
flame spread is viewed as a series of ignition steps, and the spread rate is 
governed by the combined action of a solid heating term and a gas-phase 
kinetic term [18]. Motivated by that framework, and assuming that 
although the solid may be thick the solid heating occurs in a thin layer 
close to the solid surface with a uniform temperature [26], this may be 
written in simplified form as 
