Over a comparable flow range, increasing diameter tends to weaken 
the local convective coupling unless the increase in Nu compensates 
proportionally. At the same time, larger cylinders reduce curvature ef­
fects and alter how heat is redistributed within the solid ahead of the 
front. Thus, as d increases, the ratio of available flame feedback to the 
effective thermal demand for igniting fresh material decreases. The net 
result is that larger cylinders become less favorable to the ignition-step 
sequence required for opposed spread. The flame has increasing diffi­
culty preheating virgin material to pyrolysis before the local thermal and 
chemical support weaken. The measured decrease in FSR with 
increasing diameter is therefore the sublimit manifestation of the same 
mechanism that raises LOC-FS at larger d: the flame progressively loses 
its ability to maintain pyrolysis of virgin solid ahead of the front [3,7,12,
19–23-27–29].
The pressure and oxygen trends fit naturally within the same struc­
ture. When the chemical time is small compared with the flow time, 
spread is in a thermal-dominated regime; under high oxygen and pres­
sure and low flow velocity, spread is mainly controlled by solid heating. 
As oxygen concentration and pressure are reduced, the chemical time 
increases, the Damk¨ohler number decreases, the gas phase kinetic term 
becomes comparable to the heat transfer term, and the spread rate de­
creases toward no spread.
The behavior of the measured effective LOC-FR implies a different 
controlling balance. Once spread ceases, the flame no longer needs to 
continuously ignite new virgin material upstream; instead, it only needs 
to remain sustainable over an already heated and regressing end/conical 
surface. A more appropriate descriptor for this stage is the fuel regres­
sion rate, rf, rather than the flame spread rate [10] as 
