Where τres is the local gas residence time in the burning zone near the 
regressing tip. Final extinction is expected when DaFR equals the critical 
Damk¨ohler number for extinction [28]. In this framework, DaFR depends 
on the fuel mole fraction, Xf both directly and through Tf, which de­
pends on the pyrolyzate supply, that is linked to the regression rate and 
the size of the hot pyrolyzing region. Increasing diameter can therefore 
contribute to a lower measured effective LOC-FR by increasing the 
effective pyrolyzing area and the characteristic flame holding length 
downstream of the former spread front, thereby increasing fuel vapor 
supply and local residence time. This interpretation is consistent with 
Huang et al. 
[2], who showed that thick PMMA rods can transition 
from opposed surface spread to a fuel-regression mode associated with 
the downstream recirculation region behind the cylinder end surface. 
Thus, increasing diameter appears to weaken the forward spread mode, 
raising the measured effective LOC-FS, while favoring persistence of the 
post spread regression flame, lowering measured LOC-FR. This mecha­
nism is inferred from the observed LOC trends and prior PMMA-rod 
studies, rather than from direct measurements of the local pyrolyzate 
field or recirculation structure. Conversely, as diameter decreases, the 
pyrolyzing region and flame holding length are reduced, causing the 
oxygen interval ΔXO2 = LOC −FS −LOC −FR to narrow.
The stronger separation observed in 1 g than in μg LOC suggests that 
buoyancy amplifies this effect. Although it should be noted that the 
range of diameters tested in μg is narrower than that studied in 1 g. Prior 
work on thick rods indicates that the regression-like flame in normal 
gravity is associated with a stronger downstream recirculation zone of 
the end surface [2], so the burning mode that survives after cessation of 
spread appears to be a form of flame holding in the region downstream 
from the flame front. This provides helpful insights for why the FS–FR 
separation is much more pronounced in 1 g [2,7,10,11–18].
Because the experiments were conducted in a sealed recirculating 
chamber, the gas feeding the test section was not a prescribed isothermal 
oxidizer stream. Combustion in sealed volumes can produce transient 
variations in gas temperature due to heat release, changes in gaseous 
mole number, and heat transfer to the chamber walls. In the present 
experiments, combustion products were recirculated, oxygen was pro­
gressively depleted, and the bulk gas temperature may have increased 
during the test. At constant pressure, an increase in gas temperature 
would reduce the gas density and the molar oxygen, while also 
increasing the sensible temperature of the gas entering the flame region. 
These effects can act in competing directions. Gas preheating may favor 
flame stabilization by reducing the thermal demand on the flame, 
whereas product dilution, reduced density, and reduced oxygen molar 
concentration may weaken gas phase reaction and heat feedback. This 
coupled influence of pressure, oxygen concentration, and thermal con­
ditions is consistent with previous studies showing that PMMA ignition 
and flame spread are controlled by both heat transfer and gas phase 
chemical kinetic effects [8,30]. Therefore, the present LOC-FS and 
LOC-FR values should be interpreted as effective limiting oxygen con­
centrations along the sealed chamber oxygen depletion path, rather than 
as strictly isothermal limits at prescribed oxygen concentration. Since all 
tests were performed in the same recirculating configuration and the 
dominant trends with pressure, gravity, and diameter were systematic, 
the qualitative conclusions are not expected to change; however, the 
lack of a validated gas temperature history is recognized as an experi­
mental limitation.
Pressure acts differently on the LOC. Than fuel thickness. Lowering 
pressure weakens both propagation and regression, rather than sepa­
rating them. Qualitatively, in the pressure tested range 40 to 100 kPa, 
lower pressure reduces gas density and reaction intensity and tends to 
increase flame stand-off, which weakens flame to solid coupling and 
slows spread; accordingly, both LOC-FS and LOC-FR shift upward as 
pressure is reduced. In the context of the present results, pressure 
therefore contracts the overall flammable domain between flame spread 
and regression burning. This distinction is important because it shows 
that the path from ignition to extinction cannot be characterized by a 
single mechanism alone: two fuels may have similar overall flamma­
bility trends with pressure or gravity yet exhibit different fractions of 
their burning history in the spreading and regression burning regimes 
[4,7].
5. Conclusions
A series of experiments were conducted to investigate the depen­
dence on the oxygen concentration of opposed flame spread over PMMA 
rods in its path to extinction. This path does not terminate at a single 
limit. Instead, the burning history is composed of two distinct events: 
cessation of forward spread at the LOC-FS, followed by continued 
regression burning until final extinction at the LOC-FR. The present 
results show that pressure, gravity, and diameter do not affect these two 
limits in the same way.
For the diameters and flow velocity tested, both limits are consis­
tently lower in μg, than in 1 g and the flame spread rate is systematically 
lower in 1 g at the same oxygen concentration and pressure. This in­
dicates that in μg,at the low flow velocity tested the spread rate and LOC 
