doi:10.1016/j.proci.2026.106484,doi,Quiescent flame spread and extinction over acrylic sheets in microgravity at increased oxygen concentration,,0,"Flame spread over solid fuels is a fundamental problem in fire science due to its relevance to material flammability and its critical role in fire growth. In microgravity, flames exhibit markedly different behavior from terrestrial conditions, presenting unique challenges for spacecraft fire safety. In quiescent environments (without external or buoyancy-induced flow) flame spread is governed by diffusion processes and heat losses to the surroundings rather than convection. Building on previous theoretical analyses of radiative heat losses, flame quenching is expected to occur at different fuel thicknesses depending on the ambient oxygen concentration. As part of NASA’s Solid Fuel Ignition and Extinction (SoFIE) investigation aboard the International Space Station (ISS), recent experiments examined the flammability of thermally thin acrylic sheets (0.05–1 mm) in a quiescent environment at elevated oxygen concentrations (30% by volume) and ambient pressures of 0.5 and 1 atm. The results—the first to systematically vary fuel thickness in quiescent microgravity—show that flames spread faster over thinner fuels, while for thicknesses above 0.1 mm, quenching occurred before the flame reached the sample end. Near extinction, oscillatory and pulsating flame behaviors, including re-ignitions, were observed, sometimes persisting for several minutes before quenching. Measurements in the gas-phase region indicate similar maximum temperatures, in the range of 1050–1200 °C, at both 0.5 and 1 atm, whereas the radiative heat flux at 0.5 atm is approximately 50% lower than at 1 atm. Overall, these experiments validate theoretical predictions of radiative quenching limits and advance understanding of flame dynamics and material flammability in spacecraft environments. Novelty and significance: This work advances the fundamental understanding of flame spread and extinction in quiescent microgravity conditions by bridging theoretical radiative-loss models with rare experimental data from the International Space Station. While the governing physics of low-gravity combustion are understood, this study fills a critical gap by systematically demonstrating how fuel thickness dictates radiative-controlled extinction limits under elevated oxygen conditions. By leveraging the unique quiescent environment, these results provide a necessary empirical foundation for modeling non-terrestrial fire behavior. Consequently, this research moves beyond theoretical analysis to provide insights that can inform spacecraft fire safety considerations, particularly regarding thickness-dependent flammability of materials used in long-duration space missions."
