1  Introduction 
For more than 50 years researchers have studied the spherically symmetric burning of a droplet 
in a quiescent ambient atmosphere (Godsave, 1952; Spalding, 1952). An advantage of spherical 
symmetry is that only one spatial dimension enters the description of the combustion process, 
greatly facilitating the comparison of experiments with theoretical and numerical models. 
Kumagai and Isoda (1956) were the first to use microgravity to realize spherical symmetry 
experimentally, by using a drop tower providing a little over one second of test time. In the 
classical representation, a motionless fuel droplet burns with a spherical flame in an infinite, 
quiescent ambient atmosphere (e.g., Williams, 1985). The flame surrounding the droplet can 
extinguish at a small droplet size when the residence time becomes too short for chemical 
reactions to occur (e.g., Williams, 1985). More recently, owing to the availability of long-
duration microgravity experiments, researchers identified another extinction limit whereby the 
flame surrounding the droplet extinguishes due to excessive radiative energy loss relative to the 
heat generated by combustion (Chao et al., 1990; Dietrich et al., 1996; Nayagam et al., 1998). In 
all of these instances the droplet ignites and burns with a hot flame until extinction, after which 
chemical reaction ceases completely. Drop tower experiments and related numerical modeling 
efforts (Schnaubelt et al., 2000; Tanabe et al., 1995; Cuoci et al., 2005) demonstrated two-stage 
ignition of alkane droplets where a brief cool flame precedes the hot flame, but cool-flame 
chemistry was not relevant subsequent to ignition. 
Recent experiments (Flame Extinguishment Experiment, FLEX) aboard the Internation Space 
Station (ISS) showed that following radiative extinction of large alkane droplets, the droplet can"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,4,3,"Accepted Manuscript
 
3 
continue to burn for an extended period of time with a low-temperature (cool) flame surrounding 
the droplet (Nayagam et al., 2012; Farouk and Dryer, 2014; Nayagam et al., 2015; Cuoci et al., 
2013). The cool flame surrounding the droplet then extinguishes at a finite droplet diameter after 
which the uncomsumed fuel vapor (the cool flame only burns a fraction of the fuel vaporized) 
condenses to form a large cloud surrounding the droplet. This cool flame is stable only because 
of the Negative Temperature Coefficient (NTC) region where reactivity decreases briefly with 
increasing temperature (Paczko et al., 2014). Theoretical (Nayagam et al., 2015, 2016) and 
numerical (Farouk and Dryer, 2014; Cuoci et al., 2013) models of the cool flame surrounding the 
droplet predict the experimentally observed hot- and cool-flame extinction droplet diameters and 
the hot- and cool-flame burning-rate constants for normal alkane fuels (heptane, octane and 
decane) at atmospheric pressure. Extensive experimental results (with less emphasis on cool 
flames) for these fuels in air have been published and analyzed very recently (Liu et al.,2016). 
Recent experimental measurements of the cool-flame stage of droplet burning (Nayagam et al., 
2016; Farouk et al., 2016) show reasonable agreement with the theory and models. These 
experiments and modeling efforts all considered alkane droplet burning at 0.10 MPa. 
Subsequent FLEX experiments considered burning at higher ambient pressures. Farouk et al. 
(2015) present numerical modeling results of droplet burning in hyperbaric ambient 
environments along with some results from ISS FLEX tests. The experiments at 0.30 MPa show 
a unique behavior where the fiber-supported heptane droplets in carbon-dioxide-diluted ambient 
environments (0.21/0.64/0.15 and 0.21/0.05/0.75 
2
2
2
