/
O
N
CO ) burning with a cool flame can 
spontaneously transition back to a hot flame, sometimes more than once. The modeling results"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,5,4,"Accepted Manuscript
 
4 
suggest that this behavior for heptane occurs at ambient pressures above 0.30 MPa and also 
requires the presence of carbon dioxide in the ambient environment. 
This paper presents the results of further FLEX ISS experiments involving the low-temperature 
or cool-flame burning behavior of large bi-component droplets comprised of decane and 
hexanol. The motivation of the work is to examine mixtures of a conventional model fuel 
(decane) with an oxygenated fuel with a similar boiling point (i.e., volatility to minimize 
preferential vaporization) that is a potential biofuel. The experiments are the result of a 
collaboration between NASA, the Italian Space Agency (ASI) and the Istituto Motori of the 
National Research Council of Italy (CNR). The focus of the presented work is on the low-
temperature burning behavior which is compared and contrasted with the burning behavior of 
pure alkanes from previous ISS experiments. The high-pressure burning behavior is of particular 
interest, showing behavior both consistent with and contrary to previous experiments and 
theories/models of cool-flame burning. The next section provides an overview of the 
experimental apparatus and data-analysis procedures. Sections presenting and discussing the 
experimental results follow the description of the hardware and procedures. 
2  Experimental Apparatus and Procedures 
Detailed descriptions of the experimental hardware, diagnostics and experiment procedures are 
available elsewhere (Dietrich et al., 2015, 2014). Below are the key aspects relevant to 
understanding the data reported herein."
