2.1  ISS Experiment Hardware 
The Flame Extinguishment Experiment (FLEX) uses the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR), a 
full-sized experiment rack in the Destiny module of the ISS. The CIR consists of a 90 l chamber 
and the hardware required to supply and change the gaseous environment in the chamber. The 
ambient pressures for the tests in this work were approximately 0.05, 0.10, 0.20 and 0.30 MPa. 
The ambient oxygen mole fraction was 0.21 (balance nitrogen) for all of the tests reported 
herein. To conserve gaseous resources and minimize upmass to the ISS, the tests re-used the 
gaseous environment in the CIR chamber several times before replenishing. As a result, 
combustion products and fuel vapor do accumulate inside the chamber. The accumulated carbon 
dioxide and water vapor mole fractions were typically on the order of 
4
5 10−
×
. Repeat tests in the 
chamber at different times in the test day indicate that any accumulation of combustion products 
did not appreciably effect the measured burning rate constants, extinction droplet diameters or 
flame radiance. A fan inside the CIR chamber uniformly mixed the gas inside the chamber 
between tests. 
The Multi-User Droplet Combustion Apparatus (MDCA) is an insert in the CIR specifically 
designed for droplet-combustion experiments. The MDCA provides the fuel reservoirs, fuel 
supply tubing and needles required to dispense the fuel. The fuel for all the tests reported herein 
was a mixture of decane and hexanol, equal parts of each fuel by volume. Each needle is 
mounted on a precision motor that stretches and deploys the fuel droplet. The droplet can be 
either free-floated inside the chamber or tethered to an 80 
m Silicon Carbide (SiC) fiber. Two 
igniters on either side of the droplet in the same plane as the needles ignite the droplet and pull"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,7,6,"Accepted Manuscript
 
6 
away at a pre-set time after ignition. The igniter power (voltage and current as well as the igniter 
wire impedance) was constant for all of the tests. The ignition time varied as a function of 
droplet size and ambient pressure with longer times required at larger droplet sizes and higher 
pressures. The ignition time was set to be the minimum required to successfully ignite the 
droplet. 
2.2  Experiment Diagnostics 
The CIR provided cameras to visualize both the droplet and the flame, in addition to an infrared 
camera that imaged the glowing fiber for fiber-supported tests. The droplet-view camera had a 
telecentric high-bit depth multispectral (HiBMS) imaging system, with a 12-bit, 1024 by 1024 
pixel image array, illuminated by a red Laser diode operating below the lasing threshold (making 
it a nearly monochromatic, incoherent light source). The field of view (FOV) was slightly over 3 
cm for the droplet-view camera and the camera operated at 30 frames per second for all of the 
tests. The backlight illumination was constant for all of these experiments. The flame-view 
camera was a low-light level ultraviolet (LLUV) camera with a 12-bit, 1024 by 1024 pixel array 
binned to increase the signal-to-noise ratio (creating an effectively 512 by 512 pixel array). The 
camera had an intensifier and a spectral line filter (310 nm) to visualize the chemiluminescence 
from OH*. The camera gain was also constant for all of the experiments in this article. The FOV 
was slightly over 5 cm for the flame view and the camera operated at 30 frames per second. A 
third camera imaged the infrared emission from the SiC fiber for fiber-supported tests and used 
the same detector as the droplet-view camera with a 5 cm FOV and also operated at 30 frames 
per second. This camera had a Liquid Crystal Tunable Filter (LCTF), preceeding the image"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,8,7,"Accepted Manuscript
 
7 
array, that in principle could switch between pass-bands in the 600 to 1000 nm range. The slow 
cycling rate of the LCTF, combined with the weak signal from the fiber, however, precluded 
ratio pyrometery. To maximize the signal-to-noise in this image, the camera operated with a 
binned image array and the LCTF set to a fixed 925 nm (corresponding to the maximum 
transmission). 
In addition to the CIR-provided HiBMS, LLUV and IR camera systems, the MDCA had a white 
LED and color video camera for illuminating and viewing the test region, respectively. This 
color camera had variable zoom and allowed the test operator at the Telescience Support Center 
in Cleveland to assist with the manual dispensing and stretching of the fuel droplet (with the 
illumination LED on). The camera then zoomed out with the illumination off to provide a color-
camera view of the burning droplet. The MDCA also had two thermopile radiometers, one that 
detected flame radiation from the burning droplet from the visible (
