time recording stopped until the cool flame extinguished. 
The flame radiance (subtracting out the igniter contribution) for both droplets increases quickly 
after igniter withdraw, reaching a peak of approximately 25 W for both droplets. The radiometer 
and flame histories show brief flame oscillations prior to visible or hot-flame extinction similar 
to those reported previously (Dietrich et al., 2014; Liu et al., 2016). Following visible flame 
extinction, the flame radiance drops quickly to levels on the order of 5 W and then decreases 
more slowly until cool-flame extinction. The flame radiance at cool-flame extinction for both 
droplets is less than 2.0 W, an order of magnitude smaller than the peak hot-flame radiance. This 
is consistent with the reduced temperature of the cool flame and the fact that the cool flame only 
burns a fraction of the vaporized fuel (Nayagam et al., 2012, 2015; Farouk and Dryer, 2014; 
Cuoci et al., 2013). Cool-flame extinction is clearly discernible as a slope change in the flame 
radiance, particularly for the D/H droplet. As a result, we used this slope change as the indicator 
of cool-flame extinction."
