0.05 and 0.10 MPa that exhibited cool-flame burning, a large vapor cloud formed after cool-
flame extinction. This vapor cloud appeared as a red fog in the color camera and as a decreasing 
background grayscale value in the droplet view (Nayagam et al., 2012). For the D/H test at 0.05 
MPa, there was no fog in the color camera, and the background grayscale on the droplet images 
remained constant for long after the hot flame extinguished. 
Increasing the ambient pressure to approximately 0.20 MPa showed very different burning 
behavior after visible flame extinction compared to the tests in the 0.10 MPa ambient. For fiber-
supported droplets, after visible flame extinction, the droplets burned with a cool flame. After a 
period of cool-flame burning that varied from test to test, the hot flame spontaneously re-ignited. 
The re-ignition produced a flame radiance in excess of 50 W (the saturation radiance of the wide-
band radiometer). This behavior is qualitatively similar to that in previous FLEX tests with 
heptane in 0.30 MPa environments with carbon dioxide-diluted atmospheres (Farouk et al., 
2015). In both these and the previous FLEX tests, the re-ignition occurred only for fiber-
supported droplets. Free-floating droplets in the present FLEX-ICE-GA experiments (two 
successful tests), with initial droplet diameters in the same range as the fiber-supported tests, did 
not exhibit re-ignition. For the free-floating droplets, the hot flame extinguished and the droplet"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,13,12,"Accepted Manuscript
 
