appeared to burn with a cool flame to completion; i.e., the flame extinguished when the droplet 
disappeared. After the droplet disappeared a large fog appeared in the color camera, and the 
background grayscale in the droplet images decreased significantly. 
Further increasing the ambient pressure to 0.30 MPa resulted in droplets that radiatively 
extinguished and burned with what appeared to be a cool flame to completion. This occurred for 
both fiber-supported and free-floating droplets. The spontaneous re-ignition that occurred at 
ambient pressures of approximately 0.20 MPa was not present in any tests at 0.30 MPa. The hot 
flames for the 0.30 MPa tests were noticeably dimmer and weaker than the flames in the tests at 
lower pressures (with the same ambient gas composition). In addition, following droplet burnout 
there was no appreciable fog present in the color camera, and there was no appreciable decrease 
of the background grayscale in the droplet images with time. 
