4  Discussion 
Figure 1 reveals that, unlike the very different burning behavior at elevated pressures presented 
for the first time in this work, at 0.01MPa there are certain qualitative similarities in the burning 
of large D/H droplets in the FLEX-ICE-GA experiments and large decane droplets reported 
previously (Nayagam et al., 2015). After ignition the flame grows in size as does the flame-
standoff ratio and radiative energy loss. As the flame increases in size it becomes dimmer, with 
the peak temperature also decreasing. The flame then reaches a maximum size and extinguishes 
when the radiative loss from the flame zone reduces the flame temperature to a value below 
which hot-flame combustion is no longer possible. The peak temperature measurements from the 
fiber (Figure 7) suggest that this temperature may be between 1150 and 1200 K at 0.10 MPa. 
This is consistent with computational results in the literature (e.g., Farouk and Dryer, 2014). 
The data show that during the hot flame, the droplet surface area regression rate is nearly linear, 
so that droplet vaporization can be represented by the average burning-rate constant, 
