average fuel properties for decane and hexanol to compute the hot-flame energy release in 
Equation 1, and comparing that result to the hot-flame radiance in Figure 1, shows the hot flame 
losing between 40 and 60% of its energy to radiation. This value compares favorably to solid-"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,20,19,"Accepted Manuscript
 
19 
fuel burning (T’ien and Endo, 2013) in microgravity even though it is larger than generally 
accepted values for hydrocarbon fuels in normal gravity. The flame size and standoff ratio 
growth in Figure 1 clearly indicate that the transient terms in the governing equations cannot be 
ignored for those hot flames. The cool-flame, however, especially for the decane droplet in 
Figure 1, is quite likely quasi-steady, lying much closer to the droplet. In this region the 
convective and diffusive terms balance each other and transient terms can be neglected 
(Nayagam et al., 2016). 
The flame data for the tests reported herein did not exhibit evident signs of preferential 
vaporization of one of the constituent fuels. For example, the droplet, flame, and radiometer time 
histories do not show deviations consistent with a flame contraction, a change in heat-release 
rate, or a plateau in the droplet-burning history that would suggest two-stage burning (e.g., Shaw, 
1990). To further address this possibility, we applied Raoult’s law to a liquid droplet with known 
initial composition and stepped forward in time with a quasi-steady gas phase and an isothermal, 
homogenous liquid using literature values for the vapor pressure of decane and hexanol as 
functions of temperature (Ambrose and Walton, 1988). The results of this analysis showed that, 
for the ambient conditions in this study, the hexanol mole fraction in the vapor at the droplet 
surface remained between 0.6 and 0.2, reaching the later limit only at the end of combustion and 
only in extreme cases. Decane and hexanol likely do not behave as an ideal solution in view of 
their similarity to solutions of other alkanes and alcohols for which literature data are available 
(e.g., Khare et al., 2004). Theoretical calculations using the activity coefficients of the 
constituent species (e.g., VLECalc, 2015) indicate formation of an azeotrope with a hexanol"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,21,20,"Accepted Manuscript
 
20 
mole fraction of approximately 0.2, although no experimental data appear to be available to 
confirm this. 
Recent experiments in normal gravity at the CNR yield similar conclusions to the analytical 
results. The tests involved fiber-supported droplets of D/H with an identical composition to that 
of the FLEX-ICE-GA tests. After ignition the droplet burned to approximately half its initial size 
before the flame was extinguished. A micro-syringe sampled the remaining liquid droplet on the 
fiber and input the sample to a High Pressure Liquid Chromotagraph. The average composition 
of the sampled mixture was virtually identical to the initial composition (within 1%). Based on 
all of these considerations, it seems safe to conclude that fractional distillation is not likely to be 
qualitatively significant in explaining the experimental results. 
4.1  Importance of Hexanol Addition 
Figure 1 shows that the hot-flame burning behavior of decane and 50/50 D/H droplets is nearly 
identical in air at normal atmospheric pressure. The boiling point and heat of combustion of both 
fuels are very similar. The results are, therefore, consistent with the idea that for this D/H 
mixture both fuels vaporize and burn. The result is that the burning-rate constant, flame size and 
standoff, and flame radiance are all almost identical. The D/H droplet did burn with a hot flame 
for a slightly longer time. Consequently it had a smaller 
,
ext hot
D
. These differences are small, 
however, and are consistent with test-to-test variations and differences in the thermo-physical 
properties between decane and hexanol."
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,22,21,"Accepted Manuscript
 
21 
The cool-flame burning behavior of the two droplets, however, is different. The cool-flame 
burning-rate constant of the D/H droplet is smaller than that of the decane droplet and the 
,
ext cool
D
 of the D/H droplet is significantly larger than that for the pure decane droplet. In 
addition, the flame radiance during quasi-steady cool-flame burning, an indicator of the energy-
release rate, is smaller for the the D/H droplet than for the pure alkanes at the same droplet size. 
These results suggest that the hexanol is influencing the NTC behavior of the decane, resulting in 
a weaker cool flame. Both the higher heat of vaporization of hexanol and the lack of 
participation of hexanol in the decane cool-flame chemistry may contribute to the smaller 
burning rate and the weaker cool flame. Recent experiments and modeling (Cai et al., 2015) with 
n-octanol show NTC behavior at high pressures (2.0 MPa ), much higher than the pressures in 
the present work). Based on molecular structure, one might expect hexanol to have similar NTC 
behavior to octanol, although to a slightly lesser extent. It is, therefore, not possible to 
completely rule out participation of hexanol in the cool flame. It seems likely, however, that 
under these conditions hexanol behaves more like constituents such as iso-octane, that do not 
participate in the cool flame at atmospheric pressure, for example possibly favoring concerted 
elimination reactions over the required isomerization reactions of hydroxylhexylperoxy radicals. 
4.2  Re-ignition of Decane/Hexanol Droplets 
At ambient pressures of approximately 0.20 MPa, we observed spontaneous re-ignition of the 
hot-flame after a relatively long period of cool-flame burning (e.g. > 5 s ). The present results for 
D/H droplets showed that re-ignition only occurred for fiber-supported droplets. The limited"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,23,22,"Accepted Manuscript
 
22 
free-floated data did not show re-ignition at 0.20 MPa. Figure 8 shows the burning histories for 
two droplets in the 0.20 MPa ambient, one free-floated and one fiber-supported. The graph 
clearly shows that the hot-flame burning histories for both tests were nearly identical as was the 
cool-flame burning for nearly 12 seconds. The free-floated test continued to burn with a cool 
flame and never exhibited a re-ignition. For the free-floated droplet, the droplet imaging stopped 
shortly before the test ended. As a result, it is ambiguous whether the droplet burned to 
completion (with a cool flame), disrupted at a very small size, or the cool flame extinguished at a 
very small droplet size. After the droplet disappeared there was a very large vapor cloud visible 
in the color camera, suggesting that the cool flame extinguished. The fiber-supported droplet, 
however, experienced a re-ignition of the hot flame that subsequently oscillated for several 
seconds before extinguishing nearly coincident with the droplet disappearing and no vapor cloud 
visible after the test. 
The results reported herein suggest that the fiber could be playing a significant role in the re-
ignition of the D/H droplets at 0.20 MPa in the present experiments. The cool flame 
surrounding the droplet after radiative extinction is clearly stable, at least at lower pressures. The 
theories (e.g., Nayagam et al., 2012) and models (e.g., Farouk and Dryer, 2014; Cuoci et al., 
2013) show that for a range of droplet sizes there are two stable flame solutions, a cool flame and 
a hot flame. If a perturbation to the cool flame is large enough it could, therefore, serve to push 
the stable cool flame to a (transiently) stable hot flame. The cool flame lies closer to the droplet 
surface where there is sufficient fuel for re-ignition. Even though it has a lower temperature, it 
can conduct as much or more heat to the droplet as the hot flame. The cool flame can then raise 
the temperature of the gas near the droplet surface and serve as a sufficient perturbation to"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,24,23,"Accepted Manuscript
 
23 
transition the cool flame to a hot flame. Visual inspection of some of the fiber-supported droplet 
images shows that there are droplet distortions and disruptions during cool-flame burning, 
further suggesting possible fiber effects. The mechanisms by which the fibers promote re-
ignition, however, are not entirely clear, and fibers may not always be needed for re-ignition 
(Farouk et al., 2015). 
There was no re-ignition for either free-floated or fiber-supported droplets at an ambient pressure 
of 0.30 MPa . There is insufficient data to know whether this is a fundamental change in the 
cool-flame chemistry with pressure or a result of the limited test data. While we certainly 
observed radiative extinction at 0.30 MPa , the initial droplet sizes were not quite as large as 
some of those tested at 0.20 MPa . If the re-ignition has a droplet-size dependence, it is possible 
that the initial droplet sizes were too small to observe the phenomena. Conversely, there are clear 
differences between the cool-flame behavior at 0.20 and 0.30 MPa . The flame radiance during 
cool-flame burning is higher at 0.30 MPa and there is no vapor cloud at the end of the test. This 
suggests that more fuel is consumed by the cool-flame at higher pressures and less unreacted fuel 
penetrates to the ambient atmosphere. This could also explain why re-ignition does not occur at 
the higher pressure. 
