5  Conclusions 
This paper presents experimental data of single droplets of decane and hexanol burning in a 
microgravity environment. The experiments used the droplet combustion facility aboard the 
International Space Station as part of a collaboration between NASA, the Italian Space Agency 
and the Istituto Motori of the National Research Council of Italy. The ambient environments for 
the tests were air (0.21/0.79 
2
2
/
O
N ) and pressures of approximately 0.05, 0.10, 0.20 and 0.30 
MPa . The data were backlit images of the droplet, 
*
OH chemiluminescence, infrared images, 
color camera images and flame radiance as functions of time. Large decane/hexanol droplets at 
pressures greater than or equal to 0.10 MPa exhibited low-temperature burning after radiative 
extinction of the visible flame similar to previous microgravity experiments with alkane droplets. 
The results showed that the hot-flame burning-rate constant decreased with increasing droplet 
size and increased with increasing ambient pressure. The hot flame 
*
OH chemiluminescence"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,29,28,"Accepted Manuscript
 
28 
decreased with increasing droplet size, and also decreased with increasing pressure. The droplet-
size dependence of both the burning-rate constant and 
*
OH chemiluminescence is consistent 
with increasing radiative loss, relative to total heat release, at larger droplet sizes. The decreasing 
*
OH chemiluminescence with increasing pressure is unexpected. This is especially true of the 
behavior at the highest ambient pressure where the chemiluminescence was almost undetectable 
for large droplets prior to radiative extinction. The peak flame radiance (total flame radiative 
loss) during hot-flame burning was independent of pressure. The cool-flame radiance, however, 
was strongly dependent on pressure. At 0.10 MPa the cool-flame radiance is approximately an 
order of magnitude smaller than the hot-flame radiance, while at 0.30 MPa the cool-flame 
radiance becomes a significant fraction of the peak radiance prior to radiative extinction (greater 
than one half). 
Taken together, these observations suggest that the cool flame burning is a strong function of 
ambient pressure. Further, at the highest pressure and larger droplet sizes, the cool flame is so 
strong that it inhibits the formation of the hot flame after ignition. This suggests a complex flame 
structure prior to radiative extinction of the hot flame. The hot flame may not form completely or 
both flames may co-exist with enough oxidizer leaking through the hot flame to support cool-
flame burning and enough fuel passing through the cool flame to sustain the hot flame before 
radiative extinction. The cool-flame burning is also much more dependent on the ambient 
pressure than predicted by existing theoretical analyses that produce reasonable agreement with 
ambient pressure cool-flame burning. The cool-flame heat release, burning-rate constant and 
extinction droplet diameter have a much greater pressure dependence than previous theoretical 
analyses of cool-flame burning predict. For example, in autoignition experiments cool-flame"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,30,29,"Accepted Manuscript
 
29 
behavior becomes more robust with increasing pressure as the pressure increases from 0.1 MPa 
to 40 MPa, but there is little noticeable change between 0.10 MPa and 0.30 MPa. Conversely, in 
the present experiments the change over the latter small pressure increment is dramatic, 
suggesting deficiencies in our knowledge of cool-flame chemical kinetics. 
At an ambient pressure of 0.20 MPa the fiber-supported droplets that radiatively extinguished 
showed a spontaneous hot-flame re-ignition after a period of cool-flame burning. The re-ignition 
phenomena is similar to that in previous ISS experiments. The re-ignition did not occur at an 
ambient pressure of 0.30 MPa and was only present in fiber-supported tests. While there is 
insufficient data to draw definitive conclusions, the present results suggest that the fiber may 
represent a large enough perturbation to cool-flame burning that it causes a transition back to a 
hot flame. This observation, combined with the other experimental data, show that a more 
complicated dependence of cool-flame burning on ambient pressure than currently known 
chemical kinetics imply. 
