B OH* Chemiluminescence Analysis 
The OH* flame data in Figure 1 represent the outer edge of the flame (e.g., Figure 5) as 
measured directly from the image. The OH* camera image is a two-dimensional representation 
of the three-dimensional flame. After selection of a suitable threshold value, the image is 
essentially converted to a binary image; pixel grayscale values are either above or below the 
threshold. The LLUV camera has 12-bit resolution and was very stable for all of the FLEX-ICE-
GA tests. Further, all of the tests used the same intensifier gain, as well as keeping all other"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,38,37,"Accepted Manuscript
 
37 
camera parameters (e.g., binning, frame rate, etc.) the same for every test. As a result, there is a 
considerable amount of information available in addition to simply the flame size (and shape). 
This additional information can be important in helping to interpret the results of the present 
FLEX-ICE-GA (as well as other FLEX) experiments and also in providing additional data for 
both qualitative and quantitative comparison to theoretical and numerical models of droplet 
combustion. 
