the OH* band) from the differential element dV that is a function of the radial distance from the 
center of the droplet. The integration in Equation 2 is along the line  in Figure 9. The 
transformation of Equation 2 to obtain the radial distribution of chemiluminescence from the 
measured signal is well documented in the literature (de Hoog, 1980; Deutsch and Beniaminy, 
1983; Dasch, 1992; Deans, 2000; Yuan, 2003). 
Figures 10 and 5 show typical images from the LLUV and horizontal profiles of the grayscale 
value through the center of the flame, respectively. Clearly from both the images and grayscale 
profiles, there is a considerable amount of noise. The Abel inversion involves differentiating the 
radial profile. As such, it is very sensitive to noise. To get a radial profile with minimal noise we 
performed the following steps (Long, 2014; Yuan, 2003)."
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,39,38,"Accepted Manuscript
 
38 
   1.  Get a background image with no flame present. [The Abel inversion works best when the 
input value far from the centroid approaches zero. This image, or, more specifically, the average 
grayscale from this image, is subtracted from the radial profile to approach zero in the far-field.] 
   2.  Get a radial profile from the centroid going right to the edge of the image. See the green line 
in Figure 9. 
   3.  Subtract the background grayscale of Step 1 from the radial profile obtained in Step 2. 
   4.  Repeat Steps 2 and 3 at different angles relative to the green line in Figure 9, sweeping in 
the direction of the red arrow in the figure. 
   5.  Average the radial profiles obtained in steps 2 and 3. 
   6.  Use the averaged radial profile as input to the Abel inversion algorithm. 
Figure 11 shows the resulting Abel-transformed radial grayscale profiles for a D/H droplet that 
burned to completion with a hot flame in ambient air at 0.10 MPa. The ordinate is the grayscale 
value while the absissa is the radial distance from the center of the droplet. The (low-amplitude) 
variations seen here near the center of the droplet result from the low-level noise that is amplified 
by differentiating the data. 
The grayscale values in this figure are arbitrary units that result from applying the preceding 
procedure to the 12-bit values of the pixel readings. Specific values in these arbitrary units 
depend on details of the procedures employed. It is important in comparing different data to 
employ the same procedure for all of the data, as was done in the results shown in Figure 5."
