From the data in Figure 10, one can extract the flame diameter, flame width, and relative peak 
flame luminosity as functions of time. Figure 11 shows results of a detailed flame analysis for 
the flame diameter (bottom ordinal axis), full-width, half max (FWHM) flame width (second 
ordinal axis from the bottom), flame width normalized by flame diameter (second ordinal axis 
from the top), and peak flame grayscale value (top ordinal axis), as functions of normalized time 
for the Abel-transformed radial profiles in Figure 11. The flame diameter measured directly from 
the image is also shown on the bottom ordinal plot in Figure 12. The direct flame diameter is a 
bit larger than the Abel-transformed diameter. This is not surprising, since the direct 
measurement of the flame diameter represents the outer edge of the flame, while the Abel-
transformed diameter is two times the distance from the centroid to the peak. The difference 
between the two diameter measurements is nearly equal to the flame width. 
The Abel-deconvolution described above is time-consuming and prone to large errors for images 
that are not symmetric or very dim images (with a small signal-to-noise ratio, e.g., the 0.30 MPa 
image in Figure 10). If the arbitrary grayscale units are related to W, then the total flame 
