C  Flame-Temperature Analysis 
The infrared camera uses the same detector element as the camera for the backlit view of the 
droplet. The array is 1024 by 1024 and can operate at either this full resolution or in a ‘binned’ 
mode where a square of four neighboring pixels combine to form a single pixel. For low-level 
signals, this increases the signal by a factor of nearly four. The camera can operate at a 
maximum of 30 frames per second or at several discrete reduced frame rates, which will also 
increase signal level. Finally, the camera has a four-fold video gain available to further increase 
the signal level. 
Besides the other camera optical elements and the CIR chamber window, a liquid-crystal tunable 
filter (LCTF) precedes the detector element. The filter can be continuously tuned to pass a 7 nm 
full-width, half-maximum (FWHM) band around a central peak in the wavelength range of 800"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,42,41,"Accepted Manuscript
 
41 
to 10000 nm , with a peak transmission of approximately 0.60. The pass band on the filter can be 
set by software either in an automated script or remotely from the ground through software 
commands uplinked to the CIR. The time for the LCTF to cycle through two pass bands was on 
the order of 0.50 sec . 
The original intent of the HiBMS-2 system for the FLEX tests was to perform two-color 
pyrometry. This would involve taking consecutive images with different pass bands on the 
LCTF. For a broad-band gray-body emitter like soot or the SiC fiber in the FLEX tests, the ratio 
of the grayscale pixel values at the two different pass bands would be directly related to the 
temperature, removing the need to know the soot volume fraction or soot or fiber emissivity. 
This works as long as the flame is essentially steady between images. In practice, however, the 
cycle time of the LCTF between pass bands was too large relative to the hot-flame burning time, 
so the assumption of a steady flame between images is not valid. Thus, the pyrometry 
measurements involve using the camera set to a single pass band for the entire test. 
Before launch to the ISS, the HiBMS-2 had extensive calibrations performed against a calibrated 
blackbody source on the ground. These ground calibrations were completed over a range of 
blackbody temperatures, pass bands on the LCTF, and binning and gain settings. The most 
extensive calibrations were at a full (1024 by 1024) resolution, pass bands in the 800 - 1000 nm 
wavelength range, no video gain, and 15 frames per second. The FLEX ISS tests, however, 
realized optimal system performance with no cycling of the LCTF, 30 frames per second 
acquisition rate, a 925 nm pass band on the LCTF, ‘binned’ mode, and with four-fold video 
gain."
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,43,42,"Accepted Manuscript
 
42 
Extensive calibration data for the HiBMS-2 imaging and camera system against the blackbody 
were not available for the on-orbit system settings (Figure 13). The most complete calibration 
data set was for LCTF pass bands of 900 and 950 nm , full image resolution (non ‘binning’), no 
video gain, and an acquisition rate of 15 or 30 frames per second. More limited data were 
available at the on-orbit system settings. From this limited data set, we determined the following: 
   • Operating the camera in ‘binning’ mode resulted in an increase in signal of 3.6 times the full-
image-resolution pixel grayscale value. This value was independent of frame rate, LCTF pass 
band, or blackbody temperature. 
   • The fouir-fold video gain resulted in an increase in signal of 3.3 times the grayscale value 
with no video gain. 
   • The entire HiBMS-2 system calibration with respect to blackbody temperature was 
independent of LCTF pass band in the range of 900 to 950 nm . 
   • Increasing the framing rate from 15 to 30 frames per second decreased the pixel grayscale at a 
given temperature by a factor of 1.8. 
As a result, we were able to generate a calibration curve for the grayscale as a function of 
blackbody temperature by taking the measured grayscale value, dividing by 3.3 (compensating 
for the four-fold video gain), dividing by 3.6 (compensating for the ‘binning’ mode), and 
multiplying by 1.8 (compensating for the framing-rate change). This produced a grayscale value 
for which extensive calibration data were available. While the blackbody has an emissivity of 
1.0, the SiC fibers are excellent graybody emitters, with an emissivity between 0.85 and 0.90 in"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,44,43,"Accepted Manuscript
 
43 
the wavelength range 900 to 950 nm . Getting the fiber temperature from the blackbody 
calibration data then required dividing the grayscale by the assumed emissivity of 0.87 to get the 
actual SiC fiber temperature from the calibration curve (Figure 14) 
C.1  Analysis Procedure 
Figure 15 shows an image from the HiBMS-2 camera. The downloaded images are rotated 
slightly. The first step in the processing of these images is to rotate the image (4.9 degrees) to 
make the fiber horizontal. For tests with significant sooting, the luminosity from the soot is 
visible in the HiBMS-2 image. The sooting for the test in Figure 15 was minimal and therefore 
only the glow of the fiber from the gas-phase flame is visible. The background grayscale value in 
the image is approximately 3000. 
The procedure to derive the gas temperature involved the first four steps below. The last step 
listed below requires an energy balance between the fiber and the gas-phase to derive a gas-
phase temperature. The next section describes this energy balance in detail, along with the 
estimated uncertainties in the gas-phase temperature. 
   1.  Rotate the image (4.9 degrees counterclockwise) to make the fiber horizontal on the screen. 
   2.  Acquire a line profile along the fiber (Figure 15). Most image processing software 
(commercial and open-source) have built-in functions for this. 
   3.  Perform a low-pass filter on the line profile (Figure 15). This eliminates a lot of the pixel-
to-pixel noise, facilitating the gas-phase temperature analysis. This analysis requires taking the"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,45,44,"Accepted Manuscript
 
44 
second derivative of the fiber temperature with respect to axial position along the fiber. The 
result is that small variations in pixel-to-pixel values can produce large temperature variations 
from one pixel location to the next; that is physically unrealistic and only an artifact of the 
camera. Visual comparisons made sure the filtered image represents the real line profile. 
   4.  Use the temperature calibration data to obtain the fiber temperature (Figure 14). This 
involved using the calibration data described at the end of the previous section. 
   5.  Use the fiber temperature profile to obtain the estimated gas-phase temperature. This 
involved using the energy balance on the fiber to correct for conduction along the fiber, 
convective energy transport from the gas to or from the fiber, and radiative energy loss from the 
fiber. 
C.2  Gas Temperature Analysis 
The analysis to estimate the gas temperature from the fiber temperature follows Struk et al. 
(2003). Figure 16 shows a diagram of the fiber and the relevant heat-transfer modes considered 
in the analysis. Equation 4 expresses the energy balance on the fiber. The rate of change of the 
fiber temperature results from a balance between the energy conducted through the fiber, the 
radiative energy lost from the surface of the fiber and the convective energy loss or gain that 
occurs between the fiber and the ambient. Equations 5, 6, and 8 are the expressions for the 
conductive, convective, and radiative heat-transfer rate, respectively. 
 
