Written in this way, the corrections to the gas temperature for the transient energy storage in the 
fiber (first term on the RHS of Equation 9), conductive loss/gain along the fiber (second term on 
the RHS) and radiative transfer from the fiber (third term on RHS) manifest as additions to or 
subtractions from the fiber temperature. All of the terms on the RHS are known properties of the 
fiber, measured values, or quantities that can be estimated with a reasonable degree of certainty. 
Because equation 9 involves derivatives of experimental data, one must take great care to 
minimize pixel-to-pixel noise. The best results for the FLEX-ICE-GA results presented herein 
came with a low-pass spatial filter applied to the fiber grayscale profile. This removed the pixel-
to-pixel variations of grayscale while essentially preserving the low-frequency grayscale 
variations that result from the heating of the fiber by the gas-phase flame (Figure 17). 
The convective heat-transfer coefficient between the fiber and the gas was obtined from 
correlations for the Nusselt number (Equation 7) for heat transfer over a rod. Along the lines of 
