All three of these correlation formulas are based on experiments performed on wires in 
crossflow, with the flow direction normal to the wire axis. In the present experimental 
configuration, however, the flow is mostly parallel to the wire axis. A correlation formula for 
heat transfer along the axis of a needle would therefore be more appropriate, but no such results 
are available in the Reynolds number regime for the present analysis, although related theoretical 
analyses have been pursued recently. In the absence of any useable results for the present 
configuration, the formulas selected here are the best available. Application of these formulas 
may, however, introduce errors in the calculated gas temperatures. The largest source of 
uncertainty in the gas-temperature results therefore is attributed to the use of these correlations 
and the estimates of the gas-phase thermophysical properties. 
As an initial approximation, we use the temperature-dependent thermo-physical properties for 
nitrogen (Reid et al., 1987) to calculate the heat-transfer coefficient. The thermal conductivity is 
only very weakly dependent on pressure in the 0.50 to 3.0 atm range. The initial results use the 
pressure correction suggested by Reid et al. (1987) for ideal gases. We further assume a 
characteristic gas velocity of 10 
/
cm s for 
DF
Re
. Figure 17 shows that there is little change in 
the fiber temperature profile between successive video frames, so the time-dependent term (first 
term on the RHS of Equation 9) is negligible. Future work may involve performing a low pass 
filter of the profile of a single pixel with respect to time to minimize pixel noise with respect to 
time that can cause large errors in that term. 
The error in the gas temperature measurement results primarily from errors in the fiber 
temperature correlation (e.g., pixel-to-pixel variations in responsivity), the aforementioned"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,50,49,"Accepted Manuscript
 
49 
uncertainties in heat transfer correlation, deviations from the assumed surface emmissivity and 
gas property uncertainties (i.e., the gas thermal conductivity). Considering all these sources, we 
estimate the absolute temperature measurement to be on the order of 50 K
±
 and possibly as high 
as 
100 K
±
. While the absolute temperature uncertainty is quite high, the relative temperature 
(i.e., test-to-test and within test) uncertainties are considerably less. As a result, the trend of 
decreasing peak temperature with increasing pressure in Figure 7 is valid even though the 
temperature differential between the tests is within the absolute temperature uncertainty. 
D  Radiometer Analysis 
The MDCA has two permanently mounted thermopile radiometers. One detects flame radiation 
from the burning droplet from the visible to far infrared. The other has a narrow-band infrared 
filter to detect radiation from water vapor. Both radiometers have a very wide dynamic range 
(linear response with irradiance in the range 
6
10− to 
1
2
10
/
W
cm
−
). In practice, however, the 
fixed amplifier gain combined with the 16-bit digitizer in the MDCA reduced the dynamic range 
for the FLEX experiments considerably. Complete details of the radiometer package design, 
specifications, performance and calibration are available elsewhere (Lant, 2011). The details 
most relevant are highlighted here, along with details on the estimation of flame radiance from 
the radiometer readings."
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,51,50,"Accepted Manuscript
 
50 
D.1  Radiometer Package Design Details 
The wide-band detector (WB) is a silicon-based thermopile detector from Dexter Research, 
Model S60M. A KRS-5 (Thallium Bromo-Iodide) window limits the spectral response from 0.58 
to 50 
m (Figure 17). This detector has argon as a back-fill gas, an active area of 0.36 
2
mm and 
an aperture with a diameter of 3.175 mm preceeding it. 
The narrow-band detector (NB) is a thin-film-based thermopile from Dexter Research, Model 
2M (Dexter custom P/N DX-0918). A combination of a Silicon Long-Wave-Pass filter that has a 
cut-on wavelength of 5.0 mm and a Sapphire window that cuts off at 
7.0
m
:
 isolates the 
desired spectral region of interest (
6.0 m
:
, Figure 18). This detector has xenon as a back-fill 
gas, an active area of 4.0 
2
mm and an aperture with a diameter of 4.572 mm preceeding it. Both 
the WB and NB radiometers are 110 mm away from the deployment site in the MDCA. 
The raw voltage signals from both radiometers go through fixed-gain amplifiers before being 
digitized in the MDCA avionics to 16-bit resolution. 
D.2  Radiometer Calibration 
Prior to shipment to the ISS, both radiometer packages underwent a complete calibration and 
characterization. The laboratory standard for all of the tests was a calibrated blackbody equipped 
with a variable size aperture in front. The calibration involved measuring the response of both 
the NB and WB radiometers as a function of irradiance by changing both the blackbody"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,52,51,"Accepted Manuscript
 
51 
temperature and aperture diameter. The radiometer irradiance in engineering units (W) comes 
from computing the blackbody emmitance from the set temperature and aperture diameter. 
   
Figure 18 shows the calibration data for both the wide-band and narrow-band radiometers. The 
graphs show the radiometer irradiance in engineering units, as computed from the blackbody 
temperature and aperture as a function of radiometer voltage. This display method exhibits the 
radiometer irradiance as a function of the voltage for convenience in obtaining the linear 
equation required to derive the radiometer irradiance from the voltage. The insets in both graphs 
show the linear relationships as well as the minimum and maximum radiance values. Finally, a 
second inset shows the equation employed to obtain the flame radiance in engineering as a 
function of the detector area and geometry in the MDCA. 
D.3  Obtaining Flame Radiance from FLEX Radiometer Data 
Deriving the flame radiance (the total rate of radiant energy loss from the flame) from the 
radiometer data involves the following steps. 
   1.  Obtain the radiometer data for a given test point. 
   2.  After each test day, the MDCA operator performs a dummy test (with no fuel dispensed) to 
obtain the radiometer signature for the igniter setting used that day."
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,53,52,"Accepted Manuscript
 
52 
   3.  At the end of the dummy test, the radiometer reading decays to a baseline value that can 
vary from test day to test day (likely the result of small variations in chamber temperature and 
gas composition). 
   4.  Apply the radiometer calibrations to the radiometer data in Steps 1, 2 and 3 to get the 
radiometer irradiance (Figure 19). 
   5.  The radiance for the flame itself involves subtracting the time-stamped radiometer 
irradiance from the igniter in Step 3 from the time-stamped test radiometer irradiance (Step 2). 
   6.  The final step involves computing the computing the flame radiance from the radiometer 
irradiance, again using the calibration data in Figure 19. Figure 20 shows that the narrow-band 
radiometer, which has a gain adjusted to optimally view the cool flame, saturates during ignition 
as well as during the hot flame and sometimes even into cool-flame burning. This is not a limit 
that is inherent to the radiometer, but rather it results from the fixed-gain amplifier and the 16-bit 
digitizer. 
Figure 19 shows representative results for flame radiance as a function of time for both the 
narrow-band and wide-band radiometers after performing the steps above. The gap in the 
narrow-band data between 0 and 15 seconds indicates that the signal saturated the amplifier and 
the magnitude is unknown. The vertical lines indicate certain critical events in the burning 
history such as the pre-extinction flame oscillation, visible flame extinction and extinction of the 
cool flame."
doi:10.1080/00102202.2016.1225730,doi,Experimental Observations of the Low-Temperature Burning of Decane/Hexanol Droplets in Microgravity,54,53,"Accepted Manuscript
 
53 
The uncertainty values for the flame radiance are hard to quantify. The measured irradiance 
values (i.e., Figure 19) are much more accurate and precise than the corresponding flame 
radiance values. Therefore, the largest error is likely the calculation of flame radiance from the 
radiometer irradiance. Geometric effects of the 3-D flame (off-axis and out of plane radiation) 
are not compensated. We estimate the stack-up of these errors to be as high as 
20%
±
 of the 
indicated flame radiance as a worst-case."
