doi:10.1080/00102202.2017.1404587,doi,Boundary Layer Effect on Opposed-Flow Flame Spread and Flame Length over Thin Polymethyl-Methacrylate in Microgravity,3,2,"methacrylate (PMMA). In other words, ﬂames spread against the oxidizer ﬂow,
which can have forced and natural components; due to the interaction between
ﬂow and fuel bed, the ﬂame will experience a velocity gradient that changes with
the location and modiﬁes the local residence time. In microgravity, however, buoy-
ancy does not aﬀect the ﬂame region, and therefore the induced ﬂow becomes
negligible. Moreover, it has been proven that the relative importance of buoyancy
gradually diminishes in presence of a forced ﬂow even for normal gravity levels
(Altenkirch et al., 1980; Fernandez-Pello et al., 1981).
The solid fuel is heated up by the ﬂame front until the vaporization temperature, Tv, is
reached (Bhattacharjee et al., 2003; Frey and Tien, 1976). Fuel thickness is a fundamental
parameter in the heating process, but we can simplify the problem recognizing two limits,
as suggested by de Ris (1969) and many other authors (Wichman 1992): thin fuels, where
the cross section is at uniform temperature, and thick fuels, where the propagation of the
ﬂame occurs also in the direction orthogonal to the surface. Our results are relative to the
thin fuel assumption, and this type of problem (see Figure 1) is known to be two-
dimensional (2D) (McAlevy III and Magee, 1969).
There are many factors aﬀecting the ﬂame, both in its shape and spreading velocity;
opposing ﬂow velocity and oxygen level are crucial in microgravity, because the oxidizer
can reach the central region of the ﬂame only through diﬀusion (without an opposing
velocity), and it may not be fast enough to guarantee the reactions to occur (Bhattacharjee
et al., 2016c; Olson and Miller, 2009).
Furthermore, the only heat transfer mechanisms between ﬂame and surroundings are
conduction and radiation, the latter being more important (Bhattacharjee and Altenkirch,
1990). The behavior of the ﬂame just described is deﬁned as radiative regime (Bhattacharjee
et al., 2016b). In the presence of an oxidizer velocity, for a particular range of velocity, the
ﬂame spreads with the same velocity, and it is considered to be in the so-called thermal
regime (Bhattacharjee et al., 2016a). When the ﬂow velocity is large enough, the residence
time in the burning region may not be enough to let the reactions occur, leading to a decrease
in spread rate and eventually to local or total blow-oﬀs (kinetic regime).
The presence of the boundary layer over the fuel bed has been proven to be important
for the ﬂame propagation (Wichman et al., 1982), and it is reasonable to believe that it
plays an important role also in the extinction phenomenon. In our recent work
(Carmignani et al., 2016), we have shown that the blow-oﬀextinction at a given oxidizer
level is not only a function of ﬂow velocity, but also a function of the boundary layer
development length. The eﬀect of boundary layer is so signiﬁcant that an eﬀective velocity,
function of ﬂow velocity, Prandtl number, and local Reynolds number are necessary to
Figure 1. Schematic representation of a ﬂame spreading along the sample (not in scale).
536
L. CARMIGNANI ET AL."
doi:10.1080/00102202.2017.1404587,doi,Boundary Layer Effect on Opposed-Flow Flame Spread and Flame Length over Thin Polymethyl-Methacrylate in Microgravity,4,3,"predict the distance from the sample edge where the ﬂame will extinguish (Bhattacharjee
et al., 2014; Carmignani et al., 2017).
In a microgravity environment, in the absence of buoyancy induced ﬂow, the ﬂow
velocity can be arbitrarily small. It is well known that, at low velocities, the residence time
at the ﬂame leading edge can be large enough for radiation to become a signiﬁcant
mechanism of heat loss (T’ien, 1986). In fact, ﬂow velocities below a critical value cause
the ﬂame to extinguish. This quenching velocity in the radiative regime is the counter part
of the blow-oﬀvelocity in the kinetic regime.
If the boundary layer in the kinetic regime has such a signiﬁcant eﬀect on blow-oﬀ
extinction, the question naturally arises as to if it also aﬀects ﬂame spread in the radiative
regime in a similar manner. In this work, we will present the results from a computational
model for opposed ﬂame spread over thin sheets of PMMA in a microgravity environment
to explore how the spread rate and ﬂame structure are aﬀected in a low-velocity regime.
We also present results from experiments carried out on the International Space Station
(ISS) during the Burning and Suppression of Solids-II (BASS-II) investigation and make
direct comparison with the computational results.
Experimental setup
The BASS-II apparatus onboard the ISS is shown in Figure 2. It is basically a square
combustion tunnel with 76-mm sides, where a fan forces the oxidizer (oxygen-nitrogen
mixture) through ﬂow straighteners from right to left. The velocity range is 0 cm/s to
50 cm/s, while the oxygen level can be adjusted. A hot wire anemometer is used to
calibrate the ﬂow velocity with fan voltage, which is used to determine the ﬂow velocity.
The fuel used was PMMA, and the samples were 10 mm or 20 mm wide and about
100 mm long. Samples were pinched in a sample holder and ignited by a hot wire placed
at a distance of about 100 mm from the leading edge as shown in Figure 2 (this distance
includes the sample holder). Diﬀerent thicknesses, in the range from 100 μm to 400 μm,
were used in the experiments.
The experiments were carried out in the Microgravity Science Glovebox on the ISS by
two crew members, following the operating instructions from NASA Glenn Center in real
time. After creating the desired opposing ﬂow by adjusting the fan voltage, the igniter is
Figure 2. Experimental apparatus for BASS-II used on the ISS (left) and sample holder (right).
