doi:10.1080/00102202.2017.1404587,doi,Boundary Layer Effect on Opposed-Flow Flame Spread and Flame Length over Thin Polymethyl-Methacrylate in Microgravity,5,4,"turned on until a ﬂame is observed. A low resolution digital camera recorded the
experiments from the side, while high resolution pictures of the ﬂame from the front
view were taken with a frequency of about 1 frame per second. A radiometer was used to
conﬁrm ﬂame extinction since ﬂames could be hardly visible.
In some tests, in order to reduce the number of samples to be burned, the velocity was
changed during the propagation of the ﬂame.
The frames were put together and analyzed with an image processor built for this
purpose in MATLAB and called Flame Image Analysis Tool (FIAT; Bhattacharjee et al.,
2017). The spread rate of the ﬂame is obtained by looking at the luminance intensity
values in the frames of the video that is being analyzed. After deciding an appropriate
threshold for the intensity of the ﬂame leading edge, the code tracks it on a frame-by-
frame basis. The value of the ﬂame spread rate is obtained as the slope of the location
versus time curve of the ﬂame. Setting a threshold also for the ﬂame trailing edge, it is
possible to track the ﬂame length and its variations.
Computational model
The computational model consists of a computational ﬂuids dynamics (CFD) model and a
radiation model. Indicating with Vg the oxidizer velocity (mixture of oxygen and nitro-
gen), and with Vf the ﬂame spread rate, in a ﬂame-ﬁxed reference system the oxidizer
approaches the ﬂame with a velocity Vg þ Vf . A schematic of the symmetric half of the
ﬂame spread problem is shown in Figure 3, along with the boundary and interface
Figure 3. Computational domain showing the boundary conditions and separation of gaseous and
solid phases.
538
L. CARMIGNANI ET AL."
doi:10.1080/00102202.2017.1404587,doi,Boundary Layer Effect on Opposed-Flow Flame Spread and Flame Length over Thin Polymethyl-Methacrylate in Microgravity,6,5,"conditions. Given the symmetry of the problem, only one side of the sample is analyzed
numerically, while the other half is considered to be a boundary condition along the
symmetry axes.
The CFD model was written in Fortran language by Bhattacharjee to analyze ﬂame
spread. After the iterative solution obtains a fully converged case, data such as tempera-
ture, velocity in the x and y directions, and mass fraction of oxygen and fuel (as function
of the generic location x, y) can be imported into MATLAB to plot the results.
The mathematical ﬂame model consists of the 2D, steady, elliptic, partial diﬀerential
equations describing conservation of energy, species, mass, and momentum in the gas
phase; furthermore, there are ordinary diﬀerential equations for conservation of mass and
energy in the solid phase. A single-step, second-order, overall reaction with ﬁnite rate
Arrhenius kinetics is used for the gas phase reaction and single-step, while for the
pyrolysis process the Arrhenius kinetics is of the ﬁrst order.
In the numerical model, gas and solid phases are solved sequentially and are coupled by
the interface conditions using the SIMPLER algorithm of Patankar (1980). The solution
seeks a unique value for the spread rate that anchors the ﬂame leading edge at the desired
location, x ¼ xeigen, within the computational domain. This is done by requiring the solid
absolute temperature to be a certain value (20% above ambient) at the eigen location. The
domain is divided in a non-uniform grid structure of 120 × 90, and our previous studies
show that a denser grid structure does not aﬀect the results, especially for very low
opposing velocities (Bhattacharjee et al., 2014).
The gas-phase balance equations for total mass, fuel, oxygen, and nitrogen species mass,
x- and y-momentum, and energy can be expressed in the canonical form:
