spread rate keeps decreasing with Vg until blow-oﬀextinction occurs. The development
length clearly has a tremendous impact on the blow-oﬀextinction velocity, increasing
from 55 cm/s to 111 cm/s as xd is increased from 20 mm to 60 mm. As the ﬂow
velocity is reduced, the radiative regime sets in at about 20 cm/s and the spread rate
starts decreasing leading to radiative extinction. However, unlike the kinetic regime, the
development length seems to have less impact; the quenching velocity only slightly
changes from 1 cm/s to 1.5 cm/s as xd is increased from 20 mm to 60 mm. The
oxidizer velocity at a given vertical locationdepends on the velocity gradient inside the
boundary layer. It is reasonable to expect that a steeper velocity gradient would lead to
extinction more rapidly than a lower gradient, since a higher oxidizer velocity means
lower residence time in the ﬂame region. In other words, the ﬂame location strongly
aﬀects spread rate and extinction at high velocities. However, at very low velocities, the
oxidizer can reach the reactants mostly through diﬀusion, so the eﬀect of the develop-
ment length becomes smaller.
Two ﬂame shapes (temperature contours) are shown in Figure 5 for two development
lengths, 20 mm and 60 mm. To understand the eﬀect of the boundary layer on the ﬂow
near the leading edge in the radiative regime, the u velocity proﬁles at the ﬂame leading
edge for a free stream velocity of 5 cm/s are shown below the computed ﬂames in the same
ﬁgure, where the boundary layer thickness is deﬁned as 99% of the local maximum of gas
velocity. From the velocity proﬁles, it can be seen that the boundary layer is almost twice
as thick for xd ¼ 60 mm. Even though the free stream velocity is 50 mm/s, the actual ﬂow
velocity at the leading edge of the ﬂame at a height of the diﬀusion length, Lg ¼ g=Vg ¼
1:8 mm, is only 6.9 mm/s for xd ¼ 60 mm, which increases to 17.3 mm/s for xd ¼ 20 mm.
The ﬂame leading edge is clearly deep inside the boundary layer with Lg= decreasing with
an increase in xd. The ﬂame strengthens as indicated by its size and ﬂame temperature as
the development length is reduced and the ﬂame encounters a higher eﬀective velocity.
The increase in spread rate is relatively mild, from 2.3 mm/s to 2.7 mm/s.
