doi:10.1080/00102202.2017.1404587,doi,Boundary Layer Effect on Opposed-Flow Flame Spread and Flame Length over Thin Polymethyl-Methacrylate in Microgravity,9,8,"Experiments in diﬀerent conditions (forced ﬂow velocity, thickness and oxygen level)
were numerically reproduced, so the reported experimental variables apply also to the
relative simulation. The eﬀect of development length on computed and experimental
ﬂame spread rates, whose values are indicated respectively by empty and ﬁlled symbols,
is plotted in Figure 6 for three diﬀerent opposed ﬂow velocities. The spread rate can be
seen to decrease with a decrease in Vg consistent with the radiative theory of ﬂame spread
(Bhattacharjee et al., 2016c). With a decreasing ﬂow velocity, the residence time mono-
tonically increases until it is comparable to radiation time scale, ushering in radiative
quenching. For a given Vg, Figure 6 shows that the spread rate decreases as the boundary
layer development length increases. Embedded in a thickening boundary layer the ﬂame
encounters a milder eﬀective velocity, resulting in radiative cooling of the ﬂame, and even
quenching under the right conditions.
Figure 7 shows computational results of 2D ﬂames (on the left), and experiment
pictures of the ﬂames’ front view (on the right), for PMMA with a thickness of 100 µm
and burning with a velocity of 3 cm/s (top ﬂames) and 5 cm/s (bottom ﬂames). The
oxygen level is these experiments (labeled B06 and B01 in our website ﬂame.sdsu.edu) are
20.1% and 20.6%, respectively, and a complete list of the experimental conditions can be
found in Table 3. Because the objective of the experiments was to test the hypothesis of
radiative quenching, the ﬂow was also changed during the experiments, resulting in a
limited range of development length covered by the ﬂame with a constant opposed-
velocity. Simulated ﬂame shapes clearly indicate a weakening ﬂame when xd increases,
as shown also by ﬂame length variations, and this trend is conﬁrmed by the experiments.
Flame length values in Figure 7 were obtained with FIAT for the experimental ﬂames,
setting two luminance intensity thresholds to locate ﬂame leading and trailing edge.
Subsequently, ﬂame length values of the simulated ﬂames were obtained locating points
along the x-axis corresponding to a certain temperature, with a variation of  10 K. The
