of Vg ¼ 3 cm/s the temperature considered for the ﬂame length was about 730 K, while in
the case of Vg ¼ 5cm/s, the temperature was signiﬁcantly higher, at about 1000 K. The
lower temperature for Vg ¼ 3 cm/s can be justiﬁed with the lower level of oxygen available
for the reactions, resulting in a less complete combustion and soot production.
Furthermore, smaller ﬂame length for lower Vg or higher xd is accompanied by a
reduction in the maximum ﬂame temperature and spread rate; for example, at xd ¼ 60,
the maximum ﬂame temperature is 1553 K for Vg ¼ 5 cm/s, and 1370 K for Vg ¼ 3 cm/s,
while the spread rate goes from about 2.5 mm/s to 1.71 mm/s.
The spread rate of the two experiments represented in Figure 7 is on the order of
2 mm/s, and there is a little increase along the sample as indicated by the computational
results, but the span of boundary layer distance covered is too low to make an experi-
mental case. However, ﬂames in the opposed-ﬂow conﬁguration react pretty fast to local
velocity changes in microgravity (Olson and Miller, 2009), and the ﬂame length seems to
be an easier parameter to analyze.
To explore how the ﬂame length changes with xd, a larger set of experimental test
matrix is used. Isolating the portion of the experiments where the ﬂow velocity was ﬁxed
at 5 cm/s—width (2 cm) and oxygen level (about 20.5%)—over diﬀerent sample thick-
nesses, the data plotted in Figure 8 (experiments B01, B05, and B04) clearly indicate that
ﬂame length increases with the ﬂame getting closer to the sample leading edge (decrease in
xd). Near the ignition point, the ﬂames seem to be slightly shorter for thicker fuels, but
there seems to be little thickness eﬀect as xd decreases, at least in the ﬂame length growth
rate. One possible explanation for this thickness dependence of ﬂame length is that thicker
fuels take a little longer for their pyrolysis front to reach steady-state condition. It is
interesting to notice how the ﬂame length growth rate is similar for diﬀerent thicknesses
initially, even though the absolute lengths depend on thickness as expected. It seems also
