The prediction of the radiative theory (computational ﬂames) of a strengthening ﬂame
as the boundary layer development length decreases is consistent with the experimental
ﬁndings. It should be noted that, while the radiation computation includes the three-
dimensionality of the ﬂame shape (through side losses), the CFD is completely 2D and
side losses or diﬀusion in the width-wise direction are neglected. The side-wise losses (to
the sample holder and ambient air) are expected to reduce the overall size of the ﬂame and
total radiation emitted by the ﬂame. If the sample width is decreased, we would expect the
ﬂame size to decrease and the ﬂame to be cooler (due to increase in losses through the side
as a fraction of heat produced). Flame shapes from two tests (B11 and B21) where the
conditions—fuel thickness (200 μm), Vg (5 cm/s), and oxygen level (20% and 19.7%,
respectively)—are almost identical except for the sample width of 20 mm and 10 mm, are
shown in Figure 10. The ﬂame clearly is much smaller when the width is halved.
Unfortunately, the radiometer peak signal also drastically decreases from 1.35 V to
0.212 V. However, for both cases the ﬂame length does show a similar increase in length
as the development length decreases.
Conclusions
The eﬀect of boundary layer development on opposed-ﬂow ﬂame spread over thin
PMMA sheets, with air as oxidizer in a microgravity environment, is studied computa-
tionally and compared with results from a limited set of data obtained from experiments
on the International Space Station. The computation shows that the ﬂame strengthens as
reﬂected in its size, ﬂame temperature, and spread rate, as it propagates into a thinning
boundary layer. This is anticipated from existing theory, as the radiative eﬀects decrease
Figure 9. Experiments (B3, B6, B1) showing ﬂow velocity eﬀect on the dependence of ﬂame length on
