doi:10.1080/00102202.2017.1404587,doi,Boundary Layer Effect on Opposed-Flow Flame Spread and Flame Length over Thin Polymethyl-Methacrylate in Microgravity,14,13,"with an increase in the eﬀective velocity that the ﬂame encounters as the boundary layer
thickness decreases. The experimental ﬂame shape also shows an increasing ﬂame size
and radiation measurements are consistent with a hotter ﬂame as the development
length decreases. However, the spread rate does not show any signiﬁcant increase except
for a few tests. An experiment where the ﬂame is allowed to propagate all the way to the
edge of the boundary layer is necessary before the computational results can be
deﬁnitively veriﬁed. The ﬂame length, crucial to determine the size of the ﬂame and
therefore the heat transfer to the fuel bed, seems to be a better candidate than the spread
rate to show the eﬀect of the boundary layer. The computational model should also be
improved to include transport in the width-wise direction.
