doi:10.1080/00102202.2025.2465790,doi,Flame Spread Over Thin Circular PMMA Rods: Experiments,6,5,"flow direction with minimum flow disturbance. The fuel rods are secured inside the 
steel capillary using synthetic glue applied over a length of around 5 mm at the bottom 
end and inserted inside the capillary tube. The fuels are ignited by a Nichrome coil, 
which is energized by a 24 V DC source. The flow-conditioning unit (see Figure 2(b)) of 
the combustion tunnel is a modular stainless steel duct comprised of a 12 V DC fan at 
the bottom, followed by a 25 mm long section of honeycomb flow straightener. The flow 
speed is varied by controlling a supply voltage to the fan using a voltage regulator. The 
flow speed is measured at a plane located at the center of the test section by using a one- 
directional hot wire anemometer (TSI-9535). The complete combustion tunnel is 
enclosed within an airtight 44 liter outer polycarbonate enclosure with provision for 
an inlet and outlet to fill and exhaust the gases (oxygen and nitrogen) to set an 
environment of desired oxygen concentration.
The microgravity experiments are carried out in a 2.5 s microgravity drop tower at IIT 
Madras (Nikhil et al. 2018). The experimental setup used for the microgravity experiments 
is the same as that used for the normal gravity experiments, as shown in Figure 2(a,b). The 
drop capsule (see Figure 2(c)) for the microgravity experiment is provided with the 
necessary space for accommodating the entire experimental setup along with the control 
unit and data acquisition system for the operation of the experiment. There is a power 
supply system to power all the power-consuming instruments on board the drop capsule.
The flame spread process at both normal and microgravity conditions is captured using 
a Canon M50 Mark 2 digital camera at a rate of 50 FPS. The flame spread rate is obtained by 
tracking the leading edge of a flame. The shape and growth of flame are characterized by 
tracking the height and width of the spreading flame by using an in-house image processing 
algorithm called Flame Spread Analyser (FSA) developed in MATLAB.
