Experiments are carried out on cast PMMA rods of circular cross-sections of diameters, 
which varied from 0.5 mm to 10 mm in both normal gravity and microgravity environ­
ments. The oxygen levels in the experiments are 21%, 23% and 40%. Selected experiments in 
normal gravity are also carried out at various other oxygen levels in the range of 21% to 60%. 
The externally imposed opposed flow speeds are also varied in the experiments over the 
range of 0 cm/s to 25 cm/s in steps of 5 cm/s. Table 1 presents the summary of parameter 
variation considered (like diameters, flow speed, and oxygen concentrations) in the current 
study.
Effect of flow speed on flame spread in normal gravity and microgravity
Experiments are first carried out for various fuel diameters in normal- and microgravity 
environments at atmospheric conditions of 21% oxygen and 1 atmosphere pressure. 
Figure 3 shows instantaneous images of spreading flame in normal gravity (Figure 3(a)) 
and in microgravity (Figure 3(b)) for fuel diameters from 0.5 mm to 10 mm. In normal 
gravity, the flames are slender and laminar, and their length is seen to increase with the 
increase in fuel diameter. In microgravity, there is a drastic change in flame shape. They are 
wider due to the absence of inward-induced flow due to buoyancy. For small diameters 
