doi:10.1080/00102202.2025.2465790,doi,Flame Spread Over Thin Circular PMMA Rods: Experiments,8,7,"(0.5 mm, 1 mm, and 1.5 mm), the flames are longer and wider than their normal gravity 
counterparts. For larger diameters (6 mm and 10 mm), flames have become shorter and 
wider than the corresponding normal gravity flames. Since the microgravity test duration is 
short (2.5 s), the flames are still growing. During the microgravity test duration, while 
a clear movement of the flame front is observed for fuels of diameter 1.5 mm or lesser, the 
flame front is nearly stationary for 6 mm and 10 mm fuel diameters. Further, microgravity 
tests with forced convective flow are carried out only on 1 mm and 0.5 mm fuel diameters.
Here, in normal gravity experiments, the fuel length and test times are sufficient to obtain 
steady flame spread rates. In the case of microgravity experiments, one has to choose the 
experimental parameters in such a way that one has to get steady flame spread rates in an 
available 2.5 s of microgravity duration. A flame spread process is simulated using an in-house 
CFD flame spread solver to determine the microgravity experimental parameters. The CFD 
solver was developed to simulate the flame spread process when a steadily spreading flame in 
normal gravity conditions is subjected to a change of gravity from normal gravity to micro­
gravity in a step-change manner. This process of change of gravity is the same as a change of 
gravity event that occurs in a ground-based microgravity drop tower facility. The main 
advantage of the solver is that one can simulate the flame spread process over a longer 
duration of microgravity to quantify the experimental results obtained in the short duration 
drop tests. Here, flame spread over 1 mm diameter PMMA rod fuels over the range of external 
flow speeds is simulated to decide the experimental conditions to obtain steady flame spread 
rates in a microgravity drop test. The simulations show that, for PMMA cylindrical rods of 
diameter 1 mm and less and external flow speeds of 5 cm/s and above, the transition of 
spreading flame from normal gravity to microgravity happens within 1.5 s from the change of 
gravity. Therefore, the spread rate obtained in the time period of 1.5 s < t < 2.5 s is the steady- 
state spread rate. Therefore, microgravity experiments are conducted over the 1 mm and 
0.5 mm diameter PMMA cylindrical rods with flow speeds of 5 cm/s and above. Further 
details on this is available in the Appendix.
Figure 4 shows instantaneous images of spreading flames in normal gravity (top) and 
microgravity (down) along 1 mm and 0.5 mm diameter fuel rods at three external flow 
speeds, namely, 5 cm/s, 10 cm/s, and 25 cm/s. The oxygen level is 21%, and the pressure is 1 
atmosphere. The normal gravity flames are laminar with a blue leading edge followed by an 
orange-yellow extent downstream. Flames over 0.5 mm fuel have length (h) and width (w), 
which are nearly the same, giving them a spherical appearance. Flames over 1 mm cylinder 
are taller and have a higher aspect ratio (h/w). With the increase in external opposed flow 
speed, the flame is seen to become shorter for both 0.5 mm and 1 mm diameter fuel rods.
The flames in microgravity have a typical “mushroom shape” appearance, are less 
intense, and are orange in color. The flames are larger compared to the normal gravity 
flames. The bottom of the flame is marked with random fluctuations due to the pyrolyzate 
jet emanating from bursting bubbles formed inside the PMMA rod. With an increase in the 
flow speed, the flame size reduces in both height and width, and at a high flow speed of 25  
cm/s, they tend to look similar to normal gravity flame. The fluctuations at the flame leading 
edge are also seen to reduce with an increased flow speed, probably due to increased stream- 
wise momentum. It may also be noted that bursts of fuel jet are seen only near the flame 
leading edge where the fuel (PMMA) is still hard. As the fuel softens, fuel jets become much 
less intense for visual observation. Figure 4(b) shows the top and bottom views of flame 
spreading over a 1 mm diameter fuel rod in normal gravity (upper sub-figure) and quiescent 
