doi:10.1080/00102202.2025.2465790,doi,Flame Spread Over Thin Circular PMMA Rods: Experiments,9,8,"microgravity (lower sub-figure) environments. These images of the flame are taken from 
the top and bottom of the burning fuel to get an azimuthal view of the flame and the 
fluctuations. The fuel is placed vertically on top of a transparent polycarbonate plate 
without a sample holder to get an unobstructed view of the flame. The bottom end of the 
fuel rod is carefully stuck onto the plate top using a small amount of instant glue. The 
experiments are conducted in both normal gravity and microgravity for the condition of no 
external flow. The top and bottom views of the normal gravity flame show near perfect 
circular shapes with minor distortions. For the microgravity flame, the top and the bottom 
views show distortion of circular shape due to sporadic fuel jets.
The streaks of flame coming out, clearly seen in the bottom view, are due to fuel jets from 
bursting bubbles. Note that the camera settings are different in the top and bottom views to 
capture the desired feature of the flame. The visible streaks of fuel jets are seen to traverse 
a radial distance of the order of 5 mm. While the bubble formation is present during both 
normal gravity and microgravity flame spread processes, interestingly, the flame distortion 
is visibly prominent in microgravity. This could possibly be due to the suppression of fuel 
jet momentum by opposing momentum of radially inward buoyancy induced flow of air in 
normal gravity. In a quiescent microgravity environment, there is no opposing momentum 
of air. The fuel jet streaks have also been noted by the researchers in their low-pressure 
combustion study of PMMA rods (Thomsen et al. 2019, 2020). The frequency of sporadic 
radial fuel jets released due to the bursting of bubbles decreases for larger fuel diameters 
(Thomsen et al. 2019). Also, Yang, Hamins, and Salva Donnelly (1999) reported the 
distortion of flame shape due to releasing fuel vapor by bursting bubbles in the solid fuel 
and ejection of the material itself by bursting bubbles in the combustion of polymer spheres 
in reduced gravity.
This observed mushroom shape flame in microgravity is very different from the rounder 
shape flame reported in previous studies. This may be due to the higher heating rate of the 
solid fuel by the flame. In the case of cylindrical fuels, the heat flux to the solid fuel increases 
