microgravity at different time instances in a drop test at no external flow (0 cm/s). The flame 
shape is distorted whenever there is a bursting of fuel vapor and rounder when there is no 
bursting.
Figure 5(a) shows an instantaneous image of spreading flame in microgravity along 
a 1 mm diameter fuel rod at no external flow (0 cm/s). The oxygen level is 21%, and the 
pressure is 1 atmosphere. Here, the image is taken with increased aperture setting (changed 
from f 7.1 to f 3.5) to capture the leading edge of the flame. The microgravity flame is now 
visible as mushroom-shaped fluctuating part surmounted on top of a less intense blue flame 
leading edge. This shows that the spherical shape flame is distorted by the fuel vapor jet 
emanating from the bursting bubbles. However, at higher aperture settings, flame tracking 
become less accurate due to over saturation, increased unwanted reflections and reduced 
image frame rate. Therefore, the images (like shown in Figure 4) were taken with low 
aperture setting.
