Effect of oxygen concentration on flame spread
To explore the flame spread phenomena at elevated oxygen levels, experiments are con­
ducted at two higher oxygen concentrations (23% and 40%) for 1 mm diameter fuel rods in 
both normal gravity and microgravity environments and for various external flow speeds. 
The process of filling oxygen to the combustion enclosure, measuring the concentration and 
accessing the uncertainty is available in the supplementary material.
Figure 10 shows the instantaneous images of spreading flame over a 1 mm diameter fuel 
rod at 23% and 40% oxygen levels exposed to external flow speeds of 5 cm/s, 10 cm/s, and 
25 cm/s in normal gravity and microgravity environments. At an oxygen level of 23%, the 
flame shape and trends with flow speed appear similar to ones at a 21% oxygen level, except 
that the flames are larger in size and brighter. However, at 40% oxygen level, the flames look 
very different in both normal gravity and microgravity compared to flames at 21% and 23% 
oxygen levels. The flames are immensely bright and marked with intense fluctuation. The 
flames are much longer and wider compared to flames of corresponding gravity and flow 
conditions at near-ambient oxygen levels. The length of the pyrolyzing segment of fuel, in 
the downstream of the flame leading edge is also much longer at 40% oxygen.
Figure 10. Instantaneous front view images of spreading flame along PMMA rods with a diameter of 
1 mm at 23% and 40% oxygen concentrations in normal gravity and microgravity at various opposed 
flow speeds.
