In an earlier study on opposed flow and concurrent flow flame spreads along PE-coated 
wires (Ma et al. 2022), the flames were seen to be laminar in opposed flow configuration and 
turbulent in concurrent flow configuration. The opposed flow flame height increased 
steeply with HRR up to a maximum of 30 kW/m, and turbulent concurrent flame height 
exhibited a less steep increase with HRR, starting from the value of about 30 kW/m. In 
upward flame spread studies over walls PMMA (Tsai and Drysdale 2002; Tu and Quintiere  
1991) and corrugated cardboard (Gollner, Williams, and Rangwala 2011), the flame transi­
tions from laminar to turbulent as it grows. The laminar flame height increases steeply with 
flame wattage and transitions to a turbulent flame where the flame height versus flame 
wattage curve has a shallow positive slope. The laminar to turbulent transition was noted at 
about 20–30 kW/m.
