part of the plot, contributing to the flame spread data for most thin fuel rods experimented 
so far. The size of the symbols in the plot reflects the magnitude of the opposed flow speed, 
and the color of the symbols indicates the oxygen concentration at which the experiments 
were conducted. Also shown in the figure are the curves representing analytical predictions 
of the thin model of Delichatsios et al. (2000) and thermally thin and thick models of 
Bhattacharjee and Delzeit (2022) (only for normal gravity) for specific oxygen levels 
indicated in the figures.
Conclusions
In the present work, opposed flow downward flame spread experiments are conducted on 
cast circular PMMA rods of 0.5 mm and 1 mm diameters in both normal gravity and 
microgravity environments. Flame spread, shape, and behavior are studied at ambient 
(21%) and elevated (23% and 40%) oxygen concentrations in both normal gravity and 
microgravity environments at various external, opposed flow speeds ranging from 0 cm/s to 
25 cm/s. A limited set of experiments are also conducted in normal gravity for no external 
flow at various oxygen concentrations varying from 21% to 60%. The major findings from 
the study are as follows,
(1) The present experimental work contributes to the flame spread data for cast PMMA 
in normal gravity and microgravity environments for the rods of the smallest 
diameter (1 mm and 0.5 mm) so far in the thermal regime. Such data have been 
difficult to obtain due to fabrication challenges.
(2) At low oxygen levels (here, 21% and 23%) in a normal gravity environment, the 
spreading flame is laminar. While in the microgravity environment, the flame 
acquires the shape of a mushroom with a distorted leading edge because of sporadic 
fuel jets emanating from the bursting bubbles in the solid fuel. This mushroom- 
shaped flame is very different from the round-shaped flame reported in past works 
on large-diameter cylindrical PMMA rods.
(3) At low oxygen levels (here 21% and 23%), for both fuel rod diameters of 0.5 mm and 
1 mm, the flame spreads faster in microgravity at all external flow speeds compared 
to the corresponding conditions in normal gravity. This is due to the longer preheat 
COMBUSTION SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY
897"
doi:10.1080/00102202.2025.2465790,doi,Flame Spread Over Thin Circular PMMA Rods: Experiments,21,20,"length in microgravity. Further, the flame spread rate decreases with an increase in 
the external opposed flow speed in both normal gravity and microgravity environ­
ments. The decrease of flame spread rate with external flow speed is steeper in 
microgravity. At high oxygen levels (here, 40%), the flame spread rates in normal 
gravity and microgravity are nearly the same at all external flow speeds.
(4) The sectional view of the partially burnt 1 mm PMMA rod under the microscope 
showed bubbles all over the pyrolysis and preheat segments. Numerous small 
bubbles near the surface and fewer larger bubbles within the solid are noted. The 
bubbles formed in microgravity appear bigger (50−280 µm) compared to those 
formed in normal gravity (40−180 µm). The measured solid cone half angle (or 
regression angle) of the pyrolysis segment is about 7° in normal gravity and about 
1.5° in microgravity.
(5) The laminar flame in normal gravity is seen to transition to a turbulent flame at an 
oxygen level of about 30−35%. The flame spread rate curve with oxygen has 
a shallower slope 
