for oxygen below 30% and a much steeper 
slope ( Vf
Vf;0 ¼ 0:32XO2) for oxygen levels above 35%. The analytical model in literature, 
which is essentially for laminar flames, predicts the flame spread rate at low oxygen 
reasonably well, but deviation increases at oxygen levels above 35%. At high oxygen 
levels, the flame spread rate becomes insensitive to both gravity and external flow 
speeds. It is to be noted that the distortion and fluctuation in flame shape due to 
bursting bubbles at the fuel surface is typical of thin fuels, as the ones studied here are 
not prominent in thicker fuels.
(6) The increase in oxygen concentration also increases the flame height. The flame 
height grows steeply with oxygen levels at a concentration below 30% and less steeply 
for oxygen concentration above 35%. The flame height plotted with the heat release 
rate shows a near linear increase h, _Q
