Transition of flame spread in 1g to 0g step change encountered in a typical drop test
To understand the transient response of flame, spreading steadily in normal gravity experience 
sudden change in gravity level from normal gravity to microgravity, an existing CFD solver is used 
to simulate the flame spread process.
At first, the flame spreading steadily over a 1 mm diameter PMMA rod at 21% oxygen and 5 cm/s of 
opposed flow in normal gravity conditions is subjected to a change of flow speed from 5 cm/s to 10 cm/s.
Figure A1 shows the variation of spread rate with time during the change of flame spread when 
a flow speed changes from 5 cm/s to 10 cm/s. Initially flame is spreading at the steady spread rate of 
0.56 mm/s. When the flow is changed suddenly to 10 cm/s, the spread rate decreases and reaches 
a new spread rate of 0.55 mm/s. This response is quite fast. Initially, the spread rate decreases and 
reaches 0.55 mm/s within around 0.7 s. Thereafter, it oscillates about the steady state value and attains 
a steady state. One can clearly see that this oscillation is very minimal, which is very difficult to 
capture in the experiments. With this confidence, a time response of a flame steadily in normal 
gravity when it is exposed to microgravity in a very short time, similar to the microgravity drop test, is 
simulated in the next sections.
Here, in the case of steadily spreading flame in normal gravity conditions, the gravity is changed 
from normal gravity conditions to microgravity. The process of how a steady normal gravity flame 
responds to a change in gravity is captured.
Figure A2 shows the variation of flame spread rate with time for the case of flame spread 
over 1 mm PMMA at 21% oxygen and opposed speeds of 2 cm/s, 5 cm/s, 10 cm/s, 15 cm/s, and 
25 cm/s. For time, t < 0 s represents data in normal gravity, t = 0 s is the point of change of 
gravity (1 g to 10−06 g), and t > 0 s is the data of flame spread in microgravity. For the case of 
flame spread with an opposed flow speed of 5 cm/s, in normal gravity, the flame spread rate is 
around 0.55 mm/s.
Figure A1. Variation of flame simulated spread rate with time during a steady spreading normal gravity 
flame transforms into a microgravity flame at 21% O2 and different flow speeds.
