doi:10.1080/00102202.2025.2465790,doi,Flame Spread Over Thin Circular PMMA Rods: Experiments,25,24,"Once the gravity level is changed, initially the spread rate reduces for a very short time 
(around 0.1 s). Afterwards, the spread rate increases sharply and reaches a peak of around 
1.50 mm/s at a time of around t = 3 s. Thereafter spread rate decreases and reaches a constant 
spread rate of around 1.34 mm/s (around 10% lesser compared to peak value) in a time of 
around t = 9 s. For the lower flow speed of 2 cm/s, once the gravity is changed from normal 
gravity condition to microgravity, the flame spread rate shows an increase and decreased 
nature similar to the case of 5 cm/s flow speed. However, the peak in spread rate is high, and 
it takes a longer time to reach the steady spread process. Also, it is expected that, the 
response time decreases with increasing the flow speed. Therefore, simulations are carried 
out for higher flow speeds of 10 cm/s, 15 cm/s, and 25 cm/s. The variation of flame spread 
rate with time for different flow speeds is also included in Fig. A.2. From the figure, it is seen 
that the initial increase and decrease nature of the flame spread rate has vanished as the flow 
speed increases. Also, as the flow speed increases, the flame reaches the steady state early. At 
higher flow speeds of above 15 cm/s, flame clearly reaches a steady state within the 1.5 s of 
change in gravity from normal gravity condition to microgravity condition. The transition of 
spreading flame from normal gravity to microgravity happens within 1.5 s from the change of 
gravity. Therefore the spread rate obtained in the time period of 1.5 s < t < 2.5 s is the steady 
state spread rate.
In experiments, the flame spread rate is obtained by tracking the leading edge of the flame. 
Therefore, it is more useful to construct the graph of leading edge position variation with 
time using the numerical spread rate variation curves (from Figure A.2). Figure A3 is the 
variation of the leading edge position of the flame with respect to time during the transition 
of normal gravity to microgravity for the case of flame spread with 5 cm/s opposed flow 
speed condition.
One can see that the leading edge position with a time curve looks nearly linear. Hence, it 
is difficult to observe the variation in the flame spread rates from the leading edge position 
with the time curve. From this curve, we can see that the average spread rate obtained in 
interval 1.5 s < t < 2.5 s and the steady state are nearly the same at around 1.34 mm/s. Hence, 
one can say that, for PMMA rods of diameter 1 mm and lesser and a flow speed of 5 cm/s 
and above, one can obtain a steady state flame spread rate in short duration microgravity 
drop tests.
Figure A2. Variation of flame simulated spread rate with time during a steady spreading normal gravity 
flame transforms into a microgravity flame at 21% O2 and different flow speeds.
