doi:10.1080/00102202.2025.2595114,doi,Influence of Droplet Size and Fuel Composition on Sooting Behavior in Microgravity Droplet Combustion,,0,"Soot volume fractions were evaluated for droplet combustion experiments conducted on the International Space Station using free-floating droplets of n-heptane and n-decane. Initial droplet diameters were in the range 1.19mm−3.98mm and droplets were burned in a 1atm O2/N2 environment with O2 and N2 mole fractions of 0.21 and 0.79, respectively. Data are presented for time histories of maximum local extinction coefficients, droplet sizes, and radiometer measurements as well as flame-view images in the infrared, visible, and ultraviolet regions of the spectrum, along with backlit droplet images. n-Heptane droplets exhibit decreasing peak soot volume fractions with increasing initial droplet diameter, while n-decane droplets generate higher soot levels than n-heptane droplets with a nonmonotonic droplet size dependence that appears to peak for initial diameters near 3mm. Comparison with previous analyses indicates that earlier soot volume fractions reported for n-heptane droplets were under-evaluated by a factor of two, which is confirmed by the use of synthetic images that were used to evaluate image analysis approaches. The data also show that maximum soot volume fractions rapidly grow and decay, sometimes with a double peak structure, eventually dropping to small levels early in the droplet history. Infrared and visible spectrum images revealed rapid decays in soot luminosity, while UV images showed little variation during the luminous radiation decays. Radiometer data indicate that for large droplets, the decay in soot luminosity does not play a significant role in the transition to a cool-flame burning mode. The time and droplet diameter where the soot volume fraction peaks depended on the initial droplet size but not whether the fuel was n-heptane or n-decane."
