layers and enhanced mixing of fuel and oxidizer. At characteristic relative velocities
greater than 15-20 cm/s, the flame spread rate falls gradually toward a blowoff
extinction (in air). This decrease in flame spread rate is attributed to Damkohler
number effects (Femandez-Pello and Hirano, 1983) where shorter residence times for
chemical reaction to occur actually limit the flame spread process; further thinning of
the oxygen concentration and thermal boundary layers no longer acts to enhance the
mixing and subsequent chemical reaction rate. Rather the flame spread process is
limited by the time for chemical reaction to occur. Thus the sharp peak in flame spread
rate at characteristic relative velocities of 15-20 cm/s appears to represent a critical
condition where the competing effects of limited residence time and enhanced heat
and/or mass transport rates are optimized.
At 30% oxygen, however, the flame spread rate is independent of characteristic
relative velocity over a wide range of flow velocities. In fact, from this data and that
of Fernandez-Pello et al. (1981) for higher oxygen concentrations, it is clear that an
increasing oxygen concentration extends the range over which the flame spread rate
is insensitive to flow. Over this range of characteristic relative velocities at and above
30% oxygen, the flame spread behavior is consistent with the thermally-thin fuel
predictions of de Ris (1969).
