FLAME SPREAD MECHANISMS
In this section the results of the previous sections, along with existing data from other
sources, will be used to develop a flame spread map which demarcates regimes of
flame spread as a function of oxygen concentration and characteristic relative veloc-
ity. For each regime of flame spread, controlling mechanism(s) of flame spread will
be discussed. The flammable area of the map, shown in Figure 10, is divided into
different regions which correspond to regions of flame spread away from extinction
(Region 1)and near extinction (Regions II and III). Regions II and III are divided by
a critical characteristic relative velocity line (dotted line) which connects the low
oxygen limit and characteristic relative velocities where peak flame spread rates are
obtained for 18 and 21% oxygen concentration from Figure 6. The location of these
flame spread regions is based on only a few data points so the boundaries of these
regions are only approximate.
In Region I, where oxygen is plentiful and the opposed flows are not excessive,
kinetics are sufficiently fast so the flame spread process is dominated by gas phase
conduction. In this region a simple thermal theory such as that of de Ris (1969) is
adequate to describe the flame spread process. In the thermal theory, flame spread
rates for thermally thin fuel are independent of opposed flow velocity. Data from
Figure 6 indicate that at some oxygen concentration between 21-30% oxygen the
Downloaded by [University of Alberta] at 06:13 31 December 2014"
doi:10.1080/00102209108951711,doi,Mechanisms of Microgravity Flame Spread Over a Thin Solid Fuel: Oxygen and Opposed Flow Effects,15,14,"246
S.L. OLSON
flame spread process becomes insensitive to opposed flow effects (spread rate is
constant in Figure 6 for 30% oxygen for characteristic relative velocities between
1.8-90 cm/s). This region widens in terms of opposed flow at higher oxygen concentra-
tions narrows at lower oxygen concentrations and ends at a critical oxygen concentra-
tion below which finite-rate kinetics cannot be neglected.
For this material the oxygen level which marks the lowest point of Region I is
estimated to be near 21% oxygen based upon two observations. First, in Figure 6
flame spread is never independent of flow velocity for 21% oxygen (air), but a wide
region ofconstant spread rate is observed at 30% oxygen. Additionally, the Damkoh-
ler number correlation obtained for normal gravity opposed forced convective flow
tests by Fernandez-Pello et al. (1981) reveals that a fall-off of non-dimensional flame
spread rate from its asymptotic value begins at ~ 21% oxygen; data at and below this
oxygen concentration fall below the envelope of the remaining higher oxygen con-
centration data even for very high Damkohler numbers (low velocity opposed flows).
These observations indicate that the flame spread has transitioned from the gas-phase
conduction-limited region into the residence time-limited, finite-rate kinetics Region
Il , which has historically been described by a Damkohler number correlation (Fer-
nandez-Pello and Hirano, 1983).
In Region II, which is tfie high characteristic relative velocity near-limit flame
spread region in Figure 10. flame spread is limited by residence time of the fuel and
oxygen in the flame zone. Residence times become comparable to chemical reaction
times and at small Damkohler numbers the flame is blown off. Experimental data of
Fernandez-Pello et al. indicates that as oxygen concentration is reduced, the opposed
flow velocity where finite rate chemistry becomes important also decreases because of
lower flame temperatures. Blowoff extinction limits, however, are not consistent
between different experiments either in terms of Damkohler number or dimensional
quantities such as characteristic relative velocity and oxygen concentration, as can be
seen from Figure 6. This is an indication that blowoff extinction is sensitive to the
particular velocity profile in the different experiments.
In Region III, the low characteristic velocity near-limit region in Figure 10, finite
rate kinetics is again the limiting mechanism of flame spread, but here it is through
the influence of reduced oxygen transport to the flame. At extinction the heat loss
rates as compared to the slowed heat generation rates become excessive and quenc-
hing occurs (Olson et al., 1989). This region will be discussed further in the next
section.
DISCUSSION
The changes in flame appearance in Region II I may be explained in terms of fuel and
oxidizer concentrations. As the opposed flow velocity decreases, the boundary layers
thicken and the transport rate of oxygen (convective and diffusive) to the leading edge
is decreased such that mixing of fuel and oxidizer occurs further from the fuel surface.
The blowing velocity from the fuel surface, estimated to be as high as 0.5 cm/s, should
also become relatively more significant as the opposed flow is reduced. The observa-
tions of increasing flame width and standoff distance are consistent with this reason-
ing. The dark zone next to the fuel surface at the low velocities appears to consist of
hot fuel vapors and very little oxygen. The observed reduction in intensity of the
leading edge of the flame is directly linked to its increased standoff distance, and may
too be due to a dilution of the premixing of fuel and oxidizer at the leading edge by
combustion products.
From the surface temperature histories it is apparent that pyrolysis lengths are
Downloaded by [University of Alberta] at 06:13 31 December 2014"
doi:10.1080/00102209108951711,doi,Mechanisms of Microgravity Flame Spread Over a Thin Solid Fuel: Oxygen and Opposed Flow Effects,16,15,"MICROGRAVITY FLAME SPREAD
247
longer as the opposed flow is reduced even though in most cases the pyrolysis
temperatures are similar (transition temperatures 650 K; burnout temperatures
750 K). Kashiwagi, Ohlemiller, and Werner (1987), and earlier Vovelle, Mellottee,
and Delbourgo (1982) and Blackshear and Murty (1965) demonstrated that, regard-
less of heating rate, ambient oxygen significantly increased the gasification mass flux
ofcellulose samples at a given temperature because of the strong exothermic oxidative
pyrolysis reactions. It is postulated here that the increasing length of the pyrolysis
zone with decreasing opposed flow velocity (for those tests where pyrolysis tem-
peratures are similar) is due to a reduction in oxygen concentration at the surface of
the sample as the opposed flow is reduced. This in turn reduces the exotherrnicity of
the pyrolysis reaction and subsequent gasification rates of fuel.
The tests where the transition temperatures are 525 K are intriguing. It is possible
that these flames represent an extreme of non-oxidative pyrolysis where the fuel is
pyrolyzing at 125 K less than it does in oxygen-containing environments. This type of
pyrolysis has been observed by Kashiwagi et at. (1987) as well, who found differences
of up to 200 K in pyrolysis temperatures for cellulose between air and pure nitrogen.
The fact that these flames have reasonable flame spread rates despite the low fuel
temperatures indicates that sufficient fuel is being generated to sustain the flame
spread process, apparently through endothermic non-oxidative pyrolysis.
For a thermally thin fuel, the gas-phase preheat length varies inversely with charac-
teristic relative velocity, and is commonly defined as (XI v,:, (for example Frey and T'ien,
1979). Solid phase preheat lengths for thermally-thin fuels directly reflect gas phase
preheat lengths. Thus the solid phase preheat length should increase as opposed flow
is decreased.
The solid phase temperature data, however, reveals that there is no single trend with
opposed flow. To understand this, it is important to note the regime of flame spread
for the test conditions. For example, all test conditions but the lowest velocity test for
the 30% oxygen data are within the conduction-dominated Region I of Figure 10;
opposed flow velocity has little effect on flame spread rate while preheat lengths do
show a slight increase with decreasing velocity, as expected. For the lowest velocity
test (within Region III), however, the preheat length and flame spread rate are
decreased, contrary to the anticipated trend.
For the 21 % and 18% oxygen data a similar trend is observed; for higher velocity
tests (within Region II), preheat lengths and flame spread rates increase with decreas-
ing opposed flow as expected. However, for the low velocity tests in Region III, the
preheat length is reduced, as is the flame spread rate. These results suggest that for
the lowest velocities, flame spread is controlled by a different mechanism than flame
spread in the other two regions. Preheat lengths do not scale with opposed flow; rather
they simply continue to reflect the flame spread rate. It can be concluded from this
that conductive heat transfer is not the limiting process for flame spread in Region III.
It is suggested here that oxygen transport rate from the ambient gas to the flame
zone is critical to these low gravity flames. It has been postulated above that oxidizer
penetration to the fuel surface explains the decrease in local pyrolysis rate with
decreasing velocity. While small changes in local pyrolysis rate do not appear to
influence the flame spread rate (30% oxygen), it is clear that large changes will affect
the flame spread rate through a reduction in fuel concentrations, subsequent chemical
reaction rates and resulting heat release rates. Oxygen transport decreases as either
opposed flow velocity decreases or oxygen concentration decreases, so it can be used
to describe the observed flame spread behavior.
Reduced oxygen penetration to the fuel surface has been numerically modelled by
Chen and T'ien (1986), whose model predicts a reduction in oxygen penetration
Downloaded by [University of Alberta] at 06:13 31 December 2014"
doi:10.1080/00102209108951711,doi,Mechanisms of Microgravity Flame Spread Over a Thin Solid Fuel: Oxygen and Opposed Flow Effects,17,16,"248
S.L. OLSON
beneath the flame as Damkohler number is increased (opposed flow is reduced) for
the case of no surface radiation. At the highest Damkohler number studied, no
oxygen penetration into the fuel side of the reaction zone is noted. A similar trend is
found with radiative loss included for even higher Damkohler numbers (Chen,
1986a). However, in that study fuel pyrolysis appears to be drastically reduced due to
the cooler surface temperature. Predicted pyrolysis lengths are reduced as opposed
flow is decreased, whereas in this work, pyrolysis lengths are found to increase as
opposed flow is reduced.
Numerical modelling results of Bhattacharjee et al. (l990b) have predicted a trend
in flame spread rate with opposed flow similar to those found here experimentally in
air and at 18% oxygen by including either gas phase or solid phase radiative loss. It
should be noted, however, that most of their computations were done at 50% oxygen,
which should be independent of flow over a wide range of conditions. This may be
because the fuel thickness for the computations (albeit still thermally-thin) is ap-
proximately four times as thick as the fuel in this work. Fuel thickness has been
observed (Olson, 1987) to strongly influence the transition to near-limit flame spread
and ultimately the extinction limits.
SUMMARY
Flame spread over a thermally-thin fuel has been studied in mierogravity quiescent
and low velocity opposed forced flow. Flame appearance in microgravity is strongly
affected by the opposed flow. As flow decreases flames move further from the surface
both in terms of overall width and in terms of standoff distances. This trend is
consistent with increasing boundary layer thicknesses as opposed flow is reduced. For
very low velocities a dark zone appears between the fuel and flame, which is believed
to be a very fuel rich zone containing hot pyrolysis products but very little oxygen.
Comparison with normal and elevated gravity data has revealed a non-monotonic
trend in the flame spread rate with opposed flow over the entire range of flammability
of the fuel in air. This suggests that different mechanisms control the rate of spread
at low velocities compared to high velocity opposed flows. While the high velocity
behavior is understood as being due to residence-time limitations on the chemical
reaction rates, the low velocity behavior is still not well understood. It is clear that
linite rate chemistry is again the ultimate reason for the behavior. and it is proposed
that the mechanism of flame spread at low velocities is due to oxidizer transport
limitations. Similar flame spread behavior is found at 18% oxygen, while at 30%
oxygen a range of opposed flow is found where flame spread rate is constant, which
agrees with the thermal theory of de Ris.
Surface temperature histories indicate that fuel preheat lengths in the microgravity
near-limit region are not a direct function ofopposed flow but simply reflect the flame
spread rate; as flame spread rate increases the preheat length also increases. This is in
contrast with conduction-controlled flame spread where the preheat length varies
inversely with opposed flow velocity, and the resulting flame spread is constant.
Pyrolysis lengths. however do increase with decreasing opposed flow. The increased
length with decreasing flow is believed to be due to reduced oxidative pyrolysis as flow
is red uced.
Transition temperatures, the approximate temperature where the fuel preheating
changes to pyrolysis, occur from 600-675 K; the transiton occurs at slightly higher
temperatures as oxygen concentration decreases. The exceptions to this are the very
low velocity and/or low oxygen concentration flames, where preheating of the fuel by
Downloaded by [University of Alberta] at 06:13 31 December 2014"
doi:10.1080/00102209108951711,doi,Mechanisms of Microgravity Flame Spread Over a Thin Solid Fuel: Oxygen and Opposed Flow Effects,18,17,"MICROGRAVITY FLAME SPREAD
249
the leading edge of the flame is suppressed due to the weakness and remoteness of the
gas phase reaction, and the fuel progresses from preheat to pyrolysis beneath the
flame at temperatures as low as 525 K. Burnout temperatures, when burnout is
observed, occurred very consistently at 750 K.
A flame spread map is constructed which indicates regimes where different mechan-
isms control the flame spread process. A new limiting flame spread region of oxidizer
transport-limited chemical reaction is postulated in the very low velocity and/or low
oxygen concentration region of the flame spread map. Regions of flame spread
controlled by gas phase conduction or flow residence times are outlined in terms of
oxygen concentration and characteristic relative velocity as well. While the de Ris
thermal theory is useful when infinite rate kinetics is a valid assumption, numerical
models are needed to predict the near-limit (i.e. finite-rate kinetics) flame spread
behavior.
