INTRODUCTION 
This paper studies the detailed characteristics of horizontal flame spread over a thin 
fuel in normal and elevated gravity regime. The motivation for this study is that to the 
Downloaded by [Michigan State University] at 22:11 23 February 2015"
doi:10.1080/00102209508907795,doi,A NUMERICAL ANALYSIS OF HORIZONTAL FLAME SPREAD OVER A THIN FUEL IN NORMAL AND ELEVATED GRAVITY REGIME,4,3,"HORIZONTAL FLAME SPREAD 
61 
knowledge of the authors no such theoretical analysis has been given before. This may 
be due to the following reasons. As shown in Figure 1, sketched according to the 
experimental observations of Hirano et al. (1974a), the flame configuration in normal 
gravity, with the top side separated from the bottom side, is not symmetric with respect 
to the fuel plate, unlike an upward spreading or downward spreading flame parallel to 
the direction of gravity. The interaction between fluid mechanics and combustion 
further complicates this problem. The induced flow resulting from the substantial 
density variation caused by the temperature difference between the ambient and the 
flame cannot be prescribed in advance but must be provided as a part of the solution. 
Due to the presence of the horizontal fuel plate, part of the upward induced flow turns 
around and becomes concurrent with the lower spreading flame, but the upper flame 
meets an incoming entrainment. Therefore, the horizontal flame spread appears to 
have the dual characteristics of both upward and downward flame spread. This seems 
logical since the orientation of the horizontal spreading flame lies between the latter 
two flame s~readine modes. 
- 
As just mentioned, to our knowledge no complete analysis of horizontal flame spread 
is available in the literature. However. horizontal flame s~read has been observed in 
experimental work on the effects of orientation on flame spread over a thin fuel. Hirano 
et al. (1974b) used Schlieren photography to assess qualitatively the heat transfer 
modes to the virgin fuel ahead of several spreading flame fronts over a thin fuel in 
different orientations. The corresponding flame spread rates were also measured. Two 
