types of flame spread were classified: one was constant-velocity downward flame 
spread in the range -90 to -30 degrees from the horizontal; the other was accelerative 
upward flame spread at the angle of zero to 90 degrees. The controlling mechanism for 
the former type is streamwise gas-phase conduction, whereas for the latter one it is 
concurrent convection. In Hirano et a/. (1974a), for the case of horizontal flame spread 
a gas stream flowing in the samedirection as the flamespread on the bottom side of the 
thin fuel was identified via particle track photography. At the same time, there existed 
an opposed and outward deflected flow of entrainment ahead of the flame front on the 
top side. Hirano et al. concluded that the heat received by the solid fuel to pyrolyze is 
mainly from the flame underneath. Later, Kashiwagi and Newman (1976) invedigated 
downward flame spread over a thin fuel subjected to various external radiant heat 
fluxes from the vertical to the horizontal positions. They found that the flame spread 
rate is independent of the angle of inclination as long as the lower flame is stable. 
Otherwise, the lower flame, subjected to high external radiant flux, appears unstable in 
the regime of low-value angle of inclinations, and it results in an accelerating flame 
spread. 
Ray et ul. (1980) investigated the heat transfer mechanisms from the flame front to 
the unburnt fuel for the flame propagating horizontally over a PMMA, a thick fuel, by 
experiment. Their results showed that in the early stages of the fire, heat conduction 
through solid isdominant, radiation from the flame becomes more important as the fire 
grows in size. However, for small-scale propagating flame over a horizontal thin fuel, it 
is not so sooty and the flame size will not change too much due to the burnout. 
Therefore, the portion of radiation in heat transfer for thin fuel may not beas important 
as that for thick fuel. 
A recent work by Duh (1992) should also be mentioned, since part of his theoretical 
analysis, published in Duh and Chen (1991), serves as a foundation for the present 
study. Duh successfully developed a combustion model to study downward flame 
spread over a thermally-thin fuel vertically in a gravitational field. Within the elevated 
gravity domain, the predicted flame spread rates agree very well with the measurements 
obtained by Altenkirch et al. (1980). Also, the interplay between naturally-convective 
flow and flame was clearly demonstrated. In experiments, Duh measured the flame 
spread rate (v as a function of the angle of inclination and obtained the correlation, 
