RESULTS AND DISCUSSION 
3.1 
Burning in Normal Gravity 
The thin fuel sheet, a cellulose material, is burned in the atmosphere and its orientation 
is perpendicular to the gravity vector. All of the properties are the same as those in Duh 
(1992).The normalized parameters are listed in Table 11. The flame structure at y = 1 is 
analyzed first in detail. It serves as the foundation and a reference case for the extensive 
studies, such as the effects of changing gravity. The horizontal flame spread rate is 
assumed as a constant. Note that it is not prescribed in advance, but provided as a part 
of solution. The computed flame spread rate (V') is 0.2014 cm/s in present case. As 
expected, this value is greater than the downward spread rate, 0.15 cm/s, obtained by 
Duh (1992). 
Figure 2 is a combination of isotherm distribution and velocity vector field that 
demonstrates the interaction between the flame and fluid flow. Thedomain of interest is 
truncated from the computational one in order to illustrate the significant features. 
From a global view, the flame appearance resembles that observed in experiments 
(Hirano et a/., 1974a; Hirano et al., 1974b; Kashiwagi et a/., 1976). The flame has three 
high-temperature regions. One of them is just behind the burnout point. This can be 
regarded as a combustion plume, where the reaction is still maintained without the 
existence of the fuel plate (see Fig. 4). The other two are located at the upper and the 
lower flame fronts, respectively, separated by the fuel plate. These flame fronts not only 
provide heat to pyrolyze the solid fuel but also serve as continuous ignition'sources to 
propagate the flame. 
, The bulk fluid motion is driven upward by natural convection. It carries the hot 
combustion products upward and forms a thermal plume on the top side of the fuel 
