plate. Because no reaction occuis and the products mix with ambient fluids, the 
temperature of the rising thermal plume decreases. The upward flow motion also 
causes an entrainment of air from the ambient toward the flame. Therefore, the upper 
flame front meets an incoming oxidizer flow, equivalent to the so-called opposed flame 
spread. On the bottom side, the upward flow motion is decelerated and deflected 
because of the presence of the fuel place and the blowing velocity resulting from fuel 
pyrolysis. A stagnation point (u = 0 and v =0) is found at x = - 10, y = - 1. On the 
right-hand side of x = - 10, the deflected flow passes the combustion plume and joins 
theentrainment from theambient to move toward the thermal plume. Theother part of 
the turning-around flow moves concurrently with the lower spreading flame front. 
Therefore, the lower flame front precedes the upper flame front. Also, the lower flame is 
pushed by the rising fluid flow and so is shifted closer to the fuel plate, which 
contributes more heat flux to the solid. This can be seen in Figure 6. These phenomena 
confirm that the mechanism of horizontal flame spread is determined by the flame 
underneath the fuel plate. 
