Figure 3 is the distribution of fuel and oxygen mass fraction. Thereexists a flammable 
mixture of oxygen and fuel just ahead of the respective flame front that is continuously 
ignited to sustain the flame spread. Around the burnout point area, where the 
combustion plume is, diffusion flame characteristics are exhibited. In the thermal 
plume, since no reaction occurs, the fuel is carried upward by induced flow to the 
ambient. Although the oxygen is driven to the flame by the entrainment and the 
induced flow, it is unable to penetrate the thermal plume and combustion plume. This is 
because the combustion products occupy the region surrounded by the two plumes. 
Figure 4 is the distribution of nondimensional fuel reactivity. The figure clearly 
shows that the combustion is divided into an upper flame and a lower flame by the fuel 
plate. In the upper flame, the tail of the reaction zone is so weak that it cannot stand for 
a strong flame stretch due to the accelerative gas stream (see Fig. 2). Therefore, it is 
broken into two parts. Under the fuel plate, the flame meets the upward-rising cold air 
and loses a great deal of heat to the fuel plate. Consequently, the reaction in the middle 
of the lower flame is slowed down. The most reactive zone occurs at the flame fronts, 
because they need to generate enough heat to stabilize the flame, to pyrolyze the solid 
fuel, and to initiate the following reaction to propagate the flame. The other active 
reaction zone is located at the combustion plume, where the heat and fuel are convected 
from the flame fronts and pyrolyzed solid and the oxygen is supplied by upward 
induced flow and entrainment (see Fig. 3). 
