From this figure, thecombustion of horizontal spread over a thin fuel shows afeature 
of triple reaction zones, which was not found in the opposed flow/downward and con- 
current flow flame spread problems (Chen, 1990; Duh and Chen; 1991 and Ferkul, 1993). 
Figure 5a depicts the heat flux profiles along the fuel plate. Since the flames on the 
two sides of the fuel plate are not symmetric, the heat flux profiles are displayed 
separately. The figure clearly shows that compared to the upper flame the lower flame is 
moie intense and extends further upstream toward the virgin fuel. This confirms that 
the lower flame controls the horizontal flame spread. 
Figure 5b is the response of fuel pyrolysis to the heat fluxes in Figure 5a. Because the 
solid fuel is so thin, the temperature gradient across the plate is negligible. Therefore, 
the variations of temperature, density, and blowing rate are one-dimensional. The solid 
fuel can be categorized into three regions according to their respective characteristics. 
We need to define the specific locations in advance in order to specify these regions. The 
pyrolysis front is defined at p, = 0.99, which is the onset of fuel pyrolysis. It is found to 
be x = - 15 m, coincident with the peak of the heat flux on both sides. The beginning of 
preheat is defined as the point where the solid fuel starts to sense the heat flux from the 
gas phase. The position is at x = - 22, obtained from the lower profile in Figure 5a, 
whereas the upper heat flux profile begins at x = - 16.5. 
In the preheat region, between x = - 22 and x = - 15, both the solid density and 
mass blowing rate are maintained at the ambient values. The solid temperature 
increases insignificantly up to x = - 16.5 because it receives heat from the lower flame 
only and the energy is used for heating up the fuel. After that, the temperature increases 
sharply up to the pyrolysis front (x = - 15), since the fuel obtains heat from both 
flames. At thesame time, mass blowing rate also increases steeply. The preheat length is 
