3.2 Comparisons with Experiments 
As mentioned in Introduction, horizontal flame spread over a thin fuel was observed in 
some experiments, mainly investigating the effects of orientation on flame spread. In 
these cases, the horizontal flame spread rate was found accelerative rather than 
constant. In other words, the present model is not capable of predicting the experimen- 
tal observations because of the assumption ofconstant flamespread rate. Therefore, the 
comparison on the topic of flame spread rate is meaningless. In the following, the 
comparisons with corresponding experiments are based on the observed phenomena 
from a qualitative view point. 
In the experimental works, such as Hirano er a/. (1974b), Kashiwagi and Newman 
(1976) and Duh (1992), the common finding was that the flame spread rate should be 
faster than the downward one. The computed flame spread rate in the present work is 
consistent with thisconclusion, see Table 111 for example. However, this is only true for 
regime of g < 2.5 in the present study which will be discussed later. 
In the experimental studies of Hirano e t a / . (1974b) and Duh (1992), the visual 
behaviors of propagating flame at different angles were exhibited. In the case of 
horizontal flame spread, some common features were observed. For example, the front 
of upper flame appears to be blue, then followed by a distinct yellow flame zone. The 
