3.3 
The Effect of Changing Gravity Level 
In this section, a parametric study is given by varying the g-level from normal one to 
blowoff limit, g = 5.5, under a specified ambient oxygen concentration, Yo,, = 0.233. 
The computed results such as I/,,%,T, and i,, 
for each gravity level are tabulated in 
Table 111. The reference velocity, V,, and Damkohler number, Da, are given as well. 
Note that the downward flame spread rates corresponding to the same fuel and 
ambient environment, are also included in Table 111 for the purpose of comparison. 
From Table Ill, it can be seen that the flame spread rate (v,) decreases with an 
increase of g-level. The trend is similar to the one for downward flame spread. 
Intuitively, the horizontal flame spread rate is expected to be faster than downward one 
under the sameenvironment. However, this expectation only holds up tog = 2.5. After 
that the horizontal flame spread rate becomes slower. The reason will be given later in 
the discussion of Figure 9. In other words, the descending slope of horizontal flame 
spread rate with an increase of gravity is greater than that of downward flame spread 
rate. The corresponding data are plotted in Figure 8. Using a least square technique, 
the slope of V1 with respect to Da ( x 
is about 1.57 (or V'cx g-0.71 equivalently) 
for the present study, whereas for downward flame spread is 1.0 (or v, cc g-.?.33 
equivalently). However, the horizontal flame spread can sustain to a lower blowoff limit 
relatively. 
Figure 9 is a series of combination of isotherms and velocities vector as a function of 
gravity level. For upper flame, the flame shrinks as gravity increases. Since the reference 
velocity, T, increases with an increase of gravity (see Table III), the reference length, 
$( = (i*/q)), 
appears an opposite way. Therefore, the shrinkage in real scale is more 
intense. At g = 1, we can see that isotherm T = 2 in thermal plume diffuses outward 
behind y = 30, however, it never shows the similar behavior in higher gravity regime. 
The flame front, having an opposite spreading flame feature mentioned previously, 
becomes thinner, shifts toward fuel plate and retreats to downstream as gravity 
increases. In the meantime, the local temperature is lowered. These behaviors indicate 
the flame is subjected to a flow stretch. 
For lower flame, the situation likes the counterflow diffusion; the flame is located 
near the stagnation-point, and the induced fluid flow moves toward the fuel plate. 
From g = 1 tog = 2, we can see that the whole flame is pushed closer to the plate by the 
stronger up-rising induced flow as the gravity increases, and it loses more heat to the 
fuel plate. This can be confirmed in Figure 10. (Note that the heat distributions in this 
figure are in nondimensional scale. Therefore, the dimensional value should also 
consider the multiplication of g-'I3, as mentioned above). Therefore, the temperature 
