In order to further understand the mechanism for the slowdown of horizontal flame 
spread rate relative to downward flame spread after g = 2.5, the change of series of heat 
flux distributionsalong fuel plate as afunction ofgravity, shown in Figure 10, is studied 
first. It can be seen that as g is less than 2.5 the heat flux distributions underneath fuel 
plateare similar. That is, there are two peaks: one is at the flame front and the other one 
is near the burnout. However, at g = 2.5 a small pike starts to appear x = - 6 between 
the two peaks. Refer to Figure 9, isotherm T= 3 in flame front at that position is about 
to be separated into two cells. Then, the size of middle pike grows with the increase of 
gravity. At g = 4, the peak valueand area ofthe middle pikeareeven larger than the one 
at the flame front. During this process, the heat flux profiles in the upper flame is almost 
invariant in shape, in other words, the appearance of two peaks is maintained. 
However, near the blowoff limit the situation is completely different. It can be seen that 
part of the heat flux profile on top plate becomes negative, indicating that it loses heat 
to the ambient. This is because the upper flame is almost extinct, but the lower flame is 
still strong (see Fig. 9) and it keeps supplying heat to the solid fuel plate. The heat 
penetrates the thin fuel and arrives the top surface immediately and heats the gas layer 
near the plate. 
To cooperate with the heat profilediscussed above, the solid fuel pyrolysis in various 
gravity levels, including the dimensionless solid fuel density (p,), mass blowing rate 
(M:) and fuel temperature (T,) distributions along the solid fuel surface, is presented in 
Figure 1 I. Contrary to the heat flux distribution, these profiles seem not to be affected 
by the variation of gravity except the case near the blowoff limit. The reason is that the 
appearance of new pike in heat flux profile occurs behind the pyrolysis front, from 
which a ceiling temperature is imposed as mentioned previously, and both density and 
blowing rate are determined by surface temperature directly. As to the dimensional 
pyrolysis length, it is decreased with an increase of gravity which is listed in Table 111. 
Except near the blowoff limit, the decrease of pyrolysis length with gravity is quite 
smooth. It is interesting to see that thedrop of dimensional flame spread rate is not so 
sharp as that ofdimensional pyrolysis length. The blowing rate profile near the limiting 
case appears a square-wave like shape, indicating that the fuel supply rate along the 
pyrolysis length is almost uniform, and then drops sharply to zero at burnout. This can 
be seen in Figure9f that the underneath isotherms are almost parallel to fuel platein the 
pyrolysis region. However, this profile is enlarged due to a use of nondimensional scale. 
Referring to Table 111, the dimensional pyrolysis length is 0.216 cm only. 
CONCLUSIONS 
A combustion model for horizontal flame suread over a thin solid fuel in the 
gravitational field is established, which is based on the assumption of flame spread rate 
is constant. The model is modified from Duh and Chen (1991). The flame structures in 
normal gravity, including the isotherm distribution, ;elochy vector field, fuel and 
oxidizer concentration distributions, as well as heat flux profiles and fuel pyrolysis 
along the solid fuel, are illustrated graphically. 
On the two sides of the fuel plate, we can distinguish an upper flame from a lower 
flameand they are not symmetric with respect to the fuel plate. The bulk fluid motion is 
