doi:10.1080/13647830.2010.547601,doi,Microgravity droplet combustion: effect of tethering fiber on burning rate and flame structure,3,2,"488
T. Farouk and F.L. Dryer
the coupled physical-chemical processes governing liquid fuel utilization. Because of the
simpliﬁed geometry, processes such as molecular transport, heat transfer, and oxidation
kinetics can be de-convolved in detailed numerical models of droplet burning, illuminating
the role of each process both individually and in its interaction with others.
Theories and experiments involving isolated droplet combustion date back to 1953 [1],
with the ﬁrst microgravity work appearing in 1956 [2]. Over the years there have been
many experimental investigations that focused on liquid fuel droplet combustion under
microgravity conditions that include ground based drop tower studies [3–7] as well as
space based experiments; the Droplet Combustion Experiment (DCE) [8,9], Fiber Sup-
ported Droplet Combustion (FSDC) [10] and recent Flame Extinguishment (FLEX) [11]
experiments onboard the International Space Station (ISS). A wide variety of liquid fuels
have been studied in these experiments, including methanol, ethanol, n-heptane, iso-octane,
n-decane, n-hexadecane and benzene.
Very small disturbances produced during droplet formation, deployment, and ignition
frequently occur in free droplet experiments, causing slow drift and degraded optical
measurements of the droplet and ﬂame diameter over the burning history. Thus, in a
majority of experiments, the droplet is tethered on a ﬁber in order to control the position
of the droplet. Tethers are also frequently employed to control multiple droplet spacing
in studying droplet–droplet combustion interactions. However, in the literature there are
very few works that quantitatively investigate the effects of tethering ﬁbers on droplet
combustion characteristics.
For example, Struk et al. [12] considered the shape distortion of the droplet due to
the presence of the tethering ﬁber. They compared various methods for determining the
equivalent diameter of the distorted droplet, and developed a numerical methodology for
deﬁning the static equilibrium shape of the droplet. In their analysis they showed that
the derived equivalent diameters produced linear burning rates and did not play a role in
producing the non-linear burning rates that were experimentally observed. However, no
analysis of heat transfer impacts on burning characteristics due to the ﬁber was performed.
Avedisian and Jackson [5] observed the effect of tether ﬁber on the soot patterns for n-
heptane droplets burning in a stagnant ambience exposed to microgravity. With the presence
of a tether ﬁber, the shell-like soot aggregate forming inside the ﬂame was found to evolve
into a non-spherical soot distribution around the droplet as a result of a non-symmetric
distribution of thermophoretic and Stefan drag forces around the droplet. More pronounced
distortions occurred for larger ﬁber diameters. They also observed a nonlinearity in the
variation of (d/do)2 due to the inﬂuence of the ﬁber. In a more recent work, Mikami et al. [7]
report a new droplet array generation technique and draw a comparison between the single
quartz ﬁber with bead and the bead-less silicon carbide cross string tethering arrangement.
Their experimental study showed a reduced distortion of the droplet shape using a bead-less
cross string tethering arrangement rather than a single, beaded tether.
In addition to the various experimental studies of droplet combustion, several numeri-
cal simulations have also been conducted to complement experimental ﬁndings. Numerical
models have been developed to study droplet combustion both in convection free [13–17]
and convective environments [17–20]. In the early work for high pressure droplet evap-
oration under natural convection, Kadota and Hiroyasu [17] considered the heat conduc-
tion through the tethering ﬁber. The conduction through the ﬁber was evaluated with a
one-dimensional steady-state analysis. Although of only qualitative signiﬁcance, their cal-
culations indicated signiﬁcant enhancement of the evaporation rate. Shih and Megaridis
[19] developed a mathematical model to simulate tethered droplet evaporation under forced
convection. They conducted a detailed analysis of the effect of ﬁber conduction on droplet
Downloaded by [Kungliga Tekniska Hogskola] at 07:46 07 October 2014"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2010.547601,doi,Microgravity droplet combustion: effect of tethering fiber on burning rate and flame structure,4,3,"Combustion Theory and Modelling
489
evaporation employing a transient multi-dimensional heat conduction equation. The study
showed that for a ﬁber parallel to the ﬂow, only slight enhancement of evaporation was
observed. The model predictions further indicated that when quartz tethering ﬁbers are
implemented, the droplet surface regression rates do not vary appreciably with tether ﬁber
sizes.
The literature results summarized above suggest that tethering is a well accepted exper-
imental methodology for use in studying isolated droplet burning. However, there has been
no fully coupled transient detailed numerical studies that have quantitatively considered the
effects of droplet tether properties on observations. In this work, we present a comprehen-
sive single droplet combustion model where the effect of the tethering ﬁber on transient
heat transfer is implicitly taken into account. Simulations have been used to elucidate the
inﬂuence of the tethering ﬁber presence, its diameter, and material, on the combustion
characteristics. The n-decane droplet experiments of Dietrich et al. [6] were utilized to a
priori validate the model performance. The experiments provide a signiﬁcant experimental
database that spans a wide range of ambient oxygen concentration, ambient pressure and
initial droplet diameter. The validated model was then further exercised to simulate results
as a function of tethering ﬁber diameter and materials. The ﬁndings presented here provide
researchers with guidelines in designing tethering arrangements and evaluating tethering
effects on ongoing isolated droplet combustion experiments relating to FLEX and to future
microgravity droplet combustion programs.
Mathematical model
Droplet model
The transient, spherosymmetric droplet combustion model features detailed gas phase
kinetics, spectrally resolved radiative heat transfer and multi-component transport. The
combustion behaviours of liquid fuels below their critical pressure can be described by two
domains of continuum ﬂuid ﬂow connected by a complex and dynamic boundary. The case
considered here assumes local thermodynamic equilibrium and low Mach number. Species
and energy conservation equations for the control volumes in both phases are:
