Results and discussions
Sooting effects are not considered in the present model. The tethered non sooting n-decane
microgravity droplet combustion experiments of Dietrich et al. [6] therefore provide an
extensive set of validation data. These experiments were conducted in the Japan Micro-
gravity Center (JAMIC) 10 second drop tower and the Glenn Research Center (GRC) 5.2
second drop tower. A quartz ﬁber having a rod arrangement was used as a tether. The quartz
ﬁber had a diameter of 230 µm with a bead (≈2 × ﬁber diameter) at one end to support
the droplets. Details of the experimental setup is presented in [6]. The simulations were
conducted by employing the well validated high temperature n-decane chemical kinetics
model of Zhao et al. [27] consisting of 86 species undergoing 688 reactions. The data
correlations of Daubert and Danner [28] were used to calculate the liquid properties. The
properties of the quartz ﬁber used for the simulations are ρf = 2220 kg/m3 and cp,f =
760 J/kg K [29]. A constant value of ﬁber density and speciﬁc heat was used in the simula-
tions due to the unavailability of these data over the broad combustion temperature range.
Data for quartz thermal conductivity, kf [29, 30] were interpolated to obtain a temperature
dependent thermal conductivity.
Figure 1 shows the evolution of droplet diameter and burning rate, Ko for the base case
(do = 1.7 mm, 17% O2/83% N2 ambience 190 mm Hg/0.25 atm). The evolution of the
droplet diameter is presented using the scaling of variables suggested by the classical theory
of droplet burning: (d/do)2 vs. t/do2. Comparison between the measurements and the model
predictions as well as the effect of the tethering ﬁber on the burning characteristics is also
presented in Figure 1. The simulations show that the presence of tether ﬁber signiﬁcantly
increases the droplet diameter regression rate (Figure 1(a)). Additional heat is conducted
to the droplet through the tethering ﬁber (Figure 11), from the surrounding gas phase
temperature ﬁeld, resulting in an increase in droplet burning rate. The presence of the
ﬁber also induces a non-linearity in the droplet diameter evolution; counter to the classical
theory of droplet burning. It is evident that the prediction with the inclusion of the tether
ﬁber is in good agreement with the measurements. Deviation between the simulations and
experiment is observed at the very early stage and towards the end of the combustion
process. The predicted droplet diameter evolution shows a slight increase in the droplet
diameter at the earliest stage of the burn as a result of thermal expansion of the droplet itself
(Figure 1(a)). This is not observed in the experiments due to the very small time scales.
A quasi-steady burn is achieved after ∼35% of the burn. Towards the end of the burn,
the droplet diameter evolution show increased non-linearity as the droplet size becomes
comparable with the ﬁber diameter. Extinction is also observed (most clearly visible in the
ﬂame diameter evolution, Figure 2(b)). The predicted extinction diameter was found to be
0.180 mm. The observed experimental extinction diameter is strongly inﬂuenced by the
presence of a ﬁber bead. A ‘corrected’ extinction diameter, dext corrected was determined by
subtracting the bead volume from the observed experimental extinction droplet volume.
The dext corrected for this case was found to be 0.340 mm. The dext corrected is shown as a dashed
line in Figure 1(a). The predicted extinction diameter is smaller by a factor of ∼1.9 than
that of the measurements.
The evolution of the instantaneous burning rate, Ko for the base case is presented in
Figure 1(b) together with comparison between the prediction and the measurement. The
effect of the tether on the burning rate is also summarized there. The predicted Ko shows a
large negative value at the initial stage of the burn. This corresponds to the increase in the
droplet diameter due to thermal expansion during the initial droplet heat up period. Ko then
increases sharply due to the initial liquid mass gasiﬁcation. In absence of the tether ﬁber the
