The evolution of ﬂame standoff ratio (FSR) and the ﬂame diameter for the base case
with the inclusion of the tether ﬁber is presented in Figure 2. The experimental ﬂame
diameter and standoff ratio were visually determined from ﬂame images acquired via a
CCD camera. Since visual images do not correlate with any speciﬁc species emission,
we considered several alternative markers in determining the numerically predicted FSR:
location of OHmax, Tmax and H2Omax. Predicted FSR using Tmax or H2Omax as the marker
were found to be in very good agreement with the measurements, Tmax being better (Figure
2(a)). In comparison to the measurements, locations for OHmax were found to be on the lean
side of the diffusion ﬂame structure. Identical markers were used in predicting the ﬂame
diameter evolution. The ﬂame structure evolution (Figure 2(b)) shows that the ﬂame diam-
eter (dﬂame) initially increases in size and plateaus for a signiﬁcant period of time during
half way of the burn. After reaching the maximum diameter, the ﬂame responds to the
shrinking droplet and starts to decrease. The droplet size decreases by ∼35% before the
ﬂame diameter begins to decrease, and hence there is a signiﬁcant period of vapor collection
within the ﬂame structure during the ﬁrst stages of burning. At the end of burn near extinc-
tion, a sharp rise in the ﬂame diameter is observed. This is due to the fact that the various
markers used in determining the ﬂame radius remains even as visual luminescence dis-
appears with extinction/burnout. The peak value of the ﬂame diameter using Tmax and
H2Omax as the marker is larger than the peak experimental value by a factor of 1.1
and 1.01, respectively. The discrepancy observed between the ﬂame diameter predictions
and measurements is well within the experimental uncertainties reported; ± 750 µm [6].
In the remaining comparisons reported here, we used the location of Tmax as the ﬂame
marker. The effect of the tether ﬁber on the ﬂame standoff ratio is presented in Figure
3. The presence of the tether ﬁber signiﬁcantly increases the ﬂame standoff ratio (FSR).
Apart from the very initial and extinction stages, the rate of increase of the FSR is nearly
constant (linear slope). Close to extinction the standoff ratio increases at a faster rate with
an exponential increase at extinction.
