The temporal evolution of the peak gas and ﬁber temperature (base case) is presented in
Figure 10. The peak gas temperature of 1900 K during the early stage is due to the prescribed
high temperature input used to simulate the ignition source used in the experiments. A
decrease in the peak gas temperature occurs due to the initial heating of the droplet and
ﬁber as well as endothermic fuel vapor reactions. The peak gas temperature drops to
∼1750 K at 0.0025 s. Following the decrease in temperature, a sharp rise is observed,
indicating ignition of the fuel air vapor mixture surrounding the droplet. The peak gas
temperature reaches its maximum value of ∼1950 K at 0.0085 s. This sharp increase of
∼200 K was found to occur over a short time span of 0.006 s. After the ignition process
as the quasi-steady diffusion ﬂame is established (Figure 7), the peak gas temperature
steadily decreases due to the diffusive and radiative losses. A rapid decrease in the peak
gas temperature can be seen during extinction. The temporal evolution of the peak ﬁber
temperature shows that the ﬁber responds to the evolving gas temperature slowly. This is
due to the comparatively larger size of the ﬁber (dﬁber = 230 µm) and the associated speciﬁc
heat; smaller size tether ﬁber ranging from 12 to 50 µm in diameter being employed in more
recent experiments [7, 32]. At the early stage, minimal change in the ﬁber temperature is
seen. A signiﬁcant response is observed at 0.01 s when the peak ﬁber temperature increases
by ∼46 K to a peak value of 346 K. Due to the slow response of the tether ﬁber its
contribution to the ignition of the droplet is minimal. At 1.0 s the peak ﬁber temperature
is 1550 K as the ﬁber equilibrates to the gas phase temperature (Figure 9). As a result the
ﬁber contributes signiﬁcantly to the behavior during the quasi-steady state burning phase.
Detail about the contribution from the tether ﬁber on the droplet burning is discussed in the
following section.
