The time variations of the heat, Q input/output, at the droplet surface due to radiation
and ﬁber conduction is illustrated in Figure 11(a) for do = 1.7 mm, XO2 = 0.17 and
P = 190 mm Hg/0.25 atm. In Figure 11(a), effects due to radiation and conduction through
the tether ﬁber are strongly manifested. The heat input through radiative absorption shows
a sharp increase during ignition, followed by a gradual decrease with droplet shrinkage.
At the end of the burn, the radiative heat Qradiation at the droplet surface shows a positive
value, indicating radiative heat loss to the ambient. The contribution from ﬁber conduction
shows a sharp increase during the early stages of the burn, reaching a peak value as the ﬁber
is heated up. Once the peak value is attained, the contribution from the ﬁber conduction
decreases slowly as the ﬂame temperature decreases (Figure 10). Figure 11(b) illustrates the
fraction of conductive heat input through ﬁber to the total heat input at the droplet surface.
The total heat input is comprised of gas phase conduction, ﬁber conduction and radiation
contributions. At the very early stages, the contribution from the ﬁber conduction is almost
negligible. The total heat input is dominated by the gas phase conduction in this stage. At
∼0.5 s the conduction through the ﬁber starts to contribute as much as 10% of the total
heat input and increases steadily to 40% at ∼4.0 s and sharply to 85% at the ﬁnal stage.
Even though the total heat input through the ﬁber starts to decay (Figure 11(a)), the steady
increase in the fraction is a result of the decreasing contribution from gas phase conduction
and radiation. The sharp rise at the ﬁnal stage reﬂects that the droplet starts becoming
comparable to the ﬁber (bead or diameter) size which reduces the heat input through gas
phase conduction and radiation signiﬁcantly.
It can be clearly seen that signiﬁcant contribution to heat transfer to the droplet surface
from ﬁber conduction is made and neglecting its effect results in an under prediction in
the droplet burning rate (Figures 1 and 3). In their work Dietrich et al. [6] also conducted
simulations to analyze the experimental observations. However in the simulations the inﬂu-
ence of the tether ﬁber was not taken into account and the gas phase thermal conductivity
was used as a ﬁtting variable to align simulations with the experimental observations. A
signiﬁcantly higher value of gas phase thermal conductivity was required to obtain a good
agreement between the predictions and measurements. This higher value appears to have
been a result of ﬁber effects on the experimental observations.
