temporal evolution of the droplet diameter for different oxygen mole fraction shows that
with an increase in the oxygen mole fraction, the burning rate (slope of the droplet diameter
regression) increases. For XO2 = 0.15, 0.17, and 0.19 the average burning rate was found to
be 0.53, 0.60, and 0.64 mm2/s, respectively. The predicted droplet diameter evolution is in
very good agreement with the experiments, with disparities near extinction occurring from
effects of the ﬁber bead. A dashed line in Figure 12(a) indicates the size of the extinction
diameter just due to the bead ((dbead/do)2). The large bead employed in these experiments
results in signiﬁcant perturbations of extinction processes, and an accurate measurement
