Figure 16(a) and (b) shows comparisons between the model and experiments for average
burning rate and extinction diameter as a function of pressure. The experimental initial
droplet diameters ranged from 1.5 to 2.0 mm and XO2 was maintained at 0.15. Simulations
were conducted for both the upper and lower initial diameter limit and also for do =
1.7 mm. In the simulations, the droplet ignited but failed to attain a quasi-steady burn for
ambient pressures below 80 mm Hg. A very low average burning rate is predicted near
the low pressure limit. With increasing pressure, a sharp rise in the average burning rate
occurs between 80 and 190 mm Hg, with smaller increases continuing as pressure is further
increased to 760 mm Hg. The faster burning rate at higher pressure is a consequence of the
increased chemical reaction due to the increased species density. The decrease in the average
burning rate with increasing initial droplet diameter is a consequence of radiative losses
which increases with an increase in the droplet size. The experimental average burning rate
lies between the predictions associated with the upper and lower initial diameter limits.
The model predictions for the extinction diameter match the qualitative trends of the
experiments (Figure 16(b)). Both the predictions and measurements show a decreasing
extinction diameter with increasing pressure; asymptotically reaching very small values
at higher pressures. It should be noted that the predictions at higher pressures are strictly
valid for a non sooting scenario, as sooting is not considered in the present model. Presence
of soot would result in larger extinction diameter at higher pressure due to increased
radiative losses. The large extinction diameter in the low pressure limits corresponds to
the cases where the droplets ignited but failed to attain a quasi-steady burn. Even though
the model captured the qualitative trends, the predictions do not agree quantitatively to the
measurements. In comparison to the experimental measurements the predicted extinction
diameters were found to be smaller by a factor of ∼2.0. This observed discrepancy is at
least in part due to the large sized bead (dbead = 460 µm) present in the experiments.
The presence of the bead would be a source of perturbation in the extinction diameter
measurements and would also increase the size of the observed extinction diameter.
