conductive heat input starts to take place for dﬁber = 50 µm. For dﬁber = 230 µm the
maximum Qcond ﬁber is ∼10 mW. Interestingly, the peak heat input for different sized ﬁbers
do not occur at the same time instance. With increasing size the peak also shifts towards
longer time (inset Figure 17). This is due to the fact that with larger size the tether ﬁber
takes longer time to equilibrate to the ﬂame temperature. The effect of tethering ﬁber on
the burning rate is summarized in Figure 18. The variation of the average burning rate due
to the presence of a ﬁber is presented as a function of normalized equivalent initial droplet
diameter do drop /dﬁber. The variation of the average burning rate ( ¯Ko ﬁber −¯Ko no ﬁber) ¯Ko ﬁber
is normalized by the average burning rate ¯Ko no ﬁber of a free ﬂoating droplet. The results
are obtained from simulations for a do = 1.7 mm decane droplet with varying quartz ﬁber
