It can be seen that the presence of a SiC ﬁber having a same diameter as that of quartz
ﬁber has a much greater effect on the burning rate. For a 230-µm SiC ﬁber (do drop/dﬁber =
7.4), the burning rate is found to increase by as much as 43%. The ρf cp,f for both the SiC
and quartz ﬁber are comparable however the thermal conductivity for SiC is larger by a
factor of 3.5 at room temperature (Table 1). The larger effect of SiC ﬁber on the burning
rate is due to the higher thermal conductivity/diffusivity of the SiC ﬁber. For a commonly
employed, single SiC ﬁber arrangement (droplet tethered at the center of a suspended ﬁber),
the effect is found to be more severe. The additional heat input through the ﬁber increases
the burning rate as much as 87% for a 230-µm SiC. From a design perspective, in order
for the burning rate variation to be less than 5%, the value of do drop/dﬁber for a SiC rod and
single ﬁber arrangement has to be greater than 38 (dﬁber = 45 µm) and 55 (dﬁber = 31 µm),
respectively. For the burning rate variation to be less than 1%, the bounding limit for the
do drop/dﬁber for SiC was found to be do drop/dﬁber > 104 (dﬁber = 16 µm) and do drop/dﬁber
> 154 (dﬁber = 11 µm) for the two different arrangements. These limiting results will, of
course, change if the droplet combustion event involves signiﬁcant sooting.
Conclusions
In this work, the effect of a supporting ﬁber on the combustion characteristics of an isolated
droplet in microgravity conditions have been studied employing a novel one-dimensional
droplet combustion model. Predictions from the model have been compared against the
extensive n-decane tethered droplet combustion data set of Dietrich et al. [6]. The inclusion
of the tether ﬁber effects within the model remarkably improves the agreement between the
predictions and the experiments. The presence of the tether ﬁber was found to signiﬁcantly
increase the burning rate and the ﬂame standoff ratio. The average burning rate was found
to be increased by as much as ∼20% for a 230-µm quartz ﬁber. In addition, the ﬁber
Downloaded by [Kungliga Tekniska Hogskola] at 07:46 07 October 2014"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2010.547601,doi,Microgravity droplet combustion: effect of tethering fiber on burning rate and flame structure,28,27,"Combustion Theory and Modelling
513
also induced non-linearity in the temporal evolution of the droplet diameter and burning
rate; counter to the classical theory. The non-linearity manifested as the droplet size became
comparable to the ﬁber diameter. The experimentally observed non-linearity was also found
to be in very good agreement with the model predictions. The contribution from radiation
and ﬁber conduction at the droplet surface were found to be of comparable magnitude.
Predictions from the model were also compared against experimental data obtained
over a range of ambient pressures and oxygen mole fraction. With increasing pressure and
oxygen mole fraction, the burning rate was found to increase followed by a decrease in the
ﬂame diameter and standoff ratio. The increase in the burning rate with increased pressure
and oxygen mole fraction results from increased chemical reaction rates. The predictions
from the model were found to be in favourable agreement with the experimental data.
The effects of different sized ﬁbers on the observations were investigated. The heat
input through the larger ﬁbers slowly reached a peak value because of the inertial heating
effects of the ﬁber. On the other hand conductive heat transfer through the small sized
ﬁbers attained a peak rate very quickly, but the contribution was found to be minimal
due to the small ﬁber size. The effect of the ﬁber size on the burning rate indicated an
asymptotically decreasing effect with decreasing ﬁber diameter. As do drop /dﬁber increases,
¯Ko ﬁber approaches ¯Ko no ﬁber for do drop /dﬁber > 42.5 (dﬁber = 40 µm) a variation of less
than 1% is observed. For do drop /dﬁber > 22 (corresponding to the droplets supported on
a 77-µm ﬁber) the variation of the average burning rate is found to be less than 5%. The
effect of different ﬁber materials on the burning rate was also studied. In comparison to the
quartz tethering ﬁber, the SiC ﬁber was found to have a larger effect on the burning rate for
the same ﬁber diameter and tethering arrangement. For a 230-µm SiC ﬁber having a rod
arrangement the burning rate was found to increase as much as 43%. An 87% increase in
the burning rate was observed for a 230-µm SiC ﬁber of single ﬁber arrangement, due to
the additional heat input. The limiting condition for the SiC ﬁber diameter was predicted to
be do drop /dﬁber >38 (dﬁber = 45 µm) for a rod arrangement and do drop /dﬁber >55 (dﬁber =
31 µm); in order for the effect to be insigniﬁcant. It should be noted that these predicted
limiting tether ﬁber diameters are strictly true for non-sooting conditions.
