Opposed ﬂow ﬂame spread over an array of thin solid fuel sheets in a
microgravity environment
Vinayak Malhotra, Chenthil Kumar and Amit Kumar∗
Indian Institute of Technology Madras, Chennai, Tamil Nadu, India
(Received 9 January 2013; ﬁnal version received 11 May 2013)
In this work a numerical study has been carried out to gain physical insight into the
phenomena of opposed ﬂow ﬂame spread over an array of thin solid fuel sheets in a
microgravity environment. The two-dimensional (2D) simulations show that the ﬂame
spread rates for the multiple-fuel conﬁguration are higher than those for the ﬂame
spreading over a single fuel sheet. This is due to reduced radiation losses from the
ﬂame and increased heat feedback to the solid fuel. The ﬂame spread rate exhibits
a non-monotonic variation with decrease in the interspace distance between the fuel
sheets. Higher radiation heat feedback primarily as gas/ﬂame radiation was found to be
responsible for the increase in the ﬂame spread rate with the reduction of the interspace
distance. It was noted that as the interspace distance between the fuel sheets was
reduced below a certain value, no steady solution could be obtained. However, at very
small interspace distances, steady state spread rates were obtained. Here, due to oxygen
starvation the ﬂame spread rate decreased and eventually at some interspace distance
the ﬂame extinguished. With fuel emittance (equal to absorptance) reduced to ‘0’ the
ﬂame spread rate was nearly independent of the interspace distance, except at very
small distances where the ﬂame spread rate dropped due to oxygen starvation. A ﬂame
extinction plot with the extinction oxygen level was constructed for the multiple-fuel
conﬁguration at various interspace distances. The default fuel with an emittance of 0.92
was found to be more ﬂammable in the multiple-fuel conﬁguration than in a single
fuel sheet conﬁguration. For a fuel emittance equal to zero, the extinction oxygen limit
decreases for both the single and the multiple fuel sheet conﬁgurations. However, the two
ﬂammability curves cross over at a certain fuel separation distance. The multiple-fuel
conﬁgurations become less ﬂammable compared to the single fuel sheet conﬁguration
below a certain separation distance.
Keywords: opposed ﬂow ﬂame spread; array of thin solid fuels; microgravity; radiation
ﬂame extinction
Introduction
Most studies on the phenomena of ﬂame spread reported in the literature were carried
out with a single fuel sheet [1–11]. These studies have provided valuable insight into the
mechanisms involved in the ﬂame spread phenomena and report the effect of the solid fuel
properties and the environmental variables on the ﬂame spread rates and their extinction.
However, in most practical situations, a spreading ﬂame may involve interaction with a
ﬂame from a nearby burning fuel (which acts as an external heat source). This interaction
is very likely to alter the ﬂame spreading behaviour and the extinction condition from that
of a ﬂame spreading over a single fuel sheet. Flame spread over an array of fuel sheets
∗Corresponding author. Email: amitk@ae.iitm.ac.in
C⃝2013 Taylor & Francis
Downloaded by [University of Connecticut] at 04:36 24 July 2013"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2013.809797,doi,Opposed flow flame spread over an array of thin solid fuel sheets in a microgravity environment,3,2,"2
V. Malhotra et al.
placed parallel to each other, therefore offers a conﬁguration where interaction between
the spreading ﬂames can be systematically studied in order to identify the key controlling
factors in such interactions.
Kurosaki et al. [12, 13] carried out experiments to describe a downward spreading
ﬂame along two [12] and several [13] vertical thin fuel sheets in normal gravity. In these
experiments, the ﬂame spread rate was measured for various separation distances between
the fuel sheets. The ﬂame spread rate was found to depend strongly on the separation
distance between the fuel sheets. The ﬂame spread rate increased with a reduction in
the separation distance up to a maximum at some separation distance and then dropped
with further reduction in the separation distance. The ﬂame spread rate at very small
separation distances was lower than that obtained for the burning of a single sheet of
paper.
In conﬁguration of multiple fuel sheets (three or more), the ﬂame spread rate increased
with the addition of fuel sheets and asymptotically approached a constant value. At certain
small separation distances the ﬂame extinguished for three parallel sheets of paper but
spread over twin sheets. The analysis of experiments established that for small separation
distances, convective heat transfer predominates and controls the ﬂame spread rate. On the
other hand, for the widely spaced fuel sheets where the view-factor between adjacent fuel
sheet and ﬂame is increased, radiative heat transfer from the adjacent ﬂame and embers
(formed downstream of the ﬂame) played an important role in contributing to heat feedback
to the preheat region of the fuel sheet (upstream of the ﬂame) and thus dictated the observed
ﬂame spread rate.
In a recent work, Shih [14] numerically studied the ﬂame interactions in an array of thin
solids for low speed concurrent ﬂows in a microgravity environment. The work discussed
the structure of spreading ﬂames between parallel solids along with some of their features,
like ﬂow channelling effects and interaction of ﬂame radiation. Similar to [12, 13], the inﬂu-
ence of the separation distance on the ﬂame spread rate was studied, which was reported to
exhibit a non-monotonic behaviour. The study showed that, at larger separation distances the
ﬂame spread rate increased with a reduction in the separation distance and at very small sep-
aration distances, the ﬂame spread rate dropped with a reduction in the separation distance.
The work reported that, at some intermediate separation distances steadily spreading ﬂames
were not obtained. The study also stated that radiation interactions between the ﬂames and
solids contributed to a low heat-loss system and that radiation re-absorption by ﬂames
resulted in a larger ﬂame with higher temperature, which increased conductive heat ﬂuxes
to the solids and thus the ﬂame spread rate.
In light of the above works, ﬂame interactions in an array of fuel sheets, under an
opposed ﬂow conﬁguration in a microgravity environment is an issue that has yet to be
addressed. We note that the ﬂame spread mechanism for concurrent ﬂame spread differs
from opposed ﬂows and that in the microgravity quiescent environment, the ﬂame spreads
only in an opposed ﬂow mode [15]. Similarly, the controlling transport mechanisms differ
in normal gravity and microgravity [16, 17]. Therefore, in the present work we numerically
investigate the opposed ﬂow ﬂame spread along an array of parallel thin fuel sheets in a
microgravity environment. The primary objectives of this study are to investigate the effect
of the separation distance (here denoted as 2d) between the parallel fuel sheets on opposed
ﬂow ﬂame spread and its extinction and to identify the key controlling parameters. The
separation distance, ‘2d’ between the fuel sheets is an important parameter that has been
varied in this study. Other parameters are also explored here including oxygen concentration
and the radiation property of the solid fuel sheet.
