Computational domain and numerical model
Figure 1 shows a schematic of the opposed ﬂow ﬂame spread over two adjacent fuel sheets
separated by a distance, ‘2d’. The schematic represents a repeated section in an inﬁnite
array of fuel sheets. By considering symmetry about the mid plane between two adjacent
fuel sheets, the actual computational domain (shown by a dashed-dot line) is just half the
size of the schematic. The ﬂame leading edge closely follows the pyrolysis front on the
fuel surface, therefore, the ﬂame ﬁxed co-ordinate system is located on the pyrolysis front
(x = 0, deﬁned in the model as the location where fuel thickness reduces to 95% of the
fresh fuel thickness).
A uniform forced ﬂow with velocity

U ∞

enters from the upstream (x > 0) end of the
domain. The ﬂow approaches the ﬂame (in the negative x direction) at a relative velocity of
U R(x, y) = U ∞(x, y) −V f , where V f is the ﬂame spread rate and U ∞(x, y) is the local
velocity (here 5 cm/s) and x,X and y,Y refers to non-dimensional x & y coordinates. Note
here that V f is negative with respect to the ﬂame ﬁxed co-ordinates, hence the relative
velocity will be the sum of the magnitudes of the local velocity and the V f .
The governing equations in the ﬂame ﬁxed co-ordinates are similar to those in [18],
where an unsteady ﬂame spread phenomenon is modelled. In the present work, however, this
model is extended to explore steady ﬂame spread over an array of parallel thin fuel sheets.
