Solid phase model
The thin solid fuel model comprises equations of continuity and energy in one-dimension,
along with a solid fuel pyrolysis law. The solid fuel is assumed to be both thermally and
aerodynamically thin. Microgravity experiments on thin solid fuels [21] have shown that the
ﬂame spread rate is dictated by the combustion of volatiles and not by slow combustion of
char. Hence, the simulated solid fuel is assumed to burn ideally i.e. it vaporises to form fuel
vapours without melting or charring. The solid considered here (Kimwipes) is a cellulosic
material with half thickness τ = 0.0038 cm and density ρs = 263 kg/m3. The pyrolysis of
fuel is modelled using a one-step, zeroth-order, Arrhenius kinetics. The pyrolysis model
relating fuel vapour mass ﬂux from the solid to the surface temperature can be represented
as:
