the computed heat ﬂux at the surface the local absorption coefﬁcient K is computed as
K(x, y) = CKp, where C is the correction factor to account for the optical thickness of the
ﬂame. The calculation of the correction factor C is based on the calibration of the absorption
coefﬁcient against the narrow-band results through a quasi-one-dimensional ﬂame [24, 25],
to account for the different optical lengths in different parts of the ﬂame and the effect of
spectral self-absorption of gaseous species. The calculation procedure is explained in detail
in [11, 19].
The local Plank-mean absorption coefﬁcient for the mixture can be given by Kp =
PCO2 · Kp(co2) + PH2O · Kp(H2o), where Pi represents the partial pressure of species i. The
values of Kp for each species are taken from T’ien, [26], as a function of temperature.
The above set of governing equations and boundary conditions completely deﬁnes the
problem and can be solved numerically. Optimised grids are generated for each of these
blocks (a total of 42 grid points in the Y direction and 227 grid points in the X direction).
The X-co-ordinate, along the solid fuel, is in the same direction as the oxidiser ﬂow. The
computational domain in the X-direction is 101 cm, 49 cm upstream and 52 cm downstream.
The minimum grid sizes in the X and Y directions are 0.005 and 0.001 of the thermal length
