The ﬂame width (height above the fuel surface) for the single ﬂame is approximately
1 cm from the fuel surface (Figure 3(a)). For interacting ﬂames, when the fuels sheets are
placed far apart (Figure 3(b)), the ﬂame width is still about the same magnitude. However,
when placed closer, the ﬂame width is limited by spacing between the fuel sheets and
therefore the two ﬂames are seen to be merging (Figure 3(c) and (d)). An implication of
this is that the limited available oxygen has to be shared between the two ﬂames and this
probably is the cause for the drop in the ﬂame spread rate with further decrease in ‘d’.
The maximum ﬂame temperature is seen to increase from 5.20 (1 unit = 300 K) for a
single ﬂame to about 5.81 for d = 30 cm (Figure 3(b)). This is expected as the presence
of parallel fuel sheets reduces the radiation heat loss to the ambient. At small separation
distances, the non-dimensional maximum ﬂame temperature is much higher, at about 7.99
for d = 0.50 cm and somewhat lower at 7.44 for d = 0.25 cm. The reason for this strong
increase can be attributed to enhanced heat gains due to ﬂame interactions. One can also
note that the single ﬂame has a maximum temperature nearest to the ﬂame leading edge
(i.e. near the burnout point (x = 0) and decreases away from it, because of heat loss to the
ambient.
However, for interacting ﬂames, the zone of maximum temperature can be seen shifting
downstream from the ﬂame leading edge as ‘d’ is reduced. This suggests a strong interaction
between the two adjacent ﬂames and further reduction in the heat loss.
Figure 4 presents, the solid surface temperature (Ts) (Figure 4 (a)) and the fuel thickness
(h) proﬁles (Figure 4 (b)), along the fuel length for a single ﬂame and for ﬂames over multiple
fuel sheets, as discussed above. For a single ﬂame, the surface temperature rises steeply
from an ambient temperature to the pyrolysis temperature in the preheat zone (x > 0). The
highest surface temperature occurs at the ﬂame leading edge and then continues to drop
