To gain a deeper insight, we look at the heat ﬂuxes at the fuel surface. The incoming heat
ﬂux or the heat feedback to the fuel surface strongly depends on the ﬂame temperature, the
ﬂame size and the ﬂame shape. This incoming heat feedback is comprised of convective-
conductive heat ﬂuxes which we shall refer to here as a conduction heat ﬂux ‘qc’, as the
heat transfer to the fuel at the interface is ultimately by conduction, gas phase radiation
feedback ‘qr(gas)’ and the outgoing heat ﬂux due to radiation leaving solid surface ‘qr(solid)’.
The net radiation heat ﬂux is ‘qr(net) = qr(gas) + qr(solid)’ and the net heat ﬂux is ‘q(net) =
qr(net) + qc’. The positive values of the heat ﬂuxes are used to indicate the heat gain for the
solid and the negative values represent the heat loss from the surface. First, we compare
the spatial heat ﬂux distribution for a single ﬂame (Figure 5(a)) with that obtained for
interacting ﬂames spaced far apart (here d = 30 cm) (Figure 5(b)). For a single ﬂame, the
conduction heat ﬂux ‘qc’ is maximum at the ﬂame leading edge because the ﬂame is very
close to the solid and drops in the upstream direction. For interacting ﬂames, the conduction
heat ﬂux is ampliﬁed at the ﬂame leading edge owing to an increased gas temperature. It
is interesting to note that ‘qc’ drops upstream to a low negative value for x > 0.60 cm for
both the single ﬂame and twin ﬂames (shown in the inset of the respective ﬁgures). The gas
phase radiation feedback ‘qr(gas)’ is almost constant downstream in the pyrolysis zone but
decays less steeply compared to ‘qc’, upstream.
