It is important to note that the gas radiation is positive all over the fuel surface and
serves as the only heat feedback mechanism upstream. As pointed out before, ‘qc’ for
the upstream conditions is negative, which means conduction cools the fuel that is heated
by the incoming gas radiation. For interacting ﬂames, ‘qr(gas)’ is enhanced because of
the radiation interaction from the neighbouring fuel sheet. The small increase in the gas
phase radiation feedback is a consequence of weak ﬂame interactions. The solid fuel
surface radiation loss ‘qr(solid)’ for the single ﬂame as well as for the interacting ﬂames
peaks near the ﬂame anchoring point (x = 0), as the surface temperature is highest there
and decreases slowly upstream, owing to strong emissions from the high temperature solid
fuel surface. The interaction of ﬂames results in an increase in the solid fuel surface
temperature and consequently ‘qr(solid)’ loss is higher. However, increased gas radiation
results in the net radiation heat ﬂux

qr(net) = qr(gas) + qr(solid)

being higher for the ﬂames
over multiple fuel sheets compared to a single ﬂame. One can also note that the net heat
ﬂux

q(net) = qr(net) + qc

for both the single ﬂame and ﬂames over multiple fuel sheets
follows ‘qc’ for most of the fuel length and ‘qr(net)’ in the upstream region.
