While the qualitative features of the heat ﬂuxes on the fuel surface are similar to those of
a ﬂame over a single fuel sheet, we can note that the magnitude of maximum heat ﬂux is
about one order higher than that obtained for a single ﬂame, discussed previously. However,
the span of fuel length over which heat ﬂux is incident is considerably reduced (please
note the change in scale on ordinate and abscissa axes). One may also note that the gas
radiation

qr(gas)

is a signiﬁcant contributor to the total heat feedback to the solid fuel
(Figure 6(a)) at d = 0.50 cm. As the separation distance is reduced, the net heat ﬂux is
again dominated by ‘qc’ and the contribution of ‘qr(gas)’ drops. This is due to the reduced
gas temperature because of limited oxygen supply at reduced spacing. The reduction in the
ﬂame temperature reduces ﬂame radiation drastically (∼T4), compared to the conduction
heat ﬂux (∼T).
