Figure 7 summarises the heat ﬂux distribution for the cases of ﬂames over a single
fuel sheet, multiple fuel sheets at large interspace distance and multiple fuel sheets which
are closely spaced. At large interspace distances, the net heat ﬂux over the fuel surface
increases due to the decrease in radiation loss, which results in a higher ﬂame temperature
and thus a larger radiation feedback to the fuel surface. This interaction becomes stronger
as the spacing is reduced and the ﬂame spread rate increases.
Table 1 shows the integrated heat ﬂuxes over the preheat region of the fuel. It was
mentioned earlier that the ﬂame spread rate is proportional to the integrated net heat ﬂux
over the preheat region. For a single fuel sheet, the ﬂame radiation is a net loss mechanism.
However, in the presence of an adjacent ﬂame, this radiation becomes a positive contributor.
It is interesting to note that, although the ﬂame temperature increases for the ﬂame over
multiple fuel sheets (Figure 3) with large interspace distance, and the peak conduction
heat ﬂux also increased (Figure 5), the integrated conduction heat ﬂux drops to 7.67
kW/m2 from 8.28 kW/m2 for a single ﬂame. This drop is due to an increased cooling
contribution by conduction (Figure 4 inset), when the heating of the upstream fuel is by
radiation speciﬁcally in the case of ﬂame spread over multiple fuel sheets. This trend is
observed up to d = 25 cm, below which the steady ﬂame spread, was not obtained. At
small interspace distances between the fuel sheets

d< 1 cm

, the net heat ﬂux is about
an order of magnitude higher, and reduces as the separation distance is reduced further. It
was explained that this happened due to the reduced oxygen supply to the ﬂame at small
interspace distances. Radiation was seen to be a major contributor to the total heat feedback
to the solid fuel. Table 1 shows that the integrated net radiation ﬂux is about 8 times higher
than the integrated conduction heat ﬂux for d = 0.75 cm. This resulted in a ﬂame spread
rate of Vf = 8.34 cm/s which is 6 times higher than the ﬂame spread rate for a single ﬂame.
