a monotonic drop in the ﬂame spread rate with a reduction in ‘d’. This tells us that while
the gas radiation is important, the fuel surface needs to have high emittance in order to
absorb ﬂame radiation and hence affect the ﬂame spread rates. The ﬂame spread rate for
interacting ﬂames over multiple fuel sheets, for the case where radiation is turned off, was
more than that for the single ﬂame. For this case, where the radiation transport is turned off,
there is no radiation loss and the interaction between adjacent ﬂames is only by conduction,
which increases the ﬂame spread rate only marginally.
Lastly, we look at the variation of the ﬂame spread rate with varying oxygen concen-
tration. Figure 9 shows the variation in ﬂame spread rates for selected ‘d’, with stepwise
reduction in the oxygen concentration for a single ﬂame and for ﬂames over multiple fuel
sheets. One can note that the ﬂame spread rate for a single as well as interacting ﬂames
decrease monotonically with reduction in the oxygen concentration. At a ﬁxed oxygen
level, the ﬂame spread rates for ﬂames over multiple fuel sheets were higher than that for
a single fuel sheet ﬂame spread rate. The non-monotonic variation of the ﬂame spread rate
with separation distance as explained before was observed for other oxygen levels as well.
The single sheet ﬂame extinguished at an oxygen concentration of 22.40%.
However, it is interesting to note that the extinction of the ﬂame over multiple fuel sheets
occurs at lower oxygen levels. It was noted that the extinction limit occurs at lower oxygen
levels as the separation distance is reduced. For half separation distance below 2 cm, the
extinction point is below 5% oxygen level, which is much lower than that for a single sheet
ﬂame.
Several more computations were carried out at various separation distances to predict
the extinction limit. The imposed velocity was kept constant at 5 cm/s. A material ﬂamma-
bility map for parallel fuel sheets was constructed by plotting the extinction oxygen levels
at various half separation distances ‘d’ (See Figure 10). This material ﬂammability map
comprises, a steady ﬂame region (shown by hollow circles), a region of no convergence
