where ﬂame keeps growing (solid circles) and a no ﬂame region. The ﬂame extinction limit
for the single ﬂame (represented by a solid line with no symbols) is also shown. Simulations
show that the ﬂame extinction boundary (shown by a dash-dot curve with solid symbols
with delta symbols) is a ‘V’ shaped curve and exhibits a non-monotonic variation in the ex-
tinction oxygen limit as ‘d’ is reduced. The ﬂammability map shows that ﬂames spreading
over multiple sheets have lower extinction limits compared to a single ﬂame. The extinction
limits decreased with reduction in the fuel sheet separation distance and exhibited a minima
(of about 3% O2) at a spacing of d = 0.75 cm. Since radiation was identiﬁed to play an
important role in enhancing the ﬂame spread rate in the case of the ﬂame spreading over mul-
tiple fuel sheets, the ﬂammability map was constructed for the fuel with emittance (ε) and
absorptance (α) equal to 0. It is interesting to note that when the fuel emittance (equal to ab-
sorptance) is made ‘0’, the above mentioned trend reverses, i.e. the ﬂames over multiple fuel
sheets at separation distances

d< 20cm

extinguish at oxygen levels higher than that for a
single ﬂame. This is because the ﬂammability enhancement by radiation interaction does not
take place for a radiatively non-participating fuel and oxygen starvation makes the multiple
fuel conﬁguration less ﬂammable compared to the single ﬂame. However, at large interspace
distances where oxygen supply is not limited, reduced heat losses to the ambient makes the
multiple fuel conﬁguration slightly more ﬂammable than the single fuel conﬁguration.
It is to be noted that the simulations carried out here neglect three-dimensional (3D)
effects, which when included will increase the radiative heat loss from the ﬂame [11]. The
increased radiative loss in 3D compared to the 2D computations presented here is expected
to shrink the region of no convergence or even eliminate it.
Conclusions
The numerical exploration carried out here provides some insight to understand the effects
of an external heat source (parallel fuel sheet) on the ﬂame spread process and also material
Downloaded by [University of Connecticut] at 04:36 24 July 2013"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2013.809797,doi,Opposed flow flame spread over an array of thin solid fuel sheets in a microgravity environment,20,19,"Combustion Theory and Modelling
19
ﬂammability in an opposed ﬂow conﬁguration. The study primarily focused on the charac-
teristics of twin ﬂame interactions, the structure of the spreading ﬂames and the dependence
of the ﬂame spread rate on the separation distance between the fuel sheets. The inﬂuence
of a controlling parameter, heat transfer to the unburnt fuel, was explored and results were
compared with that of ﬂame over a single fuel sheet.
A ﬂammability map was constructed for thin parallel fuel sheets in a microgravity
environment. Based on the results obtained, the following conclusions may be drawn for
this study. The typical ﬂame spread rate for a ﬂame spreading over multiple fuel arrays
is higher than that for the ﬂame spreading over a single fuel sheet. The ﬂame spread rate
exhibits a non-monotonic behaviour with increase in ‘d’. At very large separation distances
(here ‘d’> 25 cm), the presence of parallel fuel sheets reduces the radiation heat loss to the
ambient and thus there is an increase in the ﬂame temperature. Consequently, the radiation
heat feedback to the fuel surface increases. Although the peak of the conduction heat
ﬂux increases due to the increased ﬂame temperature, the net contribution of conduction
decreases as compared to the single ﬂame. This was seen to happen because, in the far
upstream region, radiation from the ﬂame heats the fuel and then conduction cools it.
The ﬂame spread rate is strongly inﬂuenced by the direct radiation exchange between
the twin ﬂames and the heated fuel surfaces. No steady 2D solution was obtained as ‘d’
was reduced. However, at very small separation distances (here ‘d’< 1 cm which is of
the order of the ﬂame width of a single ﬂame) steady state solutions were again obtained.
This happened due to a reduction in the heat release rate arising from the limited supply
of oxygen to the twin ﬂames. This resulted in the drop of the ﬂame spread rate with a
further reduction in ‘d’. At sufﬁciently small ‘d’ the ﬂame extinguishes due to oxygen
starvation.
A material ﬂammability map was constructed for both single fuel sheet ﬂame and ﬂame
over multiple fuel sheet arrays at various interspace distances. At a fuel emittance value of
0.92 the ﬂame over multiple fuel sheets was found to be more ﬂammable than over a single
fuel sheet. There was a minimum extinction limit attained for a ﬁnite interspace distance.
This happens due to reduced radiation losses and the increased absorption of radiation by
the fuel sheets. Reducing fuel emittance to ‘0’ lowers the extinction oxygen limit for both
single and multiple fuel conﬁgurations, as the fuel does not absorb any radiation even when
ﬂame radiation interactions are present. There is a trend reversal between the extinction
limits of a single fuel sheet versus the multiple fuel sheet conﬁgurations. The fuel with lower
emittance is less ﬂammable in a multiple-fuel conﬁguration for small interspace distances as
compared to the single sheet conﬁguration. However, for interspace distance beyond a limit,
the multiple-fuel conﬁguration becomes more ﬂammable. At small interspace distances,
in the case of multiple fuels, reduced availability of oxygen makes the ﬂame extinguish at
higher oxygen levels as compared to a single ﬂame, whereas at a large interspace distance,
reduced radiative heat loss from the ﬂame, owing to the presence of neighbouring fuel
sheet, makes the multiple fuel conﬁgurations more ﬂammable.
