Appendix 1.
Previous studies (Kumar, [19]) had reported a mismatch of numerical predictions with
experimental data. One reason for this may be the use of arbitrary kinetic parameters in
the gas phase reaction kinetics and solid phase Arrhenius pyrolysis law in these models. A
better estimate of these kinetic parameters needs calibration with experiments. Tolejko et al.
[22] carried out a calibration study of gas phase and solid phase kinetic parameters using
normal gravity for downward ﬂame spread experiments on a composite fuel comprising of
cellulose over an inert glass ﬁbre.
In the present model, calibrated kinetics of Tolejko et al. [22] were adopted, along with
an adaptive modiﬁcation of the heat of combustion (Kumar and Kumar [11]), to consider
the effects of dissociation, especially at elevated oxygen levels. The modiﬁcations made in
the kinetic parameters of existing opposed ﬂow ﬂame spread model are shown in Table 2.
Figure 11(a) shows the ﬂammability map in oxygen mole fraction vs. gravity accel-
erations as stated by the experimental observations. The experiments were carried out by
Sacksteder and T’ien [28] to investigate the material ﬂammability for thin cellulosic fuel
under conditions of purely buoyant downward ﬂame spread. Based on experimental data, a
ﬂammability map was constructed comprising of a steady ﬂame region, a ﬂame extinction
boundary and non-ﬂammable region. Solid symbols (circles) indicate the ﬂammable region,
solid symbols (squares) represent ﬂame extinction at different gravity levels and hollow
symbols represent the non-ﬂammable conditions. Corresponding to the experimental data,
Figure 11(a) also shows the ﬂammability boundary predicted by the preceding numerical
works of Chen and Cheng [16] and by the opposed ﬂow model [19].
The ﬂammability boundary prediction by preceding numerical work is signiﬁcantly
different from experimental data. The critical oxygen limit (lowest oxygen level at which
ﬂame can propagate) predicted by Chen and Cheng [16] was 8.50% near to 0.20ge, whereas,
Kumar [19] had predicted the critical oxygen limit as 13.80% at 0.02ge, both of which do
not conform to the experimental data. The ﬂame spread rates obtained experimentally at
21% oxygen concentration were also compared with the predictions of numerical works as
shown in Figure 11(b). It was observed that the preceding numerical works over predict
the experimental ﬂame spread rate. The above discussed issues emphasise the signiﬁcance
and the need for a numerical model that can predict better (the ﬂame extinction limits and
the ﬂame spread rates). The modiﬁed opposed ﬂow model (a modiﬁcation of [19]) was
also tested under identical conditions of purely buoyant downward ﬂame spread over a thin
solid fuel (Kimwipes). Figure 11(a) shows the ﬂammability boundary, in co-ordinates of
oxygen mole fraction and normalised gravity accelerations, was predicted by the modiﬁed
opposed ﬂow model. The ﬂame extinction boundary, when compared with experimental
results conforms to the trend qualitatively as indicated by the experiments. Comparison with
the previous numerical work shows a signiﬁcant quantitative improvement in predicting the
experimental results.
