Asymptotic analysis of quasi-steady n-heptane droplet combustion
supported by cool-ﬂame chemistry
Kalyanasundaram Seshadria∗, Norbert Petersb,‡, Forman A. Williamsa, Vedha Nayagamc
and Guenter Paczkob
aDepartment of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, University of California at San Diego, La
Jolla, USA; bInstitut f¨ur Technische Verbrennung, RWTH Aachen, Aachen, Germany; cDepartment
of Mechanical Engineering, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, USA
(Received 21 April 2016; accepted 22 September 2016)
A skeletal chemical-kinetic mechanism for n-heptane cool ﬂames is simpliﬁed to the
maximum extent possible by introduction of steady-state approximations for interme-
diaries, following procedures employed previously in addressing two-stage ignition. A
pair of ordinary differential equations in mixture-fraction space is thereby obtained,
describing the quasi-steady structures of the temperature and heptylketohydroperoxide
ﬁelds. Application of activation-energy asymptotics for the partial-burning regime to
this pair of equations is shown to provide convenient expressions for ﬂame structures
and the extinction condition associated with maximally reduced chemistry. With the
mixture-fraction co-ordinate related to radius, these results are used to address droplet-
combustion experiments that have been performed in the International Space Station.
Droplet diameters at extinction are predicted as functions of the oxygen concentration
in the atmosphere and are compared with experiment. While the results are encour-
aging concerning qualitative predictions of dependences of extinction diameters on
atmospheric conditions, there are noticeable quantitative differences that point to deﬁ-
ciencies in the analysis, likely resulting from a number of oversimpliﬁcations. Further
investigation is therefore recommended.
Keywords: asymptotic analysis; partial burning regime; cool ﬂames; droplet
combustion; extinction
1.
Introduction
Autoignition processes of normal alkanes are known to exhibit unique characteristics that
depend on the initial temperature and pressure of the combustible mixture [1–3]. These
characteristics involve different chemical-kinetic mechanisms at high and low temperatures,
and there is a temperature range in between, the negative-temperature-coefﬁcient (NTC)
range, over which ignition occurs in two stages and the total ignition delay time tends
to decrease with increasing temperature [1–3]. In homogeneous mixtures, transient cool-
ﬂame phenomena are also observed over this intermediate temperature range, leading to
the chemical mechanism being characterised as cool-ﬂame chemistry. Until recently, such
phenomena have not been associated with diffusion ﬂames, although cool-ﬂame chemistry
had been found to be of signiﬁcance in the autoignition of alkane droplets in heated at-
mospheres [4]. Experiments performed in the International Space Station (ISS), however,
∗Corresponding author. Email: seshadri@ucsd.edu
‡Deceased.
C⃝2016 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2016.1248494,doi,Asymptotic analysis of quasi-steady n-heptane droplet combustion supported by cool-flame chemistry,3,2,"2
K. Seshadri et al.
revealed instances of two-stage alkane droplet combustion in which a hot-ﬂame stage, after
extinguishing through a radiant-heat-loss mechanism, was followed by a quasi-steady stage
of combustion sustained by cool-ﬂame chemistry [5]. Because of its possible future prac-
tical importance, for example in enhancing the performance of internal-combustion piston
engines [6,7], there is interest in improving our understanding of this kind of combustion
chemistry [8], and therefore there has been continuing computational [9–11] and experi-
mental [12–14] study of alkane droplet combustion supported by cool-ﬂame chemistry.
The present contribution is an analytical investigation addressing the objective of ad-
vancing understanding of quasi-steady cool diffusion ﬂames, which occur in this NTC
region [15]. Previous work has shown how asymptotic analyses can be applied to a short
n-heptane mechanism to explain the NTC behaviour [16]. The chemical-kinetic develop-
ments employed in this previous study are applied here to describe quasi-steady droplet
burning and extinction. Use will be made of our previous formulation [15] and of results
derived in that study. In particular, based on the chemistry and stoichiometry obtained there,
the partial-burning regime of Li˜n´an [17] on diffusion-ﬂame structure will be adopted here,
as the closest approximation of any of the four regimes identiﬁed in that paper on the basis
of activation-energy asymptotics (AEA). The approach is intended to develop analytical
results for the ﬂame structure and extinction conditions, so that some of the dominant
phenomena can be identiﬁed and employed to test experimental data, even though that may
result in appreciable inaccuracies.
2.
The chemistry
The starting point for describing the chemistry is the most recent 62-step skeletal mech-
anism [15], but with certain updated reaction-rate parameters. The mechanism is very
similar to an earlier [16] 56-step mechanism, and it can be treated in the same way that
the earlier mechanism was treated [16]. The heptyl radical, one of many intermediate
species that obeys well a steady-state approximation, can decompose, unimolecularly in a
reasonable ﬁrst approximation, as it does during the high-temperature chemistry, or it can
undergo the bimolecular addition of an oxygen molecule, which initiates the path to the
low-temperature chemistry. The fraction of the radical consumed by the second of these
processes determines the fraction of the chemistry that proceeds by the low-temperature
path. If the total unimolecular rate constant for heptyl consumption is denoted by A and
the net total bimolecular rate constant is denoted by B, then the fraction following the
low-temperature path is α = B[O2]/(A + B[O2]), where [O2] denotes the oxygen concen-
tration. This description is adopted to achieve maximum simplicity, under the assumption
that bimolecular radical-removal steps are of lesser importance.
The NTC region is the only region in which the cool diffusion ﬂame is stable [15], and
this occurs in a close vicinity of α = 1/2, which is satisﬁed at a temperature T equal to T ∗,
deﬁned as the temperature at which A = B[O2]. Since A increases rapidly with T, while
B depends only weakly on temperature, α increases strongly as T decreases in the centre
of the NTC region, as it must, to describe properly the transition from high-temperature
to low-temperature chemistry. An expansion of α about T = T ∗[16] is central to an
analytical description of the NTC chemistry. This expansion is developed from the steady-
state approximation for hydroxyl, the most important active radical, while making use, in
addition, of a number of other applicable steady states. In an Arrhenius approximation
for the ratio A/B with an effective activation temperature for this ratio of Ta, a two-term
expansion that results is α = 1/2 −(T −T ∗)Ta/(4T ∗2), which is employed here, having been
found previously to be very useful for addressing two-stage ignition. This approximation"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2016.1248494,doi,Asymptotic analysis of quasi-steady n-heptane droplet combustion supported by cool-flame chemistry,4,3,"Combustion Theory and Modelling
3
may contribute to inaccuracies in the prediction of droplet diameters at cool-ﬂame extinction
if the extinction occurs beyond the range of applicability of the expansion.
The skeletal mechanism [15,16] actually distinguishes two isomers of the heptyl radical.
Their rates of high-temperature decomposition, steps 17 and 18 in Table 1 of [15], are
different and have slightly different activation energies. The sum of the two rates is needed
in the simpliﬁed description for ﬁnding A, and their activation energies are close enough
that with excellent accuracy they can be set equal to their average value, resulting in an
associated activation temperature of 14,390 K. The required sum of the pre-factors is then
3.7 × 1013 s−1. The calculation of the net rate of O2 addition is further complicated by the
fact that the rate of the reverse of that step is not small enough to permit the reverse step
to be neglected, so that, in a correct computation of the net rate, its reduction associated
with the reverse step must be taken into account. Fortunately, the species formed by the O2
addition (often denoted by RO2 when the heptyl radical is denoted by R) obeys an accurate
chemical-kinetic steady-state approximation, ultimately enabling accurate expressions for
B to be derived, as described next.
The forward rate constant for the O2 addition step, the sum of the rate constants 9f
and 10f in Table 1 of [15] for the two isomers, is 4.2 × 1012 cm3/(mol · s). Besides re-
dissociation, steps 9b and 10b, with the sum of the pre-factors 3.7 × 1015 s−1 and identical
activation temperatures of 14,120 K, the species formed by the O2 addition isomerises
to a species that does not re-dissociate, at a rate with a pre-factor 1.38 × 1011 s−1 and
an activation temperature of 8570 K as given by step 11 of Table 1 of [15]. Although
this activation temperature is less than that for re-dissociation, yielding a larger factor
multiplying the pre-exponential at temperature T ∗, above 750 K (as it turns out to be), it is
high enough that its pre-factor, less than 10−4 times that for re-dissociation, maintains the
isomerisation rate at values less than a few per cent of the re-dissociation rate. The steady-
state expression for the concentration of the species formed by O2 addition, obtained
by equating the formation rate to the sum of the isomerisation and re-dissociation rates,
then reduces to that obtained from partial equilibrium of the O2 addition, with sufﬁcient
accuracy, resulting in the net rate of addition being that of isomerisation. This results in an
Arrhenius expression for B with an activation temperature of −5550 K, a negative value,
and a pre-factor of 1.57 × 108 cm3/(mol · s). The Arrhenius approximation for the ratio
A/B then exhibits an activation temperature Ta = 20,000 K (within the accuracy of these
values), larger than that of A, with a pre-factor of 2.4 × 105 cm3/(mol · s).
Heptylketohydroperoxide, denoted as KET in [16] and simply as K hereinafter, is the
key intermediate species in the NTC region, being formed in step 14 of Table 1 of [15]. It is
the hydroxly production in the unimolecular decomposition of K that ultimately leads to the
chain branching which carries the chemistry of the low-temperature path, in contrast to the
hydroperoxyl production associated with heptyl decomposition, which carries the chemistry
in the high-temperature autoignition path [1,2,18]. The NTC chemistry transpires to produce
an autocatalytic net rate of production of K for α < 1/2 and a net rate of consumption of
K for α > 1/2, approximately. The most important chemistry in the centre of the NTC
region can then be expressed in terms of a net molar rate of production of K dominated by
(2α −1)w, with w = [K]Cexp (−TK/T )/ (1 −α), where C is a constant unimolecular
pre-factor, and TK is the associated activation temperature [16]. These rate parameters
are, in fact, the rate parameters for the unimolecular decomposition of K, so that C =
4.0 × 1013 s−1 and TK = 19, 840 K, with updated values [19] that describe the rates better,
especially at the pressures of interest herein. These rates are improvements over those
obtained from step 15 in Table 1 of [15], although further improvements may be anticipated
in the future based on future experiments, including those in the ISS of the type addressed"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2016.1248494,doi,Asymptotic analysis of quasi-steady n-heptane droplet combustion supported by cool-flame chemistry,5,4,"4
K. Seshadri et al.
here. This simple result for the reaction rate follows directly from Equation (42) of [16]
by neglecting additional terms that become important at lower or higher temperatures but
that are small in comparison with this term in the centre of the NTC region, as may be
veriﬁed by following the development in [16]. Since 19,840 ≈20,000 within the accuracy
of these values, no distinction between the two activation temperatures is made here, and
the symbol Ta will be used for both.
The energetics associated with this autocatalytic rate-controlling step lead to an expres-
sion for the rate of increase of temperature, correspondingly obtained from Equation (44)
of [16] after replacing changes of internal energy with changes of enthalpy, and it is given
by (Q/cp)(α −0.08)w, where cp is the speciﬁc heat at constant pressure (to be assumed
constant), and Q is a constant determining the ultimate net heat release associated with
the decomposition reaction of K. The resulting heat release will be less than half that for
complete combustion to the most stable products because, for example, practically no CO2
is produced in the diffusion ﬂame during the cool-ﬂame mode.
3.
Formulation
Following our previous work [15], the problem is formulated employing as the independent
variable the mixture fraction Z, having the value zero in the ambient gas, where conditions
are identiﬁed by the subscript ‘0’ and unity in the interior of the liquid, where conditions
are identiﬁed by the subscript ‘1’. The strain rate χ = 2D(dZ/dr)2 then appears in the
conservation equations, where D denotes the thermal diffusivity and r the radial co-ordinate.
The development in the ﬁrst eight equations of [15], based only on the major assumption
that all Lewis numbers are unity, is consistent with the satisfaction of correct boundary
conditions at the droplet surface. In terms of T and the mass fraction of species K, Y =
[K]WK/ρ, where WK denotes the molecular weight of K and ρ the density, with use made
of the chemistry developed in the preceding section, the equations for conservation of K
and of energy become, respectively,
