each having been selected to fall near extinction conditions in the range of values over which
solutions are obtainable; for the detailed mechanism the reference value is 250 s−1, but the
value is only 4 s−1 for the present computations because no solutions could be obtained
for values much larger than that, an expected consequence of our drastically simpliﬁed
chemistry.
The previous work [15] also introduced a 62-step reduced mechanism, a simpliﬁcation
of the 770-step mechanism whose results are shown in Figure 1, and for that mechanism
solutions were obtainable only for a range of χref around 25 s−1, and the shapes of the
curves were intermediate between those of the two cases shown in Figure 1, with the
temperature proﬁle closer to that of the detailed mechanism, but the general shape of the K
proﬁle appreciably different, although the overall level of the predicted K concentration is
only about ﬁve times that for the detailed mechanism, much closer than the factor of about
100 difference seen in Figure 1. The strong sensitivity of the needed values of χref to the
level of reduction is caused partially by the reductions’ removing some less essential steps
that nevertheless have substantial quantitative inﬂuences on the predictions and partially
by residual inaccuracies in the rate parameters that were retained, those values having been
developed mostly from autoignition data obtained at much higher pressures than those of
interest here."
doi:10.1080/13647830.2016.1248494,doi,Asymptotic analysis of quasi-steady n-heptane droplet combustion supported by cool-flame chemistry,7,6,"6
K. Seshadri et al.
The comparisons in Figure 1 display qualitative agreement in that, for example, there are
two peaks in the proﬁle of species K, one on the fuel side and the other on the air side, with
a region in between, at the highest temperature, where that species is consumed rather than
being produced, as is evident from the curvature in mixture-fraction space. An advantage
of the Z co-ordinate is that, by combining diffusive and convective effects into a single
term of diffusion form, a direct relationship between the curvature and the rate of chemical
production or consumption emerges. Despite the qualitative agreement, however, there are
important aspects of disagreement. For example, the peak production rate is higher on the
fuel side for the detailed mechanism but higher on the air side for the present mechanism.
The higher fuel-side peak is caused by additional production steps, which are faster when
the fuel concentration is larger. Those steps are not retained in the present chemistry, and a
number of such steps would be required to improve agreements appreciably. It is because
of the (1 −α) factor in (1) and the higher temperature and steeper temperature drop on the
air side than on the fuel side in Z-space that the nonlinear dependence of α on T causes the
air-side peak to be higher. These aspects of deﬁciency of the present simpliﬁed chemical
kinetics produce related discrepancies in purely homogeneous systems and are tolerated
here for the purpose of achieving a manageable diffusion-ﬂame analysis.
A further signiﬁcant difference is the larger concentrations of species K for the present
description than is obtained with the detailed chemistry, a result which might be expected
since that effectively is the only species treated here. The other important disagreement
to be observed is the appreciably larger range of Z over which the chemistry occurs in
the present approximation than with the detailed mechanism. This becomes especially
evident when comparing the temperature curves, there being a fairly extended region of
nearly constant temperature in the vicinity of the maximum with the present approximation.
This non-physical effect was found to increase with increasing rates of scalar dissipation,
thereby suggesting increased inaccuracy in the scaling of the stretched variable as extinction
conditions are approached. For this reason, the asymptotic analysis should not be expected
to produce good agreements with experimentally observed droplet extinction diameters.
Better chemistry would be needed for that. The purpose of the present work is to improve
understanding of the principal path with the simplest possible chemistry, irrespective of
inaccuracies in extinction predictions, with possible improvements in predictions left for
future work.
4.
