The calculation presumes that extinction occurs in the NTC region. As the droplet size
decreases during quasi-steady combustion in the cool-ﬂame regime, the ﬂame temperature
Tb decreases [15], and so, if the value of of de predicted by (22) is less than the droplet diam-
eter at the low-temperature end of the NTC region, then the ﬂame will become unstable and
extinguish at a diameter larger than that predicted. In that sense, the computation provides
a lower bound for the extinction diameter. The aforementioned effect of loss of K from the
reaction zone by Soret diffusion, as well as the need for O2 to diffuse through products of
the hot-ﬂame combustion to reach the cool-ﬂame reaction zone, also tend to cause experi-
mental extinction diameters to exceed those predicted. A further underprediction but with
smaller effect arises from the linear expansion in (13); a fully asymptotic analysis, which
is appreciably more involved than that presented here, respects the conditions ϕ > b and
b < 2, which cause the value of a−1 given by the integral in (19) to be slightly too large.
Table 1 and Figure 2 compare measured and predicted values of the extinction diam-
eters. In addition to the previously speciﬁed chemical-kinetic parameters, the calculations
employed an estimated thermal diffusivity of 1.13 × 10−4 m2/s for D, which is twice the
self-diffusion coefﬁcient of nitrogen at the average temperature of 550 K, and for TL, the
normal boiling temperature of the liquid n-heptane . The predicted diameters are indeed
less than those observed, the difference approaching a factor of two. This suggests that,
consistent with the effects indicated above, the lower bound predicted by the present theory
lies below the true value, although the uncertainties involved in the calculation, notably
the selection of the value of D (uncertainties in which contribute greatly to uncertainties
in the predictions), are large enough that this conclusion is not certain. In view of the
differences stemming solely from the chemical kinetics, as exhibited in Figure 1, the extent
of agreement found in Table 1 may be surprising."
