8.
Discussion and conclusions
The experimental data with which the predictions are compared in Table 1 differ from
the data against which the previous results [15] were compared. For that earlier work,
only the ﬁrst experimental results [5] were available. Newer and better experiments have
been completed [13], and the present comparisons are based on this more recent data. The
comparisons have been made here only for selected data points. All of the n-heptane data
[13] are at normal atmospheric pressure, and they distinctly demonstrate a dependence
of the extinction diameter on the initial diameter of the droplet, larger initial diameters
leading to larger extinction diameters (as previously indicated, earlier extinction is asso-
ciated with the reduced oxygen concentrations in the cool ﬂame produced by the need
for diffusion through a greater amount of hot-ﬂame reaction products). To minimise this
effect, only data corresponding to the smallest initial droplet diameters (2.4 to 2.8 mm)
were selected for use in Table 1, but that does not eliminate the effect entirely. The trend
of increasing extinction diameter with increasing dilution is shared by the predictions and
experiments, however, despite the differences in values.
While agreements may be improved by selecting different experimental results for
comparisons, it seems best to attempt comparisons with data deemed most likely to con-
form most closely to the assumptions of the analysis. The resulting comparisons lead to
the systematic differences seen in Figure 2, where the scatter would be much greater if
the other data had been included. The differences found here point to ways in which the
accuracy of the analysis may be improved. Especially important could be the elimination
of the assumption of unity Lewis number. Accounting for differences between the thermal
diffusivity and species diffusion coefﬁcients, as well as differences in diffusion coefﬁcients
of different species, and also taking into account Soret diffusion, could possibly improve
agreements greatly. Future investigations incorporating improved transient descriptions are
warranted, and not until such work is completed will it be possible to evaluate how closely
the predictions of extinction conditions by AEA of the partial-burning regime correspond
to experimental results.
The hypotheses that underlie the present formulation and analysis are based on as simple
an approximation to the true cool-ﬂame chemistry as may reasonably be possible for these"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2016.1248494,doi,Asymptotic analysis of quasi-steady n-heptane droplet combustion supported by cool-flame chemistry,13,12,"12
K. Seshadri et al.
droplet diffusion-ﬂame problems. This choice was made for the purpose of focusing as
clearly as possible on the intricacies of the type of asymptotic analysis that would be
needed to address problems of this kind, despite the deﬁciencies evident in Figure 1.
Removal of any of the chemical-kinetic or transport approximations necessarily will make
the analysis more complicated, and it may or may not ﬁnally be amenable to analytical
predictions. It may well be likely that numerical aspects may become necessary in analyses
capable of achieving good quantitative agreement even with results of future experiments
that avoid extraneous complexities such as the presence of products of earlier stages of
combustion in the atmosphere. Whether or not that turns out to be true, additional quasi-
steady numerical integrations in mixture-fraction space with better transport and gradually
more complex kinetics could help to clarify the dominant sources of differences seen for
the present experiments. Irrespective of the outcomes of such future investigations, the
demonstration here of the ability to obtain analytical results for cool-ﬂame chemistry in
non-premixed systems by use of AEA for the partial-burning regime, including predictions
of experimentally observed trends, seems noteworthy.
