doi:10.1080/13647830.2025.2539131,doi,Flame spread over thin circular PMMA rods: numerical modelling,2,1,"Combustion Theory and Modelling, 2025
Vol. 29, No. 6, 615–642, https://doi.org/10.1080/13647830.2025.2539131
Flame spread over thin circular PMMA rods: numerical modelling
Basavanahalli Vrushabendra Manua,b, Kambam Naresha and Amit Kumara∗
aNational Centre for Combustion Research and Development and Department of Aerospace
Engineering, Indian Institute of Technology Madras, Chennai, India bDepartment of Aeronautical
Engineering, Acharya Institute of Technology, Bengaluru, India
(Received 28 January 2025; accepted 30 June 2025)
In this article, ﬂame spread phenomena over cylindrical fuel geometry are stud-
ied in spacecraft environments and contrasted with those on Earth. An in-house 2D
axisymmetric computational ﬂuid dynamics model is used to study the ﬂame spread
phenomena. Experimental ﬂame spread results, in normal gravity and microgravity
conditions, are used for model validation. The effect of uniform external opposed
ﬂow (0–25 cm/s) and surrounding oxygen concentrations (17.5–35%) is investigated
in detail for a thin fuel rod of diameter 1 mm. The ﬂow ﬁelds around the ﬂame spread-
ing in normal gravity and microgravity are very different. In normal gravity, buoyancy
accelerates the ﬂow and entrains surrounding air into the ﬂame. In microgravity, the
ﬂow velocities increase only moderately due to thermal expansion. The change in the
ﬂow ﬁeld results in signiﬁcantly larger ﬂames in microgravity, which spreads faster than
the normal gravity ﬂames. A detailed heat transfer analysis is carried out for the solid
fuel. In general, the radial heat conduction of heat from the ﬂame to fresh fuel ahead
of the ﬂame is the most prominent mode of heat transfer that controls the ﬂame spread
rate. However, in normal gravity conditions, near the low oxygen extinction limit, the
contribution of axial heat conduction through the solid fuel also becomes signiﬁcant
and may contribute to about 20% of the total heat input to the fresh solid fuel. In nor-
mal gravity, the ﬂame spread rate decreases with the increase in external ﬂow speed. On
the other hand, in microgravity, an increasing and decreasing trend in ﬂame spread rate
is observed with the increase in external ﬂow speed. The ﬂame spread rate increases
with the increase in oxygen in normal gravity and microgravity environments.
Keywords: ﬂame spread; thin cylindrical PMMA fuel rods; numerical modelling;
normal gravity; microgravity
Introduction
The study of a diffusion ﬂame spreading over combustible solid materials is important in
understanding the combustion and ﬁre safety aspects. The diffusion ﬂame spreads over
solid fuels by supplying heat to the fresh fuel ahead of it. This heating produces fresh
fuel vapour by pyrolysis, which diffuses and mixes with the surrounding air and forms a
ﬂame to propagate forward [1–3]. On Earth, the convective ﬂow generated due to gravity
inﬂuences the ﬂame spread. On the other hand, in a spacecraft environment, the absence
of natural convection, microgravity, small ﬂows due to ventilation systems and often ele-
vated oxygen levels makes the ﬂame behave differently [4–6]. Due to these conditions,
the materials’ burning behaviour may be different in the spacecraft environment from with
∗Corresponding author. Email: amitk@ae.iitm.ac.in
© 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2025.2539131,doi,Flame spread over thin circular PMMA rods: numerical modelling,3,2,"616
B.V. Manu et al.
those on Earth. Hence, it is essential to study the ﬂame spread process over materials in
microgravity conditions and compare it with those observed under Earth’s gravity con-
ditions. Over several decades, researchers have conducted studies on different materials
(charring and non-charring) in different geometries (like planar, spherical and cylindrical).
Of these, the ﬂame spread phenomena over ﬂat solid surfaces have been studied extensively
by experiments and numerical simulations, and the progress has been reported periodically
in reviews [7–9]. In addition to planar geometry, cylindrical geometry also has signiﬁcant
practical applications, including those of electrical wires, optical ﬁbres, human hair, etc.
Therefore, several ﬂame-spread studies have also been conducted on cylindrical geome-
tries; however, most of the studies are conducted on thick cylindrical fuel rods of diameters
( > 6 mm), and studies on thin cylindrical materials are signiﬁcantly limited. Sibulkin and
Lee [10] conducted ﬂame spread experiments on PMMA cylinders with diameters from
1.6 mm to 12.7 mm, in different orientations with respect to the gravity vector. Detailed
temperature measurements in the solid and gas phases and an energy balance analysis for
one case of (12.7 mm diameter) PMMA, were carried out. It was reported that approx-
imately 40% of the heat transferred to the unburnt fuel ahead of the ﬂame was due to
conduction within the solid, while the remaining 60% resulted from surface heat transfer
from the gas phase (ﬂame). It was estimated that only 6% of the heat released by combus-
tion in the ﬂame was returned to the fuel rod. After accounting for losses, just 1% of the
total heat contributed to the propagation of the ﬂame. Fernandez Pello and Santoro [11]
conducted normal gravity ﬂame spread experiments on PMMA rods with two extreme
diameters, 1.6 and 50 mm. Temperature measurements in the solid and gas phases revealed
that the dominant mode of heat transfer to the fuel ahead of the ﬂame depends on the
radius of the cylinder. For thinner fuels, heat transfer via gas phase conduction play a more
signiﬁcant role, whereas for thicker fuels, solid phase conduction became the more impor-
tant mechanism. Long-duration microgravity ﬂame spread experiments are conducted over
thick PMMA rods (>6.4 mm diameter) aboard the ISS [12] and the SJ 10 Satellite [13].
The experiments showed a non-monotonic increase-decrease variation in ﬂame spread rate
with opposed ﬂow speed. Further, around 21% of oxygen ﬂame could spread faster in a
microgravity environment compared to a normal gravity environment. Bundy [14] reported
on ﬂame spread experiments and CFD simulations. The study is conducted on clear cast
PMMA rods (2 and 6.4 mm diameter) and cellulose cylinders (7.5 and 12 mm diameter).
The experiments on PMMA rods were performed in quiescent microgravity at a 50% oxy-
gen concentration, while the cellulose cylinders were tested in normal gravity at 21%
oxygen with opposed airﬂow velocities ranging from 2 to 9 cm/s. It was concluded that
steady ﬂame spread does not occur in microgravity for fuel rods with a diameter of 10 mm.
Tarifa et al. [15] conducted reduced gravity (aboard a KC-135 aircraft) ﬂame spread exper-
iments on clear cast PMMA rods (1.5, 2.0 and 2.5 mm diameters). All the experiments
are conducted in a quiescent, oxygen-rich (35% oxygen mass fraction) environment. It
was reported that the ﬂame spread faster in reduced gravity (10−3g) compared to nor-
mal gravity. Interestingly, the ﬂame shape is very different from the commonly observed
round shape ﬂame in microgravity. The ﬂame appeared distorted and was marked with
random ﬂuctuations. Olson et al. [16] performed normal and microgravity ﬂame spread
experiments on strands of human hair, with diameters ranging from 60 to 70 microns. The
oxygen concentration was varied between 21% and 34% in nitrogen, at pressures between
8 and 14.7 psia, with a forced airﬂow of 20 cm/s. Flame extinguished below the oxygen
level of 30%. For oxygen concentration equal to and above 30%, the ﬂame spread occurred
in opposed and in ﬂow directions, in normal gravity and microgravity environments. The"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2025.2539131,doi,Flame spread over thin circular PMMA rods: numerical modelling,4,3,"Combustion Theory and Modelling
617
spread rate increased with higher oxygen concentrations. The concurrent ﬂame spread rate
was higher in normal gravity than microgravity. However, in opposed ﬂow conditions, the
trend is reversed, with ﬂame spreading faster in microgravity. Korobeinichev et al. [17]
studied downward ﬂame spread over single pine needles with a 0.6 mm × 1 mm cross-
section in normal gravity. Detailed chemical and thermal characteristics of the ﬂame were
measured, including species concentrations in the gas phase and temperature ﬁelds in the
gas and solid phases. Naresh et al. [18] numerically simulated the ﬂame spread experiments
on pine needles conducted by Korobeinichev et al. [17]. The pine needles were modelled
as thin cellulose cylinders with a diameter of 0.8 mm, and a 2D axisymmetric numerical
model was developed to simulate the ﬂame spread process, incorporating char formation
and oxidation. The simulation results showed good agreement with the experimental data.
In addition, several studies reported on ﬂame spread over electrical wires [19–22], where
the effects of surrounding oxidiser, ﬂow speed, core diameter, melting, dripping and other
factors are considered.
The above literature survey shows that the ﬂame spread studies over a thermally thin
circular rod [23], which are typically of a diameter of the order of a millimtre or below,
are scarce, and those available have limited scope. Limited microgravity experiments on
thin fuel rods in a quiescent environment have also shown a ﬂuctuating ﬂame behaviour
not noted over thick fuel rods. The work here, therefore, presents a more comprehen-
sive study, primarily comprising simulations and supported by experiments [24] in normal
gravity and microgravity environments. The numerical simulations are carried out using
an extensively validated in-house Computational Fluid Dynamics ﬂame spread model for
cylindrical fuels. Here, the ﬂame spread behaviour is examined for the smallest possible
diameter (1 mm) of cast PMMA cylinders for a range of ﬂow speeds and oxygen levels.
The detailed heat transport process inside the gas and solid phases is also obtained from
the simulations and discussed to explain the ﬂame spread process in thin cylindrical fuels.
Experiments
The details of the ﬂame spread experiments over thin PMMA circular rod fuels in normal
gravity and microgravity environments have been reported in a recent work by the authors
[24]. Here, the work is summarised for adequate understanding and completeness of infor-
mation required for numerical simulation. The ﬂame spread experiments were carried out
over clear cast PMMA cylindrical rods of 1 mm diameter. A picture of a ﬂame spreading
over a PMMA cylindrical rod with a sectional view of the combustion tunnel and experi-
mental drop capsule of a microgravity drop tower is shown in Figure 1. The experiments
are conducted in normal gravity and microgravity conditions. The microgravity experi-
ments are conducted in a ground-based microgravity drop tower. The videos of the ﬂame
spread process are captured using a Canon M-50 Mark-2 digital camera at the rate of 50
frames per second. The ﬂame spread rates are obtained by the digital image processing
technique.
Numerical model and solution
An in-house 2D-axisymmetric, aerodynamically and thermally thick, numerical model for
laminar ﬂame spread over cylindrical solid fuels is used to simulate the ﬂame spread at
different ﬂow speeds and oxygen levels in normal gravity and microgravity environments."
doi:10.1080/13647830.2025.2539131,doi,Flame spread over thin circular PMMA rods: numerical modelling,5,4,"618
B.V. Manu et al.
Figure 1.
(a) Picture of the opposed ﬂame spreading over a PMMA cylindrical rod, (b) sectional
view of the combustion tunnel and (c) picture of experimental drop capsule of a microgravity drop
tower (colour online).
The present numerical model is capable of simulating ﬂame spread over cylindrical rod
fuels, and it is developed from an existing 2D-axisymmetric, thin charring fuel, opposed
ﬂow ﬂame spread model, which successfully predicted various aspects of ﬂame spread
over thin pine needles [18]. The charring fuel version of the present model is reported in
[25], where the model predicted the ﬂame spread, ﬂame structure and ﬂow ﬁeld aspects of
the ﬂames spreading in normal gravity and microgravity environments at various oxygen
concentrations. Here, thermally thick fuels mean there exists a temperature gradient in the
solid fuel, and pyrolysis occurs in depth, and aerodynamically thick fuel means that the
solid fuel offers a resistance to the incoming ﬂow. Here, the circular clear cast PMMA rod
fuels are non-charring and burn ideally without melting and dripping. Upon pyrolysis, the
solid fuel yields gaseous pyrolysate (vapour of Methyl methacrylate) without leaving any
residue.
Figure 2(a) shows the schematic of the opposed ﬂow ﬂame spread over cylindrical rod
fuels. Figure 2(b,c) shows the computational domain with boundaries and the typical grid
structure used in the simulation. Also shown in Figure 2(a) is the enlarged preheat region.
The domain has a length of 110 cm in the axial direction (X) and 30 cm in the radial direc-
tion (R). A symmetry about an axis is assumed, and variations only in radial and axial
directions are considered. The location where the fuel density is 95% of the virgin solid
fuel is the origin (O (0,0)) in the ﬂame ﬁxed coordinates, about which the ﬂame anchors
above the fuel surface. The location X > 0 is upstream, and X < 0 is downstream of the
ﬂame. In the upstream, the solid fuel extends up to 10 cm and in the downstream solid
fuel extends up to 1 cm. The grids have clustered structures, with ﬁner grids in the region
of high gradients, where the ﬂame anchors close to the fuel surface and at walls to cap-
ture the gradients accurately. Appropriate domain size and number of grids are arrived at
by conducting a grid-independent study and ensuring that the solutions are independent
of domain and grid sizes. In the present model, the governing equations are presented in"
