a non-dimensional form. A detailed description of the non-dimensionalisation procedure
is reported in [26,27]. The governing equations are solved with boundary and interface
boundary conditions to obtain the solution for the problem. The governing equations for
solid and gas phases in non-dimensional form, along with the boundary conditions, are
presented in the following section.
Solid phase model
As already mentioned, the solid fuel is modelled as thermally thick, and the pressure in the
solid fuel is constant. Also, the thermal swelling, shrinkage, melting, dripping and surface
regression (change of shape and thickness of the solid fuel) due to pyrolysis are not consid-
ered. Therefore, only mass conservation and energy conservation equations are solved. The
change in the solid fuel is accounted for by considering the density change of the fuel until
burnout. The solid fuel undergoes an in-depth pyrolysis and generates a volatile pyrolysate.
This pyrolysis process is modelled according to the ﬁrst-order Arrhenius kinetics.
