Numerical scheme and solution
The gas-phase equations are discretised using ﬁnite volume technique and solved numer-
ically using the SIMPLER algorithm. The velocities are stored at staggered grid locations
with respect to scalar variables. The resulting set of algebraic equations is solved by
sweeping the x-r plane. Along each plane, a line-by-line procedure is used, which is a
combination of Gauss-Seidel and the tri-diagonal matrix algorithm. Here, the gas-phase
equations are coupled to the solid-phase equations. The solid-phase equations are discre-
tised by the ﬁnite volume technique and solved numerically using a power law scheme.
The resulting algebraic equations are solved by a combination of the Gauss-Seidel and the
tridiagonal matrix algorithm methods. The steady ﬂame spread rate is the eigenvalue of the
whole system. It is determined iteratively using a bisection method to force the pyrolysis
front (95% of the fresh fuel density) to occur at X = 0. Here, the combustion and ﬂow
equations are coupled with the radiation equations. Therefore, they are solved iteratively.
The radiation routine is invoked every 20 gas–solid iterations. Convergence to a steady-
state solution is determined by a ﬁxed ﬂame spread rate and the satisfaction of continuity
to the order of less than 10−3. The computation typically requires 7500–10,000 iterations
for complete convergence and hence 36–48 h. A grid-independent study is conducted for
a case of ﬂame spread over a PMMA cylindrical rod of 1 mm diameter at 21% oxygen
concentration in a normal gravity condition. The ﬂame spread rates for different sizes of
grids in axial (X-direction) and radial direction (R-direction) are presented in Table 1. The
grid size of 286 × 182 grids in axial and radial directions is chosen for the simulation.
There can be many other sets of pre-exponential factors which result in the same spread
rates, but trends and extinction limits are not predicted properly. In these simulations, some
of the material properties are measured, some are obtained from the literature and pre-
exponential factors for solid and gas phases are calibrated to match the spread rates, trend
and extinction obtained in experiments in normal gravity conditions. The properties used
are listed in Table 2.
Results and discussion
Numerical simulations are carried out for a PMMA fuel diameter of 1 mm in normal grav-
ity and microgravity environments, where the external opposed ﬂow speed is from 0 to
25 cm/s and the oxygen level is from 18% to 35%. The experiments were carried out in a
2.5 s drop tower [31] and reported in [24] are compared to the present simulations in the
aspects of ﬂame shape and ﬂame spread rate. In the following section, the details of ﬂame
