the gas phase are discussed for a base case of 21% oxygen and opposed ﬂow of 5 cm/s.
Subsequently, the heat feedback from the ﬂame to the fuel upstream in the preheat region is
examined for normal gravity and microgravity ﬂames. Heat transport inside the solid fuel
is also discussed. Finally, the effect of applied external ﬂow and surrounding oxygen con-
centrations on the ﬂame spread rate and heat transport is discussed to bring out the key heat
transport mechanism, dictating the ﬂame spread rate in normal gravity and microgravity
environments.
Comparison of experimental and simulated ﬂame shapes and spread rates
Figure 3 compares the images of normal gravity and microgravity ﬂames from experi-
ments with those obtained from simulations. Figure 3(a) shows the photographs of ﬂames
spreading over 1 mm PMMA fuel rods in normal gravity (left) and in microgravity (right)
conditions at 21% oxygen concentration and 5 cm/s of the imposed external ﬂow speed.
The photograph of the spreading ﬂame in microgravity is obtained at the end of the micro-
gravity test duration. The ﬂames have a blue leading edge, followed by an orange-yellow
colour tip. In microgravity, ﬂame appears orange and has a mushroom-shaped. The spread-
ing ﬂame in normal gravity is around 7 mm in height and 5 mm in width compared to the
ﬂame spreading in microgravity around 5 mm in height and 9 mm in width. This is due to
faster transport processes in a buoyancy-driven normal gravity environment. While in nor-
mal gravity, the ﬂame appears laminar, in microgravity, random ﬂuctuations are observed
near the downstream of the leading edge of the ﬂame. These ﬂuctuations are due to the fuel
vapour jet coming out from the bursting of bubbles formed inside the solid fuel.
Figure 3(b) shows the simulated ﬂame images in normal gravity and microgravity under
identical conditions of the base case of the experiments. The visible ﬂames in experiments
are qualitatively represented by contours of fuel mass fraction (left half) and temperature
(right half). As seen in the experiments, the simulation captures the essential features of
ﬂames observed in the experiments. The ﬂames in the simulations are for steady spread"
