rates. However, in the microgravity experiments, the fuel is ignited at the top station
using a Nichrome coil in normal gravity and then dropped once the steady ﬂame spread
is achieved. In 2.5 s of microgravity, the leading edge of the ﬂame reaches a steady state,
but the ﬂame height and width continue to grow till the end of the drop test. While the
ﬂame shape in normal gravity is like that in the experiments, the microgravity ﬂame
shape in simulations is a larger and rounder version of the normal gravity ﬂame, unlike
the mushroom-shaped appearance in the experiments. Microgravity ﬂame images taken
with increased aperture setting in the camera to capture the brighter ﬂame images (see
Figure 3(c)) clearly show a blue colour leading edge of the ﬂame with brightness saturated
in the downstream portion of the ﬂame, which is distorted by the fuel vapour jets from
bursting bubbles. Further, microgravity experiments at lower oxygen level of 17.8% at no
ﬂow condition (see Figure 3(d)) where the frequency of bursts is reduced, in the duration
between two bubble burst events, a clear, blue-coloured round and large ﬂame is observed
similar to that in simulations and to ones reported in the literature [12,13] for thicker fuels.
The distortion of the ﬂame shape by issuing fuel vapour jets during bursting can also be
seen in the lower half of Figure 3(d).
The differences in ﬂame shapes observed and expected in normal gravity and micrograv-
ity are illustrated by a sketch in Figure 3(e). It is interesting to note that the bursting bubbles
occur only over a small segment of the pyrolysis region downstream of the ﬂame leading
edge, where the fuel is semi-hard/soft (semi-hard zone). Upstream of this segment, PMMA
is so hard that the fuel vapours remain trapped inside (hard zone). Further downstream of
the semi-hard zone, where PMMA is heated to a temperature high enough to be in a molten
state and the vapour bubbles formed here have lower inside pressure and therefore burst
with much lower intensities (soft zone) to distort the ﬂame (see Figure 3(e,f)).
The process of periodic fuel vapour ejection by bursting bubbles in the solid fuel has not
been modelled in the present simulations, and hence, the discrepancy between the observed
and predicted ﬂame shape. However, it is important to note that since the ﬂame leading"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2025.2539131,doi,Flame spread over thin circular PMMA rods: numerical modelling,15,14,"628
B.V. Manu et al.
edge shape is not affected (although sometimes not seen in the images due to camera
setting issues) in the experiments. Hence, the ﬂame spread prediction by the numerical
model and the associated heat and mass transport processes are not expected to be affected
by ﬂame distortion.
In experiments, the ﬂame spread rates are obtained by tracking the leading edge of the
ﬂame by image processing. The spread rate in normal gravity, at 21% oxygen concentration
and 5 cm/s opposed ﬂow, is around 0.6 mm/s, and it is 1.35 mm/s at microgravity for the
same oxygen concentration and opposed ﬂow speed. The ﬂame spread rates in simulations
are the Eigenvalue of the problem, which is obtained by enforcing X = 0 at 95% virgin fuel
density. The simulated ﬂame spread rates agree well with the experimental ﬂame spread
rates. For the conditions identical to experiments, the simulated ﬂame spread rate in normal
gravity is 0.55 mm/s and that in microgravity is 1.34 mm/s.
The ﬂame spread rate is higher in microgravity than the ﬂame spread rate in normal
gravity. The ﬂame spread rate depends on the preheating of the fresh fuel in the preheat
region ahead of the ﬂame. Figure 2 shows an enlarged preheat region ahead of the ﬂame
enclosed in a control volume. Heat feedback from the ﬂame to this region by conduction
(¯qcond,g) and radiation (¯qrad,g) along with surface heat loss by re-radiation (¯qrad,s) to the
environment, is also shown in the ﬁgure. The heat is also transported to this control volume
from the ﬂame via solid phase conduction (¯qcond,s). In a ﬂame-ﬁxed coordinate system, the
fuel moves towards the ﬂame at the speed of the ﬂame spread rate (Vf). This fuel movement
introduces a convective heat input (¯qconv,in) and output (¯qconv,out) to the control volume.
The gas phase conduction, radiation and surface re-radiation heating rates are obtained by
integrating the surface heat ﬂuxes over the preheat region as follows.
