Surface heat ﬂux distribution
The difference in the ﬂow dynamics in the normal gravity and microgravity conditions
(explained in a later section) affects the transport of the species, hence the ﬂame shape.
The ﬂame shape and position affect the heat transfer to the preheat region, which affects
the ﬂame spread rate. Figure 4(a,b) shows the distribution of heat ﬂuxes at the fuel surface
due to different modes of heat transfer for a ﬂame spreading over 1 mm diametre fuel rod
in normal gravity and microgravity conditions for a speciﬁc case (base case) at 21% of
oxygen level and an external ﬂow speed of 5 cm/s. Here, the heat ﬂuxes on the surface of
the fuel from gas comprise conduction/convection heat ﬂux, net radiation heat ﬂux and net
heat ﬂux. The net radiation heat ﬂux (¯q′′
rad,net = ¯q′′
rad,g + ¯q′′
rad,s) is the sum of radiation heat
ﬂux from the gas phase to the surface and the re-radiation heat ﬂux from the solid surface.
The net heat ﬂux (¯q′′
cond,g + ¯q′′
rad,net) is the summation of conduction/convection heat ﬂux
and net radiation heat ﬂux.
The heat ﬂux distribution over the surface of the fuels shows that the conduction heat
ﬂux is the dominant mode of heat input to the surface for the ﬂame spread in normal grav-
ity and microgravity. For both cases of ﬂame spread, the conduction heat ﬂux peaks around
the ﬂame leading edge. The magnitude of the peak conduction heat ﬂux for the spreading
ﬂame in normal gravity is around 15 W/cm2, while in the case of the ﬂame in microgravity,
it is lower at about 12 W/cm2. According to [23], the heating rate due to gas phase conduc-
tion for the cylindrical fuels is higher than ﬂat fuels due to the curvature effect deﬁned by a
coefﬁcient CR > 1. In the upstream region of the ﬂame, the conduction heat ﬂux extends
over a longer span (1 cm) in microgravity conditions compared to its counterpart ﬂame in
normal gravity (0.3 cm). This is because, in normal gravity, the buoyancy-induced convec-
tive ﬂow is in a direction opposite to the ﬂame spread and hence opposes the conduction
heat transfer from the ﬂame to the upstream region. In microgravity, the buoyancy-induced
convection is absent, hence the heat conduction extends over a longer distance ahead of
the ﬂame. The net radiation heat ﬂux distribution is negative all over the surface of the
fuel, including the preheat region. This indicates that, unlike planar fuels [32] where ﬂame
radiation is signiﬁcant, in thin cylindrical fuel, the solid re-radiation loss is more prominent
than the radiation feedback from the ﬂame to the surface. The peak in the net radiation heat
