ﬂux is higher for the ﬂame in normal gravity ( −9 W/cm2) than the ﬂame in microgravity
( −6 W/cm2). This is due to the lower surface temperature of the solid fuel in microgravity
(discussed in the next sub-section). From the surface heat ﬂux distribution, it appears that
the dominant mode of heat input to the preheat region is the conduction from the ﬂame.
Note that the conduction through the solid fuel can also happen and will be discussed in
the following section.
Temperature and heat ﬂux distribution in the solid phase
Figure 5(a,b) presents the temperature and conduction heat ﬂux distribution inside the solid
fuel for a spreading ﬂame over a 1 mm diameter PMMA rod at an oxygen concentration of
21% and external ﬂow speed of 5 cm/s in normal gravity and microgravity environments,
respectively. The top sub-ﬁgure shows the temperature distribution along the axis at radial
positions of 0.05 cm (surface) and 0.01 cm from the centre of the fuel. Also, shown in
the inset is the variation of solid temperature along the radius at a ﬁxed axial location of
X = 0.03 cm (starting of the preheat region). The middle sub-ﬁgure shows the temperature
contours inside the solid fuel and the fuel proﬁle (dark shade region) represented by a
contour of 99% of the virgin fuel density. The bottom sub-ﬁgure shows the variation of
radial conduction heat ﬂux inside the solid fuel along the axis at the ﬁxed radial distances
of 0.05 cm (surface) and 0.01 cm from the centre of the fuel. The inset in the sub-ﬁgure
shows the variation of axial conduction heat ﬂux along the radius at a ﬁxed axial location
of X = 0.125 cm.
As already mentioned, solid fuel thermal degradation is modelled by a ﬁrst-order pyrol-
ysis law without regression (change of shape of the solid fuel) [33]. Therefore, as the solid
fuel pyrolyses, the solid fuel density decreases to a low value (due to ﬁrst-order pyrolysis
law), but the fuel shape remains the same without forming a cone in the downstream, as
