is observed in the experiments. Due to low-density fuel in the burnout region downstream
of the pyrolysis region, the temperature increases much above the pyrolysis temperature.
However, this does not affect the ﬂame spread rate and the heat transfer dynamics at the
upstream region of the ﬂame leading edge. Hence, the present model can be used to explain
the temperature distribution and heat transfer phenomenon in the upstream and around the
leading edge of the ﬂame, which governs the ﬂame spread rates.
From the temperature contours (top sub-ﬁgures), it is noted in normal gravity and micro-
gravity ﬂames, the highest temperature gradient occurs in the region around the origin, at
the ﬂame leading edge. In the upstream preheat region, the temperature decreases in the
axial direction. In microgravity, this axial temperature gradient is lower than in normal
gravity owing to lower feedback heat ﬂux from the ﬂame.
The surface temperature upstream of X = 0 cm reduces to the ambient temperature of
300 K at about X = 0.2 cm in normal gravity and at about X = 0.7 cm in microgravity. Far
away from X = 0 (both upstream and downstream), the temperature near the axis of fuel
is the same as the surface temperature, but in the vicinity of X = 0, there is a signiﬁcant
difference between temperatures at the fuel surface and axis. The maximum temperature
difference occurs in the preheat region. The maximum difference between the temperatures
at the surface and axis is around 130 K in normal gravity and micro-gravity environments.
However, upstream locations of their occurrence are different, X = 0.03 cm in normal
gravity and X = 0.25 cm in microgravity. This indicates that fuel is not really a thin fuel,
although classiﬁed as thin based thermally thin limit (diameter less than 2.5 mm) [23].
Here, the temperature in the solid phase near the pyrolysis front reaches approximately
900–1000 K, which is substantially higher than the typical PMMA pyrolysis temperatures
(600–700 K) observed in typical TGA data. This is probably due to higher heating rates
encountered in ﬂame spreading over thin cylindrical fuels. The higher heating rates are due
to the higher curvature effect in thin cylindrical fuels. It may be noted that at higher heating
rates, the temperature range, over which the major mass loss rate occurs, can shift to a
higher temperature range [34]. This also affects solid phase pyrolysis kinetic parameters.
The pre-exponential factors for solid and gas phases are calibrated to match the spread rates
trend and ﬂame spread rate values and minimum oxygen concentration for ﬂame extinction
obtained in experiments in normal gravity conditions. Since the ﬂame spread rate is directly
proportional to integrated heat ﬂux over the preheat region, relevant discussion on the heat
transport mechanisms can be carried out.
From the sub-ﬁgures in the middle, one can note that the temperature contours are clus-
tered more at the surface than at the axis, which also shows that there is a higher axial
temperature gradient at the surface compared to that at the axis of the solid fuel. The
curved temperature contours across the fuel also indicate differences in surface and axial
temperatures along the length of the fuel in the vicinity of the ﬂame leading edge.
Radial heat ﬂux from the ﬂame to the solid surface is shown in the lower sub-ﬁgure
of Figure 5(a,b). The peak in value of surface radial heat ﬂuxes (¯q′′
r ) is around 13 W/cm2
in normal gravity and around 10 W/cm2 in microgravity condition. Also shown in the
sub-ﬁgures are the radial heat ﬂuxes near the axis (0.01 cm from the axis) of the fuel. It
is interesting to note that the radial heat ﬂuxes are greatly reduced, with a peak value of
about 3 W/cm2 in normal gravity and 1 W/cm2 in microgravity. For a given heating rate,
the radial heat ﬂuxes are expected to increase closer to the axis due to reduced surface area.
However, here, the radial conduction heat ﬂuxes are smaller near the axis as the incoming
heat is used for sensible heating of the solid fuel in the preheat region. The peak of radial
heat ﬂux falls over the preheat region (see the density contour in the middle-sub-ﬁgure)."
