x) is around 5 W/cm2 to 8 W/cm2 in normal gravity and it is around
1 W/cm2–2 W/cm2 in micro-gravity condition. It is interesting to note that the magnitude
of axial conduction ﬂux is comparable to radial conduction heat ﬂux in normal gravity;
however, it is about an order lower in micro-gravity. The relative roles of axial and radial
conduction heat transfer on ﬂame spread rate also depend on the surface area on which
these heat ﬂuxes are acting.
The contribution of various heat transport mechanisms to the preheat region (see
Figure 2(a)), namely, ¯qcond,g, ¯qcond,s, ¯qrad,g and ¯qrad,s are obtained using Equation (22). For
the base case of ﬂame spread over a 1 mm PMMA fuel rod at 21% oxygen and 5 cm/s of
external ﬂow speed, the radial conductive heating by the ﬂame is ¯qcond,g = 0.39 W in nor-
mal gravity and about three times, ¯qcond,g = 1.14 W in microgravity condition. The axial
heating rate due to conduction through the solid fuel is ¯qcond,s = 0.06 W in normal gravity
and 0.01 W in microgravity.
In normal gravity, while the axial conduction heat ﬂux and radial heat ﬂux in the preheat
region are of the same order, the integrated heat input from the axial conduction (0.06 W)
accounts for only 13% of the total conduction heating (0.45 W). This is due to the small
cross-sectional area of the fuel (¯a2 = 0.78 mm2) compared to the signiﬁcantly larger
surface area of the solid fuel in the preheat region (2¯alph = 6.3 mm2). Here lph is the
preheat length. In microgravity, the contribution of axial conduction is still lower (0.01 W)
and accounts for less than 1% of the total conduction heat input (1.15 W). This is due to
low axial heat ﬂux in addition to the area effect.
The radiation heat input (¯qrad,g) to the preheat region is insigniﬁcant in normal grav-
ity (0.002 W) and microgravity (0.009 W). The radiation contribution is relatively higher
in microgravity due to a larger ﬂame size. In both normal gravity and microgravity envi-
ronments, the conduction mode of heat transfer is the major contributor to heat input in
the preheat region. Hence, the total heat input values in the preheat region (¯qin) are close
to ¯qcond,g values, which are 0.45 W in normal gravity and 1.16 W in microgravity. Even
though ﬂame temperature is lower in microgravity, the amount of heat transferred to the
preheat region is higher on account of longer preheat length (0.3 cm in normal gravity, vs
1 cm in microgravity) and hence higher ﬂame spread rate in microgravity environment.
While ﬂame radiation is insigniﬁcant, heat loss due to solid surface re-radiation is the
main heat loss mechanism from the solid fuel; the radiation loss is about −0.16 W in
normal gravity and higher at −0.26 W in microgravity. This is due to the longer preheat
length in microgravity.
Effect of ﬂow speed on ﬂame spread rate
Figure 6 shows the variation of the ﬂame spread rates with ﬂow speeds for 1 mm diameter
PMMA cylindrical fuel at 21% and 23% oxygen levels in normal gravity and micrograv-
ity conditions. Figure 6(a) shows the variation of computed ﬂame spread rates with ﬂow
speeds in normal gravity compared to experimental ﬂame spread rates reported in [24].
The simulated ﬂame spread rates agree well with the experimental ﬂame spread rates. At
23% oxygen concentration, the ﬂame spread rates are slightly higher than the ﬂame spread
rate values for 21% oxygen and decrease with an increase in ﬂow speed.
Figure 6(b) shows the variation of computed ﬂame spread rates with ﬂow speeds in
microgravity compared to experimental ﬂame spread rates reported in [24]. Here, the"
