temperature contours for the solid phase are plotted for different ﬂow speeds (see Figure 7).
In normal gravity, the ﬂame is bigger at no ﬂow condition, and its height and width
shrink as the ﬂow increases. This trend agrees well with the experimental observation of
a decreased ﬂame height and width with increased ﬂow speed. The maximum gas phase
temperature noted is around 6 (1800K) for all the ﬂow speeds; hence, a notable decrease in
ﬂame temperature is not observed within the ﬂow speed of 25 cm/s. No ﬂame is observed
in a no-ﬂow condition in microgravity. A self-sustained ﬂame is observed at 2 cm/s and
above the external ﬂow speed. The ﬂame appears to be the same size at low ﬂow speed
(up to 5 cm/s). As the ﬂow speed increases, the ﬂame size shrinks. From the gas phase
temperature contours, it is noted that at a low ﬂow speed of 2 cm/s, the maximum ﬂame
temperature observed is around 5 (1500 K), and as the ﬂow speed increases (5 cm/s and
above), the maximum ﬂame temperature is around 6 (1800K). Also, at a higher ﬂow speed
of 25 cm/s, the ﬂame characteristics appear similar to the normal gravity ﬂame. How-
ever, for all the ﬂow speeds, the microgravity ﬂames appear bigger than the corresponding
ﬂames in normal gravity. In normal gravity and microgravity environments, the solid phase"
