2. In normal gravity conditions, the buoyancy effect gener-
ates a convective ﬂow; hence, the ﬂow will get accelerated in the downstream of the ﬂame.
This phenomenon is represented by plotting the velocity proﬁle at the axial distance of
X: −1.5 cm. Here, at low ﬂow conditions (around 0–5 cm/s), the velocity vectors around
the ﬂames are bent inwards, while at higher ﬂow speeds (> 5 cm/s), velocity vectors run
almost in the axial direction. It represents that at low ﬂow conditions, the hot products
in the ﬂame move upwards against gravity; hence, the cold gas moves inwards, which is
the least resistance path. As the ﬂow increases, the hot products move upwards, and the
applied convective ﬂow occupies the space. In microgravity conditions, no convective ﬂow
is generated, and a minimal increase in velocity is observed downstream. This increase in
velocity may be due to the thermal expansion of hot products.
As the ﬂow ﬁeld around the ﬂame changes, the ﬂame shape, heat transport and mass
transport change in a coupled manner; therefore, it is very interesting to know how the"
