Table 3 presents various external ﬂow speeds, ﬂame spread rates and integrated heat
ﬂuxes over the preheat region (¯qcond,g, ¯qcond,s, ¯qrad,g, and¯qrad,s), total heat input (¯qin =
¯qcond,g + ¯qcond,s + ¯qrad,g), power of the ﬂame (¯P), which is the mass burning rate times
the heat of combustion of fuel and ¯qin as a percentage of ¯P, which is responsible for
self-sustained ﬂame spread. The data are presented for normal gravity and microgravity
conditions. The oxygen level is 21%, and the external ﬂow speed ranges from 0 cm/s to
25 cm/s.
In normal gravity and microgravity environments, for ﬂame spread over cylindrical
PMMA rods of 1 mm diameter, the conduction heat transfer from the ﬂame to the fresh
solid ahead of the ﬂame contributes signiﬁcantly more than other heat transfer modes.
Conduction heat from ﬂame accounts for 82–87% of the total heating rate in normal grav-
ity, with a higher percentage at no external ﬂow condition. The corresponding numbers in
microgravity are 98 –90%. The decrease in conduction heating rate from ﬂame at higher
external ﬂow speeds to ﬂames at lower ﬂow speeds is compensated by the increase in
axial conduction heating rate from the pyrolysis region to the preheat region internally
through the solid fuel. In normal gravity, it accounts for 12–17% of the total heating rate;
microgravity accounts for 1–8% of the total heating rate. The contribution of gas phase
radiation is negligible in normal gravity and microgravity conditions, accounting for less
than 1% of the total heat input rate. In general, as the external ﬂow speed increases, the
axial conduction along the solid fuel increases and the gas phase conduction heat feedback
decreases.
It is very interesting to know the variation of the power of the spreading ﬂame in normal
gravity and microgravity conditions. In normal gravity, the ﬂame power decreases (from
14 to 12 W) with an increased ﬂow speed. Meanwhile, in microgravity, the ﬂame power
has an increase and decrease with increasing ﬂow speed. The power of the ﬂame is around
5 W at a ﬂow speed of 2 cm/s. It peaks at around 24 W at a ﬂow speed of 10 cm/s and
decreases to 18 W for a ﬂow speed of 25 cm/s.
For ﬂow speeds of 5 cm/s and higher, the power generated by the ﬂame in microgravity
conditions is higher than in normal gravity conditions. Interestingly, at a low ﬂow speed of
around ≈0–2 cm/s, the power generated by the ﬂame in normal gravity (14 W) is higher
than that generated by the ﬂame in microgravity (5 W). Even though the power generated is
less and the ﬂame temperature is less, the ﬂame spreads faster in microgravity conditions
( ≈1 mm/s) compared to normal gravity conditions (≈0.6 mm/s). This is because, in
normal gravity, only around 3% (0.4 W) of the total power produced (14 W) contributes
to ﬂame spread at all ﬂow speeds. While in microgravity, at a low ﬂow speed of 2 cm/s,
around 17% (0.9 W) of the total power produced (5 W) is transferred to the preheat region.
In normal gravity and microgravity, surface re-radiation accounts for loss. In normal
gravity, the surface re-radiation rate is around 0.16 W for all the ﬂow speeds considered.
While in microgravity, the surface re-radiation loss is around 0.3 W at 2 cm/s ﬂow speed
and decreases with increasing ﬂow speed, to around 0.16 W at 25 cm/s. This higher loss at
low ﬂow speed is due to a longer preheating length at low speed microgravity conditions.
