The interesting things to be noted here are the extinction limits in normal gravity and
microgravity conditions for no-ﬂow and an externally applied ﬂow of 5 cm/s conditions. In
experiments, the ﬂame spreads around 2.3 mm/s at 35% oxygen concentration in normal
gravity and no-ﬂow conditions. The ﬂame spread rate reduces with the reduction of the
oxygen concentration and extinguishes at an oxygen level of 18%. The numerical model
also predicts spread rates and trends well with experiments, and here, the ﬂame extin-
guishes at an oxygen level of 17.5%. As the ﬂow speed increases to 5 cm/s, the spread
rates and trend in experiments and simulations are the same as in the case of no-ﬂow con-
ditions. While in microgravity, in a no-ﬂow condition, the simulation shows that the ﬂame
does not spread at low oxygen levels. A spreading ﬂame is observed only above 31.5%
of the oxygen level. However, as the ﬂow speed increases to 5 cm/s, the ﬂame spreads at
lower oxygen levels. Interestingly, even below the oxygen concentrations, where the ﬂame
in normal gravity does not spread. That is, at a 5 cm/s ﬂow condition, the extinction limit is
around 17.5% in normal gravity and around 17% in microgravity. A similar phenomenon
of no ﬂame spread at no ﬂow microgravity conditions and more ﬂammable, when a small
external ﬂow is applied, is observed for thick PMMA rods [12,13].
