the atmospheric oxygen level (> 21%), the conduction heat transfer from the ﬂame to
the fresh solid ahead of the ﬂame contributes signiﬁcantly more than other heat transfer
modes. Conduction from the ﬂame accounts for 87%–93% of the total heating rate, with a
higher percentage at high oxygen conditions. The decrease in conduction heating rate from
ﬂames at high oxygen concentration to ﬂames at low oxygen concentration is compensated
by the increase in axial conduction heating rate from the pyrolysis region to the preheat
region internally through the solid fuel. It accounts for 7–12% of the total heating rate. The
contribution of gas phase radiation is negligible, accounting for less than 1% of the total
heat input rate.
In a microgravity environment, for all the oxygen concentrations considered (17.5–
35%), the conduction heat transfer from the ﬂame to the fresh solid ahead of the ﬂame
contributes signiﬁcantly more than other heat transfer modes. Heat conduction from ﬂame
accounts for 96–99% of the total heating rate, with a higher percentage at high oxygen
conditions. The decrease in conduction heating rate from ﬂames at high oxygen concen-
tration to ﬂames at low oxygen concentration is compensated by an increase in the axial
conduction heating rate from the pyrolysis region to the preheat region internally through
the solid fuel. It accounts for 1% to 4% of the total heating rate. The contribution of gas
phase radiation is negligible, accounting for less than 1% of the total heat input rate.
For the oxygen concentrations below the atmospheric oxygen level (21–18%), even
though the conduction heat transfer from the ﬂame to the fresh solid ahead of the ﬂame is
the primary mode of heat transfer (≈80%), the axial conduction heating also contributes
signiﬁcantly (≈20%) to the total heating rate. The contribution of gas phase radiation is
negligible, accounting for less than 1% of the total heat input rate.
This signiﬁcant contribution of axial conduction heating rate, only in the low oxygen
extinction limit in normal gravity conditions, not in microgravity conditions, is due to the
ﬂame, which forms very close to the solid fuel surface in normal gravity. Figure 8(b) shows
the ﬂames represented by the area enclosed by the reaction rate contour (10−4 g/cm3 s) and
temperature contour (1 unit: 300 K) for a ﬂame spreading over PMMA rods in normal
gravity (XO2 : 18%) and microgravity (XO2 : 17.5%) near extinction oxygen concentra-
tions. Here, we can see that, even though the gas phase temperatures are the same, the
ﬂame in normal gravity is very close to the solid fuel surface. Meanwhile, in microgravity,
the ﬂame is located considerably farther away from the solid fuel surface.
Conclusions
The present work analyses the ﬂame spread process over thin PMMA circular cylinders
of 1 mm diameter in normal gravity and microgravity environments. The ﬂame spread
phenomena are numerically simulated using an in-house 2D-axisymmetric computational
ﬂuid dynamics model and compared with reported experimental results. The CFD model
solves for reacting ﬂow in the gas phase and heat transport and pyrolysis processes in the
solid phase in a coupled manner. Simulations are carried out for several ﬂow speeds and
oxygen concentrations in normal gravity and microgravity environments. The simulated
results agree well with the experimental results. From the present study, the following
conclusions may be drawn about ﬂame spread along thin cylindrical fuels.
(1) The current numerical model predicts a slender ﬂame in normal gravity that resembles
a teardrop-shaped or a candle ﬂame. In microgravity, the predicted ﬂame is larger and
near spherical. While the experiments show a dull, large spherical ﬂame, its shape is"
doi:10.1080/13647830.2025.2539131,doi,Flame spread over thin circular PMMA rods: numerical modelling,27,26,"640
B.V. Manu et al.
distorted downstream of the ﬂame’s leading edge. A bright mushroom-shaped region
is formed due to sporadic fuel jets released from bursting bubbles in the solid fuel. The
formation of bubbles in the solid is not modelled in the present work, and hence no
distortion is observed in the predicted ﬂame shape.
(2) In normal gravity, the ﬂame spread rate decreases with an increase in external opposed
ﬂow speed. While in microgravity, an increasing and decreasing, non-monotonic trend
of ﬂame spread rate with an increase in the ﬂow speed is observed.
(3) In normal gravity and microgravity, the ﬂame spread rates increase almost linearly with
an increase in the oxygen concentration. This is consistent with previously reported
trends for thin fuels.
(4) The ﬂames over thin cylindrical fuel in microgravity spread faster than the nor-
mal gravity ﬂames under identical conditions due to the longer preheat length in
microgravity and therefore higher heat input.
(5) In general, the power generated in faster spreading ﬂames in microgravity is higher
than corresponding slower normal gravity ﬂames. However, at low ﬂow speeds (here
< 2 cm/s at near ambient oxygen), the trend is reversed. The microgravity ﬂames
can have much lower power than normal gravity ﬂames and yet spread faster. This is
because of an increased percentage of heat feedback from the ﬂame in microgravity
on account of a larger preheat length.
(6) In normal gravity and microgravity, the contribution of radiation heat feedback is neg-
ligible. Conduction accounts for almost all preheating of the virgin fuel. Radiation
primarily acts as a heat loss mechanism from the ﬂame and the fuel surface. Radiation
feedback is important for ﬂame spread over planar fuels in microgravity and possibly
for thicker cylindrical fuels.
(7) The radial conduction or the heat input from the ﬂame to the preheat region by conduc-
tion is the dominant mode of heat transfer in microgravity ﬂames, accounting for more
than 95% of the total heat input received in the preheat region. The balance is by axial
conduction through the solid fuel. In normal gravity, the contribution of axial conduc-
tion is higher (about 10%) than in microgravity. This contribution increases further at
low oxygen levels near the extinction limit to about 20% of the total heat input.
