doi:10.1088/1364-7830/7/4/005,doi,A three-dimensional model of steady flame spread over a thin solid in low-speed concurrent flows,5,4,"680
H-Y Shih and J S T’ien
1. Introduction
Flame spread over solid fuels is a classic combustion phenomenon involving the interaction of
ﬂuid dynamics, heat transfer and chemical reactions for a complex two-phase, non-premixed
ﬂame. In the study of ﬂame spreading mechanisms [1–6], distinctions have been made by
considering the direction of ﬂow of the oxidizer relative to the direction of the ﬂame spread.
In concurrent-ﬂow ﬂame spread, the ﬂow convection and ﬂame spread are in the same direction
and the ﬂame is driven ahead of the pyrolysis front by the ﬂow. The fuel surface is exposed
to the hot combustion products, often leading to a longer ﬂame, faster propagation and a
more hazardous situation compared to opposed-ﬂow ﬂame spread.
While this traditional
view holds in high-speed ﬂows in normal gravity, there are exceptions in low-speed ﬂow in
microgravity [7]. In this paper, a detailed computational study on concurrent-ﬂow ﬂame spread
in microgravity has been carried out.
Earlier, theoretical analyses of concurrent-ﬂame spread include a purely solid heat-up
model with prescribed gas-phase heat-ﬂux distribution [8], boundary layer combustion model
with fast kinetics [9, 10] and numerical elliptic thermal-diffusive model using a prescribed
ﬂow ﬁeld and constant density [11].
Recent modelling efforts for ﬂame spread over
solids in concurrent ﬂows seek to improve the accuracy of the velocity description and to
include radiative heat transfer, which has been proved to be important in low-speed ﬂows in
microgravity [12–17]. The low-speed ﬂow effects and radiative extinction in concurrent ﬂame
spread have been examined by these models, which solve elliptic Navier–Stokes equations
with solid radiative loss or gas-phase radiation in zero gravity [14, 15] and in reduced gravity
environments [16].
Experiments to study ﬂame spreading in forced ﬂow in spacecraft require a ﬂow tunnel.
Because of restrictions on both the size of the hardware and the amount of fresh gases in
a spacecraft experiment, a ﬂow tunnel with a large width for a potentially two-dimensional
ﬂame is not feasible. Realistically, a ﬂow tunnel of ﬁnite cross-sectional area and limited
length has to be used. As a result, the spreading concurrent ﬂames are expected to have
many three-dimensional features because of the narrowness of the tunnel and the solid
fuel.
Even without the wall interaction, usually, most ﬂame spreads in concurrent ﬂow
can have three-dimensional characteristics, because concurrent ﬂame can have a ratio of
ﬂame length to ﬂame width greater than unity even at modest free-stream velocities. Most
theoretical studies on concurrent ﬂames mentioned earlier have, however, assumed a two-
dimensional conﬁguration. In addition, with the exception of [18], where ﬂame spread in
a two-dimensional ﬂow tunnel is modelled, all the above computations used an unbounded
domain.
The ﬁrst unsteady, three-dimensional ﬂame spread computation appears to be that in [19].
Predictions have been made of concurrent and opposed ﬂame spread in slow forced ﬂow over
the thin solid under a gravitational acceleration normal to the fuel surface, and the ﬂame
spread over a vertical wall in an enclosed chamber under a gravitational acceleration parallel
to the solid fuel. The values for the acceleration due to gravity are 10−2g, 10−3g and 10−4g.
Steady ﬂame spread is not achieved during the 50 s of simulated real time in concurrent ﬂow
cases. Although this is a three-dimensional model, most of the results and discussions focus
on the unsteadiness of the ﬂame and the inﬂuence of gravity on the ﬂame, rather than the
three-dimensional effects and the ﬂame structure. Besides, the computational domain in this
work is taken to be 180 cm × 60 cm × 20 cm with a non-uniform 48 × 16 × 14 grid, which is
rather coarse, and grid-independence has not been demonstrated. In [20], three-dimensional,
time-dependent numerical solutions to the Navier–Stokes equations are used to simulate the
transition process over a thermal-thin solid in the two- and three-dimensional conﬁgurations.
