681
The physical model is similar to that used earlier for the study of axi-symmetric ignition
processes [21].
It was found that the two-dimensional ﬂame quenched more easily than
the three-dimensional ﬂame during the transition period.
Signiﬁcant diffusion of oxygen
from the sides to the centreline plane occurred in the three-dimensional case. For the wind
velocity range considered in this work (∼1 cm s−1), ﬂame spread occurs only in the upwind
direction (opposed-ﬂow) and the focus is on investigation the inﬂuence of ﬂame geometry
on the transition from ignition to ﬂame spread in the near-quenching regime. A subsequent
computation [22] studied the three-dimensional effect of opposed ﬂame spread over solid
samples of ﬁnite width in an unbounded domain. The authors note that because of thermal
expansion, the streamlines approaching the ﬂame are deﬂected sideways so that the effective
oxygen mass ﬂux convected into the reaction zone is less than the upstream speciﬁed value.
This is most pronounced in low-speed ﬂow. It should be noted that the thermal expansion
effect is also present in two-dimensional ﬂames but the streamline deﬂection is limited to the
upward direction away from the solid surface. A second interesting result from this study
is that when the approaching ﬂow velocity is low (∼2 cm s−1), the ﬂame spreads faster for
narrower samples than for wider samples. This can also be attributed to the oxygen side
diffusion effect.
Recently, a three-dimensional model for a steady ﬂame spread over a thin solid in low-
speed concurrent ﬂows has been developed and applied to a three-dimensional ﬂow tunnel
[23]. This model is similar to the earlier two-dimensional models [14–16] in terms of physical
assumptions. In the three-dimensional computation, the interaction of the ﬂame with the tunnel
side walls as a function of upstream oxygen percentage was emphasized. Most recently, a three-
dimensional simulation of opposing ﬂame ignition and spread has also been performed in a
ﬂow tunnel [24]. These studies show profound three-dimensional effects and the importance
of tunnel conﬁnement on the ﬂame behaviour.
One special feature of low-speed ﬂow on concurrent ﬂame spreading that needs to be
mentioned is that concurrent ﬂames can achieve a steady spread rate with a constant limiting
length. This has been demonstrated both theoretically and experimentally [14–16, 23, 25–28].
Honda and Ronney [27] argued that the limiting length was either due to the radiative
loss and/or the side entrainment loss effect, consistent with the modelling assumptions
made in [14–16, 23, 28].
Consequently, after the ﬂame attains this limiting length, it
will not spread until the upstream ﬂame base moves [29, 30].
Thus, the solid burnout
rate at the ﬂame base controls the spread rate for steady concurrent ﬂames.
This is
qualitatively different from the unsteady growing phase of the concurrent ﬂame, or the opposing
spreading ﬂame, where the spread rates are determined primarily by the heat-up rate in the
preheat zone.
One consequence of the above assertion is that we need to model the details of the upstream
ﬂame stabilization zone for steady concurrent ﬂame spread. This can be accomplished by
numerically solving the Navier–Stokes equations with the proper grid structure near the
solid burnout front. We note that the original motivation for applying the Navier–Stokes
equation in this region comes, instead, from the need to study ﬂame stabilization and extinction
[7, 30].
In this paper, purely forced concurrent-ﬂow steady ﬂame spread is computationally
examined in detail for ﬁnite-width samples in three-dimensional ﬂow tunnels. In addition
to providing more details on the model formulation, emphasis is placed on the effect of ﬂow
velocity, samplewidth, wallinteraction, sampleholderpropertiesandtheoxygensidediffusion.
One of the reasons for varying these parameters is to understand the underlying physics of
three-dimensional ﬂame propagation in conﬁnement; another reason is to provide guidance in
designing a space experiment [31].
