2. Theoretical formulation
The geometry of the ﬂow tunnel and the ﬂame conﬁguration are shown schematically in
ﬁgure 1. In this three-dimensional ﬂow tunnel, the X-coordinate, along the solid fuel, is 21 cm
long (6 cm upstream and 15 cm downstream). The Y-coordinate is the height of the tunnel,
which is 10 cm. The surface plane of Y = 0 is the solid fuel, which is placed in the middle
plane (in the Y-direction) of the tunnel. Thus, the half height of the tunnel is 5 cm. The
Z-coordinate is the width of the tunnel, which is 4 cm in this ﬁgure. However, for the purpose
of this scientiﬁc study where three-dimensional effects are sought, the width of the tunnel (or
the width of solid fuel) will be one of the important parameters to vary in the calculations.
The centreline plane (the plane at Z = 2 cm in the ﬁgure) is assumed to be the plane of
symmetry. Therefore, the real computation domain is only one quarter of the size of the full
three-dimensional tunnel. The top wall is at an ambient temperature of 300 K. In most cases
in the parametric study, the temperature of the side walls is also 300 K (cold wall condition),
but the insulated condition also has be used to isolate the wall cooling effect.
The oxidizer ﬂows into the tunnel with uniform velocity U∞, at the entrance. In ﬁgure 1,
the ﬂame is shown stabilized over the fuel, which burns out completely at X = Y = 0 on the
centreline plane. A steady formulation is used in this model, which assumes that the ﬂame can
achieve a constant length. Once the ﬂame reaches a steady state, it is maintained stationary
relative to the tunnel by feeding the solid fuel into the ﬂame at a spread rate, VF . This approach
simulates the central idea of the concept for the proposed space experiment SIBAL [31], where
the steady ﬂame spread rate is sought and the ﬂame is maintained ﬁxed with respect to the
tunnel by feeding fuel into the ﬂame at the necessary rate. The reference velocity, ¯UR, which
is the velocity seen by the ﬂame near the stabilization zone, is obtained simply by ¯UR = U∞.
The thermally thin solid is composed of two kinds of materials, 80% fuel and 20% inert
mass. The fuel is a thin cellulose tissue paper with chemical formula C6H10O5. The inert part
is not combustible and will be left after the fuel is completely consumed. In order to simplify
the problem, the properties of the inert part are assumed to be the same as the fuel. The solid
is assumed to be radiatively opaque with emissivity equal to 0.92.
The essential elements of the combustion model are as follows.
(1) The gas-phase model comprises three-dimensional, laminar, elliptic partial differential
equations for the conservation of mass, momentum, energy and species in the gas.
