2.4. Numerical method
The system of coupled elliptic partial differential equations in the gas phase is solved
numerically by the SIMPLER algorithm [36]. Basically, this is an iterative solution method to
deal with the non-linear nature of equation sets, which are discretized using a ﬁnite-difference
technique. Velocity is stored at staggered grid locations. The resulting set of discretization
equations is solved by sweeping plane-by-plane in each of the three directions. Along each
plane, the line-by-line procedure was used, which is a combination of the Gauss–Seidel and
the tri-diagonal matrix algorithm (TDMA).
The solid-phase equations are solved by the general ﬁnite-difference technique. The
steady ﬂame spread rate VF is found by using the bisection method to force the burnout point
of the centreline to occur at X = Y = 0. The gas and solid-phase equations are solved in turn,
each providing input in the form of boundary conditions for the other.
