H-Y Shih and J S T’ien
Variable grid distribution is used to satisfy different requirements. The minimum cell
size is 0.1 times the thermal length and is concentrated in the stabilization region in order to
capture the dramatic changes in temperature and ﬂow ﬁeld. The cells expand by a factor of
1.1 upstream and downstream. In the Y-direction, because of the presence of the wall, the
cells expand ﬁrst until they reach the half-domain and then contract in the other half-domain
at the same rate. In the Z-direction, uniform, minimum cells are used for the narrow samples
(under 6 cm) because they are equally important in the span-wise direction, and various grid
resolutions were tested to ensure grid independence of the results (e.g. the maximum ﬂame
temperature, ﬂame spread rates). However, for wider samples, variable grid structure similar
to that in the Y-direction was used considering the tradeoff between numerical accuracy and
computational cost.
For the cases of an inﬂow velocity of 5 cm s−1 and 15% oxygen concentration in the 4 cm
wide tunnel, a typical three-dimensional grid contains about 130 000 points (67 × 41 × 47).
Convergence for this case needs about 50 h of CPU time on a DEC personal workstation of
600 MHz. For the cases of higher inﬂow velocity and a wider tunnel, the convergence time is
even larger.
3. Results
Computations on steady ﬂame spread are carried out for different tunnel widths over several
forced ﬂow velocities at one atmosphere pressure. The three-dimensional effects on the ﬂame
shape, ﬂame length, ﬂame spread rate and extinction limits are examined. In most of the
computations, the solid fuel is assumed to ﬂush with the ﬂow tunnel walls, i.e. there are no
inert strips on the side of the fuel. The effect of the inert strip on the combustion behaviour
is examined in a separate section. The results presented in this paper are for one atmospheric
total pressure at 15% oxygen. The effect of varying the oxygen percentage can be found from
[23, 32].
While the computations were carried out in non-dimensional form, some of the results
will be presented in terms of dimensional quantities for the purposes of experimental design
and comparisons in the future. Note that in the governing equations, the space coordinates
(X, Y, Z) were non-dimensionalized by the thermal length, and the thermal length is inversely
proportional to the inﬂow velocity. Over the range of velocities in this study (3–7 cm s−1), the
thermal length varied from 0.304 to 0.71 cm.
