plot are used to show different aspects of the ﬂame and ﬂow features. In the lower half, the
non-dimensional temperature isotherms are superposed on the projected velocity vectors on
that plane. Here, the highest temperature is found to be located off-centre at Z ∼1 cm. Near
the leading edge (ﬂame base region), the temperature contours are generally ﬂat (like a two-
dimensional distribution) except close to the wall. On the other hand, strong three-dimensional
characteristics are observed in the downstream part of the ﬂame, as expected from aspect ratio
considerations. The upper half of the plot contains the streamline projections, oxygen mass
ﬂux vectors and the ﬂame reaction rate contours. Because of thermal expansion, the ﬂow in
the ﬂame base region is deﬂected towards the sides where the temperature is lower. As shown
in the ﬁgure, there is signiﬁcant oxygen diffusion from the quenched layer to the ﬂame. The
side diffusion of oxygen is comparable in magnitude with the convective oxygen ﬂow and it is
almost perpendicular to the convective stream. In two-dimensional ﬂames, oxygen supply in
this third dimension is absent. The side diffusion of oxygen enhances the ﬂame locally, causing
the hook shape of the reaction zone there. It is one of the most important three-dimensional
features in diffusion ﬂames in low-speed ﬂow where oxygen supply is a limiting factor [20].
A number of trend reversal phenomena to be discussed later will be attributed to this side
oxygen diffusion effect.
The two-dimensional solid temperature distribution for the 4 cm wide sample case can be
found in the middle plot in ﬁgure 7. The solid temperatures are similar to the cone-shaped gas-
phase temperature proﬁles in ﬁgure 2 and the maximum solid temperature is near the burnout
front. The solid thickness proﬁle is shown in the middle plot of ﬁgure 8. Note that the solid
contains 80% fuel and 20% inert (non-combustible) material. The ﬁgure shows the percentage
of unburned fuel from 99% to 0% (from the right to the left). The 0% curve indicates the
burnout front where the fuel is completely consumed. After that, the remaining solid consists
only of inert material. Due to ﬂame quenching near the cold tunnel side-walls, some fuel is
left unburned and partially burned on the sides, as shown in the domain x < 0 in ﬁgure 8.
Unburned fuel residue adjacent to a cold plate is an observation familiar to experimenters.
In ﬂame-spreading experiments, the pyrolysis region has often been recognized as the
‘blackened’ part of the solid. This is not a scientiﬁcally precise deﬁnition but is commonly
used. In the computation, the deﬁnition of the pyrolysis region is similarly arbitrary. For
example, we can choose the contour of 99% unburned fuel as the pyrolysis front (in [14],
99.9% unburned fuel is deﬁned as the pyrolysis front) and above that, it is the solid preheat
region. Based on this deﬁnition, the pyrolysis length at the centreline is around 3.6 cm in this
case and is close to the ﬂame length (3.5 cm) (note the ﬂame length is deﬁned as the length
of the reaction rate contour of 10−4 g cm−3 s−1 on the centreline plane, similar to that used in
[14, 15, 18]). Suitable criteria for the pyrolysis and preheat regions need to be examined and
compared in more detail with the experiments.
3.2. Effect of inlet ﬂow velocity and solid sample (tunnel) width
The effects of changing inlet ﬂow velocities (3, 5, 7 cm s−1) and solid fuel sample widths are
examined in this section. For all the cases computed here, the width of the solid sample is also
assumed to be the width of the ﬂow tunnel. In addition, the temperature of the tunnel walls is
ﬁxed at 300 K.
The computed results of the ﬁnite-width samples are compared with the two-
