On the other hand, for all the cases in ﬁgure 10 (3 cm s−1 inﬂow velocity), the ﬂame
spread rate is larger than that of the two-dimensional ﬂame, and the spread rate increases with
decreasing width (down to 2 cm). The ﬂame lengths (except for the 2 cm narrow sample) are
also longer than the two-dimensional case. This indicates that in this near-limit, low-speed
convective environment, oxygen supply is the limiting factor and the effect of oxygen side
diffusion becomes dominant (we also note that ﬂame lengths are considerably shorter than the
5 cm s−1 case and the lateral heat loss effect decreases with ﬂame length). When the solid is
wider, the effect of oxygen side diffusion is less important, resulting in the decrease of ﬂame
spread rate with width. Normally, one would think in concurrent ﬂow that the longer ﬂame
spreads faster, which we already see in the two-dimensional simulation. However, it appears
that this is not the case with the near-limit three-dimensional ﬂames such as what we have here.
For the 2 cm narrow sample, the ﬂame is the shortest but the ﬂame-spread rate is the highest.
The mechanism for this trend reversal is the enhanced oxygen side diffusion. The ﬂame near the
quench limit is short of oxygen supply. By transporting more oxygen to the ﬂame base through
side diffusion, the ﬂame at the base comes closer to the solid surface in order to generate more
fuel vapour to meet the stoichiometric requirement (the closer ﬂame is veriﬁed from computed
ﬂame proﬁles [32]). As mentioned in the introduction, for a steady concurrent ﬂame spread,
the solid burnout controls the spread rate. Thus, for the narrow samples, the enhanced ﬂame
base burning from oxygen side diffusion explains the spread rate reversal phenomenon near
the quenching limit.
3.3. Oxygen side diffusion and Lewis number effect
The opposite trends in ﬁgures 9 and 10 reﬂect the difference between a ﬂame near the low-
speed quenching limit, as represented by the 3 cm s−1 case, and an away-from-limit ﬂame,
Downloaded by [University of California Santa Barbara] at 08:49 13 June 2016"
