represented by the 5 cm s−1 case. Low-speed quenching of solid fuel is due to the increase in
heat loss rate (surface radiation and conduction) relative to the combustion heat generation rate.
The rate of heat generation in a solid-fuel ﬂame is controlled by the oxygen supply rate to the
ﬂame. When the ambient oxygen percentage is ﬁxed, this supply rate decreases with the ﬂow
convective velocity. However, in low-speed ﬂow over a ﬁnite-width sample, oxygen diffusion
from the sides of the ﬂame can be a signiﬁcant mechanism by which oxygen is supplied to
the ﬂames. This can be seen in ﬁgure 2 and has been pointed out previously in [20, 22, 23].
It has been argued that in this oxygen-limited situation, the supplement of oxygen from the
ﬂame side edge pushes the ﬂame base closer to the solid (due to stoichiometric requirement).
Therefore, solid burns out faster. As pointed out in the introduction, in the regime of low-speed
ﬂow, steady concurrent ﬂame spreading velocity is controlled by the solid burnout rate. Hence,
this three-dimensional ﬂame spreads faster than the two-dimensional ﬂame despite having a
shorter ﬂame length.
To further conﬁrm this interpretation, a numerical experiment has been carried out in
this work. The oxygen diffusion coefﬁcient has been varied in the z-direction (perpendicular
to the incoming ﬂow and parallel to the sample surface) to effect a change of the amount
of oxygen side diffusion. Mathematically, this is equivalent to a change of the z-direction
Lewis number in the species equation (the thermal diffusivity is ﬁxed). Figure 11 shows the
results of this experiment. When the z-direction oxygen diffusion coefﬁcient is increased
(ratio of LeO2z /LeO2ORI <1), the ﬂame spread rate is increased. When the z-direction oxygen
diffusion coefﬁcient is decreased (ratio of LeO2z /LeO2ORI >1), the ﬂame spread rate is decreased.
Furthermore, when this ratio is sufﬁciently large, the ﬂame goes out. It should be noted that
for the condition tested (15% O2, 3 cm s−1 and 4 cm width), the ﬂame is quenched in two-
dimensions (corresponding to LeO2z →∞) [32]. So the existence of a ﬂame in this condition
is only possible when there is sufﬁcient oxygen diffusion from the side; a ﬁnite-width sample
