3.4. Effects of tunnel wall thermal condition: a cold wall versus an insulated wall
All the computations performed thus far have employed the cold wall boundary conditions,
that is, the temperature of the side walls is ﬁxed at 300 K. The cold walls conduct heat away
from the ﬂame, which introduces heat loss and produces a quenched layer. But through the
quenched layer, the oxygen ﬁnds its way to the ﬂame by side diffusion.
In this section, an insulated side wall condition is tested, which eliminates the lateral
heat loss (the top wall is still maintained at 300 K. Conductive interaction with the top wall
is minimal [18]). The ﬂame structures at 15% oxygen and 5 cm s−1 inﬂow velocity in a 4 cm
width tunnel are shown in ﬁgure 12, which includes the temperature proﬁles and ﬂow ﬁeld on
the horizontal plane and the solid thickness proﬁles. Comparing, respectively, the three plots
