3.5. Effect of inert strips along the fuel edges
All the computed cases we have discussed so far assume that the width of the tunnel is the
same as the width of the solid fuel. In other words, the solid fuel spans across the ﬂow duct
completely. However, in many solid ﬂame spread experiments, the edges of the fuel may be
taped with some inert strips or metal plates in order to be able to hold the fresh fuel properly.
These strips, which will be on both edges of the solid sample are chemically inert but can
interact with the ﬂame thermally and aerodynamically.
In addition to a better simulation of the experimental set-up, the introduction of inert
strips can also help to answer the scientiﬁc question of the importance of wall heat loss against
the oxygen side diffusion. These two competing effects (heat loss weakens the ﬂames and
oxygen side diffusion strengthens the near-limit ﬂames) are coupled together in the set-up we
have discussed so far since a quenched layer is needed for the side diffusion of oxygen. With
inert strips of sufﬁcient width, oxygen side diffusion can be present with minimal heat loss to
the wall. Thus, the oxygen side diffusion effect can be isolated.
The effect of this inert strip is examined by comparing the ﬂame structures of three different
cases here. The test condition is 15% oxygen with 5 cm s−1 inﬂow velocity using a 4 cm wide
tunnel. The ﬁrst case has fuel that is 2 cm wide in the middle and 1 cm wide strips on both
sides of the solid in a 4 cm wide tunnel. The second case uses fuel that is 3 cm wide in the
middle and has two 0.5 cm wide strips on the sides. For these two cases, the area thermal
inertia of the strips (H = ρτCP , product of area density and speciﬁc heat) are assumed to
be the same as that of the fuel. For the third case, the width of the fuel is the same as that
in the ﬁrst case but the thermal inertia of the strip is 100 times of that of the virgin fuel. In
all the computations, the solid fuel and the strips are assumed to be thermally thin and heat
conduction parallel to the fuel and the strip is negligible.
The temperature proﬁles at two horizontal planes are shown in ﬁgures 14 and 15 for cases 1
and 3. The upper plot in ﬁgure 14 shows that the temperature proﬁle at Y = 0.23 cm is narrower
and shorter for case 3 with inert strips having larger thermal inertia (Hstrip = 100 × Hfuel).
The surface temperature proﬁles in the lower plot in ﬁgure 14 show quite a drastic difference
between the two cases. Basically, with strip thermal inertia the same as that of the fuel, the
strip is heated up under the expanded ﬂame (expanded laterally due to thermal expansion).
With much larger thermal inertia (100 × Hfuel), the strip is not heated up and remains at a
lower temperature. This produces a thermal quenching effect that is manifested in the solid
thickness proﬁles shown in ﬁgure 15. In the upper plot, there is no unburned solid fuel left over
after the passage of the ﬂame, whereas in the lower plot, leftover fuel exists next to the strips.
The thermal quenching by the larger thermal inertia strips also has a noticeable effect on the
pyrolysis front (as indicated by the solid thickness proﬁles). Compared with ﬁgures 8 and 12,
the solid fuel burnout proﬁle shown in ﬁgure 15 is concave towards the downstream while in
ﬁgures 8 and 12 they are towards the upstream. It appears that the burnout shape is determined
