The ﬂame spread rates also change signiﬁcantly with the strips. Shown in ﬁgure 16, with
only the 0.5 cm width strip, the ﬂame spread rate increases a little in comparison with that of
the no strip case. When the width of the strip is 1 cm, the ﬂame spread increases by 25%. The
ﬂame will spread slower if the temperature of the strip is lower (the thicker strip case), but
the rate is still 20% more than that of the case without the inert strip. As mentioned earlier,
the inﬂuence of the inert strip on the ﬂame spread rate is to allow oxygen side diffusion with
minimal wall heat loss (e.g. the 1 cm strip case).
4. Conclusions
A three-dimensional, steady, laminar ﬂame spread model over a thin solid in low-speed
concurrent ﬂows has been formulated and numerically solved. Steady concurrent-ﬂow spread
implies a constant ﬂame length. This is achieved when the ﬂame base moves with the same
speed as that of the ﬂame tip, a condition more commonly observed in low-speed ﬂows. To
model the ﬂame base movement, the detailed ﬂame structure needs to be resolved in that
region. The present model consists of full Navier–Stokes equations for the conservation of
mass, momentum, energy and species in the gas. The species equations are for the fuel vapour,
