H-Y Shih and J S T’ien
oxygen, carbon dioxide and water vapour. A one-step, second-order ﬁnite-rate Arrhenius
reaction between fuel vapour and oxygen is assumed. The solid obeys a zeroth-order pyrolysis
relation and is thermally and aerodynamically thin. Surface radiation loss is included but gas
radiation is neglected. The model has been solved numerically. The computational grids are
chosen such that the ﬂame structure in the ﬂame base region can be resolved.
The computation has been performed by placing the solid fuel within a three-dimensional
ﬂow tunnel. By varying the ﬂow velocity, the tunnel and solid fuel widths, and for wall
conditions with or without inert sample edge plates, the three-dimensional effects on ﬂame
and solid structures and the ﬂame spread and extinction characteristics are computed and
investigated.
The detailed three-dimensional ﬂame characteristics including gas-phase temperature,
fuel reaction rate contours, ﬂow structures and solid-phase proﬁles have been presented, which
demonstrate the capability of the computational model. Several three-dimensional effects due
to the presence of the tunnel walls are observed. First, the wall changes the velocity proﬁles
due to the no-slip boundary condition. Second, the cold side walls conduct heat away from
the ﬂame, which produces heat loss and a quenched layer. Third, the quenched layer near the
walls creates the space for oxygen side diffusion to the ﬂame.
Three-dimensional ﬂow affects the ﬂame and the solid proﬁles both in the downstream
ﬂame tip and the upstream ﬂame base regions.
In the higher ﬂow velocity, away-from-
the-extinction-limit cases, the three-dimensional effect is mostly heat transfer in origin and
manifests itself mostly in the downstream by pointed ﬂame and pyrolysis front shapes. Wider
samples have higher ﬂame spread rates. For the low-speed near-limit ﬂames, oxygen supply
is limiting. Since solid burnout rate at the ﬂame base determines the steady state spread rate,
oxygen side diffusion there becomes a dominant mechanism for boosting the solid burning.
This produces an interesting trend reversal: moderately narrow solid samples can be more
ﬂammable and spread faster than wider samples.
The available mechanisms of wall heat loss, wall quenching and oxygen side diffusion
results in the versatility of the three-dimensional ﬂames.
Further examination of these
competing mechanisms are made by changing the boundary condition from cold side walls
to insulated walls.
With insulated side walls, the lateral heat loss is eliminated but the
edge oxygen diffusion is also forbidden.
Compared with the cold wall cases, the ﬂame
is stronger and the ﬂame spread rate is higher without wall heat loss in the region away
from the limit. On the other hand, although there is no heat loss to the wall, the ﬂames
without the oxygen side diffusion effect become weaker and spread slower in the near-limit
region.
The scenario that minimizes heat loss but provides the space for oxygen side diffusion
is accomplished by using inert strips on each side of the solid fuel. With the inert strips, the
ﬂame spread rate increases greatly due to the decrease of the wall heat loss and the promotion
of the oxygen side diffusion. Another interesting aspect related to the oxygen side diffusion is
the variation of the Lewis number of oxygen in the lateral direction. This can be done only in
the numerical experiment and provides direct proof of the importance of oxygen side diffusion
on the oxygen-limited ﬂames.
The model provides a tool for examining the three-dimensional ﬂame spread and extinction
characteristics. It can be used to plan and guide the space experiment. As an example, the
model has been helpful in the selection of fuel sample width to be used in the proposed ﬂight
experiment. It is also being used to evaluate the sensitivity of the spread rate to combustion
products in the design of the re-circulating ﬂow tunnel and the heat loading to the tunnel
windows [40, 41]. Further improvements in modelling can include gas-phase radiation and
more detailed kinetics, in both the solid and gas phases.
