doi:10.1089/space.2013.0035,doi,Early Fire Detection Technology in Manned Spacecraft,2,1,"With the fast development of the semiconductor and laser tech-
nology, the near-infrared (near-IR) laser diode forward scattering
smoke detectors were used in the International Space Station (ISS).
The detector consists of a two-pass laser diode to sense forward
scattered light (30). There is also a zero-degree obscuration system
that is used as a measure of the beam strength. As shown in Fig. 2, the
near-IR laser beam emerges from the enclosure into the top assembly
(A) and is reflected by two mirrors [one visible at top right (B)] and is
then reflected back to the sensors in the enclosure (C). One sensor
detects the forward scattered light and is referenced by another
sensor that looks directly at the incident beam. The system is designed
to alarm based on the magnitude of the scattered light signal refer-
enced to the beam intensity. Supported by Comparative Soot Diag-
nostics (CSD) experiment data6 indicating that early smoldering fires
produce larger particulate than established flaming fires, the sensi-
tivity is designed to be greatest for particles larger than 1 mm with
sensitivity extending down to 0.6 mm.
Recently, NASA conducted the Smoke Aerosol Measurement Ex-
periment (SAME)3 sought to avoid the problems experienced by the
CSD experiment by obtaining the particulate size statistics on orbit
with a reduced dependence upon sample return to Earth. The mea-
surements were made using smoke generated by overheated space-
craft materials in much the same manner as the CSD experiment, but
the sample temperature, flow field, and particle aging time were more
rigorously controlled. However, the experimental results show that,
for the materials tested, a substantial portion of the smoke particles is
below 500 nm in diameter, which will make detection of this smoke
challenging for light scattering devices.
As described by Friedman,4 there have been six overheat and
failed component failures in the NASA Orbiter fleet in addition to
several similar incidents that have occurred on the ISS. None of these
events spread into a real fire, but as mission durations increase, the
likelihood of failures increases. The experience on Mir in 1997 has
shown that failure of oxygen generation systems can have significant
consequences. Smoke detectors on the ISS have proven to be sensi-
tive to dust and have produced numerous false alarms. For this reason
and other design constraints that will be imposed on future space-
craft, there is a need for new fire detector technology that eliminates
nuisance alarms yet provides early prefire warning.
In this article, a state-of-the-art hybrid fire-detecting instrument is
proposed to increase the reliability of fire detection and reduce the
response time for space missions. It integrates the laser scattering
smoke detection technology with the near-IR laser photoacoustic
(PA) trace gas measurement technology. Since PA sensor measures
the acoustic pressure wave that is directly related to the absorbed
energy by gas molecules and detected by second harmonic tech-
nology, it is immune to the background signal and easily achieves
ppm level of detection limits. The concentration of carbon monoxide
(CO) is very low at normal atmosphere, but dramatically increases in
nearly all types of fire events, so it was selected as the target gas for
the fire signature. The PA spectroscopy principle, the instrument
design, and the experiment results are presented in the following
sections.
PHOTOACOUSTIC SPECTROSCOPY PRINCIPLE
The PA effect in solids7,8 was first discovered by Bell in 1880, and
the same effect exists in liquids and gases as well. The PA effect in
gases can be divided into three main steps:9 (1) localized heat release
in the sample gas because of relaxation of absorbed light energy
