doi:10.1089/space.2013.0035,doi,Early Fire Detection Technology in Manned Spacecraft,3,2,"through molecular collisions; (2) acoustic and thermal wave gener-
ation because of localized transient heating and expansion; and (3)
detection of the acoustic signal in the PA cell with a microphone.
The fundamental theory governing absorption spectroscopy is
embodied by the Beer–Lambert law. Molecular absorption of photons
from the incident light results in the excitation of molecular energy
levels (rotational, vibrational, electronic). The excited state loses its
energy by radiation processes, such as spontaneous or stimulated
emission, and/or by collisional relaxation, in which the energy is
transformed into translational energy. In the case of vibrational
excitation, radiative emission and chemical reactions do not play an
important role, because the radiative lifetimes of vibrational levels
are long compared with the time needed for collisional deactivation
at pressures used in PAs (*1 bar), and the photon energy is too small
to induce chemical reactions.10 Thus, in practice the absorbed energy
is completely released as heat, appearing as translational kinetic
energy of the gas molecules. The deposited heat power density is
proportional to the absorption coefficient and the incident light in-
tensity. A detailed schematic illustrating the series processes occur-
ring during PA signal generation is presented in Figure 3.
PA gas analysis is based on the detection of the acoustic signal. The
source term of the acoustic wave equation is proportional to the
deposited heat power density. The spatial size and shape of the source
volume depend on the light-beam geometry and on the absorption
length in the gas, while the time dependence of heat deposition is
controlled by the time dependence of laser excitation. Thus, a laser
pulse generates a heat pulse, which excites an acoustic pulse, while a
modulated laser beam generates periodic sound because of the pe-
riodic localized heating of the gas. The sound signal is generated in
those locations where light is absorbed by the species to be moni-
tored. In general, all PA trace gas measurements are carried out in an
enclosure, called a ‘‘PA cell,’’ including the resonator, buffer volumes,
acoustic filters, windows, gas inlets and outlets, and microphone.
The concentration of the absorbing gas species is related to the
sound signal as follows:
