Laser PA spectroscopy (LPAS) provides an efficient tool to perform
continuous, quasi-real time, highly sensitive, and selective detection
of several gaseous species without any irreversible modification in
the analyzed mixture. LPAS technique provides several advantages
over standard absorption spectroscopy methods: the absorbed energy
(not the transmitted energy) in the gas is measured so that no signal is
produced in the absence of the gas species and thus no background
signal is present; the PA signal is detected using a sensitive micro-
phone and is thus wavelength independent; the response of the LPAS
system is linear over several orders of magnitude with the gas con-
centration. Theoretically, one calibration point is sufficient to char-
acterize the sensor response; a simple sensor configuration makes
low (sub-ppm) to extremely low (ppb) detection limits possible; small
