INSTRUMENT DESIGN
In general, CO is the consequential product for most fires, espe-
cially in the early fire initiation process. Therefore, it is very effective
to use the concentration variation of CO in the indoor atmosphere air
as the signature of early warning of a fire hazard. It is easy for LPAS
technology to achieve the detection of limit less than 1 ppm, which is
sufficient to identify the potential fire risk early. However, there are
two factors to be considered to obtain the accurate CO information.
They are (1) the light attenuation effect caused by the smoke particles,
and (2) the effect of the variation of the vapor concentration during
the combustion.
In this article, a state-of-the-art hybrid fire detector combining
gaseous and particulate sensors is proposed to increase the reliability
of fire detection and reduce the response time for space missions. The
schematic of the system setup is shown in Figure 4. The system in-
cludes a smoke detector, a PAS gas detector, and a pump.
The smoke detector consists of a light laser diode (LD1) as the
incident light source, a 2-pass laser diode to sense forward scattered
light at 30 in respect to the incident light beam, and a labyrinth
structure. On the basis of the magnitude of the scattered light signal
referenced to the beam intensity, the smoke detector is designed to
monitor the obscuration rate, which is directly related to the smoke
particle size and the particle number density.
A gas detector using PA spectroscopy technology is integrated into
the system to simultaneously monitor the concentration of CO and
vapor in the sample gas. The gas detector is composed of two laser
diodes (LD2 for CO, LD3 for water vapor); a PA cell, where the PA
effect happens; and a high-sensitive microphone to measure the PA
signal. Because of the kinetic cooling effect, the PA signal of CO is
dependent on the air humidity. So it is necessary to measure the
vapor concentration in the sample gas to calculate the real CO con-
centration by using the PA signal. Moreover, the smoke particles
bring on the light attenuation effect to reduce the laser intensity that
