concentration of CO, the intensity of LD2 is compensated by the
measurement results of the obscuration rate from the smoke detector.
The laser diode sources, LD2 and LD3, are carefully selected at the
near-IR region where the lasers are commonly available with stable
product quality. The character wavelength of LD2 for CO detection is
selected at 2331.93 nm, where the cross interferences by H2O and CO2
are negligible as shown in Figure 5, while 1501.87 nm for H2O de-
tection. The maximum intensities of LD2 and LD3 are 4.5 and 10mW,
respectively.
The actual image of the integrated system for early fire detection is
shown in Figure 6. The sample gas is sucked from the atmospheric air
through the micropump to reduce the instrument response time less
than 2 s, even in the microgravity environment. The instrument is
designed to reliably operate 5 years on orbit, with a lightweight
(1.5 kg) and low power consumption (2 W).
EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS
The proposed instrument for early warning of fire
is first calibrated by the gas sampling system. The gas
sampling system comprises a dry laboratory nitrogen
source and a sample gas source, which are connected
to mass flow controllers; T-connectors in Teflon
tubing that mix the nitrogen and sample gas and
create the bypass flow for the PAS gas detector; and
in the end of the sample line is a flow meter, which
confirms that enough sample is provided to the gas
detector (Fig. 7).
The detection limits of the gas detector for CO and
H2O are calculated as the variation (2 · standard de-
viation) of 10 successive measurement results in 1 s
by setting the pure (99.9995%) nitrogen gas flow
bypass in the gas line. The typical measurement re-
sults of the CO and H2O gas detectors for 100 s are
shown in Figure 8. The statistical standard deviations are 1.69 mV for
CO and 1.77 mV for H2O. The zero-offset DC voltages are 5.34 mV for
CO and 10.65 mV for H2O.
The gas detector is calibrated by using the standard gases. The
calibrated concentration points are 23.2, 49.9, 68.0, and 94.1 ppm for
CO, and 0.53%, 1.48%, 2.20%, and 2.89% for H2O. The CO gas is mixed
with 2% H2O to eliminate the kinetic cooling effect of CO. The curve of
the calibrated concentrations with respect to the output signal after
subtracting the zero-offset voltage is shown in Figure 9. The sensi-
tivities of the gas detector are determined by the calibrated curve,
calculated as 11.16 mV/ppm for CO and 538 mV/% for H2O. Therefore,
the detection limits are 0.3 ppm for CO and 65.8 ppm for H2O.
To investigate the kinetic cooling effect of CO molecules, a series of
experiments were also conducted by using the CO gases with a
