shown in Figure 10, the reduction of the original CO output signal
increases with the decrease of the concentration of the water vapor.
The dependence relation can be fitted by a quadratic function, which
is used to compensate the measurement error of CO caused by the
kinetic cooling effect in the instrument.
The laser light scattering smoke detector is calibrated by using a
standard EN-54-type smoke alarm test tunnel. It is indicated that the
detection limit of the high-sensitive smoke detector can achieve
obscuration of 0.05% per meter. The experiment to monitor the
variation of CO and smoke during the early stage of a fire estab-
lishment was conducted. An electric cable covered by PVC plastic,
which is heated by the current 10 times more than the rating value,
was used as the fire source. The measurement results of the con-
centration of CO and smoke were recorded for 40 s as shown in Figure
11. The time when the CO detector or the smoke detector begins to
increase over the detection limits was set to t = 0. The cable was
burned at t = 41 s. The results showed that both the CO concentration
and the smoke obscuration started to increase simultaneously at
t = 6 s. The first peak of CO concentration was detected at t = 10 s,
while the smoke obscuration at t = 12 s. When t > 32 s, the measure-
ment values of both CO and smoke increased distinctly. The experi-
ment results testified that the proposed instrument, combining the PA
gas detector of CO with the laser scattering smoke detector, is capable
of providing the early warning of fire.
CONCLUSIONS
To provide an early warning of fire, which is a potential fatal risk
on a manned spacecraft, a long-term reliable, real-time, and accurate
fire detecting instrument is proposed in this article. The laser scat-
tering smoke detection technology and the near-IR laser PA trace gas
measurement technology are integrated in the new instrument. Be-
sides monitoring smoke obscuration with high sensitivity, the in-
strument is capable of accurately measuring the concentration
variation of CO, which is found to dramatically increase in nearly all
types of fire events.
The instrument is designed to reliably operate 5 years on orbit with
low weight (1.5 kg) and low power consumption (2 W). The mea-
surement error caused by the kinetic cooling effect of CO was com-
pensated simultaneously by the concentration of water vapor. The
detection limit of CO is lower to 0.3 ppm and obscuration sensitivity
of the smoke is 0.05%/m. The experiments show that the instrument
is reliable for providing early warning of fire.
