I.
Introduction
C
OOL flames have been studied extensively in the past, mainly in
premixed systems in connection with multistage ignition of fuel
mixtures (see, e.g., [1]). Recent droplet combustion experiments
conducted on board the International Space Station, however, have
shown that cool flames can support quasi-steady combustion of
normal-alkane (n-alkane) fuel droplets in a diffusion-flame mode [2],
which is a phenomenon previously unexpected. In these experiments,
a large, single n-alkane droplet is ignited in microgravity and initially
burns with a visible hot flame; then, it extinguishes through excessive
radiative heat loss. Following radiative extinction, the droplet
continues to burn vigorously but without any detected visible flame,
in a stage termed cool-flame combustion, which also ends abruptly at
a finite droplet radius and is followed by pure evaporation without
heat release. Since the cool flame consumes only part of thevaporized
fuel, a condensation cloud is also found to form, surrounding the
droplet toward the end of the cool-flame combustion process.
These
experimental
observations
have
been
successfully
simulated numerically, employing detailed chemistry for n-heptane
that included both low- and high-temperature kinetics [3–5]. A quasi-
steady theoretical model with reduced chemical kinetics for
n-heptane, formulated in mixture-fraction space, has also been
developed to predict cool-flame extinction diameters and the flame
structure [6]. These studies show that the cool-flame combustion of
droplets is stable only in the negative-temperature-coefficient (NTC)
regionwhere the reactivity decreases with increasing temperature [6].
An asymptotic analysis of cool-flame combustion is being developed
[7] for n-heptane droplets using the results of the “partial-burning
regime” of Liñán’s [8] general diffusion-flame structure.
Liñán, in his pioneering work [8], showed that, in the limit of large
activation energies, the structure of a diffusion flame, including
ignition and extinction, can be captured in terms of four separate
regimes: 1) a nearly frozen flow regime where ignition occurs, 2) a
partial-burning regime where both fuel and oxygen leak through the
flame, 3) a premixed-flame regime where only one of the reactants
leaks through the flame, and 4) a near-equilibrium diffusion-flame
regime. The partial-burning regime corresponds to the unstable
middle section of the S curve for hot flames but may be stabilized by
the chemical kinetics in the NTC stage [7].
Since computational results show that fuel vapor and oxygen both
leak through the flame substantially in this cool-flame droplet
combustion [6], the partial-burning regime is the most appropriate of
the four. This regime has a flame temperature centered around a
crossover temperature [9] in the middle of the NTC region. Above
