doi:10.2514/1.j054437,doi,Partial-Burning Regime for Quasi-Steady Droplet Combustion Supported by Cool Flames,2,1,"this temperature, the alkyl radicals are consumed mostly by high-
temperature chemistry involving hydrogen-oxygen chain branching.
Below the crossover temperature, the alkyl radicals are consumed
mostly by low-temperature chemistry involving a branched chain
carried by hydroxyl generated in the production and consumption of
alkylketohydroperoxides (AKHPs). These AKHP isomers, taken
together, can be considered a key molecule created through two
successive oxygen-molecule additions to an alkyl radical. The AKHP
effectively experiences autocatalytic production below crossover and
consumption above, thereby stabilizing the partial-burning regime.
Although this detailed chemistry involves more than 50 elementary
steps, it can be approximated by only a few steps, and as few as
one [7].
In this study, we show that the partial-burning regime can be used
to derive analytical expressions for n-alkane droplet-burning-rate
constants and for flame standoff ratios. The results are shown to
compare favorably in some respects, with the burning-rate data
presented in [10] and with computed flame standoff ratios [3], as well
as a new measurement of flame standoff ratios, reported here.
