A.
Flame Temperature
For normal diffusion flames controlled by hot-flame chemistry, the
flame temperature is related to the heat of combustion of the fuel, since
the fuel is completely consumed and equilibrium products are formed
at the flame. The cool flames, however, exist in the low-temperature
NTC region, and their flame temperature Tb is controlled by the
chemical kinetics of the NTC region [7]. A good approximation for the
flame temperature then is the crossover temperature T located in the
middle of the NTC region, where half of the alkyl radicals are oxidized
by the low-temperature chemical-kinetic mechanism and the other half
by the high-temperature mechanism. Seshadri et al. [7] provided an
empirical approximation for T for n-heptane using the 62-step
skeletal mechanism as a function of the ambient pressure patm and
oxygen mole fraction XO2, namely,
