III.
Results and Discussions
The predictions of Eq. (6) can be tested against the experimental
results for the cool-flame burning-rate constants reported in [10] for
n-heptane, n-octane, and n-decane. We assume that the crossover
temperature calculated with Eq. (4) is a good approximation for the
flame temperature Tb for all three n-alkane fuels considered here. In
calculating K using Eq. (6), we have followed the well-known
empirical recipe to evaluate the effective thermal conductivity and the
specific heat [11]. Both of these choices are somewhat questionable
for these cool flames and may be expected to introduce inaccuracies.
The primary variables in these tests were the droplet initial diameter,
which varied between 2 and 4 mm; the ambient oxygen concentration
between 14 and 21% by volume; and the level of carbon dioxide
dilution between 0 and 50% by volume. All of the tests considered
here were conducted at 1 atm pressure; the publication [10] also
reports results for four n-decane tests carried out at 1∕2 atm, but they
exhibit noticeably lower burning rates than calculated; whereas other
results at higher pressures (up to 3 atm) have more robust cool-flame
combustion than anticipated, suggesting that there is an as yet
unexplained pressure dependence in need of future investigation.
Figure 2 shows the 28 predicted values of K plotted versus the initial
droplet diameter, as well as a shaded region within which all of the
experimental values lie. Over the range of conditions of these
experiments, the calculated variations are quite small, with values of
B all lying between 5.34 and 6.70, with Zb at about 0.425, and with
Zl at about 0.856.
The predictions shown in Fig. 2 lie in the center of the range of the
experimental data, thereby providing support for the validity of the
theoretical analysis. The theory, however, predicts that there is no
influence of the initial diameter on the burning-rate constant, but the
data, despite their typical scatter of 20% or more, clearly suggest a
decrease of K with decreasing initial diameter. Although the value of
B is believed to decreasewith time during burning [6] so that a droplet
with a smaller initial diameter, which would be expected to enter its
cool-flame stage at a smaller diameter, would have a smaller average
burning rate, this effect seems unlikely to be large enough to explain
thedependence suggested by Fig.2. Further study of the influences of
the initial droplet diameter, as well as of pressure, are therefore
warranted. It should be noted that some of the experimental data
presented by Liu et al. [12] that corresponded to cool-flame
combustion (K5 in [12]) followed a similar trend with with initial
droplet diameter. For illustrative purposes, two experimental points
for heptane-air, taken from figure 27 of that paper, have been added
to Fig. 2.
In the absence of experimental measurements for the flame
standoff ratio (FSR) rb∕rl for cool flames, we compared the
theoretical predictions of Eq. (8) with the numerical computations of
Farouk and Dryer [3] for n-heptane droplets. Their results showed
that the FSR was essentially independent of the droplet initial
diameter and CO2 concentration, as long as the ambient oxygen
concentration remained fixed. With the O2 mole fraction fixed at 0.21
and the initial droplet diameter varying in the range of 2 to 7 mm, the
computed FSR varied slowly between 2.7 and 3.3 as the droplet
burned with cool-flame chemistry, reaching a maximum value just
before extinction. For a 3 mm droplet with an ambient CO2 mole
fraction varying between 0 and 0.7, the rest being nitrogen, at one
atmosphere pressure, the FSR varies again between 2.6 and 3.3
during cool-flame combustion in these computations. The time
variation in the computational results is consistent with the value of B
in our formula [Eq. (8)], decreasing with time during cool-flame
combustionas a result of the kinetically controlled flame-temperature
decrease, predicted by computation [6] but not included in the present
calculation because of the selected simplification in Eq. (4). The
theory presented here predicts an FSR of 3.1 for air and when CO2
replaces nitrogen with the O2 mole fraction fixed at 0.21, which falls
in the range of the computations, the time variation not having been
addressed here. Although this close agreement may appear somewhat
surprising because the corresponding classical quasi-steady theory
for hot flames overpredicts the FSR by as much as an order of
magnitude, if we consider the fact that the hot flames for n-alkanes
typically lie in the outer transient-diffusive region, where quasi-
steady theory is not valid, while the cool flames are established well
within the quasi-steady gas-phase region, close to the droplet surface,
then the results become understandable.
Very recently, in an effort to render the cool flames visible aboard
the International Space Station with the apparatus currently available
there, modifications were made to the experimental hardware and
procedures reported previously [13]. The spectral filter was removed
from the flame-view camera, and its intensifier gain was set to the
maximum value, to gather as much lightas possible from thevery dim
and weak cool flames. In addition, the illumination backlight for the
droplet view was set at its lowest possible value, to minimize any
stray light not arriving from the cool flame. Feedback from the
radiometers was then employed on the basisof previous experienceto
determine when the cool flame would be present, and the intensifier
gain was set essentially to zero during the hot flame to minimize the
possibility of damaging the camera, which was then automatically
increased to the maximum after the cool flames were detected by the
radiometers. The new experiments involved fiber-supported droplets
of decane burning in a 0.21/0.79 oxygen/nitrogen (by volume)
ambient environment at 1 atm pressure. The resultin"
doi:10.2514/1.j054437,doi,Partial-Burning Regime for Quasi-Steady Droplet Combustion Supported by Cool Flames,4,3,"IV.
Conclusions
A simplified quasi-steady theory using Liñán’s partial-burning
regime [8] is developed to predict droplet-burning-rate constants and
flame standoff ratios for n-alkane fuels. According to this prediction,
the burning-rate constant and the flame standoff ratio primarily
depend on the ambient oxygen concentration, which determines the
flame temperature in the negative-temperature-coefficient region,
and they are influenced very little by CO2 dilution. The predictions
are shown to compare reasonably well in magnitude with
experimental measurements for burning rates for three different
n-alkanes (namely, n-heptane, n-octane, and n-decane, at 1 atm),
although functional dependencies on pressure and on initial droplet
diameters appear not to be captured by the present model and deserve
further attention. The deficiencies likely are associated with
oversimplified transport descriptions, such as the assumption of a
Lewis number of unity, and not with the assumption of the chemistry
lying in the partial-burning regime, which should be quite reasonable
for these cool flames. In addition, both numerical simulations and the
present new experimental measurements for flame standoff ratios for
n-heptane (computationally) and decane (experimentally) are shown
to agree well with the present predictions, which is a further
indication that reasonable values for the transfer number B are
predicted by the assumed partial-burning regime. These results
should help in developing experimental requirements for the cool-
flame investigation planned for the International Space Station
by NASA.
