I. Introduction 
AN Diego State University’s Narrow Channel Apparatus (NCA) is being studied as a possible replacement for 
or complement to NASA-STD-(I)-6001A Test 1, NASA’s current test method used to conduct material 
flammability tests on non-metallic materials to be used onboard spacecraft [1]. Since Test 1 is an upward flame 
propagation test, buoyancy plays an important role in the flame behavior for these tests conducted in normal gravity.  
SDSU’s NCA is able to more appropriately simulate actual spacecraft ventilation conditions by controlling the 
opposed flow oxidizer velocity and suppressing buoyant effects on a horizontally spreading flame by spatially 
confining the flow with the top and bottom plates of the channel [2, 3].  Also, what sets the SDSU NCA apart from 
versions at NASA and Michigan State University is the ability to control the pressure [4]. These capabilities allow 
for flammability tests to be conducted with the partial pressure of oxygen kept constant at normal Earth levels, 
which is also known as a normoxic equivalent atmosphere.  These test conditions are of interest because future 
spacecraft cabin atmospheres are proposed to operate along the normoxic curve.  While Test 1 offers a simple 
pass/fail criterion for material flammability (in normal gravity), the SDSU NCA offers the capability to determine 
material flammability behavior at near-limit conditions in a forced-convective simulated microgravity environment 
at varying pressures, oxidizer velocities, and oxygen concentrations.  Examples of some of these comprehensive 
flammability maps are present in [3, 5, 6].  The SDSU NCA offers the possibility for a lower-cost alternative to 
achieve simulated microgravity flame spread over thin and thick fuels, while allowing for extensive studies on the 
effect of flow velocity, pressure, and oxygen concentration on the flame spread. At atmospheric conditions, the total 
gap height that offers an optimum between reducing buoyancy and heat loss to the confining walls was found to be 
10mm for a thin fuel [7]. This paper will report results for flame spread over Kimwipes®, a thin cellulose fuel, at 
varying pressures, oxygen concentrations, and velocities with a 10mm total gap height.  Free-fall flammability tests 
in the 5.2 second Drop Tower in the Zero-Gravity Research Facility at NASA Glenn Research Center achieve actual 
microgravity conditions, and are used to establish a reference for comparing simulated microgravity results from 
tests with Kimwipes® in Narrow Channel Apparatuses.    
 
II. Experimental Apparatus  
The SDSU Narrow Channel Apparatus is described in detail in a previous article [8].  A schematic of the test section 
is shown in Figure 1.  One change was made to the apparatus for the tests presented here.  A new stainless steel 
sample holder was machined in order to accommodate the shorter Kimwipe® samples.  In previous tests, a thicker 
cellulose fuel was used, known as Whatman 44 filter paper, and was sized to accommodate velocity or oxygen 
ramping tests because they burn slower than the thinner Kimwipes®.  This sample holder also allows for adjusting 
the gap height by using five pairs of 0-80 x 1” setscrews that run the length of the sample holder.  The new sample 
holder accommodates a 30.5 cm x 5.1 cm sample, is machined from Stainless Steel 302, and has a thickness of 
0.025”.  All tests were conducted with a 
constant gap height of 10 mm, leaving 5 
mm from the sample to the top quartz 
window, and 5 mm from the sample to 
the bottom plate.  The test section is 8.3 
cm wide and 100 cm long in the flow 
direction.  The inlet and outlet flow 
chambers 
contain 
honeycomb 
flow 
straighteners to ensure uniform flow at 
the 
inlet, 
and 
to 
minimize 
flow 
disturbances at the outlet.  A color camera 
is mounted above the channel and views the flame spread through the quartz window mounted in the lid of the 
channel.  Quick-release latches allow the top to be quickly removed to change the sample.  
 
 
The flow velocity and oxygen concentration in the SDSU NCA is controlled via remote computer commands 
to Alicat MC-50SLPM-D and MC-1SLPM-D mass flow controllers from a graphical user interface (GUI) created in 
IGOR Pro.  The two controlled gases, oxygen and nitrogen, each have a dedicated 50 SLPM and 1 SLPM Mass 
Flow Controller that, from calculations done in the GUI, allow the user to control average velocity and the mole 
fraction of oxygen in the flow through the channel.  Reduced pressure in the SDSU NCA is achieved with the use of 
a vacuum pump and vacuum regulator downstream of the test section [8].  The ability to control pressure and 
