III. Experimental Procedure 
The experiments were run with the oxidizer 
being a mix of dry, bottled nitrogen and oxygen, 
and Kimwipes® as the fuel.  The 5.1 cm wide by 
30.5 cm long sample is tautly taped to the 
stainless steel sample holder.  The sample holder 
is then positioned with the igniter wire cutout at 
the downstream end of the sample, to allow for 
opposed-flow burn tests.  Using dry, bottled air 
at a 5 cm/s opposed-flow velocity at 1 atm., the 
sample is dried for 10 minutes while the 
humidity in the outlet is measured.  Once the 
sample drying phase is complete (relative 
humidity stabilizes at about 8%), the desired 
flow velocity, oxygen composition, and pressure 
are set and desired flow is initiated.  The 
oxidizer flow temperature is measured with a 
thermistor installed upstream of the test section.  
Temperature and pressure corrections are made 
in the flow velocity set points by the IGOR 
program, if necessary. The sample is ready for 
ignition at this stage.  The sample is ignited by 
passing a current through a 27 gauge Kanthal® 
igniter wire, which heats the sample to ignition 
temperature.  Digital video is acquired by the 
computer once the flame spreads into the video 
Area of Interest (AOI). Video acquisition is 
complete once the flame reaches the end of the 
AOI.  Subsequent tests are performed by 
repeating the outlined procedure. 
                     
IV. Flame Spread Results 
The position of the leading edge of the flame 
is tracked using Spotlight-16 Software [9]. The 
program automatically follows the flame in the AOI by tracking, after image filtering, a user-specified intensity 
along a straight line in the direction of the flame spread.  The program outputs data in the form of position versus 
time of each tracked flame front, which can then be plotted to determine the flame spread rate.  Figure 2 shows an 
example of this plotted data for Kimwipes®, which indicates a characteristic linear position vs. time relationship.  
The results in Figure 2 are from a flame spread test with Kimwipes® done with 70.3 kPa pressure, 34% oxygen 
percentage, and 10 cm/s opposed flow velocity.  The AOI in Figure 2, “AOI1,” is at the center of the flame front.  
For the described test conditions, a linear curve fit of the data resulted in a flame spread rate of 1.85 cm/s, with an R-
squared correlation coefficient of 0.9996. 
 
Figure 3 shows the pressure and oxygen percent volume for all tests conducted with Whatman 44 and 
Kimwipes®.  Tests with Kimwipes were conducted off the normoxic curve at 34 % O2 and 40 % O2 to match tests 
from [10].   
 
 
 
