The results from this reduced 
pressure test follow the same 
trend as tests done at atmospheric pressure.  The series specified by red circles in Figure 4 show results from tests 
performed at conditions matching tests done in actual microgravity and in another Narrow Channel Apparatus at 
NASA Glenn Research Center [10].  One can see that at the higher flow velocity of approximately 10 cm/s and 1 
atm pressure, the SDSU NCA results (red circles) closely match results from actual microgravity tests done in 
NASA’s drop-tower (blue diamonds), as well as tests performed in NASA’s Narrow Channel Apparatus (purple 
squares).    At lower pressure, though, the spread rate data for the SDSU NCA is significantly lower than that of 
actual microgravity tests from NASA.  One should note that a monotonic spread rate is expected above flow 
velocities of approximately 10 cm/s [11], so the highest velocity data points for NASA 0g and NASA NCA are 
assumed to set the trend for these data series to allow comparison to the SDSU NCA results. 
 
The dissimilarity between the NASA and SDSU results at approximately 5 cm/s flow velocity could be 
explained by increased heat loss to the side walls of the SDSU NCA, which exhibits a much narrower channel width 
at 8.32 cm compared to NASA’s Narrow Channel.  The excess heat loss from the flame to the walls would cause a 
decrease in the flame spread rate that would not be present for an unenclosed flame.  The narrower channel width 
was chosen for SDSU’s NCA in order to 
minimize the bypass of oxidizer flow around the 
spreading flame that was evident in NASA’s 
wider channel.  In the NASA NCA, the flames 
were encountering oxidizer flow velocities lower 
than desired due to this bypass caused by the 
flame acting as a flow obstruction.  
