D. Scaling Analysis 
Previous work has been done in [7] to find the optimum gap height to achieve the best compromise between heat 
loss to the top and bottom boundaries and bouyancy suppression in the NCA.  At atmospheric pressure, the optimum 
gap height was found to be 10 mm, as previously mentioned.  Sidebotham et al. [7] performed a detailed scaling 
analysis for the overall stoichiometry of a flame spreading in a NCA, and also explored the effect of gap height on 
the gas preheat length.  Using the method outlined in [7], this paper presents a scaling analysis on the effect of 
pressure on the gas preheat length.  It is important to know the prescribed change in flame length scales in order to 
estimate to gap height requirements for future tests to be done at varying gap heights and reduced pressures.   
 
As presented in [7], the gas preheat length, δg, can be estimated by Equation 1: 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
