Where αg is the thermal diffusivity of the opposed flow gas mixture, h is the half gap-height, and Vox is the 
average oxidizer opposed flow velocity.  For tests done at atmospheric pressure, as done in [7], Equation 1 can be 
further simplified, but since tests in this paper were conducted at varying pressures, the equation cannot be 
simplified and the effect of pressure on the thermal diffusivity must be accounted for.  The thermal diffusivity is 
defined by:       (       )
⁄
. Here, kg is the gas thermal conductivity, ρox is the gas density, and cp,g is the gas 
constant-pressure specific heat capacity.  The thermal conductivity kg is taken to be constant as kair at 300K, and cp, g 
is taken to be constant as cp, air at 300K, since neither of these properties change appreciably with pressure.  After 
making these assumptions, one can see that the gas mixture density is the only parameter that is pressure dependent.  
Equation 1 is used to determine the effect of pressure on the gas preheat length.  The results of the analysis are 
presented in Figure 8.  
Figure 8 clearly shows that as pressure is decreased, the gas preheat length increases accordingly.  As pressure is 
reduced from 99 kPa to 28 kPa, the gas preheat length is almost doubled.  Assuming that the flame standoff distance, 
δq,  varies on the same scale as the gas preheat length, one could conclude that the gap height must be increase to 
allow for the larger flame standoff distance at the lower pressures.  This is why it is important to conduct reduced 
pressure tests at larger gap heights in the future. 
V. Conclusion 
A Narrow Channel Apparatus at San Diego State University has been shown to match NASA zero-gravity flame 
spread results at 1 atm pressure, and opposed flow velocity greater than 5 cm/s.  Tests conducted with the NCA at 
 
