reduced pressures did not agree so well with true microgravity results, but a scaling analysis shows that the gap 
height will need to be increased at lower pressures to account for the increase in gas preheat length and flame 
standoff distance.  Also, tests conducted at pressures lower than approximately 65 kPa exhibited a transition from a 
slower flame spread region to a region of much higher flammability that appears to be acoustically driven as 
evidenced by the sounds emanating from the NCA.  This could impose a limit on the NCA for use at low pressure, 
since the effect does not appear in microgravity and appears to be apparatus dependent.  
